Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00003 "α, β")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00003 "Stereodescriptors, used in a number of different ways.Relative stereodescriptors used in carbohydrate nomenclature to describe the configuration at the anomeric carbon by relating it to the anomeric reference atom. For simple cases the anomeric reference atom is the same as the configurational reference atom. Thus in $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!{-ᴅ-glucopyranose}}$ the reference atom is $\\ce{C-5}$ and the $\\ce{OH}$ at $\\ce{C-1}$ is on the same side as the $\\ce{OH}$ at $\\ce{C-5}$ in the Fischer projection.Relative stereodescriptors used by Chemical Abstracts Service to describe the configuration of a cyclic molecule (including suitable polycyclic systems) with several stereogenic centres whereby the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ side of the reference plane is the side on which the substituent with CIP priority lies at the lowest numbered stereogenic centre. The other side is $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$. Absolute stereodescriptors originally devised for steroid nomenclature. However in this sense it is only meaningful if there is an agreed absolute configuration and orientation of the structure so as to define the plane and which way up the molecule is represented. Substituents above the plane of the steroid are described as $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$ and are shown as a solid line ( or ); those below the plane are described as $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and are shown by a broken line ( or ). The extension of this system to tetrapyrroles has been documented and it has been widely used elsewhere.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00003 "alpha, beta")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00004 "α-cleavage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00004 "The fission of a bond originating at an atom which is adjacent to one assumed to bear the charge; the definition of $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}}$, $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ cleavage then follows automatically. The process:  would thus be described as $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}fission}$ of a ketone with expulsion of a radical $\\ce{R^{1.}}$ The carbon atoms of the radical $\\ce{R^{1.}}$ are called the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}}$, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}}$, $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}carbons}$, starting with the atom nearest the functional group.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00004 "alpha-cleavage")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00005 "α-decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00005 "Radioactive decay in which an alpha particle is emitted.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00005 "alpha-decay")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00006 "α-effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00006 "A positive deviation of an $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}nucleophile}$ (a nucleophile bearing an unshared pair of electrons on an atom adjacent to the nucleophilic site) from a Brønsted-type plot of \\(\\log k_{\\rm{nuc}}\\) vs. \\({\\rm{p}}K_{\\rm{a}}\\) constructed for a series of related normal nucleophiles. More generally, it is the influence of the atom bearing a lone pair of electrons on the reactivity at the adjacent site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00007 "α-elimination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00007 "A transformation of the general type:  where the central atom Z is commonly carbon. The reverse reaction is called α-addition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00009 "α-oxo carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00009 "Synonymous with acyl carbenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00010 "α-particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00010 "Nucleus of the $\\ce{^{4}He}$ atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00010 "alpha-particle")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00012 "'A' value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00012 "The conformational preference of an equatorial compared to an axial substituent in a monosubstituted cyclohexane. This steric substituent parameter equals \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) in \\(\\pu{kcal mol-1}\\) for the equatorial to axial equilibration on cyclohexane. The values are also known as 'Winstein–Holness' A values.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00012 "Winstein–Holness A value")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00013))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00013 "abatement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00013 "Action taken to reduce air pollution which involves the use of control equipment or some new process. This refers to a reduction or lessening as opposed to elimination of a type of discharge or pollutant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00014 "<i>abeo-</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00014 "Bond migration, i.e. the formal transfer of one end of a skeletal bond to another position, with a compensating transfer of a hydrogen atom, can be indicated by a prefix of the form '\\(x(y\\rightarrow z)abeo-\\)'. The prefix is compiled as follows: a numeral denoting the stationary (unchanged) end of the migrating bond (x) is followed by parentheses enclosing the locant denoting the original position (y) from which the other end of this bond has migrated, an arrow, and the locant (z) denoting the new position to which the bond has moved. The closing parenthesis is followed by the italicized prefix abeo- (Latin: I go away) to indicate bond migration. The original numbering is retained for the new compound and is used for the numbers x, y and z. It is always necessary to specify the resulting stereochemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00016 "abiotic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00016 "Not associated with living organisms. Synonymous with abiological.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00016 "abiological")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00017 "abiotic transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00017 "Process in which a substance in the environment is modified by non-biological mechanisms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00018))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00018 "absolute activation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00018 "A kind of activation analysis in which the elemental concentrations in the material are calculated from known nuclear constants, irradiation and measurement parameters, rather than by comparing with known standards.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00019 "absolute activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00019 "The exponential of the ratio of the chemical potential, \\(\\mu\\), to \\(R\\,T\\) where \\(R\\) is the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature, \\(\\lambda=\\rm{e}^{\\,\\mu/RT}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00020))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00020 "absolute configuration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00020 "The spatial arrangement of the atoms of a chiral molecular entity (or group) and its stereochemical description, e.g., R or S.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00021 "absolute counting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00021 "A measurement under such well-defined conditions that the activity of a sample can be derived directly from the observed counting rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00022 "absolute electrode potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00022 "The electrode potential of a metal measured with respect to a universal reference system (not including any additional metal/solution interface).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00023 "absolute full energy peak efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00023 "Of a radiation spectrometer, the counting efficiency when considering only the events recorded in the full energy peak.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00024 "absolute lethal concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00024 "Lowest concentration of a substance in an environmental medium which kills 100% of test organisms or species under defined conditions. This value is dependent on the number of organisms used in its assessment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00025 "absolute lethal dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00025 "Lowest amount of a substance which kills 100% of test animals under defined conditions. This value is dependent on the number of organisms used in its assessment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00026 "absolute photopeak efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00026 "Of a γ-ray spectrometer, the counting efficiency when only the events recorded in the photopeak are considered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00027 "absolute preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00027 "An operation (process) as the result of which microcomponents are transferred from the sample of larger mass into the sample of smaller mass, so that the concentration of the microcomponents is increased. Examples include the decrease in solvent volume during distillation or evaporation, and the transfer of microcomponents from an aqueous solution into a smaller volume of organic solvent by extraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00028 "absorbance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00028 "Logarithm of the ratio of incident to transmitted radiant power through a sample (excluding the effects on cell walls). Depending on the base of the logarithm a decadic and Napierian absorbance are used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00028 "extinction")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00028 "optical density")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00029 "absorbance matching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00029 "A procedure where the concentration of a known analyte may be determined by diluting the sample with solvent until the absorbance matches the absorbance of the analyte in the reference cell. This method is particularly useful if the Beer-Lambert law does not hold.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00030 "absorbed dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00030 "Amount of a substance absorbed into an organism or into organs and tissues of interest.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00031))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00031 "absorbed dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00031 "Energy imparted to matter by ionizing radiation in a suitable small element of volume divided by the mass of that element of volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00032 "absorbed electron coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00032 "Number of absorbed electrons per primary electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00033 "absorbed electrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00033 "The (excess) electrons present in a specimen under electron bombardment which are led to ground and measured as specimen current. The number of absorbed electrons per unit time (or the specimen current) equals the number of primary electrons minus the number of back scattered, secondary and transmitted electrons per unit time. Therefore the fraction of electrons being absorbed depends on many parameters, including the composition and thickness of the specimen, the primary electron energy, the electron incidence angle and local electrostatic fields when present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00034 "absorber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00034 "A device used commonly for sampling by absorption in which a gaseous or liquid material is removed from another gas or liquid by selective absorption; these include: scrubber, impinger, packed column, spray chamber, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00035 "absorptance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00035 "Ratio of the absorbed to the incident radiant power. When \\(\\alpha \\leq 1\\), \\(\\alpha \\approx A_{\\rm{e}}\\), where \\(A_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the Napierian absorbance.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00035 "absorption factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00036 "absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00036 "The process of one material (absorbate) being retained by another (absorbent); this may be the physical solution of a gas, liquid, or solid in a liquid, attachment of molecules of a gas, vapour, liquid, or dissolved substance to a solid surface by physical forces, etc. In spectrophotometry, absorption of light at characteristic wavelengths or bands of wavelengths is used to identify the chemical nature of molecules, atoms or ions and to measure the concentrations of these species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00037 "absorption coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00037 "Linear decadic (\\(a\\),\\(K\\)) and Napierian absorption coefficients (\\(\\alpha \\)) are equal to the corresponding absorbances divided by the optical path length through the sample. The molar absorption coefficients (decadic \\(\\varepsilon\\), Napierian \\(\\kappa \\)) are the linear absorption coefficients divided by the amount concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00038))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00038 "absorption cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00038 "Molar Napierian absorption coefficient divided by the Avogadro constant. When integrated against the logarithm of wavenumber (or frequency) it is called the integrated absorption cross-section.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00040 "absorption intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00040 "Differently defined physical quantities describing the absorption of radiation by a sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00041 "absorption line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00041 "A narrow range of wavelengths in which a substance absorbs light; a series of discrete absorption lines can be used as an unambiguous identification for many relatively simple chemical species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00042 "absorption pathlength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00042 "The length of the radiation path through the absorbing medium; it is equal to the cell path length, \\(l\\), in the case of single-pass cells at normal incidence of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00043))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00043 "absorption spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00043 "The wavelength dependence of the absorption cross-section (or absorption coefficient); usually represented as a plot of absorption cross-section versus wavelength \\(\\lambda\\) (or \\(\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\)) of the light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00044))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00044 "absorptivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00044 "Absorptance divided by the optical path length. For very low attenuance it approximates the absorption coefficient [within the approximation \\((1 - e^{-A}) \\sim A\\)]. The use of this term is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00045 "abstraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00045 "A chemical reaction or transformation, the main feature of which is the bimolecular removal of an atom (neutral or charged) from a molecular entity. For example:  (proton abstraction from acetone)  (hydrogen abstraction from methane)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00046 "abstraction process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00046 "In abstraction and extraction processes, an adsorptive or adsorbate species extracts an adsorbed atom or a lattice atom respectively. Abstraction process: Extraction process:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00048 "abundance sensitivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00048 "The ratio of the maximum ion current recorded at a mass \\(m\\) to the ion current arising from the same species recorded at an adjacent mass (\\(m\\pm 1\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00050 "accelerating voltage scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00050 "This is an alternative method of producing a momentum (mass) spectrum in magnetic deflection instruments by varying the accelerating voltage. This scan can also be used, in conjunction with a fixed radial electric field, to produce an ion kinetic energy spectrum.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00050 "high voltage scan")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00051 "acceleration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00051 "Vector quantity equal to the derivative of velocity with respect to time. For the acceleration of free fall the symbol \\(g\\) is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00053 "acceleration of free fall")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00053 "Acceleration of free fall in vacuum due to gravity. Also called acceleration due to gravity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00056 "acceptor number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00056 "A quantitative measure of Lewis acidity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00057 "accommodation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00057 "A measure of the efficiency of capture of molecules or atoms which collide with aerosol particles, cloud droplets, etc. The accommodation coefficient is the fraction of the collisions which result in the capture of the molecules (atoms, radicals, etc.) by the particle, cloud droplet, etc.; fraction of colliding molecules which are not reflected but which enter the surface of an aqueous aerosol. Synonymous with sticking coefficient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00058 "accretion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00058 "The process by which aerosols grow in size by external addition of various chemical species; a form of agglomeration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00059 "accuracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00059 "Ability of a measuring instrument to give responses close to a true value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00060 "accuracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00060 "Closeness of the agreement between the result of a measurement and a true value of the measurand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00061 "acenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00061 "Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons consisting of fused benzene rings in a rectilinear arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00062 "acetals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00062 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C(OR')2}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$) and thus diethers of geminal diols. Originally, the term was confined to derivatives of aldehydes (one $\\ce{R = H}$), but it now applies equally to derivatives of ketones (neither $\\ce{R = H}$). Mixed acetals have different $\\ce{R'}$ groups.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00064 "acetonides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00064 "Cyclic acetals derived from acetone and diols, usually vicinal diols, or polyhydroxy compounds. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00065 "acetylene black")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00065 "A special type of carbon black formed by an exothermic decomposition of acetylene. It is characterized by the highest degree of aggregation and crystalline orientation when compared with all types of carbon black.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00066 "acetylenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00066 "Acyclic (branched or unbranched) and cyclic (with or without side chain) hydrocarbons having one or more carbon–carbon triple bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00067 "acetylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00067 "Compounds arising by replacement of one or both hydrogen atoms of acetylene (ethyne) by a metal or other cationic group. E.g., $\\ce{NaC#CH}$ monosodium acetylide. By extension, analogous compounds derived from terminal acetylenes, $\\ce{RC#CH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00068))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00068 "Acheson graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00068 "A synthetic graphite made by the Acheson process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00071 "acid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00071 "A molecular entity or chemical species capable of donating a hydron (proton) (see Brønsted acid) or capable of forming a covalent bond with an electron pair (see Lewis acid).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00072 "acid anhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00072 "Compounds consisting of two acyl groups bonded to the same oxygen atom acyl-O-acyl. Symmetric and mixed anhydrides have identical and different acyl groups, respectively. Examples,acetic anhydride  acetic benzenesulfonic anhydride  (thiobenzoic) anhydride")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00072 "anhydrides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00075 "acid–base indicator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00075 "An acid or base which exhibits a colour change on neutralization by the basic or acidic titrant at or near the equivalence point of a titration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00077 "acid deposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00077 "The acids deposited by transfer from the atmosphere either by precipitation (rain, fog, snow, etc.), called wet deposition, or by direct transfer to the surface of the earth induced by the flow of acid-containing air masses over the earth's surface, called dry deposition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00079 "acidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00079 "Of a compound: For Brønsted acids the tendency of a compound to act as a hydron donor. It can be quantitatively expressed by the acid dissociation constant of the compound in water or some other specified medium. For Lewis acids it relates to the association constants of Lewis adducts and π adducts.Of a medium: The use of the term is mainly restricted to a medium containing Brønsted acids, where it means the tendency of the medium to hydronate a specific reference base. It is quantitatively expressed by the appropriate acidity function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00080 "acidity constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00080 "The equilibrium constant \\(K_{\\rm{a}n}\\) for splitting off the nth proton from a charged or uncharged acid, to be defined. One may write \\(K_{\\rm{a}}\\) for \\(K_{\\rm{a}1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00081 "acidity function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00081 "Any function that measures the thermodynamic hydron-donating or -accepting ability of a solvent system, or a closely related thermodynamic property, such as the tendency of the lyate ion of the solvent system to form Lewis adduct. (The term 'basicity function' is not in common use in connection with basic solutions.) Acidity functions are not unique properties of the solvent system alone, but depend on the solute (or family of closely related solutes) with respect to which the thermodynamic tendency is measured. Commonly used acidity functions refer to concentrated acidic or basic solutions. Acidity functions are usually established over a range of composition of such a system by UV/VIS spectrophotometric or NMR measurements of the degree of hydronation (protonation or Lewis adduct formation) for the members of a series of structurally similar indicator bases (or acids) of different strength: the best known of these functions is the Hammett acidity function \\(H_{0}\\) (for uncharged indicator bases that are primary aromatic amines).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00082 "acidosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00082 "Pathological condition in which the hydrogen ion substance concentration of body fluids is above normal and hence the pH of blood falls below the reference interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00083 "acid rain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00083 "Rain with \\(\\rm{pH}\\) values &lt; about 5; commonly results from acids formed from pollutants. 'Pure' rain water equilibrated with atmospheric $\\ce{CO2}$ and naturally occurring acids in relatively clean air usually has a \\(\\rm{pH} > 5\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00085 "<i>aci</i>-nitro compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00085 "A class name for hydrocarbylideneazinic acids, $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}(–O^{−})OH}$. The use of aci-nitro as a prefix in systematic nomenclature to name specific compounds is abandoned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00086 "actinic flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00086 "The quantity of light available to molecules at a particular point in the atmosphere and which, on absorption, drives photochemical processes in the atmosphere. It is calculated by integrating the spectral radiance \\(L \\left(\\lambda,\\,\\theta,\\,\\varphi\\,\\right)\\) over all directions of incidence of the light, \\(S_{\\lambda} = \\int _{\\theta}\\, \\int _{\\phi} \\{L\\left (\\lambda,\\theta,\\varphi \\right)/(hc/\\lambda)\\}\\, \\rm{sin}\\,\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\varphi\\). If the radiance is expressed in \\(\\pu{J m-2 s-1 st-1 nm-1}\\) and \\(hc/\\lambda\\) is the energy per quantum of light of wavelength \\(\\lambda\\), the actinic flux has units of \\(\\rm{quanta cm}^{-2}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). This important quantity is one of the terms required in the calculation of j-values, the first order rate coefficients for photochemical processes in the sunlight-absorbing, trace gases in the atmosphere. The actinic flux is determined by the solar radiation entering the atmosphere and by any changes in this due to atmospheric gases and particles (e.g. Rayleigh scattering absorption by stratospheric ozone, scattering and absorption by aerosols and clouds), and reflections from the ground. It is therefore dependent on the wavelength of the light, on the altitude and on specific local environmental conditions. The actinic flux has borne many names (e.g. flux, flux density, beam irradiance, actinic irradiance, integrated intensity) which has caused some confusion. It is important to distinguish the actinic flux from the spectral irradiance, which refers to energy arrival on a flat surface having fixed spatial orientation (\\(\\pu{J m-2 nm-1}\\)) given by: \\[E_{\\lambda} = \\int _{\\theta}\\, \\int _{\\phi} L\\left (\\lambda,\\theta,\\varphi \\right )\\, \\rm{cos}\\,\\theta  \\: \\rm{sin}\\,\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\varphi\\] The actinic flux does not refer to any specific orientation because molecules are oriented randomly in the atmosphere. This distinction is of practical relevance: the actinic flux (and therefore a j-value) near a brightly reflecting surface (e.g. over snow or above a thick cloud) can be a factor of three higher than that near a non-reflecting surface. The more descriptive name of spectral spheradiance is suggested for the quantity herein called actinic flux.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00087 "actinometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00087 "A chemical system or physical device which determines the number of photons in a beam integrally or per unit time. This name is commonly applied to devices used in the ultraviolet and visible wavelength ranges. For example, solutions of iron(III) oxalate can be used as a chemical actinometer, while bolometers, thermopiles, and photodiodes are physical devices giving a reading that can be correlated to the number of photons detected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00088 "action spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00088 "A plot of a relative biological or chemical photoresponse (= \\(\\Delta y\\)) per number of incident photons against wavelength or energy of radiation under the same radiant power of light. This form of presentation is frequently used in the studies of biological or solid state systems, where the nature of the absorbing species is unknown. This type of action spectrum is sometimes called spectral responsivity or sensitivity spectrum. The precise action spectrum is a plot of the spectral or quantum effectiveness. By contrast, a plot of the biological or chemical change or response per absorbed photon (quantum efficiency) versus wavelength is the efficiency spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00089 "activated adsorption process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00089 "If the temperature coefficient of the rate of adsorption is substantial, an adsorption process is said to be activated (i.e. to have a significant activation energy). In this case, the sticking coefficient is small. In general, the activation energy of activated adsorption is a function of coverage and it usually increases with increasing coverage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00090))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00090 "activated carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00090 "A porous carbon material, a char which has been subjected to reaction with gases, sometimes with the addition of chemicals, e.g. $\\ce{ZnCl2}$, before, during or after carbonization in order to increase its adsorptive properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00091 "activated charcoal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00091 "A traditional term for activated carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00092 "activated complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00092 "An activated complex, often characterized by the superscript ‡, is defined as that assembly of atoms which corresponds to an arbitrary infinitesimally small region at or near the col (saddle point) of a potential energy surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00093 "activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00093 "This word is used in different senses:   Input of external energy into a chemical system is said to bring about activation of the system.   An added substance that increases the rate of a catalysed reaction is known as an activator, and the effect is called activation. The degree of activation \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{a}}\\) is defined by: \\[\\varepsilon_{\\rm{a}}=\\frac{v\\,-\\,v_{0}}{v_{0}} = \\frac{v}{v_{0}} - 1\\] where \\(v_{0}\\) is the rate of the catalysed reaction in the absence of the activator and \\(v\\) is the rate of the catalysed reaction in the presence of the activator.  When some of the energy required for a reaction to occur is provided by a previous exothermic chemical reaction there is said to be chemical activation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00094 "activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00094 "The process of transition from the passive to the active state by removal of the passivating film. A necessary condition for activation is an electrode potential negative to the equilibrium potential of formation of the passivating film. Activation is achieved by cathodic currents, by a reduced substance in the adjacent solution, or by contact with an electronic conductor having a suitably negative corrosion potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00095 "activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00095 "The process of inducing radioactivity by irradiation. In general, a specification is added of the type of incident radiation (e.g. nuclear, neutron, photon) or its energy (e.g. thermal, fast).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00096 "activation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00096 "A kind of elemental or isotopic analysis based on the measurement of characteristic radiation from nuclides formed directly or indirectly by activation of the test portion. In general, a specification is added of the type of the incident radiation (e.g. neutron, photon) and its energy (e.g. thermal, fast).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00096 "nuclear activation analysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00101 "activation cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00101 "The cross-section for the formation of a radionuclide by a specified reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00102 "activation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00102 "An empirical parameter characterizing the exponential temperature dependence of the rate coefficient, \\(k\\), \\(E_{a} = R\\, T^{2}\\frac{\\rm{d(ln}k\\rm{)}}{\\rm{d}T}\\), where \\(R\\) is the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature. The term is also used for threshold energies in electronic potential surfaces, in which case the term requires careful definition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00102 "Arrhenius activation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00102 "energy of activation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00103 "activation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00103 "Process leading from the reactants to the formation of an activated complex or transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00104))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00104 "activator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00104 "A substance, other than the catalyst or one of the substrates, that increases the rate of a catalysed reaction without itself being consumed; the process is called activation. An activator of an enzyme-catalysed reaction may be called enzyme activator, if it acts by binding to the enzyme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00107 "active metal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00107 "A metal corroding in the active state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00108))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00108 "active site")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00108 "The term is often applied to those sites for adsorption which are the effective sites for a particular heterogeneous catalytic reaction. The terms active site and active centre are often used as synonyms, but active centre may also be used to describe an ensemble of sites at which a catalytic reaction takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00109 "active solid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00109 "A porous solid with adsorptive properties by means of which chromatographic separations may be achieved. The separations resulting from this action follow laws different from those deriving from the partitioning action of the liquid phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00111))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00111 "active state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00111 "Corrosion in the active state occurs by direct transfer (which may involve one or several steps) of metal ions from the metallic phase to the adjacent electrolyte. The corrosion current of an active metal usually increases when the electrode potential is made more positive, other conditions remaining constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00112))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00112 "active transport")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00112 "The carriage of a solute across a biological membrane from low to high concentration which requires the expenditure of (metabolic) energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00114))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00114 "activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00114 "The number of nuclear decays occurring in a given quantity of material in a small time interval, divided by that time interval. Often, this term is referred to as absolute activity. Symbol \\(A=- \\frac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\). Synonymous with disintegration rate or decay rate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00114 "decay rate")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00114 "disintegration rate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00115 "activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00115 "Defined by the equation \\[a = e^{\\frac{\\mu - \\mu^{o}}{R\\,T}}\\] where \\(R\\) is the gas constant, \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature, \\(\\mu\\) the chemical potential and \\(\\mu^{o}\\) the standard chemical potential the definition of which depends on the choice of the standard state.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00115 "relative activity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00116 "activity coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00116 "The activity coefficient \\(f_{\\rm{B}}\\) of a substance B in a liquid or solid mixture containing mole fractions \\(x_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(x_{\\rm{C}}\\), ... of the substances B, C, ... is a dimensionless quantity defined in terms of the chemical potential \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}\\) of B in the mixture by: \\[R\\ T\\ \\ln (x_{\\rm{B}}\\ f_{\\rm{B}})=\\mu _{\\rm{B}}\\left(\\rm{cd},\\,T,\\,P,\\,x\\right)- \\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{*}\\left(\\rm{cd},\\,T,\\,p\\right)\\] where \\(x\\) denotes the set of mole fractions \\(x_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(x_{\\rm{C}}\\), ... . The activity coefficient \\(\\gamma _{\\rm{B}}\\) of a solute B in a solution (especially a dilute liquid solution) containing molalities \\(m_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(m_{\\rm{C}}\\), ... of solutes B, C, ... in a solvent A is a dimensionless quantity defined in terms of the chemical potential \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}\\) by: \\[R\\ T\\ \\ln \\left(\\frac{m_{\\rm{B}}\\ \\gamma _{\\rm{B}}}{m^{\\ominus}}\\right)=\\mu _{\\rm{B}}- \\left\\{\\mu _{\\rm{B}}- R\\ T\\ \\ln \\left(\\frac{m_{\\rm{B}}}{m^{\\ominus }}\\right)\\right\\}^{\\infty }\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00119 "acylals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00119 "Diesters of geminal diols; i.e.  E.g. benzylidene diacetate:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00121 "acyl carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00121 "Any compound . In organic chemistry, an unspecified acyl carbene is commonly a carboxylic acyl carbene, .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00123 "acyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00123 """Groups formed by removing one or more hydroxy groups from oxoacids that have the general structure $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$), and replacement analogues of such acyl groups. In organic chemistry an unspecified acyl group is commonly a carboxylic acyl group. E.g.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00124 "acyl halides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00124 "Compounds consisting of an acyl group bonded to halogen, e.g. $\\ce{CH3S(=O)2Cl}$, methanesulfonyl chloride; $\\ce{CH3COCl}$, acetyl chloride; cyclohexanecarboximidoyl chloride,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00126))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00126 "acyloins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00126 "$\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}Hydroxy}$ ketones, $\\ce{RCH(OH)C(=O)R}$. So named from the fact that they are formally derived from reductive coupling of carboxylic acyl groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00127))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00127 "acyloxyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00127 "Oxygen-centered radicals consisting of an acyl radical bonded to an oxygen atom. E.g. .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00129))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00129 "acyl species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00129 """Acyl intermediates include acyl anions, acyl radicals and acyl cations (synonym acylium ions) which are formally derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by removal of a hydroxyl cation $\\ce{HO^{+}}$, a hydroxyl radical $\\ce{HO^{.}}$ or a hydroxyl anion $\\ce{HO^{-}}$, respectively, and replacement analogues of such intermediates. Acyl anions, radicals and cations can formally be represented by canonical forms having a negative charge, an unpaired electron or a positive charge on the acid-generating element of the oxoacid.Acyl anions, e.g. Acyl radicals, e.g. Acyl cations, e.g.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00132 "addition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00132 "Refers to addition reaction or addition transformation.Loosely, the formation of an adduct. (For an example, see Lewis acid.)Loosely, any association or attachment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00133))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00133 "addition reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00133 "A chemical reaction of two or more reacting molecular entities, resulting in a single reaction product containing all atoms of all components, with formation of two chemical bonds and a net reduction in bond multiplicity in at least one of the reactants. The reverse process is called an elimination reaction. The addition may occur at only one site (α-addition, 1/1/addition), at two adjacent sites (1/2/addition) or at two non-adjacent sites (1/3/- or 1/4/addition, etc.). For example:   If the reagent or the source of the addends of an addition are not specified, then it is called an addition transformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00134 "additive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00134 "A substance added to a sample for any of a variety of purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00135 "additive name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00135 "The formal assembly of names for the components of a compound without loss of atoms or groups of atoms from any componentThe addition or attachment of atoms or groups of atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00136 "additivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00136 "The process by which each chemical species present in the ion source at a certain partial pressure makes a contribution to the total mass spectrum which is the same as that which it would give if that chemical species alone were present in the ion source at a pressure equal to this certain partial pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00137))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00137 "additivity principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00137 "The hypothesis that each of several structural features of a molecular entity makes a separate and additive contribution to a property of the substance concerned. More specifically, it is the hypothesis that each of the several substituent groups in a parent molecule makes a separate and additive contribution to the standard Gibbs energy change (or Gibbs energy of activation) corresponding to a particular equilibrium (or rate of reaction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00138))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00138 "adduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00138 "A new chemical species AB, each molecular entity of which is formed by direct combination of two separate molecular entities A and B in such a way that there is change in connectivity, but no loss, of atoms within the moieties A and B. Stoichiometries other than 1:1 are also possible, e.g. a bis-adduct (2:1). An intramolecular adduct can be formed when A and B are groups contained within the same molecular entity. This is a general term which, whenever appropriate, should be used in preference to the less explicit term complex. It is also used specifically for products of an addition reaction. For examples, see Lewis adduct, Meisenheimer adduct, π-adduct.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00139 "adduct ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00139 "An ion formed by interaction of two species, usually an ion and a molecule, and often within the ion source, to form an ion containing all the constituent atoms of one species as well as an additional atom or atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00140 "adhesional wetting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00140 "A process in which an adhesional joint is formed between two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00141 "adiabatic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00141 "This word is used with various different meanings, and when it is used it should be defined. In thermodynamics 'adiabatic' is used in a macroscopic sense to refer to a process occurring in a thermally insulated system, so that there is no flow of heat to or from the surroundings. In reaction dynamics, the word has been used in a microscopic sense, with a range of meanings which have only a tenuous relationship to the thermodynamic meaning or the etymology. Whereas the thermodynamic meaning relates to conditions imposed on a process by an observer, the microscopic meaning relates to conditions under which the process occurs naturally. The microscopic meanings, as used in reaction dynamics, all have in common the feature that quantum states remain unchanged during the course of reaction. Different quantum states may be referred to: A reaction that is not adiabatic is referred to as nonadiabatic or diabatic, and some workers make a distinction between the two words.A reaction in which there is no change of electronic state or multiplicity has been called adiabatic, or more specifically electronically adiabatic. A reaction in which there is no change of vibrational state during the course of reaction has been said to be vibrationally adiabatic. More loosely, the expression has been applied to a process in which excess vibrational energy in the reactants appears as vibrational energy in the products, or in which ground-state vibration in the reactants leads to ground state vibration in the products.A reaction in which excess rotational energy in the reactants appears as rotational energy in the products, or in which ground-state rotation in the reactants leads to ground-state rotation in the products, has been referred to as rotationally adiabatic.In the Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus (RRKM) theory of unimolecular reactions, a degree of freedom whose quantum number is more or less preserved during energization and subsequent reaction has been called 'adiabatic'; the word 'inactive' has also been applied to it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00142 "adiabatic electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00142 "Electron transfer process in which the reacting system remains on a single electronic surface in passing from reactants to products. For adiabatic electron transfer the electronic transmission factor is close to unity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00143 "adiabatic ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00143 "A process whereby an electron is either removed from or added to an atom or molecule producing an ion in its ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00144 "adiabatic lapse rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00144 "The rate of decrease in temperature with increase in altitude of an air parcel which is expanding slowly to a lower atmospheric pressure without exchange of heat; for a descending parcel it is the rate of increase in temperature with decrease in altitude. Theory predicts that for dry air it is equal to the acceleration of gravity divided by the specific heat of dry air at constant pressure (approximately \\(9.8\\ ^{\\unicode{x26ac}} \\rm{C}\\ \\rm{km}^{-1}\\)). The moist adiabatic lapse rate is less than the dry adiabatic lapse rate and depends on the moisture content of the air mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00145 "adiabatic photoreaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00145 "Within the 'Born-Oppenheimer' approximation, a reaction of an excited state species that occurs on a single 'potential-energy surface'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00146 "adiabatic transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00146 "A form of transition-state theory in which the system is assumed to preserve its internal quantum states as it moves over the potential-energy surface. A detailed state-to-state scattering theory version of adiabatic transition-state theory has been referred to as the adiabatic channel model.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00147 "adiabatic treatments of reaction rates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00147 "These are treatments of reaction rates in which the system is assumed to remain on a single potential-energy surface during the entire course of reaction, or to remain in a conserved internal state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00148 "adjacent re-entry model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00148 "A model of crystallinity in which chain folds regularly connect adjacent stems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00150 "adjuvant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00150 "In pharmacology, a substance added to a drug to speed or increase the action of the main component.In immunology, a substance (such as aluminium hydroxide) or an organism (such as bovine tuberculosis bacillus) which increases the response to an antigen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00152 "adsorbate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00152 "A molecular species of gas, dissolved substance or liquid which adheres to or is adsorbed in an extremely thin surface layer of a solid substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00153 "adsorbent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00153 "A condensed phase at the surface of which adsorption may occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00154 "adsorber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00154 "Solid material used to capture either a gas or liquid; often activated carbon is employed as the solid because of its high surface area per unit mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00155))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00155 "adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00155 "An increase in the concentration of a dissolved substance at the interface of a condensed and a liquid phase due to the operation of surface forces. Adsorption can also occur at the interface of a condensed and a gaseous phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00156 "adsorption capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00156 "For strongly adsorbed solutes of limited solubility, the value of the amount of adsorbed substance reached in a saturated solution is called the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for a specific solute; its value depends also, in general, on the nature and, in the case of more than two components, on the relative composition of the bulk liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00157))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00157 "adsorption chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00157 "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences between the adsorption affinities of the sample components for the surface of an active solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00158 "adsorption complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00158 "The entity constituted by the adsorbate and the part of the adsorbent to which it is bound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00159 "adsorption current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00159 "A faradaic current whose magnitude depends on the applied potential, and, at any particular applied potential, on the rate or extent of the adsorption of an electroactive substance (or the product obtained from the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance) onto the surface of the indicator or working electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00161 "adsorption hysteresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00161 "Adsorption hysteresis is said to occur when adsorption and desorption values deviate from one another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00162 "adsorption indicator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00162 "A substance which is adsorbed or desorbed with concomitant colour change at or near the equivalence point of a titration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00163 "adsorption isobar")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00163 "The function relating the amount, mass, or volume, or corresponding excess of substance adsorbed by a given amount of solid, to the temperature at constant pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00164 "adsorption isostere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00164 "The function relating the equilibrium pressure to the temperature at a constant value of the amount, or excess amount, of substance adsorbed by a given amount of solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00166 "adsorptive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00166 "The material that is present in one or other (or both) of the bulk phases and capable of being adsorbed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00168 "advection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00168 "The transport of air, its properties (such as heat), trace materials, fog, cold air, etc., solely by mass motion of the atmosphere, generally in a horizontal direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00169 "aeration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00169 "The process by which a volume filled with a liquid becomes permeated with air or another gas; aeration is often accomplished by spraying the liquid into the air, bubbling air through a liquid or agitating the liquid to promote surface absorption of air.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00170 "aerobe")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00170 "An organism that needs oxygen for respiration and hence for growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00171 "aerobic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00171 "Requiring molecular oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00172))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00172 "aerobic conditions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00172 "Conditions for growth or metabolism in which the organism is sufficiently supplied with oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00173 "aerogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00173 "Gel comprised of a microporous solid in which the dispersed phase is a gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00174 "aerometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00174 "Instrument used to measure the density of gases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00175 "aerometric measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00175 "A measurement of the temperature, pressure, air motion (velocity vectors), or other physical measurement to characterize an air mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00176 "aerosol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00176 "Sol in which the dispersed phase is a solid, a liquid or a mixture of both and the continuous phase is a gas (usually air).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00177 "affinity chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00177 "An expression characterizing the particular variant of chromatography in which the unique biological specificity of the analyte and ligand interaction is utilized for the separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00178 "affinity of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00178 "Negative partial derivative of Gibbs energy with respect to extent of reaction at constant pressure and temperature. It is positive for spontaneous reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00179 "after mass analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00179 "The sum of all the separate ion currents carried by the different ions contributing to the spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00180 "ageing of precipitate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00180 "The time-dependent change of those properties of a precipitate, e.g. loss of water, growth of crystals, recrystallization, decrease of the specific surface, loss of coprecipitated substances, which generally improve the filtering properties. The process of ageing is very often promoted by maintaining the precipitate and precipitation medium together at elevated temperatures for a period of time. The terms chemical, physical and thermal ageing may be used in cases in which some of the (usually combined) effects named above are to be emphasized specifically.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00182 "agglomeration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00182 "Process of contact and adhesion whereby dispersed particles are held together by weak physical interactions ultimately leading to phase separation by the formation of precipitates of larger than colloidal size.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00182 "coagulation")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00182 "flocculation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00183 "agglutination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00183 "An immunochemical reaction leading to the aggregation of particulate matter such as bacteria, erythrocytes or other cells, or synthetic particles such as plastic beads coated with antigens or antibodies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00184 "aggregate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00184 "Certain materials used as catalysts or supports consist of spheroids smaller than \\(10\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in diameter, cemented into larger entities. A primary particle should be defined as the smallest discrete identifiable entity and the method of identification should be mentioned (e.g. transmission electron microscopy, scanning electron microscopy). An assemblage of such primary particles exhibiting an identifiable collective behaviour (e.g. chemical nature of the aggregated primary particles, texture of the aggregate, resistance to mechanical separation upon grinding) constitutes an aggregate. When describing the aggregates the criterion of identification should be mentioned. Strongly bonded aggregates are called agglomerates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00185 "aglycon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00185 "The non-sugar compound remaining after replacement of the glycosyl group from a glycoside by a hydrogen atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00185 "aglycone")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00186 "agonist")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00186 "Substance which binds to cell receptors normally responding to naturally occurring substances and which produces a response of its own.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00187))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00187 "agostic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00187 "The term designates structures in which a hydrogen atom is bonded to both a carbon atom and a metal atom. The term is also used to characterize the interaction between a $\\ce{CH}$ bond and an unsaturated metal centre, and to describe similar bonding of a transition metal with $\\ce{Si–H}$ compounds. The expression '$\\mu$-hydrido-bridged' is also used to describe the bridging hydrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00188 "agranular carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00188 "A monogranular or monolithic carbon material with homogeneous microstructure which does not exhibit any structural components distinguishable by optical microscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00190))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00190 "air contaminant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00190 "A substance, gaseous material or aerosol, which is present in an air mass at levels greater than in clean air. An air contaminant has been added commonly by anthropogenic activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00191 "air-lift bioreactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00191 "A bioreactor in which the reaction medium is kept mixed and gassed by introduction of air or another gas (mixture) at the base of a column-like reactor equipped either with a draught tube or another device (e.g. external tube) by which the reactor volume is separated into a gassed and an ungassed region thus generating a vertically circulating flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00192 "air mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00192 "A qualitative term to describe a widespread body of air with approximately uniform characteristics which had been identified at a given time over a particular region of the earth's surface. Sometimes an air mass is marked by inert tracers such as $\\ce{SF6}$ which may be added to it. The composition of a given air mass undergoes alteration as it migrates, chemical changes occur, compounds are removed by dry and wet deposition and new impurities are added to the mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00193))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00193 "air monitoring station")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00193 "A site at which monitoring of the concentration of one or more pollutants is carried out (e.g. the BAPMoN stations, Background Air Pollution Monitoring Network of the WMO, World Meteorological Organization).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00194 "air pollutant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00194 "A substance, gaseous material or aerosol which has been introduced into the air (either by human activity or by natural processes) in sufficient concentration to produce a measurable effect on humans, animals, vegetation or materials (monuments, etc.): $\\ce{SO2}$, $\\ce{NO2}$, $\\ce{H2S}$, $\\ce{CO}$, hydrocarbons, etc.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00194 "pollutant")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00194 "pollution")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00195 "air pollution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00195 "Usually the presence of substances in the atmosphere, resulting either from human activity or natural processes, present in sufficient concentration, for a sufficient time and under circumstances such as to interfere with comfort, health or welfare of persons or the environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00196 "air pollution index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00196 "A mathematical combination of the concentrations of air pollutants (weighted in some fashion to reflect the estimated health impact of the specific pollutant) which gives an approximate numerical measure of the quality of the air at a given time. These indices have little scientific basis but have been used to inform the public (in a qualitative fashion) of the degree of pollution present at a given time. It is recommended that the actual measured pollutant concentrations be used by all information services when possible with simultaneous reference given to the corresponding concentrations which are considered by health authorities to be hazardous to human health.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00196 "air quality index")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00197 "air pollution survey")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00197 "A study of the concentrations and geographical distribution of specified air pollutants in a given area and an assessment of the damage, if any, which the pollutants have caused.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00198))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00198 "air quality characteristic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00198 "One of the quantifiable properties relating to an air sample: concentration of a constituent, wind speed, temperature, etc. The quantity of air quality characteristic is the true value of the characteristic being investigated; it is recognized that in practice, this value can only be approximated by existing methods.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00199 "air resource management")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00199 "The detailed planning and the implementation of air pollution control programs designed to preserve the health and welfare of the people in the region, the plant and animal life, physical property, good visibility and other factors which determine the air quality and the maintenance of an aesthetically acceptable environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00200 "air sampling network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00200 "A number of air sampling stations which are established in a given geographical region at which measurements of both pollutant concentrations and meteorological quantities (wind speed, direction, rain fall, humidity, etc.) are made to determine the extent and the nature of the air pollution and to establish trends in the concentrations of the air pollutants with time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00201))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00201 "Aitken particles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00201 "Aerosol particles below \\(0.1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter. These generally are the most numerous among all particles in the air. Their concentrations can be determined with the Aitken counter which measures total particle number density. Owing to their small size, Aitken particles contribute little to the total mass concentration of all aerosol particles; this is determined primarily by particles of diameter \\(> 0.1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00202 "albedo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00202 "The fraction of the energy of electromagnetic radiation reflected from a body (or surface) relative to the energy incident upon it. The reflection of light from a surface is, of course, dependent on the wavelength of the light, the nature of the surface and its angle of incidence with the surface. The term albedo usually connotes a broad wavelength band (visible, ultraviolet or infrared), whereas the terms reflectivity and spectral albedo are used to describe the reflection of monochromatic (single wavelength or small band of wavelengths) radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00203))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00203 "alcoholates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00203 "Synonymous with alkoxides. Alcoholate should not be used for solvates derived from an alcohol such as $\\ce{CaCl2.nROH}$, for the ending -ate often occurs in names for anions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00204))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00204 "alcohols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00204 "Compounds in which a hydroxy group, $\\ce{-OH}$, is attached to a saturated carbon atom $\\ce{R3COH}$. The term 'hydroxyl' refers to the radical species, $\\ce{HO^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00206))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00206 "aldaric acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00206 "Polyhydroxy dicarboxylic acids having the general formula $\\ce{HOC(=O)[CH(OH)]_{n}C(=O)OH}$, formally derived from an aldose by oxidation of both terminal carbon atoms to carboxyl groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00207))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00207 "aldazines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00207 "Azines of aldehydes $\\ce{RCH=NN=CHR}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00208))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00208 "aldehydes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00208 "Compounds $\\ce{RC(=O)H}$, in which a carbonyl group is bonded to one hydrogen atom and to one $\\ce{R}$ group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00209))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00209 "aldimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00209 "Imines derived from aldehydes $\\ce{RCH=NR}$. E.g. $\\ce{EtCH=NH}$, $\\ce{PhCH=NMe}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00210 "alditols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00210 "Acyclic polyols having the general formula $\\ce{HOCH2[CH(OH)]nCH2OH}$ (formally derivable from an aldose by reduction of the carbonyl group).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00210 "glycitols")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00211 "aldoketoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00211 "A now less preferred synonym for ketoaldoses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00212))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00212 "aldonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00212 "Polyhydroxy acids having the general formula $\\ce{HOCH2[CH(OH)]_{n}C(=O)OH}$ and therefore derived from an aldose by oxidation of the aldehyde function, e.g. ᴅ-gluconic acid.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00212 "glyconic acids")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00213))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00213 "aldoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00213 "Aldehydic parent sugars (polyhydroxyaldehydes $\\ce{H[CH(OH)]_{n}C(=O)H}$, $n \\ge 2$) and their intramolecular hemiacetals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00214 "aldoximes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00214 "Oximes of aldehydes $\\ce{RCH=NOH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00215))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00215 "alert levels")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00215 "Designated concentrations of certain key pollutants at which some degree of danger to public health is expected. In many areas in which a relatively high level of pollution is often encountered, several levels of alert are often established. For example, a first alert may signify a potential problem exists; a second alert becomes a signal for the curtailment of certain significant emission sources; the third alert signifies the need for some specified emergency action which must be taken to ensure the public safety.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00216 "alicyclic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00216 "Aliphatic compounds having a carbocyclic ring structure which may be saturated or unsaturated, but may not be a benzenoid or other aromatic system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00217 "aliphatic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00217 "Acyclic or cyclic, saturated or unsaturated carbon compounds, excluding aromatic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00218 "aliquot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00218 "A known amount of a homogeneous material, assumed to be taken with negligible sampling error. The term is usually applied to fluids. The term 'aliquot' is usually used when the fractional part is an exact divisor of the whole; the term 'aliquant' has been used when the fractional part is not an exact divisor of the whole (e.g. a \\(15\\ \\rm{ml}\\) portion is an aliquant of \\(100\\ \\rm{ml}\\)). When a laboratory sample or test sample is 'aliquoted' or otherwise subdivided, the portions have been called split samples.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00220))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00220 "alkaloids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00220 "Basic nitrogen compounds (mostly heterocyclic) occurring mostly in the plant kingdom (but not excluding those of animal origin). Amino acids, peptides, proteins, nucleotides, nucleic acids, amino sugars and antibiotics are not normally regarded as alkaloids. By extension, certain neutral compounds biogenetically related to basic alkaloids are included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00221))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00221 "alkalosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00221 "Pathological condition in which the hydrogen ion substance concentration of body fluids is below normal and hence the pH of blood rises above the reference interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00222))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00222 "alkanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00222 "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having the general formula $\\ce{C_{n}H_{2n+2}}$, and therefore consisting entirely of hydrogen atoms and saturated carbon atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00223 "alkanium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00223 "Carbocations derived from alkanes by $C$-hydronation containing at least one pentacoordinate carbon atom, e.g. $\\ce{^{+}CH5}$ methanium, $\\ce{[C2H7]^{+}$ ethanium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00224))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00224 "alkenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00224 "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having one carbon–carbon double bond and the general formula $\\ce{C_{n}H_{2n}}$. Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having more than one double bond are alkadienes, alkatrienes, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00225 "alkoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00225 "Compounds, $\\ce{ROM}$, derivatives of alcohols, $\\ce{ROH}$, in which $\\ce{R}$ is saturated at the site of its attachment to oxygen and $\\ce{M}$ is a metal or other cationic species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00226))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00226 "alkoxyamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00226 """O-Alkyl hydroxylamines (with or without substitution on $\\ce{N}$) $\\ce{R'ONR2}$ ($\\ce{R'
eq H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00227 "alkylenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00227 "An old term, which is not recommended, for alkenes, especially those of low molecular weight.An old term for alkanediyl groups commonly but not necessarily having the free valencies on adjacent carbon atoms, e.g. $\\ce{–CH(CH3)CH2\\bond{–}}$ propylene (systematically called propane-1,2-diyl).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00228 "alkyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00228 """Univalent groups derived from alkanes by removal of a hydrogen atom from any carbon atom $\\ce{–C_{n}H_{2n+1}}$. The groups derived by removal of a hydrogen atom from a terminal carbon atom of unbranched alkanes form a subclass of normal alkyl ($n$-alkyl) groups $\\ce{H(CH2)_{n}}$. The groups $\\ce{RCH2}$, $\\ce{R2CH}$ ($\\ce{R 
eq H}$), and $\\ce{R3C}$ ($\\ce{R 
eq H}$) are primary, secondary and tertiary alkyl groups, respectively.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00230 "alkylideneaminoxyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00230 "Radicals having the structure $\\ce{R2C=N–O^{.}}$. Synonymous with iminoxyl radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00231))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00231 "alkylideneaminyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00231 "Radicals having the structure $\\ce{R2C=N^{.}}$. Synonymous with iminyl radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00232 "alkylidene groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00232 "The divalent groups formed from alkanes by removal of two hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom, the free valencies of which are part of a double bond, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)2C=}$ propan-2-ylidene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00233 "alkylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00233 "Carbenes $\\ce{R2C{:}}$ formed by mono or dialkyl substitution of methylene, $\\ce{H2C{:}}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2CH{:}}$ propylidene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00234))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00234 "alkylidynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00234 "Carbenes $\\ce{R2C…}$ containing a univalent carbon atom, atom, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2C…}$ propylidyne")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00235))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00235 "alkyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00235 "Carbon-centered radicals derived formally by removal of one hydrogen atom from an alkane, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2C^{.}H2}$ propyl.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00236 "alkynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00236 "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having a carbon-carbon triple bond and the general formula $\\ce{C_{n}H_{2n-2}}$, $\\ce{RC#CR}$. Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having more than one triple bond are known as alkadiynes, alkatriynes, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00237))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00237 "allele")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00237 "One of several alternate forms of a gene which occur at the same locus on homologous chromosomes and which become separated during meiosis and can be recombined following fusion of gametes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00238 "allenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00238 "Hydrocarbons (and by extension, derivatives formed by substitution) having two double bonds from one carbon atom to two others $\\ce{R2C=C=CR2}$. (The simplest member, propadiene, is known as allene).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00239 "allo-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00239 "Amino acids with two chiral centres were named in the past by allotting a name to the first diastereoisomer to be discovered. The second diastereoisomer, when found or synthesized, was then assigned the same name but with the prefix allo-. This method can be used only with trivial names but not with semisystematic or systematic names. It is now recommended that allo should be used only for alloisoleucine and allothreonine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00240 "allosteric enzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00240 "Enzymes which contain regions to which small, regulatory molecules (cf. effector) may bind in addition to and separate from substrate binding sites. On binding the effector, the catalytic activity of the enzyme towards the substrate may be enhanced, in which case the effector is an activator, or reduced, in which case it is an inhibitor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00241))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00241 "allostery")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00241 "A phenomenon whereby the conformation of an enzyme or other protein is altered by combination, at a site other than the substrate-binding site, with a small molecule, referred to as an effector, which results in either increased or decreased activity by the enzyme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00243 "allotropes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00243 "Different structural modifications of an element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00244 "allotropic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00244 "A transition of a pure element, at a defined temperature and pressure, from one crystal structure to another which contains the same atoms but which has different properties. Examples: The transition of graphite to diamond, that of body-centred-cubic iron to face-centred-cubic iron, and the transition of orthorhombic sulfur to monoclinic sulfur.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00244 "allotriomorphic transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00245 "allylic groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00245 "The group $\\ce{CH2=CHCH2}$ (allyl) and derivatives formed by substitution. The term 'allylic position' or 'allylic site' refers to the saturated carbon atom. A group, such as $\\ce{–OH}$, attached at an allylic site is sometimes described as 'allylic'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00246 "allylic intermediates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00246 "Carbanions, carbenium ions or radicals, formally derived by detachment of one hydron, hydride or hydrogen from the $\\ce{CH3}$ group of propene or derivatives thereof. E.g. $\\ce{H2C=CHCH2^{+}}$ allyl cation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00247 "allylic substitution reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00247 "A substitution reaction occurring at position 1/ of an allylic system, the double bond being between positions 2/ and 3/. The incoming group may be attached to the same atom 1/ as the leaving group, or the incoming group becomes attached at the relative position 3/, with movement of the double bond from 2/3 to 1/2. For example:  (written as a transformation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00249 "alternant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00249 "A conjugated system of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ is termed alternant if its atoms can be divided into two sets so that no atom of one set is directly linked to any other atom of the same set.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00250 "alternating copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00250 "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules comprising two species of monomeric units in alternating sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00251))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00251 "alternating copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00251 "A copolymerization in which an alternating copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00252 "alternating current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00252 "Current with sinusoidal wave forms; all other wave forms should be termed 'periodic'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00254))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00254 "alternating voltage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00254 "This term should be applied only to sinusoidal phenomena; the term periodic voltage should be used for other wave forms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00256 "altocumulus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00256 "A dappled layer of patch or cloud composed of flattened globules that may be arranged in groups, lines or waves collectively known as billows; \\(\\rm{2000–6000 m}\\); vertical velocities of \\(\\rm{0.05–0.1 m s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00257 "altostratus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00257 "A grey, uniform, striated or fibrous sheet but without halo phenomena, and through which the sun is seen only as a diffuse, bright patch or not at all; usually at elevations \\(\\rm{2000–6000 m}\\); vertical velocities of \\(\\rm{0.05–0.1 m s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00258 "ambident")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00258 "A description applied to a chemical species whose molecular entities each possess two alternative and strongly interacting distinguishable reactive centres, to either of which a bond may be made in a reaction: the centres must be connected in such a way that reaction at either site stops or greatly retards subsequent attack at the second site. The term is most commonly applied to conjugated nucleophiles, for example the enolate ion:  (which may react with electrophiles either at the $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}carbon atom}$ or at oxygen) or $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}pyridones}$, and also to the vicinally ambident cyanide ion, cyanate ion, thiocyanate ion, sulfinate ion, nitrite ion and unsymmetrical hydrazines. Ambident electrophiles are exemplified by carboxylic esters $\\ce{RC(=O)OCR3}$ which react with nucleophiles either at the carbonyl carbon or the alkoxy carbon. Molecular entities, such as dianions of dicarboxylic acids, containing two non-interacting (or feebly interacting) reactive centres, are not generally considered to be ambident and are better described as 'bifunctional'. The Latin root of the word implies two reactive centres, but the term has in the past also incorrectly been applied to chemical species with more than two reactive centres. For such species the existing term 'polydent' (or, better, 'multident') is more appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00259))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00259 "ambient air")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00259 "The outdoor air in the particular location.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00260 "ambient air quality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00260 "A general term used to describe the quality of the outside air. Usually adjectives such as good, fair, bad, etc. are used by the media to describe this; often some form of air pollution or air quality index is employed to determine the specific descriptive term to be used. These are very qualitative terms of little or no scientific value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00261 "<i>ambo</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00261 "A prefix used to indicate that a molecule with two (or more) chiral elements is present as a mixture of the two racemic diastereoisomers in unspecified proportions. For example, the dipeptide formed from ʟ-alanine and ᴅʟ-leucine is ʟ-alanyl-ambo-leucine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00262 "Ames/salmonella test")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00262 "A screening test employed in predicting the mutagenic and the potential carcinogenic activities of chemicals in the environment. It employs Ames test strains of salmonella bacteria ($\\ce{his^{-}}$) which lack the ability to produce histidine. The compound to be tested, the bacteria and a small amount of histidine (insufficient to permit colony growth but enough to allow sufficient growth for expression of mutations) are added to agar. The bacteria are allowed to incubate for about \\(63\\ \\rm{h}\\) at \\(37\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\). If a significant increase in colonies above background is observed in the sample containing the test compound, then it is concluded that the chemical tested is a direct mutagen for the particular Ames strain of bacteria. This is taken as a qualitative indication of the possible carcinogenic activity of this chemical in other biological systems. This procedure seems to be of qualitative value in a preliminary screening of potential carcinogens.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00263 "amic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00263 "Carbamoyl carboxylic acids, i.e. compounds containing a carboxy and a carboxamide group, e.g. 5-carbamoylnicotinic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00265))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00265 "amide oximes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00265 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(NH2)=NOH}$ and derivatives formed by substitution; formally the oximes of carboxamides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00266))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00266 "amides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00266 """Derivatives of oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}\\ (l 
eq 0)$ in which an acidic hydroxy group has been replaced by an amino or substituted amino group. Chalcogen replacement analogues are called thio-, seleno- and telluro-amides. Compounds having one, two or three acyl groups on a given nitrogen are generically included and may be designated as primary, secondary and tertiary amides, respectively, e.g.  benzamide, N,N-dimethylmethanesulfonamide,  secondary amides (see imides),  tertiary amides,  phenylphosphonamidic acid.Notes:Amides with $\\ce{NH2}$, $\\ce{NHR}$ and $\\ce{NR2}$ groups should not be distinguished by means of the terms primary, secondary and tertiary.Derivatives of certain acidic compounds $\\ce{R_{n}E(OH)_{m}}$, where $\\ce{E}$ is not carbon (e.g. sulfenic acids, $\\ce{RSOH}$, phosphinous acids, $\\ce{R2POH)}$, having the structure $\\ce{R_{n}E(NR2)_{m}}$ may be named as amides but do not belong to the class amides proper, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2SNH2}$ ethanesulfenamide or ethylsulfanylamine.The term applies also to metal derivatives of ammonia and amines, in which a cation replaces a hydrogen atom on nitrogen. Such compounds are also called azanides, e.g.  lithium diisopropylamide, synonym lithium diisopropylazanide.See also: carboxamides, lactams, peptides, phosphoramides, sulfonamides""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00267 "amidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00267 "Derivatives of oxoacids $\\ce{R_{n}E(=O)OH}$ in which the hydroxy group is replaced by an amino group and the oxo group is replaced by $\\ce{=NR}$. Amidines include carboxamidines, sulfinamidines and phosphinamidines, $\\ce{R2P(=NR)NR2}$. In organic chemistry an unspecified amidine is commonly a carboxamidine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00268))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00268 "amidium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00268 "Cations formally derived by the addition of one hydron to the $\\ce{N}$ or $\\ce{O}$ atom of an amide and $N$-hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. In organic chemistry an unspecified amidium ion is commonly a carboxamidium ion  or  The term does not imply knowledge concerning the position of the cationic centre, e.g. $\\ce{PhC(=O)N^{+}Me3}$ N,N,N-trimethylbenzamidium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00269 "amidrazones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00269 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(=NH)NHNH2}$ or $\\ce{RC(NH2)=NNH2}$, formally derived from carboxylic acids. These tautomers are named hydrazide imides and amide hydrazones, respectively. Also included are $N$-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00270 "aminals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00270 "Compounds having two amino groups bonded to the same carbon, $\\ce{R2C(NR2)2}$. Also called geminal diamines. [The term aminal has also been used, with consequent ambiguity, for $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino}$ ethers (hemiaminal ethers); such use is discouraged.]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00271 "amine imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00271 "Compounds formally derived from the attachment of an amine $\\ce{R3N}$ to a nitrene $\\ce{RN{:}}$ . The structure $\\ce{R3N^{+}–N−R}$ expresses the 1,2-dipolar character of amine imides. They may be named systematically as substituted diazan-2-ium-1-ides, e.g. $\\ce{Me3N^{+}–N−Me}$, 1,2,2,2-tetramethyldiazan-2-ium-1-ide or trimethylamine $N$-methylimide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00273 "amine oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00273 "Compounds derived from tertiary amines by the attachment of one oxygen atom to the nitrogen atom $\\ce{R3N^{+}–O^{−}}$. By extension the term includes the analogous derivatives of primary and secondary amines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00274 "amines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00274 "Compounds formally derived from ammonia by replacing one, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups, and having the general structures $\\ce{RNH2}$ (primary amines), $\\ce{R2NH}$ (secondary amines), $\\ce{R3N}$ (tertiary amines).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00275))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00275 "amine ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00275 "Synonymous with ammonium ylides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00277 "aminium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00277 "Cations $\\ce{HR3N^{+}}$ formed by hydronation of an amine $\\ce{R3N}$. 'Non-quaternary ammonium ions' is a synonymous term, e.g. prolinium, $\\ce{PhN^{+}HMe2}$, $N,N$-dimethylanilinium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00278 "aminiumyl radical ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00278 "Radicals cations, $\\ce{R3N^{.+}}$, derivable from aminium ions, $\\ce{R3NH^{+}}$, by removal of a hydrogen atom. Aminiumyl radical ions are, except for $\\ce{H3N^{.+}}$, synonymous with the ammoniumyl radical ions. As the term ammonium is well known, ammoniumyl radical ions is the more desirable class name.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00279 "amino-acid residue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00279 "When two or more amino acids combine to form a peptide, the elements of water are removed, and what remains of each amino acid is called an amino-acid residue. $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}Amino-acid}$ residues are therefore structures that lack a hydrogen atom of the amino group ($\\ce{–NH–CHR–COOH}$), or the hydroxyl moiety of the carboxyl group ($\\ce{NH2–CHR–CO\\bond{–}}$), or both ($\\ce{–NH–CHR–COO\\bond{–}}$); all units of a peptide chain are therefore amino-acid residues. (Residues of amino acids that contain two amino groups or two carboxyl groups may be joined by isopeptide bonds, and so may not have the formulas shown.) The residue in a peptide that has an amino group that is free, or at least not acylated by another amino-acid residue (it may, for example, be acylated or formylated), is called N-terminal; it is at the N-terminus. The residue that has a free carboxyl group, or at least does not acylate another amino-acid residue, (it may, for example, acylate ammonia to give $\\ce{–NH–CHR–CO–NH2}$), is called C-terminal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00283 "amino sugars")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00283 "Monosaccharides having one alcoholic hydroxy group (commonly but not necessarily in position 2) replaced by an amino group; systematically known as $x$-amino-$x$-deoxymonosaccharides. (Glycosylamines are excluded.) E.g. ᴅ-glucosamine or 2-amino-2-deoxy-ᴅ-glucopyranose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00284 "aminoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00284 "The anion $\\ce{H2N–O^{−}}$, aminoxide and its $\\ce{N}$ hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce{R2N–O^{−}}$; formally derived from hydroxylamines, $\\ce{R2N–OH}$, by removing a hydron from the hydroxy group, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)2N–O^{−}}$ dimethylaminoxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00285 "aminoxyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00285 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2N–O^{.}  R2N^{.+}–O^{−}}$; they are radicals derived from hydroxylamines by removal of the hydrogen atom from the hydroxy group, and are in many cases isolable. The synonymous terms 'nitroxyl radicals' and 'nitroxides' erroneously suggest the presence of a nitro group; their use is not desirable. Example, $\\ce{(ClCH2)2N–O^{.}}$ bis(chloromethyl)aminoxyl.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00285 "aminooxyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00285 "iminooxy radicals")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00289))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00289 "aminyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00289 "The nitrogen-centered radical $\\ce{H2N^{.}}$, formally derived by the removal of a hydrogen atom from ammonia, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce{R2N^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00292))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00292 "ammonium ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00292 "1,2-Dipolar compounds of general structure $\\ce{R3N^{+}–C^{−}R2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00293 "ammoniumyl radical ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00293 "$\\ce{H3N^{.+}}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)3N^{.+}}$ trimethylammoniumyl, $\\ce{PhN^{.+}H2}$ phenylammoniumyl or benzenaminiumyl.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00294))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00294 "amorphous carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00294 "A carbon material without long-range crystalline order. Short-range order exists, but with deviations of the interatomic distances and/or interbonding angles with respect to the graphite lattice as well as to the diamond lattice.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00295 "amount concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00295 "Amount of a constituent divided by the volume of the mixture. Also called amount-of-substance concentration, substance concentration (in clinical chemistry) and in older literature molarity. For entities B it is often denoted by \\(\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\). The common unit is mole per cubic decimetre (\\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\)) or mole per litre (\\(\\pu{mol L-1}\\)) sometimes denoted by \\(M\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00296))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00296 "amount fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00296 "Amount of a constituent divided by the total amount of all constituents in the mixture. It is also called mole fraction. Amount fraction is equal to the 'number fraction': the number of entities of one constituent divided by the total number of entities in the mixture.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00296 "mole fraction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00297 "amount of substance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00297 "The amount of substance, symbol \\(n\\), of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom, a molecule, an ion, an electron, any other particle or specified group of particles.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00297 "chemical amount")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00298 "amount-of-substance concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00298 "Synonymous with amount concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00299 "amount-of-substance fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00299 "Synonymous with amount fraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00300))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00300 "ampere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00300 "The ampere, symbol \\(\\rm{A}\\), is the SI unit of electric current. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the elementary charge \\(e\\) to be \\(1.602\\ 176\\ 634\\times10^{-19}\\) when expressed in the unit \\(\\rm{C}\\), which is equal to \\(\\rm{A s}\\), where the second is defined in terms of ΔνCs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00301 "amperometric detection method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00301 "A detection method in which the current is proportional to the concentration of the species generating the current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00302 "amphipathic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00302 "The property of surface activity is usually due to the fact that the molecules of the substance are amphipathic or amphiphilic, meaning that each contains both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic (lipophilic) group. This assumes that one of the two phases is aqueous, and the other non-aqueous. If both are non-aqueous (e.g. oil/air), molecules containing organophilic and organophobic groups may be amphipathic and surface active.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00303))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00303 "amphiphilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00303 """A term used to describe a compound containing a large organic cation or anion which possesses a long unbranched hydrocarbon chain, e.g. \r
$\\ce{CH3(CH2)_{n}CO2^{−}M^{+}}$$\\ce{CH3(CH2)_{n}N^{+}(CH3)3X^{−}}$ ($n \\gt 7$)$\\ce{CH3(CH2)_{n}SO3^{−}M^{+}}$.The existence of distinct polar (hydrophilic) and nonpolar (hydrophobic) regions in the molecule promotes the formation of micelles in dilute aqueous solution.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00304 "amphiprotic solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00304 "Self-ionizing solvent possessing both characteristics of Brønsted acids and bases, for example $\\ce{H2O}$ and $\\ce{CH3OH}$, in contrast to aprotic solvent.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00304 "amphiprotic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00306 "amphoteric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00306 "A chemical species that behaves both as an acid and as a base is called amphoteric. This property depends upon the medium in which the species is investigated: $\\ce{H2SO4}$ is an acid when studied in water, but becomes amphoteric in superacids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00307))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00307 "amplification reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00307 "A reaction which replaces the conventional reaction used in a particular determination so that a more favourable measurement can be made. The sequence can be repeated to provide a further favourable increase in measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00310 "amplitude of alternating current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00310 "Half the peak-to-peak amplitude of a sinusoidal alternating current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00311))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00311 "amplitude of alternating voltage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00311 "This term should denote half of the peak-to-peak amplitude. Peak-to-peak and r.m.s. amplitudes should be so specified.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00312 "AM 0 sunlight")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00312 "Solar irradiance in space just above the atmosphere of the earth on a plane perpendicular to the direction of the sun (air mass, AM, zero). Also called extraterrestrial irradiance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00313 "AM 1 sunlight")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00313 "Solar irradiance at sea level, i.e., traversing the atmosphere, when the direction of the sun is perpendicular to the surface of the earth. Also called terrestrial global irradiance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00314 "anabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00314 "The processes of metabolism that result in the synthesis of cellular components from precursors of low molecular weight.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00315 "anaerobe")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00315 "An organism that does not need free-form oxygen for growth. Many anaerobes are even sensitive to free oxygen. Obligate (strict) anaerobes grow only in the absence of oxygen. Facultative anaerobes can grow either in the presence or in the absence of molecular oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00316 "anaerobic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00316 "Not requiring molecular oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00317))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00317 "anaesthetic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00317 "Substance which produces loss of feeling or sensation: general anaesthetic produces loss of consciousness; local or regional anaesthetic renders a specific area insensible to pain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00318))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00318 "analgesic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00318 "Substance which relieves pain, without causing loss of consciousness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00319 "analogue metabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00319 "Process by which a normally non-biodegradable compound is biodegraded in the presence of a structurally similar compound which can induce the necessary enzymes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00320 "analogue to digital converter (pulse)")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00320 "A pulse amplitude analyser which, for each pulse processed, produces an integer proportional to the height of that pulse.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00331 "analyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00331 "The component of a system to be analysed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00332))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00332 "analytical function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00332 "A function which relates the measured value \\(\\hat{C}_{\\rm{a}}\\) to the instrument reading, \\(X\\), with the value of all interferants, \\(C_{i}\\), remaining constant. This function is expressed by the following regression of the calibration results. \\[\\hat{C}_{\\rm{a}} = f\\left(X\\right)\\] The analytical function is taken as equal to the inverse of the calibration function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00333 "analytical instrument")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00333 "A device or a combination of devices used to carry out an analytical process. The analytical process is all or part of the analytical procedure that encompasses all steps from the introduction of the sample or the test portion to the production of the result. An analytical instrument may carry out single or multiple analytical procedures. In the latter case it may be selective, i.e. designed to carry out any requested combination of procedures within the set, on each specimen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00334 "analytical intercomparison")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00334 "A procedure which gives insight to the accuracy of results of analytical procedures by comparing the results obtained in the analyses of identical samples at different laboratories and preferably with different analytical methods.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00336 "analytical pyrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00336 "The characterization, in an inert atmosphere, of a material or a chemical process by a chemical degradation reaction(s) induced by thermal energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00337 "analytical quality control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00337 "Procedures which give insight into the precision and accuracy of analysis results.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00338 "analytical radiochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00338 "That part of analytical chemistry in which the application of radioactivity is an essential step in the analytical procedures. Synonymous with radioanalytical chemistry.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00338 "radioanalytical chemistry")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00342 "analytical unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00342 "An assembly of subunits comprising: suitable apparatus permitting the introduction and removal of the gas, liquid or solid to be analysed and/or calibration materials; a measuring cell or other apparatus which, from the physical or chemical properties of the components of the material to be analysed, gives signals allowing their identification and/or measurement; signal processing devices (amplification, recording) or, if need be, data processing devices.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00342 "analyser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00344 "aneroid barometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00344 "An instrument for monitoring the atmospheric pressure in which no liquid is employed, but rather changes in pressure between the atmosphere and a closed vessel bend a diaphram which moves a pointer on a scale.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00345 "Anger camera")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00345 "A camera in which a large diameter scintillator is coupled to an array of photomultiplier tubes by fibre optics. X-ray imaging may also be achieved in multi-crystal cameras where many small crystals individually scintillate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00346 "angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00346 "The angle between two half-lines terminating at the same point is the ratio of the length of the included arc of the circle (with its centre at that point) to the radius of the circle. Symbols: \\(\\alpha \\), \\(\\beta \\), \\(\\gamma \\) ... This is a quantity of dimension one with the SI unit radian (\\(\\pu{rad}\\)), but very often degrees of arc are used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00346 "plane angle")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00348 "angle of optical rotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00348 "Angle through which plane polarized light is rotated clockwise, as seen when facing the light source, in passing through an optically active medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00349 "angle strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00349 "Strain due to a departure in bond angle from 'normal' values. The term is often used in the context of non-aromatic cyclic compounds in which the internal angles differ from the regular tetrahedral angle of \\(109^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\,28^{\\prime }\\); in this sense angle strain is also known as 'Baeyer strain'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00349 "Baeyer strain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00351 "angular distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00351 "With reference to the center of mass, the products of a bimolecular reaction are scattered with respect to the initial velocity vector, and the distribution of scattering angles is known as the angular distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00352 "angular frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00352 "Frequency multiplied by $2\\uppi\\!$. Also called pulsatance. It was formerly called circular frequency.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00352 "circular frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00352 "pulsatance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00353 "angular momentum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00353 "Angular momentum, or moment of momentum of a particle about a point is a vector quantity equal to the vector product of the position vector of the particle and its momentum, \\(L=r\\times p\\). For special angular momenta of particles in atomic and molecular physics different symbols are used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00355 "anhydro bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00355 "Compounds resulting from internal acid–base neutralization (with loss of water) in iminium hydroxides containing an acidic site conjugated with the iminium function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00356))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00356 "anilides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00356 """Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}\\ (l 
eq 0)$ by replacing an $\\ce{OH}$ group by the NHPh group or derivative formed by ring substitution; N-phenyl amides, e.g. $\\ce{CH3C(=O)NHPh}$ acetanilide.See: amidesSalts formed by replacement of a nitrogen-bound hydron of aniline by a metal or other cation, e.g. $\\ce{NaNHPh}$ sodium anilide.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00357 "anils")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00357 "A term for the subclass of Schiff bases $\\ce{R2C=NR'}$, where $\\ce{R'}$ = phenyl or substituted phenyl group. Thus, $N$-phenyl imines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00358 "anion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00358 "A monoatomic or polyatomic species having one or more elementary charges of the electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00359))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00359 "anion exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00359 "The process of exchanging anions between a solution and an anion exchanger.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00360))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00360 "anion exchanger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00360 "Ion exchanger with anions as counter-ions. The term anion-exchange resin may be used in the case of solid organic polymers. The base form of an anion exchanger is the ionic form in which the counter-ions are hydroxide groups ($\\ce{OH–}$ form) or the ionogenic groups form an uncharged base, e.g. $\\ce{-NH2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00361 "anionic polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00361 "An ionic polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are anions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00362))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00362 "anionotropic rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00362 "A rearrangement in which the migrating group moves with its electron pair from one atom to another.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00362 "anionotropy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00365 "annelation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00365 "Alternative, but less desirable term for annulation. The term is widely used in the German and French languages.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00366 "annihilation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00366 "An interaction between a particle and its antiparticle in which they both disappear.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00366 "energy pooling")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00367 "annulation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00367 "A transformation involving fusion of a new ring to a molecule via two new bonds. Some authors use the term 'annelation' for the fusion of an additional ring to an already existing one, and 'annulation' for the formation of a ring from one or several acyclic precursors, but this distinction is not made generally.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00368))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00368 "annulenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00368 "Mancude monocyclic hydrocarbons without side chains of the general formula $\\ce{C_{n}H_{n}}$ ($n$ is an even number) or $\\ce{C_{n}H_{n+1}}$ ($n$ is an odd number). In systematic nomenclature an annulene with seven or more carbon atoms may be named [$n$]annulene, where $n$ is the number of carbon atoms, e.g. annulene for cyclonona-1,3,5,7-tetraene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00369 "annulenylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00369 "Carbenes, derived by formal insertion of a divalent carbon atom into an even-membered annulene, e.g. cycloheptatrienylidene:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00370 "anode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00370 "Electrode at which oxidation takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00371 "anodic transfer coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00371 "Defined by analogy with cathodic transfer coefficient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00372 "anomeric effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00372 "Originally the thermodynamic preference for polar groups bonded to C-1 (the anomeric carbon of a glycopyranosyl derivative) to take up an axial position.  This effect is now considered to be a special case of a general preference (the generalized anomeric effect) for synclinal (gauche) conformations about the bond C–Y in the system X–C–Y–C where X and Y are heteroatoms having nonbonding electron pairs, commonly at least one of which is nitrogen, oxygen or fluorine. For example in chloro(methoxy)methane the anomeric effect stabilizes the synclinal conformation.  In alkyl glycopyranosides the anomeric effect operates at two sites (i) along the endocyclic C-1 oxygen bond (endo-anomeric effect) and (ii) along the exocyclic C-1 oxygen bond (exo-anomeric effect). The opposite preference is claimed for some systems, e.g., glycopyranosyltrialkylammonium salts, and has been referred to as the reverse anomeric effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00373))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00373 "anomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00373 "Diastereoisomers of glycosides, hemiacetals or related cyclic forms of sugars, or related molecules differing in configuration only at C-1 of an aldose, C-2 of a 2-ketose, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00374))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00374 "ansa compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00374 "Benzene derivatives having para positions (or meta) bridged by a chain (commonly 10 to 12 atoms long) (Latin ansa, handle). By extension, any arene bridged by a chain constrained to lie over one of the two faces of the arene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00375))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00375 "antagonism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00375 "Combined effect of two or more factors which is smaller than the solitary effect of any one of those factors. In bioassays, the term may be used when a specified response is produced by exposure to either of two factors but not by exposure to both together.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00376 "antagonist")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00376 "Substance that reverses or reduces the effect induced by an agonist.Substance that attaches to and blocks cell receptors that normally bind naturally occurring substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00377 "antarafacial")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00377 "When a part of a molecule ('molecular fragment') undergoes two changes in bonding (bond-making or bond-breaking), either to a common centre or to two related centres, external to itself, these bonding changes may be related in one of two spatially different ways. These are designated as 'antarafacial' if opposite faces of the molecular fragment are involved, and 'suprafacial' if both changes occur at the same face. The concept of 'face' is clear from the diagrams in the cases of planar (or approximately planar) frameworks with isolated or interacting p-orbitals [diagrams (a) and (b)].  The terms antarafacial and suprafacial are, however, also employed in cases in which the essential part of the molecular fragment undergoing changes in bonding comprises two atoms linked only by a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$. In these cases it is customary to refer to the phases of the local $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$ orbital: occurrence of the two bonding changes at sites of like orbital phase is regarded as suprafacial, whereas that at two sites of opposite phase is antarafacial. The possibilities are shown for $\\ce{C–C}$ and $\\ce{C–H}$ $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ in diagrams (c) and (d). There may be two distinct and alternative stereochemical outcomes of a suprafacial process involving a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between saturated carbon atoms, i.e. either retention or inversion at both centres. The antarafacial process results in inversion at one centre and retention at the second. For examples of the use of these terms, see cycloaddition, sigmatropic rearrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00378 "anthelmintic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00378 "Substance intended to kill parasitic intestinal worms, such as helminths.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00378 "anthelminthic")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00378 "anthelminth")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00379 "anthocyanidins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00379 "Aglycons of anthocyanins; they are oxygenated derivatives of flavylium (2-phenylchromenylium) salts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00380))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00380 "anthocyanins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00380 "Plant pigments of the flavonoid class; they are glycosides that on hydrolysis yield coloured aglycons called anthocyanidins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00381 "anti")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00381 "In the representation of stereochemical relationships 'anti' means 'on opposite sides' of a reference plane, in contrast to 'syn' which means 'on the same side', as in the following examples.Two substituents attached to atoms joined by a single bond are anti if the torsion angle (dihedral angle) between the bonds to the substituents is greater than $\\pu{90\\!^{\\circ}}$, or syn if it is less than $\\pu{90\\!^{\\circ}}$. (A further distinction is made between antiperiplanar, synperiplanar, anticlinal and synclinal.)In the older literature the terms anti and syn were used to designate stereoisomers of oximes and related compounds. That usage was superseded by the terms 'trans' and 'cis' or E and Z, respectively.When the terms are used in the context of chemical reactions or transformations, they designate the relative orientation of substituents in the substrate or product:Addition to a carbon-carbon double bond:  Alkene-forming elimination:  In the examples described under (1) and (2) anti processes are always antarafacial, and syn processes are suprafacial.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00382))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00382 "antiaromatic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00382 """Compounds that contain \\(4\\ n\\) (\\(n
eq 0\\)) $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ in a cyclic planar, or nearly planar, system of alternating single and double bonds, e.g. cyclobuta-1,3-diene.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00383 "antibiotic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00383 "Substance produced by, and obtained from, certain living cells (especially bacteria, yeasts and moulds), or an equivalent synthetic substance, which is biostatic or biocidal at low concentrations to some other form of life, especially pathogenic or noxious organisms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00384 "antibody")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00384 "A protein (immunoglobulin) produced by the immune system of an organism in response to exposure to a foreign molecule (antigen) and characterized by its specific binding to a site of that molecule (antigenic determinant or epitope).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00385 "anticholinergic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00385 "(adjective). Preventing transmission of parasympathetic nerve impulses.(noun). Substance which prevents transmission of parasympathetic nerve impulses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00386 "anticircular elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00386 "The opposite of circular development. Here the sample as well as the mobile phase is applied at the periphery of a circle and both move towards the centre.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00386 "anticircular development")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00389 "anticodon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00389 "A sequence of three nucleotides in the anticodon-loop of a tRNA, which recognizes and binds the complementary triplet sequence (codon) of the mRNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00390 "anti-Compton γ-ray spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00390 "A gamma-ray spectrometer in which the effect of the Compton scattering is at least partly compensated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00391 "anticyclone")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00391 "A large system of winds that rotate about a centre of high atmospheric pressure, clockwise (viewed from above) in the northern hemisphere and counterclockwise in the southern hemisphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00394 "antigen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00394 "A substance that stimulates the immune system to produce a set of specific antibodies and that combines with the antibody through a specific binding site or epitope.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00398 "antimetabolite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00398 "Substance, structurally similar to a metabolite, which competes with it or replaces it, and so prevents or reduces its normal utilization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00399))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00399 "antimony–xenon lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00399 "An intense source of ultraviolet, visible, and near infra-red radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a mixture of antimony vapour and xenon under high pressure. Its output in the ultraviolet region is higher than that of the mercury–xenon arc.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00399 "antimony–xenon arc")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00400 "antimycotic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00400 "Substance used to kill a fungus or to inhibit its growth. Synonymous with fungicide.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00400 "fungicide")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00401 "antiparticle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00401 "Of a given particle, a particle with the same rest mass, where the two can annihilate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00404 "<i>antiprismo</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00404 "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a rectangular antiprism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00405 "antiresistant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00405 "Substance used as an additive to a pesticide formulation in order to reduce the resistance of insects to the pesticide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00406))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00406 "antiserum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00406 "Serum containing antibodies to a particular antigen either because of immunization or after an infectious disease.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00408))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00408 "anti-Stokes type radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00408 "Fluorescence radiation occurring at shorter wavelengths than absorption.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00408 "anti-Stokes type fluorescence")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00409 "anti-thixotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00409 "Opposite of thixotropy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00412 "apex current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00412 "In measurement of non-faradaic admittance (or tensammetry), a plot of alternating current against applied potential shows a minimum or maximum when a non-electroactive substance undergoes adsorption or desorption at the surface of the indicator electrode. Such a maximum or minimum may be called an 'apex' to emphasize its non-faradaic origin and distinguish it from a 'summit', which would result from a charge-transfer process. The highest value of the current on such an apex may be called an 'apex current', and the corresponding applied potential may be called an 'apex potential'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00414))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00414 "aphicide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00414 "Substance intended to kill aphids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00415 "apical")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00415 "In trigonal bipyramidal structures (e.g. a five-coordinate trigonal bipyramid with phosphorus as central atom) the term apical refers to the two positions that are collinear with the central atom or to the bonds linking these positions to the central atom. The three equivalent bonds (or positions) in a plane passing through the central atom and perpendicular to the direction of the apical bonds are described as equatorial.  The term apical is also used for the bond pointing from the atom at or near the centre of the base to the apex of a pyramidal structure. The positions at or near the base of the pyramid, or the bonds linking those positions to the central atom of the base are described as 'basal'. The apical bonds have also been called axial.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00416 "apo-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00416 "An unitalicized prefix, preceded by a locant, used to indicate that all of the molecule beyond the carbon atom corresponding to that locant has been replaced by hydrogen atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00417 "apoenzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00417 "The protein part of an enzyme without the cofactor necessary for catalysis. The cofactor can be a metal ion, an organic molecule (coenzyme), or a combination of both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00418 "apparent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00418 "A term, indicated by a superscript ', which means that a process is not well known or that its value carries uncertainties which are not known, e.g. \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}{'}\\) is the apparent standard Gibbs energy change. In the context of partial molar quantities the word is used in a different sense; as a symbol for 'apparent' in this connection the use of subscript \\(\\varphi \\), as in \\(Y_{\\varphi}\\), is recommended. Other notations employed for this property include \\(^{\\varphi}X\\) and \\(\\varphi_{X}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00418 "apparent quantity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00420 "apparent viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00420 "The ratio of stress to rate of strain, calculated from measurements of forces and velocities as though the liquid were Newtonian. If the liquid is actually non-Newtonian, the apparent viscosity depends on the type and dimensions of the apparatus used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00421))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00421 "appearance energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00421 "Refers to ionization of a molecule or atom by electron collision or photon absorption. In mass spectrometry it has often been reported as the voltage which corresponds to the minimum energy of the electrons in the ionizing beam necessary for the production of a given fragment ion. In photoionization it is the minimum energy of the quantum of light which produces ionization of the absorbing molecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00421 "appearance potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00423 "appearance temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00423 "The temperature of the atomization surface at which the analyte signal/noise (S/N) ratio reaches a value of 3 when the quantity of analyte in the atomizer is one hundred times the characteristic mass for peak high absorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00424 "applied potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00424 "The difference of potential measured between identical metallic leads to two electrodes of a cell. The applied potential is divided into two electrode potentials, each of which is the difference of potential existing between the bulk of the solution and the interior of the conducting material of the electrode, an \\(i\\ R\\) or ohmic potential drop through the solution, and another ohmic potential drop through each electrode. In the electroanalytical literature this quantity has often been denoted by the term voltage, whose continued use is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00425 "aprotic solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00425 "Non-protogenic (in a given situation). (With extremely strong Brønsted acids or bases, solvents that are normally aprotic may accept or lose a proton. For example, acetonitrile is in most instances an aprotic solvent, but it is protophilic in the presence of concentrated sulfuric acid and protogenic in the presence of potassium tert-butoxide. Similar considerations apply to benzene, trichloromethane, etc.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00425 "aprotic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00426))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00426 "aquation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00426 "The incorporation of one or more integral molecules of water into another species with or without displacement of one or more other atoms or groups. For example, the incorporation of water into the inner ligand sphere of an inorganic complex is an aquation reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00427))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00427 "<i>arachno</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00427 "An affix used in names to designate a boron structure intermediate between nido- and hypho- in degree of openness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00428 "Archibald's method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00428 "A sedimentation method based on the fact that at the meniscus and at the bottom of the centrifuge cell there is never a flux of the solute across a plane perpendicular to the radial direction and the equations characterizing the sedimentation equilibrium always apply there, even though the system as a whole may be far from equilibrium. The use of the term 'approach to sedimentation equilibrium' for Archibald's method is discouraged, since it has a more general meaning.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00429))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00429 "area")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00429 "This is understood to be the geometrical or projected area, and to ignore surface roughness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00430 "area of interface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00430 "In all measurements it is desirable that quantities like the charge and the capacity be related to unit, true, surface area of the interface. While this is relatively simple for a liquid-liquid interface, there are great difficulties when one phase is solid. In any report of these quantities it is essential to give a clear statement as to whether they refer to the true or the apparent (geometric) area and, especially if the former is used, precisely how it was measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00432 "areic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00432 "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by area. Areic charge is the charge on a surface divided by the surface area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00433 "arene epoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00433 "Epoxides derived from arenes by 1,2-addition of an oxygen atom to a formal double bond, e.g. 5,6-epoxycyclohexa-1,3-diene.  (Common usage has extended the term to include examples with the epoxy group bridging nonadjacent atoms.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00433 "arene oxides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00435))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00435 "arenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00435 "Monocyclic and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00436 "arenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00436 "Cations derived formally by the addition of a hydron or other cationic species to any position of an arene, e.g. $\\ce{C6H7^{+}}$, benzenium. The term includes:Arenium σ-adduct (sigma-adduct) (Wheland intermediates) (which are considered to be intermediates in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions) and other cyclohexadienyl cations. Arenium π-adduct (pi-adduct), such as:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00437 "arenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00437 "Synonymous with phenols (but rarely used).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00439 "argon ion laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00439 "A CW or pulsed laser emitting lines from \\(334\\) to \\(529\\ \\rm{nm}\\) from singly ionized argon. Principal emissions are at \\(488.0\\) and \\(514.5\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00440 "arithmetic mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00440 "The sum of a series of observations divided by the number of observations. Symbol: \\(\\bar{x}\\). It can be calculated by the formula: \\[\\bar{x}=\\frac{\\sum x_{i}}{n}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00440 "average")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00441 "aromatic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00441 "In the traditional sense, 'having a chemistry typified by benzene'.A cyclically conjugated molecular entity with a stability (due to delocalization) significantly greater than that of a hypothetical localized structure (e.g. Kekulé structure) is said to possess aromatic character. If the structure is of higher energy (less stable) than such a hypothetical classical structure, the molecular entity is 'antiaromatic'. The most widely used method for determining aromaticity is the observation of diatropicity in the $\\ce{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum.See also: Hückel (4n + 2) rule, Möbius aromaticityThe terms aromatic and antiaromatic have been extended to describe the stabilization or destabilization of transition states of pericyclic reactions The hypothetical reference structure is here less clearly defined, and use of the term is based on application of the Hückel (4n + 2) rule and on consideration of the topology of orbital overlap in the transition state. Reactions of molecules in the ground state involving antiaromatic transition states proceed, if at all, much less easily than those involving aromatic transition states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00442 "aromaticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00442 "The concept of spatial and electronic structure of cyclic molecular systems displaying the effects of cyclic electron delocalization which provide for their enhanced thermodynamic stability (relative to acyclic structural analogues) and tendency to retain the structural type in the course of chemical transformations. A quantitative assessment of the degree of aromaticity is given by the value of the resonance energy. It may also be evaluated by the energies of relevant isodesmic and homodesmotic reactions. Along with energetic criteria of aromaticity, important and complementary are also a structural criterion (the lesser the alternation of bond lengths in the rings, the greater is the aromaticity of the molecule) and a magnetic criterion (existence of the diamagnetic ring current induced in a conjugated cyclic molecule by an external magnetic field and manifested by an exaltation and anisotropy of magnetic susceptibility). Although originally introduced for characterization of peculiar properties of cyclic conjugated hydrocarbons and their ions, the concept of aromaticity has been extended to their homoderivatives (see homoaromaticity), conjugated heterocyclic compounds (heteroaromaticity), saturated cyclic compounds ($\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}aromaticity}$) as well as to three-dimensional organic and organometallic compounds (three-dimensional aromaticity). A common feature of the electronic structure inherent in all aromatic molecules is the close nature of their valence electron shells, i.e., double electron occupation of all bonding MOs with all antibonding and delocalized nonbonding MOs unfilled. The notion of aromaticity is applied also to transition states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00443 "arrester")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00443 "Equipment designed to remove particles from a gaseous medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00446 "Arrhenius equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00446 "An equation that represents the dependence of the rate constant \\(k\\) of a reaction on the absolute temperature \\(T\\): \\[k = A\\ \\rm{e}^{\\frac{-E_{\\rm{a}}}{R\\ T}}\\] In its original form the pre-exponential factor \\(A\\) and the activation energy \\(E_{\\rm{a}}\\) are considered to be temperature-independent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00447 "arsanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00447 "The saturated hydrides of tervalent arsenic, having the general formula $\\ce{As_{n}H_{n+2}}$. Individual members having an unbranched arsenic chain are named arsane, diarsane, triarsane, etc. The name of a saturated hydride of arsenic where one or more arsenic atoms have bonding number 5 is formed by prefixing locants and \\(\\lambda ^{5}\\) symbols to the name of the corresponding arsane, e.g. $\\ce{H2AsAsHAsH2}$ triarsane, $\\ce{H4AsAsH3AsH4}$ 1\\(\\lambda ^{5}\\),2\\(\\lambda ^{5}\\),3\\(\\lambda ^{5}\\)-triarsane. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce{AsH3}$ belong to the class arsines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00448))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00448 "arsanylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00448 "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{R–As{:}}$ (former IUPAC name is arsinediyls). A common non-IUPAC synonym is arsinidenes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00448 "arsinidenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00449))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00449 "arsanylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00449 "The arsanyl cation, $\\ce{H2As^{+}}$, and derivatives by substitution. The name arsinylium (systematically derived from arsine) is not applied as it already designates $\\ce{H2As(=O)^{+}}$, the acylium ion derived from arsinic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00450 "arsenides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00450 "Compounds obtained from arsines $\\ce{AsR3}$ by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms by a metal, e.g. $\\ce{CaAsPh}$ calcium phenylarsenide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00451 "arsine oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00451 "$\\ce{H3As=O}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives (analogously arsine imides and arsine sulfides), e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)3As=O}$ trimethylarsine oxide or trimethylarsane oxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00452))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00452 "arsines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00452 """AsH3 and compounds derived from it by substituting one, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups: $\\ce{R3As}$, $\\ce{RAsH2}$, $\\ce{R2AsH}$, $\\ce{R3As}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$) are called primary, secondary and tertiary arsines, respectively. A specific arsine is preferably named as a substituted arsane, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2AsH2}$ ethylarsane.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00453 "arsinic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00453 "$\\ce{H2As(=O)OH}$ and its $\\ce{As}$-hydrocarbyl derivatives, e.g. $\\ce{Me2As(=O)OH}$, dimethylarsinic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00455 "arsinous acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00455 "$\\ce{H2AsOH}$ and its As-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00456 "arsonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00456 "$\\ce{HAs(=O)(OH)2}$ and its $\\ce{As}$-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00457 "arsonium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00457 "Salts (including hydroxides) $\\ce{[R4As]^{+}X^{−}}$ containing tetracoordinate arsonium ion and the associated anion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00458 "arsonous acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00458 "$\\ce{HAs(OH)2}$ and its As-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00459))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00459 "arsoranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00459 "The mononuclear hydride $\\ce{AsH5}$, systematically named \\(\\lambda ^{5}\\)-arsane, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. By extension the term also applies to arsonium ylides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00461))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00461 "artificial radioactivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00461 "Synonymous with induced radioactivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00462))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00462 "aryl cations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00462 "Carbocations formally derived by removal of a hydride ion from a ring carbon atom of an arene. E.g. phenyl cation or phenylium:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00463))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00463 "arylene groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00463 "Bivalent groups derived from arenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from two ring carbon atoms. A synonym is arenediyl groups. E.g. o-phenylene or benzene-1,2-diyl:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00464))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00464 "aryl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00464 "Groups derived from arenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from a ring carbon atom. Groups similarly derived from heteroarenes are sometimes subsumed in this definition. E.g. o-tolyl:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00465))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00465 "aryne")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00465 "A hydrocarbon derived from an arene by abstraction of two hydrogen atoms from adjacent carbon atoms; thus 1,2-didehydroarene. Arynes are commonly represented with a formal triple bond. The analogous heterocyclic compounds are called heteroarynes or hetarynes. For example, benzyne:  Arynes are usually transient species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00466))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00466 "ascending elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00466 "A mode of operation in which the paper or plate is in a vertical or slanted position and the mobile phase is supplied to its lower edge; the upward movement depends on capillary action.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00466 "ascending development")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00467 "ash")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00467 "The solid residue which remains after the combustion of a fuel such as coal. Ash consists largely of heat treated mineral matter, but it may contain some products of the incomplete combustion of the fuel as well.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00468 "ashing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00468 "Dry or wet mineralization as a method of preconcentration of trace substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00469 "aspirator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00469 "Any apparatus that produces a movement of a fluid by suction (e.g. a squeeze bulb, pump, Venturi, etc.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00470 "assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00470 "A set of operations having the object of determining the value of a quantity. In analytical chemistry, this term is synonymous with measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00471))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00471 "assay kit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00471 "A set of components (reagents and other necessary materials) and procedural instructions packaged together and designed for the estimation in vitro of a value of a specified quantity, when used according to the instructions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00472 "association")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00472 "The assembling of separate molecular entities into any aggregate, especially of oppositely charged free ions into ion pairs or larger and not necessarily well-defined clusters of ions held together by electrostatic attraction. The term signifies the reverse of dissociation, but is not commonly used for the formation of definite adducts by colligation or coordination.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00473 "association reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00473 "The reaction of a (slow moving) ion with a neutral species wherein the reactants combine to form a single ionized species.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00473 "associative combination")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00475))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00475 "associative ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00475 "This occurs when two excited gaseous atoms or molecular moieties interact and the sum of their internal energies is sufficient to produce a single, additive ionic product.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00477))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00477 "<i>asym</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00477 "An affix used in names to denote asymmetrical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00478 "asymmetric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00478 "Lacking all symmetry elements (other than the trivial one of a one-fold axis of symmetry), i.e. belonging to the symmetry point group \\(C_{1}\\). The term has been used loosely (and incorrectly) to describe the absence of a rotation–reflection axis (alternating axis) in a molecule, i.e. as meaning chiral, and this usage persists in the traditional terms asymmetric carbon atom, asymmetric synthesis, asymmetric induction, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00479))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00479 "asymmetric carbon atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00479 "The traditional name (van 't Hoff) for a carbon atom that is attached to four different entities (atoms or groups), e.g. Cabcd.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00482))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00482 "asymmetric film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00482 "A film bounded by two different bulk phases. When the bulk phases are identical the film is described as symmetric.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00483))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00483 "asymmetric induction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00483 "The traditional term describing the preferential formation in a chemical reaction of one enantiomer or diastereoisomer over the other as a result of the influence of a chiral feature present in the substrate, reagent, catalyst or environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00484 "asymmetric synthesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00484 "A traditional term used for stereoselective synthesis of chiral compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00485 "asymmetric transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00485 "The conversion of a racemate into a pure enantiomer or into a mixture in which one enantiomer is present in excess, or of a diastereoisomeric mixture into a single diastereoisomer or into a mixture in which one diastereoisomer predominates. This is sometimes called deracemization. If the two enantiomers of a chiral substrate A are freely interconvertible and if an equal amount or excess of a non-racemizing second enantiomerically pure chemical species, say ($R$)-B, is added to a solution of racemic A, then the resulting equilibrium mixture of adducts A·B will, in general, contain unequal amounts of the diastereoisomers ($R$)-A$\\cdot$($R$)-B and ($S$)-A$\\cdot$($R$)-B. The result of this equilibration is called asymmetric transformation of the first kind. If, in such a system, the two diastereoisomeric adducts differ considerably in solubility so that only one of them, say ($R$)-A$\\cdot$($R$)-B, crystallizes from the solution, then the equilibration of diastereoisomers in solution and concurrent crystallization will continue so that all (or most) of the substrate A can be isolated as the crystalline diastereoisomer ($R$)-A$\\cdot$($R$)-B. Such a 'crystallization-induced asymmetric transformation' is called an asymmetric transformation of the second kind.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00485 "deracemization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00486 "asymmetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00486 "Denoting absence of any symmetry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00487 "asymmetry potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00487 "The measured potential difference of a symmetrical cell with identical solutions and reference electrodes on each side of the glass membrane. There is rarely the need, nor the possibility, of measuring the asymmetry potential of commercial glass electrodes. Drifts in glass electrode potentials with time and variations from day to day in the potential measured in a standard buffer may be attributed to changes in asymmetry potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00488 "atactic macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00488 "A regular macromolecule in which the configurational (base) units are not all identical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00489))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00489 "atactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00489 "A polymer composed of atactic macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00490 "atmosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00490 "Non-SI unit of pressure, \\(1\\ \\rm{atm} = 101\\,325\\ \\rm{Pa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00491 "atmosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00491 "The entire mass of air surrounding the earth which is composed largely of nitrogen, oxygen, water vapour, clouds (liquid or solid water), carbon dioxide, together with trace gases and aerosols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00492 "atmospheric pressure ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00492 "An ambiguous term; in essence, it is used to describe chemical ionization at atmospheric pressure. It is recommended that use of the term be discontinued.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00493 "atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00493 "Smallest particle still characterizing a chemical element. It consists of a nucleus of a positive charge (\\(Z\\) is the proton number and \\(e\\) the elementary charge) carrying almost all its mass (more than 99.9%) and \\(Z\\) electrons determining its size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00494 "atomic fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00494 "A combined process of photon absorption by an atom followed by spontaneous photon emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00495 "atomic laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00495 "A gas laser which is pumped using energy transfer from other atoms or molecules. Examples are the helium-neon ($\\ce{HeNe}$) and the copper vapour laser.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00496 "atomic mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00496 "Rest mass of an atom in its ground state. The commonly used unit is the unified atomic mass unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00497 "atomic mass constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00497 "One twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom in its nuclear and electronic ground state, \\(m_{\\rm{u}} = \\pu{1.6605402(10)E-27 kg}\\). It is equal to the unified atomic mass unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00499 "atomic number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00499 "The number of protons in the atomic nucleus.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00499 "proton number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00500 "atomic orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00500 "One-electron wavefunction obtained as a solution of the Schrödinger equation for an atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00502 "atomic spectral lines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00502 "Atomic and ionic spectral lines originate from specified electronic transitions between energy levels of atoms and ions, respectively. In the past it has been common usage to denote atomic lines as arc lines and ionic lines as spark lines. This usage is now considered to be incorrect. The correct way to indicate that lines are due to atomic or ionic transitions is: Element symbol I wavelength, e.g., Cu I \\(324.7\\ \\rm{nm}\\); and Element symbol II wavelength, e.g., Cu II \\(213.6\\ \\rm{nm}\\). Similarly for higher states of ionization, the type of line is represented by III, IV, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00503 "atomic symbol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00503 "One, two or three letters used to represent the atom in chemical formulae.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00504 "atomic units")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00504 "The units designed to simplify the form of the fundamental equations of quantum mechanics by eliminating from them fundamental constants. The atomic unit of length is the Bohr radius, \\(a_{\\rm{o}}=\\frac{h^{2}}{4\\ \\pi ^{2}\\ m\\ e^{2}}=5.291\\ 77249\\times 10^{-11}\\ \\rm{m}\\) (\\(0.529177249\\ \\mathring{\\rm{A}}\\)). Energy is measured in hartrees, where \\(1\\ \\rm{hartree} = \\frac{e^{2}}{a_{\\rm{o}}}=4.359\\ 7482\\times 10^{-18}\\ \\rm{J}\\). Masses are specified in terms of atomic mass unit, \\(\\pu{amu} = \\pu{1.6605402E-27 kg}\\) and of the electron mass unit, \\(m_{\\rm{e}} = \\pu{0.910953E-30 kg}\\). The advantage of atomic units is that if all calculations are directly expressed in such units, the results do not vary with any revision of the numerical values of the fundamental constants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00506 "atomization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00506 "The conversion of volatilized analyte into free atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00507 "atomization surface temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00507 "The temperature of the support from which the sample is atomized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00508 "atomize")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00508 "To subdivide a liquid into very small particles; methods include: impact with a jet of gas, use of a spinning disk generator, vibrating orifice generator, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00509 "atomizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00509 "Any system which is capable of converting the analyte into atomic vapour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00510 "atom–molecule complex mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00510 "A mechanism that sometimes applies to the combination of atoms, but rarely of free radicals. In this mechanism the atom A first combines with a third body or chaperon, $$\\ce{A + M -> AM}$$ and the complex AM then forms $\\ce{A2 + M}$ by collision with another atom $\\ce{A}$. Contrast the energy-transfer mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00511 "atropisomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00511 "A subclass of conformers which can be isolated as separate chemical species and which arise from restricted rotation about a single bond, e.g. ortho-substituted biphenyl, 1,1,2,2-tetra-tert-butylethane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00512))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00512 "attachment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00512 "A transformation by which one molecular entity (the substrate) is converted into another by the formation of one (and only one) two-centre bond between the substrate and another molecular entity and which involves no other changes in connectivity in the substrate. For example, the formation of an acyl cation by attachment of carbon monoxide to a carbenium ion ($\\ce{R^{+}}$):  The product of an attachment may also be the adduct of the two reactants, but not all adducts can be represented as the products of an attachment. (For example, the Diels–Alder cycloaddition:  results in an adduct of buta-1,3-diene and ethene, but the reaction cannot be described as an attachment since bonds are formed between more than two centres.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00513 "attenuance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00513 "Analogous to absorbance, but taking into account also the effects due to scattering and luminescence. It was formerly called extinction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00514 "attenuance filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00514 "An optical device (filter) which reduces the radiant power of a light beam by a constant factor over all wavelengths within its operating range. Sometimes called attenuator or neutral density filter.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00514 "neutral-density filter")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00515))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00515 "attenuation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00515 "The reduction of a radiation quantity upon passage of radiation through matter resulting from interactions of the radiation with the matter it traverses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00516 "attenuation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00516 "Analogous to absorption coefficient but taking into account also the effects due to scattering and luminescence. It was formerly called extinction coefficient.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00516 "extinction coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00517 "atto")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00517 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-18}\\) (symbol: \\(\\rm{a}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00518 "attractive–mixed–repulsive classification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00518 "A classification of potential-energy surfaces in which a highly attractive surface is at one extreme and a highly repulsive surface is at the other. The energy release in intermediate cases is referred to as mixed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00519 "attractive potential-energy surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00519 "A potential-energy surface for a process A + B–C in which the initial descent of the system into the product valley is associated with a substantial decrease in the A–B distance and with little separation between the products A–B and C. In terms of a potential-energy profile, the energy barrier occurs in the early stage of the reaction path. Attractive surfaces are also called early-downhill surfaces, and the barrier in such a surface is called a Type-I barrier.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00519 "early-downhill surface")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00520))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00520 "Auger effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00520 "The emission of an electron from an atom accompanying the filling of a vacancy in an inner electron shell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00521 "Auger electron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00521 "Electron originating in the Auger effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00522 "Auger electron spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00522 "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with \\(\\mathrm{keV}\\)-energy electrons or X-rays, and the energy distribution of the electrons produced through radiationless de-excitation of the atoms in the sample (Auger electrons) is recorded. The derivative curve may also be recorded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00523 "Auger electron yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00523 "The fraction of the atoms having a vacancy in an inner orbital which relax by emission of an Auger electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00524 "Auger yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00524 "For a given excited state of a specified atom the probability that the de-excitation occurs by the Auger effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00525 "autocatalytic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00525 "A chemical reaction in which a product (or a reaction intermediate) also functions as a catalyst. In such a reaction the observed rate of reaction is often found to increase with time from its initial value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00526 "auto-ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00526 "This occurs when an internally supra-excited atom or molecular moiety loses an electron spontaneously without further interaction with an energy source. (The state of the atom or molecular moiety is known as a pre-ionization state.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00527 "automation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00527 "Mechanization with process control, where process means a sequence of manipulations. One or several functions in an analytical instrument may be automated. The corresponding adjective is automated and the verb is automate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00528 "automerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00528 "Synonymous with degenerate rearrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00529))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00529 "autophobicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00529 "If adsorption equilibrium and mutual saturation of the phases is not achieved instantly, it is possible to distinguish the initial spreading tension, \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{i}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\), from the final spreading tension, \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{i}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\), when equilibrium has been reached. In the case in which \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{i}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\) is positive, while \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{i}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\) is negative, the system is said to exhibit autophobicity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00531 "autoprotolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00531 "A proton (hydron) transfer reaction between two identical molecules (usually a solvent), one acting as a Brønsted acid and the other as a Brønsted base. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00532 "autoprotolysis constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00532 "The product of the activities (or, more approximately, concentrations) of the species produced as the result of autoprotolysis. For solvents in which no other ionization processes are significant the term is synonymous with 'ionic product'. The autoprotolysis constant for water, \\(K_{\\rm{w}}\\), is equal to the product of activities: \\[a\\left(\\rm{H}_{3}\\rm{O}^{\\rm{+}}\\right)\\ a\\left(\\rm{OH}^{\\rm{-}}\\right) = 1.0\\times 10^{-14}\\] at \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00533 "autoradiograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00533 "A radiograph of an object containing radioactive substance, produced by placing the object adjacent to a photographic plate or film or a fluorescent screen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00534 "autoradiolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00534 "Radiolysis of a radioactive material resulting directly or indirectly from its radioactive decay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00535 "auxiliary electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00535 "Three-electrode cells comprise (1) an indicator (or test) electrode or a working electrode, at the surface of which processes that are of interest may occur, (2) a reference electrode and (3) a third electrode, the auxiliary or counter electrode, which serves merely to carry the current flowing through the cell, and at the surface of which no processes of interest occur. If processes of interest occur at both the anode and the cathode of a cell (as in differential amperometry or controlled current potentiometric titration with two indicator electrodes), the cell should be said to comprise two indicator (or test) working electrodes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00536 "auxochrome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00536 "An atom or group which, when added to or introduced into a chromophore, causes a bathochromic shift and/or a hyperchromic effect in a given band of the chromophore, usually in that of lowest frequency. This term is obsolete.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00537 "auxotrophy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00537 "The inability of an organism to synthesize a particular organic compound required for its growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00539))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00539 "average current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00539 "The average current density, \\(j\\), is defined by \\[j = A^{-1}\\ \\int _{A}j_{x}\\ \\rm{d}A\\] where \\(A\\) is the electrode area, \\(j_{x}\\) is the local current density and \\(\\rm{d}A\\) is an infinitesimal surface element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00540 "average degree of polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00540 "Any average, \\(\\bar{X}_{\\rm{k}}\\), of the degree of polymerization, where \\(\\rm{k}\\) specifies the type of average.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00541 "average life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00541 "Defined for an atom or nuclear system in a specified state. For an exponentially decaying system, it is the average time for the number of atoms or nuclei in a specified state to decrease by a factor e. Synonymous with mean lifetime.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00542 "average rate of flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00542 "The ratio of the mass of a drop, at the instant when it is detached from the tip of the capillary, to the drop time \\(t_{1}\\); the average value of the instantaneous rate of flow over the entire life of the drop.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00543 "Avogadro constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00543 "The Avogadro constant, \\(N_{\\rm{A}} = \\pu{6.02214076E23 mol-1}\\), is a proportionality constant between the quantity amount of substance (\\(n\\), with unit mole) and the quantity for counting entities (\\(N\\) with unit one, symbol 1), \\(N = N_{\\rm{A}}\\ n\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00544 "avoided crossing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00544 "Frequently, two Born–Oppenheimer electronic states (A, B) change their energy order as molecular geometry (x) is changed continuously along a path. In the process their energies may become equal at some points (the surfaces are said to cross, dotted lines in the figure), or only come relatively close (the crossing of the surfaces is said to be avoided). If the electronic states are of the same symmetry, the surface crossing is always avoided in diatomics and usually avoided in polyatomics.   Synonymous with intended crossing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00545 "Avrami equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00545 "An equation, describing crystallization kinetics, of the form: \\[1 - \\varphi _{c} = \\rm{e}^{K\\ t^{n}}\\] where \\(\\varphi _{c}\\) is the crystalline volume fraction developed at time \\(t\\) and constant temperature, \\(K\\) and \\(n\\) are suitable parameters. \\(K\\) is temperature dependent. According to the original theory, \\(n\\) should be an integer from 1 to 4, the value of which should depend only on the type of the statistical model; however, it has become customary to regard it as an adjustable parameter that may be non-integral.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00546 "axial")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00546 "In the chair form of cyclohexane ring bonds to ring atoms (and molecular entities attached to such bonds) are termed axial or equatorial according to whether the bonds make a relatively large or small angle, respectively, with the plane containing or passing closest to a majority of the ring atoms. Thus the axial bonds are approximately parallel to the $C_{3}$ axis and the equatorial bonds approximately parallel to two of the ring bonds. These terms are also used for the chair form of other saturated six-membered rings. The corresponding bonds occurring at the allylic positions in mono-unsaturated six-membered rings are termed pseudo-axial (or quasi-axial) and pseudo-equatorial (or quasi-equatorial). The terms axial and equatorial have similarly been used in relation to the puckered conformation of cyclobutane, crown conformer of cyclooctane, etc. and the terms pseudo-axial and pseudo-equatorial in the context of the non-planar structures of cyclopentane and cycloheptane.  See apical for an alternative use of axial and equatorial with bipyramidal structures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00547 "axial chirality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00547 "Term used to refer to stereoisomerism resulting from the non-planar arrangement of four groups in pairs about a chirality axis. It is exemplified by allenes $\\ce{abC=C=Ccd}$ (or $\\ce{abC=C=Cab}$) and by the atropisomerism of ortho-substituted biphenyls. The configuration in molecular entities possessing axial chirality is specified by the stereodescriptors $R_{\\rm{a}}$ and $S_{\\rm{a}}$ (or by $P$ or $M$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00548))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00548 "axialite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00548 "A multilayer aggregate, consisting of lamellar crystals splaying out from a common edge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00553 "azanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00553 "Saturated acyclic nitrogen hydrides having the general formula $\\ce{N_{n}H_{n+2}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00555 "azides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00555 "Compounds bearing the group $\\ce{N3}$, viz. $\\ce{–N=N^{+}=N^{-}}$; usually attached to carbon, e.g. $\\ce{PhN3}$ phenyl azide or azidobenzene.Salts of hydrazoic acid, $\\ce{HN3}$, e.g. $\\ce{NaN3}$ sodium azide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00557 "azines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00557 "Condensation products, $\\ce{R2C=NN=CR2}$, of two moles of a carbonyl compound with one mole of hydrazine  (this term should not be confused with the ending -azine appearing in Hantzsch–Widman names for some heterocycles).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00558 "azinic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00558 "Derivatives of the parent structure $\\ce{H2N^{+}(O^{−})OH}$, of which the alkylidene derivatives, $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}(O^{−})OH}$ (tautomers of nitroalkanes), are the most commonly encountered. The alkylideneazinic acids are known as nitronic acids, or synonymously as aci-nitro compounds, e.g. $\\ce{CH2=N^{+}(O^{−})OH}$ methylideneazinic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00559 "azlactones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00559 "Oxazol-5(4$H$)-ones I, compounds derived by cyclization of $N$-acyl $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino}$ carboxylic acids, $\\ce{RC(=O)NHCR2C(=O)OH}$, through formal loss of the elements of water. 4-Hydrocarbylideneazlactones II are often referred to as 'unsaturated azlactones'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00560 "azo compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00560 "Derivatives of diazene (diimide), $\\ce{HN=NH}$, wherein both hydrogens are substituted by hydrocarbyl groups, e.g. $\\ce{PhN=NPh}$ azobenzene or diphenyldiazene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00561 "azo imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00561 "N-Imides of azo compounds, analogous to azoxy compounds, having a delocalized structure  commonly but undesirably referred to as 'azimines'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00561 "azimines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00562 "azomethine imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00562 "The 1,3-dipolar N-imides of azomethines having the structure   The term azo ylides, derived from the second resonance form, has also been used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A00562 "azo ylides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00564 "azomethines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00564 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RN=CR2}$ ($\\ce{R
eq H}$). Many consider the term to include the compounds $\\ce{RN=CRH}$ ($\\ce{R
eq H}$), thus making azomethines synonymous with Schiff bases.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00565 "azomethine ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00565 "1,3-Dipolar compounds having the structure")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00566 "azonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00566 "$N$-Hydrocarbyl derivatives of the parent structure $\\ce{HN^{+}(O^{−})(OH)2}$. Cf. phosphonic acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00567 "azoxy compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00567 "$N$-Oxides of azo compounds of structure $\\ce{RN=N^{+}(O^{−})R}$, e.g. $\\ce{PhN=N^{+}(O^{−})Ph}$ azoxybenzene or diphenyldiazene oxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A04388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A04388 "π-adduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A04388 "An adduct formed by electron-pair donation from a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ into a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$, or from a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ into a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$, or from a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ into a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$. For example:  Such an adduct has commonly been known as a '$\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}complex}$', but, as the bonding is not necessarily weak, it is better to avoid the term complex.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:A04388 "π-complex")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A05436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A05436 "σ-adduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A05436 "The product formed by the attachment of an electrophilic or nucleophilic entering group or of a radical to a ring carbon of an aromatic species so that a new $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ is formed and the original conjugation is disrupted. (This has generally been called a '$\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}complex}$', but adduct is more appropriate than complex according to the definitions given.) The term may also be used for analogous adducts to unsaturated (and conjugated) systems in general.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06761))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06761 "albumin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06761 "A type of protein, especially a protein of blood plasma which transports various substances, including metal ions, drugs and xenobiotics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06766 "anation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06766 "Replacement of the ligand water by an anion in a coordination entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06767 "apoprotein")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06767 "A protein without its characteristic prosthetic group or metal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06776 "anisotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06776 "The property of molecules and materials to exhibit variations in physical properties along different molecular axes of the substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06794 "acid-labile sulfur")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06794 "Refers to sulfido ligands, e.g., the bridging ligands in iron-sulfur proteins, which are released as $\\ce{H2S}$ at acid pH.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06796 "autacoid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06796 "A biological substance secreted by various cells whose physiological activity is restricted to the vicinity of its release; it is often referred to as a local hormone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06797))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06797 "absorption coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06797 "Ratio of the absorbed quantity (uptake) of a substance to the administered quantity (intake).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06799))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06799 "acceptable daily intake")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06799 "Estimate by JECFA of the amount of a food additive, expressed on a body-weight basis, that can be ingested daily over a lifetime without appreciable health risk.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06800 "acute toxicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06800 "Adverse effects of finite duration occurring within a short time (up to $\\pu{14 d}$) after administration of a single dose (or exposure to a given concentration) of a test substance or after multiple doses (exposures), usually within $\\pu{24 h}$ of a starting point (which may be exposure to the toxicant, or loss of reserve capacity, or developmental change, etc.)Ability of a substance to cause adverse effects within a short time of dosing or exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06803))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06803 "allometric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06803 "Pertaining to a systematic relationship between growth rates of different parts of an organism and its overall growth rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06804 "apoptosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06804 "Active process of programmed cell death requiring metabolic energy, often characterised by fragmentation of DNA, and without associated inflammation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06805))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06805 "attributable risk")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06805 "Part of a risk that is identified as due to exposure to a defined substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06809 "adverse effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06809 "Change in biochemistry, morphology, physiology, growth, development, or lifespan of an organism which results in impairment of functional capacity or impairment of capacity to compensate for additional stress or increase in susceptibility to other environmental influences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06849))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06849 "aging")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06849 "Processes that occur in a polymeric material during a specified period of time, and that usually result in changes in physical and/or chemical structure and the values of the properties of the material.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT06849 "ageing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06862))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06862 "asymmetric membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06862 "Membrane constituted of two or more structural planes of non-identical morphologies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06934 "adsorption isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06934 "Isotherm describing adsorption of the sample component on the surface of the stationary phase from the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06947))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06947 "artificial neural networks")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06947 "Artificial neural networks (ANN) are algorithms simulating the functioning of human neurons and may be used for pattern recognition problems, e.g., to establish quantitative structure-activity relationships.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06983))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06983 "<i>ab initio</i> quantum mechanical methods")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06983 "Methods of quantum mechanical calculations independent of any experiment other than the determination of fundamental constants. The methods are based on the use of the full Schrödinger equation to treat all the electrons of a chemical system. In practice, approximations are necessary to restrict the complexity of the electronic wavefunction and to make its calculation possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06984 "agostic interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06984 "The manner of interaction (termed according to the Greek 'to hold or clasp to oneself as a shield') of a coordinatively unsaturated metal atom with a ligand, when the metal atom draws the ligand towards itself. An important type of agostic interaction is the C–H–Metal coordination providing for the activation of the C–H bond in transition metal complexes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06985 "alternancy symmetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06985 "A topological property of the molecular graphs of alternant hydrocarbons which allows the carbon atoms to be divided into two subsets in such a way that no two atoms of the same subset are adjacent. A consequence of this property is the symmetrical arrangement of the energy levels of bonding and antibonding Hückel MOs relative to the level of a nonbonding orbital (energy level of the p AO of a carbon atom).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06986 "angular overlap model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06986 "A method of description of transition metal–ligand interactions and main group stereochemistry, whose basic assumption is that the strength of a bond formed using atomic orbitals on two atoms is related to the magnitude of overlap of the two orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06987 "antiaromaticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06987 "Those cyclic molecules for which cyclic electron delocalization provides for the reduction (in some cases, loss) of thermodynamic stability compared to acyclic structural analogues are classified as antiaromatic species. In contrast to aromatic compounds, antiaromatic ones are prone to reactions causing changes in their structural type, and display tendency to alternation of bond lengths and fluxional behavior (see fluxional molecules) both in solution and in the solid. Antiaromatic molecules possess negative (or very low positive) values of resonance energy and a small energy gap between their highest occupied and lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals. In antiaromatic molecules, an external magnetic field induces a paramagnetic electron current. Whereas benzene represents the prototypical aromatic compound, cyclobuta-1,3-diene exemplifies the compound with most clearly defined antiaromatic properties.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT06987 "antithetical to aromaticity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06988 "antibonding molecular orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06988 "The molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons decreases the total bonding (as usual, increases the total energy) of a molecule. The energy level of an antibonding MO lies higher than the average of the valence atomic orbitals of the atoms constituting the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06989))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06989 "antisymmetry principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06989 "The postulate that electrons must be described by wavefunctions which are antisymmetric with respect to interchange of the coordinates (including spin) of a pair of electrons. A corollary of the principle is the Pauli exclusion principle. All particles with half-integral spin (fermions) are described by antisymmetric wavefunctions, and all particles with zero or integral spin (bosons) are described by symmetric wavefunctions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06990 "apicophilicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06990 "In trigonal bipyramidal structures with a five-coordinate central atom, the stabilization achieved through a ligand changing its position from equatorial to apical (axial). The apicophilicity of an atom or a group is evaluated by either the energy difference between the stereoisomers (permutational isomers) containing the ligand in apical and equatorial positions or the energy barrier to permutational isomerization (see also Berry pseudorotation). In general, the greater the electronegativity and the stronger the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$-withdrawing properties of a ligand (as for $\\ce{Cl}$, $\\ce{F}$, $\\ce{CN}$), the higher is its apicophilicity. The notion of apicophilicity has been extended to four-coordinate bisphenoidal and three-coordinate T-shaped structures, which can be viewed as trigonal bipyramidal species where, respectively, one or two vertices are occupied by phantom ligands (lone electron pairs).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06992 "atom-atom polarizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06992 "A quantity used in perturbation HMO theory as a measure of the change in electron density, \\(q\\), of atom \\(s\\) caused by a change in the electronegativity (or coulomb integral) of atom \\(r\\): \\[\\pi _{\\rm{sr}}=\\frac{\\partial q_{\\rm{s}}}{\\partial a_{\\rm{r}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06993 "atom-bond polarizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06993 "A quantity used in perturbation HMO theory as a measure of the change in electron density, \\(q\\), of atom \\(m\\) caused by a change in the resonance integral, \\(\\beta \\), of bond \\(rs\\): \\[\\pi _{\\rm{m,rs}}=\\frac{\\partial q_{\\rm{m}}}{\\partial \\beta _{\\rm{rs}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06994 "atomic charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06994 """The charge attributed to an atom \\(A\\) within a molecule defined as \\(\\zeta =Z_{\\rm{A}}- q_{\\rm{A}}\\), where \\(Z_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the atomic number of \\(A\\) and \\(q_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the electron density assigned to \\(A\\). The method of calculation of \\(q_{\\rm{A}}\\) depends on the choice of the scheme of partitioning electron density. In the framework of the Mulliken population analysis \\(q_{\\rm{A}}\\) is associated with the so-called gross atomic population: \\(q_{\\rm{A}}=\\sum q_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}\\), where \\(q_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}\\) is a gross population for an orbital \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\) in the basis set employed defined according to \\[q_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}} = P_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}+\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r

u 
eq \\unicode[Times]{x3BC} \r
\\end{array}}P_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}
u}\\ S_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}
u}\\] where \\(P_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BD} }\\) and \\(S_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BD}}\\) are the elements of density matrix and overlap matrix, respectively (see overlap integral). In the Hückel molecular orbital theory (where \\(S_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}
u} = \\delta _{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}
u}\\)), \\(q_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}} = n_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}\\ P_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}\\), where \\(n_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}}\\) is the number of electrons in the \\(\\rm{MO}\\,\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT06996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT06996 "aufbau principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT06996 "A rule for building up the electronic configuration of atoms and molecules. It states that a maximum of two electrons are put into orbitals in the order of increasing orbital energy: the lowest-energy orbitals are filled before electrons are placed in higher-energy orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07196 "ampholytic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07196 "Polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing both cationic and anionic groups, or corresponding ionizable groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07198))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07198 "anionic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07198 "Polymer composed of negatively charged macromolecules and an equivalent amount of counter-cations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07312 "absorbed photon flux density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07312 "Number of photons of a particular wavelength per time interval (spectral photon flux, number basis, \\(q_{\\rm{p},\\lambda }\\), or spectral photon flux, amount basis, \\(q_{n,\\rm{p},\\lambda }\\)) absorbed by a system per volume, \\(V\\). On number basis, SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-4}\\), and the  common unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{cm}^{-3}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). On amount basis, SI unit is \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-4}\\), and a common unit is \\(\\pu{einstein s-1 cm-3 nm-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07312 "absorbed spectral photon flux density")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07313 "absorbed radiant power density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07313 "Spectral radiant energy per time interval (spectral radiant power, \\(P_{\\lambda}\\)) absorbed by a system per volume, \\(V\\). SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-4}\\); common unit is \\(\\rm{W cm}^{-3}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07313 "absorbed spectral radiant power density")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07314 "actinic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07314 "Applied or referred to actinism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07315 "actinism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07315 "Chemical changes on living and non-living materials caused by optical radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07317))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07317 "acyl shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07317 "Photochemical process with skeletal isomerization by shift of an acyl group from position 1 to position 2 or 3.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07317 "1")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07317 "2-acyl shift")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07317 "1")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:AT07317 "3-acyl shift")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07318))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07318 "alkene photocycloaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07318 "Photochemical process leading to formation of a cyclobutane upon addition of an electronically excited alkene to another ground-state alkene or of an electronically excited $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!,\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}unsaturated}$ carbonyl compound to a ground-state alkene. The latter is called a de Mayo reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07319 "alkene photodimerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07319 "Photochemical process leading to the formation of a cyclobutane from two identical alkenes, i.e., from the photocycloaddition of an electronically excited alkene to a ground-state alkene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07320 "alkene photoisomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07320 "Photochemical process with geometrical isomerization of a carbon–carbon double bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07321 "alkene photorearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07321 "Photochemical process leading to the skeletal rearrangement of an alkene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07326))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07326 "amalgam lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07326 "Intense source of ultraviolet (\\(185\\) and \\(253.7\\ \\rm{nm}\\)) radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a lamp with the inner side covered by an amalgam of mercury with another element such as indium or gallium to control the vapour pressure of the mercury. These lamp have 2-3 times the UV output for the same wavelength as the standard low-pressure mercury lamp.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07327 "aromatic photocycloaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07327 "Inter- and intramolecular photochemical processes involving the addition of a $\\ce{C–C}$ double (or triple) bond (i) to the 1,2-positions of an arene in which case it is called an ortho photocycloaddition, with formation of a benzocyclobutene (or a benzocyclobutadiene) derivative, (ii) to the 1,3-positions of an arene in which case it is called a meta photocycloaddition, with formation of tricyclo[3.3.0.02,8]oct-3-ene (or octa-3,6-diene) derivatives, or (iii) to the 1,4-positions of an arene in which case it is called a para photocycloaddition, with formation of bicyclo[2,2,2]oct-2-ene (or octa-2,5-diene) derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07328))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07328 "asymmetric photochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07328 "Photochemical process leading to a chiral substance from an achiral precursor such that one enantiomer predominates over the other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07329 "aza-di-π-methane rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07329 "Photochemical reaction of a 1-aza-1,4-diene or a 2-aza-1,4-diene in the triplet excited state to form the corresponding cyclopropylimine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07502 "agglomerate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07502 "Cluster of molecules or particles that results from agglomeration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07516 "alcogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07516 "Gel in which the swelling agent consists predominantly of an alcohol or a mixture of alcohols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07517 "aquagel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07517 "Hydrogel in which the network component is a colloidal network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07591 "affine chain behaviour")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07591 "Behaviour of a polymer network in which the junction points deform uniformly with the macroscopic deformation of the network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07606 "aerosol hydrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07606 "Hydrolysis of the dispersed component of an aerosol.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07607 "agglomeration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07607 "Process in which dispersed molecules or particles assemble rather than remain as isolated single molecules or particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:AT07608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:AT07608 "aggregation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:AT07608 "Process whereby dispersed molecules or particles form aggregates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00571 "β-cleavage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00571 "Fission next but one to a heteroatom or functional group producing a radical and an ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00572 "β-decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00572 "Nuclear decay in which a β-particle is emitted or in which orbital electron capture occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00573 "β-particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00573 "Electron ejected from a radioactive nucleus.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00575 "back electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00575 "A term often used to indicate thermal reversal of excited state electron transfer restoring the donor and acceptor in their original oxidation level. In using this term one should also specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00576 "back extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00576 "Synonymous with stripping (by extraction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00577 "background")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00577 "The term employed to designate the value indicated by a radiation measuring device in the absence of the source whose radiation is to be measured, when the device is placed under its normal conditions of operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00578 "background concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00578 "The concentration of a given species in a pristine air mass in which anthropogenic impurities of a relatively short lifetime are not present. The background concentrations of relatively long-lived molecules, methane, carbon dioxide, halocarbons ($\\ce{CF3Cl}$, $\\ce{CF2Cl2}$, etc.) and some other species continue to rise due to anthropogenic input, so the composition of background air is undergoing continual change. Background concentration of a given species is sometimes considered to be the concentration of that impurity in a given air mass when the contribution from anthropogenic sources under study is absent. Synonymous with baseline concentration.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00578 "baseline concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00578 "background level")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00579 "background mass spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00579 "The mass spectrum observed when no sample is intentionally introduced into the mass spectrometer or spectrograph.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00580 "background radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00580 "Radiation from any source other than the one it is desired to detect or measure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00581 "backscatter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00581 "Scattering of radiation in a generally backward direction. In the assay of radioactivity, it applies to the scattering of radiation into the radiation detector from any material except the sample and the detector. In light scattering, it is said to occur when the scattering angle, \\(\\theta \\), is \\(180^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\). Conversely, forward scattering occurs when \\(\\theta \\) is \\(0^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00582 "back scatter coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00582 "Number of back scattered electrons (BSEs) generated per primary electron for a given specimen and experimental condition. It depends on (mean) atomic number of the excited area of the sample, angle between electron beam and sample surface, primary electron energy and thickness of the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00583 "back scattered electrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00583 "All primary electrons which are scattered out of the original direction and retransmitted through the surface of the solid. In practice, electrons emitted from the surface of a solid under electron bombardment which have a kinetic energy in the range between \\(50\\ \\rm{eV}\\) and excitation energy (\\(E_{0}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00587 "baffle chamber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00587 "A chamber used in incinerator design to promote the settling of fly ash and coarse particulate matter by changing the direction and/or reducing the velocity of the gases produced by the combustion of the refuse.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00588 "bag filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00588 "A large bag constructed of a suitable fabric which is often tubular in shape, into which a particle-containing air stream flows. Modern bags are constructed of a fabric which is capable of collecting all but very fine particles in the gas stream. The efficiency of the removal of particles of various size ranges changes with the amount of particles captured by the filter and the filtering time. The bag operates on the same principle as the one on a household vacuum cleaner.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00588 "fabric filter")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00589 "baghouse")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00589 "An installation which contains many bag filters in parallel so that the resistance to air flow in a large installation is not seriously increased by the addition of these controls.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00590 "Bainite transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00590 "A term that is sometimes used in metallurgy for the martensitic transition that describes the transition between a face-centred-cubic lattice and a body-centred-tetragonal lattice. Example: The transition between the face-centred-cubic lattice of austenite and the body-centred-tetragonal lattice of a martensite.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00591 "baking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00591 "The process in which the carbonaceous binder, usually coal tar pitch or petroleum pitch, as part of a shaped carbon mix is converted to carbon yielding a rigid carbon body by the slow application of heat. The process can take as little as \\(14\\ \\rm{days}\\) in coarse-grained, electrothermic grades (low binder level) and as long as \\(36\\ \\rm{days}\\) in ultra-fine-grained, specialty grades (high binder level). The final baking temperature can be in the range of \\(1100{-}1500\\ \\rm{K}\\), depending on the grade.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00592 "Baldwin's rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00592 "A set of empirical rules for certain formations of 3- to 7-membered rings. The predicted pathways are those in which the length and nature of the linking chain enables the terminal atoms to achieve the proper geometries for reaction. The disfavoured cases are subject to severe distortions of bond angles and bond distances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00593 "bandgap energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00593 "The energy difference between the bottom of the conduction band and the top of the valence band in a semiconductor or an insulator.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00594 "bandpass filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00594 "An optical device which permits the transmission of radiation within a specified wavelength range and does not permit transmission of radiation at higher or lower wavelengths. It can be an interference filter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00595 "band spectra")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00595 "The combination of many different spectral lines resulting from vibrational, rotational and electronic transitions. Unresolved band spectra may appear as a spectral continuum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00596))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00596 "bar")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00596 "Non-SI unit of pressure, \\(\\rm{bar} = 10^{5}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00597 "barbiturates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00597 "Pyrimidine-2,4,6(1H,3H,5H)-trione (trivial name barbituric acid) and derivatives:  (keto tautomeric form)Salts of barbituric acid and its derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00598 "barn")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00598 "Non-SI unit of area, \\(\\rm{b} = 10^{-28}\\ \\rm{m}^{2}\\), used to express cross sections in nuclear reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00599 "Barton reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00599 "Photolysis of a nitrite to form a $\\ce{\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}nitroso alcohol}$. The mechanism is believed to involve a homolytic $\\ce{RO–NO}$ cleavage, followed by $\\ce{\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen}$ abstraction and radical coupling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00601 "base")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00601 "A chemical species or molecular entity having an available pair of electrons capable of forming a covalent bond with a hydron (proton) (see Brønsted base) or with the vacant orbital of some other species (see Lewis base).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00602 "base electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00602 "Synonymous with supporting electrolyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00603 "base kind of quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00603 "A kind of quantity considered dimensionally independent of other kinds of quantities, i.e. it is not defined by an equation containing other kinds of quantities, e.g. length, mass, amount of substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00604 "baseline")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00604 "The portion of the chromatogram recording the detector response when only the mobile phase emerges from the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00606 "baseline error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00606 "An error which arises if the two beams in a double-beam spectrometer are not fully equivalent in transmitted power nor are corrected to be so (e.g. due to unmatched cells).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00607 "base pairing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00607 "The specific association between two complementary strands of nucleic acids that results from the formation of hydrogen bonds between the base components of the nucleotides of each strand: A=T and G=C in DNA, A=U and G=C (and sometimes G=U) in RNA (the lines indicate the number of hydrogen bonds). Single-stranded nucleic acid molecules can adopt a partially double-stranded structure through intrastrand base pairing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00608 "base peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00608 "The peak in a mass spectrum corresponding to the separated ion beam which has the greatest intensity. This term may be applied to the spectra of pure substances or mixtures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00609 "base quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00609 "One of the quantities that, in a system of quantities, are conventionally accepted as functionally independent of one another. In the SI, these are: length, mass, time, electric current, temperature, amount of substance, and luminous intensity. All other physical quantities (and units) are regarded as being derived from these base quantities (and base units).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00610))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00610 "base unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00610 "Unit of measurement of a base quantity in a given system of quantities. By international agreement, a set of seven dimensionally independent units form the SI base units: the metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole and candela.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00611 "basicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00611 "For Brønsted bases, the tendency of a compound to act as hydron (proton) acceptor. The basicity of a chemical species is normally expressed by the acidity of the conjugate acid (see conjugate acid–base pair). For Lewis bases it relates to the association constants of Lewis adducts and π-adducts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00613))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00613 "batch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00613 "A quantity of material which is known or assumed to be produced under uniform conditions. Some vocabularies assume 'lot' and 'batch' to be synonymous. The distinction made here with respect to knowledge of production history permits a lot to consist of one or more batches and is useful in interpreting the results of analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00615 "batch operation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00615 "The operation of an analytical instrument in such a way that one or more analytical procedures must be completed for a sequence of samples before the next sequence can be started. This term batch usually implies a sequence of a variably sized group of samples, the size of which is not related to a particular type of instrument.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00616 "batch reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00616 "In a batch reactor the reactants and the catalyst are placed in the reactor which is then closed to transport of matter and the reaction is allowed to proceed for a given time whereupon the mixture of unreacted material together with the products is withdrawn. Provision for mixing may be required.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00618 "bathochromic shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00618 "Shift of a spectral band to lower frequencies (longer wavelengths) owing to the influence of substitution or a change in environment. It is informally referred to as a red shift and is opposite to hypsochromic shift (blue shift).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00618 "bathochromic effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00619 "bead-rod model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00619 "A model simulating the hydrodynamic properties of a chain macromolecule consisting of a sequence of beads, each of which offers hydrodynamic resistance to the flow of the surrounding medium and is connected to the next bead by a rigid rod which does not. The mutual orientation of the rods is random.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00620 "bead-spring model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00620 "A model simulating the hydrodynamic properties of a chain macromolecule consisting of a sequence of beads, each of which offers hydrodynamic resistance to the flow of the surrounding medium and is connected to the next bead by a spring which does not contribute to the frictional interaction but which is responsible for the elastic and deformational properties of the chain. The mutual orientation of the springs is random.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00621 "beam current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00621 "The number of primary electrons reaching the surface of the specimen per unit time expressed as electrical current. Recommended symbol: \\(i_{\\rm{B}}\\); unit: \\(\\rm{A}\\); typical range: \\(\\mathrm{nA}\\). Recommended measurement technique: Faraday cage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00622 "beam diameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00622 "Diameter of the beam between which 68% of the electrons fall. For Gaussian beam shapes this corresponds to the 2\\(\\sigma \\)-value of the intensity distribution of the beam. Measurement can be carried out by scanning a beam across a sufficiently sharp edge and recording the transmitted electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00624 "becquerel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00624 "SI derived unit of radioactivity equal to one disintegration per second; admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health, symbol: \\(\\rm{Bq}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00625 "bed volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00625 "Synonymous with column volume for a packed column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00626 "Beer–Lambert law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00626 "The absorbance of a beam of collimated monochromatic radiation in a homogeneous isotropic medium is proportional to the absorption path length, \\(l\\), and to the concentration, \\(c\\), or — in the gas phase — to the pressure of the absorbing species. The law can be expressed as: \\[A = \\rm{log}_{10}\\left ( \\frac{P_{\\lambda }^{0}}{P_{\\lambda }} \\right ) = \\varepsilon \\:c\\:l\\] or \\[P_{\\lambda }=P_{\\lambda }^{0}10^{-\\varepsilon \\: c\\: l}\\] where the proportionality constant, \\(\\varepsilon\\), is called the molar (decadic) absorption coefficient. For \\(l\\) in \\(\\pu{cm}\\) and \\(c\\) in \\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\) or \\(\\rm{M}\\), \\(\\varepsilon\\) will result in \\(\\pu{dm3 mol-1 cm-1}\\) or \\(\\rm{M}^{-1}\\ \\rm{cm}^{-1}\\), which is a commonly used unit. The SI unit of \\(\\varepsilon\\) is \\(\\pu{m2 mol-1}\\). Note that spectral radiant power must be used because the Beer–Lambert law holds only if the spectral bandwidth of the light is narrow compared to spectral linewidths in the spectrum.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00626 "Beer–Lambert–Bouguer law")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00627 "before mass analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00627 "The sum of all the separate ion currents for ions of the same sign (usually positive) before mass analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00628))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00628 "Bell–Evans–Polanyi principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00628 "The linear relation between energy of activation \\((E_{\\rm{a}})\\) and enthalpy of reaction \\((\\Delta H_{\\rm{r}})\\) sometimes observed within a series of closely related reactions. \\[E_{\\rm{a}} = A + B\\ \\unicode[Times]{x394}H_{\\rm{r}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00629 "bending of energy bands")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00629 "The distribution of potential in the space charge region of a semiconductor results in a change in the electron energy levels with distance from the interface. This is usually described as 'bending of the energy bands'. Thus the bands are bent, upwards if \\(\\sigma >0\\) and downwards if \\(\\sigma")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00630))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00630 "benzenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00630 "Arenium ions, derived from benzene or substituted derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00632 "benzylic groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00632 "Arylmethyl groups and derivatives formed by substitution: $\\ce{ArCR2^{–}}$, Benzyl, $\\ce{C6H5CH2^{–}}$, is the prototype. The term 'benzylic position' or 'benzylic site' refers to the saturated carbon atom. A group, such as $\\ce{–OH}$, attached at a benzylic site is sometimes referred to as 'benzylic'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00633))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00633 "benzylic intermediates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00633 "Carbanions, carbenium ions or radicals, derived formally by detachment of one hydron, hydride or hydrogen, respectively, from the $\\ce{CH3}$ group of toluene or substitution derivatives thereof, e.g. $\\ce{C6H5CH2^{.}}$, benzyl radical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00634 "benzynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00634 "1,2-Didehydrobenzene (the aryne derived from benzene) and its derivatives formed by substitution; see arynes, dehydroarenes. The terms m- and p-benzyne are occasionally, but erroneously, used for 1,3- and 1,4-didehydrobenzene, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00637 "betaines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00637 "Originally, the compound betaine, $\\ce{(CH3)3N^{+}–CH2C(=O)O^{−}}$ $N,N,N$-trimethylammonioacetate, and similar zwitterionic compounds derived from other amino acids. By extension, neutral molecules having charge-separated forms with an onium atom which bears no hydrogen atoms and that is not adjacent to the anionic atom. Betaines cannot be represented without formal charges. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00638 "betweenanenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00638 "Bicyclic alkenes having a double bond between the bridgehead atoms and a trans attachment of each branch to the double bond. Thus trans-bicyclo[m.n.0]alk-1 (m+2)-enes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00640 "biased linear pulse amplifier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00640 "A pulse amplifier which, within the limits of its normal operating characteristics, has a constant gain for that portion of an input pulse that exceeds the threshold value and that produces no output for pulses whose amplitude is below the threshold.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00642))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00642 "bifunctional catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00642 "Catalysis (usually for hydron transfer) by a bifunctional chemical species involving a mechanism in which both functional groups are implicated in the rate-controlling step, so that the corresponding catalytic coefficient is larger than that expected for catalysis by chemical species containing only one of these functional groups. The term should not be used to describe the concerted action of two different catalysts ('concerted catalysis').")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00642 "polyfunctional catalysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00644))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00644 "bimodal distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00644 "The occurrence of two maxima in a frequency distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00646))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00646 "binder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00646 "Usually a coal tar pitch or petroleum pitch (but may include thermosetting resins or mesophase pitch powders) which, when mixed with a binder coke or a filler, constitutes a carbon mix. This is used in preparation of the formation of shaped green bodies and subsequently carbon artifacts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00647))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00647 "binder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00647 "An additive used to hold the solid stationary phase to the inactive plate or sheet in thin-layer chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00648 "binder coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00648 "A constituent of a carbon (or ceramic) artifact resulting from carbonization of the binder during baking.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00649))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00649 "binding site")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00649 "A specific region (or atom) in a molecular entity that is capable of entering into a stabilizing interaction with another molecular entity. An example of such an interaction is that of an active site in an enzyme with its substrate. Typical forms of interaction are by hydrogen bonding, coordination and ion pair formation. Two binding sites in different molecular entities are said to be complementary if their interaction is stabilizing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00650 "Bingham flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00650 "Many colloidal dispersions show Bingham flow which is characterized by a $\\upsigma\\!$-D diagram as shown. At rates of shear greater than that at point A, the following relation applies: \\[\\sigma - \\sigma _{\\rm{B}}=\\eta _{\\Delta }\\ D\\] where \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{B}}\\) (or \\(\\tau _{\\rm{B}}\\)) is called the Bingham yield stress, \\(\\eta _{\\Delta }\\) is the differential viscosity, \\(D\\) is the shear rate, and \\(\\sigma \\) is the average of three normal stress components if the deformation is purely dilatational.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00651 "bioassay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00651 "A procedure for determining the concentration or biological activity of a substance (e.g. vitamin, hormone, plant growth factor, antibiotic) by measuring its effect on an organism or tissue compared to a standard preparation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00652))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00652 "biocatalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00652 "An enzyme or enzyme complex consisting of, or derived from, an organism or cell culture (in cell-free or whole-cell forms) that catalyses metabolic reactions in living organisms and/or substrate conversions in various chemical reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00653 "biochemical oxygen demand")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00653 "The amount of oxygen, divided by the volume of the system, taken up through the respiratory activity of microorganisms growing on the organic compounds present in the sample (e.g. water or sludge) when incubated at a specified temperature (usually \\(\\pu{20^{\\circ}C}\\)) for a fixed period (usually \\(\\rm{5\\ days}\\), \\(\\rm{BOD}_{5}\\)). It is a measure of that organic pollution of water which can be degraded biologically. In practice, it is usually expressed in milligrams \\(\\ce{O2}\\) per litre.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00653 "biological oxygen demand")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00654 "biochip")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00654 "An integrated circuit whose electrical and logical functions are performed by protein molecules appropriately manipulated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00656 "biodegradation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00656 "Breakdown of a substance catalysed by enzymes in vitro or in vivo. This may be characterized for purposes of hazard assessment as:Primary. Alteration of the chemical structure of a substance resulting in loss of a specific property of that substance.Environmentally acceptable. Biodegradation to such an extent as to remove undesirable properties of the compound. This often corresponds to primary biodegradation but it depends on the circumstances under which the products are discharged into the environment.Ultimate. Complete breakdown of a compound to either fully oxidized or reduced simple molecules (such as carbon dioxide/methane, nitrate/ammonium and water).It should be noted that the products of biodegradation can be more harmful than the substance degraded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00657))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00657 "bioelectronics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00657 "The application of biomolecular principles to microelectronics such as in biosensors and biochips.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00658 "biological half life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00658 "For a substance the time required for the amount of that substance in a biological system to be reduced to one half of its value by biological processes, when the rate of removal is approximately exponential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00659 "bioluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00659 "Luminescence produced by living systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00660 "biomass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00660 "Material produced by the growth of microorganisms, plants or animals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00661))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00661 "biopolymers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00661 "Macromolecules (including proteins, nucleic acids and polysaccharides) formed by living organisms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00662))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00662 "bioreactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00662 "An apparatus used to carry out any kind of bioprocess; examples include fermenter or enzyme reactor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00663 "biosensor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00663 "A device that uses specific biochemical reactions mediated by isolated enzymes, immunosystems, tissues, organelles or whole cells to detect chemical compounds usually by electrical, thermal or optical signals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00664))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00664 "biosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00664 "That part of the globe that encompasses all forms of life on the earth. It extends from the ocean depths to a few thousand metres of altitude in the atmosphere, and includes life forms at the earth's surface, in soils and constituents which exchange materials with atmospheres, oceans or surfaces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00665 "biosynthesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00665 "The production of a chemical compound by a living organism (cf. biotransformation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00666 "biotechnology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00666 "The integration of natural sciences and engineering sciences in order to achieve the application of organisms, cells, parts thereof and molecular analogues for products and services.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00667))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00667 "biotransformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00667 "Any chemical conversion of substances that is mediated by living organisms or enzyme preparations derived therefrom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00667 "bioconversion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00668 "biphotonic excitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00668 "Simultaneous (coherent) absorption of two photons (either same or different wavelength), the energy of excitation being the sum of the energies of the two photons. Also called two-photon excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00669))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00669 "biphotonic process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00669 "Resulting from biphotonic excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00671 "biradical")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00671 "An even-electron molecular entity with two (possibly delocalized) radical centres which act nearly independently of each other, e.g.  Species in which the two radical centres interact significantly are often referred to as 'biradicaloids'. If the two radical centres are located on the same atom, the species are more properly referred to by their generic names: carbenes, nitrenes, etc. The lowest-energy triplet state of a biradical lies below or at most only a little above its lowest singlet state (usually judged relative to \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T\\), the product of the Boltzmann constant \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\) and the absolute temperature \\(T\\)). The states of those biradicals whose radical centres interact particularly weakly are most easily understood in terms of a pair of local doublets. Theoretical descriptions of low-energy states of biradicals display the presence of two unsaturated valences (biradicals contain one fewer bond than permitted by the rules of valence): the dominant valence bond structures have two dots, the low energy molecular orbital configurations have only two electrons in two approximately nonbonding molecular orbitals, two of the natural orbitals have occupancies close to one, etc. Although this term has been recommended in the past for diradicals, specialists working in the field prefer the latter term.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00673 "bisecting conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00673 "For a structure containing the grouping $\\ce{R3C–C(Y)=X}$ (with identical or different groups $\\ce{R}$) the conformation in which the torsion angle is such that $\\ce{X}$ is antiperiplanar to one of the groups $\\ce{R}$, and, in a Newman projection, the double bond $\\ce{C=X}$ bisects one of the $\\ce{R–C–R}$ angles. In this conformation the bond $\\ce{C–Y}$ eclipses one of the $\\ce{C–R}$ bonds. The other conformation, in which $\\ce{X}$ is synperiplanar to one of the groups $\\ce{R}$, is called an eclipsing conformation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00673 "eclipsing conformation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00674 "bismuthanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00674 "The saturated hydrides of tervalent bismuth, having the general formula $\\ce{Bi_{n}H_{n+2}$. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce{BiH3}$ belong to the class bismuthines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00675))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00675 "bismuthines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00675 """$\\ce{BiH3}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups: $\\ce{R3Bi}$, $\\ce{RBiH2}$, $\\ce{R2BiH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{RH}$) are called primary, secondary and tertiary bismuthines, respectively. A specific bismuthine is preferably named as a substituted bismuthane, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)3Bi}$ trimethylbismuthane.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00676 "bisphenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00676 "By usage, the methylenediphenols, $\\ce{HOC6H4CH2C6H4OH}$, commonly $p,p$-methylenediphenol, and their substitution products (generally derived from condensation of two equivalent amounts of a phenol with an aldehyde or ketone).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00677))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00677 "bivane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00677 "A wind vane used in turbulence studies to obtain horizontal and vertical components of the wind vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00678))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00678 "black film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00678 "A film thinner than about \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) wavelength of visible light. Black films are often equilibrium film, but equilibrium films may be considerably thicker under some conditions. In soap films, two types of equilibrium film are often observed: the one characterized by thickness of the order of \\(7\\ \\rm{nm}\\) or more which varies significantly with minor changes in composition such as ionic strength is called 'common black film'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00679 "blank value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00679 "A reading or result originating from the matrix, reagents and any residual bias in the measurement device or process, which contributes to the value obtained for the quantity in the analytical procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00680))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00680 "blaze-angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00680 "The angle (\\(\\beta \\)) between the operating facet of the grooves and the overall plane of a grating.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00681 "bleaching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00681 "The loss of absorption or emission intensity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00682 "block")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00682 "A portion of a macromolecule, comprising many constitutional units that has at least one feature which is not present in the adjacent portions. Where appropriate, definitions relating to 'macromolecule' may also be applied to 'block'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00683 "block copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00683 "A copolymer that is a block polymer. In the constituent macromolecules of a block copolymer, adjacent blocks are constitutionally different, i.e. adjacent blocks comprise constitutional units derived from different species of monomer or from the same species of monomer but with a different composition or sequence distribution of constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00684))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00684 "block macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00684 "A macromolecule which is composed of blocks in linear sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00685 "block polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00685 "A polymer composed of block macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00686))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00686 "blotting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00686 "A technique used for transferring DNA, RNA or protein from gels to a suitable binding matrix, such as nitrocellulose or Nylon paper, while maintaining the same physical separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00687))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00687 "blowdown")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00687 "Hydrocarbons purged during refinery shutdowns and startups which should be piped to storage systems for safe venting, flaring or recovery. This term also applies to the purging of water in boiler operation, and serves in the control of dissolved solids in the boiler water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00688 "blue shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00688 "Informal expression for hypsochromic shift.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00691 "bohr")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00691 "Atomic unit of length, \\[a_{0}\\approx 5.291\\ 772\\ 49(24)\\times 10^{-11}\\ \\rm{m}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00692 "Bohr magneton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00692 "Electromagnetic fundamental physical constant: \\[\\mu _{\\rm{B}} = \\frac{e\\ \\mathrm{\\hslash }}{2\\ m_{\\rm{e}}} = 9.274\\hspace{0.5em}0154\\hspace{0.25em}\\left(31\\right)\\times 10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{J}\\ \\mathrm{T}^{-1}\\] where \\(e\\) is the elementary charge, \\(\\mathrm{\\hslash}\\) the Planck constant divided by \\(2\\ \\pi \\) and \\(m_{\\rm{e}}\\) the electron rest mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00693 "Bohr radius")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00693 "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of length: \\[a_{0}=\\frac{4\\ \\pi \\ \\varepsilon_{0}\\ \\mathrm{\\hslash }^{2}}{m_{\\rm{e}}\\ e^{2}}=5.291 \\ 772\\ 49\\ (24)\\times 10^{-11}\\ \\rm{m}\\] where \\(e\\) is the elementary charge, \\(\\mathrm{\\hslash}\\) is the Planck constant divided by \\(2\\ \\pi \\) and \\(m_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the electron rest mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00694))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00694 "bolometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00694 "A detector constructed from a material having a large temperature coefficient of resistance. Absorption of radiation gives rise to a change in resistance. A bolometer is named according to its active component, e.g. thermistor bolometer, semiconductor bolometer, superconductor bolometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00695 "Boltzmann constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00695 "The Boltzmann constant, \\(k\\), is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units, the SI, with \\(k = \\pu{1.380649E-23 J K-1}\\). The Boltzmann constant is a proportionality constant between the quantities temperature (with unit kelvin) and energy (with unit joule).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00696 "bomb-digestion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00696 "Materials which are not fully dissolved by acid-digestion at atmospheric pressure may require a more vigorous treatment in pressure vessels lined with polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) glass, silica or vitreous (glassy) carbon or in sealed silica tubes; this treatment is called bomb-digestion. The test sample and acids are heated in such a closed vessel, so that the digestion is carried out at higher temperature and pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00697))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00697 "bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00697 "There is a chemical bond between two atoms or groups of atoms in the case that the forces acting between them are such as to lead to the formation of an aggregate with sufficient stability to make it convenient for the chemist to consider it as an independent 'molecular species'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00698))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00698 "bond dissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00698 "(In ordinary usage the term refers to homolysis only.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00699 "bond-dissociation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00699 "The enthalpy (per mole) required to break a given bond of some specific molecular entity by homolysis, e.g. for , symbolized as \\(D\\left(\\rm{CH}_{3}\\,-\\rm{H}\\right)\\) (cf. heterolytic bond dissociation energy).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00700 "bonded phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00700 "A stationary phase which is covalently bonded to the support particles or to the inside wall of the column tubing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00701))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00701 "bond energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00701 "The average value of the gas-phase bond dissociation energies (usually at a temperature of \\(298\\ \\rm{K}\\)) for all bonds of the same type within the same chemical species. The mean bond energy for methane, for example, is one-fourth the enthalpy of reaction for:  Tabulated bond energies are generally values of bond energies averaged over a number of selected typical chemical species containing that type of bond.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00701 "mean bond energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00702 "bond-energy-bond-order method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00702 "An empirical procedure for estimating activation energies, involving empirical relationships between bond length, bond dissociation energy, and bond order.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00703 "bonding number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00703 "The bonding number \\(n\\) of a skeletal atom is the sum of the total number of bonding equivalents (valence bonds) of that skeletal atom to adjacent skeletal atoms in a parent hydride, if any, and the number of attached hydrogen atoms, if any. Examples: $\\ce{SH2}$: for $\\ce{S}$, \\(n=2\\); $\\ce{SH6}$: for $\\ce{S}$, \\(n=6\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00705 "bond number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00705 "The number of electron-pair bonds between two nuclei in any given Lewis formula. For example, in ethene the bond number between the carbon atoms is two, and between the carbon and hydrogen atoms it is one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00707))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00707 "bond order")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00707 "Theoretical index of the degree of bonding between two atoms relative to that of a single bond, i.e. the bond provided by one localized electron pair. In molecular orbital theory it is the sum of the products of the corresponding atomic orbital coefficients (weights) over all the occupied molecular spin-orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00709 "boranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00709 "The molecular hydrides of boron, e.g. $\\ce{B5H9}$ pentaborane(9).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00710 "boranylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00710 "The species $\\ce{RB{:}}$ containing an electrically neutral univalent boron atom with two formally non-bonding electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00711 "borderline mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00711 "A mechanism intermediate between two extremes, for example a nucleophilic substitution intermediate between \\(\\rm{S}_{\\rm{N}}1\\) and \\(\\rm{S}_{\\rm{N}}2\\), or intermediate between electron transfer and \\(\\rm{S}_{\\rm{N}}2\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00712 "borenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00712 "A traditional term for boranylidenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00713 "borinic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00713 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2BOH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00714))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00714 "boronic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00714 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RB(OH)2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00715 "borylenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00715 "A traditional term for boranylidenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00716 "boson")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00716 "Particle of integer spin quantum number following Bose–Einstein statistics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00717))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00717 "boundary layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00717 "That well-mixed region of the lower atmosphere in which the turbulence is maintained largely by convective buoyancy induced by the upward heat flux originating from the solar-heated surface of the earth. During the afternoon this often extends from \\(1\\ \\rm{km}\\) to \\(5\\ \\rm{km}\\) in height. The surface boundary layer is that region of the lower atmosphere where the shearing stress is constant. It is separated by the Ekman layer from the free atmosphere, where the behaviour of the atmosphere approaches that of an ideal fluid in approximate geostrophic equilibrium (horizontal coriolis force balances the horizontal pressure force at all points in the field).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00718))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00718 "bound fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00718 "The fraction of the incubation mixture which, after separation, contains the analyte bound to the binding reagent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00719))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00719 "bowsprit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00719 "In the boat form of cyclohexane and related structures there are two ring atoms lying out of the plane of the other four; exocyclic bonds to these two atoms pointing in a direction roughly parallel to that plane are called bowsprit, the other two are called flagpole.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00720))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00720 "branch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00720 "An oligomeric or polymeric offshoot from a macromolecular chain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00720 "side chain")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:B00720 "pendant chain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00721))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00721 "branched chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00721 "A chain with at least one branch point intermediate between the boundary units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00722 "branched polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00722 "A polymer the molecules of which are branched chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00723 "branching chain reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00723 "A chain reaction which includes a propagation step or steps in which there is an increase in the number of active intermediates; for example,  is a branching reaction since one active intermediate ($\\ce{H}$) gives rise to two ($\\ce{OH}$ and $\\ce{O}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00724))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00724 "branching decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00724 "Nuclear decay which can proceed in two or more different ways.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00725))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00725 "branching fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00725 "In branching decay the fraction of nuclei which decay in a specified way.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00726 "branching index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00726 "A parameter, \\(g\\), characterizing the effect of long-chain branches on the size of a branched macromolecule in solution and defined as the ratio of the mean-square radius of gyration of a branched molecule, \\(< s_{\\rm{b}}^{2} >\\), to that of an otherwise identical linear molecule, \\(< s _{\\rm{l}}^{2} >\\), with the same relative molecular mass in the same solvent and at the same temperature, i.e. \\(g = \\frac{< s_{\\rm{b}}^{2} >}{< s_{\\rm{l}}^{2} >}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00727))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00727 "branching ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00727 "The ratio of alternative products formed in a reaction (e.g. the $\\ce{HF}$/$\\ce{DF}$ ratio in the reaction between $\\ce{F}$ and $\\ce{HD}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00728 "branch point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00728 "Point on a polymer chain at which a branch is attached.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00729 "branch unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00729 "A constitutional unit containing a branch point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00730 "break")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00730 "The process involving the coalescence of gas bubbles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00731 "break")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00731 "The flocculation of an emulsion, viz. the formation of aggregates may be followed by coalescence. If coalescence is extensive it leads to the formation of a macrophase and the emulsion is said to break.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00732 "Bredt's rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00732 "A double bond cannot be placed with one terminus at the bridgehead of a bridged ring system unless the rings are large enough to accommodate the double bond without excessive strain. For example, while bicyclo[2.2.1]hept-1-ene is only capable of existence as a transient, its higher homologues having a double bond at the bridgehead position have been isolated: e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00733))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00733 "breeching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00733 "The passage or conduit through which the exhaust products of combustion are carried to the stack or chimney.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00734))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00734 "breeze")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00734 "A term sometimes used to describe very fine particles of coke. Also a meteorological term for a gentle wind.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00735))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00735 "Bremsstrahlung")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00735 "X-radiation emitted as a result of the retardation of high energy particles by matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00736 "bridge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00736 "A valence bond or an atom or an unbranched chain of atoms connecting two different parts of a molecule. The two tertiary carbon atoms connected through the bridge are termed bridgeheads.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00737))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00737 "bridged carbocation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00737 "A carbocation (real or hypothetical) in which there are two (or more) carbon atoms that could in alternative Lewis formulae be designated as carbenium centres but which is instead represented by a structure in which a group (a hydrogen atom or a hydrocarbon residue, possibly with substituents in non-involved positions) bridges these potential carbenium centres. One may distinguish 'electron-sufficient bridged carbocations' and 'electron-deficient bridged carbocations'. Examples of the former are phenyl-bridged ions (for which the trivial name 'phenonium ion' has been used), such as (A). These ions are straightforwardly classified as carbenium ions. The latter type of ion necessarily involves three-centre bonding. Structures (C) and (D) contain five-coordinate carbon atoms. The 'hydrogen-bridged carbocation' (B) contains a two-coordinate hydrogen atom. hypercoordination, which includes two-coordination for hydrogen and five- but also higher coordination for carbon is generally observed in bridged carbocations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00739))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00739 "bridge index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00739 "In a coordination entity, the number of central atoms linked by a particular bridging ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00740 "bridge solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00740 "Solution of high concentration of inert salt, preferably comprising cations and anions of equal mobility, optionally interposed between the reference electrode filling and both the test and standard solution, when the test solution and filling solution are chemically incompatible. This procedure introduces into the operational cell a second liquid junction formed usually in a similar way to the first. (This definition refers to a bridge solution of a double junction reference electrode.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00741))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00741 "bridging ligand")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00741 "A ligand attached to two or more, usually metallic, central atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00744 "Brønsted acid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00744 "A molecular entity capable of donating a hydron (proton) to a base, (i.e. a 'hydron donor') or the corresponding chemical species. For example: $\\ce{H2O}$, $\\ce{H3O^{+}}$, $\\ce{CH3CO2H}$, $\\ce{H2SO4}$, $\\ce{HSO4^{−}}$, $\\ce{HCl}$, $\\ce{CH3OH}$, $\\ce{NH3}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00745 "Brønsted base")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00745 "A molecular entity capable of accepting a hydron (proton) from an acid (i.e. a 'hydron acceptor') or the corresponding chemical species. For example: $\\ce{OH−}$, $\\ce{H2O}$, $\\ce{CH3CO2^{−}}$, $\\ce{HSO4^{−}}$, $\\ce{SO4^{2−}}$, $\\ce{Cl^{−}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00746 "Br&#xF8;nsted relation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00746 "The term applies to either of the equations: \\[\\frac{k_{\\rm{HA}}}{p} = G\\left ( \\frac{q\\ K_{\\rm{HA}}}{p} \\right )^{\\alpha}\\] \\[\\frac{k_{\\rm{A}}}{q} = G\\left ( \\frac{q\\ K_{\\rm{HA}}}{p} \\right )^{-\\beta} \\] (or their logarithmic forms) where \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\) and \\(G\\) are constants for a given reaction series (\\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are called 'Brønsted exponents'), \\(k_{\\rm{HA}}\\) and \\(k_{\\rm{A}}\\) are catalytic coefficients (or rate coefficients) of reactions whose rates depend on the concentrations of $\\ce{HA}$ and/or of $\\ce{A^{-}}$. \\(K_{\\rm{HA}}\\) is the acid dissociation constant of the acid $\\ce{HA}$, \\(p\\) is the number of equivalent acidic protons in the acid $\\ce{HA}$, and \\(q\\) is the number of equivalent basic sites in its conjugate base $\\ce{A^{-}}$. The chosen values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) should always be specified. (The charge designations of $\\ce{H}$ and $\\ce{A}$ are only illustrative.) The Brønsted relation is often termed the 'Brønsted catalysis law' (or the 'Catalysis law'). Although justifiable on historical grounds, this name is not recommended, since Brønsted relations are known to apply to many uncatalysed and pseudo-catalysed reactions (such as simple proton (hydron) transfer reactions). The term 'pseudo-Brønsted relation' is sometimes used for reactions which involve nucleophilic catalysis instead of acid–base catalysis. Various types of Brønsted parameters have been proposed such as \\(\\beta_{\\rm{lg}}\\), \\(\\beta_{\\rm{nuc}}\\), \\(\\beta_{\\rm{eq}}\\) for leaving group, nucleophile and equilibrium constants, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00747 "Brooks and Taylor structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00747 "The structure of the anisotropic spheres which precipitate from isotropic pitch during pyrolysis. The structure of the spheres consists of a lamellar arrangement of aromatic molecules in parallel layers which are perpendicular to the polar axis of the sphere and which are perpendicular to the mesophase-isotropic phase interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00748 "Brownian motion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00748 "The movement of particles in a colloidal system such as an aerosol caused by collision with the molecules in the fluid in which the particles are imbedded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00750))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00750 "bubble column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00750 "A bioreactor, in the shape of a column, in which the reaction medium is kept mixed and aerated by introduction of air into the bottom (cf. air lift bioreactor).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00751 "bubbler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00751 "An apparatus used to absorb certain water soluble components in a gas stream for later analysis. Usually it involves the use of a glass fritted tube which forces the air into small bubbles of high surface area during operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00752 "buffer-addition technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00752 "A technique in which an additive (called a spectrochemical buffer) is added to both the sample and reference solutions for the purpose of making the measure of the analyte less sensitive to variations in interferent concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00753 "bulk concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00753 "In any technique that involves the establishment of a concentration gradient, either within the material from which an electrode is made or in the solution that is in contact with an electrode, the bulk concentration of a substance B is the total or analytical concentration of B at points so remote from the electrode-solution interface that the concentration gradient for B is indistinguishable from zero at the instant under consideration. In common practice the bulk concentration of B is taken to be the total or analytical concentration of B that would be present throughout the electrode or solution if there were no current flowing through the cell and if the electrode and solution did not interact in any way. In the absence of any homogeneous reaction or other process that produces or consumes B, the bulk concentration of B is the total or analytical concentration of B that is present before the excitation signal is applied.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00754))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00754 "bulk mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00754 "A continuous anisotropic phase formed by coalescence of mesophase spheres. Bulk mesophase retains fluidity and is deformable in the temperature range up to about \\(770\\ \\rm{K}\\), and transforms into green coke by further loss of hydrogen or low-molecular-weight species. This bulk mesophase can sometimes be formed directly from the isotropic pitch without observation of intermediate spheres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00755 "bulk rheology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00755 "Rheology may be conveniently divided into bulk rheology, in which effects due to the surface of the system can be neglected, and surface rheology, in which such effects are predominant. It should be noted that in surface rheology the neglect of bulk behaviour is permissible only in exceptional circumstances, such as for very thin films surrounded by a gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00756 "bulk sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00756 "The sample resulting from the planned aggregation or combination of sample units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00758 "Bunnett–Olsen equations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00758 "The equations for the relation between \\(\\log _{10}(\\frac{\\rm{[SH}^{+}\\rm{]}}{[S]})+\\rm{H}_{0}\\) and \\(\\log _{10}\\rm{[H}^{+}\\rm{]}+\\rm{H}_{0}\\) for base S in aqueous mineral acid solution, where \\(\\rm{H}_{0}\\) is Hammett's acidity function and \\(\\log _{10}\\rm{[H}^{+}\\rm{]}+\\rm{H}_{0}\\) represents the activity function \\(\\frac{\\log _{10}(\\gamma _{S}\\ \\gamma _{H^{+}})}{\\gamma _{\\rm{SH}^{+}}}\\) for the nitroaniline reference bases to build \\(\\rm{H}_{0}\\). \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{[\\rm{SH}^{+}]}{[\\rm{S}]})- \\log _{10}[\\rm{H}^{+}]=(\\varPhi - 1)\\ (\\log _{10}\\rm{[H}^{+}\\rm{]}+\\rm{H}_{0})+pK_{\\rm{SH}^{+}}\\] \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{[\\rm{SH}^{+}]}{[\\rm{S}]})+H_{0}=\\varPhi \\ (\\log _{10}[\\rm{H}^{+}]+\\rm{H}_{0})+pK_{\\rm{SH}^{+}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00759))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00759 "Bunsen burner")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00759 "Premix burners are distinguished as Bunsen-, Meker-, or slot-burners according to whether they have one large hole, a number of small holes, or a slot as outlet port(s) for the gas mixture, respectively. When several parallel slots are present, they are identified as multislot burners (e.g. a three-slot burner).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00760 "Bunte salts")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00760 "Salts (usually sodium salts) of $S$-alkylthiosulfuric acid, of general structure $\\ce{RSS(=O)2O^{−}M^{+}}$. Use of this term is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00762 "burning velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00762 "The mean velocity of the flame front (in \\(\\rm{mm s}^{-1}\\)) towards the unburnt gas mixture (usually vertically downwards). The quantity applies to gas mixtures and not to injection burners, and depends on the flame temperature and the solvent nebulized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00763 "burn-up")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00763 "Induced nuclear transformation of atoms during reactor operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00764))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00764 "burn-up fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00764 "The fraction of an initial quantity of a given nuclide that has undergone burn-up.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:B00766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:B00766 "bypass injector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:B00766 "A sample injector by means of which the eluent (carrier gas) may be temporarily diverted through a sample chamber so that the sample is carried to the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06756 "bioconjugate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06756 "A molecular species produced by living systems of biological origin when it is composed of two parts of different origins, e.g. a conjugate of a xenobiotic with some groups, e.g. glutathione, sulfate or glucuronic acid, to make it soluble in water or compartmentalized within the cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06768))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06768 "biomimetic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06768 "Refers to a laboratory procedure designed to imitate a natural chemical process. Also refers to a compound that mimics a biological material in its structure or function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06798 "bioisostere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06798 "A compound resulting from the exchange of an atom or group of atoms with another, broadly similar, atom or group of atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06813))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06813 "bioavailability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06813 "Ratio of the systemic exposure from extravascular (ev) exposure to that following intravenous (iv) exposure as described by the equation: \\[F=\\frac{A_{\\rm{ev}}\\ D_{\\rm{iv}}}{B_{\\rm{iv}}\\ D_{\\rm{ev}}}\\] where \\(F\\) is the bioavailabilty, \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are areas under the (plasma) concentration-time curve following extravascular and intravenous administration, respectively, and \\(D_{\\rm{ev}}\\) and \\(D_{\\rm{iv}}\\) are the administered extravascular and intravenous doses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06847 "bioavailability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06847 "Extent of absorption of a substance by a living organism compared to a standard system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06885 "backflush")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06885 "Temporary reversal of the direction of the permeable flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06899 "benchmark dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06899 "The statistical lower bound on a dose corresponding to a specified level of risk.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT06899 "lower effective dose")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06900))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06900 "biological effect monitoring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06900 "Continuous or repeated measurement of early biological effects of exposure to a substance to evaluate ambient exposure and health risk by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and biological effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06901))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06901 "biological exposure index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06901 "Reference values intended as guidelines for the evaluation of potential health hazards in the practice of industrial hygiene. BEIs represent the levels of determinants that are most likely to be observed in specimens collected from a healthy worker who has been exposed to chemicals to the same extent as a worker with inhalation exposure to the TLV.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06902 "biological monitoring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06902 "Continuous or repeated measurement of potentially toxic substances or their metabolites in tissues, secreta, excreta, expired air, or any combination of these to evaluate occupational or environmental exposure and health risk by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and resultant health effects. Sometimes also called 'biomonitoring'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06903 "biological tolerance values for working materials")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06903 "The maximum permissible quantity of a chemical compound or its metabolites, or any deviation from the norm of biological parameters induced by those substances in exposed humans.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT06903 "Biologische Arbeitsstoff Toleranz Werte")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06904))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06904 "biomarker")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06904 "A parameter that can be used to identify a toxic effect in an individual organism and can be used in extrapolation between species, or as an indicator signalling an event or condition in a biological system or sample and giving a measure of exposure, effect, or susceptibility. The term 'biomarker' is generally used in scientific publications. In occupational hygiene and health, 'biological monitoring' and 'biological effect monitoring' are used with the meaning 'monitoring biomarkers'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06905 "body burden")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06905 "Total amount of substance of a chemical present in an organism at a given time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06997 "back donation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06997 "A description of the bonding of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}conjugated}$ ligands to a transition metal which involves a synergic process with donation of electrons from the filled $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ or lone electron pair orbital of the ligand into an empty orbital of the metal (donor–acceptor bond), together with release (back donation) of electrons from an nd orbital of the metal (which is of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$ with respect to the metal–ligand axis) into the empty $\\ce{\\uppi\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}antibonding orbital}$ $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}complex}$ of the ligand.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT06997 "Chatt–Dewar–Duncanson model")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06998))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06998 "basis function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06998 "A one-electron function used in the expansion of the molecular orbital function. Basis functions are commonly represented by atomic orbitals (see also Slater-type orbital or Gaussian-type orbital) centered on each atom of the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT06999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT06999 "basis set")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT06999 "A set of basis functions employed for the representation of molecular orbitals. One may distinguish the minimal basis set (includes one basis function for each SCF occupied atomic orbital with distinct principal and angular momentum quantum numbers); split valence basis set (includes two or more sizes of basis function for each valence orbital); double zeta (DZ) basis set (a split valence basis set that includes exactly twice as many functions as the minimal basis set); extended basis set (the set larger than the double zeta basis set); polarized basis set (incorporates basis functions of higher angular quantum number beyond what is required by the atom in its electronic ground state; allows orbitals to change not only a size, but also a shape); basis set with diffuse functions and others.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07000))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07000 "Bent's rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07000 "In a molecule, smaller bond angles are formed between electronegative ligands since the central atom, to which the ligands are attached, tends to direct bonding hybrid orbitals of greater p character towards its more electronegative substituents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07001))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07001 "binding energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07001 "The difference between the total energy of a molecular system and the sum of the energies of its isolated $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$. The value of binding energy depends upon the geometrical arrangement of the isolated subunits (molecules). According to another definition, the term to be subtracted from the total energy is the sum of the energies of the separate atoms in the corresponding valence states, which compose the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07002))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07002 "bond energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07002 "The energy required to break a given type of bond between atoms in certain valence states. An averaged bond energy is commonly derived by dissecting the heat of atomization of a molecule into contributions of individual bonds. For molecules with localized bonds, the heats of atomization (formation) are usually well approximated by the sum of pertinent averaged bond energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07003 "bond length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07003 "The distance between atomic centers involved in a chemical bond. The notion of bond length is defined differently in various experimental methods of determination of molecular geometry; this leads to small (usually \\(0.01{-}0.02\\ \\mathring{\\rm{A}}\\)) differences in bond lengths obtained by different techniques. For example, in gas-phase electron-diffraction experiments, the bond length is the interatomic distance averaged over all occupied vibrational states at a given temperature. In an X-ray crystal structural method, the bond length is associated with the distance between the centroids of electron densities around the nuclei. In gas-phase microwave spectroscopy, the bond length is an effective interatomic distance derived from measurements on a number of isotopic molecules, etc. A number of empirical relationships between bond lengths and bond orders in polyatomic molecules were suggested, see, for example, fractional bond number (the Pauling's bond order).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07004 "bond orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07004 "A localized molecular orbital related to a certain $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$, $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$, or $\\ce{\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07005 "bond order")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07005 """The electron population in the region between atoms A and B of a molecular entity at the expense of electron density in the immediate vicinity of the individual atomic centers. Different schemes of partitioning electron density give rise to different definitions of bond orders. In the framework of the Mulliken population analysis, bond order is associated with the total overlap population, \\[\\rm{q}_{\\rm{AB}}=2\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
\\unicode[Times]{x3BC} 
\\end{array}}^{\\rm{A}}\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}

u 
\\end{array}}^{\\rm{B}}\\rm{P}_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC} \\unicode[Times]{x3BD} }\\ \\rm{S}_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC} \\unicode[Times]{x3BD} }\\] where \\(\\rm{P}_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BD}}\\) and \\(\\rm{S}_{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\unicode[Times]{x3BD}}\\) are respectively the elements of the density matrix and overlap matrix (see overlap integral). A large positive value of bond order signifies strong bonding between the atoms of the molecular entity, whereas negative values of \\(\\rm{q}_{\\rm{AB}}\\) imply that electrons are displaced away from the inter-atomic region and point to an anti-bonding interaction. In valence bond theory, bond order is given by a weighted average of the formal bond orders (i.e. by the number of electron pairs in a given Lewis structure) between the atoms in the resonance structures (see resonance hybrid).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07006 "bond-dissociation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07006 "For a diatomic molecule, the maximum vibrational energy that a molecule can have prior to its decomposition into the ground electronic states of the constituent atoms (spectroscopic bond-dissociation energy, \\(D_{\\rm{e}}\\)). The \\(D_{\\rm{e}}\\) value is related to the chemical dissociation energy: \\(D_{\\rm{0}}=D_{\\rm{e}}- \\mathrm{E}_{\\rm{vib}}\\left(0\\right)\\), where \\(\\mathrm{E}_{\\rm{vib}}\\left(0\\right)\\) is zero-point vibrational energy. This definition is usually extended to the dissociation of polyatomic molecules into certain molecular fragments through homolytic or heterolytic bond cleavages.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07007 "bonding molecular orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07007 "The molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons increases the total bonding (usually, lowers the total energy) of a molecule. Generally, the energy level of a bonding MO lies lower than the average of the valence orbitals of the atoms constituting the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07008 "Born–Oppenheimer approximation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07008 "Representation of the complete wavefunction as a product of an electronic and a nuclear part, \\[\\Psi \\left(\\rm{r,R}\\right)=\\Psi _{\\rm{e}}\\left(\\rm{r,R}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{\\rm{N}}\\left(\\rm{R}\\right)\\] where the two wave-functions may be determined separately by solving two different Schroedinger equations. The validity of the Born–Oppenheimer approximation is founded on the fact that the ratio of electronic to nuclear mass (\\(\\frac{m}{M}\\cong 5\\times 10^{-4}\\)) is sufficiently small and the nuclei, as compared to the rapidly moving electrons, appear to be fixed. The approximation breaks down near a point where two electronic states acquire the same energy (see Jahn–Teller effect). The BO approximation is often considered as being synonymous with the adiabatic approximation. More precisely, the latter term denotes the case when \\(\\Psi _{\\rm{e}}\\) diagonalize the electronic Hamiltonian. Thus, the adiabatic approximation is an application of the BO approximation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT07008 "BO approximation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07169 "biodegradable polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07169 "Polymer susceptible to degradation by biological activity, with the degradation accompanied by a lowering of its molar mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07330))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07330 "bicycle-pedal mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07330 "Volume-conserving mechanism proposed for the photoisomerization of conjugated double bonds. Two alternating double bonds rotate concertedly with only the two $\\ce{CH}$ units turning in and out of the plane of the molecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT07330 "BP mechanism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07331 "bicycle rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07331 "Photochemical rearrangement of unsaturated substrates by group migration over a $\\ce{\\uppi\\! perimeter}$ following the movement of a bicycle pedal. An example is:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07334 "bipolarons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07334 "Bound pairs of polarons mutually attracted by the lattice distortion in a solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07335 "branching plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07335 "At a conical intersection point, the plane spanned by the gradient difference vector (\\(\\boldsymbol{x_{1}}\\)) and the gradient of the interstate coupling vector (\\(\\boldsymbol{x_{2}}\\)): \\[x_{1} = \\frac{\\delta(E_{2}-E_{1})}{\\delta Q}\\boldsymbol{q}\\] \\[x_{2} = \\boldsymbol{q}\\] where \\(\\boldsymbol{C_{1}}\\) and \\(\\boldsymbol{C_{2}}\\) are the configuration interaction eigenvectors (i.e., the excited and ground-state adiabatic wavefunctions) in a conical intersection problem, \\(H\\) is the conical intersection Hamiltonian, \\(\\textbf{Q}\\) represents the nuclear configuration vector of the system, and thus \\(\\textbf{q}\\) is a unit vector in the direction of vector \\(\\textbf{q}\\). \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(E_{2}\\) are the energies of the lower and upper states, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07336 "Brewster angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07336 "When an unpolarized planar electromagnetic wavefront impinges on a flat dielectric surface, there is a unique angle (\\(\\theta_{\\rm{B}}\\)), commonly referred to as Brewster angle, at which the reflected waves are all polarized into a single plane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07337 "brightness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07337 "Obsolete term. This term is reserved for non-quantitative reference to physiological perception of light and is not recommended as a quantitative measure of the radiance of an emitting device, e.g., a lamp.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07338 "brightness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07338 "Product of the fluorescence quantum yield (\\(\\varPhi _{\\rm{f}}\\)) of a dye and the molar decadic absorption coefficient at the excitation wavelength, \\(\\varepsilon\\left(\\lambda \\right)\\), i.e., \\(\\varPhi _{\\rm{f}}\\ \\varepsilon\\left(\\lambda \\right)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:BT07564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:BT07564 "bimodal network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:BT07564 "Polymer network comprising polymer chains having two significantly different molar-mass distributions between adjacent junction points.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:BT07564 "bimodal polymer network")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00767 "χ-parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00767 "A numerical parameter, $\\chi$, employed in the Flory–Huggins theory, which accounts in the main for the contribution of the non-combinatorial entropy of mixing and for the enthalpy of mixing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00769 "cage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00769 "An aggregate of molecules, generally in the condensed phase, that surrounds the fragments formed, for example, by thermal or photochemical dissociation. Because the cage hinders the separation of the fragments by diffusion, they may preferentially react with one another ('cage effect') but not necessarily to reform the precursor species. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00770 "cage compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00770 "A polycyclic compound having the shape of a cage. The term is also used for inclusion compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00771 "cage effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00771 "When in a condensed phase, or in a dense gas, reactant molecules come together, or species are formed in proximity to one another, and are caged in by surrounding molecules, they may undergo a set of collisions known as an encounter; the term 'cage effect' is then applied.The cage effect is also known as the Franck–Rabinowitch effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00773 "calcination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00773 "Heating to high temperatures in air or oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00774))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00774 "calcined coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00774 "A petroleum coke or coal-derived pitch coke obtained by heat treatment of green coke to about \\(1600\\ \\rm{K}\\). It will normally have a hydrogen content of less than \\(0.1\\ \\rm{wt.}\\%\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00775 "calibration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00775 "The set of operations which establish, under specified conditions, the relationship between values indicated by the analytical instrument and the corresponding known values of an analyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00776 "calibration component")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00776 "A component of a calibration mixture, present in the gaseous or vapour state, quantitatively and qualitatively defined, and used directly for testing and for calibration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00778 "calibration function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00778 "The functional (not statistical) relationship for the chemical measurement process, relating the expected value of the observed (gross) signal or response variable \\(E(y)\\) to the analyte amount \\(x\\). The corresponding graphical display for a single analyte is referred to as the calibration curve. When extended to additional variables or analytes which occur in multicomponent analysis, the 'curve' becomes a calibration surface or hypersurface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00779 "calibration gas mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00779 "A gas mixture of known composition, generally comprising one or more calibration components and a complementary gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00780 "calibration material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00780 "A material of known composition or properties which can be presented to the analytical instrument for calibration purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00781))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00781 "calibration mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00781 "A gaseous or liquid mixture of known composition, generally comprising one or more calibration components and an inert diluent, used directly for testing and calibration of analytical instruments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00782 "calibration sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00782 "The test portion or test solution used for calibration of an analytical procedure. The calibration sample is normally of known weight or volume and is prepared according to specifications.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00783 "calixarenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00783 "Originally macrocyclic compounds capable of assuming a basket (or 'calix') shaped conformation. They are formed from p-hydrocarbyl phenols and formaldehyde. The term now applies to a variety of derivatives by substitution of the hydrocarbon cyclo{oligo[(1,3-phenylene)methylene]}.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00784))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00784 "calorie")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00784 "Non-SI unit of energy. There are three differently defined calories: thermochemical calorie (\\(\\rm{cal}_{\\rm{th}} = 4.184\\ \\rm{J}\\)), international steam table calorie (\\(\\rm{cal}_{\\rm{IT}} = 4.1868\\ \\rm{J}\\)), \\(15\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\) calorie (\\(\\rm{cal}_{15} = 4.1855\\ \\rm{J}\\)), however only the symbol \\(\\rm{cal}\\) is usually used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00785))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00785 "calorimetric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00785 "A titration performed in a calorimeter which produces a plot of heat change versus volume of titrant. This is the preferred term for experiments in which the main goal is the measurement of thermodynamic parameters. Because such experiments may be performed in any of a variety of types of calorimeters with varying degrees of calorimetric accuracy, it is important that the reporting of such results include an assessment of accuracy and precision. The term stands in contrast to the main emphasis in enthalpimetric analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00786))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00786 "calorimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00786 "A general term describing any experiment in which heat is measured as some chemical reaction or physical process occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00787 "candela")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00787 "The candela, symbol \\(\\pu{cd}\\), is the SI unit of luminous intensity in a given direction. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the luminous efficacy of monochromatic radiation of frequency \\(\\pu{540E12 Hz}\\), \\(K_{\\rm{cd}}\\), to be \\(683\\) when expressed in the unit \\(\\pu{lm W^{-1}}\\), which is equal to \\(\\pu{cd sr W^{-1}}\\), or \\(\\pu{cd sr kg-1 m-2 s3}\\), where the kilogram, metre and second are defined in terms of h, c and ΔνCs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00789))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00789 "canonical rate constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00789 "The rate constant for a system in which the reactants are in thermal equilibrium at a given temperature. In statistical mechanics the expression 'canonical ensemble' is used to refer to a closed system in thermal equilibrium, the species being present in a statistical distribution. By contrast, a microcanonical ensemble is composed of systems all having the same energy: a canonical ensemble therefore consists of a statistical distribution of microcanonical ensembles. The canonical or thermal rate constant can be obtained from the microcanonical rate constant by summing over the energy, taking into account the statistical distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00790 "canonical variational transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00790 "A development of transition-state theory in which the position of the dividing surface is varied so as to minimize the rate constant at a given temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00791))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00791 "capacitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00791 "The charge on a plate divided by the potential difference between the plates.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00791 "electric capacitance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00792 "capacitance hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00792 "Hygrometer using the capacitance variations of a capacitor whose dielectric medium consists of the gas or of a material in contact with this gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00793 "capillary column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00793 "A general term for columns having a small diameter. A capillary column may contain a packing or have the stationary phase supported on its inside wall. The former case corresponds to packed capillary column while the latter case corresponds to an open-tabular column. Due to the ambiguity of this term its use without an adjective is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00794 "capillary condensation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00794 "Capillary condensation is said to occur when, in porous solids, multilayer adsorption from a vapour proceeds to the point at which pore spaces are filled with liquid separated from the gas phase by menisci. The concept loses its sense when the dimensions of the pores are so small that the term meniscus ceases to have a physical significance. Capillary condensation is often accompanied by hysteresis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00795 "captodative effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00795 "Effect on the stability of a carbon-centred radical determined by the combined action of a captor (electron withdrawing) and a dative (electron releasing) substituent, both attached to the radical centre. The term is also used for certain unsaturated compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00796 "capture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00796 "A process in which an atomic or nuclear system acquires an additional particle. In general, a specification is added of the type of the captured particle or its energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00798 "capture cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00798 "The cross-section for capture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00800 "capture γ-radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00800 "The γ-radiation emitted in radiative capture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00802 "carbaboranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00802 "A class of boron compound of general formula $\\ce{[(CH)_{a}(BH)_{m}H_{b}]^{c}}$ where $c$ can be positive, negative or zero. The $\\ce{CH}$ groups occupy polyhedron vertices, and other hydrogen atoms are either bridging or terminal.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00802 "carboranes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00803))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00803 "carbamates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00803 "Salts or esters of carbamic acid, $\\ce{H2NC(=O)OH}$, or of $N$-substituted carbamic acids $\\ce{R2NC(=O)OR'}$, ($\\ce{R'}$ = hydrocarbyl or a cation). The esters are often called urethanes or urethans, a usage that is strictly correct only for the ethyl esters.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00804 "carbanion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00804 "Generic name for anions containing an even number of electrons and having an unshared pair of electrons on a tervalent carbon atom (e.g. $\\ce{Cl3C^{−}}$ or $\\ce{HC#C^{−}}$) or — if the ion is mesomeric (see mesomerism) — having at least one significant contributing structure with an unshared pair of electrons on a tervalent carbon atom, for example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00806 "carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00806 "The electrically neutral species $\\ce{H2C{:}}$ (methylene) and its derivatives, in which the carbon is covalently bonded to two univalent groups of any kind or a divalent group and bears two nonbonding electrons, which may be spin-paired (singlet state) or spin-non-paired (triplet state). In systematic name formation, carbene is the name of the parent hydride $\\ce{C{:}H2}$ hence, the name dichlorocarbene for $\\ce{C{:}Cl2}$. However, names for acyclic and cyclic hydrocarbons containing one or more divalent carbon atoms are derived from the name of the corresponding all-\\(\\lambda ^{4}\\)-hydrocarbon using the suffix -ylidene. E.g. prop-2-en-1-ylidene, $\\ce{H2C=CHCH{:}}$ ethenylidene, $\\ce{H2C=C{:}}$; cyclohexylidene,Subclasses of carbenes include acyl carbenes , imidoyl carbenes,  and vinyl carbenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00807))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00807 "carbene analogues")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00807 """The electrically neutral mononuclear hydrides of group 14 having two non-bonding electrons, the electrically neutral mononuclear hydrides of group 15 having four non-bonding electrons, and also the compound $\\ce{HB{:}}$ . The names of a number of these are shown below.

$\\ce{RB{:}}$boranylidenes$\\ce{RAs{:}}$arsanylidenes
$\\ce{RN{:}}$nitrenes$\\ce{R2Sn{:}}$stannylidenes
$\\ce{R2Si{:}}$silylenes$\\ce{RSb{:}}$stibanylidenes
$\\ce{RP{:}}$phosphanylidenes$\\ce{R2Pb{:}}$plumbylidenes
$\\ce{R2Ge{:}}$germylidenes

Note that if $\\ce{R} = \\ce{H}$, these compounds are parent hydrides; derivatives formed by substitution are named accordingly. However, if the substituent's first atom, bearing the free valence, is of the same element as the atom of the carbene analogue, other parent compounds may be required (see carbenes), e.g. $\\ce{HN{:}}$ nitrene; $\\ce{CH3N{:}}$ methylnitrene; $\\ce{H2NN{:}}$ diazanylidene (synonym isodiazene, not aminonitrene).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00809 "carbene radical anions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00809 "Species $\\ce{R2C^{_{\\vdots}−}}$, having three non-bonding electrons, formally derived by addition of an electron to a carbene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00810 "carbene radical cations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00810 "Species $\\ce{R2C^{.+}}$ having one non-bonding electron, formally derived by subtraction of an electron from a carbene, e.g. $\\ce{H2C^{.+}}$ methyliumyl.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00811 "carbenium centre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00811 "The three-coordinate carbon atom in a carbenium ion to which the excess positive charge of the ion (other than that located on heteroatoms) may be formally considered to be largely attributed, i.e. which has one vacant p-orbital. (N.B. It is not always possible to uniquely identify such an atom.) This formal attribution of charge often does not express the real charge distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00812 "carbenium ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00812 "A generic name for carbocations, real or hypothetical, that have at least one important contributing structure containing a tervalent carbon atom with a vacant p-orbital. (The name implies a protonated carbene or a substitution derivative thereof.) The term was proposed (and rejected) as a replacement for the traditional usage of the name carbonium ion. To avoid ambiguity, the name should not be used as the root for the systematic nomenclature of carbocations. The corresponding difficulty confused carbonium ion nomenclature for many years. For example, the term 'ethylcarbonium ion' has at times been used to refer either to $\\ce{CH3CH2^{+}}$ (ethyl cation) or (correctly) to $\\ce{CH3CH2CH2^{+}}$ (propyl cation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00813))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00813 "carbenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00813 "Complexed carbene-like entities that display the reactivity characteristics of carbenes, either directly or by acting as sources of carbenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00817 "carbocation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00817 "A cation containing an even number of electrons with a significant portion of the excess positive charge located on one or more carbon atoms. This is a general term embracing carbenium ions, all types of carbonium ions vinyl cations, etc. Carbocations may be named by adding the word 'cation' to the name of the corresponding radical. Such names do not imply structure (e.g. whether three-coordinated or five-coordinated carbon atoms are present).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00818 "carbocyclic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00818 "Cyclic compounds in which all of the ring members are carbon atoms, e.g. 1,2-dihydronaphthalene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00819 "carbodiimides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00819 "Carbodiimide, $\\ce{HN=C=NH}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00820))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00820 "carbohydrates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00820 "Originally, compounds such as aldoses and ketoses having the stoichiometric formula $\\ce{C_{n}(H2O)_{n}}$ hence 'hydrates of carbon'. The generic term carbohydrate includes monosaccharides, oligosaccharides and polysaccharides as well as substances derived from monosaccharides by reduction of the carbonyl group (alditols), by oxidation of one or more terminal groups to carboxylic acids, or by replacement of one or more hydroxy group(s) by a hydrogen atom, an amino group, thiol group or similar groups. It also includes derivatives of these compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00821))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00821 "carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00821 "Element number 6 of the periodic table of elements (electronic ground state \\(1\\rm{s}^{2}2\\rm{s}^{2}2\\rm{p}^{2}\\)). For a description of the various types of carbon as a solid the term carbon should be used only in combination with an additional noun or a clarifying adjective.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00822 "carbonaceous mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00822 "A liquid-crystalline state of pitch which shows the optical birefringence of disc-like (discotic) nematic liquid crystals. It can be formed as an intermediate phase during thermolysis (pyrolysis) of an isotropic molten pitch or by precipitation from pitch fractions prepared by selective extraction. Generally, the spherical mesophase precipitated from a pyrolysing pitch has the Brooks and Taylor structure. With continuous heat treatment the carbonaceous mesophase coalesces to a state of bulk mesophase before solidification to green coke with further loss of hydrogen or low-molecular-weight compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00823 "carbon artifact")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00823 "An 'artificially produced' solid body which consists mainly of carbonaceous material in a distinct shape.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00825 "carbon–carbon composite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00825 "A carbon fibre-reinforced carbon matrix material. The carbon matrix phase is typically formed by solid, liquid or gaseous pyrolysis of an organic precursor material. The matrix is either a graphitizable carbon or non-graphitizable carbon, and the carbonaceous reinforcement is fibrous in form. The composite may also contain other components in particulate or fibrous forms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00826 "carbon cenospheres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00826 "Porous or hollow carbonaceous sphere-like particles (frequently in the size range of a few to several hundreds of µm diameter) formed during pyrolysis, also in the course of combustion, of carbonaceous liquid droplets (e.g. heavy fuel) or solid particles (e.g. coal).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00827 "carbon cloth")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00827 "A textile material consisting of carbon fibres oriented at least in two directions. Carbon cloth is not necessarily woven.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00828 "carbon dioxide laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00828 "A continuous or pulsed source of coherent radiation normally tunable through the $\\ce{CO2}$ vibration–rotation band centred near \\(10.6\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00828 "CO<sub>2</sub> laser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00829 "carbon electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00829 "An electrode for an electrical application. In its green state it comprises granular carbon material bound with pitch. The granular carbon material may be either needle coke, fine-grained or isotropic coke or reclaimed graphite powder. Electrodes for use in steel production can only be manufactured from needle coke, and the green electrodes are fired at temperatures above around \\(2800\\ \\rm{K}\\) to produce highly graphite electrodes. The other granular carbon materials may be used for aluminium electrodes where the duty is not so severe, and the green electrodes are generally fired to lower temperatures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00830))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00830 "carbon felt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00830 "A textile material consisting of, in approximation, randomly oriented and intertwined carbon fibres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00831 "carbon fibre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00831 "Fibres (filaments, tows, yarns, rovings) consisting of at least $\\pu{92\\%}$ (mass fraction) carbon, usually in the non-graphitic state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00832))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00832 "carbon fibre fabrics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00832 "Woven textile materials made of carbon fibres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00833 "carbon fibres type HM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00833 "Carbon fibres type HM (high modulus) are carbon fibres with a value of Young's modulus (tensile modulus) larger than \\(300\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) (nearly $\\pu{30\\%}$ of the \\(C_{11}\\) elastic constant of a graphite single crystal).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00834 "carbon fibres type HT")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00834 "Carbon fibres type HT are carbon fibres with values of Young's modulus between \\(150\\) and \\(275\\) to \\(300\\ \\rm{GPa}\\). The term HT, referring to high tensile strength, was early applied because fibres of this type display the highest tensile strengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00835 "carbon fibres type IM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00835 "Carbon fibres type IM (intermediate modulus) are related to carbon fibres type HT because of the comparable values of tensile strength, but are characterized by greater stiffness (Young's modulus up to approximately $\\pu{35\\%}$ of the theoretical \\(C_{11}\\) value).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00836 "carbon fibres type LM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00836 "Carbon fibres type LM (low modulus) are carbon fibres with isotropic structure, tensile modulus values as low as $\\pu{10\\%}$ of the \\(C_{11}\\) values of the graphite single crystal, and low strength values.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00837 "carbon fibres type UHM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00837 "Carbon fibres type UHM (ultra-high modulus) designates a class of carbon fibres having very high values of Young's modulus larger than \\(600\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) (i.e. greater than $\\pu{55\\%}$ of the theoretical \\(C_{11}\\) value of graphite).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00838 "carbonitriles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00838 "In systematic nomenclature, the suffix -carbonitrile is used to name compounds $\\ce{RC#N}$ where the suffix includes the carbon atom of the $\\ce{–CN}$. However, carbonitrile is not a class name for nitriles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00839 "carbonium ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00839 "The term should be used with great care since several incompatible meanings are currently in use. It is not acceptable as the root for systematic nomenclature for carbocation.In most of the existing literature the term is used in its traditional sense for what is here defined as carbenium ion.A carbocation, real or hypothetical, that contains at least one five-coordinate carbon atom.A carbocation, real or hypothetical, whose structure cannot adequately be described by two-electron two-centre bonds only. (The structure may involve carbon atoms with a coordination number greater than five.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00840 "carbonization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00840 "A process by which solid residues with increasing content of the element carbon are formed from organic material usually by pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00841 "carbon material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00841 "A solid high in content of the element carbon and structurally in a non-graphitic state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00842 "carbon mix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00842 "A mixture of filler coke, e.g. grains and/or powders of solid carbon materials and a carbonaceous binder and selected additives, prepared in heated mixers at temperatures in the range of \\(410{-}445\\ \\rm{K}\\) as a preliminary step for the formation of shaped green bodies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00844 "carbonyl compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00844 "Compounds containing the carbonyl group, $\\ce{C=O}$. The term is commonly used in the restricted sense of aldehydes and ketones, although it actually includes carboxylic acids and derivatives.Metal carbonyls, in which carbon monoxide is a formal ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00845 "carbonyl imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00845 "1,3-dipolar compounds having the structure:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00847 "carbonyl oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00847 "1,3 dipolar compounds having the structure  Also called peroxo compounds.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00847 "peroxo compounds")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00848))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00848 "carbonyl ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00848 "1,3-Dipolar compounds having the structure")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00850 "carboxamides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00850 "Amides of carboxylic acids, having the structure $\\ce{RC(=O)NR2}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic name formation to denote the $\\ce{–C(=O)NH2}$ group including its carbon atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00851 "carboxamidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00851 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(=NR)NR2}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic nomenclature to denote the $\\ce{–C(=NH)NH2}$ group including its carbon atom, e.g. acetamidine, $\\ce{CH3C(=NH)NH2}$; 2-butyl-4,5-dihydroimidazole:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00852 "carboxylic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00852 "Oxoacids having the structure $\\ce{RC(=O)OH}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic name formation to denote the $\\ce{–C(=O)OH}$ group including its carbon atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00854 "carbynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00854 "The neutral species $\\ce{HC_{^\\vdots}}$ (methylidyne) and its derivatives formed by substitution in which a univalent carbon atom is covalently bonded to one group and also bears three nonbonding electrons. (This term carries no implication about spin-pairing).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00856 "carbynium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00856 "The cationic species $\\ce{H2C^{.+}}$ or substitution derivatives thereof, formally derived by adding a hydron to a carbyne or subtracting an electron from a carbene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00857 "carotenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00857 "Hydrocarbon carotenoids (a subclass of tetraterpenes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00858 "carotenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00858 "Tetraterpenoids ($\\ce{C40}$), formally derived from the acyclic parent, $\\ce{\\uppsi\\!,\\!\\uppsi\\!-carotene}$ I by hydrogenation, dehydrogenation, cyclization, oxidation, or combination of these processes. This class includes carotenes, xanthophylls and certain compounds that arise from rearrangement of the skeleton of I or by loss of part of this structure. retinoids are excluded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00859 "carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00859 "A substance in appreciable amount which, when associated with a trace of a specified substance, will carry the trace with it through a chemical or physical process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00860 "carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00860 "A substance in appreciable amount which, when associated with a tracer of a specified substance, will carry the tracer with it through a chemical or physical process, or prevent the tracer from undergoing nonspecific processes due to its low concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00861 "carrier atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00861 "A participant atom which is neither core nor peripheral and which carries other groups to and from the molecule containing the core atom(s) but which does not itself become covalently attached to that molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00863 "carrier gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00863 "A gas introduced in order to transport a sample for analytical purposes. In gas chromatography it is the gas which is passed continuously through the column and whose passage promotes the elution of the components of the sample. The carrier gas together with the portions of the sample present in this phase constitutes the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00866 "carry-over")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00866 "A process by which materials are carried into a reaction mixture to which they do not belong. These materials can be either parts of a specimen, or reagents including the diluent or wash solution. In such cases, carry-over means the transfer of material (specimen or reagents) from one container, or from one reaction mixture, to another one. It can be either unidirectional or bidirectional in a series of specimens or assays. The term carry-over effect is used for carry-over from specimen to specimen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00869 "cascade impactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00869 "An instrument used for the classification of aerosols according to size and for possible subsequent chemical analysis. Air is drawn through a series of orifices of decreasing size; the air flow is normal to collecting surfaces on which aerosols are collected by inertial impaction. The particles, separated stepwise by their momentum differences into a number of size ranges, are collected simultaneously.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00870 "catabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00870 "Reactions involving the oxidation of organic substrates to provide chemically available energy (e.g. ATP) and to generate metabolic intermediates.Generally, the process of breakdown of complex molecules into simpler ones, often providing biologically available energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00871 "catabolite repression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00871 "A decrease in the activity of certain auxiliary catabolic enzymes when a surplus of an (easily metabolizable) substrate is available. Commonly this effect is caused by glucose (glucose repression) or by metabolites produced from glucose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00872))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00872 "catalymetric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00872 "A titration process, which involves a catalyst, where the end-point is detected by the sudden increase or decrease of the rate of a reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00873))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00873 "catalysed rate of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00873 "The observed rate of reaction, \\(\\xi \\), minus the spontaneous rate of reaction, \\(\\xi _{0}\\), observed under otherwise similar conditions in the absence of added catalytic material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00874 "catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00874 "The action of a catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00876 "catalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00876 "A substance that increases the rate of a reaction without modifying the overall standard Gibbs energy change in the reaction; the process is called catalysis. The catalyst is both a reactant and product of the reaction. The words catalyst and catalysis should not be used when the added substance reduces the rate of reaction (see inhibitor). Catalysis can be classified as 'homogeneous catalysis', in which only one phase is involved, and 'heterogeneous catalysis', in which the reaction occurs at or near an interface between phases. Catalysis brought about by one of the products of a reaction is called autocatalysis. Catalysis brought about by a group on a reactant molecule itself is called intramolecular catalysis. The term catalysis is also often used when the substance is consumed in the reaction (for example: base-catalysed hydrolysis of esters). Strictly, such a substance should be called an activator.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00876 "surface catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00876 "accelerator")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00876 "kinetic synergist")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00877))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00877 "catalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00877 "A substance included in the solvent to increase the rate of transfer without affecting the position of equilibrium. The term accelerator may also be used but kinetic synergist is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00878 "catalyst ageing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00878 "Catalyst deactivation resulting from changes in the structure or in the texture of the catalyst. Changes of this kind are usually irreversible and the catalyst cannot be regenerated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00880 "catalyst decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00880 "The conversion in a catalytic reaction performed under constant conditions of reaction often decreases with time of run or time on stream. This phenomenon is called catalyst deactivation or catalyst decay.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00880 "catalyst deactivation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00881))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00881 "catalytic activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00881 "Increase in the rate of reaction of a specified chemical reaction that an enzyme produces in a specific assay system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00881 "enzyme activity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00882))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00882 "catalytic activity concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00882 "Catalytic activity of the component divided by the volume of the system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00882 "catalytic concentration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00883))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00883 "catalytic activity content")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00883 "Catalytic activity of the component divided by the mass of the system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00883 "catalytic content")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00884 "catalytic activity fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00884 "Quotient of the catalytic activity of the isozyme and the catalytic activity of all the isozymes of the same enzyme in the system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00884 "catalytic fraction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00885 "catalytic coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00885 """If the rate of reaction, \\(
u\\), is expressible in the form: \\[
u = (k_{0}+\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}k_{i}\\ [C_{i}]^{n_{i}})\\ [A]^{\\alpha }\\ [B]^{\\beta }\\ ...\\] where $\\ce{A}$, $\\ce{B}$, ... are reactants and \\(C_{i}\\) represents one of a set of catalysts, then the proportionality factor \\(k_{i}\\) is the catalytic coefficient of the particular catalyst \\(C_{i}\\). Normally the partial order of reaction \\(n_{i}\\) with respect to a catalyst is unity, so that \\(k_{i}\\) is an \\(\\left ( \\mathit{\\alpha } + \\mathit{\\beta } + ... + 1 \\right )\\)th order rate coefficient. The proportionality factor \\(k_{0}\\) is the \\(\\left ( \\mathit{\\alpha } + \\mathit{\\beta } + ...\\right )\\)th order rate coefficient of the uncatalysed component of the total reaction. For example, if there is catalysis by hydrogen and hydroxide ions, and the order of reaction can be expressed in the form: \\[k = k_{0}+k_{\\rm{H}^{+}}\\ [\\rm{H}^{+}]+k_{\\rm{OH}^{-}}\\ [\\rm{OH}^{-}]\\] then \\(k_{H^{+}}\\) and \\(k_{\\rm{OH}^{-}}\\) are the catalytic coefficients for $\\ce{H^{+}}$ and $\\ce{OH^{-}}$, respectively. The constant \\(k_{0}\\) relates to the uncatalysed reaction.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00889 "catalytic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00889 "The faradaic current that is obtained with a solution containing two substances B and A may exceed the sum of the faradaic currents that would be obtained with B and A separately, but at the same concentrations and under the same experimental conditions. In either of the two following situations the increase is termed a catalytic current. B is reduced or oxidized at the electrode-solution interface to give a product B' that then reduces or oxidizes A chemically. The reaction of B with A may yield either B or an intermediate in the overall half-reaction by which B' was obtained from B. In this situation the increase of current that results from the addition of A to a solution of B may be termed a regeneration current. The presence at the electrode-solution interface of one substance, which may be either A or the product A' of its reduction or oxidation, decreases the over-potential for the reduction or oxidation of B. In either case the magnitude of the catalytic current depends on the applied potential. If the current observed with a mixture of A and B is smaller than the sum of the separate currents, the term non-additive current should be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00891 "catalytic dehydrocyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00891 "A reaction in which an alkane is converted into an aromatic hydrocarbon and hydrogen. For example,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00892 "catalytic domain of a polypeptide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00892 "Any part of a polypeptide chain that possesses a catalytic function. It may contain more than one structural domain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00894 "catalytic graphitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00894 "The transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment in the presence of certain metals or minerals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00895 "catalytic hydrocracking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00895 "A process similar to catalytic cracking in its industrial purpose but effected under hydrogen pressure and on a catalyst containing an ingredient with a hydrogenating function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00896 "catalytic hydrodesulfurization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00896 "A process in which, in the presence of hydrogen, sulfur is removed as hydrogen sulfide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00897 "catalytic hydrogenolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00897 "Ordinarily, a reaction in which $\\ce{A + H2}$ gives $\\ce{B}$.   For example,    The term may also be used for cleavage of bonds other than $\\ce{A}$, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00898 "catalytic methanation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00898 "A process for removing carbon monoxide from gas streams or for producing methane by the reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00899 "catalytic reforming")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00899 "A process for increasing the octane number of naphthas. It involves isomerization of alkanes, dehydrogenation of cyclohexanes to aromatic hydrocarbons, isomerization and dehydrogenation of alkylcyclopentanes, and dehydrocyclization of alkanes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00902 "catecholamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00902 "4-(2-Aminoethyl)pyrocatechol [4-(2-aminoethyl)benzene-1,2-diol] and derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00903 "<i>catena</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00903 "An affix used in inorganic nomenclature to denote a chain structure; often used to designate linear polymeric substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00904))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00904 "catenanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00904 "Hydrocarbons having two or more rings connected in the manner of links of a chain, without a covalent bond. More generally, the class catena compounds embraces functional derivatives and hetero analogues, e.g:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00904 "catena compounds")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00905 "cathode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00905 "Electrode at which reduction takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00906 "cathodic transfer coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00906 """For a reaction with a single rate-determining step \\[\\frac{\\alpha _{\\rm{c}}}{
u } = -\\frac{R\\ T}{n\\ F}\\ (\\frac{\\partial (\\ln (|I_{\\rm{c}}|))}{\\partial E})_{T,p,c_{i},...}\\] where \\(\\alpha _{\\rm{c}}\\) is the cathodic transfer coefficient (number), \\(R\\) is the gas constant, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, and \\(
u \\) the stoichiometric number giving the number of identical activated complexes formed and destroyed in the completion of the overall reaction as formulated with the transfer of \\(n\\) electrons.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00907 "cation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00907 "A monoatomic or polyatomic species having one or more elementary charges of the proton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00908 "cation exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00908 "The process of exchanging cations between a solution and a cation exchanger.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00909 "cation exchanger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00909 "Ion exchanger with cations as counter-ions. The term cation-exchange resin may be used in the case of solid organic polymers. The acid form of a cation exchanger is the ionic form of a cation exchanger in which counter-ions are hydrogen ions (H-form) or the ionogenic groups have added a proton forming an undissociated acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00910))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00910 "cationic polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00910 "An ionic polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are cations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00914 "cavitands")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00914 "Compounds constrained by structure to having or accommodating a cavity large enough to host other molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00915 "cavity dumping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00915 "Periodic removal of coherent radiation from a laser cavity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00917 "ceilometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00917 "An automatic, recording instrument for reading the height of the cloud-base.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00918 "cell constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00918 "Defined as: \\[\\kappa _{\\rm{cell}} = \\kappa \\ R\\] where \\(R\\) is the measured resistance of the cell and \\(\\kappa \\) is the conductivity (formerly called the specific conductance).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00922))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00922 "cell error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00922 "The error which results if the incident beam does not fall perpendicularly to the windows of the cell, or if the cell windows are contaminated or have some other imperfection.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00928 "Celsius temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00928 "Thermodynamic temperature minus \\(273.15\\ \\rm{K}\\), invariably expressed in the SI unit degree Celsius (\\(\\pu{^{\\circ}C}\\)) which is equal to the kelvin. Sometimes the misnomer centigrade temperature is used for Celsius temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00929 "centi")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00929 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-2}\\) (symbol: \\(\\rm{c}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00930 "central atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00930 "The atom in a coordination entity which binds other atoms or groups of atoms (ligands) to itself, thereby occupying a central position in the coordination entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00931))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00931 "centre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00931 "The centre of a Mössbauer spectrum is defined as the Doppler-velocity at which the resonance maximum is (or would be) observed when all magnetic dipole, electric quadrupole, etc. hyperfine interactions are (or would be) absent. The contribution of the second order Doppler shift (\\(\\delta _{\\rm{T}}\\)) should be indicated, if possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00933 "centrifugal acceleration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00933 "The acceleration of a component as a result of a uniform rotational motion. Centrifugal acceleration is a vector quantity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00933 "centripetal acceleration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00934 "centrifugal barrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00934 "In a reaction without an electronic energy barrier, or its reverse, the rotational energy of the transition state gives rise to a reaction barrier, which is known as the centrifugal barrier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00936))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00936 "centrifugal force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00936 "Fictitious force acting on a body as a result of centripetal acceleration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00937))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00937 "centrifugal radius")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00937 "The radius at which a component is spinning at the end of the period of centrifugation. For a component sedimented from a dilute suspension, it can be equated with radius of rotation at the bottom of the centrifuge tube.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00938))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00938 "cephalins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00938 "Compounds derived from glycerol in which a primary and the secondary hydroxy groups are esterified with long-chain fatty acids, and the remaining primary one with the mono(2-aminoethyl) ester of phosphoric acid, or with the monoserine ester of phosphoric acid. The term is not recommended. These compounds are preferably designated as (3-phosphatidyl)ethanolamines and (3-phosphatidyl)serines respectively.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00938 "kephalins")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00939))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00939 "cephalosporins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00939 "Cephems having the basic structure shown.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00940 "cephams")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00940 "Natural and synthetic antibiotics containing the 5-thia-1-azabicyclo[4.2.0]octan-8-one nucleus; generally assumed to have the 6R configuration, unless otherwise specified. The numbering used differs from that of the von Baeyer named bicyclic system. Where they differ, the von Baeyer numbering is shown in parentheses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00941 "cephems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00941 "2,3-Didehydrocephams: 5-thia-1-azabicyclo[4.2.0]oct-2-en-8-ones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00942 "ceramic filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00942 "A component of a stack sampling system which is suitable for high temperature use; also known as a ceramic thimble.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00943 "Cerenkov detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00943 "A charged particle detector based on the Cerenkov effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00944 "Cerenkov effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00944 "Emission of radiation in the visible and ultraviolet spectrum arising when a charged particle crosses a medium with a velocity greater than that of light in the same medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00945 "Cerenkov radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00945 "Radiation resulting from the Cerenkov effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00946))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00946 "chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00946 "The whole or part of a macromolecule, an oligomer molecule or a block, comprising a linear or branched sequence of constitutional units between two boundary constitutional units, each of which may be either an end-group, a branch point or an otherwise-designated characteristic feature of the macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00947))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00947 "chain axis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00947 "The straight line parallel to the direction of chain extension, connecting the centres of mass of successive blocks of chain units, each of which is contained within an identity period.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00948 "chain branching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00948 "When in a chain reaction there is a net increase in the number of chain carrier there is said to be chain branching. A simple example of a chain-propagating reaction leading to chain branching is:  in which there is one chain carrier (an oxygen atom) on the left and two chain carriers (a hydrogen atom and a hydroxyl radical) on the right.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00949))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00949 "chain carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00949 "A species, such as an atom or free radical, which is involved in chain-propagating reactions is known as a chain carrier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00950 "chain conformational repeating unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00950 "The smallest structural unit of a polymer chain with a given conformation that is repeated along that chain through symmetry operations.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00950 "conformational repeating unit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00952 "chain fission yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00952 "The fraction of fissions giving rise to nuclei of a particular mass number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00953))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00953 "chain folding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00953 "The conformational feature in which a loop connects two parallel stems belonging to the same crystal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00955))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00955 "chain initiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00955 "The process in a chain reaction that is responsible for the formation of a chain carrier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00956 "chain length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00956 "In a chain reaction, the average number of times the closed cycle of reactions (involving the chain-propagating reactions) is repeated. It is equal to the rate of the overall reaction divided by the rate of the initiation reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00957))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00957 "chain-orientational disorder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00957 "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical coexistence within the crystals of identical chains with opposite orientations. A typical example is provided by the up-down statistical coexistence of anticlined chains in the same crystal structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00958))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00958 "chain polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00958 "A chain reaction in which the growth of a polymer chain proceeds exclusively by reaction(s) between monomer(s) and reactive site(s) on the polymer chain with regeneration of the reactive site(s) at the end of each growth step.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00959 "chain-propagating reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00959 "A chain-propagating reaction, or more simply a propagating reaction, is an elementary step in a chain reaction in which one chain carrier is converted into another. The conversion can be a unimolecular reaction or a bimolecular reaction with a reactant molecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00959 "propagating reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00960 "chain reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00960 "A reaction in which one or more reactive reaction intermediates (frequently radicals) are continuously regenerated, usually through a repetitive cycle of elementary steps (the 'propagation step'). For example, in the chlorination of methane by a radical mechanism, $\\ce{Cl^{.}}$ is continuously regenerated in the chain propagation steps:   In chain polymerization reactions, reactive intermediates of the same types, generated in successive steps or cycles of steps, differ in relative molecular mass, as in:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00961 "chain scission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00961 "A chemical reaction resulting in the breaking of skeletal bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00963))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00963 "chain transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00963 "The abstraction, by the radical end of a growing chain-polymer, of an atom from another molecule. The growth of the polymer chain is thereby terminated but a new radical, capable of chain propagation and polymerization, is simultaneously created. For the example of alkene polymerization cited for a chain reaction, the reaction:  represents a chain transfer, the radical $\\ce{Cl3C^{.}}$ inducing further polymerization:   The phenomenon occurs also in other chain reactions such as cationic polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00964 "chair, boat, twist")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00964 "If carbon atoms 1, 2, 4 and 5 of cyclohexane occupy coplanar positions and when carbon atoms 3 and 6 are on opposite sides of the plane the conformation (of symmetry group $D_{\\rm{3d}}$) is called a chair form. The same term is applied to similar conformations of analogous saturated six-membered ring structures containing hetero-atoms and/or bearing substituent groups, but these conformations may be distorted from the exact $D_{\\rm{3d}}$ symmetry. For cyclohexane and most such analogues, the chair form is the most stable conformation. If the cyclohexane conformation has no centre of symmetry but possesses two planes of symmetry, one of them bisecting the bonds between atoms 1 and 2 and between 4 and 5 and the other plane passing through atoms 3 and 6 (which lie out of the plane and on the same side of the plane containing 1, 2, 4 and 5), that conformation (of symmetry group $C_{\\rm{2v}}$) is called a boat form and it is generally not a stable form. Again, this term is also applied to structural analogues. The conformation of $D_{2}$ symmetry passed through in the interconversion of two boat forms of cyclohexane is called the twist form (also known as skew boat, skew form and stretched form).  In a five-membered ring a conformation in which two adjacent atoms are maximally displaced, in opposite directions, relative to the plane containing the other three carbon atoms has been called a half-chair but is better called a twist conformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00966 "chalcones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00966 "1,3-Diphenylpropenone (benzylideneacetophenone), $\\ce{ArCH=CH(=O)Ar}$, and its derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00967 "chamber saturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00967 "Uniform distribution of the eluent vapour throughout the chamber prior to chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00968 "change of a quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00968 "The increment of the value of a quantity \\(Q\\) with time. The change may be expressed either infinitesimally at time \\(t\\) by the differential \\(\\rm{d}Q\\) or \\(\\rm{d}Q(t)\\), or in practice it may be expressed by a finite increment over the time interval \\((t_{1};t_{2})\\), i.e. \\(Q(t_{2})- Q(t_{1})\\), which may be written \\(\\Delta Q\\) or \\(\\Delta Q(t_{1};t_{2})\\) \\[\\Delta Q = \\Delta Q(t_{1};t_{2}) = Q(t_{2})- Q(t_{1})\\] Examples are: mass change, \\(\\Delta m\\); amount of substance change, \\(\\Delta n\\); volume change, \\(\\Delta V\\); substance concentration change, \\(\\Delta c\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00969))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00969 "change ratio of a quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00969 "A term which may be expressed infinitesimally at time \\(t\\) by a ratio of differentials \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}Q_{1}(t)}{\\rm{d}Q_{2}(t)}\\) where the kind of quantities are the same but for different components in the same system. In practice, the ratio for a finite interval is: \\[\\frac{\\Delta Q_{1}(t_{1};\\,t_{2})}{\\Delta Q_{2}(t_{1};t_{2})}\\] Examples are: mass change ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m_{1}(t)}{\\rm{d}m_{2}(t)}\\); amount of substance change ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{1}(t)}{\\rm{d}n_{2}(t)}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00970 "channel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00970 "Regions of potential-energy surfaces where there are valleys are sometimes referred to as arrangement channels. The reactant channel or entrance channel is that corresponding to configurations similar to those of the reactants; the product channel or exit channel relates to configurations similar to those of the products. In collision theory, the specification of a complete set of quantum numbers in a given arrangement channel is called a channel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00973 "chaperon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00973 "A species that facilitates a combination reaction between atoms or radicals, or the reverse process, is known as a chaperon. It is a special case of a third body.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00974 "char")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00974 "A solid decompostion product of a natural or synthetic organic material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00975))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00975 "characteristic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00975 "A property or attribute of a material that is measured, compared or noted. Attributes are ordinarily qualitative characteristics, but quantitative characteristics (variables) may be converted into attributes by assigning items to certain categories on the basis of the measured values. The value of the characteristic determined as a result of an observation or test is the observed value; when determined by a specified test method, it is called the test result. The concentration or quantity of an analyte as estimated by use of a sample is usually the characteristic of interest in analytical chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00976 "characteristic group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00976 "A single heteroatom, for example, $\\ce{–Cl}$ and $\\ce{=O}$; a heteroatom bearing one or more hydrogen atoms or other heteroatoms, for example, $\\ce{–NH2}$, $\\ce{–OH}$, $\\ce{–SO3H}$, $\\ce{–PO3H2}$ and $\\ce{-IO2}$; or a heteroatomic group attached to or containing a single carbon atom, for example, $\\ce{–CHO}$, $\\ce{–C#N}$, $\\ce{–COOH}$ and $\\ce{–NCO}$, attached to a parent hydride.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00977 "characteristic length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00977 "The term characteristic length or scale refers, in general, to the parameter which characterizes the density profile (of a given physical quantity). The static (equilibrium) or dynamic character of a characteristic length must be specified. The terms out of plane and in plane refer to characteristic lengths normal or parallel to the interface, respectively. Since interfacial layer 'thickness' and characteristic length correspond to various concepts, the current usage where an out of plane characteristic length is referred to as the interfacial layer thickness, is confusing and should be abandoned.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00977 "characteristic scale")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00978 "characteristic mass for integrated absorbance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00978 "That mass of analyte which produces an integrated absorbance signal whose net area is equal to \\(0.0044\\ \\rm{s}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00979 "characteristic mass for peak absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00979 "That mass of analyte which produces a defined peak absorbance of 0.0044 (or 1% absorbance).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00980 "characteristic mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00980 "The mass of analyte which, when atomized electrothermally, produces the uniquely defined absorption signal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00981 "characteristic potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00981 "An applied potential that is characteristic of a charge-transfer process and the experimental conditions (such as the composition of the solvent and supporting electrolyte and the temperature) under which it is investigated, and whose nature depends on the technique that is employed. Some typical characteristic potentials are the half-wave potential in polarography, the quarter-transition-time potential in chronopotentiometry, the peak potential and the half-peak potential in linear-sweep voltammetry, and the summit potential in ac polarography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00982 "characteristic ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00982 "The ratio of the mean-square end-to-end distance, \\(< r^{2} >_{0}\\), of a linear polymer chain in a theta state to \\(N\\cdot L^{2}\\), where \\(N\\) is the number of rigid sections in the main chain, each of length \\(L\\); if all of the rigid sections are not of equal length, the mean-square value of \\(L\\) is used, i.e. \\[L^{2}=\\sum _{i}\\frac{L_{i}^{\\bar{2}}}{N}\\] In simple single-strand chains, the bonds are taken as the rigid sections. The recommended symbol is: \\(C_{N}\\) (\\(C_{\\infty }\\) when \\(N\\rightarrow \\infty \\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00985 "characteristic X-ray emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00985 "X-ray emission originates from the radiative decay of electronically highly excited states of matter. Excitation by electrons is called primary excitation and by photons, secondary or fluorescence excitation. Particle induced X-ray emission (PIXE) is produced by the excitation of heavier particles such as protons, deuterons or heavy atoms in varying degrees of ionization. Emission of photons in the X-ray wavelength region also occurs from ionized gases or plasmas at high temperatures, from nuclear processes (low-energy end of the gamma-ray spectrum) and from radiative transitions between muonic states. Characteristic X-ray emission consists of a series of X-ray spectral lines with discrete frequencies, characteristic of the emitting atom. Other features are emission bands from transitions to valence levels. In a spectrum obtained with electron or photon excitation the most intense lines are called diagram lines or normal X-ray lines. They are dipole allowed transitions between normal X-ray diagram levels.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00985 "characteristic X-radiation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00986 "charcoal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00986 "A traditional term for a char obtained from wood, peat, coal or some related natural organic materials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00988 "charge density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00988 "Electric charge divided by the volume it occupies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00989))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00989 "charge-exchange ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00989 "This occurs when an ion/atom or ion/molecule reaction takes place in which the charge on the ion is transferred to the neutral species without any dissociation of either. Synonymous with charge-transfer ionization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00990 "charge-exchange reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00990 "Synonymous with charge-transfer reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00991 "charge hopping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00991 "Electron or hole transfer between equivalent sites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00992 "charge-inversion mass spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00992 "Charge inversion processes of the types:  or   or respectively, occuring in a collision cell (containing a gas, X) located in a field-free region preceding a magnetic and electric sector combination placed in either order, may be detected as follows. If the instrument slits are wide, and if the connections to the two sectors, appropriate to transmission of either positive or negative main-beam ions, are simply reversed, the negative or positive product ions of the two processes, respectively, will be transmitted. If the magnetic field is scanned, a spectrum of such product ions will be obtained, and this spectrum is called a charge-inversion mass spectrum. These spectra are sometimes referred to as −E and +E spectra, respectively. The terms '2E, E/2, −E or +E mass spectrum' should not be used without prior explanation of the meaning 2E, E, +E or −E.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00993 "charge number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00993 "Ratio of the charge of a particle to the elementary charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00994 "charge number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00994 "The magnitude of the charge on an ion, given in arabic numerals followed by the sign of the charge, in parentheses without a space, immediately after the name of an ion, e.g. iron(2+), hexacyanoferrate(4−).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00994 "Ewens–Bassett number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00995))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00995 "charge number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00995 "Number of electrons transferred according to the cell reaction equation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00996 "charge-permutation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00996 "A general term to describe an ion/neutral species reaction where there is a change in the magnitude and/or sign of the charges of the reactant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00997 "charge population")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00997 "The net electric charge on a specified atom in a molecular entity, as determined by some prescribed definition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00998))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00998 "charge recombination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00998 "Reverse of charge separation. In using this term it is important to specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00999 "charge separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00999 "A process in which, under the influence of a suitable driving force (e.g. provided by photoexcitation), electronic charge moves in a direction that increases the difference in local charges between donor and acceptor sites. electron transfer between neutral species is one of the most important examples.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01000))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01000 "charge shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01000 "A process in which under the influence of a suitable driving force (e.g. provided by photoexcitation) electronic charge moves without changing the difference in local charges between donor and acceptor sites. Electron transfer reversing the charges in a system composed of a neutral donor and a cationic acceptor or of a neutral acceptor and an anionic donor provide prominent examples.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01001))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01001 "charge-stripping reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01001 "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the charge on the reactant ion is made more positive.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01002))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01002 "charge-transfer adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01002 "Oxidative or reductive chemisorption where 'reductive' and 'oxidative' refer to electron gain or loss on species in the solid. In simple cases it is non-dissociative, i.e. there is a mere transfer of charge between adsorptive and adsorbent in forming the adsorbate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01003 "charge-transfer complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01003 "An electron-donor–electron-acceptor complex, characterized by electronic transition(s) to an excited state in which there is a partial transfer of electronic charge from the donor to the acceptor moiety.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01003 "electron-donor-acceptor complex")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01004 "charge-transfer device")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01004 "A charge-transfer device has a metal oxide semiconductor (MOS) structure that is composed of many independent pixels where charge is stored in such a way that the charge pattern corresponds to the irradiation pattern. These devices can be linear or two-dimensional. According to the method used to detect the charge pattern, two types of charge-transfer devices can be distinguished: charge-coupled devices (CCDs) and charge-injection devices (CIDs).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01005 "charge-transfer reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01005 "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the total charge on the reactant ion is transferred initially to the reactant neutral species so that the reactant ion becomes a neutral entity. Considering some of the possible reactions of ions $\\ce{M^{2+}}$, $\\ce{M^{+}}$ and $\\ce{M^{−}}$ with a neutral species $\\ce{X}$, these would be further categorized as follows:    All are ion/neutral species reactions and also charge permutation reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01006 "charge-transfer state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01006 "A state related to the ground state by a charge-transfer transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01006 "CT state")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01007 "charge-transfer step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01007 "An elementary step in which charge is transferred from one phase to the other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01008 "charge-transfer transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01008 "An electronic transition in which a large fraction of an electronic charge is transferred from one region of a molecular entity, called the electron donor, to another, called the electron acceptor (intramolecular CT) or from one molecular entity to another (intermolecular CT). Typical for donor-acceptor complexes or multichromophoric molecular entities. In some cases the charge-transfer absorption band may be obscured by the absorption of the partners.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01008 "CT transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01009 "charge-transfer transition to solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01009 "Electronic transition which is adequately described by single electron transfer between a solute and the solvent, as opposed to excitation followed by electron transfer to solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01010 "charring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01010 "Pyrolysis of samples containing organic matter. In the presence of oxygen this is referred to as ashing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01012 "chelation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01012 "The formation or presence of bonds (or other attractive interactions) between two or more separate binding sites within the same ligand and a single central atom. A molecular entity in which there is chelation (and the corresponding chemical species) is called a 'chelate'. The terms bidentate (or didentate), tridentate, tetradentate, ... multidentate are used to indicate the number of potential binding sites of the ligand, at least two of which must be used by the ligand in forming a 'chelate'. For example, the bidentate ethylenediamine forms a chelate with $\\ce{CuI}$ in which both nitrogen atoms of ethylenediamine are bonded to copper. (The use of the term is often restricted to metallic central atoms.) The phrase 'separate binding sites' is intended to exclude cases such as $\\ce{[PtCl3(CH2=CH2)]^{−}}$, ferrocene and (benzene)tricarbonylchromium in which ethene, the cyclopentadienyl group and benzene, respectively, are considered to present single binding sites to the respective metal atom, and which are not normally thought of as chelates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01014 "cheletropic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01014 "A form of cycloaddition across the terminal atoms of a fully conjugated system with formation of two new $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ to a single atom of the ('monocentric') reagent. There is formal loss of one $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ in the substrate and an increase in coordination number of the relevant atom of the reagent. An example is the addition of sulfur dioxide to butadiene:  The reverse of this type of reaction is designated 'cheletropic elimination'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01015 "chelotropic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01015 "Alternative (and etymologically more correct) name for cheletropic reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01016 "chemical actinometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01016 "A detector in which the amount of a chemical formed is proportional to the numbers of photons absorbed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01017 "chemical activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01017 "When some of the energy required for a reaction is provided by a preceding exothermic chemical reaction there is said to be chemical activation. For example, in the scheme:   some or all of the energy required for X to decompose is provided by the first reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01020))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01020 "chemical decomposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01020 "The breakdown of a single entity (normal molecule, reaction intermediate, etc.) into two or more fragments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01021 "chemical dosimeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01021 "A dosimeter in which the dose is measured by observing the extent, under specified conditions, of a chemical reaction caused by the ionizing radiation to be measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01022 "chemical element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01022 "A species of atoms; all atoms with the same number of protons in the atomic nucleus.A pure chemical substance composed of atoms with the same number of protons in the atomic nucleus. Sometimes this concept is called the elementary substance as distinct from the chemical element as defined under (1), but mostly the term chemical element is used for both concepts.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01022 "element")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01023 "chemical equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01023 "Reversible processes [processes which may be made to proceed in the forward or reverse direction by the (infinitesimal) change of one variable], ultimately reach a point where the rates in both directions are identical, so that the system gives the appearance of having a static composition at which the Gibbs energy, \\(G\\), is a minimum. At equilibrium the sum of the chemical potentials of the reactants equals that of the products, so that: \\[\\Delta G_{\\rm{r}} = \\Delta G_{\\rm{r}}^{o}+R\\ T\\ \\ln K=0\\] \\[\\Delta G_{\\rm{r}}^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} = -R\\ T\\ \\ln K\\] The equilibrium constant, \\(K\\), is given by the mass-law effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01024 "chemical flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01024 "A concept related to rate of reaction, particularly applicable to the progress in one direction only of component reaction steps in a complex system or to the progress in one direction of reactions in a system at dynamic equilibrium (in which there are no observable concentration changes with time). Chemical flux is a derivative with respect to time, and has the dimensions of amount of substance per unit volume transformed per unit time. The sum of all the chemical fluxes leading to destruction of B is designated the 'total chemical flux out of B' (symbol \\(\\sum \\varphi_{-\\ce{B}}\\)); the corresponding formation of B by concurrent elementary reactions is the 'total chemical flux into B or A' (symbol \\(\\sum \\varphi_{\\ce{B}}\\)). For the mechanism:  the total chemical flux into C is caused by the single reaction (1): \\[\\sum \\varphi_{\\ce{C}} = \\varphi_{1}\\] whereas the chemical flux out of C is a sum over all reactions that remove C: \\[\\sum\\varphi_{-\\ce{C}} = \\varphi_{-1} + \\varphi_{2}\\] where \\(\\varphi_{-1}\\) is the 'chemical flux out of C into B (and/or A)' and \\(\\varphi_{2}\\) is the 'chemical flux out of C into E'. The rate of reaction of C is then given by: \\[\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{C}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\sum\\varphi_{\\ce{C}} - \\sum\\varphi_{-\\ce{C}}\\] In this system \\(\\varphi_{1}\\) (or \\(\\sum \\varphi_{-\\ce{A}}\\)) can be regarded as the hypothetical rate of decrease in the concentration of A due to the single (unidirectional) reaction (1) proceeding in the assumed absence of all other reactions. For a non-reversible reaction: \\[\\ce{A ->[1] P}\\] \\[-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\varphi_{1}\\] If two substances A and P are in chemical equilibrium: \\[\\ce{A  P}\\] then: \\[\\sum\\varphi_{\\ce{A}} = \\sum\\varphi_{-\\ce{A}} = \\sum\\varphi_{\\ce{P}} = \\sum\\varphi_{-\\ce{P}}\\] and \\[-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{P}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = 0\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01024 "total chemiflux")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01025 "chemical induction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01025 "When in a chemical system one reaction accelerates another there is said to be chemical induction or coupling. It is due to an intermediate or product of the inducing reaction participating in the second reaction. Chemical induction is often observed in oxidation–reduction reactions, chain reactions, and biological reactions. Sometimes a reaction having \\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} > 0\\) is induced by a simultaneous process having \\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} < 0\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01025 "chemical coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01025 "coupling")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01026 "chemical ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01026 "This concerns the process whereby new ionization species are formed when gaseous molecules interact with ions. The process may involve transfer of an electron, proton or other charged species between the reactants. When a positive ion results from chemical ionization, the term may be used without qualification. When a negative ion is involved, the term negative ion chemical ionization should be used; note that negative ion formation by attachment of a free electron does not fall within this definition. Chemical ionization and chemi-ionization are two terms which should not be used interchangeably.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01027 "chemical isotope exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01027 "Exchange of isotopes between different types of molecules or ions in the course of a chemical reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01028 "chemical laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01028 "A continuous wave or pulsed laser in which the excitation and population inversion of the emitting species result from a chemical reaction. Typical examples are $\\ce{HF}$ and $\\ce{DF}$ lasers emitting many lines in the IR region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01030 "chemical measurement process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01030 "An analytical method of defined structure that has been brought into a state of statistical control, such that its imprecision and bias are fixed, given the measurement conditions. This is prerequisite for the evaluation of the performing characteristics of the method, or the development of meaningful uncertainty statements concerning analytical results.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01031))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01031 "chemical oxygen demand")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01031 "A measure of the amount of oxygen, divided by the volume of the system, required to oxidize the organic (and inorganic) matter in wastewater using a chemically oxidizing agent. In practice, it is usually expressed in milligrams $\\ce{O2}$ per litre.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01032 "chemical potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01032 """The chemical potential of a substance B in a mixture of substances B, C ... is related to the Gibbs energy \\(G\\) of the mixture by: \\[\\mu _{\\rm{B}}=(\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial n_{\\rm{B}}})_{T,p,n_{\\rm{C}
eq \\rm{B}}}\\] where \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, \\(p\\) is the pressure and \\(n_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(n_{\\rm{C}}\\), ... are the amounts of substance of B, C, ... . For a pure substance B, the chemical potential \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{*}\\) is given by: \\[\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{*}=\\frac{G^{*}}{n_{\\rm{B}}}=G_{\\rm{m}}^{*}\\] where \\(G_{\\rm{m}}^{*}\\) is the molar Gibbs energy, and where the superscript * attached to a symbol denotes the property of the pure substance. The superscript \\(^{⦵}\\) or \\(^{\\circ}\\) attached to a symbol may be used to denote a standard thermodynamic quantity.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01032 "partial molar Gibbs energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01033 "chemical reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01033 "A process that results in the interconversion of chemical species. Chemical reactions may be elementary reactions or stepwise reactions. (It should be noted that this definition includes experimentally observable interconversions of conformers.) Detectable chemical reactions normally involve sets of molecular entities as indicated by this definition, but it is often conceptually convenient to use the term also for changes involving single molecular entities (i.e. 'microscopic chemical events').")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01033 "reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01034 "chemical reaction equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01034 "Symbolic representation of a chemical reaction where the reactant entities are given on the left hand side and the product entities on the right hand side. The coefficients next to the symbols and formulae of entities are the absolute values of the stoichiometric numbers. Different symbols are used to connect the reactants and products with the following meanings: \\(=\\) for a stoichiometric relation; \\(\\rightarrow \\) for a net forward reaction; \\(\\leftrightarrows \\) for a reaction in both directions; \\(\\leftrightharpoons \\) for equilibrium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01035 "chemical relaxation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01035 "If the equilibrium mixture of a chemical reaction is disturbed by a sudden change, especially of some external parameter (such as temperature, pressure or electrical field strength), the system will readjust itself to a new position of the chemical equilibrium or return to the original position, if the perturbation is temporary. The readjustment is known as chemical relaxation. In many cases, and in particular when the displacement from equilibrium is slight, the progress of the system towards equilibrium can be expressed as a first-order law: \\[C_{t}- \\left(C_{\\rm{eq}}\\right)_{2} = [\\left(C_{\\rm{eq}}\\right)_{1}- \\left(C_{\\rm{eq}}\\right)_{2}]\\ \\rm{e}^{\\frac{-t}{\\tau }}\\] where \\(\\left(C_{\\rm{eq}}\\right)_{1}\\) and \\(\\left(C_{\\rm{eq}}\\right)_{2}\\) are the equilibrium concentrations of one of the chemical species involved in the reaction before and after the change in the external parameter, and \\(C_{t}\\) is its concentration at time \\(t\\). The time parameter \\(\\tau \\), named relaxation time, is related to the rate constants of the chemical reaction involved. Measurements of the relaxation times by relaxation methods [involving a temperature jump (T-jump), pressure jump, electric field jump or a periodic disturbance of an external parameter, as in ultrasonic techniques] are commonly used to follow the kinetics of very fast reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01036 "chemical shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01036 """The fractional variation of the resonance frequency of a nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy in consequence of its magnetic environment. The chemical shift of a nucleus, \\(\\delta \\), is expressed as a ratio involving its frequency, \\(
u _{\\mathrm{cpd}}\\), relative to that of a standard, \\(
u _{\\mathrm{ref}}\\), and defined as: \\[\\delta = \\frac{
u _{\\rm{cpd}}- 
u _{\\rm{ref}}}{
u _{ref}}\\] \\(\\delta \\)-values are normally expressed in \\(\\mathrm{ppm}\\). For $\\ce{^{1}H}$ and $\\ce{^{13}C}$ NMR the reference signal is usually that of tetramethylsilane (TMS), strictly speaking in dilute solution in $\\ce{CDCl3}$. Other references are used in the older literature and for other solvents, such as $\\ce{D2O}$. Resonance lines to high frequency from the TMS signal have positive, and resonance lines to low frequency from TMS have negative, \\(\\delta \\)-values (arising from relative deshielding and shielding respectively). The archaic terms 'downfield' and 'upfield' should no longer be used. For nuclei other than $\\ce{^{1}H}$, chemical shifts are expressed either in the same manner relative to an agreed substance containing the relevant nucleus or relative to the $\\ce{^{1}H}$ resonance of TMS as \\(\\mathit{\\Xi}\\) values, defined in the references below.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01037 "chemical shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01037 "The displacement of photoelectron or Auger peak energies originating from changes in electron binding energies as a consequence of differences in the chemical environment of the atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01039))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01039 "chemical substance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01039 "Matter of constant composition best characterized by the entities (molecules, formula units, atoms) it is composed of. Physical properties such as density, refractive index, electric conductivity, melting point etc. characterize the chemical substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01040 "chemical vapour generation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01040 "A system in which the analyte is separated from the sample matrix by the generation of gaseous species as a result of a chemical reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01041 "chemical yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01041 "The fraction of the amount of an element or chemical compound following a specified chemical reaction or separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01042 "chemiexcitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01042 "Generation, by a chemical reaction, of electronically excited molecular entities from reactants in their ground electronic states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01044))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01044 "chemi-ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01044 "This refers to a process whereby gaseous molecules are ionized when they interact with other internally excited gaseous molecules or molecular moieties. Chemi-ionization and chemical ionization are two terms which should not be used interchangeably.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01045 "chemiluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01045 "Emission of radiation resulting from a chemical reaction. The emitting species may be a reaction product or a species excited by energy transfer from an excited reaction product. The excitation may be electronic, vibrational or rotational; if the luminescence occurs in the infrared the expression infrared chemiluminescence is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01046 "chemiluminescence analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01046 "An instrument consisting of a reaction chamber with separate inlets for the sample and reagent gas, an optical filter, a photomultiplier and a signal processing device. The reactive gas is introduced in excess. The quantity of light produced is proportional to the sample flow rate and the concentration of the measured substance in the sample under specified temperature and pressure conditions. The filter limits the wavelength to the region of interest and helps to eliminate interferences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01047 "chemiluminescent methods of detection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01047 "Methods which utilize gas or liquid phase reactions between two molecules which produce a third molecule in an excited state. The wavelength distribution of the light emission from the excited molecule is characteristic of the species; in some circumstances this may be used to identify and to determine the concentration of one of the reacting species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01048 "chemisorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01048 "Adsorption which results from chemical bond formation (strong interaction) between the adsorbent and the adsorbate in a monolayer on the surface.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01048 "chemical adsorption")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01051 "chemoselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01051 "Chemoselectivity is the preferential reaction of a chemical reagent with one of two or more different functional groups. A reagent has a high chemoselectivity if reaction occurs with only a limited number of different functional groups. For example, sodium tetrahydroborate is a more chemoselective reducing agent than is lithium tetrahydroaluminate. The concept has not been defined in more quantitative terms. The term is also applied to reacting molecules or intermediates which exhibit selectivity towards chemically different reagents. Some authors use the term 'chemospecificity' for 100% chemoselectivity. However, this usage is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01053 "chemostat")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01053 "A bioreactor in which constant growth conditions for microorganisms are maintained over prolonged periods of time by supplying the reactor with a continuous input of nutrients and continuous removal of medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01055))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01055 "chimney effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01055 "A vertical movement of a localized mass of air or other gases which occurs due to local temperature differences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01057 "chiral")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01057 "Having the property of chirality. As applied to a molecule the term has been used differently by different workers. Some apply it exclusively to the whole molecule, whereas others apply it to parts of a molecule. For example, according to the latter view, a meso-compound is considered to be composed of two chiral parts of opposite chirality sense; this usage is to be discouraged. In its application to an assembly of molecules, some restrict the term to an assembly in which all of the molecules have the same chirality sense, which is better called enantiopure. Others extend it to a racemic assembly, which is better just called a racemate. Use of the term to describe molecular assemblies should be avoided.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01058 "chirality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01058 "The geometric property of a rigid object (or spatial arrangement of points or atoms) of being non-superposable on its mirror image; such an object has no symmetry elements of the second kind (a mirror plane, \\(\\sigma =S_{1}\\), a centre of inversion, \\(i=S_{2}\\), a rotation-reflection axis, \\(S_{2n}\\)). If the object is superposable on its mirror image the object is described as being achiral.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01059 "chirality axis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01059 "An axis about which a set of ligands is held so that it results in a spatial arrangement which is not superposable on its mirror image. For example with an allene $\\ce{abC=C=Ccd}$ the chiral axis is defined by the $\\ce{C=C=C}$ bonds; and with an ortho-substituted biphenyl the atoms $\\ce{C-1}$, $\\ce{C-1'}$, $\\ce{C-4}$ and $\\ce{C-4'}$ lie on the chiral axis.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01059 "axis of chirality")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01060 "chirality centre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01060 "An atom holding a set of ligands in a spatial arrangement which is not superposable on its mirror image. A chirality centre is thus a generalized extension of the concept of the asymmetric carbon atom to central atoms of any element, for example $\\ce{N^{+}abcd}$, $\\ce{Pabc}$ as well as $\\ce{Cabcd}$ .")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01060 "centre of chirality")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01060 "asymmetric centre")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01061 "chirality element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01061 "General name for a chirality axis, chirality centre or chirality plane. Also referred to as an element of chirality.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01061 "element of chirality")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01062 "chirality plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01062 "A planar unit connected to an adjacent part of the structure by a bond which results in restricted torsion so that the plane cannot lie in a symmetry plane. For example with (E)-cyclooctene the chiral plane includes the double bond carbon atoms and all four atoms attached to the double bond; with a monosubstituted paracyclophane the chiral plane includes the monosubstituted benzene ring with its three hydrogen atoms and the three other atoms linked to the ring (i.e. from the substituent and the two chains linking the two benzene rings).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01063 "chirality sense")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01063 "The property that distinguishes enantiomorphs. The specification of two enantiomorphic forms by reference to an oriented space e.g. of a screw, a right threaded one or a left threaded one. The expression 'opposite chirality' is short for 'opposite chirality sense'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01064 "chiroptic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01064 "A term referring to the optical techniques (using refraction, absorption or emission of anisotropic radiation) for investigating chiral substances [e.g. measurements of optical rotation at a fixed wavelength, optical rotatory dispersion (ORD), circular dichroism (CD), and circular polarization of luminescence (CPL)].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01065 "chirotopic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01065 "The description of an atom (or point, group, face, etc. in a molecular model) that resides within a chiral environment. One that resides within an achiral environment has been called achirotopic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01066 "chloramines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01066 "Amines substituted at nitrogen with one or two chlorine atoms (a contracted form of N-chloroamines).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01067 "chlorocarbons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01067 "Compounds consisting wholly of chlorine and carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01071 "chromatogram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01071 "A graphical or other presentation of detector response, concentration of analyte in the effluent or other quantity used as a measure of effluent concentration versus effluent volume or time. In planar chromatography 'chromatogram' may refer to the paper or layer with the separated zones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01072 "chromatograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01072 "The assembly of apparatus for carrying out chromatographic separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01073 "chromatograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01073 "To separate by chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01074 "chromatographic detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01074 "A device that measures the change of composition of the effluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01075 "chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01075 "A physical method of separation in which the components to be separated are distributed between two phases, one of which is stationary (stationary phase) while the other (the mobile phase) moves in a definite direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01076 "chromophore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01076 "The part (atom or group of atoms) of a molecular entity in which the electronic transition responsible for a given spectral band is approximately localized. The term arose in the dyestuff industry, referring originally to the groupings in the molecule that are responsible for the dye's colour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01077 "chromosome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01077 "A self-replicating structure consisting of DNA complexed with various proteins and involved in the storage and transmission of genetic information; the physical structure that contains genes (cf. plasmid). Eukaryotic cells have a characteristic number of chromosomes per cell (cf. ploidy) and contain DNA as linear duplexes. The chromosomes of bacteria consist of double-standed, circular DNA molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01079 "chemically induced dynamic nuclear polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01079 "Non-Boltzmann nuclear spin state distribution produced in thermal or photochemical reactions, usually from colligation and diffusion, or disproportionation of radical pairs, and detected by NMR spectroscopy by enhanced absorption or emission signals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01081 "cine-substitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01081 "A substitution reaction (generally aromatic) in which the entering group takes up a position adjacent to that occupied by the leaving group. For example,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01082 "CIP priority")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01082 "In the CIP rules the conventional order of ligands established for the purpose of unambiguous designation of stereoisomers. It is deduced by application of 'sequence rules', the authoritative statement of which appears in R.S. Cahn, C.K. Ingold and V. Prelog, Angew. Chem.78, 413-447 (1966), Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 5, 385-415, 511 (1966); and V. Prelog and G. Helmchen, Angew. Chem. 94, 614-631 (1982), Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 21, 567-583 (1982).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01082 "priority")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01083 "C. I. P. system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01083 "Short for Cahn–Ingold–Prelog system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01087 "<i>cis</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01087 "A prefix designating two groups occupying adjacent positions in a coordination sphere, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01088 "<i>cis</i> conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01088 "Conformations referring to torsion angles $\\uptheta\\!$ ($\\ce{A}$, $\\ce{B}$, $\\ce{C}$, $\\ce{D}$), where $\\ce{A}$, $\\ce{B}$, $\\ce{C}$, $\\ce{D}$ are main-chain atoms, can be described as: cis or synperiplanar (\\(\\rm{C}\\)); gauche or synclinal (\\(\\rm{G}\\)); anticlinal (\\(\\rm{A}\\)); and trans or antiperiplanar (\\(\\rm{T}\\)), corresponding to torsion angles within $\\pu{\\pm 30\\!^{\\circ}}$ of, respectively, $\\pu{\\pm 0\\!^{\\circ}}$, $\\pu{\\pm 120\\!^{\\circ}}$ and $\\pu{\\pm 180\\!^{\\circ}}$. The letters shown in parentheses (upper case \\(\\rm{C}\\), \\(\\rm{G}\\), \\(\\rm{A}\\), \\(\\rm{T}\\)) are the recommended abbreviations. The symbols \\(\\rm{G}^{+}\\), \\(\\rm{G}^{-}\\) (or \\(\\rm{A}^{+}\\), \\(\\rm{G}^{-}\\), for example) refer to torsion angles of similar type but opposite known sign, i.e. $\\pu{\\sim +60^{\\circ}}$, $\\pu{\\sim -60^{\\circ}}$ (or $\\pu{\\sim +120^{\\circ}}$, $\\pu{\\sim -120^{\\circ}}$). The notation \\(\\rm{G}\\), \\(\\bar{\\rm{G}}\\) ; \\(\\rm{A}\\), \\(\\bar{\\rm{A}}\\) (and \\(\\rm{T}\\), \\(\\bar{\\rm{T}}\\) ; \\(\\rm{C}\\), \\(\\bar{\\rm{C}}\\) - whenever the torsion angles are not exactly equal to $\\pu{180\\!^{\\circ}}$ and $\\pu{0\\!^{\\circ}}$, respectively) is reserved for the designation of enantiomorph conformations, i.e. conformations of opposite but unspecified sign. Where necessary, a deviation from the proper value of the torsion angle can be indicated by the sign ($\\sim$), as in the following examples: \\(\\rm{G(\\sim)}\\); \\(\\rm{G}^{+}(\\sim)\\); \\(\\rm{G}^{-}\\rm{(\\sim)}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01089 "<i>cis</i>-fused")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01089 "Steric relations at saturated bridgeheads common to two rings are denoted by cis or trans, followed by a hyphen and placed before the name of the ring system, according to the relative positions of the exocyclic atoms or groups attached to the bridgeheads. Such rings are said to be cis-fused or trans-fused.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01091 "cistactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01091 "A tactic polymer in which the main-chain double bonds of the configurational base units are entirely in the cis arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01092 "<i>cis</i>, <i>trans</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01092 "Descriptors which show the relationship between two ligands attached to separate atoms that are connected by a double bond or are contained in a ring. The two ligands are said to be located cis to each other if they lie on the same side of a plane. If they are on opposite sides, their relative position is described as trans. The appropriate reference plane of a double bond is perpendicular to that of the relevant $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ and passes through the double bond. For a ring (the ring being in a conformation, real or assumed, without re-entrant angles at the two substituted atoms) it is the mean plane of the ring(s). For alkenes the terms cis and trans may be ambiguous and have therefore largely been replaced by the E, Z convention for the nomenclature of organic compounds. If there are more than two entities attached to the ring the use of cis and trans requires the definition of a reference substituent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01093 "<i>cis</i>-<i>trans</i> isomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01093 "Stereoisomeric olefins or cycloalkanes (or hetero-analogues) which differ in the positions of atoms (or groups) relative to a reference plane: in the cis-isomer the atoms are on the same side, in the trans-isomer they are on opposite sides.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01093 "geometric isomerism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01094 "class (a) metal ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01094 "A metal ion that combines preferentially with ligands containing ligating atoms that are the lightest of their Periodic Group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01095 "class (b) metal ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01095 "A metal ion that combines preferentially with ligands containing ligating atoms other than the lightest of their Periodic Group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01096 "class of helix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01096 "The number of skeletal chain atoms contained within the helix residue.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01097 "clathrates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01097 "Inclusion compounds in which the guest molecule is in a cage formed by the host molecule or by a lattice of host molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01098 "clausius")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01098 "Non-SI unit of entropy, \\(\\rm{Cl} = 4.184\\ \\rm{J K}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01099))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01099 "clean surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01099 "An experimental surface having no surface contamination observable by means of the used method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01100 "clean up")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01100 "A process caused by sputtering, trapping (or adsorbing) metallic atoms, gas atoms or molecules on the walls of the vessel or anode. This has the effect of reducing the gas pressure in sealed lamps, and may be useful for cleaning up or gettering unwanted gases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01103 "clone")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01103 "A population of genetically identical cells produced from a common ancestor. Sometimes, 'clone' is also used for a number of recombinant DNA molecules all carrying the same inserted sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01106 "<i>closo</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01106 "An affix used in names to denote a cage or closed structure, especially a boron skeleton that is a polyhedron having all triangular faces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01107 "cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01107 "An aerosol of the atmosphere which is dense enough to be perceptible to the eye. Usually refers to an assembly of water droplet aerosols suspended in the atmosphere, although dust clouds are also recognized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01115 "cluster ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01115 "An ion formed by the combination of more ions or atoms or molecules of a chemical species often in association with a second species. For example, $\\ce{[(H2O)_{n}H]^{+}}$ is a cluster ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01116 "coacervation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01116 "The separation into two liquid phases in colloidal systems. The phase more concentrated in colloid component is the coacervate, and the other phase is the equilibrium solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01117 "coagulation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01117 "When a sol is colloidally unstable (i.e. the rate of aggregation is not negligible) the formation of aggregates is called coagulation or flocculation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01117 "flocculation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01118))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01118 "coal-derived pitch coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01118 "The primary industrial solid carbonization product obtained from coal tar pitch mainly produced in chamber or delayed coking processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01119 "coalescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01119 "The disappearance of the boundary between two particles (usually droplets or bubbles) in contact, or between one of these and a bulk phase followed by changes of shape leading to a reduction of the total surface area. The flocculation of an emulsion, viz. the formation of aggregates, may be followed by coalescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01120))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01120 "coalification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01120 "A geological process of formation of materials with increasing content of the element carbon from organic materials that occurs in a first, biological stage into peats, followed by a gradual transformation into coal by action of moderate temperature (about \\(500\\ \\rm{K}\\)) and high pressure in a geochemical stage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01121 "coal tar pitch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01121 "A residue produced by distillation or heat treatment of coal tar. It is a solid at room temperature, consists of a complex mixture of numerous predominantly aromatic hydrocarbons and heterocyclics, and exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01123 "codon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01123 "The sequence of three consecutive nucleotides that occurs in mRNA which directs the incorporation of a specific amino acid into a protein and includes also the starting or termination signals of protein synthesis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01124 "coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01124 "Proportionality constant between two quantities of different dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01125))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01125 "coefficient of haze")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01125 "One technique of measurement of the amount of filterable particulate matter suspended in air which has been used in the past depends upon drawing a measured sample of air (usually 1 000 linear feet) through a paper or membrane filter. A measurement is made of the intensity of light transmitted through the dust spot formed relative to that transmitted through an identical clean filter. The dirtiness of the air is reported in terms of the \\(\\rm{COH}\\) unit. This relates to the quantity of particulate material which produces an optical density, \\[\\log_{10}(\\frac{I_{0}}{I})\\] of 0.01 when measured by light transmission at \\(\\pu{400 nm}\\) and relative to the transmission of an identical dust-free filter taken as 100%. Thus a filter which transmitted 50% relative to the clean filter has an absorbance of 0.301 or 30.1 \\(\\rm{COH}\\) units. This is not a recommended measure of filterable particulate matter since the size, colour and other properties of the aerosol and the air in which it is suspended affect the results.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01126))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01126 "coenzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01126 "The dissociable, low-relative-molecular-mass active group of an enzyme which transfers chemical groups, hydrogen or electrons. A coenzyme binds with its associated protein (apoenzyme) to form the active enzyme (holoenzyme).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01127))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01127 "coextraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01127 "Formation of mixed-species aggregates in a low-polarity organic phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01128))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01128 "cofactors")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01128 "Organic molecules (cf. coenzymes) or ions (usually metal ions) that are required by an enzyme of its activity. They may be attached either loosely or tightly prosthetic group) to the enzyme. A cofactor binds with its associated protein (apoenzymes), which is functionally inactive, to form the active enzyme (holoenzyme).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01129))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01129 "coherence length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01129 "The distance over which order is maintained. As an example, there can be long-range atomic or molecular order, i.e. coherence. Coherence lengths are significantly larger than molecular size. Normally, coherence length is estimated from electron, neutron or X-ray scattering and scales the size of ordered domains in material where long range ordering occurs (as in liquid crystals, for example). The term coherence length is also used for the scale characterizing the profile of molecular axis orientation in the distorted, so-called, transition layers formed at a solid/liquid-crystal boundary when an appropriate external field is applied (e.g. when the orientation of anchored surface molecules of a nematic liquid crystal is unaffected by an external field -- magnetic or electric -- whereas the bulk molecules reorient freely). This scale is referred to as electric coherence length, \\(\\zeta _{\\rm{E}}\\) or magnetic coherence length, \\(\\zeta _{\\rm{M}}\\), depending on the nature of the applied field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01130))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01130 "coherent radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01130 "Radiation emitted by a source when all the elementary waves emitted have a phase difference constant in space and time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01131))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01131 "coherent scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01131 "Scattering is coherent whenever the phases of the signals arising from different scattering centres are correlated and incoherent whenever these phases are uncorrelated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01132 "coherent source")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01132 "Those sources where the radiation has a constant phase relationship between waves spatially as well as temporally, e.g. lasers. Non-coherent optical sources emit radiation which is randomly distributed in phase, spatially as well as temporally. Most sources which are used in spectroscopy and for spectrochemical analysis conform to this latter group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01133))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01133 "coherent structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01133 "If the net of an ordered adsorbed phase is in registry with the lattice of the adsorbent the structure is called coherent, if not it is called incoherent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01134 "coherent system of units of measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01134 "System of units of measurement in which all of the derived units are coherent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01135 "coherent unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01135 "Derived unit of measurement that may be expressed as a product of powers of base units with the proportionality factor one.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01135 "coherent derived unit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01136 "coincidence circuit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01136 "An electronic circuit which produces a usable output pulse only when each of its inputs receives a pulse within a specified time interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01139 "coincidence resolving time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01139 "The greatest time interval that can elapse between the occurrence of two or more consecutive signal pulses, in order that the measuring device processes them as a coincidence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01141 "co-ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01141 "Ions of low relative molecular mass with a charge of the same sign as that of a given colloidal ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01142 "coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01142 "A solid high in content of the element carbon and structurally in the non-graphitic state. It is produced by pyrolysis of organic material which has passed, at least in part, through a liquid or liquid-crystalline state during the carbonization process. Coke can contain mineral matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01143 "coke breeze")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01143 "A by-product of coke manufacture; it is the residue from the screening of heat-treated coke. The particle size is less than \\(10\\ \\rm{mm}\\). Generally, coke breeze has a volatile matter content of \\(")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01144 "coking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01144 "Many reactions involving hydrocarbons and particularly those run at higher temperatures lead to the deposition on the catalyst of high molecular weight compounds of carbon and hydrogen which deactivate the catalyst. This phenomenon is called coking or fouling. Catalysts so deactivated can often be regenerated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01145 "col")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01145 "A mountain-pass in a potential-energy surface is known as a col or saddle point. It is a point at which the gradient is zero along all coordinates, and the curvature is positive along all but one coordinate, which is the reaction coordinate, along which the curvature is negative.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01145 "saddle point")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01147 "cold neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01147 "Neutrons with a neutron temperature considerably lower than normal room temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01148 "collection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01148 "The planned removal from solution of a micro- or macro-component by the intentional formation of a contaminated host precipitate, or by the adsorption or entrapment of the component by an added solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01149))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01149 "collection efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01149 "A term which characterizes an entire sampling and sample pretreatment procedure, usually represented as a percentage of the original amount of the analyte which is left for measurement (signal formation) after having passed through this procedure. This term also applies to the efficiency of collection of an air pollutant by an arrestment plant. For particulates, the collection efficiency is size related.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01150 "collection gas flow system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01150 "A system in which the analyte is carried to a collecting device called the collector which may be an amalgamator for mercury, a cold trap for some of the more stable hydrides, an absorbing solution in which the analyte is absorbed by some chemical reaction, an electrothermal atomizer or a reservoir.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01151 "collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01151 "A device for removing and retaining contaminants from air or other gases; a term which is often applied to cleaning devices in exhaust systems. Also used to designate a device for removing and retaining samples from media in different environmental compartments to be investigated. A collector is sometimes used to describe a scavenger.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01152 "collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01152 "A solid substance added to or formed within a solution to collect a micro- or macro-component.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01152 "scavenger")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01153 "colligation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01153 "The formation of a covalent bond by the combination or recombination of two radicals (the reverse of unimolecular homolysis).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01153 "radical combination")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01154 "collimation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01154 "The limiting of a beam of radiation to the required dimensions and angular spread.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01155))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01155 "collimator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01155 "An arrangement of absorbers used for collimation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01156 "collinear reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01156 "This is a reaction assumed to occur via an activated complex in which all of the atoms directly involved in the process lie along a straight line.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01157))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01157 "collisional activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01157 "An ion/neutral species process wherein excitation of a projectile ion of high translational energy is brought about by the same mechanism as in collision-induced dissociation. (The ion may decompose subsequently.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01157 "collisionally activated dissociation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01158 "collisional broadening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01158 "Collisions of the emitting or absorbing particle with other particles cause collisional broadening as well as collisional shift of the spectral line. When collisions occur between unlike, neutral particles the term foreign-gas broadening is used, or van der Waals' broadening when both collision partners are neutral. When the colliding particles are of the same species, the term resonance broadening is employed. The term Lorentz broadening was previously used for neutral particle collision broadening, and Holtsmark broadening for cases of van der Waals' broadening when collisions took place with like particles. Both terms are now discouraged. Stark broadening refers to collisions with charged particles or particles with a strong permanent electrical dipole moment. Whereas a strong chaotic electrical field causes Stark broadening, an applied static electrical field induces a Stark shift.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01158 "Lorentz broadening")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01158 "Holtsmark broadening")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01159 "collisional excitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01159 "An ion/neutral species process wherein there is an increase in the reactant ion's internal energy at the expense of the translational energy of either (or both) of the reacting species. The scattering angle may be large.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01160 "collision complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01160 "An ensemble formed by two reaction partners for which the distance is the sum of their van der Waals radii. As such it constitutes a subclass of the species indicated as encounter complex.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01161 "collision cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01161 "In simple hard sphere collision theory the area of the circle with radius equal to the collision diameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01162 "collision density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01162 "Total collision frequency for all molecules within a volume divided by that volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01163 "collision diameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01163 "In simple hard sphere collision theory the sum of radii of two colliding spherical particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01164 "collision efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01164 "The collision efficiency, or de-energization efficiency, is defined by: \\[k_{\\rm{-1}} = B_{\\rm{c}}\\ k_{\\rm{-1}}^{\\rm{sc}}\\] where \\(k_{-1}\\) is the rate constant for a particular substance \\(\\rm{M}\\) when it brings about the de-energization process  and \\(k_{-1}^{\\rm{sc}}\\) is the corresponding rate constant for a reference molecule \\(\\rm{M}_{\\rm{r}}\\) that de-energizes \\(\\rm{A}^{*}\\) on every collision; that is, the reference molecule \\(\\rm{M}_{\\rm{r}}\\) undergoes strong collisions, and by definition has a collision efficiency \\(B_{\\rm{c}}\\) of unity. The species \\(\\rm{A}^{*}\\) is usually in a vibrationally-excited state, and \\(\\rm{A}\\) has energy less than that required for reaction to occur.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01164 "de-energization efficiency")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01166 "collision frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01166 "Average number of collisions experienced by a molecule A with other molecules A (or B) in a time interval divided by that time interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01167 "collision-induced dissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01167 "An ion/neutral species process wherein the projectile ion is dissociated as a result of interaction with a target neutral species. This is brought about by conversion of part of the translational energy of the ion to internal energy in the ion during the collision.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01169 "collision number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01169 "Now called collision density.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01170 "collision theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01170 "Various collision theories, dealing with the frequency of collision between reactant molecules, have been put forward. In the earliest theories reactant molecules were regarded as hard spheres, and a collision was considered to occur when the distance \\(d\\) between the centres of two molecules was equal to the sum of their radii. For a gas containing only one type of molecule, A, the collision density is given by simple collision theory as: \\[Z_{\\mathrm{AA}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\ \\pi \\ \\sigma ^{2}\\ u\\ N_{\\rm{A}}^{2}}{2}\\] Here \\(N_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the number density of molecules and \\(u\\) is the mean molecular speed, given by kinetic theory to be \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\ k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{\\pi \\ m}}\\), where \\(m\\) is the molecular mass, and \\(\\sigma =\\pi \\ d_{\\mathrm{AA}}^{2}\\). Thus: \\[Z_{\\mathrm{AA}}=2\\ N_{\\rm{A}}^{2}\\ \\sigma ^{2}\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi \\ k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{m}}\\] The corresponding expression for the collision density \\(Z_{\\mathrm{AB}}\\) for two unlike molecules A and B, of masses \\(m_{\\rm{A}}\\) and \\(m_{\\rm{B}}\\) is: \\[Z_{\\mathrm{AB}}=N_{\\rm{A}}\\ N_{\\rm{B}}\\ \\sigma ^{2}\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi \\ k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{\\mu }}\\] where \\(\\mu \\) is the reduced mass \\(\\frac{m_{\\rm{A}}\\ m_{\\rm{B}}}{m_{\\rm{A}}+m_{\\rm{B}}}\\), and \\(\\sigma =\\pi \\ d_{\\mathrm{AB}}^{2}\\). For the collision frequency factor these formulations lead to the following expression: \\[z_{\\mathrm{AA}}\\quad \\rm{or}\\quad z_{\\mathrm{AB}}=L\\ \\sigma ^{2}\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{8\\ \\pi \\ k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{\\mu }}\\] where \\(L\\) is the Avogadro constant. More advanced collision theories, not involving the assumption that molecules behave as hard spheres, are known as generalized kinetic theories.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01170 "kinetic theory of collisions")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01171 "colloid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01171 "A short synonym for colloidal system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01172))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01172 "colloidal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01172 "The term refers to a state of subdivision, implying that the molecules or polymolecular particles dispersed in a medium have at least in one direction a dimension roughly between \\(1\\ \\rm{nm}\\) and \\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\), or that in a system discontinuities are found at distances of that order.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01173 "colloidal carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01173 "A particulate carbon with particle sizes below ca.\\(1000\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in at least one dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01174 "colloidal dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01174 "A system in which particles of colloidal size of any nature (e.g. solid, liquid or gas) are dispersed in a continuous phase of a different composition (or state). The name dispersed phase for the particles should be used only if they have essentially the properties of a bulk phase of the same composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01175 "colloidal electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01175 "An electrolyte which gives ions of which at least one is of colloidal size. This term therefore includes hydrophobic sols, ionic association colloids, and polyelectrolytes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01176 "colloidally stable")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01176 "Particles which do not aggregate at a significant rate: the precise connotation depends on the type of aggregation under consideration. For example, a concentrated paint is called stable by some people because oil and pigment do not separate out at a measurable rate, and unstable by others because the pigment particles aggregate into a continuous network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01177 "colloidal suspension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01177 "A suspension in which the size of the particles lies in the colloidal range.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01178 "colloid osmotic pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01178 "The pressure difference which has to be established between a colloidal system and its equilibrium liquid to prevent material transfer between the two phases when they are separated by a membrane, permeable to all components of the system, except the colloidal ones.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01178 "Donnan pressure")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01179 "colorimeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01179 "An instrument used for colour measurement based on optical comparison with standard colours.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01180 "colour indicator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01180 "An indicator which is classified as one- or two-colour, depending on whether it is colourless on one side of the transition interval or possesses a different colour on each side of this range.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01180 "color indicator")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01180 "indicator")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01181 "column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01181 "The tube and the stationary phase contained within, through which the mobile phase passes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01182 "column chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01182 "A separation technique in which the stationary bed is within a tube. The particles of the solid stationary phase or support coated with a liquid stationary phase may fill the whole inside volume of the tube (packed column) or be concentrated on or along the inside tube wall leaving an open, unrestricted path for the mobile phase in the middle part of the tube (open-tubular column).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01183 "column volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01183 "The geometric volume of the part of the tube that contains the packing: \\[V_{\\rm{c}} = A_{\\rm{c}}\\ L\\] where \\(A_{\\rm{c}}\\) is the internal cross-sectional area of the tube and \\(L\\) the length of the packed part of the column. In the case of wall-coated open-tubular columns the column volume corresponds to the geometric volume of the whole tube having a liquid or a solid stationary phase on its wall.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01184 "combination electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01184 "An ion-selective electrode and an external reference electrode combined in a single unit, thereby avoiding the need for a separate external reference electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01185 "combined sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01185 "A sample obtained by removing specific fractions by separation or selection techniques (e.g. heavy liquid, magnetic, sieving, etc.), analysing the fractions separately, and combining the results mathematically. When not combined, the sample is a modified sample. This term should not be confused with composite sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01186 "comb macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01186 "A macromolecule comprising a main chain with multiple trifunctional branch points from each of which a linear side-chain emanates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01187))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01187 "comb polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01187 "A polymer composed of comb macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01188 "combustion gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01188 "A gas or vapour produced in furnaces, combustion chambers or in open burning.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01189 "commensurate–incommensurate transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01189 "A transition between two states having magnetic or crystallographic structures with a basic lattice and an imposed super-periodicity. In the commensurate (lock-in) state this super-periodicity is a simple rational multiple of the basic unit cell. In the incommensurate state the ratio of the super-periodicity repeat distance to the basic lattice repeat distance is irrational and may show continuous variation with variation in some degree of freedom (e.g. temperature, pressure, composition) of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01191 "common-ion effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01191 "A reduction in the rate of reaction of certain reactions of a substrate RX in solution [by a path that involves a pre-equilibrium with formation of $\\ce{R^{+}}$ (or $\\ce{R^{−}}$) ions as reaction intermediates] caused by the addition to the reaction mixture of an electrolyte solute containing the 'common ion' $\\ce{X^{−}}$ (or $\\ce{X+}$). For example, the rate of solvolysis of diphenylmethyl chloride in acetone-water is reduced by the addition of salts of the common ion $\\ce{Cl^{−}}$ which causes a decrease in the quasi-equilibrium concentration of the diphenylmethyl cation in the scheme:   This phenomenon is a direct consequence of the mass-law effect on ionization equilibria in electrolytic solution. More generally, the common-ion effect is the influence of the 'common ion' on the reactivity due to the shift of the dissociation equilibrium. It may also lead to an enhancement of the rate of reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01192 "communities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01192 "Naturally occuring groups of different (micro)organisms inhabiting a common environment, interacting with each other, especially through food relationships and relatively independent of other groups. Communities may be of varying sizes, and larger communities may contain smaller ones.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01192 "consortia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01192 "syntrophic associations")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01192 "synergistic associations")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01194 "comparator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01194 "A known amount of element that is simultaneously irradiated with the test portion in the context of activation analysis. If one comparator is used (single comparator method), it is essentially identical to a flux monitor (except that this term is not necessarily linked to activation analysis).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01195 "compensation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01195 "For the process: \\[k = A\\ \\rm{e}^{\\frac{-E}{R\\ T}}\\] where \\(k\\) is a rate constant, \\(A\\) the frequency factor and \\(E\\) the activation energy, \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) sometimes exhibit compensation, i.e. they change in the same direction with change in catalyst for a given reaction or with change in reaction for a given catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01196 "compensation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01196 "For internal compensation, see compound.See: external compensation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01197 "compensation effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01197 "In a considerable number of cases plots of \\(T\\ \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S\\) vs. \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\), for a series of reactions, e.g. for a reaction in a range of different solvents, are straight lines of approximately unit slope. Therefore, the terms \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\) and \\(T\\ \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S\\) in the expression partially compensate, and \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G = \\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H - T\\ \\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S\\] often is a much simpler function of solvent (or other) variation than \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\) or \\(T\\ \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S\\) separately.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01199 "competitive binding assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01199 "An assay based on the competition between a labelled and an unlabelled ligand in the reaction with a receptor binding agent (e.g. antibody, receptor, transport protein).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01200 "competitive inhibition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01200 "The reduction in rate due to reduction or loss of activity of a catalyst caused by adsorption of poison which is not greatly preferred to adsorption of reactant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01202 "complementary DNA")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01202 "A single-stranded DNA molecule with a nucleotide sequence that is complementary to an RNA molecule; cDNA is formed by the action of the enzyme reverse transcriptase on an RNA template. After conversion to the double-stranded form, cDNA is used for molecular cloning or for hybridization studies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01203))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01203 "complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01203 "A molecular entity formed by loose association involving two or more component molecular entities (ionic or uncharged), or the corresponding chemical species. The bonding between the components is normally weaker than in a covalent bond. The term has also been used with a variety of shades of meaning in different contexts: it is therefore best avoided when a more explicit alternative is applicable. In inorganic chemistry the term 'coordination entity' is recommended instead of 'complex'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01204))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01204 "complex coacervation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01204 "Coacervation caused by the interaction of two oppositely charged colloids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01205 "complex mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01205 "This term is sometimes applied to an elementary reaction which proceeds via an intermediate species having a lifetime longer than a few rotational periods, in contrast to a direct reaction. However, in view of the danger of confusion with a reaction occurring in more than one step (a composite reaction) this usage is not to be recommended; it is better to use the terms complex-mode mechanism or complex-mode reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01206))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01206 "complex-mode reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01206 "An elementary reaction which proceeds via an intermediate species having a lifetime longer than a few rotational periods is known as a complex-mode reaction, or as an indirect reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01209))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01209 "component")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01209 "Constituent of a mixture the amount or concentration of which can be varied independently. The number of components in a given system is the minimum number of independent species necessary to define the composition in all the phases of a system. It may vary with external conditions since additional chemical equilibria reduce the number of components. The term component is also often used in the more general sense as defined here under constituent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01210 "composite mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01210 "A reaction that involves more than one elementary reaction is said to occur by a composite mechanism. The terms complex mechanism, indirect mechanism, and step-wise mechanism are also commonly used. There are two main kinds of evidence for a composite mechanism: The kinetic equation for the reaction does not correspond to its stoichiometry.There is experimental evidence, direct or indirect, for intermediates of such a nature that it is necessary to conclude that more than one elementary reaction is involved. There are many types of composite mechanisms, for example: Reactions occurring in parallel, such as:  are called parallel reactions or simultaneous reactions. When there are simultaneous reactions there is sometimes  competition, as in the scheme:  where B and C compete with one another for A.Reactions occurring in forward and reverse directions are called opposing reactions: Reactions occurring in sequence, such as  are known as consecutive reactions.Reactions are said to exhibit feedback if a substance formed in one step affects the rate of a previous step. For example, in the scheme: The intermediate Y may catalyse the reaction  (positive feedback) or it may inhibit it (negative feedback).Chain reactions")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01211 "composite reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01211 "A chemical reaction for which the expression for the rate of disappearance of a reactant (or rate of appearance of a product) involves rate constants of more than a single elementary reaction. Examples are 'opposing reactions' (where rate constants of two opposed chemical reactions are involved), 'parallel reactions' (for which the rate of disappearance of any reactant is governed by the rate constants relating to several simultaneous reactions to form different respective products from a single set of reactants), and stepwise reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01212))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01212 "composite sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01212 "Often prepared as a representative mixture of several different (usually bulk) samples, and from which the laboratory sample is taken.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01213))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01213 "compositional heterogeneity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01213 "The variation in elemental composition from molecule to molecule usually found in copolymers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01214 "composition of pure air")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01214 "The composition of air is variable with respect to several of its components (e.g. $\\ce{CH4}$, $\\ce{CO2}$, $\\ce{H2O}$) so 'pure' air has no precise meaning; it is commonly considered to be air which is free of dust, aerosols and reactive gaseous contaminants of anthropogenic origin. The composition of the major components in dry air is relatively constant (percent by volume given): nitrogen, 78.084; oxygen, 20.946; argon, 0.934; carbon dioxide, 0.033; neon, 0.0018; helium, 0.000524; methane, 0.00016; krypton, 0.000114; hydrogen 0.00005; nitrous oxide, 0.00003; xenon, 0.0000087. The concentrations of carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide, the chlorofluorocarbons and some other species of anthropogenic origin are increasing measurably with time. Relative clean air which is free of most reactive anthropogenic pollution ($\\ce{NO}$, $\\ce{NO2}$, $\\ce{SO2}$, non-methane hydrocarbons, etc.), often used as a reference sample in the calibration and operation of instruments, is purchased under the designation of zero air.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01216 "compression factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01216 "Product of pressure and molar volume divided by the gas constant and thermodynamic temperature. For an ideal gas it is equal to 1.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01216 "compressibility factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01217 "comproportionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01217 "The reverse of disproportionation. The term 'symproportionation' is also used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01218 "Compton effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01218 "The elastic scattering of a photon by an electron which afterwards occurs as a free electron. Part of the energy and momentum of the incident photon is transferred to the electron and the remaining part is carried away by the scattered photon. Synonymous with Compton scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01218 "Compton scattering")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01219 "Compton electron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01219 "The energetic electron resulting from the Compton effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01222))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01222 "concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01222 "Group of four quantities characterizing the composition of a mixture with respect to the volume of the mixture (mass, amount, volume and number concentration).Short form for amount-of-substance concentration ('substance concentration' in clinical chemistry).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01224))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01224 "concentration-cell corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01224 "The local variation of the corrosion rate due to the action of a corrosion cell resulting from inhomogeneous composition of the environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01225 "concentration distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01225 "The ratio of the analytical concentration of a component in the stationary phase to its analytical concentration in the mobile phase. \\[D_{\\rm{c}}=\\frac{\\rm{amount of component}/\\rm{cm}^{3}\\rm{of stationary phase}}{\\rm{amount of component}/\\rm{cm}^{3}\\rm{of mobile phase}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01227 "concentration gradient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01227 "Differential change in concentration of a component in a given direction in a small distance divided by the distance in that direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01229 "concentration in experimental surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01229 "The amount of the material of interest divided by the total amount of substances in the volume of interest. Concentration may be defined in terms of numbers of atoms (particles) (\\(\\rm{ppma}\\)) or in terms of weight (\\(\\frac{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{g}}{\\rm{g}}\\)).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01229 "surface concentration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01230 "concentration overpotential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01230 "The concentration overpotential of an electrode reaction at a given electrode current density (\\(\\rm{c.d.}\\)) is basically the difference in equilibrium potentials across the diffusion layer. More precisely, it is the potential of a reference electrode (of the same electrode reaction as the working electrode) with the interfacial concentrations which establish themselves at \\(\\rm{c.d.}\\), relative to the potential of a similar reference electrode with the concentrations of the bulk solution. From such a measured potential difference, with \\(\\rm{c.d.}\\) flowing, one needs to subtract the ohmic potential drop prevailing between the two electrodes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01232 "concentration-sensitive detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01232 "A device the response of which is proportional to the concentration of a sample component in the eluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01234))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01234 "concerted process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01234 "Two or more primitive changes are said to be concerted (or to constitute a concerted process) if they occur within the same elementary reaction. Such changes will normally (though perhaps not inevitably) be 'energetically coupled'. (In the present context the term 'energetically coupled' means that the simultaneous progress of the primitive changes involves a transition state of lower energy than that for their successive occurrence.) In a concerted process the primitive changes may be synchronous or asynchronous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01235))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01235 "condensation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01235 "The physical process of converting a material from a gaseous or vapour phase to a liquid or solid phase; this commonly results when the temperature is lowered and/or the vapour pressure of the material is increased. The tendency exists for condensation to occur when the partial pressure of a given component of a gaseous mixture at a given temperature exceeds the vapour pressure of the liquid or solid form of that component at the given temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01236 "condensation nuclei")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01236 "Particles, either liquid or solid, or ions upon which condensation of water vapour (or other substances) begins in the atmosphere. Condensation nuclei are usually very small hygroscopic aerosols (\\(\\pu{0.001 \\upmu m}\\) to \\(\\pu{0.1 \\upmu m}\\) in diameter), but these are not as abundant as the smaller particles. The number of CN which are active (initiate condensation) in a given air mass may be a function of the relative humidity. Usually CN are counted as the active nuclei at about $\\ce{300\\%}$ relative humidity, while cloud condensation nuclei (CCN) are counted as the number of active nuclei at relative humidity less than or equal to $\\ce{102\\%}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01237))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01237 "condensation polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01237 "Polymerization by a repeated condensation process (i.e. with elimination of simple molecules).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01237 "polycondensation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01238 "condensation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01238 "A (usually stepwise) reaction in which two or more reactants (or remote reactive sites within the same molecular entity) yield a single main product with accompanying formation of water or of some other small molecule, e.g. ammonia, ethanol, acetic acid, hydrogen sulfide. The mechanism of many condensation reactions has been shown to comprise consecutive addition and elimination reactions, as in the base-catalysed formation of (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde) from acetaldehyde, via 3-hydroxybutanal (aldol). The overall reaction in this example is known as the aldol condensation. The term is sometimes also applied to cases where the formation of water or another simple molecule does not occur, as in 'benzoin condensation'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01240 "conditional potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01240 """Conditional (formal) potential, \\(E^{0'}\\), is related to the potential of a cell reaction, \\(E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\), by an equation analogous to that relating \\(E_{\\mathrm{cell}}\\) to the standard potential \\(E_{0}\\), with the activity, \\(a_{i}\\), replaced by any composition variable, \\(c_{i}\\) (to be indicated by a subscript). For example: \\[E_{\\mathrm{cell}}=E_{\\rm{c}}^{0'}- \\frac{R\\ T}{n\\ F}\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}
u _{i}\\ \\ln c_{i}\\]""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01240 "formal potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01242 "conditioning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01242 "Synonymous with pre-equilibration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01244 "conduction band")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01244 "A vacant or only partially occupied set of many closely spaced electronic levels resulting from an array of a large number of atoms forming a system in which the electrons can move freely or nearly so. This term is usually used to describe the properties of metals and semiconductors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01245 "conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01245 "Reciprocal of resistivity. This quantity is a tensor in an anisotropic medium. It was formerly called 'specific conductance'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01245 "electric conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01245 "electrical conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01245 "specific conductance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01246 "confidence level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01246 "The probability of covering the expected value of an estimated parameter with an interval estimated for the parameter (symbol \\(1 - \\alpha\\)). The confidence level can be expressed as a number between 0 and 1, or in percent. The complementary quantity is known as the significance level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01247 "confidence limits")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01247 "Symmetric confidence limits (\\(\\pm C\\)) about the estimated mean, which cover the population mean with probability \\(1 - \\alpha \\). The quantity \\(C\\) is calculated by the formula: \\[C = \\frac{t_{\\rm{p},v^{S}}}{\\sqrt{n}}\\] Here \\(t_{\\rm{p},v}\\), is the critical value from the \\(t\\)- (or Student) distribution function corresponding to the confidence level \\(1 - \\alpha \\) and degrees of freedom \\(v\\). The symbol \\(p\\) represents the percentile (or percentage point) of the \\(t\\)-distribution. For 1-sided intervals, \\(p=1- \\alpha \\); for 2-sided intervals, \\(p=1- \\frac{\\alpha }{2}\\). In each case, the confidence level is \\(1 - \\alpha \\). The confidence interval is given as \\(\\overline{x}\\ \\pm \\ C\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01248 "configuration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01248 "A distribution of the electrons of an atom or a molecular entity over a set of one-electron wavefunctions called orbitals, according to the Pauli principle. From one configuration several states with different multiplicities may result. For example, the ground electronic configuration of the oxygen molecule ($\\ce{O2}$) is: \\(1\\sigma _{\\rm{g}}^{2}\\), \\(1\\sigma _{\\rm{u}}^{2}\\), \\(2\\sigma _{\\rm{g}}^{2}\\), \\(2\\sigma _{\\rm{u}}^{2}\\), \\(1\\pi _{\\rm{u}}^{4}\\), \\(3\\sigma _{\\rm{g}}^{2}\\), \\(1\\pi _{\\rm{g}}^{2}\\) resulting in the \\(^{3}\\Sigma_{\\rm{g}}\\), \\(^{1}\\Delta_{\\rm{g}}\\) and \\(^{1}\\Sigma_{\\rm{g}}^{+}\\) multiplets.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01248 "electron configuration")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01248 "electronic configuration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01249 "configuration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01249 "In the context of stereochemistry, the term is restricted to the arrangements of atoms of a molecular entity in space that distinguishes stereoisomers, the isomerism between which is not due to conformation differences.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01249 "stereochemical configuration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01250 "configurational base unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01250 "A constitutional repeating unit in a regular macromolecule, a regular oligomer molecule, regular block or a regular chain, the configuration of which is defined at least at one site of stereoisomerism in the main chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01251))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01251 "configurational disorder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01251 "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical co-crystallization of different configurational repeating units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01252 "configurational homosequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01252 "A constitutional homosequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at one or more sites of stereoisomerism in each constitutional unit in the main chain of a polymer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01253 "configurational repeating unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01253 "The smallest set of successive configurational base units that prescribes configurational repetition at one or more sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a regular macromolecule, a regular oligomer molecule, a regular block or a regular chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01254))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01254 "configurational sequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01254 "The whole or part of a chain comprising one or more species of configurational unit(s) in defined sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01255))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01255 "configurational unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01255 "A constitutional unit having at least one site of defined stereoisomerism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01256 "configuration interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01256 "The mixing of many-electron wavefunctions constructed from different electronic configurations to obtain an improved many-electron state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01258 "conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01258 "The spatial arrangement of the atoms affording distinction between stereoisomers which can be interconverted by rotations about formally single bonds. Some authorities extend the term to include inversion at trigonal pyramidal centres and other polytopal rearrangements.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01259))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01259 "conformational analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01259 "The assessment of the relative energies (or thermodynamic stabilities), reactivities, and physical properties of alternative conformations of a molecular entity, usually by the application of qualitative or semi-quantitative rules or by semi-empirical calculations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01260 "conformational disorder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01260 "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical coexistence within the crystals of identical configurational units with different conformations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01262 "conformer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01262 "One of a set of stereoisomers, each of which is characterized by a conformation corresponding to a distinct potential energy minimum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01264))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01264 "congruent transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01264 "A transition in which the two-phase equilibrium of melting, vaporization or allotropism of a compound involves phases of the same composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01265))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01265 "coning and quartering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01265 "The reduction in size of a granular or powdered sample by forming a conical heap which is spread out into a circular, flat cake. The cake is divided radially into quarters and two opposite quarters are combined. The other two quarters are discarded. The process is repeated as many times as necessary to obtain the quantity desired for some final use (e.g. as the laboratory sample or as the test sample). If the process is performed only once, coning and quartering is no more efficient than taking alternate portions and discarding the others.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01266))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01266 "conjugate acid–base pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01266 "The Brønsted acid $\\ce{BH^{+}}$ formed on protonation of a base $\\ce{B}$ is called the conjugate acid of $\\ce{B}$, and $\\ce{B}$ is the conjugate base of $\\ce{BH^{+}}$. (The conjugate acid always carries one unit of positive charge more than the base, but the absolute charges of the species are immaterial to the definition.) For example: the Brønsted acid $\\ce{HCl}$ and its conjugate base $\\ce{Cl^{−}}$ constitute a conjugate acid–base pair.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01267 "conjugated system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01267 "In the original meaning a conjugated system is a molecular entity whose structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds: e.g. $\\ce{CH2=CH–CH=CH2}$, $\\ce{CH2=CH–C#N}$. In such systems, conjugation is the interaction of one p-orbital with another across an intervening $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ in such structures. (In appropriate molecular entities d-orbitals may be involved.) The term is also extended to the analogous interaction involving a p-orbital containing an unshared electron pair, e.g. $\\ce{{:}Cl–CH=CH2}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01267 "conjugation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01268))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01268 "conjugate solutions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01268 "Two solutions that co-exist in equilibrium at a given temperature and pressure and, at constant pressure (temperature), change their compositions and relative proportions with a variation of temperature (pressure). The term usually refers to two immiscible liquids, but it is also applicable to two immiscible solid solutions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01270 "conjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01270 "The contact-dependent transfer of a part or all of its genome from one bacterial cell (donor) to another (recipient).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01271 "conjugation labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01271 "Labelling of a substance by conjugation with a labelled molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01273 "conjunctive name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01273 "A name for assemblies of functionalized acyclic parent hydrides and cyclic systems implying the loss of an appropriate number of hydrogen atoms from each.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01274 "connectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01274 "In a chemical context, the information content of a line formula, but omitting any indication of bond multiplicity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01276))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01276 "consecutive reactions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01276 "Reactions occurring in sequence, such as:  The overall process is said to occur by consecutive steps.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01276 "consecutive steps")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01278 "consignment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01278 "A quantity of material transferred on one occasion and covered by a single set of shipping documents. It may consist of one or more lots or portions of lots. The presence of different lots in a consignment is important from the point of view of the sampling plan and the interpretation of the results of analysis. The term 'population' is used as the general term for the quantity of parent material being sampled when it is immaterial if the parent body is a consignment, lot, batch, entity, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01279 "consistency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01279 "A general term to describe the property of a material by which it resists permanent change of shape.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01281))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01281 "constituent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01281 "Chemical species present in a system; often called a component, although the term component has a more restricted meaning in physical chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01282 "constitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01282 "The description of the identity and connectivity (and corresponding bond multiplicities) of the atoms in a molecular entity (omitting any distinction arising from their spatial arrangement).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01283 "constitutional heterogeneity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01283 "The variation in constitution from molecule to molecule in polymers with molecules uniform with respect to elemental composition. An example is a polymer composed of linear and branched molecules; another example is a statistical copolymer comprising two isomeric constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01284 "constitutional homosequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01284 "A constitutional sequence which contains constitutional units of only one species and in one sequential arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01285 "constitutional isomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01285 "Isomerism between structures differing in constitution and described by different line formulae e.g. $\\ce{CH3OCH3}$ and $\\ce{CH3CH2OH}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01286))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01286 "constitutional repeating unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01286 "The smallest constitutional unit the repetition of which constitutes a regular macromolecule, a regular oligomer molecule, a regular block or a regular chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01287))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01287 "constitutional sequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01287 "The whole or part of a chain comprising one or more species of constitutional unit(s) in defined sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01288))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01288 "constitutional unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01288 "An atom or group of atoms (with pendant atoms or groups, if any) comprising a part of the essential structure of a macromolecule, an oligomer molecule, a block or a chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01289))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01289 "constitutive enzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01289 "Enzymes which are produced constitutively by the cell under all physiological conditions. Therefore, they are not controlled by induction or repression.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01290))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01290 "contact angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01290 """When a liquid does not spread on a substrate (usually a solid), a contact angle \\(\\left(\\theta\\right) \\) is formed which is defined as the angle between two of the interfaces at the three-phase line of contact. It must always be stated which interfaces are used to define \\(\\theta\\). It is often necessary to distinguish between the 'advancing contact angle' \\((\\theta _{\\rm{a}}) \\), the 'receding contact angle' \\((\\theta _{\\rm{r}}) \\) and the 'equilibrium contact angle' \\((\\theta _{\\rm{e}}) \\). When \\((\\theta _{\\rm{r}}) 
eq (\\theta _{\\rm{a}}) \\) the system is said to exhibit contact angle hysteresis""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01291 "contact corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01291 "The enhancement of the corrosion rate of the material with the more negative corrosion cell resulting from the contact between different electron-conducting phases.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01291 "galvanic corrosion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01293 "contact potential difference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01293 "The electric potential difference between one point in the vacuum close to the surface of M1 and another point in the vacuum close to the surface of M2, where M1 and M2 are two uncharged metals brought into contact.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01293 "Volta potential difference")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01295 "contamination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01295 "Carry-over from a preceding sample probe into a following specimen cup, which will influence not just one result, but all assays on that specimen, or with that reagent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01297 "continuity inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01297 "A change in the mutual dispersion of two phases in contact.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01298 "continuous analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01298 "An analyser in which subassemblies operate continuously.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01300))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01300 "continuous-flow cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01300 "A cell which allows liquid (or gaseous) samples to pass through the cell continuously while absorption measurements are made.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01301 "continuous flow enthalpimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01301 "A term used to describe methods wherein a reagent is continuously fed into a flowing analyte stream and the temperature difference is measured before and after a reactor (mixing chamber). Alternatively, the temperature may be measured in a differential manner between a reference and a reactor chamber. The analyte concentration is directly proportional to the measured temperature difference. Synonymous with continuous flow enthalpimetric analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01302 "continuous indication analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01302 "An analysis with a permanent indication related to the sample concentration. To obtain a continuous indication, the sampling and measuring cell need to be continuous. A time lag may exist between sampling and indication of measured concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01304 "continuous measuring cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01304 "A measuring cell which operates continuously.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01305))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01305 "continuous operation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01305 "The operation of an analytical instrument in such a way that no analytical procedure needs to be completed on any sample before the next procedure can be started.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01306 "continuous precipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01306 "A diffusional reaction in a multi-component system in which atoms are transported to the growing nuclei by diffusion over relatively large distances in the parent phase and during which the mean composition of the parent phase changes continuously towards its equilibrium value. Synonymous with nucleation and growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01308))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01308 "contour length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01308 "The maximum end-to-end distance of a linear polymer chain. For a single-strand polymer molecule, this usually means the end-to-end distance of the chain extended to the all-transconformation. For chains with complex structure, only an approximate value of the contour length may be accessible. The sum of the lengths of all skeletal bonds of a single-strand polymer molecule is occasionally termed 'contour length'. This use of this term is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01309 "contributing structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01309 "The definition is based on the valence-bond formulation of the quantum mechanical idea of the wavefunction of a molecule as composed of a linear combination of wavefunctions, each representative of a formula containing bonds that are only single, double or triple with a particular pairing of electron spins. Each such formula represents a contributing structure, also called 'resonance structure' to the total wavefunction, and the degree to which each contributes is indicated by the square of its coefficient in the linear combination. The contributing structures, also called 'canonical forms', themselves thus have a purely formal significance: they are the components from which wavefunctions can be built. Structures may be covalent (or non-polar) or ionic (or polar). The representation is frequently kept qualitative so that we speak of important or major contributing structures and minor contributing structures.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01309 "canonical form")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01310 "controlled atmosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01310 "A synthetic gaseous sample of pure air which may contain carefully determined amounts of certain contaminants; this may be used as a standard for the calibration of analytical techniques, as a simulated environment for the study of biological responses, or for other purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01311))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01311 "control material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01311 "A material to be used for the assessment of the performance of an analytical procedure or part thereof.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01312 "control sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01312 "The test portion or test solution used for assessment of the performance of an analytical procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01313 "convection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01313 "Vertical motion of the air induced by the expansion of the air heated by the earth's surface and its resulting buoyancy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01314 "convenience sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01314 "A sample chosen on the basis of accessibility, expediency, cost, efficiency or other reason not directly concerned with sampling parameters.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01316 "conventional true value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01316 "Value attributed to a particular quantity and accepted, sometimes by convention, as having an uncertainty appropriate for a given purpose. Examples are the CODATA recommended values of fundamental physical constants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01317))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01317 "conversion electron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01317 "Electron ejected from the atom in the process of internal conversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01319 "conversion spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01319 """A plot of a quantity related to the absorption (absorbance, cross section, etc.) multiplied by the quantum yield for the considered process against a suitable measure of photon energy, such as frequency, \\(
u \\), wavenumber, \\(\\sigma \\), or wavelength, \\(\\lambda \\), e.g. the conversion cross section, \\(\\sigma \\varphi \\), has the SI unit \\(\\mathrm{m}^{2}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01321 "co-oligomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01321 "An oligomer derived from more than one species of monomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01322 "co-oligomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01322 "Oligomerization in which a co-oligomer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01324))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01324 "cooperative transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01324 "A transition that involves a simultaneous, collective displacement or change of state of the atoms and/or electrons in the entire system. Examples: An order–disorder transition of atoms or electrons, as in an alloy, a ferromagnet or superconductor; a Jahn–Teller or ferroic transition; a martensitic transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01325))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01325 "cooperativity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01325 "Interaction between the substrate binding sites of an allosteric enzymes. Binding of a substrate molecule to one binding site changes the affinity of the binding sites on the other subunits (cf. allosteric enzymes) to the substrate by induction of a conformation change at the other binding sites. Cooperative enzymes typically display a sigmoid (S-shaped) plot of the reaction rate against substrate concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01329 "coordination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01329 "The formation of a covalent bond, the two shared electrons of which have come from only one of the two parts of the molecular entity linked by it, as in the reaction of a Lewis acid and a Lewis base to form a Lewis adduct; alternatively, the bonding formed in this way. In the former sense, it is the reverse of unimolecular heterolysis. 'Coordinate covalence' and 'coordinate link' are synonymous (obsolescent) terms. The synonym 'dative bond' is obsolete. (The origin of the bonding electrons has by itself no bearing on the character of the bond formed. Thus, the formation of methyl chloride from a methyl cation and a chloride ion involves coordination; the resultant bond obviously differs in no way from the $\\ce{C–Cl}$ bond in methyl chloride formed by any other path, e.g. by colligation of a methyl radical and a chlorine atom.) The term is also used to describe the number of ligands around a central atom without necessarily implying two-electron bonds.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01329 "coordinate covalence")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01329 "coordinate link")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01330))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01330 "coordination entity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01330 "An assembly consisting of a central atom (usually metallic) to which is attached a surrounding array of other groups of atoms (ligands).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01331 "coordination number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01331 "The coordination number of a specified atom in a chemical species is the number of other atoms directly linked to that specified atom. For example, the coordination number of carbon in methane is four, and it is five in protonated methane, $\\ce{CH5^{+}$. (The term is used in a different sense in the crystallographic description of ionic crystals.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01332))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01332 "coordination polyhedron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01332 "In a coordination entity, the solid figure defined by the positions of the ligand atoms directly attached to the central atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01332 "polygon")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01333 "coordinatively saturated complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01333 "A transition metal complex that has formally 18 outer shell electrons at the central metal atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01334 "coordinatively unsaturated complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01334 "A transition metal complex that possesses fewer ligands than exist in the coordinatively saturated complex. These complexes usually have fewer than 18 outer shell electrons at the central metal atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01335 "copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01335 "A polymer derived from more than one species of monomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01336 "copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01336 "Polymerization in which a copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01337 "copolymer micelle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01337 "A micelle formed by one or more block or graft copolymer molecules in a selective solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01338 "copper vapour laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01338 "A pulsed source of coherent radiation emitting at \\(578.2\\ \\rm{nm}\\) and \\(510.5\\ \\rm{nm}\\) from excited copper atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01339 "coprecipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01339 "The simultaneous precipitation of a normally soluble component with a macro-component from the same solution by the formation of mixed crystals, by adsorption, occlusion or mechanical entrapment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01340 "copy number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01340 "The number of copies of a plasmid or a gene within a cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01341))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01341 "core atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01341 "The primary reference atom in a mechanistic change; usually either of the two atoms of a transformed multiple bond or the single atom at which substitution occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01343 "corrected emission spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01343 "Obtained after correcting for instrumental and sample effects and usually represented by a graph of \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} _{\\lambda}\\) against wavelength, where \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} _{\\lambda}\\) is the spectral (radiant) energy flux.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01344 "corrected excitation spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01344 "Obtained if the photon flux incident on the sample is held constant. If the solution is sufficiently dilute that the fraction of the exciting radiation absorbed is proportional to the absorption coefficient of the analyte, and if the quantum yield is independent of the exciting wavelength, the corrected excitation spectrum will be identical in shape to the absorption spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01345 "corrected retention volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01345 "The corrected retention volume, \\(V_{\\rm{R}}^{0}\\), is given by \\[V_{\\rm{R}}^{0}=j\\ V_{\\rm{R}}\\] where \\(j\\) is the pressure gradient correction factor for a homogeneously filled column of constant diameter and \\(V_{\\rm{R}}\\) is the retention volume. This quantity is of limited use because it is influenced by the volumes of the sample injector and detector, as well as the interstitial volume of the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01346 "correlation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01346 "The use of empirical correlations relating one body of experimental data to another, with the objective of finding quantitative estimates of the factors underlying the phenomena involved. Correlation analysis in organic chemistry often uses linear free-energy relations for rates or equilibria of reactions, but the term also embraces similar analysis of physical (most commonly spectroscopic) properties and of biological activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01347 "correlation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01347 """A measure of the degree of interrelationship which exists between two measured quantities, \\(x\\) and \\(y\\); the correlation coefficient (\\(r\\)) is defined by the following relation: \\[r=\\frac{\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i=1
\\end{array}}^{n}(x_{i}- \\overline{x})\\ (y_{i}- \\overline{y})}{\\sqrt{\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i=1
\\end{array}}^{n}(x_{i}- \\overline{x})^{2}\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i=1
\\end{array}}^{n}(y_{i}- \\overline{y})^{2}}}\\] where \\(x_{i}\\) and \\(y_{i}\\) are the measured values in the \\(i\\)th experiment of \\(n\\) total experiments, \\(\\overline{x}\\) and \\(\\overline{y}\\) are the arithmetic means of \\(x_{i}\\) and \\(y_{i}\\): \\[\\overline{x}=\\frac{\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i=1
\\end{array}}^{n}x_{i}}{n}\\] (similar expression for \\(\\overline{y}\\)). The linear correlation coefficient indicates the degree to which two quantities are linearly related. If \\(x=a\\ y\\) is followed then \\(r=1\\), and departures from this relationship decrease \\(r\\); if interpretations of data based on the linear correlation coefficient are to be made, one should consult a book on statistics.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01348 "correlation diagram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01348 "A diagram which shows the relative energies of orbitals, configurations, valence bond structures, or states of reactants and products of a reaction, as a function of the molecular geometry, or another suitable parameter. An example involves the interpolation between the energies obtained for the united atoms and the values for the separated atoms limits.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01349 "correlation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01349 "The difference between the Hartree–Fock energy calculated for a system and the exact non-relativistic energy of that system. The correlation energy arises from the approximate representation of the electron-electron repulsions in the Hartree–Fock method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01350 "corrinoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01350 "Derivatives of the corrin nucleus, which contains four reduced or partly reduced pyrrole rings joined in a macrocycle by three  groups and one direct carbon–carbon bond linking $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ positions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01351 "corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01351 "An irreversible interfacial reaction of a material (metal, ceramic, polymer) with its environment which results in consumption of the material or in dissolution into the material of a component of the environment. Often, but not necessarily, corrosion results in effects detrimental to the usage of the material considered. Exclusively physical or mechanical processes such as melting or evaporation, abrasion or mechanical fracture are not included in the term corrosion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01352 "corrosion cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01352 "A galvanic cell resulting from inhomogeneities in the material or in its environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01357 "corrosion potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01357 "The electrode potential spontaneously acquired by a corroding material in a particular environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01358 "corrosion rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01358 """The amount of substance transferred per unit time at a specified surface. Using Faraday's law, the corrosion rate, \\(
u_{\\mathrm{cor}}\\), can be formally expressed as an electric current which at the corrosion potential is called the corrosion current, \\(I_{\\mathrm{cor}}\\), e.g. for the anodic dissolution of one component of a material with \\(
u_{\\mathrm{cor}}\\) in \\(\\pu{mol s-1}\\) and \\(I_{\\mathrm{cor}}\\) in \\(\\mathrm{A}\\) one obtains \\(I_{\\mathrm{cor}}=n\\ F\\ 
u_{\\mathrm{cor}}\\), \\(n\\) being the charge number of the electrode reaction and \\(F\\) the Faraday constant.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01361 "co-solvency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01361 "The dissolution of a polymer in a solvent comprising more than one component, each component of which by itself is a non-solvent for the polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01364))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01364 "cotectic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01364 "The conditions of pressure, temperature and composition under which two or more solid phases crystallize at the same time from a single liquid over a finite range of decreasing temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01365 "coulomb")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01365 "SI derived unit of electric charge, \\(\\rm{C} = \\rm{A}\\cdot \\rm{s}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01366 "coulomb integral")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01366 "Integral over space of the type \\(\\int \\psi _{r}\\ast \\ \\hat{H}\\ \\psi _{r}\\ \\rm{d}\\tau \\) where \\(\\hat{H}\\) is the hamiltonian operator containing electrostatic potential energy terms and \\(\\psi \\) is a one-electron wavefunction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01367 "coulometric detection method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01367 "A detection method in which the current is directly proportional to the flow rate of the substance involved in the electrochemical reaction, and the amount of charge which flows is proportional to the amount of substances taking part in the reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01369 "coumarins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01369 "2H-Chromen-2-one (older name 1,2-benzopyrone), trivially named coumarin, and its derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01370 "count")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01370 "1. Information corresponding to a pulse processed for counting.2. Number of pulses recorded during a measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01371 "counter-ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01371 "The mobile exchangeable ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01373))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01373 "counter tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01373 "Radiation detector consisting of a gas-filled tube or valve whose gas amplification is much greater than one, and in which the individual ionizing events give rise to discrete electrical pulses. Often an expression is added indicating the geometry (e.g. end window), composition of the gas (e.g. helium) or the physical process for its operation (e.g. proton recoil, fission).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01377 "counting efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01377 "The ratio between the number of particles or photons counted with a radiation counter and the number of particles or photons of the same type and energy emitted by the radiation source.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01377 "efficiency")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01378 "counting loss")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01378 "A reduction of the counting rate resulting from phenomena such as the resolving time or the dead time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01379 "counting rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01379 "The number of counts occuring in unit time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01380))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01380 "coupled reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01380 "A reaction which follows the (slower) reaction of kinetic interest, so as to provide means of monitoring the formation of a reaction product. This reaction is sometimes referred to as the indicator reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01380 "indicator reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01381 "coupled simultaneous techniques")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01381 "The application of two or more techniques to the same sample when the two instruments involved are connected through an interface, e.g. simultaneous thermal analysis and mass spectrometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01383 "coupling constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01383 "A quantitative measure for nuclear spin-spin, nuclear-electron (hyperfine coupling) and electron-electron (fine coupling in EPR) coupling in magnetic resonance spectroscopy. The 'indirect' or scalar NMR coupling constants are in a first approximation independent of the external magnetic field and are expressed in \\(\\mathrm{Hz}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01383 "spin-spin coupling constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01384 "covalent bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01384 "A region of relatively high electron density between nuclei which arises at least partly from sharing of electrons and gives rise to an attractive force and characteristic internuclear distance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01386 "Cox–Yates equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01386 "A modification of the Bunnett–Olsen equation of the form: \\[\\log_{10}(\\frac{[\\ce{SH^{+}}]}{[\\ce{S}]}) - \\log_{10}[\\ce{H^{+}}] = m^{*}X + \\pu{p}K_{\\ce{SH}^{+}}\\] is the activity function \\[\\log_{10}(\\frac{\\gamma_{\\rm{S}}\\ \\gamma_{\\rm{H}^{+}}}{\\gamma_{\\rm{SH}^{+}}})\\] for an arbitrary reference base. The function \\(X\\) is called the excess acidity because it gives a measure of the difference between the acidity of a solution and that of an ideal solution of the same concentration. In practice \\[X = -(\\rm{H}_{0} + \\log_{10}[\\rm{H}^{+}])\\] and \\[m^{*} = 1 - \\mathit{\\Phi}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01387 "cracking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01387 "The thermal or catalytic decomposition of a compound such as a hydrocarbon into chemical species of smaller molecular weight.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01387 "hydrocarbon cracking")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01387 "catalytic cracking")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01388 "cream")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01388 "A highly concentrated emulsion formed by creaming of a dilute emulsion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01389 "creaming")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01389 "The macroscopic separation of a dilute emulsion into a highly concentrated emulsion, in which interglobular contact is important, and a continuous phase under the action of gravity or a centrifugal field. This separation usually occurs upward, but the term may still be applied if the relative densities of the dispersed and continuous phases are such that the concentrated emulsion settles downward.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01390 "cream volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01390 "The volume of cream formed in an emulsion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01391 "cresols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01391 "The monomethylphenols and their derivatives formed by substitution on the ring with substituents other than $\\ce{–OH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01393))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01393 "critical energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01393 "A chemical reaction cannot occur, except by quantum-mechanical tunnelling unless the total energy available is greater than a certain energy, which is known as the critical energy, or the threshold energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01393 "threshold energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01394 "critical excitation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01394 "Minimum energy to excite a specific analytical signal. The term is most frequently used in connection with the generation of X-ray spectra, designating the ionization (binding) energy of the orbital on which the transition ends.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01395 "critical micelle concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01395 "There is a relatively small range of concentrations separating the limit below which virtually no micelle are detected and the limit above which virtually all additional surfactant molecules form micelles. Many properties of surfactant solutions, if plotted against the concentration, appear to change at a different rate above and below this range. By extrapolating the loci of such a property above and below this range until they intersect, a value may be obtained known as the critical micellization concentration (critical micelle concentration), symbol \\(c_{\\rm{M}}\\), abbreviation cmc (or c.m.c.). As values obtained using different properties are not quite identical, the method by which the cmc is determined should be clearly stated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01396 "critical point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01396 "The temperature and pressure at which the liquid and vapour intensive properties (density, heat capacity, etc.) become equal. It is the highest temperature (critical temperature) and pressure (critical pressure) at which both a gaseous and a liquid phase of a given compound can coexist.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01397))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01397 "critical pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01397 "The minimum pressure which would suffice to liquefy a substance at its critical temperature. Above the critical pressure, increasing the temperature will not cause a fluid to vaporize to give a two-phase system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01400 "critical solution point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01400 "The point, with coordinates critical solution temperatures and critical composition, on a temperature-composition phase diagram at which the distinction between co-existent phases vanishes. In solid-solid, solid-liquid and liquid-liquid systems both upper and lower critical solution temperatures and corresponding 'critical solution compositions' can occur. Synonymous with consolute point.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01400 "consolute point")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01402 "critical temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01402 "That temperature, characteristic of each gas, above which it is not possible to liquefy a given gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01403 "critical thickness of a film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01403 "A film often thins gradually to a thickness at which it either ruptures or converts abruptly to an equilibrium film. This thickness is sometimes well enough defined statistically to be considered a critical thickness, \\(t_{\\rm{c}}\\) or \\(h_{\\rm{c}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01404 "cross-conjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01404 "In a system $\\ce{XC6H4GY}$ this is conjugation involving the substituent $\\ce{X}$, the benzene ring and the side-chain connective-plus-reaction site $\\ce{GY}$, i.e. either $\\ce{X}$ is a $+R$ group and $\\ce{GY}$ is a $-R$ group, or $\\ce{X}$ is a $-R$ group and $\\ce{GY}$ is a $+R$ group. In Hammett correlations this situation can lead to the need to apply exalted substituent constants \\(\\sigma ^{+}\\) or \\(\\sigma ^{-}\\), respectively, as in electrophilic or nucleophilic aromatic substitution, respectively. The term 'through resonance' is synonymous. Cross conjugation has also been used to describe the interactions occurring in 2-phenylallyl and similar systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01405 "crossed electric and magnetic fields")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01405 "Electric and magnetic fields with the electric field direction at right angles to the magnetic field direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01407))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01407 "cross-flow filtration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01407 "Method of operating a filtration device where retained fluid is circulated over the membrane (filter) surface thus preventing undue build-up of filtered material on membrane (filter).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01408))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01408 "crossing over")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01408 "The usually reciprocal exchange of genetic material between chromosomes; part of natural genetic recombination.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01409 "crosslink")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01409 "A small region in a macromolecule from which at least four chains emanate, and formed by reactions involving sites or groups on existing macromolecules or by interactions between existing macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01410 "cross-over concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01410 "The concentration range at which the sum of the volumes of the domains occupied by the solute molecules or particles in solution is approximately equal to the total volume of that solution. The term 'domain' refers to the smallest convex body that contains the molecule or particle in its average shape.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01411 "cross reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01411 "The ability of substances other than the analyte to bind to the binding reagent and the ability of substances other than the binding reagent to bind the analyte in competitive binding assays.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01420 "crowding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01420 "The displacement of an impurity from an extract phase by contact with a solution containing the main extractable solute. The main solute need not be present in a pure solution but should have a higher distribution ratio than the impurities present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01421))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01421 "crown")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01421 "A molecular entity comprising a monocyclic ligand assembly that contains three or more binding sites held together by covalent bonds and capable of binding a guest in a central (or nearly central) position. The adducts formed are sometimes known as 'coronates'. The best known members of this group are macrocyclic polyethers, such as '18-crown-6', containing several repeating units $\\ce{–CR2–CR2O–}$ (where $\\ce{R}$ is most commonly $\\ce{H}$), and known as crown ethers.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01421 "coronands")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01421 "coronates")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01422))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01422 "crown conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01422 "A conformation of a saturated cyclic molecular entity, containing an even number (≥8) of atoms in the ring, in which these atoms lie alternately in each of two parallel planes and are symmetrically equivalent (\\(D_{4\\rm{d}}\\) for cyclooctane, \\(D_{5\\rm{d}}\\) for cyclodecane etc). It is analogous to the chair conformation of cyclohexane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01423 "crud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01423 "A deposit or emulsion at the interface between two partially settled phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01424 "cryogenic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01424 "Low temperature processes, apparatus, etc.; usually applied to systems operated at the temperature of liquid nitrogen, helium or other condensed gas which boils at a very low temperature (at atmospheric pressure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01425 "cryogenic sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01425 "The collection of trace compounds from gaseous media by co-condensation with a major constituent (e.g. water vapour, $\\ce{CO2}$, $\\ce{N2}$, $\\ce{Ar}$) of the matrix.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01426))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01426 "cryptand")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01426 "A molecular entity comprising a cyclic or polycyclic assembly of binding sites that contains three or more binding sites held together by covalent bonds, and which defines a molecular cavity in such a way as to bind (and thus 'hide' in the cavity) another molecular entity, the guest (a cation, an anion or a neutral species), more strongly than do the separate parts of the assembly (at the same total concentration of binding sites). The adduct thus formed is called a 'cryptate'. The term is usually restricted to bicyclic or oligocyclic molecular entities. Example:  Corresponding monocyclic ligand assemblies crowns are sometimes included in this group, if they can be considered to define a cavity in which a guest can hide. The terms 'podand' and 'spherand' are used for certain specific ligand assemblies. Coplanar cyclic polydentate ligands, such as porphyrins, are not normally regarded as cryptands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01428 "crystal diffraction spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01428 "A spectrometer, in which diffraction by a crystal is used to obtain the energy spectra of electromagnetic radiation as well as of slow neutrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01429))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01429 "crystal field splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01429 "The removal of a degeneracy of the energy levels of molecular entities or ions due to the lower site symmetry created by a crystalline environment. This term is sometimes incorrectly used synonymously with the term ligand field splitting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01430 "crystal laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01430 "A solid state laser in which the active medium is composed of ionic species supported in a crystalline host. The first laser used a ruby crystal in which chromium ions as the active medium were supported in an aluminium oxide (sapphire) crystal. One of the most common crystal lasers involves neodymium atoms in a yttrium aluminium garnet host crystal (the YAG:Nd laser).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01431))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01431 "crystalline electrodes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01431 "Electrodes which contain mobile ions of one sign and fixed sites of opposite sign. They may be homogeneous or heterogeneous.  Homogeneous membrane electrodes are ion-selective electrodes in which the membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or a homogeneous mixture of compounds (i.e. $\\ce{Ag2S}$, $\\ce{AgI}$/$\\ce{Ag2S}$).Heterogeneous membrane electrodes are formed when an active substance, or mixture of active substances, is mixed with an inert matrix, such as silicone rubber or PVC, or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy, to form the heterogeneous sensing membrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01432 "crystalline polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01432 "A polymer showing crystallinity. One or two-dimensional order leads to mesophase structure. The range of order may be as small as about \\(2\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in one (or more) crystallographic direction(s) and is usually below \\(50\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in at least one direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01433 "crystallinity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01433 "The presence of three-dimensional order on the level of atomic dimensions. Crystallinity may be detected by diffraction techniques, heat-of-fusion measurements, etc. The amount of disorder within the crystalline region is not incompatible with this concept.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01434))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01434 "crystallization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01434 "The formation of a crystalline solid from a solution, melt vapour or a different solid phase, generally by the lowering of the temperature or by evaporation of a solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01435))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01435 "CT")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01435 "Abbreviation for charge-transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01437 "cumulative double bonds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01437 "Those present in a chain in which at least three contiguous carbon atoms are joined by double bonds; non-cumulative double bonds comprise every other arrangement of two or more double bonds in a single structure. The generic name 'cumulene' is given to compounds containing three or more cumulative double bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01438 "cumulative fission yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01438 "The fraction of fissions which have resulted in the production of a nuclide either directly or indirectly, up to a specified time. If no time is specified, the yield is considered to be the asymptotic value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01439 "cumulative sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01439 "A sample collected over an extended period of time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01440 "cumulenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01440 "Hydrocarbons (and by extension, derivatives formed by substitution) having three or more cumulative double bonds, e.g. $\\ce{R2C=C=C=CR2}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01441 "cumulonimbus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01441 "Heavy masses of dense cloud whose cumuliform summits rise in the forms of towers, the upper parts having a fibrous texture and often spreading out into the shape of an anvil; these clouds generally produce showers of rain and sometimes of snow, hail or soft hail, and often develop into thunderstorms; up to \\(12\\ 000\\ \\rm{m}\\); summits may be as cold as \\(\\pu{-50 ^{\\circ}C}\\); strong convective motions with vertical velocities of \\(3\\) to greater than \\(30\\ \\rm{m s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01442 "cumulus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01442 "Detached, dense clouds with a dome-shaped upper surface with sharp-edged, rounded protuberances and a nearly horizontal base; \\(600{-}6000\\ \\rm{m}\\) or more; convective motion with rising, large bubbles of warm air with vertical speeds of \\(1{-}5\\ \\rm{m s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01443 "cupola")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01443 "A vertical shaft furnace used for melting metals; the melting of ores is accomplished in a blast furnace.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01444 "curie")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01444 "Non-SI unit of radioactivity, \\(\\rm{Ci}\\approx 3.7\\times 10^{10}\\ \\rm{Bq}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01452))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01452 "current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01452 "The current density \\(j_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) of a species B in a given point of the solution is obtained by multiplying the flux density of that species at the given point by the Faraday constant \\(F\\) and by the charge number \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) of the species: \\[j_{\\mathbf{B}} = z_{\\rm{B}}\\ F\\ N_{\\mathbf{B}}\\] where \\(j_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) is a vector which indicates the direction in which the charges transported by the species B flow and which gives the number of these charges going through a plane oriented perpendicular to the vector, divided by time and by area, and \\(N_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) is the flux density of a minor constituent of the solution with respect to a fixed frame of reference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01456 "current distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01456 "The ratio of current density at a point X on an interface to the average current density (\\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j}\\)) is called the 'relative local current density'. The current distribution is described by the function \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x)\\) (or more generally, \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x,\\,y,\\,z)\\) where \\(x\\) or \\((x,\\,y,\\,z)\\) are the coordinates of the points of the electrode-solution interface. The 'primary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of overpotential is negligible. The 'secondary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential cannot be neglected but concentration overpotential is negligible. The secondary distribution is often described in terms of dimensionless numbers of the form \\[\\mathrm{Wa}=\\frac{\\kappa }{l}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\eta }{\\mathrm{d}j}\\] where \\(\\kappa \\) is the conductivity of the solution, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}\\eta }{\\rm{d}j}\\) the slope of the overpotential-current curve under the above conditions and \\(l\\) a characteristic length of the system, for instance the radius of a disc electrode. \\(\\mathrm{Wa}\\) is the 'Wagner number'. It is a quantity which determines the throwing power and characterizes the equalizing influence of overpotential on the current distribution. In electroplating the 'throwing power' is qualitatively defined as 'the ability of a solution to deposit metal uniformly upon a cathode of irregular shape'. The 'tertiary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential (including concentration overpotential) cannot be neglected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01458 "current efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01458 """If several reactions take place simultaneously at the electrode a partial electrode current density (c.d.) \\(j_{k}\\) can be assigned to each reaction. It is given by the stoichiometry of the reaction and by the amount of substance of B reacting (per unit time and per unit electrode area) in the reaction considered. The current efficiency of reaction \\(k\\), \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) is defined as the ratio of \\(j_{k}\\) to the total c.d.: \\[\\varepsilon_{k}=\\frac{j_{k}}{\\sum_{m} j_{m}}\\] Note that \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) may be larger than one if cathodic and anodic reactions take place simultaneously at the same electrode. However, \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) still gives correctly the product yield, which is the quantity of industrial interest. The product yield is the amount of substance of B produced per unit charge and is equal to \\(\\frac{\\varepsilon_{k}\\ 
u _{B,k}}{n_{k}\\ F}\\) (in the absence of a chemical reaction which is consecutive to the electrode reaction and which consumes or produces species B). \\(n_{k}\\) is the charge number of electrode reaction \\(k\\). Note that in the case of simultaneous electrode reactions the distribution of the partial c.d. \\(j_{k}\\) may be different from that of the total c.d., i.e. the function \\(\\frac{\\left(j_{k}\\right)_{\\rm{x}}}{j}=f_{k}\\left(x\\right)\\) may be different from \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x)\\). In electroplating the term 'metal distribution' is sometimes used to designate the distribution \\(f_{k}\\left(x\\right)\\) of the partial c.d. for metal deposition.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01480 "Curtin–Hammett principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01480 "In a chemical reaction that yields one product (X) from one conformational isomer (A') and a different product (Y) from another conformational isomer (A'') (and provided these two isomers are rapidly interconvertible relative to the rate of product formation, whereas the products do not undergo interconversion) the product composition is not in direct proportion to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers in the substrate; it is controlled only by the difference in standard free energies (\\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B4} \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }G\\)) of the respective transition states.  It is also true that the product composition is formally related to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers A' and A'' (i.e. the conformational equilibrium constant) and the respective rate constants of their reactions; these parameters are generally — though not invariably — unknown. The diagram below represents the energetic situation for transformation of interconverting isomers A and A' into products X and Y.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01481 "cut off")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01481 "The size of particles at which the retention efficiency of an instrument device drops below a specified value under defined conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01482))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01482 "cut-off filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01482 "An optical device which only permits the transmission of radiation of wavelengths that are longer than or shorter than a specified wavelength. Usually, the term refers to devices which transmit radiation of wavelengths longer than the specified wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01484 "CW")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01484 "Abbreviation for continuous wave, i.e. non-pulsed source of electromagnetic radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01485 "cyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01485 "Salts and esters of cyanic acid, $\\ce{HOC#N}$, e.g. $\\ce{KOCN}$ potassium cyanate, $\\ce{PhOCN}$ phenyl cyanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01486 "cyanides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01486 "Salts and $C$-organyl derivatives of hydrogen cyanide, $\\ce{HC#N}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3C#N}$ methyl cyanide (acetonitrile), $\\ce{NaCN}$ sodium cyanide, $\\ce{PhC(=O)CN}$ benzoyl cyanide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01487 "cyanine dyes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01487 "Synthetic dyes with the general formula $\\ce{R2N[CH=CH]_{n}CH=N^{+}R2 }$ $\\ce{R2N^{+}=CH[CH=CH]_{n}NR2}$ ($n$ is a small number) in which the nitrogen and part of the conjugated chain usually form part of a heterocyclic system, such as imidazole, pyridine, pyrrole, quinoline and thiazole, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01488 "cyanogenic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01488 "Compounds able to produce cyanide; examples are the cyanogenic glycosides such as amygdalin in peach and apricot stones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01489))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01489 "cyanohydrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01489 "Alcohols substituted by a cyano group, most commonly, but not limited to, examples having a cyano and a hydroxy group attached to the same carbon atom, formally derived from aldehydes or ketones by the addition of hydrogen cyanide. An individual cyanohydrin can systematically be named as a hydroxy nitrile, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)2C(OH)C#N}$ 'acetone cyanohydrin' (2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile), $\\ce{HOCH2CH2C#N}$ 'ethylene cyanohydrin' (3-hydroxypropanenitrile).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01490 "cybotactic region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01490 "That part of a solution in the vicinity of a solute molecule in which the ordering of the solvent molecules is modified by the presence of the solute molecule. The term solvent 'cosphere' of the solute has also been used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01490 "cosphere")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01492 "cyclic acid anhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01492 """Acid anhydrides derived by loss of water between two oxoacid functions $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) (carboxylic, sulfonic, etc.) in the same molecule so as to close a ring. Oxo oxygen replacement analogues are included, e.g.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01492 "cyclic anhydrides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01493 "cyclitols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01493 "Hydroxylated cycloalkanes containing at least three hydroxy groups, each attached to a different ring carbon atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01494 "cyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01494 "Formation of a ring compound from a chain by formation of a new bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01495 "cyclo-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01495 "A prefix used in names to designate a ring structure, usually italicized in inorganic, but not in organic nomenclature. Examples:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01496 "cycloaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01496 "A reaction in which two or more unsaturated molecules (or parts of the same molecule) combine with the formation of a cyclic adduct in which there is a net reduction of the bond multiplicity. The following two systems of notations have been used for the more detailed specification of cycloadditions, of which the second, more recent system [described under (2)] is preferred:   A(i + j + ...) cycloaddition is a reaction in which two or more molecules (or parts of the same molecule), respectively, provide units of i, j, ... linearly connected atoms: these units become joined at their respective termini by new $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ so as to form a cycle containing (i + j + ...) atoms. In this notation, (a) a Diels–Alder reaction is a (4+2) cycloaddition, (b) the initial reaction of ozone with an alkene is a (3+2) cycloaddition, and (c) the reaction shown below is a (2+2+2) cycloaddition. (N.B.: parentheses (...) are used in the description based on numbers of atoms.)   The symbolism [i + j + ...] for a cycloaddition identifies the numbers i, j, ... of electrons in the interacting units that participate in the transformation of reactants to products. In this notation the reaction (a) and (b) of the preceding paragraph would both be described as [2+4] cycloadditions, and (c) as a [2+2+2] cycloaddition. The symbol a or s (a = antarafacial, s = suprafacial) is often added (usually as a subscript after the number to designate the stereochemistry of addition to each fragment. A subscript specifying the orbitals, viz. $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!}$, $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ (sigma, pi) with their usual significance) or n (for an orbital associated with a single atom only), may be added as a subscript before the number. Thus the normal Diels–Alder reaction is a \\(\\left [4_{\\rm{a}}\\: +\\: 2_{\\rm{s}} \\right ]\\) or \\(\\left [_{\\pi }4_{\\rm{a}}\\: +\\: _{\\pi }2_{\\rm{s}} \\right ]\\) cycloaddition, whilst the reaction:  would be a \\(\\left [14_{\\rm{a}}\\:  +\\: 2_{\\rm{s}} \\right ]\\) or \\(\\left [_{\\pi }14_{\\rm{a}}\\:  +\\: _{\\pi }2_{\\rm{s}} \\right ]\\) cycloaddition. (N.B. Square brackets are used in the descriptions based on numbers of electrons.) Cycloadditions may be pericyclic reactions or (non-concerted stepwise reactions. The term 'dipolar cycloaddition' is used for cycloadditions of 1,3-dipolar compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01497 "cycloalkanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01497 "Saturated monocyclic hydrocarbons (with or without side chains), e.g. cyclobutane. Unsaturated monocyclic hydrocarbons having one endocyclic double or one triple bond are called cycloalkenes and cycloalkynes, respectively. Those having more than one such multiple bond are cycloalkadienes, cycloalkatrienes, etc. The inclusive terms for any cyclic hydrocarbons having any number of such multiple bonds are cyclic olefins or cyclic acetylenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01498))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01498 "cycloalkyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01498 "Univalent groups derived from cycloalkanes by removal of a hydrogen atom from a ring carbon atom, e.g. 2-methylcyclopropyl.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01500 "cyclodextrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01500 "Cyclic oligoglucosides containing five to ca. ten glucose residues in which an enclosed tubular space allows reception of a guest molecule to form a clathrate. The synonymous term Schardinger dextrins is not recommended. ($\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}Cyclodextrin}$ has six glucose residues; $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}Cyclodextrin}$ has seven.) Semi-systematically $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}cyclodextrin}$ is called cyclomaltohexaose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01501 "cycloelimination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01501 "The reverse of cycloaddition. The term is preferred to the synonyms 'cycloreversion', 'retro-addition' and 'retrocycloaddition'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01501 "cycloreversion")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01501 "retro-addition")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01501 "retrocycloaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01501 "retroaddition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01502 "cyclohexadienyl cations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01502 "A subclass of arenium ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01503 "cyclone collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01503 "A dust, grit or droplet separator utilizing essentially the centrifugal force derived from the motion of the gas. The flow of gases containing suspended particles into the device is transformed into a confined vortex from which centrifugal forces tend to drive the suspended particles to the wall of the cyclone body. The agglomerated particles are subsequently removed from the cyclone by gravitational action.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C01503 "cyclone")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01504 "cyclophanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01504 "The term originally applied to compounds having two p-phenylene groups held face to face by $\\ce{–[CH2]_{n}\\bond{–}}$ bridges. It now designates compounds having (i) mancude-ring systems, or assemblies of mancude-ring systems, and (ii) atoms and/or saturated or unsaturated chains as alternate components of a large ring. E.g.(1,4)(1,4)cyclophane [or 1(1,4),4(1,4)-dibenzenacyclohexaphane].  Many chemists include in this class any bridged aromatic system, irrespective of the attachment positions of the bridge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01505 "cyclopolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01505 "A polymerization in which the number of cyclic structures in the constitutional units of the resulting macromolecules is larger than in the monomer molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01507 "cyclosilazanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01507 "Compounds having rings of alternating silicon and nitrogen atoms:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01508 "cyclosiloxanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01508 "Compounds having rings of alternating silicon and oxygen atoms, e.g. 2,2-dimethylcyclotrisiloxane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01509 "cyclotron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01509 "A particle accelerator in which the particles travel in a succession of semicircular orbits of increasing radii under the influence of a magnetic field and are accelerated at the beginning of each such orbit by traversing an electric field produced by a high-frequency generator.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01510 "cytochromes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01510 "Conjugated proteins containing haem as the prosthetic group and associated with electron transport and with redox processes. The terminal electron transport chain of oxidative respiration contains at least five different cytochromes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C05438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C05438 "σ-constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C05438 "Specifically the substituent constant for meta- and for para-substituents in benzene derivatives as defined by Hammett on the basis of the ionization constant of a substituted benzoic acid in water at \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), i.e. \\(\\log _{10}(\\frac{K_{\\rm{a}}}{K_{\\rm{a}}^{0}})\\), where \\(K_{\\rm{a}}\\) is the ionization constant of a m- or p-substituted benzoic acid and \\(K_{\\rm{a}}^{0}\\) that of benzoic acid itself. The term is also used as a collective description for related electronic substituent constants based on other standard reaction series, of which, \\(\\sigma ^{+}\\), \\(\\sigma ^{-}\\) and \\(\\sigma ^{0}\\) are typical; also constants which represent dissected electronic effects such as \\(\\sigma _{I}\\) and \\(\\sigma _{R}\\). For this purpose it might be better always to spell out the term in full, i.e. as 'Hammett sigma constant', and restrict \\(\\sigma \\)-constants to the scale of substituent constants which is based on benzoic acid. A large positive \\(\\sigma \\)-value implies high electron-withdrawing power by inductive and/or resonance effect, relative to H; a large negative \\(\\sigma \\)-value implies high electron-releasing power relative to H.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT01038))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT01038 "chemical species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT01038 "An ensemble of chemically identical molecular entities that can explore the same set of molecular energy levels on the time scale of the experiment. The term is applied equally to a set of chemically identical atomic or molecular structural units in a solid array. For example, two conformational isomers may be interconverted sufficiently slowly to be detectable by separate NMR spectra and hence to be considered to be separate chemical species on a time scale governed by the radiofrequency of the spectrometer used. On the other hand, in a slow chemical reaction the same mixture of conformers may behave as a single chemical species, i.e. there is virtually complete equilibrium population of the total set of molecular energy levels belonging to the two conformers. Except where the context requires otherwise, the term is taken to refer to a set of molecular entities containing isotopes in their natural abundance. The wording of the definition given in the first paragraph is intended to embrace both cases such as graphite, sodium chloride or a surface oxide, where the basic structural units may not be capable of isolated existence, as well as those cases where they are. In common chemical usage generic and specific chemical names (such as radical or hydroxide ion) or chemical formulae refer either to a chemical species or to a molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT01101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT01101 "clearance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT01101 "Product of the concentration of a component in an output system and the volume flow rate of that output system divided by the concentration of this component in the input system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT01101 "volume rate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06757))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06757 "chemical diffusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06757 "Diffusion under the influence of a gradient in chemical composition. In concentrated solid solutions, e.g. $\\ce{A_{1-x}B_{x}}$, or in diffusion couples, the motion of one constituent causes a counterflow of the other constituent(s) or vacancies. In this case one can define a diffusion coefficient for the intermixing, which is called the chemical diffusion coefficient or interdiffusion coefficient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06762 "catalase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06762 "A hemeprotein which catalyses the disproportionation of dihydrogen peroxide to $\\ce{O2}$, and water; it also catalyses the oxidation of other compounds, such as ethanol, by dihydrogen peroxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06769 "cluster")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06769 "A number of metal centres grouped close together which can have direct metal bonding interactions or interactions through a bridging ligand, but are not necessarily held together by these interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06770 "crystal field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06770 "The average static electric field experienced by an ion, molecule or atom in a crystal generated by all the other surrounding atoms, molecules, or ions. The concept introduced by becquerel and developed by Bethe has been extended to refer to the field generated by the ligands (anions or neutral species) surrounding a transition metal ion in a coordination compound. With the understatement that some covalency may exist in the metal-ligand bond, it is often referred to as ligand field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06777))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06777 "circular dichroism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06777 "A spectroscopic method which measures the difference in absorbance of left- and right-handed circularly polarised light by a material, as a function of the wavelength. Most biological molecules, including proteins and nucleic acids, are chiral and show circular dichroism in their ultraviolet absorption bands, which may be used as an indication of secondary structure. Metal centres that are bound to such molecules, even if they have no inherent chirality, usually exhibit CD in absorption bands associated with ligand-based or ligand-metal charge-transfer transitions. CD is frequently used in combination with absorption and MCD studies to assign electronic transitions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06814))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06814 "carrier protein")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06814 "Protein to which a specific ligand or hapten is conjugated.Unlabeled protein introduced into an assay at relatively high concentrations which distributes in a fractionation process in the same manner as labeled protein analyte, present in very low concentrations.Protein added to prevent non-specific interaction of reagents with surfaces, sample components, and each other.Protein found in cell membranes, which facilitates transport of a ligand across the membrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06815))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06815 "ceiling value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06815 "Airborne concentration of a potentially toxic substance which should never be exceeded in a worker’s breathing zone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06816))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06816 "cell line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06816 "Defined unique population of cells obtained by culture from a primary source through numerous generations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06817 "chronic toxicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06817 "Adverse effects following chronic exposure.Effects which persist over a long period of time whether or not they occur immediately upon exposure or are delayed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06818 "compartmental analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06818 "Mathematical process leading to a model of transport of a substance in terms of compartments and rate constants, usually taking the form \\[C=A\\ \\rm{e}^{-\\alpha\\ t}+B\\ \\rm{e}^{-\\beta \\ t}\\rm{...}\\] where each exponential term represents one experiment. \\(C\\) is the substance concentration; \\(A\\), \\(B\\),… are proportionality constants; \\(\\alpha \\), \\(\\beta \\),… are rate constants; and \\(t\\) is time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06819 "congener")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06819 "One of two or more substances related to each other by origin, structure, or function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06820))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06820 "critical study")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06820 "Investigation yielding the no observed adverse effect level that is used by the USEPA as the basis of the reference dose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06821))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06821 "cytochrome P450")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06821 "Member of a superfamily of heme-containing monooxygenases involved in xenobiotic metabolism, cholesterol biosynthesis, and steroidogenesis, in eukaryotic organisms found mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum and inner mitochondrial membrane of cells. ‘P450’ refers to a feature in the carbon monoxide absorption difference spectrum at \\(450\\ \\rm{nm}\\) caused by the presence of a thiolate in the axial position of the heme opposite to the carbon monoxide ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06850 "crazing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06850 "Formation of cavities, when a polymer is stressed, which contain load-bearing fibrils spanning the gap between the surfaces of each cavity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06857 "clearing point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06857 "The temperature at which the transition between the mesophase with the highest temperature range and the isotropic phase occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06859 "chemical species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06859 "Specific form of an element defined as to isotopic composition, electronic or oxidation state, and/or complex or molecular structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06860 "clearance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06860 "Volume of blood or plasma or mass of an organ effectively cleared of a substance by elimination (metabolism and excretion) divided by time of elimination.Note:Total clearance is the sum of the clearances of each eliminating organ or tissue for a given substance.(in pulmonary toxicology) Volume or mass of lung cleared divided by time of elimination; used qualitatively to describe removal of any inhaled substance which deposits on the lining surface of the lung.(in renal toxicology) Quantification of the removal of a substance by the kidneys by the processes of filtration and secretion; clearance is calculated by relating the rate of renal excretion to the plasma concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06863 "composite membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06863 "Membrane having chemically or structurally distinct layers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06864))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06864 "counter-current flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06864 "Flow through a membrane module in which the fluids on the upstream and downstream sides of the membrane move parallel to the membrane surface but in opposite directions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06916))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06916 "continuous bed packing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06916 "A column packing, which is a single entity, rather than being composed of individual particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06917 "carbon loading")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06917 "Mass fraction of the packing material which is carbon. Usually taken as a guide to the extent of alkyl substitution on the surface. Usually reported as percentage carbon determined using elemental analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06920))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06920 "chiral stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06920 "A stationary phase which incorporates a chiral selector. If not a constituent of the stationary phase as a whole, the chiral selector can be chemically bonded to (chiral bonded stationary phase ) or immobilised onto the surface of a solid support or column wall (chiral coated stationary phase), or simply dissolved in the liquid stationary phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06930 "chiral mobile phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06930 "A mobile phase containing a chiral selector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06931))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06931 "chiral selector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06931 "The chiral component of the separation system capable of interacting enantioselectively with the enantiomers to be separated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06937))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06937 "convex isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06937 "Distribution isotherm, the slope of which is continuously decreasing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06938))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06938 "concave isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06938 "Distribution isotherm, the slope of which is continuously increasing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06948 "chemometrics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06948 "Chemometrics is the application of statistics to the analysis of chemical data (from organic, analytical or medicinal chemistry) and design of chemical experiments and simulations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06949))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06949 "CLOGP values")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06949 "CLOGP values are calculated 1-octanol/water partition coefficients, frequently used in structure-property correlation or quantitative structure-activity relationship (SPC/QSAR) studies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06950 "cluster analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06950 "Cluster analysis is the clustering, or grouping, of large data sets (e.g., chemical and/or pharmacological data sets) on the basis of similarity criteria for appropriately scaled variables that represent the data of interest. Similarity criteria (distance based, associative, correlative, probabilistic) among the several clusters facilitate the recognition of patterns and reveal otherwise hidden structures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06951))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06951 "comparative molecular field analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06951 "Comparative Molecular Field Analysis (CoMFA) is a 3D-QSAR method that uses statistical correlation techniques for the analysis of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a set of compounds with a special alignment, and their three-dimensional electronic and steric properties. Other properties, such as hydrophobicity and H-bonding, can also be incorporated into the analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06952 "computational chemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06952 "Computational chemistry is a discipline using mathematical methods for the calculation of molecular properties or for the simulation of molecular behaviour. It also includes, e.g., synthesis planning, database searching, combinatorial library manipulation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06953))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06953 "computer-assisted drug design")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06953 "Computer-assisted drug design involves all computer-assisted techniques used to discover, design and optimize biologically active compounds with a putative use as drugs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06954))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06954 "computer-assisted molecular design")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06954 "Computer-assisted molecular design involves all computer-assisted techniques used to discover, design and optimize compounds with desired structure and properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06955))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06955 "computer-assisted molecular modeling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06955 "Computer-assisted molecular modeling is the investigation of molecular structures and properties using computational chemistry and graphical visualization techniques.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT06956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT06956 "conformational analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT06956 "Conformational analysis consists of the exploration of energetically favourable spatial arrangements (shapes) of a molecule (conformations) using molecular mechanics, molecular dynamics, quantum chemical calculations or analysis of experimentally-determined structural data, e.g, NMR or crystal structures.Molecular mechanics and quantum chemical methods are employed to compute conformational energies, whereas systematic and random searches, Monte Carlo, molecular dynamics, and distance geometry are methods (often combined with energy minimization procedures) used to explore the conformational space.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07009 "chemical bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07009 "When forces acting between two atoms or groups of atoms lead to the formation of a stable independent molecular entity, a chemical bond is considered to exist between these atoms or groups. The principal characteristic of a bond in a molecule is the existence of a region between the nuclei of constant potential contours that allows the potential energy to improve substantially by atomic contraction at the expense of only a small increase in kinetic energy. Not only directed covalent bonds characteristic of organic compounds, but also bonds such as those existing between sodium cations and chloride anions in a crystal of sodium chloride or the bonds binding aluminium to six molecules of water in its environment, and even weak bonds that link two molecules of $\\ce{O2}$ into $\\ce{O4}$, are to be attributed to chemical bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07010 "closed shell molecular systems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07010 "Even-electron atomic or molecular systems whose electron configurations consist of doubly occupied orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07011 "concerted reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07011 "A single-step reaction through which reactants are directly transformed into products, i.e., without involvement of any intermediates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07012 "conservation of orbital symmetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07012 "The orbital symmetry control of concerted reactions; this requires transformation of the molecular orbitals of reactants into those of products to proceed continuously by following a reaction path along which the symmetry of these orbitals remains unchanged. Reactions which adhere to this requirement are classified as symmetry-allowed reactions, and those which do not as symmetry-forbidden reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07013))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07013 "Coulomb repulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07013 "The potential energy component corresponding to the electrostatic interaction between each pair of charged particles: \\[V=\\frac{1}{4\\: \\pi\\: \\varepsilon_{0}}\\ \\sum _{i}\\sum _{j \\lt i}e_{i}\\ e_{j}\\ \\Delta r_{ij}\\] where \\(\\varepsilon _{0}\\) is the permittivity of a vacuum, \\(\\Delta r_{ij}\\) is the distance between the two particles, and \\(e_{i}\\) and \\(e_{j}\\) are the charges on particles \\(i\\) and \\(j\\). In molecular orbital theory, the electrostatic repulsion between the two electrons occupying the orbitals \\(\\Psi _{i}\\) and \\(\\Psi _{j}\\). In the Hartree–Fock method, the mean Coulomb repulsion is determined by the value of the coulomb integral \\[J_{ij}=\\int \\int \\Psi _{i\\rm{*}}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{1}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{i}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{1}\\right)\\ \\left(\\frac{e^{2}}{r_{12}}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{j\\rm{*}}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{2}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{j}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{2}\\right)  \\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}_{1} \\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}_{2} = \\lt ij|ij \\gt\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07014 "coupled cluster method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07014 "A method employed in ab initio quantum mechanical calculations providing for a higher-level treatment of electron correlation beyond MP4.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07014 "CC method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07016 "curve-crossing model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07016 "Model of organic reactivity that generates a reaction profile from curves that describe the energies of reactant, product, and intermediate electronic configurations (or, alternatively, reactant, product and intermediate electronic states) as a function of the reaction coordinate. The crossing reflects the electronic reorganization that accompanies the transformation of reactants and products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07134 "chemical amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07134 "Process consisting of a chemical reaction that generates a species that catalyzes another reaction and also the succeeding catalyzed reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07135 "chemical modification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07135 "Process by which at least one feature of the chemical constitution of a polymer is changed by chemical reaction(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07136 "crosslinking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07136 "Reaction involving sites or groups on existing macromolecules or an interaction between existing macromolecules that results in the formation of a small region in a macromolecule from which at least four chains emanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07137))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07137 "curing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07137 "Chemical process of converting a prepolymer or a polymer into a polymer of higher molar mass and then into a network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07154 "chelating polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07154 "Polymer containing ligand groups capable of forming bonds (or other attractive interactions) between two or more separate binding sites within the same ligand group and a single atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07170 "conducting polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07170 "Polymeric material that exhibits bulk electric conductivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07200 "cationic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07200 "Polymer composed of positively charged macromolecules and an equivalent amount of counter-anions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07201))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07201 "conducting polymer composite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07201 "Electrically-conducting composite comprising a non-conducting polymer matrix and an electrically-conducting material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07202 "critical ion-concentration in an ionomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07202 "Concentration of ionic groups in an ionomer matrix above which ionic aggregation occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07339 "chemically induced dynamic electron polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07339 "Non-Boltzmann electron spin-state population produced in thermal or photochemical reactions, either from a combination of radical pairs (called radical-pair mechanism), or directly from the triplet state (called triplet mechanism) and detectable by ESR spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07340 "chemically initiated electron exchange luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07340 "Type of luminescence resulting from a thermal electron-transfer reaction. Also called catalyzed chemiluminescence.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07340 "catalyzed chemiluminescence")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07341))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07341 "colourability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07341 "Ability of a colourless or slightly coloured photochromic material to develop colour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07342 "color scale")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07342 "Series of ordered numbers that represents observable gradations of a given attribute or gradations of a combination of attributes of color perception.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07343 "complete active space self-consistent field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07343 "Computational scheme employed in multiconfigurational SCF theory especially suitable for studies of reactivity of excited states. The wavefunction is defined by selecting the set of active orbitals involved in the excitation or chemical reaction under investigation and is constructed as a linear expansion in the set of configuration functions that can be generated by occupying the active orbitals in all ways consistent with an overall spin and space symmetry (full configuration interaction, CI).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07344 "complete active space self-consistent field second-order perturbation theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07344 "Theoretical scheme suitable for computations of accurate excitation energies and reaction barriers. In the first step, electron correlation is taken into account only to a certain extent by using a CASSCF formalism (the so called non-dynamic correlation), whereas the remaining electron correlation (the so called dynamic correlation) is included through the use of second-order perturbation theory.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07345 "computational photochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07345 "Aspects of research that address the study of photochemical events by means of computer simulations using specialized software tools and strategies in order to get an understanding of the process at a microscopic level. The aim is to uncover the mechanism of known photochemical processes, design new photochemical systems, and predict molecular properties that are experimentally inaccessible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07346 "concentration depolarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07346 "Loss of emission anisotropy due to transfer of electronic excitation from photo-selected molecules to other molecules with different orientations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07347 "conical intersection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07347 "Point of crossing between two electronic states of the same spin multiplicity (most commonly singlets or triplets).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07348 "continuous wave laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07348 "CW operation of a laser means that the laser is continuously pumped and continuously emits electromagnetic radiation. The emission can occur in a single cavity mode or on multiple modes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07348 "CW laser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07349 "conversion cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07349 "Product of the quantum yield by the absorption cross-section, \\(\\sigma \\ \\varPhi \\). SI unit is \\(\\pu{m2}\\); commonly expressed in \\(\\pu{cm2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07350 "crystal photochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07350 "Photochemical process in the crystal lattice of a photoactive compound or of a chromophore in the crystal lattice of host molecules. Often asymmetric photochemistry takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07351 "cut-on filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07351 "Optical device that only permits the transmission of radiation of wavelength shorter than a specified wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07503 "chemical functionality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07503 "Ability of functional groups present within a polymer or polymer network to participate in chemical reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07504 "connectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07504 "Number of covalent bonds emanating from a constitutional unit of an oligomer molecule or a macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07518 "colloidal gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07518 "Gel in which the network component comprises particles of colloidal dimensions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07539))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07539 "ceramer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07539 "Chemically bonded hybrid material which is a crosslinked organic–inorganic polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07540 "ceramic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07540 "Rigid material that consists of an infinite three-dimensional network of sintered crystalline grains comprising metals bonded to carbon, nitrogen or oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07541 "ceramic precursor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07541 "Material that is converted to a ceramic through pyrolysis.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07541 "pre-ceramic")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07541 "pre-ceramic material")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07542 "ceramic-reinforced polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07542 "Polymer composite consisting of a polymer continuous phase and disperse phase domains of microscopic ceramic particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07543 "ceramic yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07543 "Mass of ceramic expressed as a percentage of the mass of the ceramic precursor used in the ceramization process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07545 "composite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07545 "Multicomponent material comprising multiple, different (non-gaseous) phase domains in which at least one type of phase domain is a continuous phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07546 "creep")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07546 "Time-dependent change of the dimensions of a material under a constant load.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07554 "chemically bonded hybrid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07554 "Hybrid material in which the different components are bonded to each other by covalent or partially covalent bonds.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07554 "chemically bonded hybrid material")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07555 "clay hybrid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07555 "Organic–inorganic composite material in which one of the components is a clay, the particles of which are dispersed in a polymer.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07555 "polymer-clay composite")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:CT07555 "polymer-clay hybrid")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07563 "colloidal network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07563 "Network comprising particles of colloidal dimensions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07581 "compatible polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07581 "Immiscible polymer blend that exhibits macroscopically uniform physical properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07593 "chain entanglement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07593 "Interlocking of polymer chains in a polymer material forming a transient or permanent network junction over the time-scale of the measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07596))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07596 "crosslink density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07596 "Number of crosslinks per unit volume in a polymer network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07597 "crosslinking site")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07597 "Site on a macromolecule or region in a polymer material that takes part in the formation of chemical or physical crosslinks.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07609 "carbo-reduction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07609 "Process in which a metal oxide is reduced in the presence of carbon or a carbon-containing compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07610))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07610 "ceramization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07610 "Process in which a ceramic precursor is converted into a ceramic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:CT07612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:CT07612 "colloidal processing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:CT07612 "Sol-gel processing in which a network of precipitated colloidal particles is treated by a conventional processing technique, such as cold pressing, hot pressing or sintering, in order to produce a ceramic article.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01511 "Δ, Λ")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01511 "Designations of stereoisomers of tris(didentate) metal complexes and other octahedral complexes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01511 "delta, lambda")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01513 "d, l, dl")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01513 "Obsolete (usage strongly discouraged) alternatives for, respectively, the prefixes (+)- and (−)- [used to designate the sign of optical rotation of enantiomers under specified condition (and hence to distinguish the enantiomers)] and (±)- for a racemate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01513 "plus")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01513 "minus")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01513 "+")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01513 "-")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01514 "dalton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01514 "Non-SI unit of mass (symbol \\(\\mathrm{Da}\\)), equal to the unified atomic mass unit (atomic mass constant). It is often used in biochemistry and molecular biology although it was never approved by the Conférence Général des Poids et Mesures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01517 "dark photochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01517 "Chemical reactions involving electronically excited molecular entities which are generated thermally rather than by absorption of electromagnetic radiation. The use of this term is discouraged.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01517 "photochemistry without light")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01518 "dark reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01518 "A chemical reaction that does not require or depend on the presence of light. Contrasts with a photochemical reaction which is initiated by light absorption by one or more of the reactants.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01518 "darkness reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01521 "data reduction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01521 "The process of transforming the initial representation of a spectrometer output into a form which is amenable to interpretation; for example, a bar graph or a table of ion currents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01523 "dative bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01523 "The coordination bond formed upon interaction between molecular species, one of which serves as a donor and the other as an acceptor of the electron pair to be shared in the complex formed, e.g, the $\\ce{N\\!\\rightarrow\\!B}$ bond in $\\ce{H3N\\!\\rightarrow\\!BH3}$. In spite of the analogy of dative bonds with covalent bonds, in that both types imply sharing a common electron pair between two vicinal atoms, the former are distinguished by their significant polarity, lesser strength, and greater length. The distinctive feature of dative bonds is that their minimum-energy rupture in the gas phase or in inert solvent follows the heterolytic bond cleavage path.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01524 "daughter ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01524 "An electrically charged product of reaction of a particular parent ion. In general such ions have a direct relationship to a particular precursor ion and indeed may relate to a unique state of the precursor ion. The reaction need not necessarily involve fragmentation. It could, for example, involve a change in the number of charges carried. Thus, all fragment ions are daughter ions, but not all daughter ions are necessarily fragment ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01525 "daughter product")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01525 "Any nuclide which follows a specified radionuclide in a decay chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01526 "Davydov splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01526 "The splitting of bands in the electronic or vibrational spectra of crystals due to the presence of more than one (interacting) equivalent molecular entity in the unit cell.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01526 "factor-group splitting")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01527 "day")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01527 "Non-SI unit of time, \\(\\rm{d}=86\\ 400\\ \\rm{s}\\). The day is not exactly defined in terms of the second since so-called leap seconds are added or subtracted from the day semiannually in order to keep the annual average occurrence of midnight at 24:00 on the clock.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01528 "deactivation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01528 "Any loss of energy by an excited molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01529))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01529 "dead time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01529 "Of a radiation counter, the constant and known value imposed on the resolving time, usually in order to make the correction for resolving time losses more accurate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01530 "dead time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01530 "The time which elapses between the moment at which a sudden change in concentration (or a new sample) is introduced and the moment at which the detector response indication reaches the measurement threshold of the analyser, a value conventionally fixed at 10% of the final change in indication.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01531 "dead time correction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01531 "Correction to be applied to the observed number of pulses in order to take into account the number of pulses lost during the resolving or dead time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01532 "dead-volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01532 "This term is used to express the extra-column volume. Strictly speaking, the term 'dead-volume' refers to volumes within the chromatographic system which are not swept by the mobile phase. On the other hand, mobile phase is flowing through most of the extra-column volumes. Due to this ambiguity the use of term 'dead-volume' is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01533 "debye")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01533 "Non-SI unit of electric dipole moment. It is equal to the electric dipole moment for two charges of \\(10^{-10}\\ \\rm{franklin}\\) separated by \\(1\\ \\rm{ångström}\\), \\(\\pu{D} = \\pu{E-18 Fr cm} \\approx \\pu{3.33564E-30 C m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01534 "Debye–Hückel equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01534 "The chemical potential or activity of ions cannot be determined on a purely thermodynamic basis. This is due to the fact that the effects of an ion cannot be separated from the effects of the accompanying counter-ion, or in other terms, the electrochemical potential of the ion cannot be separated into the chemical and the electrical component. Such a separation must necessarily be based on a non-thermodynamic convention. The present convention is based on the assumption that the molal activity coefficient of the chloride ion in dilute aqueous solutions (\\(\\pu{I \\lt 0.10 mol kg-1}\\)) can be estimated by means of the Debye–Hückel equation: \\[-\\log_{10}\\gamma_{\\rm{B}}=\\frac{z_{\\rm{B}}^{2}\\ A\\ \\sqrt{I}}{1 + \\mathring{a}\\ B\\ \\sqrt{I}}\\] where \\(I\\) is ionic strength, \\(z\\) is the charge number of the ion, \\(\\mathring{a}\\) is ion size parameter and \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are temperature-dependent constants. According to the Bates–Guggenheim convention \\(\\mathring{a}\\ B\\) is taken to be \\(1.5 \\sqrt{\\pu{mol kg-1}}\\) at all temperatures and for all compositions of the solutions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01535 "deca")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01535 "SI prefix for 10 (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{da}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01536 "decadic absorbance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01536 "The negative decadic logarithm of one minus absorptance as measured on a uniform sample: \\[A=- \\log _{10}(1- \\alpha )\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01537 "decay chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01537 "A series of nuclides in which each member transforms into the next through nuclear decay until a stable nuclide has been formed. Synonymous with radioactive chain and radioactive series.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01537 "radioactive chain")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01537 "radioactive series")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01538))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01538 "decay constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01538 "Proportionality constant relating the activity of a radioactive substance, A, to the number of decaying particles B, \\(N_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(A=\\lambda \\ N_{\\rm{B}}\\). probability of a nuclear decay within a time interval divided by that time interval. Synonymous with 'disintegration constant'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01538 "disintegration constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01539))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01539 "decay curve")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01539 "A graph showing the relative amount of radioactive substance remaining after any time interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01541 "decay rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01541 "The rate at which a pollutant is removed from the atmosphere either by reaction with reactive transient species such as the $\\ce{HO}$ radical, $\\ce{O3}$, etc., by photodecomposition initiated by light absorption by the impurity, or by loss at the surface of aerosols, the earth, etc. The decay rate as applied to radioactive materials is related to the radioactive half-life (\\(t_{1/2}\\)) of the particular isotopic species A and its concentration \\([A]_{t}\\), at the given time (\\(t\\)): \\[\\rm{Rate}=\\frac{[A]_{t}\\ \\ln 2}{t_{1/2}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01543 "decay scheme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01543 "A graphical representation of the energy levels of the members of a decay chain showing the way along which nuclear decay occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01544 "decay time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01544 "The time on stream during which the rate constant for the catalytic reaction, \\(k\\), falls to a specified fraction of its original value, often 0.5.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01544 "time of deactivation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01545 "deci")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01545 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-1}\\) (symbol: \\(\\rm{d}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01546 "decibel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01546 "Non-SI unit for expressing power levels, \\(\\mathrm{dB}=0.1151293\\ \\mathrm{Np}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01547 "decomposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01547 "The breakdown of a single phase into two or more phases. The term applies also to other chemical entities such as a normal molecule and a reaction intermediate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01549 "decontamination factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01549 "The ratio of the proportion of contaminant to product before treatment to the proportion after treatment. It is the reciprocal of the enrichment factor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01553 "definitive method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01553 "A method of exceptional scientific status which is sufficiently accurate to stand alone in the determination of a given property for the certification of a reference material. Such a method must have a firm theoretical foundation so that systematic error is negligible relative to the intended use. Analyte masses (amounts) or concentrations must be measured directly in terms of the base units of measurements, or indirectly related through sound theoretical equations. Definitive methods, together with certified reference materials, are primary means for transferring accuracy, i.e. establishing traceability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01554 "deflection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01554 "(In terms of divisions of the pointer scale). The other point of reversal of an ideal (undamped) swing starting at the zero point of the pointer scale. Since the points of reversal of an ideal swing are located symmetrically about the rest point, the deflection is equal to twice the rest point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01555 "deflocculation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01555 "The reversal of coagulation or flocculation, i.e. the dispersion of aggregates to form a colloidally stable suspension or emulsion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01555 "peptization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01556 "degeneracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01556 "Number of states having the same energy. Symbols: \\(g\\), \\(d\\), \\(W\\), \\(\\omega \\), \\(\\beta \\). Also called statistical weight.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01557 "degenerate chain branching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01557 "Sometimes chain branching is brought about by an intermediate which has a long lifetime compared with an ordinary free radical. If this intermediate can break down in different ways, only one of which leads to branching, there may be a relatively slow increase in the number of radicals, and there is said to be degenerate chain branching.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01559 "degenerate rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01559 "A molecular rearrangement in which the principal product is indistinguishable (in the absence of isotopic labelling) from the principal reactant. The term includes both 'degenerate intramolecular rearrangements' and reactions that involve intermolecular transfer of atoms or groups ('degenerate intermolecular rearrangements'): both are degenerate isomerizations. The occurrence of degenerate rearrangements may be detectable by isotopic labelling or by dynamic NMR techniques. For example: thesigmatropic rearrangement of hexa-1,5-diene (Cope rearrangement):  Synonymous but less preferable terms are 'automerization', 'permutational isomerism', 'isodynamic transformation', 'topomerization'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01560 "degree")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01560 "Non-SI unit of plane angle, \\(1\\ ^{\\unicode{x26ac}} = \\frac{\\pi }{180}\\ \\rm{rad}\\approx 0.017\\ 453\\ 29\\,...\\,\\rm{rad}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01561 "degree Celsius")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01561 "SI derived unit of Celsius temperature equal to the kelvin (symbol: \\(\\pu{^{\\circ}C}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01562 "degree Fahrenheit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01562 "Non-SI unit of Fahrenheit temperature, \\(\\pu{^{\\circ}F = \\frac{5}{9} K}\\). The Fahrenheit temperature is related to the Celsius temperature by \\(\\frac{\\theta_{\\rm{F}}}{^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} \\rm{F}} = \\frac{9}{5}\\ \\frac{\\theta}{^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} \\rm{C}}\\,+\\,32\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01564 "degree of association")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01564 "The number of surfactant ions in the micelle. It does not concern the location of the counterions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01565 "degree of crystallinity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01565 "The fractional amount of crystallinity in the polymer sample (\\(w_{c}\\) for mass fraction; \\(\\psi_{c}\\) for volume fraction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01566 "degree of dissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01566 "Degree of reaction for a dissociation reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01568 "degree of ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01568 "Degree of reaction for an ionization reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01569 "degree of polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01569 "The number of monomeric units in a macromolecule or oligomer molecule, a block or a chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01570 "degree of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01570 "Extent of reaction divided by the maximum extent of reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01571 "degrees of cistacticity and transtacticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01571 "For a regular polymer containing double bonds in the main chain of the constitutional repeating units these are the fractions of such double bonds that are in the cis and trans configurations, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01572 "degrees of freedom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01572 """A statistical quantity indicating the number of values which could be arbitrarily assigned within the specification of a system of observations. For simple replication, with \\(n\\) measurements and one estimated parameter (the mean), \\(
u =n- 1\\). More generally, for multivariable computations, the number of degrees of freedom equals the number of observations minus the number of fitted parameters.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01573 "degrees of triad isotacticity, syndiotacticity, and heterotacticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01573 "The fractions of triads in a regular vinyl polymer that are of the mm, rr and mr = rm types, respectively. In cases where triad analysis is not attainable, the diad isotacticity and diad syndiotacticity may be defined as the fractions of diads in a regular vinyl polymer that are of the m and r types, respectively. (m = meso; r = racemic).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01574 "dehydroarenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01574 "Species, usually transient, derived formally by the abstraction of a hydrogen atom from each of two ring atoms of an arene. The name for specific compounds requires the numerical prefix di-. E.g. 1,8-didehydronaphthalene or naphthalene-1,8-diyl.  1,2-Didehydroarenes are called arynes and are commonly represented with a formal triple bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01575 "dehydrobenzenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01575 "A subclass of dehydroarenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01576 "delayed coincidence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01576 "The occurrence of two or more events separated by a short but measurable time interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01577 "delayed coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01577 "A commonly used term for a primary carbonization product (green or raw coke) from high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions (heavy residues of petroleum or coal processing) produced by the delayed coking process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01578 "delayed coking process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01578 "A thermal process which increases the molecular aggregation or association in petroleum-based residues or coal tar pitches leading to extended mesophase domains. This is achieved by holding them at an elevated temperature (usually \\(750{-}765\\ \\rm{K}\\)) over a period of time (\\(12\\ \\rm{to}\\ 36\\ \\rm{h}\\)). It is performed in a coking drum and is designed to ultimately produce delayed coke. The feed is rapidly pre-heated in a tubular furnace to about \\(760\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01579 "delayed fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01579 "Three types of delayed fluorescence are known:   E-type delayed fluorescence: The process in which the first excited singlet state becomes populated by a thermally activated radiationless transition from the first excited triplet state. Since in this case the populations of the singlet and triplet states are in thermal equilibrium, the lifetimes of delayed fluorescence and the concomitant phosphorescence are equal.   P-type delayed fluorescence: The process in which the first excited singlet state is populated by interaction of two molecules in the triplet state (triplet-triplet annihilation) thus producing one molecule in the excited singlet state. In this biphotonic process the lifetime of delayed fluorescence is half the value of the concomitant phosphorescence.   Recombination fluorescence: The first excited singlet state becomes populated by recombination of radical ions with electrons or by recombination of radical ions of opposite charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01580 "delayed luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01580 "Luminescence decaying more slowly than that expected from the rate of decay of the emitting state. The following mechanisms of luminescence provide examples:triplet-triplet or singlet-singlet annihilation to form one molecular entity in its excited singlet state and another molecular entity in its electronic ground state (sometime referred to as P type),  thermally activated delayed fluorescence involving reversible intersystem crossing (sometimes referred to as E type), and   combination of oppositely charged ions or of an electron and a cation. For emission to be referred to in this case as delayed luminescence at least one of the two reaction partners must be generated in a photochemical process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01581 "delayed neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01581 "Neutrons emitted by fission products formed by nuclear decay (the observed delay is due to the preceding nuclear decay or decays).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01582 "deliquescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01582 "The process that occurs when the vapour pressure of the saturated aqueous solution of a substance is less than the vapour pressure of water in the ambient air. When water vapour is collected by the pure solid compound, a mixture of the solid and liquid or an aqueous solution of the compound forms until the substance is dissolved and is in equilibrium with its environment; at this time the vapour pressure of water over the aqueous solution will equal the partial pressure of water in the atmosphere in contact with it. A crystalline salt aerosol particle will deliquesce in the atmosphere when the relative humidity surpasses a characteristic value, the so-called deliquescence point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01583 "delocalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01583 "A quantum mechanical concept most usually applied in organic chemistry to describe the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$ in a conjugated system. This bonding is not localized between two atoms: instead, each link has a 'fractional double bond character' or bond order. There is a corresponding 'delocalization energy', identifiable with the stabilization of the system compared with a hypothetical alternative in which formal (localized) single and double bonds are present. Some degree of delocalization is always present and can be estimated by quantum mechanical calculations. The effects are particularly evident in aromatic systems and in symmetrical molecular entities in which a lone pair of electrons or a vacant p-orbital is conjugated with a double bond (e.g. carboxylate ions, nitro compounds, enamines, the allyl cation). Delocalization in such species may be represented by partial bonds or as resonance (here symbolized by a two-headed arrow) between contributing structures.   These examples also illustrate the concomitant delocalization of charge in ionic conjugated systems. Analogously, delocalization of the spin of an unpaired electron occurs in conjugated radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01585 "demister")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01585 "Apparatus made of wire mesh or glass fibre which is used to help remove acid mist as in the manufacture of sulfuric acid. Demisters are also components of wet arrestment plants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01586 "denaturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01586 "The process of partial or total alteration of the native structure of a macromolecule resulting from the loss of tertiary or tertiary and secondary structure that is a consequence of the disruption of stabilizing weak bonds. Denaturation can occur when proteins and nucleic acids are subjected to elevated temperature or to extremes of pH, or to non-physiological concentrations of salt, organic solvents, urea or other chemical agents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01587 "denaturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01587 "Addition of methanol or acetone to alcohol to make it unfit for drinking.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01588 "dendrite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01588 "A crystalline morphology produced by skeletal growth, leading to a 'tree-like' appearance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01589 "denitrification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01589 "The reduction of nitrates to nitrites, dinitrogen oxide (nitrous oxide) or dinitrogen catalysed by microoganisms, e.g. facultative aerobic soil bacteria under anaerobic conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01590 "density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01590 "Mass of a sample or body divided by its volume.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01590 "mass density")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01590 "specific weight")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01591 "density inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01591 "The interchange of the denser and less dense phases due to changes in solute concentration. phase inversion is often used in this context but is ambiguous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01593 "density of states")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01593 "Number of states within an energy interval divided by volume and that energy interval. Symbol: \\(N_{E}\\).Number of states within an energy interval divided by that energy interval. Symbol: \\(\\rho(E)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01594 "denticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01594 "In a coordination entity the number of donor groups from a given ligand attached to the same central atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01595 "denuder system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01595 "An apparatus used to separate gases and aerosols (over a given diameter) which is based upon the difference in diffusion velocity between gases and aerosol particles. Usually a tube containing a selective internal wall coating which removes the gaseous compounds at the wall.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01596))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01596 "deodorizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01596 "Equipment for the removal of noxious gases and odours, which may consist of combustion, absorption, or adsorption units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01597 "deoxyribonucleic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01597 "High molecular weight, linear polymers, composed of nucleotides containing deoxyribose and linked by phosphodiester bonds; DNA contain the genetic information of organisms. The double-stranded form consists of a double helix of two complementary chains that run in opposite directions and are held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of the complementary nucleotides and Hoogsteen (stacking) forces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01598 "depolarization of scattered light")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01598 "The phenomenon, due primarily to the anisotropy of the polarizability of the scattering medium, resulting from the fact that the electric vectors of the incident and scattered beams are not coplanar and that, therefore, light scattered from a vertically (horizontally) polarized incident beam contains a horizontal (vertical) component.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01600 "depolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01600 "The process of converting a polymer into a monomer or a mixture of monomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01601 "deposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01601 "Deposition is normally considered to be one of two types: dry deposition is the process by which aerosols and gases in the air are deposited on the surface of the earth (soil, water, rock, plants, etc.); this is termed 'dry' deposition even when the receptor surface is moist. Wet deposition is that process which involves the transport of chemicals to the surface of the earth by water droplets or snow crystals which scavenge pollutants as they form and fall through the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01602 "deposition velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01602 "The ratio of flux density (often given in units of \\(\\pu{g cm-2 s-1}\\)) of a substance at a sink surface to its concentration in the atmosphere (corresponding units of \\(\\pu{g cm-3}\\)). While the units of this ratio are clearly those of velocity (in this case \\(\\pu{cm s-1}\\)), the ratio is not a flow velocity in the normal sense of the word.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01603 "depsides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01603 "Intermolecular esters formed from two or more molecules of the same or different phenolic benzoic acids. Depending on the number of the component units, di-, tri-, etc. depsides may result, e.g. 4-O-galloylgallic acid:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01604 "depsipeptides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01604 "Natural or synthetic compounds having sequences of amino and hydroxy carboxylic acid residues (usually $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino}$ and \\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}hydroxy acids}), commonly but not necessarily regularly alternating. In cyclodepsipeptides, the residues are connected in a ring.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01605 "depth of penetration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01605 "The inverse of the absorption coefficient. If the decadic absorption coefficient, \\(a\\), is used, the depth of penetration (\\(\\frac{1}{a}\\)) is the distance at which the radiant power, \\(P_{\\lambda }\\), decreases to one tenth of its incident value, \\(P_{\\lambda }^{0}\\). If the Naperian absorption coefficient, \\(\\alpha \\), is used, the depth of penetration (\\(\\frac{1}{\\alpha } = \\beta \\) in this case) is the distance at which the radiant power decreases to \\(\\frac{1}{\\rm{e}}\\) of its incident value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01606 "depth profile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01606 "Dependence of concentration on depth perpendicular to the surface in a solid sample. It can be obtained by a simultaneous or sequential process of erosion and surface analysis or by measurement of the energy loss of primary backscattered particles produced by nuclear reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01607 "depth resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01607 "The distance between the 84 and 16 per cent levels of the depth profile of an element in a perfect sandwich sample with an infinitesimally small overlap of the components. These limits correspond to the 2\\(\\sigma \\)-value of the Gaussian distribution of the signal at the interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01609 "derivative potentiometric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01609 "A titration that involves measuring, recording, or computing the first derivative of the potential of a single indicator electrode with respect to the volume or otherwise added amount of reagent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01610))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01610 "derivative spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01610 """The first (second, ...) derivative absorption spectrum of a molecule is defined as the first (second, ...) derivative, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}A\\left(\\overset{˜}{
u }\\right)}{\\rm{d}\\overset{˜}{
u }}\\)[\\(\\frac{\\rm{d}^{2}A\\left(\\overset{˜}{
u }\\right)}{\\rm{d}\\overset{˜}{
u }^{2}}\\), ... ] of the absorbance \\(A\\) as a function of wavenumber, \\(\\overset{˜}{
u }\\). Wavelengths may be used in place of wavenumbers but the shape of the derivative spectra will be slightly different. When derivative spectra are obtained at low temperature, they are called first (second, ...) derivative low temperature absorption spectra (specifying the solvent, temperature and solute concentration).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01611 "derived coherent unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01611 "A unit constructed exclusively from base units, e.g. \\(\\pu{mol kg-1}\\), \\(\\pu{kg m-3}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01612 "derived kind of quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01612 "A kind of quantity characterized by an equation between base kinds of quantities, e.g. \\(\\rm{mass concentration} = \\rm{mass}\\ \\left(\\rm{length}\\right)^{-3}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01613))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01613 "derived non-coherent unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01613 "A unit constructed from base units and numerical factors, which may be named and symbolized, e.g. \\(\\mathrm{mg}\\), \\(\\pu{mol l-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01614 "derived quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01614 "Quantity defined, in a system of quantities, as a function of base quantities of that system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01615 "derived unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01615 "Unit of measurement of a derived quantity in a given system of quantities. Derived units are expressed algebraically in terms of base units by means of the mathematical symbols of multiplication and division.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01616 "descending elution/development")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01616 "A mode of operation in which the mobile phase is supplied to the upper edge of the paper or plate and the downward movement is governed mainly by gravity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01618 "desiccant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01618 "Drying agent.In agriculture, a substance used for drying up plants and facilitating their mechanical harvesting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01619 "desolvation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01619 "The process of evaporation of the solvent from an aerosol of fine droplets (the droplets being the result of nebulizing a solution). Desolvation converts the aerosol into a dry aerosol consisting of a suspension of solid or molten particles of the solute.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01620 "desorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01620 "The converse of adsorption, i.e. the decrease in the amount of adsorbed substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01621 "desorption by displacement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01621 "The process in which expulsion of a previously adsorbed component from the interfacial layer is effected by subsequent stronger adsorption of another component.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01622 "desulfurization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01622 "The process by which sulfur is removed from a material such as coal or oil. It may involve one of many techniques including elutriation, froth flotation, laundering, magnetic separation, chemical treatment, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01623 "desymmetrization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01623 "The modification of an object which results in the loss of one or more symmetry elements, such as those which preclude chirality (mirror plane, centre of inversion, rotation-reflection axis), as in the conversion of a prochiral molecular entity into a chiral one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01624 "desymmetrization step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01624 "The removal of the smallest possible number of symmetry elements from a molecule with or without the restriction to pointwise substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01625 "detachment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01625 "The reverse of an attachment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01626 "detailed balancing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01626 "When equilibrium is reached in a reaction system (containing an arbitrary number of components and reaction paths), as many atoms, in their respective molecular entities will pass forward, as well as backwards, along each individual path in a given finite time interval. Accordingly, the reaction path in the reverse direction must in every detail be the reverse of the reaction path in the forward direction (provided always that the system is at equilibrium). The principle of detailed balancing is a consequence for macroscopic systems of the principle of microscopic reversibility.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01626 "principle of detailed balancing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01627 "detection efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01627 "The ratio between the number of particles or photons detected and the number of similar particles or photons emitted by the radiation source.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01629 "detection limit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01629 "The minimum single result which, with a stated probability, can be distinguished from a suitable blank value. The limit defines the point at which the analysis becomes possible and this may be different from the lower limit of the determinable analytical range.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01643))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01643 "detergent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01643 "A surfactant (or a mixture containing one or more surfactants) having cleaning properties in dilute solutions (soaps are surfactants and detergents).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01644))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01644 "detoxification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01644 "Process, or processes, of chemical modification which make a toxic molecule less toxic.Treatment of patients suffering from poisoning in such a way as to promote physiological processes which reduce the probability or severity of harmful effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01645 "deuteriation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01645 "A term used to represent replacement of protium by deuterium. The use of the word deuteration is also acceptable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01648 "deuterium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01648 "A specific name for the atom $\\ce{^{2}H}$. The cation $\\ce{2H^{+}}$ is a deuteron the species $\\ce{2H^{−}}$ is a 'deuteride' anion and $\\ce{^{2}H}$ is the 'deuterio' group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01649))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01649 "deuteron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01649 "Nucleus of the $\\ce{^{2}H}$ (deuterium) atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01650 "deviation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01650 "The difference between an observed value and the arithmetic mean of the set to which it belongs. It can be calculated by the formula: @D01650-1@")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01651 "devolatilizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01651 "Material added to a sample to decrease its volatilization or that of some component of it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01652))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01652 "dew point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01652 "That temperature at which water vapour present in the atmosphere is just sufficient to saturate it. When air is cooled below the dew point, the excess of water vapour appears as tiny droplets or crystals of ice depending on the temperature of the air mass. This term is also applicable to acid gases as in the problem of acid corrosion and acid smut formation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01653 "dew point hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01653 "Instrument in which the sample is passed over a cooled surface. The temperature at which dew forms on the cooled surface is a function of the water content of the gas passing over the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01654 "Dexter excitation transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01654 "Excitation transfer occurring as a result of an electron exchange mechanism. It requires an overlap of the wavefunctions of the energy donor and the energy acceptor. It is the dominant mechanism in triplet-triplet energy transfer. The transfer rate constant, \\(k_{\\mathrm{ET}}\\), is given by: \\[k_{\\rm{ET}}\\propto \\frac{h}{2\\ \\pi }\\ P^{2}\\ J\\ \\rm{e}^{\\frac{-2\\ r}{L}}\\] where \\(r\\) is the distance between donor (D) and acceptor (A), \\(L\\) and \\(P\\) are constants not easily related to experimentally determinable quantities, and \\(J\\) is the spectral overlap integral. For this mechanism the spin conservation rules are obeyed.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01654 "electron exchange excitation transfer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01655))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01655 "dextrans")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01655 "Branched poly-$\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$-ᴅ-glucosides of microbial origin having glycosidic bonds predominantly $\\ce{C-1\\!\\rightarrow\\!C-6}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01656 "dextrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01656 "Poly-$\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$-ᴅ-glucosides of intermediate chain length derived from starch components (amylopectins) by the action of amylases (starch hydrolysing enzymes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01658 "diabatic coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01658 "Energy coupling between two potential-energy surfaces is sometimes known as diabatic coupling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01659 "diabatic electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01659 "Electron transfer process in which the reacting system has to cross over between different electronic surfaces in passing from reactants to products. For diabatic electron transfer the electronic transmission factor is ≪ 1 (see Marcus equation). The term 'non-adiabatic electron transfer' has also been used and is in fact more widespread, but should be discouraged because it contains a double negation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01659 "non-adiabatic electron transfer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01660 "diabatic photoreaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01660 "Within the Born–Oppenheimer approximation, a reaction beginning on one excited state 'potential-energy surface' and ending, as a result of radiationless transition, on another surface, usually that of the ground state. Also called non-adiabatic.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01660 "nonadiabatic photoreaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01661))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01661 "diacylamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01661 "Compounds having two acyl groups substituted on ammonia or a primary amine acyl-NR-acyl. They are also known as secondary amides and, especially the cyclic examples derived from diacids, as imides, e.g. $\\ce{[RC(=O)]2NR}$, $\\ce{RS(=O)2NHC(=O)R}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01663 "diagram level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01663 "A level described by the removal of one electron from the configuration of the neutral ground state. These levels form a spectrum similar to that of a one-electron or hydrogen-like atom but, being single-valency levels, have the energy scale reversed relative to that of single-electron levels. Diagram levels may be divided into valence levels and core levels according to the nature of the electron vacancy. Diagram levels with orbital angular momentum different from zero occur in pairs and form spin doublets.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01665 "dialysate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01665 "After (complete or incomplete) dialysis two solutions are obtained. The one free from colloidal material is called dialysate; the other one, containing the colloidal particles may be called retentate, dialysis residue, or simply residue, but should not be called dialysate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01665 "dialysis residue")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01665 "residue")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01666 "dialysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01666 "The process of separating a colloidal sol from a colloid-free solution by a membrane permeable to all components of the system except the colloidal ones, and allowing the exchange of the components of small molar mass to proceed for a certain time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01668 "diamagnetic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01668 "Substances having a negative magnetic susceptibility are diamagnetic. They are repelled out of a magnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01670 "diamidides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01670 "Analogues of acyclic carboxylic acid anhydrides in which $\\ce{=O}$ has been replaced by $\\ce{=NR}$ and $\\ce{–O–}$ by $\\ce{–NR–}$: $\\ce{RC(=NR)NRC(=NR)R}$, N-imidoyl amidines. The name 1,3,5-triazapentadienes is sometimes applied as a class name to diamidides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01671 "diamond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01671 "An allotropic form of the element carbon with cubic structure (space group \\(O_{h}^{7}- Fd3m\\)) which is thermodynamically stable at pressures above \\(6\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) at room temperature and metastable at atmospheric pressure. At low pressures diamond converts rapidly to graphite at temperatures above \\(1900\\ \\rm{K}\\) in an inert atmosphere. The chemical bonding between the carbon atoms is covalent with \\(\\mathrm{sp}^{3}\\) hybridization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01672))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01672 "diamond by CVD")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01672 "Diamond by CVD (chemical vapour deposition) is formed as crystals or as films from various gaseous hydrocarbons or other organic molecules in the presence of activated, atomic hydrogen. It consists of \\(\\rm{sp}^{3}\\)-hybridized carbon atoms with the three-dimensional crystalline structure of the diamond lattice.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01672 "CVD diamond")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01672 "low-pressure diamond")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01673 "diamond-like carbon films")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01673 "Diamond-like carbon (DLC) films are hard, amorphous films with a significant fraction of \\(\\mathrm{sp}^{3}\\)-hybridized carbon atoms and which can contain a significant amount of hydrogen. Depending on the deposition conditions, these films can be fully amorphous or contain diamond crystallites. These materials are not called diamond unless a full three-dimensional crystalline lattice of diamond is proven.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01674 "dianions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01674 "Molecular entities bearing two negative charges, which may be located on a single atom or on different atoms or may be delocalized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01675))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01675 "diastereoisomer excess")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01675 "This is defined by analogy with enantiomeric excess, as \\(D_{1}- D_{2}\\) [and the percent diastereoisomer excess as \\(100\\ (D_{1}-D_{2})\\)], where the mole fractions of two diastereoisomers in a mixture or the fractional yields of two diastereoisomers formed in a reaction are \\(D_{1}\\) and \\(D_{2}\\) (\\(D_{1}+D_{2}=1\\)). The term is not applicable if more than two diastereoisomers are present. Frequently this term is abbreviated to \\(\\rm{d.e.}\\)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01675 "diastereoisomeric excess")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01677))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01677 "diastereoisomeric units")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01677 "Two non-superposable configurational units that correspond to the same constitutional unit are considered to be diastereomeric if they are not mirror images.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01678))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01678 "diastereoisomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01678 "The interconversion of diastereoisomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01679 "diastereoisomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01679 "Stereoisomerism other than enantiomerism. Diastereoisomers (or diastereomers) are stereoisomers not related as mirror images. Diastereoisomers are characterized by differences in physical properties, and by some differences in chemical behaviour towards achiral as well as chiral reagents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01681 "diastereomeric ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01681 "This is defined by analogy with enantiomeric ratio as the ratio of the percentage of one diastereoisomer in a mixture to that of the other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01683 "diastereomorphism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01683 "The relationship between objects (or models) analogous to that between diastereoisomeric molecular entities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01685 "diastereotopic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01685 "Constitutionally equivalent atoms or groups of a molecule which are not symmetry related. Replacement of one of two diastereotopic atoms or groups results in the formation of one of a pair of diastereoisomers. In the example below the two hydrogen atoms of the methylene group C-3 are diastereotopic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01688 "diazenyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01688 "Radicals of structure $\\ce{RN=N^{.}}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01689))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01689 "diazoamino compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01689 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RN=N–NR2}$ (not all $\\ce{R = H}$, and one $\\ce{R}$ commonly aryl). In systematic nomenclature, diazoamino prefixed to the name of $\\ce{RH}$ names the compound $\\ce{RN=NNHR'}$ ($\\ce{R = R'}$), e.g. $\\ce{PhN=N–NPhMe}$, N-methyldiazoaminobenzene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01690))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01690 "diazoates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01690 "Salts $\\ce{RN=NO−M^{+}}$ ($\\ce{R}$ commonly aryl) of the compounds $\\ce{RN=NOH}$ (hydrocarbon)diazohydroxides, e.g. $\\ce{PhN=NO−K^{+}}$ potassium benzenediazoate. In the IUPAC 'Blue Book' these compounds are named as hydrocarbyldiazenolates and hydrocarbyldiazenols, respectively. E.g. $\\ce{PhN=NOH}$ phenyldiazenol. $\\ce{RN=NO−M^{+}}$ are also known by the non-IUPAC name diazotates.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01690 "diazotates")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01691 "diazo compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01691 "Compounds having the divalent diazo group, $\\ce{=N^{+}=N^{−}}$, attached to a carbon atom, e.g. $\\ce{CH2=N2}$ diazomethane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01692 "diazonium salts")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01692 "Compounds of structure $\\ce{RN2^{+}Y^{−}}$, in which $\\ce{R}$ is generally but not necessarily aryl, and the cations of which are usually formulated as $\\ce{RN^{+}\\!#N}$, E.g. $\\ce{PhN^{+}\\!#N}$ benzenediazonium chloride. They may also be named, from the canonical form $\\ce{RN=N^{+}}$, hydrocarbyldiazenylium salts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01693 "diazooxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01693 "Diazocyclohexadienones, which may also be considered as dipolar diazonio phenoxides, obtained by diazotizing aromatic primary amines having a hydroxy group in an ortho or para position. Also known as diazophenols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01695 "dicarbenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01695 "Species carrying two positive charges, formally located on tervalent carbon atoms, e.g. $\\ce{^{+}CH2–\\!^{+}CH2}$ ethane-1,2-diyl dication, $\\ce{^{+}CH2CH2–\\!^{+}CH2}$ propane-1,3-bis(ylium).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01696 "dichotomous sampler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01696 "Device for dividing a polydispersed aerosol particle population into two size fractions during sampling. The fractionation is based on the momentum differences of the particles which allow the larger particles to pass through a zone of stagnant gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01698))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01698 "dielectric polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01698 "The difference between the electric displacement vector and the product of the electric field strength with the permittivity of vacuum, \\(P = D - \\varepsilon_{0}\\ E\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01698 "dipole moment per volume")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01699 "dienes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01699 "Compounds that contain two fixed double bonds (usually assumed to be between carbon atoms). Dienes in which the two double-bond units are linked by one single bond are termed conjugated, e.g. $\\ce{CH2=CH–CH=CH2}$ buta-1,3-diene. Dienes in which the double bonds are adjacent are called cumulative, e.g. $\\ce{CH3–CH=C=CH2}$ buta-1,2-diene. Those in which one or more of the unsaturated carbon atoms is replaced by a heteroatom may be called heterodienes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01700 "dienophile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01700 "The olefin component of a Diels–Alder reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01701))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01701 "difference absorption spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01701 "A highly concentrated analyte in an analytical sample can be determined with better precision by replacing the blank (reference) cell by one containing a solution of the analyte or other absorber of known concentration; this is known as difference absorption spectroscopy. Difference spectra can also be obtained by computer or other subtraction methods.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01702 "differential capacitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01702 "Differential capacitance (per unit area of interphase or electrode; SI unit \\(\\pu{F m-2}\\)) is given by \\[C=(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial E})_{T,p,\\mu _{i},...}\\] where \\(E\\) is the potential of the electrode with respect to a reference electrode, \\(\\mu _{i}\\) is a set of chemical potentials which are held constant, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, \\(Q\\) is the electric charge (per unit area of interphase or electrode), and \\(p\\) is the pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01703 "differential detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01703 "A device which measures the instantaneous difference in the composition of the column effluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01704))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01704 "differential diffusion coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01704 """Defined by \\[D_{i} = \\frac{-J_{i}}{
abla c_{i}}\\] where \\(J_{i}\\) is the amount of species \\(i\\) flowing through unit area in unit time and \\(
abla c_{i}\\) is the concentration gradient of species \\(i\\). Different diffusion coefficients may be defined depending on the choice of the frame of reference used for \\(J_{i}\\) and \\(
abla c_{i}\\). For systems with more than two components, the flow of any component and hence its diffusion coefficient depends on the concentration distribution of all components. The limiting differential diffusion coefficient is the value of \\(D_{i}\\) extrapolated to zero concentration of the diffusing species: \\[[D_{i}]=\\lim _{c_{i}\\rightarrow 0}D_{i}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01705 "differential molar energy of adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01705 "When the addition of a differential amount \\(\\mathrm{d}n_{i}^{\\sigma }\\) or \\(\\mathrm{d}n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}\\) is effected at constant gas volume, the differential molar energy of adsorption of component \\(i\\), \\(\\Delta _{a}U_{i}^{\\sigma }\\) or \\(\\Delta _{a}U_{i}^{\\rm{s}}\\), is defined as: \\[\\Delta _{a}U_{i}^{\\sigma }=U_{i}^{\\sigma }- U_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\] or \\[\\Delta _{a}U_{i}^{\\rm{s}} = U_{i}^{\\rm{s}}- U_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\] where the differential molar surface excess energy, \\(U_{i}^{\\sigma }\\), is given by \\[U_{i}^{\\sigma }=(\\frac{\\partial U^{\\sigma }}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}})_{T,m,n_{j}^{\\sigma }}=(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\sigma }})_{T,m,V^{\\rm{g}},p_{i},n_{j}^{\\sigma }}\\] and the differential molar interfacial energy, \\(U_{i}^{s}\\), by \\[U_{i}^{\\rm{s}}=(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}})_{T,m,V^{\\rm{g}},p_{i},n_{j}^{\\sigma }}=(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}})_{T,m,V^{\\rm{g}},V^{\\rm{s}},p_{i},n_{j}^{\\rm{s}}}\\] \\(U_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\) is the differential molar energy of component \\(i\\) in the gas phase, i.e. \\[(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{g}}})_{T,V,n_{i}^{\\rm{g}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01708))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01708 "differential scanning calorimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01708 "A technique in which the difference in energy inputs into a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) and a reference material is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance and reference material are subjected to a controlled temperature programme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01709 "differential thermal analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01709 "A technique in which the temperature difference between a substance and a reference material is measured as a function of temperature, while the substance and reference material are subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01710 "differential viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01710 "The derivative \\(\\eta _{\\Delta }\\) of stress with respect to the rate of shear at a given shear rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01711 "diffraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01711 "A modification which light (or electron, neutron beams, etc.) undergoes in passing by the edges of opaque bodies or through narrow slits or in being reflected from ruled surfaces (or crystalline materials). The light waves, owing to their wave-like nature appear to be deflected and produce fringes of parallel light and dark bands corresponding to regions of constructive reinforcement or destructive interference, respectively, of the waves.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01712 "diffraction analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01712 "The application of diffraction techniques (X-rays, electrons, neutrons) which are sometimes used to identify the presence of certain solid aerosols and dust particles through the characteristic diffraction patterns which result from each unique crystal structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01713 "diffused junction semiconductor detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01713 "A semiconductor detector in which the p–n or n–p junction is produced by diffusion of donor or acceptor impurities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01714))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01714 "diffuse layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01714 "The region in which non-specifically adsorbed ions are accumulated and distributed by the contrasting action of the electric field and thermal motion. Counter and co-ions in immediate contact with the surface are said to be located in the Stern layer. Ions farther away from the surface form the diffuse layer or Gouy layer.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01714 "Gouy layer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01715 "diffuser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01715 "A porous plate or tube, commonly made of carborundum, alundum or silica sand, through which air is forced and divided into minute bubbles for diffusion in liquids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01716 "diffusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01716 "The spreading or scattering of a gaseous or liquid material. eddy diffusion in the atmosphere is the process of transport of gases due to turbulent mixing in the presence of a composition gradient. Molecular diffusion is the net transport of molecules which results from their molecular motions alone in the absence of turbulent mixing; it occurs when the concentration gradient of a particular gas in a mixture differs from its equilibrium value. eddy diffusion is the most important mixing process in the lower atmosphere, while molecular diffusion becomes significant at the lower pressures of the upper atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01717))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01717 "diffusional transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01717 "A transition that requires the rearrangement of atoms, ions or molecules in a manner that cannot be accomplished by a cooperative atomic displacement; it may require the movement of atoms, ions or molecules over distances significantly larger than a unit cell. Example: The transition of graphite (hexagonal sheets of three-coordinated carbon atoms) to diamond (infinite three-dimensional framework of four-coordinated carbon atoms) at high temperature and pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01718))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01718 "diffusion battery")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01718 "An aerosol sizing instrument for particles with diameters below \\(0.2\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\). The fractionation is based on different diffusivities of the small particles and their deposition on the walls of the long parallel or circular channels, formed by equally spaced plates, bundles of small bore parallel tubes or sets of stainless wire screens.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01719))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01719 "diffusion coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01719 """Proportionality constant \\(D\\), relating the flux of amount of entities B to their concentration gradient \\(J_{n}=- D\\ 
abla c_{\\rm{B}}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01722 "diffusion current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01722 "A faradaic current whose magnitude is controlled by the rate at which a reactant in an electrochemical process diffuses toward an electrode-solution interface (and, sometimes, by the rate at which a product diffuses away from that interface). For the reaction mechanism  there are two common situations in which a diffusion current can be observed. In one, the rate of formation of B from electroinactive C is small and the current is governed by the rate of diffusion of B toward the electrode surface. In the other, C predominates at equilibrium in the bulk of the solution, but its transformation into B is fast; C diffuses to the vicinity of the electrode surface and is there rapidly converted into B, which is reduced.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01722 "diffusion-controlled current")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01723 "diffusion current constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01723 "The empirical quantity defined by \\[i=\\frac{i_{\\rm{d},l}}{c_{\\rm{B}}\\ m^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\ t_{1}^{\\frac{1}{6}}}\\] the equation where \\(i_{\\rm{d},l}\\) = limiting diffusion current, \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\) = bulk concentration of the substance B whose reduction or oxidation is responsible for the wave in question, \\(m\\) = average rate of (mass) flow of mercury (or other liquid metal) and \\(t_{1}\\) = drop time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01725))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01725 "diffusion layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01725 "The region in the vicinity of an electrode where the concentrations are different from their value in the bulk solution. The definition of the thickness of the diffusion layer is arbitrary because the concentration approaches asymptotically the value \\(c_{0}\\) in the bulk solution (see diagram).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01725 "concentration boundary layer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01726 "diffusion layer thickness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01726 "Defined by the equation: \\[\\delta = \\frac{D}{k_{\\rm{d}}}=\\frac{n\\ F\\ D\\ c\\ A}{l_{i}}\\] where \\(D\\) is the diffusion coefficient, \\(k_{\\rm{d}}\\) is the heterogeneous diffusion rate constant, \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant, \\(c\\) is the concentration, \\(A\\) is the geometric area of the electrode, \\(l_{i}\\) is the limiting current and \\(n\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01728 "diffusionless transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01728 "A transition that does not involve long-range diffusion of atomic species over distances significantly greater than a typical interatomic distance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01729 "diffusion potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01729 """For an ideal dilute solution, \\(\\Delta \\mathit{\\Phi }_{\\rm{d}}\\) is the integral of \\(
abla \\mathit{\\Phi }\\) (given by the following equation) across the boundary between two regions of different concentrations. \\[
abla \\mathit{\\Phi }=\\frac{R\\ T\\ \\sum D_{i}\\ z_{i}\\ 
abla c_{i}}{F\\ \\sum s_{i}^{2}\\ D_{i}\\ c_{i}}\\] where \\(D_{i}\\) is the diffusion coefficient of species \\(i\\), \\(z_{i}\\) is the charge number of species \\(i\\), \\(c_{i}\\) is the concentration of species \\(i\\), \\(R\\) is the gas constant, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, and \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01730 "dihedral angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01730 "The angle between two intersecting planes on a third plane normal to the intersection of the two planes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01731 "diisotactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01731 "An isotactic polymer that contains two chiral or prochiral atoms with defined stereochemistry in the main chain of the configurational base unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01732 "dilatancy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01732 "An increase in volume of the system which usually accompanies shear thickening. This term has formerly been used for the phenomenon of shear thickening.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01733))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01733 "dilational transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01733 "A transition in which the crystal structure is dilated (or compressed) along one (or more) crystallographic direction(s) while the symmetry about that direction is retained. Examples:The transition of a $\\ce{CsCl}$-type structure to a rock salt structure in which dilation occurs along the three-fold axis.The transition at \\(T_{\\rm{D}}\\) in quenched $\\ce{NiS}$ in which volume expansion occurs without change of symmetry on going from a metallic state (\\(T > T_{\\rm{D}}\\)) to a semiconducting state (\\(T < T_{\\rm{D}}\\)).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01733 "dilatational transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01735))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01735 "diluent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01735 "The liquid or homogeneous mixture of liquids in which extractant(s) and possible modifier(s) may be dissolved to form the solvent phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01735 "cosolvent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01736 "diluent gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01736 "A gas of known quality introduced for analytical purposes so that it quantitatively lowers the concentration of the components of a gaseous sample; this may also be the complementary gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01738))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01738 "diluter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01738 "A device used to add a measured volume or amount of the sample to a measured volume of a diluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01739))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01739 "dilute solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01739 "Solution in which the sum of amount fractions of all the solutes is small compared to 1.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01740 "dilution rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01740 "The ratio of the ingoing volume flow rate (\\(\\frac{\\rm{d}V}{\\rm{d}t}\\)) and the culture volume (\\(V\\)). In continuous fermentation, a measure of a rate at which the existing medium is replaced with fresh medium, \\(D\\) is the reciprocal of the hydraulic retention time (\\(\\mathrm{HRT}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01741))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01741 "dimension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01741 "Product of powers of the base dimensions (the dimensions attributed to the base quantities of a system) which parallels the expression of the quantity in terms of the base quantities. When all the exponents of the base dimensions are zero the quantity has the dimension one and is often called dimensionless.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01742))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01742 "dimensionless quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01742 "Quantities of dimension one, or numerical quantities, are numbers which may be integers (or exceptionally half-integers) when obtained by counting (e.g. number of molecules, quantum numbers, ...), rational numbers such as ratios, factors and fractions when obtained as ratios of two quantities of the same kind or real numbers when obtained by taking logarithms (e.g. absorbance, power levels, ...).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01743))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01743 "dimeric ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01743 "An ion formed either when a chemical species exists in the vapour as a dimer and can be detected as such, or when a molecular ion can attach to a neutral molecule within the ion source to form an ion such as $\\ce{[M2]^{+.}}$ where $\\ce{M}$ represents the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01744 "dimerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01744 "The transformation of a molecular entity $\\ce{A}$ to give a molecular entity $\\ce{A2}$ . For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01745 "di-π-methane rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01745 "Photochemical reaction of a molecular entity comprising two $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}systems}$, separated by a saturated carbon atom (a 1,4-diene or an allyl-substituted aromatic analogue), to form an ene- (or aryl-) substituted cyclopropane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01746 "Dimroth–Reichardt <em>E</em><sub>T</sub> parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01746 """A measure of the ionizing power (loosely polarity) of a solvent, based on the maximum wavenumber of the longest wavelength electronic absorption band of:  in a given solvent. \\(E_{\\rm{T}}\\), called \\(E_{\\rm{T}}(30)\\) by its originators, is given by: \\[E_{\\rm{T}} = 2.859\\times 10^{-3}\\ 
u = 2.859\\times 10^{4}\\ \\lambda ^{-1}\\] where \\(E_{\\rm{T}}\\) is in \\(\\pu{kcal mol-1}\\), \\(
u \\) is in \\(\\pu{cm-1}\\) and \\(\\lambda \\) is in \\(\\rm{nm}\\). The so-called normalized \\(E_{\\rm{T}}^{\\rm{N}}\\) scale is defined as: \\[E_{\\rm{T}}^{\\rm{N}}=\\frac{E_{\\rm{T}}\\left(\\rm{solvent}\\right)- E_{\\rm{T}}\\left(\\rm{Si}\\rm{Me}_{4}\\right)}{E_{\\rm{T}}\\left(\\rm{water}\\right)- E_{\\rm{T}}\\left(\\rm{Si}\\rm{Me}_{4}\\right)}=\\frac{E_{\\rm{T}}\\left(\\rm{solvent}\\right)- 30.7}{32.4}\\]""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01746 "ET-value")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01747 "diode laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01747 "A solid state laser in which the active medium is the p-n junction between p-type and n-type semiconductor host materials. This term is preferred over the sometimes-used term 'semiconductor laser'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01747 "semiconductor laser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01748 "diols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01748 "Compounds that contain two hydroxy groups, generally assumed to be, but not necessarily, alcoholic. Aliphatic diols are also called glycols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01749 "diosphenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01749 "Cyclic $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}diketones}$, which exist predominantly in an enolic form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01750))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01750 "dioxin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01750 "Colloquial (short) name of a toxic by-product (and sometimes contaminant) of herbicides; the full name of this species is: 2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo[b,e][1,4]-dioxin.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01751 "dipolar aprotic solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01751 "A solvent with a comparatively high relative permittivity (or dielectric constant), greater than ca. 15, and a sizable permanent dipole moment, that cannot donate suitably labile hydrogen atoms to form strong hydrogen bonds, e.g. dimethyl sulfoxide. The term (and its alternative 'polar aprotic solvent') is a misnomer and is therefore discouraged. Such solvents are usually not aprotic but protophilic (and at most weakly protogenic). In describing a solvent it is better to be explicit about its essential properties, e.g. dipolar and non-protogenic.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01751 "polar aprotic solvent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01752 "dipolar bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01752 "A bond formed (actually or conceptually) by coordination of two neutral moieties, the combination of which results in charge-separated structures, e.g.  The term is preferred to the obsolescent synonyms 'coordinate link', 'coordinate covalence', 'dative bond', 'semipolar bond'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01753 "dipolar compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01753 """Electrically neutral molecules carrying a positive and a negative charge in one of their major canonical descriptions. In most dipolar compounds the charges are delocalized; however the term is also applied to species where this is not the case. 1,2-Dipolar compounds have the opposite charges on adjacent atoms. The term 1,3-dipolar compounds is used for those in which a significant canonical resonance form can be represented by a separation of charge over three atoms (in connection with 1,3-dipolar cycloadditions). Subclasses of 1,3-dipolar compounds include:
Allyl type $\\ce{X=Y^{+}-Z^{−}}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{−}-Y^{+}=Z}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{+}-Y-Z^{−}}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{−}=Y-Z^{+}}$ $(\\ce{X, Z = C, N, or O; Y = N or O})$See: azo imides, azomethine imides, azomethine ylides, azoxy compounds, carbonyl imides, carbonyl oxides, carbonyl ylides, nitro compounds, nitrones
Propargyl type $\\ce{X#N^{+}-Z^{−}}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{−}=N^{+}=Z}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{−}=N-Z^{+}}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X-N=Z}$ $(\\ce{X = C or O, Z = C, N, or O})$See: azides, diazo compounds, nitrile imides, nitrile oxides, nitrile ylides, nitrilium betaines
Carbene type $\\ce{X{:}-C=Z}$$\\ce{}$$\\ce{X^{+}=C-Z^{−}}$ $(\\ce{X = C or N; Z = C, N, or O})$See: acyl carbenes, betaines, imidoyl carbenes, vinyl carbenes""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01756 "dipolar mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01756 "Synonymous with Förster excitation transfer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01758 "dipole–dipole interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01758 "Intermolecular or intramolecular interaction between molecules or groups having a permanent electric dipole moment. The strength of the interaction depends on the distance and relative orientation of the dipoles. The term applies also to intramolecular interactions between bonds having permanent dipole moments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01760 "dipole length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01760 "Electric dipole moment divided by the elementary charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01763 "dipyrrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01763 "Compounds containing two pyrrole rings linked through a methine, $\\ce{–CH=}$, group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01765 "diradicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01765 "Molecular species having two unpaired electrons, in which at least two different electronic states with different multiplicities [electron-paired (singlet state) or electron-unpaired (triplet state)] can be identified, e.g. $\\ce{H2C^{.}–CH2–C^{.}H2}$ propane-1,3-diyl (trimethylene).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01766 "direct amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01766 "A type of amplification reaction where the constituent to be determined is amplified directly and it is this constituent which is finally measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01767 "direct current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01767 "This term and its symbol, \\(i_{\\mathrm{dc}}\\) or \\(I_{\\mathrm{dc}}\\), should be used (in preference to 'current' alone) only to denote the steady (time-dependent) component of a current that also has a periodic component.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01769 "direct fission yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01769 "The fraction of fissions giving rise to a particular nuclide before any nuclear decay has occurred.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01770 "direct-injection burner")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01770 "A burner in which oxidant and fuel emerge from separate ports and are mixed above the burner orifices through their turbulent motion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01771 "direct-injection enthalpimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01771 "An analytical method in which a reactant is injected into a calorimetric vessel containing another reagent. The enthalpy change of the ensuing reaction is measured and directly related to the amount of the limiting reagent (usually the analyte). If the experiment determines information other than amounts of analytes, an acceptable synonym is 'batch injection calorimetry'. Other terms which have been used in the literature but are not recommended include the 'concentration thermometric technique' and the 'direct thermometric method'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01771 "batch injection calorimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01771 "concentration thermometric technique")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01771 "direct thermometric method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01772 "direct isotope dilution analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01772 "Isotope dilution analysis used for the determination of a non-radioactive element with the aid of one of its radionuclides.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01772 "direct radiochemical isotope dilution analysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01773 "direct reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01773 "A chemical process in which the reactive complex has a lifetime that is shorter than its period of rotation. In a molecular-beam experiment the reaction products of a direct reaction are scattered, with reference to the centre of mass of the system, in preferred directions rather than at random. Some direct reactions are impulsive which means that there is an energy exchange that is very fast compared to the vibrational time scale. A direct reaction is to be contrasted with an indirect reaction, also known as a complex-mode reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01775 "direct transfer gas flow system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01775 "A system in which the purge gas transports the analyte directly to the sampling or excitation sources.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01776 "disaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01776 "Compounds in which two monosaccharides are joined by a glycosidic bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01777))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01777 "discomfort threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01777 "The lowest value (e.g. concentration of an impurity, etc.) at which a sensation of discomfort is perceived; a measure which varies from person to person.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01778 "discontinuous analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01778 "An analyser with at least one discontinuous subassembly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01779 "discontinuous indication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01779 "Indication related to the concentration during intervals of time which are not continuous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01780 "discontinuous measuring cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01780 "A measuring cell which operates intermittently and not necessarily at fixed time intervals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01782 "discontinuous precipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01782 "A diffusional reaction in a multi-component system in which structural and compositional changes occur in regions immediately adjacent to the advancing interface. The parent phase remains unchanged until swept over by the interface; the transition is complete in regions over which the interface has passed. Synonymous with nucleation and growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01783 "discontinuous simultaneous techniques")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01783 "The application of coupled techniques to the same sample when sampling for the second technique is discontinuous, e.g. discontinuous thermal analysis and gas chromatography, when discrete portions of evolved volatile(s) are collected from the sample situation in the instrument used for the first technique.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01785))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01785 "discriminator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01785 "A basic function unit comprising an electronic circuit which gives an output pulse for each input pulse whose amplitude lies above a given threshold value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01787 "disintegration energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01787 "Energy liberated in a decay process and carried as kinetic energy of the emitted alpha or beta particle and the recoil energy of the product atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01790 "disjoining pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01790 "The force \\(\\mathit{\\Pi} _{\\rm{d}}\\) per unit area which can be obtained as the derivative of \\(-g_{\\rm{t}}\\) with respect to the distance, where \\(g_{\\rm{t}}\\) is the total Gibbs energy of interaction per unit area of each of the two flat and parallel plates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01792 "dispenser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01792 "A device used to deliver a measured amount of material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01793 "dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01793 "Dispersion of a material \\(= \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n}{{\\rm{d}}\\lambda}\\) where \\(n\\) = refractive index and \\(\\lambda \\) wavelength; angular dispersion \\(= \\frac{{\\rm{d}}\\mathit{\\varPhi}}{{\\rm{d}}\\lambda}\\) where \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) = angle; and linear dispersion \\(= \\frac{{\\rm{d}}x}{{\\rm{d}}\\lambda}\\) where \\(x\\) = separation of spectral lines. The reciprocal of the last-named quantity is more frequently used \\(\\left(\\frac{{\\rm{d}}\\lambda}{{\\rm{d}}x}\\right)\\), and is commonly expressed in \\(\\pu{\\unicode{x212B} mm-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01794 "dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01794 "The dilution of a pollutant by spreading in the atmosphere due to diffusion or turbulent action (eddy diffusion).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01797))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01797 "displacement chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01797 "A procedure in which the mobile phase contains a compound (the displacer) more strongly retained than the components of the sample under examination. The sample is fed into the system as a finite slug.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01798 "displacive transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01798 "A transition in which a displacement of one or more kinds of atoms or ions in a crystal structure changes the lengths and/or directions of bonds, without severing the primary bonds. Examples: The transitions of the low-temperature polymorphs of $\\ce{SiO2}$ (quartz, tridymite and cristobalite) to their respective high-temperature polymorphs, which involve distortions or rotations of the $\\ce{SiO4}$ tetrahedra. Also Jahn–Teller and ferroic transitions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01799))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01799 "disproportionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01799 "Any chemical reaction of the type , where A, A' and A'' are different chemical species. For example:  The reverse of disproportionation is called comproportionation. A special case of disproportionation (or 'dismutation') is 'radical disproportionation', exemplified by:  Reactions of the more general type:  are also loosely described as 'radical disproportionations'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01799 "dismutation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01801))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01801 "dissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01801 "The separation of a molecular entity into two or more molecular entities (or any similar separation within a polyatomic molecular entity). Examples include unimolecular heterolysis and homolysis, and the separation of the constituents of an ion pair into free ions.   The separation of the constituents of any aggregate of molecular entities.   In both senses dissociation is the reverse of association.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01802 "dissociation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01802 "Energy required to dissociate a molecule into two parts. Subscripts 0 and e, i.e. \\(D_{0}\\) and \\(D_{\\rm{e}}\\), are used to denote the dissociation from the ground state and potential energy minimum, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01803))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01803 "dissociative adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01803 "Adsorption with dissociation into two or more fragments, both or all of which are bound to the surface of the adsorbent. The process may be homolytic, as in the chemisorption of hydrogen: \\[\\rm{H}_{2}+2\\rm{*}\\rightarrow 2\\rm{H}^{\\rm{*}}\\] where * represents a surface site, or heterolytic, as in: \\[\\rm{H}_{2}+\\rm{M}^{n+}+\\rm{O}^{2-}\\rightarrow \\rm{H}^{-}\\rm{M}^{n+}+\\rm{HO}^{-}\\] where \\(\\rm{M}^{n+}\\) and \\(\\rm{O}^{2- }\\) are surface sites in which the ions are of lower coordination than the ions in the bulk phase. Associative desorption is the reverse of dissociative adsorption.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01803 "dissociative chemisorption")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01804 "dissociative ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01804 "An ionization process in which a gaseous molecule decomposes to form products, one of which is an ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01805))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01805 "dissociative surface reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01805 "Dissociative surface reaction and its reverse, associative surface reaction, are defined by: \\[2^{\\rm{*}}+\\rm{C}_{2}\\rm{H}_{5}^{\\rm{*}}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm{H}^{\\rm{*}}+\\,^{\\rm{*}}{\\rm{CH}_{2}\\rm{CH}_{2}}^{\\rm{*}}\\] where * represents a surface site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01806 "dissolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01806 "The mixing of two phases with the formation of one new homogeneous phase (i.e. the solution).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01808))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01808 "dissymmetry of scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01808 "The ratio of two Rayleigh ratios for different angles of observation, i.e. \\[z(\\theta _{1},\\, \\theta _{2}) = \\frac{R(\\theta _{1})}{R(\\theta _{2})}\\] \\[\\theta _{1} < \\theta _{2}\\] The angles must be specified; in light scattering it is customary to let \\(\\theta = 1 - \\theta _{1}\\) and, most frequently, \\(\\theta _{1} = 45 ^{\\circ}\\) and \\(\\theta _{2} = 135 ^{\\circ}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01809 "distonic radical cation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01809 "A radical cation in which charge and radical sites are separated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01810 "distribuend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01810 "The substance that is distributed between two immiscible liquids or liquid phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01811 "distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01811 "The apportionment of a solute between two phases. The term partition or extraction may also be used in this sense where appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01814))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01814 "distribution constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01814 "The concentration of a component in or on the stationary phase divided by the concentration of the component in the mobile phase. Since in chromatography a component may be present in more than one form (e.g. associated and dissociated forms), the analytical condition used here refers to the total amount present without regard to the existence of various forms. These terms are also called the distribution coefficients. However, the present term conforms more closely to the general usage in science.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01815))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01815 "distribution function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01815 "A normalized function giving the relative amount of a portion of a polymeric substance with a specific value, or a range of values, of a random variable or variables.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01817 "distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01817 "The ratio of the total analytical concentration of a solute in the extract (regardless of its chemical form) to its total analytical concentration in the other phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01817 "distribution coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01817 "extraction coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01817 "scrubbing coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01817 "stripping coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01818 "disyndiotactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01818 "A syndiotactic polymer that contains two chiral or prochiral atoms with defined stereochemistry in the main chain of the configurational base unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01819 "ditactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01819 "A tactic polymer that contains two sites of defined stereoisomerism in the main chain of the configurational base unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01820))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01820 "diterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01820 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C20}$ skeleton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01822 "diurnal variation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01822 "Indicates variations which follow a distinctive pattern which recurs with a daily cycle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01823 "dividing surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01823 "A surface, usually taken to be a hyperplane, constructed at right angles to the minimum-energy path on a potential-energy surface. In conventional transition-state theory it passes through the highest point on the minimum-energy path. In generalized versions of transition-state theory the dividing surface can be at other positions; in variational transition-state theory the position of the dividing surface is varied so as to get a better estimate of the rate constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01824 "DM-interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01824 "Interference in which the analytical signal is influenced by a different mechanism than the analyte e.g. the formation of non-dissociating species in emission spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01826 "DNA probe")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01826 "A short sequence of DNA labelled isotopically or chemically that is used for the detection of a complementary nucleotide sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01827 "Dobson unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01827 "Unit sometimes used in the description of the total ozone in a column of air overhead. It is given as the thickness (in units of \\(10^{-3}\\ \\rm{cm}\\) or \\(10^{-5}\\ \\rm{m}\\)) of that layer which would be formed by the total ozone in a column reduced to the pressure of \\(760\\ \\rm{Torr}\\) and temperature of \\(0\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01828 "<i>dodecahedro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01828 "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a dodecahedron with triangular faces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01829 "dolichols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01829 "A group of prenol derivatives in which \\(n\\) in the general formula $\\ce{H–[CH2–C(CH3)=CH–CH2]_{n}–OH}$ is greater than four and in which the residue that carries the hydroxy group is saturated, i.e. 2,3-dihydropolyprenols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01830))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01830 "Donnan emf")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01830 "The potential difference \\(E_{\\rm{D}}\\) at zero electric current between two identical salt bridges, usually saturated $\\ce{KCl}$ bridges (conveniently measured by linking them to two identical electrodes) inserted into two solutions in Donnan equilibrium.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01830 "Donnan potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01831 "Donnan equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01831 "The equilibrium characterized by an unequal distribution of diffusible ions between two ionic solutions (one or both of the solutions may be gelled) separated by a membrane which is impermeable to at least one of the ionic species present, e.g. because they are too large to pass through the pores of the membrane. The membrane may be replaced by other kinds of restraint, such as gelation, the field of gravity, etc., which prevent some ionic components from moving from one phase to the other, but allow other components to do so.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01833 "donor number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01833 "A quantitative measure of Lewis basicity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01834 "doping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01834 "The action of adding a small amount of foreign atoms to form a solid solution in the lattice of a non-metallic catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01835 "Doppler broadening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01835 "Broadening due to the random motion of the emitting or absorbing atoms. A Doppler broadened line has a Gaussian shape.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01837 "dosage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01837 "As applied to an air pollutant in an exposure chamber, dosage is commonly defined as the concentration of the pollutant times the duration of exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01839 "dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01839 "A general term denoting the quantity of radiation or energy absorbed. For special purposes, it must be appropriately qualified e.g. absorbed, maximum permissible, mean lethal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01841 "dose equivalent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01841 "Product of the absorbed dose of ionizing radiation, a quality factor and a modifying factor to take into account the effect of radiation on different biological tissues.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01842 "double-beam spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01842 "Double (spectral) beam spectrometers are used where two samples are to be excited by two different wavelengths. A double- (synchronous) beam spectrometer is a luminescence spectrometer in which both the excitation and emission monochromators scan the excitation and emission spectra simultaneously, usually with a fixed wavelength difference between excitation and emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01843))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01843 "double escape peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01843 "In a gamma-ray spectrum, the peak due to pair production in the detector and escape, from the sensitive part of the detector, of two photons of \\(511\\ \\rm{keV}\\) resulting from annihilation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01844 "double-focusing mass spectrograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01844 "An instrument which uses both direction and velocity focusing, and therefore an ion beam initially diverging in direction and containing ions of different kinetic energies is separated into beams according to the quotient mass/charge, these beams being focused on to a photographic plate or film.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01845 "double-focusing mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01845 "An instrument which uses both direction and velocity focusing, and therefore an ion beam of a given mass/charge is brought to a focus when the ion beam is initially diverging and contains ions of the same mass and charge with different translational energies. The ion beam is measured electrically.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01847 "double-layer current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01847 "The non-faradaic current associated with the charging of the electrical double layer at an electrode-solution interface, given by: \\[i_{\\mathrm{DL}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}(\\sigma \\ A)}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] where \\(\\sigma \\) = surface charge density of the double layer, \\(A\\) = area of the electrode-solution interface and \\(t\\) = time. Capital letters should be used as subscripts to avoid the possibility of confusing this symbol with that for the limiting diffusion current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01849))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01849 "double-strand chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01849 "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through three or four atoms, two on one side and either one or two on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01850 "double-strand copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01850 "A copolymer the macromolecules of which are double-strand macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01851 "double-strand macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01851 "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through three or four atoms, two on one side and either one or two on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01852 "double-strand polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01852 "A polymer the macromolecules of which are double-strand macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01853))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01853 "doublet state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01853 "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01854 "double-wavelength spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01854 "The effect of spectral background due to impurities, solvent or radiation scattering may be reduced if the difference in the absorbances of a sample measured at two selected wavelengths is obtained. This is often achieved by repetitively switching from one wavelength to the other. Double-wavelength spectroscopy does this automatically by allowing two beams of radiation of different wavelengths to pass through the cell. One beam is fixed at a longer wavelength and the other measures absorbance while being scanned over a limited wavelength range at shorter wavelengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01855))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01855 "doubling time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01855 "The time (\\(\\min \\), \\(\\mathrm{h}\\)) required for a cell population to double either the number of cells or the active cell mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01857 "down-time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01857 "The loss of time that should be available for analysis. This might be due to breakdown, maintenance or other factors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01858 "downwash")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01858 "As applied to the action of chimney gases, it is the downward motion of the chimney gases brought on by eddies which form in the lee of a chimney when the wind is blowing. It may result in bringing the flue gases to the ground prematurely.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01859 "drift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01859 "A slow non-random change in signal with time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01860 "driving force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01860 "The decrease in Gibbs energy on going from the reactants to the products of a chemical reaction (\\(-\\Delta G\\)).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01860 "affinity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01861 "droplet")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01861 "A small liquid particle. The size of droplets encountered in the atmosphere extends over a wide range; e.g. liquid aerosol solutions which make up the fine particle fraction of continental tropospheric aerosol are usually \\(2\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter. Cloud water droplets usually have diameters in the range of \\(5{-}70\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\), while rain droplets commonly have diameters ranging from \\(0.1{-}3\\ \\rm{mm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01862))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01862 "drop time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01862 "The time that elapses between the instants at which two successive drops of liquid metal are detached from the tip of the capillary.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01863 "drug")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01863 "Any substance which when absorbed into a living organism may modify one or more of its functions. The term is generally accepted for a substance taken for a therapeutic purpose, but is also commonly used for abused substances. Synonymous with medicine, pharmaceutical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01864))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01864 "dry bulb temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01864 "In psychrometry, the temperature of the gas measured by a dry bulb thermometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01865 "drying agent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01865 "A substance which removes water (liquid or gaseous).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01866 "dual substituent-parameter equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01866 "In a general sense, any equation which expresses substituent effects in terms of two parameters. However, in practice the term is used more specifically for an equation for summarizing the effects of meta- or para- substituents (i = m or p) $\\ce{\\textbf{X}}$ on chemical reactivity, spectroscopic properties, etc. of a probe site $\\ce{\\textbf{Y}}$ in benzene or other aromatic system. \\[P^{\\rm{i}} = \\rho_{I}^{\\rm{i}}\\ \\sigma_{I} + \\rho_{R}^{\\rm{i}} \\sigma_{R}\\] \\(P\\) is the magnitude of the property $\\ce{\\textbf{Y}}$ for substituent $\\ce{\\textbf{X}}$, expressed relative to the property for $\\ce{X} = \\ce{H}$; \\(\\sigma _{I}\\) and \\(\\sigma _{R}\\) are inductive or polar and resonance substituent constants, respectively, there being various scales for \\(\\sigma _{R};\\,\\rho _{I}\\) and \\(\\rho _{R}\\) are the corresponding regression coefficients.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01867))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01867 "dust")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01867 "Small, dry, solid particles projected into the air by natural forces, such as wind, volcanic eruption and by mechanical or man-made processes such as crushing, grinding, milling, drilling, demolition, shoveling, conveying, screening, bagging and sweeping. Dust particles are usually in the size range from about \\(1{-}100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter and they settle slowly under the influence of gravity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01868 "dust collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01868 "A device for monitoring dust emissions. Also the equipment used to remove and collect dust from process exhaust gases; this may employ simply sedimentation (dustfall jars, coated slides, papers, etc.), inertial separation (cyclones, impactors, impingers, etc.), precipitation (thermal and electrostatic) or filtration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01869 "dust fall")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01869 "Solid particles in the air which fall to the ground under the influence of gravity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01870 "dye laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01870 "A CW or pulsed source of coherent radiation in which the active medium is usually a solution of a fluorescent organic molecule (the dye) pumped with a suitable pump laser or with a flash lamp. These lasers can be tuned over a large part of the fluorescence band of the dye.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01871 "dynamic fields mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01871 "A mass spectrometer in which the separation of a selected ion beam depends essentially on the use of fields, or a field, varying with time. These fields are generally electric.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01871 "dynamic field mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01871 "dynamic fields mass spectrometer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01874 "dynamic range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01874 "The ratio between the maximum usable indication and the minimum usable indication (detection limit). A distinction may be made between the linear dynamic range, where the response is directly proportional to concentration, and the dynamic range where the response may be non-linear, especially at higher concentrations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01875 "dynamic surface tension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01875 "In certain circumstances, one may measure surface tensions that are different from the equilibrium value. Such a surface tension is called the dynamic surface (or interfacial) tension (\\(\\gamma ^{\\rm{dyn}}\\) or \\(\\sigma ^{\\mathrm{dyn}}\\)). The equilibrium value is then called the static surface (or interfacial) tension (\\(\\gamma ^{\\rm{st}}\\) or \\(\\sigma ^{\\mathrm{st}}\\)). The modifying signs may be omitted if there is no danger of ambiguity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01875 "dynamic interfacial tension")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01876 "dynamic thermomechanometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01876 "A technique in which the dynamic modulus and/or damping of a substance under oscillatory load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01877))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01877 "dynamic viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01877 "For a laminar flow of a fluid the ratio of the shear stress to the velocity gradient perpendicular to the plane of shear.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:D01877 "viscosity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01878 "dyne")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01878 "cgs unit of force, \\(\\rm{dyn} = 10^{-5}\\ \\rm{N}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01879))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01879 "dyotropic rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01879 "An uncatalysed process in which two $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ simultaneously migrate intramolecularly, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:D01880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:D01880 "dypnones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:D01880 "1,3-Diphenylbut-2-en-1-one, $\\ce{PhC(=O)CH=C(CH3)Ph}$, and its ring-substituted derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06810 "dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06810 "Total quantity of a substance administered to, taken up, or absorbed by an organism, organ, or tissue.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06866 "disruptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06866 "A chemical group used to disrupt the linearity of the backbone of molecules MCPLCs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06874 "downstream")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06874 "Side of a membrane from which permeate emerges.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06875 "dynamic membrane formation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06875 "Process in which an active layer is formed on the membrane surface by the deposition of substances contained in the fluid being treated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06889 "Donnan exclusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06889 "Reduction in concentration of mobile ions within an ion exchange membrane due to the presence of fixed ions of the same sign as the mobile ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06906 "dose-response and dose-effect relationships")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06906 "The graph of the relation between dose and the proportion of individuals responding with an all-or-none effect; it is essentially the graph of the probability of an occurrence (or the proportion of a population exhibiting an effect) against dose. Typical examples of such all-or-none effects are mortality or the incidence of cancer. The dose-effect curve is the graph of the relation between dose and the magnitude of the biological change produced measured in appropriate units. It applies to measurable changes giving a graded response to increasing doses of a drug or xenobiotic. It represents the effect on an individual animal or person, when biological variation is taken into account. An example is the increased effect of lead on the haem synthesis, e.g., on activity of the enzyme 6-amino laevulinic acid dehydratase in blood serum or coproporphyrin levels in urine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06932 "distribution isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06932 "The equilibrium relation between the concentration of a sample component in the stationary phase \\(c_{\\rm{S}}\\), and in the mobile phase \\(c_{\\rm{M}}\\), expressed as a function \\(c_{\\rm{S}}=f(c_{\\rm{M}})\\). In some versions of chromatography, a distribution isotherm can be seen as a partition isotherm, an adsorption isotherm, or a combination of these, depending on the mechanism of separation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:DT06932 "extraction isotherm")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06957))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06957 "discriminant analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06957 "Discriminant analysis is a statistical technique to find a set of descriptors which can be used to detect and rationalize separation between activity classes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06958))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06958 "docking studies")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06958 "Docking studies are computational techniques for the exploration of the possible binding modes of a substrate to a given receptor, enzyme or other binding site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06959 "three-dimensional quantitative structure–activity relationships")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06959 "Three-dimensional quantitative structure-activity relationships (3D-QSAR) involve the analysis of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a set of compounds and their three-dimensional properties using statistical correlation methods.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT06960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT06960 "drug design")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT06960 "Drug design includes not only ligand design, but also pharmacokinetics and toxicity, which are mostly beyond the possibilities of structure- and/or computer-aided design. Nevertheless, appropriate chemometric tools, including experimental design and multivariate statistics, can be of value in the planning and evaluation of pharmacokinetics and toxicological experiments and results. Drug design is most often used instead of the correct term 'Ligand Design'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07018))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07018 "degenerate orbitals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07018 "Orbitals whose energy levels are equal in the absence of external fields. Any linear combination of the functions corresponding to a given set of degenerate orbitals serves as an equivalent representation of this set of orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07019 "delocalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07019 "Redistribution of the valence-shell electron density throughout a molecular entity as compared with some localized models (individual atoms in their valence states, separated bonds or fragments). Different topological modes of the electron delocalization include: ribbon delocalization of either $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ (i.e., electrons occupying respectively $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbitals}$)surface delocalization of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ occurring through an overlap of radially oriented $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ of a cyclic molecule, as is the case of cyclopropane; andvolume delocalization of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ through an overlap of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ directed inside a molecular polyhedron, as is the case in tetrahedrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07020))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07020 "delocalization energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07020 "The difference between the actual $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ energy of a molecular entity and the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ energy of a hypothetical species with a localized form of the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system. These energies are normally evaluated within Hückel molecular orbital theory.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07021 "dynamic reaction path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07021 "A classical trajectory method based on molecular orbital calculations which determines atomic accelerations, velocities and positions using the energy gradient, and does not require prior knowledge of the potential energy surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07203))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07203 "dopant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07203 "Charge-transfer agent used to generate, by oxidation or reduction, positive or negative charges in an intrinsically conducting polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07204))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07204 "doping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07204 "Oxidation or reduction process brought about by a dopant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07352 "DAS")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07352 "Acronym for decay-associated spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07353 "Dauben–Salem–Turro rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07353 "Set of rules proposed in 1975 to classify photochemical reactions based on correlation diagrams and under the assumption that all photochemical processes are controlled by the generation of primary products with characteristics of diradicals, i.e., considering the physical fact that the initial single excitation separates two electrons of a pair into two distinct orbitals. The reactions are classified according to their topicity, i.e., the total number and nature of available radical sites generated in the primary photochemical product.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07354))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07354 "de Mayo reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07354 "Photochemical process leading to the formation of cyclobutanols by cycloaddition of an alkene to a 1,3-dicarbonyl-enol. The enol originates in most cases from the tautomerization of a 1,3-dicarbonyl compound. The products of the de Mayo reaction are susceptible to undergo a retro-aldol ring opening resulting in the formation of 1,5-dicarbonyl compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07356))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07356 "dichroic mirror")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07356 "A mirror used to reflect light selectively according to its wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07357 "dichroism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07357 "Dependence of absorbance of a sample on the type of polarization of the measuring beam. This polarization may be linear, corresponding to linear dichroism (LD) in which the difference in absorption for two perpendicularly linearly polarized beams is measured, \\(\\Delta A_{\\rm{l}} = A_{\\rm{Z}} - A_{\\rm{Y}}\\), or circular dichroism (CD) in which the difference in absorption for left minus right circularly polarized beams is measured, \\(\\Delta A_{\\rm{C}} = A_{\\rm{L}} - A_{\\rm{R}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07358 "diradicaloid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07358 "Diradical-like. Often used to refer to a species in which the two radical centres interact significantly.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:DT07358 "biradicaloid")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07360))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07360 "di-π-silane rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07360 "Silicon version of the di-π-methane rearrangement with a $\\ce{Si}$ atom at position 3 of the basic 1,4-diene chromophore.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07361 "dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07361 "Energy or amount of photons absorbed per volume (or per mass) by an irradiated object during a particular exposure time. SI units are \\(\\pu{J m-3}\\) or \\(\\pu{J g-1}\\) and \\(\\pu{mol m-3}\\) or \\(\\pu{mol g-1}\\), respectively. Common units are \\(\\pu{einstein m-3}\\) or \\(\\pu{einstein g-1}\\), respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07362))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07362 "downconversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07362 """Process by which a photon with frequency \\(
u _{1}\\) interacts with a non-linear medium and splits into two simultaneously emitted photons with frequencies \\(
u _{2}\\) and \\(
u _{3}\\) so that the energy is conserved: \\[
u _{1}=
u _{2}+
u _{3}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07363))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07363 "driving force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07363 "Term widely used to indicate the negative of the standard Gibbs energy change for outer-sphere electron transfer (\\(\\Delta _{\\rm{ET}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07364))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07364 "Drude–Nernst equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07364 "Equation describing the contraction (\\(\\Delta V_{\\rm{el}}\\)) taking place in a dielectric medium of relative static permittivity \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\) (formerly called dielectric constant) upon introduction of an ion of charge number \\(z\\) and radius \\(r\\): \\[\\Delta V_{\\rm{el}} = -\\frac{\\left ( z\\,e \\right )^{2}}{2\\,r\\,\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}}\\frac{\\partial\\left ( \\rm{ln}\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\right)}{\\partial\\,p}\\] with \\(e\\) the elementary charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07365 "dual fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07365 "Systems or molecular species showing two fluorescence bands. Several non-trivial molecular properties may be at the basis of a dual fluorescence, such as conformational equilibrium in the ground state and a mixture of excited states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07366 "dual-mode photochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07366 "Photochromism occurring in complex systems and triggered alternatively by two different external stimuli, such as light and an electric current. In such a case photochromism and 'electrochromism' are mutually regulated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07534 "drying control chemical additive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07534 "Co-solvent included to facilitate the rapid drying of gels without cracking.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:DT07618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:DT07618 "densification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:DT07618 "Removal of impurities and the elimination of pores from a xerogel to give a material of as near bulk density as possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E00008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E00008 "α-expulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E00008 "A general reaction by which a group attached to the alpha carbon of an excited chromophore is expelled either as an odd electron species or as an anionic species. This reaction should be distinguished from an α-(alpha-)cleavage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01882))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01882 "<i>E</i>, <i>Z</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01882 """The approved stereodescriptors of stereoisomeric alkenes $\\ce{R1R2C=CR3R4}$ ($\\ce{R1} 
eq \\ce{R2} , \\ce{R3} 
eq \\ce{R4}$); neither $\\ce{R1}$ nor $\\ce{R2}$ need be different from $\\ce{R3}$ or $\\ce{R4}$), cumulenes $\\ce{R1R2C(=C=C)_{n}=CR3R4}$ and related systems e.g. $\\ce{R1R2C=NOH}$, $\\ce{HON=C\\{[CH2]_{n}\\}2C=NOH}$. The group of highest CIP priority attached to one of the terminal doubly bonded atoms of the alkene, oxime, etc. or cumulene (i.e. $\\ce{R1}$ or $\\ce{R2}$) is compared with the group of highest precedence attached to the other (i.e. $\\ce{R3}$ or $\\ce{R4}$). The stereoisomer is designated as $Z$ (zusammen = together) if the groups lie on the same side of a reference plane passing through the double bond and perpendicular to the plane containing the bonds linking the groups to the double-bonded atoms; the other stereoisomer is designated as $E$ (entgegen = opposite). The descriptors may be applied to structures with a fractional bond order between one and two; and to double bonds involving elements other than carbon. They are not used to describe ring substitution relationships.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01884 "eclipsed")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01884 "Two atoms or groups attached to adjacent atoms are said to be eclipsed if the torsion angle between the three bonds is zero (or approximately so).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01886))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01886 "eclipsing strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01886 "Intramolecular strain present due to nonbonded interactions between two eclipsed atoms or groups. It is, for example, one of the interactions responsible for the existence of a rotational barrier and hence restricted rotation about the $\\ce{C–C}$ bond in ethane. It has also been called Pitzer strain, torsional strain and bond opposition strain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01886 "bond opposition strain")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01886 "Pitzer strain")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01886 "torsional strain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01888))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01888 "eddy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01888 "In turbulent fluid motion, a blob of the fluid that has some definitive character and moves in some way differently from the main flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01889 "eddy dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01889 "The process by which substances are mixed in the atmosphere or in any fluid system due to eddy motion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01889 "diffusion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01890))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01890 "educt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01890 "Term used mainly in the German literature for starting material (reactant). It should be avoided in English, because there it means 'something that comes out' and not 'something that goes in'. The German use of the term is in fact also incorrect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01891 "effective cadmium cut-off energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01891 "In a given experimental configuration, the energy value determined by the condition that the detector response would be unchanged if the cadmium cover surrounding the detector was replaced by a fictitious cover opaque to neutrons with energy below this value and transparent to neutrons with energy above this value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01892 "effective charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01892 "Change in effective charge is a quantity obtained by comparison of the polar effect of substituents on the free energies of rate or equilibrium processes with that on a standard ionization equilibrium. Provided the effective charges on the states in the standard equilibrium are defined, then it is possible to measure effective charges for states in the reaction or equilibrium under consideration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01893 "effective chimney height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01893 "A height used for the purposes of calculating the dispersion of emitted gases from a chimney, and which differs from the real chimney height by an amount which depends on such factors as the exit velocity, buoyancy effects and wind speed; it may also be affected by the local topography. It denotes the maximum height of the centre of a plume path above the level of the ground. The effective height may be above or below the actual chimney height, although the former case is most common.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01893 "stack height")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01894 "effective half life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01894 "For a radioactive substance, the time required for the amount of that substance in a biological system to be reduced to one half of its value by both radioactive decay and biological processes, when the rate of removal is approximately exponential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01895 "effectively infinite thickness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01895 "Solid or liquid samples of one gram or more are generally used in X-ray spectroscopy. If the sample is thick enough so that the intensity of characteristic fluorescent radiation is not significantly modified by an increase in thickness, the sample is described as a bulk sample of effectively infinite thickness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01896 "effective molarity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01896 "The ratio of the first-order rate constant of an intramolecular reaction involving two functional groups within the same molecular entity to the second-order rate constant of an analogous intermolecular elementary reaction. This ratio has the dimension of concentration. The term can also apply to an equilibrium constant.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01896 "effective concentration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01898 "effective theoretical plate number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01898 "A number indicative of column performance when resolution is taken into account: \\[N=\\frac{16\\ R_{\\rm{s}}^{2}}{(1- \\alpha )^{2}}\\] where \\(R_{\\rm{s}}\\) is the peak resolution and \\(\\alpha \\) is the separation factor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01899 "effective thermal cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01899 "A fictitious cross-section for a specified reaction, which, when multiplied by the 2200 metre-per-second flux density, gives the correct reaction rate for thermal neutrons.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01899 "Westcott cross-section")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01901))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01901 "effector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01901 "A small molecule which increases (activator) or decreases (inhibitor) the activity of an (allosteric) protein by binding to the protein at the regulatory site (which is different from the substrate-binding catalytic site).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01902 "efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01902 "The ratio between the useful energy delivered or bound and the energy supplied, i.e. energy output/energy input. It is also used in the sense of a quantitative measure of the relative rate of a given step involving a species with respect to the sum of the rates of all of the parallel steps which depopulate that species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01906 "efficiency of atomization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01906 "The ratio of the amount of analyte that passes through the flame cross-section at the observation height, as free neutral (or ionized) atoms, to the amount of analyte aspirated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01907 "efficiency of nebulization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01907 "The substance fraction of component entering the flame in the amount of component consumed. The efficiency of nebulization is related to the amount of component and not to the amount of solvent. It cannot be calculated directly from the ratio: volume rate of sample drained from the spray chamber divided by volume rate of fluid consumed. Corrections usually have to be made to take account of differences in component concentrations in the drained and consumed solutions, respectively, because of evaporation of some solvent from mist droplets deposited on the walls.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01908 "efficiency spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01908 "A plot of the efficiency of a step (\\(\\eta \\)) against wavelength or photon energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01909 "efflorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01909 "The reverse of deliquescence: the drying of a salt solution when the vapour pressure of water in the saturated solution of a substance is greater than the partial pressure of water in the ambient air. Also refers to the loss of water of crystallization from a solid salt such as $\\ce{Na2CO3.10H2O}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01910))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01910 "effluent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01910 "Any spent liquors or other waste materials which are emitted by a source (waste from plating shops, pickling tanks, sewage treatment plants, chemical manufacturing plants, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01911 "effluent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01911 "The mobile phase leaving the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01913))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01913 "eighteen-electron rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01913 "An electron-counting rule to which an overwhelming majority of stable diamagnetic transition metal complexes adhere. The number of nonbonded electrons at the metal plus the number of electrons in the metal-ligand bonds should be 18. The 18 electron rule in transition metal chemistry is a full analogue of the 'Lewis octet rule'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01914 "einstein")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01914 "One mole of photons. Although widely used, it is not an IUPAC sanctioned unit. It is sometimes defined as the energy of one mole of photons. This use is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01915 "elastic collision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01915 "A particle (atom or molecule) can undergo a change in its state of excitation as a result of collisions with other particles. In an elastic collision an exchange only of kinetic energy takes place between the colliding species; in an inelastic collision there is an interchange between the kinetic energy and the internal energy of the particle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01916))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01916 "elastic scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01916 "When in a molecular collision there is no transfer of energy among different degrees of freedom, there is said to be elastic scattering.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01917 "elastic scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01917 "Radiation may be scattered by its transmission through a medium containing particles. If the scatter results in no significant change in the wavelength relative to the primary radiation it is called elastic scattering. In cases where the scattering centres are small compared to the wavelength of the radiation the elastic scattering is called Rayleigh scattering and Mie scattering if this condition is not fulfilled.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01918 "electrical arc")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01918 "A self-sustaining electrical discharge between at least two electrodes, characterized by a comparatively small cathode fall voltage, a low burning voltage and a relatively high current density. The burning voltage of an arc is the voltage across the electrode gap during an arc discharge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01921))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01921 "electrical hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01921 "A hygrometer whose sensitive element has electrical properties which vary with the humidity of the gas which traverses the hygrometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01923 "electric charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01923 "Integral of the electric current over time. The smallest electric charge found on its own is the elementary charge, \\(e\\), the charge of a proton.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01923 "charge")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01925))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01925 "electric conductance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01925 "Reciprocal of the electric resistance.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01925 "conductance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01927))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01927 "electric current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01927 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01928 "electric current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01928 "A vector quantity the scalar product of which with the cross sectional area vector is equal to the electric current. By magnitude it is the electric current divided by the cross sectional area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01929 "electric dipole moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01929 "Vector quantity, the vector product of which with the electric field strength, \\(E\\), of a homogeneous field is equal to the torque. \\(T=p\\times E\\). The direction of the dipole moment is from the negative to the positive charge.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01929 "dipole moment")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01930 "electric displacement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01930 "Product of the permittivity and electric field strength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01931))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01931 "electric field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01931 "Force acting on a charge divided by the charge.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01931 "electric field strength")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01932 "electric mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01932 "Speed of ions divided by the electric field strength in the phase concerned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01933 "electric polarizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01933 "Tensor quantity relating the induced electric dipole moment, \\(p_{\\rm{i}}\\), to the applied electric field strength, \\(E\\), \\(p_{\\rm{i}}=\\alpha \\ E\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01934 "electric potential difference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01934 "Difference in the potentials of electrodes on the right and left of a galvanic cell. When \\(\\Delta V\\) is positive, positive charge flows from left to right through the cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01935))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01935 "electric potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01935 "At a point, the work required to bring a charge from infinity to that point in the electric field divided by the charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01936))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01936 "electric resistance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01936 "To direct current, the potential difference divided by the current when there is no electromotive force in the conductor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01938))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01938 "electric sector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01938 "An arrangement of two conducting sheets forming a capactor and giving a radial electrostatic field which is used to deflect and focus ion beams of different energies. The capacitor may, for example, be cylindrical, spherical or toroidal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01939))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01939 "electrified interphase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01939 "An interphase between phases containing free charged components which are usually accumulated or depleted in the surface region thus giving rise to net charges on the phase. This definition includes the special case when the net charge on each of the phases reduces to zero. Charged components may or may not cross the interface between two phases, so that interphases may be divided into the limiting types unpolarizable and polarizable, respectively. Ideally unpolarizable interphases are those for which the exchange of common charged particles between the phases proceeds unhindered. Ideally polarizable interphases are those for which there are no common components between the phases or the exchange of these is hindered. This condition may arise as a result of the equilibrium conditions or from the kinetics of charge transfer and leads to an interphase which is impermeable to electric charge. Real interphases may approach more or less well one of these idealized cases. Polarisibility or non-polarisibility is not an absolute property of an interphase but depends on a number of conditions, e.g. time scale of the experiment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01940 "electroactive substance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01940 "In voltammetry and related techniques, a substance that undergoes a change of oxidation state, or the breaking or formation of chemical bonds, in a charge-transfer step. If an electroactive substance B is formed, in the solution or electrode, by a chemical reaction from another substance C, the substance C should be called the precursor of B. In potentiometry with ion-selective electrodes, a material containing, or in ion-exchange equilibrium with, the sensed ion. The electroactive substance is often incorporated in an inert matrix such as poly(vinyl chloride) or silicone rubber.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01940 "depolarizer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01941 "electrocapillarity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01941 "The dependence of the interfacial tension on the electrical state of the interphase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01942 "electrocapillary equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01942 "A form of the Gibbs adsorption equation which includes an expression of the phenomenon of electrocapillarity: \\[s\\ \\mathrm{d}T- \\tau \\ \\mathrm{d}p+\\mathrm{d}\\gamma +\\sigma ^{\\alpha }\\ \\mathrm{d}E+\\sum \\mathit{\\Gamma }_{j}\\ \\mathrm{d}\\mu _{j}=0\\] where \\(s\\) is the surface excess of entropy of unit area of interphase, \\(T\\) is the temperature, \\(\\tau \\) is the thickness or excess volume of unit area of the interphase, \\(p\\) is the external pressure, \\(\\gamma \\) is the interfacial tension, \\(\\sigma ^{\\alpha }\\) is the free surface charge density on phase \\(\\alpha \\) (areal amount of charge on the surface of phase \\(\\alpha \\)), \\(E\\) is the generalized potential, \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{j}\\) is the surface excess, \\(\\mu _{j}\\) is the chemical potential and \\(j\\) is an electrically neutral component of one or other of the phases; the sum is over all the components but one in each phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01943 "electrochemical detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01943 "An electrochemical cell which responds to certain substances in the carrier gas eluting from the column. The electrochemical process may be an oxidation, reduction or a change in conductivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01944 "electrochemical method of detection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01944 "A method in which either current or potential is measured during an electrochemical reaction. The gas or liquid containing the trace impurity to be analysed is sent through an electrochemical cell containing a liquid or solid electrolyte and in which an electrochemical reaction specific to the impurity takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01945 "electrochemical potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01945 "Of a substance in a specified phase, the partial molar Gibbs energy of the substance at the specified electric potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01948 "electrocyclic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01948 "A molecular rearrangement that involves the formation of a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between the termini of a fully conjugated linear $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system (or a linear fragment of a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system) and a decrease by one in the number of , or the reverse of that process. For example:  The stereochemistry of such a process is termed 'conrotatory' or antarafacial if the substituents at the interacting termini of the conjugated system both rotate in the same sense, e.g.  or 'disrotatory' (or suprafacial) if one terminus rotates in a clockwise and the other in a counter-clockwise sense, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01950 "electrodecantation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01950 "Electrodialysis may lead to local differences in concentration and density. Under the influence of gravity these density differences lead to large scale separation of sols of high and of low (often vanishingly low) concentrations. This process is called electrodecantation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01950 "electrophoresis convection")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01952 "electrode current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01952 """If the charging current is negligible, in the case of a single electrode reaction, the electrode current density (\\(\\rm{c.d.}\\)) of the electric current flowing through the electrode is related to the flux density of a species B by the equation: \\[j = n\\ 
u _{\\rm{B}}^{-1}\\ F\\ \\left(N_{\\rm{B}}\\right)_{e}\\] where \\(\\left(N_{\\rm{B}}\\right)_{e}\\) is the normal component of the vector \\(N_{\\rm{B}}\\) at the electrode-solution interface, \\(n\\) is the charge number of the electrode reaction and \\(
u _{\\rm{B}}\\) is the stoichiometric number of species B. The ratio \\(\\frac{n}{
u _{\\rm{B}}}\\) is to be taken as positive if the species B is consumed in a cathodic reaction or produced in an anodic reaction. Otherwise it is to be taken as negative. With the convention that the normal distance vector points into the electrolytic solution, a cathodic current is then negative, an anodic current positive.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01955))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01955 "electrodeposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01955 "The deposition of dissolved or suspended material by an electric field on an electrode. (Includes electro-crystallization).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01956 "electrode potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01956 "Electromotive force of a cell in which the electrode on the left is a standard hydrogen electrode and the electrode on the right is the electrode in question.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01959 "electrode process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01959 "The totality of changes occurring at or near a single electrode during the passage of current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01960 "electrode reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01960 "For an electrode process at a given potential the current is controlled by the kinetics of a number of steps which include the transport of reactants to and from the interface and the interfacial reaction itself. The latter, which is called the electrode reaction, must always include at least one elementary step in which charge is transferred from one phase to the other, but may also involve purely chemical steps within the interfacial region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01961 "electrode reaction rate constants")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01961 """The electrode reaction rate constants are related to the partial currents by \\[k_{\\mathrm{ox}}=\\frac{l_{\\rm{a}}}{n\\ F\\ A\\ \\Pi\\: c_{i}^{
u _{i}}}\\] \\[k_{\\mathrm{red}}=\\frac{l_{\\rm{c}}}{n\\ F\\ A\\ \\Pi\\:c_{i}^{
u _{i}}}\\] where \\(k_{\\mathrm{ox}}\\) and \\(k_{\\mathrm{red}}\\) are the rate constants for the oxidizing (anodic) and reducing (cathodic) reactions respectively, \\(n\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction, \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant and \\(A\\) is the geometric area of the electrode, the product \\(\\Pi\\:c_{i}^{
u _{i}}\\) includes all the species \\(i\\) which take part in the partial reaction, \\(c_{i}\\) is the volume concentration of species \\(i\\) and \\(
u _{i}\\) is the order of the reaction with respect to species \\(i\\). The conditional rate constant of an electrode reaction is the value of the electrode reaction rate constant at the conditional (formal) potential of the electrode reaction. When \\(\\alpha \\) the transfer coefficient is independent of potential, \\[k_{\\rm{c}} = \\frac{k_{\\rm{ox}}}{\\rm{e}^{\\frac{\\alpha _{\\rm{a}}\\ (E- E_{\\rm{c}}^{0'})\\ n\\ F}{
u \\ R\\ T}}} = \\frac{k_{\\rm{red}}}{\\rm{e}^{\\frac{-\\alpha _{\\rm{c}}\\ (E- E_{\\rm{c}}^{0'})\\ n\\ F}{
u \\ R\\ T}}}\\] where \\(\\alpha _{\\rm{a}}\\) and \\(\\alpha _{\\rm{c}}\\) are the anodic and cathodic transfer coefficients respectively, \\(E\\) is the electric potential difference, \\(E_{\\rm{c}}\\) is the conditional (formal) potential, \\(
u \\) is the stoichiometric number, \\(R\\) is the gas constant and \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature. Similar rate constants can be defined using activities in place of concentrations in the first two equations, and the standard electrode potential in place of the conditional potential in the latter two equations. This type of rate constant is called the standard rate constant of the electrode reaction. The observable electrode rate constant is the constant of proportionality expressing the dependence of the rate of the electrode reaction on the interfacial concentration of the chemical species involved in the reaction.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01965 "electrofuge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01965 "A leaving group that does not carry away the bonding electron pair. For example, in the nitration of benzene by $\\ce{NO2^{+}}$, $\\ce{H^{+}}$ is the electrofuge. The adjective of electrofuge is electrofugal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01966 "electrogenerated chemiluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01966 "Luminescence produced by electrode reactions. Also called electroluminescence or electrochemiluminescence.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01966 "electrochemiluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01966 "electroluminescence")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01967 "electrographite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01967 "A synthetic graphite made by electrical heating of graphitizable carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01968 "electrokinetic potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01968 "Potential drop across the mobile part of the double layer, that is responsible for electrokinetic phenomena. \\(\\zeta \\) is positive if the potential increases from the bulk of the liquid phase towards the interface. In calculating the electrokinetic potential from electrokinetic phenomena it is often asumed that the liquid adhering to the solid wall and the mobile liquid are separated by a sharp shear plane. As long as there is no reliable information on the values of the permittivity and the viscosity in the electrical double layer close to the interface, the calculation of the electrokinetic potential from electrokinetic experiments remains open to criticism. It is therefore essential to indicate in all cases which equations have been used in the calculation of \\(\\zeta \\). It can be shown, however, that for the same assumptions about the permittivity and viscosity all electrokinetic phenomena must give the same value for the electrokinetic potential.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01968 "ζ-potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01972 "electrolytic hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01972 "Hygrometer using a hygroscopic substance (for example, diphosphorus pentaoxide, $\\ce{P2O5}$) which is transformed into an electrolyte (phosphoric acid, $\\ce{H3PO4}$) in contact with the moisture in the gas. The electrolyte (phosphoric acid) is electrolysed continuously and the electrolysis current is measured. At a constant flow of the gas to be analysed, the electrolysis current is a linear function of the water concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01973 "electromeric effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01973 "A molecular polarizability effect occurring by an intramolecular electron displacement (sometimes called the 'conjugative mechanism' and, previously, the 'tautomeric mechanism') characterized by the substitution of one electron pair for another within the same atomic octet of electrons. It can be indicated by curved arrow symbolizing the displacement of electron pairs, as in:  which represents the hypothetical electron shift  The term has been deemed obsolescent or even obsolete (see mesomeric effect, resonance effect). It has long been custom to use phrases such as 'enhanced substituent resonance effect' which imply the operation of the electromeric effect, without using the term, and various modern theoretical treatments parametrize the response of substituents to 'electronic demand', which amounts to considering the electromeric effect together with the inductomeric effect.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01973 "tautomeric effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01974 "electromotive force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01974 "Energy supplied by a source divided by the electric charge transported through the source. For a galvanic cell it is equal to the electric potential difference for zero current through the cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01975))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01975 "electron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01975 "Elementary particle not affected by the strong force having a spin quantum number \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\), a negative elementary charge and a rest mass of \\(0.000\\ 548\\ 579\\ 903(13)\\ \\rm{u}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01975 "negaton")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01976 "electron acceptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01976 "A substance to which an electron may be transferred; for example 1,4-dinitrobenzene or the dication 1,1'-dimethyl-4,4'-bipyridyldiium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01977 "electron affinity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01977 "Energy required to detach an electron from the singly charged negative ion (energy for the process ). The equivalent more common definition is the energy released (\\(E_{\\rm{initial}} - E_{\\rm{final}}\\)) when an additional electron is attached to a neutral atom or molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01979 "electron attachment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01979 "The transfer of an electron to a molecular entity, resulting in a molecular entity of (algebraically) increased negative charge. It is not an attachment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01980 "electron capture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01980 "A nuclear transformation in which the nucleus captures an orbital electron. Often the shell, from which the electron is captured, is indicated. (K-, L-, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01981 "electron capture detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01981 "A small radioactive source containing $\\ce{^{3}H}$ or $\\ce{^{63}Ni}$ ionizes the molecules of the carrier gas (nitrogen or argon–methane), and a potential difference creates a small current. This current is reduced when an electronegative substance (such as a halocarbon) is introduced. The reduction in current is a measure of the concentration of the electronegative substance. The detection limit (threshold) varies greatly according to the substances to be analysed and can reach a mixing ratio of \\(10^{-12}\\). The linear dynamic range may be \\(10^{4}\\) but the maximum measuring value generally lies below \\(1\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01982 "electron charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01982 "The negative electric charge which appears on the electron or univalent ions [ \\(1.602\\times 10^{-19}\\) coulombs or \\(4.803\\times 10^{-10}\\) electrostatic units (\\(\\mathrm{esu}\\))].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01984 "electron correlation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01984 "The adjustment of electron motion to the instantaneous (as opposed to time-averaged) positions of all the electrons in a molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01985 "electron-deficient bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01985 "A single bond between adjacent atoms that is formed by less than two electrons, as in $\\ce{B2H6}$:  The $\\ce{B–H–B}$ bonds are called 'two-electron three-centre bonds'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01986 "electron density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01986 "If \\(P(x,\\,y,\\,z)\\ \\rm{d}x\\ \\rm{d}y\\ \\rm{d}z\\) is the probability of finding an electron in the volume element \\(\\rm{d}x\\ \\rm{d}y\\ \\rm{d}z\\) at the point of a molecular entity with coordinates \\(x,\\,y,\\,z\\), then \\(P(x,\\,y,\\,z)\\) is the electron density at this point. For many purposes (e.g. X-ray scattering, forces on atoms) the system behaves exactly as if the electrons were spread out into a continuously distributed charge. The term has frequently been wrongly applied to negative charge population.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01987 "electron detachment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01987 "The reverse of an electron attachment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01988 "electron donor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01988 "A molecular entity that can transfer an electron to another molecular entity, or to the corresponding chemical species.[obsolete] A Lewis base. This use is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01990 "electronegativity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01990 "Concept introduced by L. Pauling as the power of an atom to attract electrons to itself. There are several definitions of this quantity. According to Mulliken it is the average of the ionization energy and electron affinity of an atom, but more frequently a relative scale due to Pauling is used where dimensionless relative electronegativity differences are defined on the basis of bond dissociation energies, \\(E_{\\rm{d}}\\), expressed in electronvolts: \\[\\chi _{\\rm{r}}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)- \\chi _{\\rm{r}}\\left(\\rm{B}\\right)=\\left(\\rm{eV}\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\ \\sqrt{E_{\\rm{d}}\\left(\\rm{AB}\\right)- \\frac{1}{2}\\ [E_{\\rm{d}}\\left(\\rm{AA}\\right)+E_{\\rm{d}}\\left(\\rm{BB}\\right)]}\\] The scale is chosen so as to make the relative electronegativity of hydrogen \\(\\chi _{\\rm{r}} = 2.1\\). The sign of the square root was chosen intuitively by Pauling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01991 "electron energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01991 "The potential difference through which electrons are accelerated before they are used to bring about electron ionization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01992 "electroneutrality principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01992 "The principle expresses the fact that all pure substances carry a net charge of zero.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01993 "electron exchange excitation transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01993 "Synonymous with Dexter excitation transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01994 "electronically excited state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01994 "A state of an atom or molecular entity which has greater electronic energy than the ground state of the same entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01996 "electronic effect of substituents: symbols and signs")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01996 "The inductive effect has universally been represented by the symbol \\(\\rm{I}\\). This is now commonly taken to include both through-bonds and through-space transmission, but \\(\\rm{I}\\) is also used specifically for through-bonds transmission; through-space transmission is then symbolized as \\(\\rm{F}\\) (for field effect). The symbols for the influence of substituents exerted through electron delocalization have variously been \\(\\rm{M}\\) (mesomeric), \\(\\rm{E}\\) (electromeric), \\(\\rm{T}\\) (tautomeric), \\(\\rm{C}\\) (conjugative), K (konjugativ) and \\(\\rm{R}\\) (resonance). Since the present fashion is to use the term resonance effect, \\(\\rm{R}\\) is the most commonly used symbol, although \\(\\rm{M}\\) is still seen quite often. Both the possible sign conventions are in use. The Ingold sign convention associates electronegativity (relative to hydrogen atom) with a negative sign, electropositivity with a positive sign. Thus the nitro group is described as electronwithdrawing by virtue of its \\(-\\rm{I}\\) and \\(-\\rm{M}\\) effects; chloro is described as a \\(-\\rm{I}\\), \\(+\\,\\rm{M}\\) substituent, etc. For correlation analysis and linear free-energy relationships this convention has been found inconvenient, for it is in contradiction to the sign convention for polar substituent constants ($\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}constants}$). Authors concerned with these fields often avoid this contradiction by adopting the opposite sign convention originally associated with Robinson, for electronic effects. This practice is almost always associated with the use of R for the electron delocalization effect: thus the nitro group is a \\(+\\,\\rm{I}\\), \\(+\\,\\rm{R}\\) substituent; chloro a \\(+\\,\\rm{I}\\), \\(-\\rm{R}\\) substituent, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01997 "electronic energy migration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01997 "The movement of electronic excitation energy from one molecular entity to another of the same species, or from one part of a molecular entity to another of the same kind (e.g. excitation migration between the chromophores of an aromatic polymer). The migration can happen via radiative or radiationless processes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01997 "electronic energy hopping")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01997 "energy migration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E01999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E01999 "electron ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E01999 "This is the term used to describe ionization of any species by electrons. The process may, for example, be written:   for atoms or molecules: \\[\\rm{M} + e \\rightarrow \\rm{M}^{+\\cdot } + 2e\\]   for radicals: \\[\\rm{M}^{\\cdot}  + e \\rightarrow \\rm{M}^{+} + 2e\\]   The term 'electron impact' should not be used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01999 "electron impact ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E01999 "electron impact")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02000))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02000 "electron jump")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02000 "The term can be applied to a reaction in which there is an electron transfer with the formation of an ionic intermediate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02001))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02001 "electron kinetic energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02001 "The difference between the energy of the exciting photon and the ionization energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02003 "electron-pair acceptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02003 "A synonym for Lewis acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02004 "electron-pair donor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02004 "A synonym for Lewis base.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02005 "electron paramagnetic resonance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02005 "Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) and/or electron spin resonance (ESR) is defined as the form of spectroscopy concerned with microwave-induced transitions between magnetic energy levels of electrons having a net spin and orbital angular momentum. The term electron paramagnetic resonance and the symbol EPR are preferred and should be used for primary indexing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02006 "electron probe microanalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02006 "General term for methods using bombardment of a solid specimen by electrons which generate a variety of signals providing the basis for a number of different analytical techniques.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02007 "electron probe X-ray microanalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02007 "Any analytical technique which uses the excitation and evaluation of the characteristic X-ray spectrum of a solid specimen by a focused electron beam (typically of a diameter of less than \\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)). Qualitative and quantitative X-ray analysis is the main feature of EPXMA. For obtaining supplementary information the secondary, back scattered and absorbed electrons are also frequently observed. Qualitative and quantitative X-ray analysis is, however, the main feature of EPXMA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02008 "electron rest mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02008 "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of mass, \\(m_{\\rm{e}} = \\pu{9.1093897(54)E-31 kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02009 "electron spectroscopy for chemical analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02009 "Type of spectroscopy involving the measurement of the kinetic energy of electrons emitted by chemical substances usually as a result of excitation by monochromatic X-rays.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02010 "electron stopping power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02010 "When a beam of electrons strikes a target or specimen there are three ways in which the electrons may lose energy (low energy collisions, X-ray production, and formation of a spectral continuum). The average energy loss per unit distance travelled along the electron path is called electron stopping power, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}E}{\\rm{d}x}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02011 "electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02011 "The transfer of an electron from one molecular entity to another, or between two localized sites in the same molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02012 "electron-transfer catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02012 "The term indicates a sequence of reactions such as shown in equations (1)–(3), leading from $\\ce{A}$ to $\\ce{B}$:  \\[\\rm{A} + \\rm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\rm{A}^{\\cdot- }\\]$\\quad$(1)\\[\\rm{A}^{\\cdot- } \\rightarrow \\rm{B}^{\\cdot- }\\]$\\quad$(2)\\[\\rm{B}^{\\cdot- } + \\rm{A} \\rightarrow \\rm{B} + \\rm{A}^{\\cdot- }\\]$\\quad$(3) An analogous sequence involving radical cations ($\\ce{A^{+.}}$, $\\ce{B^{+.}}$) is also observed. The most notable example of electron-transfer catalysis is the \\(\\rm{S}_{\\rm{RN}}1\\) (or \\(\\rm{T}+\\rm{D}_{\\rm{N}}+\\rm{A}_{\\rm{N}}\\)) reaction of aromatic halides. The term has its origin in a suggested analogy to acid-base catalysis, with the electron instead of the proton. However, there is a difference between the two catalytic mechanisms, since the electron is not a true catalyst, but rather behaves as the initiator of a chain reaction. 'Electron-transfer induced chain reaction' is a more appropriate term for the situation described by equations (1)–(3).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02013))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02013 "electron transfer photosensitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02013 "Photochemical process in which a reaction of a non-absorbing substrate is induced by electron transfer (not energy transfer) with an excited light-absorbing sensitizer. The overall process must be such that the sensitizer is recycled. Depending on the action of the excited sensitizer as electron donor or acceptor the sensitization is called reductive or oxidative.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02014 "electronvolt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02014 "Non-SI unit of energy, \\(\\rm{eV}\\approx 1.602\\ 176\\ 487(40)\\times 10^{-19}\\ \\rm{J}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02015 "electron work function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02015 "The minimum work needed to extract electrons from the Fermi level of a metal M across a surface carrying no net charge. It is equal to the sum of the potential energy and the kinetic Fermi energy taken with the reverse sign: \\[\\mathit{\\Phi}^{M} = -(V_{\\rm{e}} + \\varepsilon_{\\rm{e}}^{F})\\] where \\(V_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the potential energy for electrons in metals and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{F}}\\) is the kinetic energy of electrons at the Fermi level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02017 "electro-osmotic pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02017 "The pressure difference \\(\\Delta p\\) across the membrane, plug, etc., needed just to stop electro-osmotic volume flow. \\(\\Delta p\\) is positive if the higher pressure is on the high potential side.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02019 "electro-osmotic volume flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02019 "The volume flow per unit time per unit field strength through the whole plug.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02020))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02020 "electrophile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02020 "An electrophile (or electrophilic reagent) is a reagent that forms a bond to its reaction partner (the nucleophile) by accepting both bonding electrons from that reaction partner. An 'electrophilic substitution reaction' is a heterolytic reaction in which the reagent supplying the entering group acts as an electrophile. For example:  Electrophilic reagents are Lewis acids. 'Electrophilic catalysis' is catalysis by Lewis acids. The term 'electrophilic' is also used to designate the apparent polar character of certain radicals as inferred from their higher relative reactivities with reaction sites of higher electron density.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02020 "electrophilic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02021 "electrophilicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02021 "The property of being electrophilic (see electrophile).The relative reactivity of an electrophilic reagent. (It is also sometimes referred to as 'electrophilic power'.) Qualitatively, the concept is related to Lewis acidity. However, whereas Lewis acidity is measured by relative equilibrium constants, electrophilicity is measured by relative rate constants for reactions of different electrophilic reagents towards a common substrate (usually involving attack at a carbon atom).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02022 "electrophoresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02022 "The motion of colloidal particles in an electric field. The term 'cataphoresis' should be abandoned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02024 "electrophoretic mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02024 "The observed rate of migration of a component (\\(v\\)) divided by electric field strength (\\(E\\)) in a given medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02025 "electrophotography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02025 "Processes of photoimaging which are based on photo-induced changes of electric fields (photo-conductive or photo-electrostatic effects).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02027 "electrostatic filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02027 "Filters for which an electrostatic charge is applied to the filter element. A fibrous filter material is often pleated in between V-shaped supports consisting of electrostatically charged, metal rods which are insulated from the supporting frame.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02028 "electrostatic precipitator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02028 "A device which separates particles from a gas stream by passing the carrier gas between pairs of electrodes across which a unidirectional, high-voltage potential is placed. The particles are charged before passing through the field and migrate to an oppositely charged electrode. These devices are very efficient collectors of small particles, and their use in removing particles from power plant plumes and in other industrial applications is widespread.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02029 "electrothermal atomizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02029 "A device which is heated to the temperature required for analyte atomization by the passage of electrical current through its body. In open atomizers the sample vapour, together with analyte atoms, leaving the atomizer surface may move freely into the surrounding space. In confined atomizers the sample vapour is restricted by the atomizer wall except for the openings where the optical beam enters and leaves the atomizer and the sample introduction hole.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02030 "electroviscous effects")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02030 "For dispersions of charged particles, these are those components of the viscosity connected with the charge of the particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02032 "elementary charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02032 "The elementary charge, \\(e\\), is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units, the SI, with \\(e = \\pu{1.602176634E-19 C}\\). The elementary charge \\(e\\) corresponds to a coupling strength of the electromagnetic force via the fine-structure constant and is considered as a fundamental constant of nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02033 "elementary entity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02033 "Any countable object or event, but usually a molecule, an ion or a specified group of atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02034 "elementary particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02034 "A particle in which, at the present, no structure can be observed at moderate energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02035 "elementary reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02035 "A reaction for which no reaction intermediates have been detected or need to be postulated in order to describe the chemical reaction on a molecular scale. An elementary reaction is assumed to occur in a single step and to pass through a single transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02036 "element effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02036 "The ratio of the rate constants of two reactions that differ only in the identity of the element of the atom in the leaving group, e.g. \\(\\frac{k_{\\rm{Br}}}{k_{\\rm{Cl}}}\\). As for isotope effects, a ratio of unity is regarded as a 'null effect'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02038))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02038 "elimination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02038 "The reverse of an addition reaction or transformation. In an elimination two groups (called eliminands) are lost most often from two different centres (1/2/elimination or 1/3/elimination, etc.) with concomitant formation of an unsaturation in the molecule (double bond, triple bond) or formation of a new ring. If the groups are lost from a single centre ($\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}elimination}$, 1/1/elimination) the resulting product is a carbene or a 'carbene analogue'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02039))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02039 "eluate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02039 "The effluent from a chromatographic bed emerging when elution is carried out.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02040 "eluent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02040 "The liquid or gas entering a chromatographic bed and used to effect a separation by elution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02041 "elute")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02041 "To chromatograph by elution chromatography. This term is preferred to the term 'develop', which has been used in paper chromatography and in thin-layer chromatography. The process of elution may continue until the components have left the chromatographic bed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02045 "elution band")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02045 "Synonymous with chromatographic peak.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02046 "elution chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02046 "A procedure for chromatographic separation in which the mobile phase is passed through the chromatographic bed after the application of the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02047 "elution curve")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02047 "A chromatogram or part of a chromatogram recorded when elution techniques are used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02053 "elutriation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02053 "The process of separating the lighter particles of a powder from the heavier ones by means of an upward directed stream of fluid (gas or liquid).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02054 "emanation thermal analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02054 "A thermoanalytical technique in which the release of radioactive emanation from a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature programme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02055))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02055 "embryo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02055 "An aggregate of a small number of atoms, molecules or ions. An embryo of such a size that the Gibbs energy at constant pressure and temperature is a maximum is called a critical embryo. An embryo larger than a critical embryo is called a homogeneous nucleus. Colloidal sols may be formed by condensation methods. When a condensation method is applied, molecules (or ions) are deposited on nuclei which may be of the same chemical species as the colloid (homogeneous nucleation) or different (heterogeneous nucleation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02056 "emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02056 "Radiative deactivation of an excited state; transfer of energy from a molecular entity to an electromagnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02057 "emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02057 "The total rate at which a solid, liquid or gaseous pollutant is emitted into the atmosphere from a given source; usually expressed as mass per unit time. Primary emissions are those substances which are emitted directly to the atmosphere (e.g. $\\ce{NO}$, $\\ce{SO2}$, etc.), while secondary emissions are formed from the primary emissions through thermal or photochemical reactions (e.g. ozone, aldehydes, ketones, sulfuric acid, nitric acid, etc.). The point or area from which the discharge takes place is called the source; the area in which the emission or its transformed products (e.g. in the case of aerosols, acidic deposition, etc.) may be deposited is called the receptor area or sink. Emission may be applied to noise, heat, etc., as well as pollutants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02058 "emission control equipment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02058 "Air pollution control equipment which either converts the pollutant chemically to a non-polluting substance or collects the pollutant by some means including gravity settling chambers, inertial separators, cyclonic separators, filters, electrical precipitators, scrubbers, (spray towers, jet scrubbers, Venturi scrubbers, inertial scrubbers, mechanical scrubbers and packed scrubbers). Certain gases and odoriferous compounds are controlled by combustion, absorption (spray chambers, mechanical contactors, bubble cap or sieve plate contactors and packed towers) and adsorption units (packed beds or fluidized beds).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02059 "emission flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02059 "The emission per unit area of the appropriate surface of an emitting source.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02060 "emission spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02060 """Plot of the emitted spectral radiant power (spectral radiant exitance) or of the emitted spectral photon irradiance (spectral photon exitance) against a quantity related to photon energy, such as frequency, \\(
u \\), wavenumber, \\(\\sigma \\), or wavelength, \\(\\lambda \\). When corrected for wavelength-dependent variations in the equipment response, it is called a corrected emission spectrum.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02062 "emittance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02062 "Ratio of radiant exitance of a thermal radiator to that of a full radiator (black body) at the same temperature. In ISO this quantity is called emissivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02063 "empirical formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02063 "Formed by juxtaposition of the atomic symbols with their appropriate subscripts to give the simplest possible formula expressing the composition of a compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02064 "emulsifier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02064 "A surfactant which when present in small amounts facilitates the formation of an emulsion, or enhances its colloidal stability by decreasing either or both of the rates of aggregation and coalescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02065 "emulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02065 "A fluid colloidal system in which liquid droplets and/or liquid crystals are dispersed in a liquid. The droplets often exceed the usual limits for colloids in size. An emulsion is denoted by the symbol O/W if the continuous phase is an aqueous solution and by W/O if the continuous phase is an organic liquid (an 'oil'). More complicated emulsions such as O/W/O (i.e. oil droplets contained within aqueous droplets dispersed in a continuous oil phase) are also possible. Photographic emulsions, although colloidal systems, are not emulsions in the sense of this nomenclature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02066 "enamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02066 "Alkenylamines; by usage the term refers specifically to vinylic amines, which have the structure $\\ce{R2NCR=CR2}$ .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02069 "enantiomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02069 "One of a pair of molecular entities which are mirror images of each other and non-superposable.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02069 "optical isomer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02070))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02070 "enantiomeric excess")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02070 "For a mixture of \\(\\left(+\\right)\\)- and \\(\\left(-\\right)\\)-enantiomers, with composition given as the mole or weight fractions \\(F_{\\left(\\!+\\!\\right)}\\) and \\(F_{\\left(\\!-\\!\\right)}\\) (where \\(F_{\\left(\\!+\\!\\right)}\\,+\\,F_{\\left(\\!-\\!\\right)}=1\\)) the enantiomer excess is defined as \\(|F_{\\left(\\!+\\!\\right)} - F_{\\left(\\!-\\!\\right)}|\\) (and the percent enantiomer excess by \\(100\\ |F_{\\left(\\!+\\!\\right)} - F_{\\left(\\!-\\!\\right)}|\\)). Frequently this term is abbreviated e.e.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02070 "enantiomer excess")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02071 "enantiomerically enriched")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02071 "A sample of a chiral substance whose enantiomeric ratio is greater than \\(50\\ \\colon\\ 50\\) but less than \\(100\\ \\colon\\ 0\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02071 "enantioenriched")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02072 "enantiomerically pure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02072 "A sample all of whose molecules have (within limits of detection) the same chirality sense. Use of homochiral as a synonym is strongly discouraged.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02072 "enantiopure")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02072 "homochiral")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02073 "enantiomeric groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02073 "Chiral groups that are mirror images of one another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02075 "enantiomeric ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02075 "The ratio of the percentage of one enantiomer in a mixture to that of the other e.g. \\(70\\left(\\!+\\!\\right)\\,\\colon\\,30\\left(\\!-\\!\\right)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02076 "enantiomeric units")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02076 "Two configurational units that correspond to the same constitutional unit are considered to be enantiomeric if they are non-superposable mirror images.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02077 "enantiomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02077 "The interconversion of enantiomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02078 "enantiomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02078 "The isomerism of enantiomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02079 "enantiomorph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02079 "One of a pair of chiral objects or models that are non-superposable mirror images of each other. The adjective enantiomorphic is also applied to mirror-image related groups within a molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02083 "enantiotopic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02083 "Constitutionally identical atoms or groups in molecules which are related by symmetry elements of the second kind only (mirror plane, inversion centre or rotation–reflection axis). For example the two groups c in a grouping Cabcc are enantiotopic. Replacement of one of a pair of enantiotopic groups forms one of a pair of enantiomers. Analogously, if complexation or addition to one of the two faces defined by a double bond or other molecular plane gives rise to a chiral species, the two faces are called enantiotopic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02085 "encapsulation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02085 "Enzymes or cells which are of relatively large size may be entrapped in a maze of polymeric molecules (a gel). This procedure is called immobilization by inclusion. When the biocatalyst is enclosed inside a semipermeable membrane, usually approximately spherical, the method is known as encapsulation. In the process of reticulation the primary biocatalyst particles (individual enzyme molecules, cofactors or individual cells) are covalently attached to each other by organic chains, into a three-dimensional network. The term grafting is also used in this context. Attachment to the support by adsorption forces is called immobilization by adsorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02086 "encounter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02086 "A set of collisions between reactant molecules in solids, liquids or dense gases, occurring in rapid succession as a result of the cage effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02087 "encounter complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02087 "A complex of molecular entities produced at an encounter-controlled rate, and which occurs as an intermediate in a reaction mechanism. When the complex is formed from two molecular entities it is called an 'encounter pair'. A distinction between encounter pairs and (larger) encounter complexes may be relevant in some cases, e.g. for mechanisms involving pre-association.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02089 "encounter-controlled rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02089 "A rate of reaction corresponding to the rate of encounter of the reacting molecular entities. This is also known as 'diffusion-controlled rate' since rates of encounter are themselves controlled by diffusion rates (which in turn depend on the viscosity of the medium and the dimensions of the reactant molecular entities). For a bimolecular reaction between solutes in water at \\(\\pu{25^{\\circ}C}\\) an encounter-controlled rate is calculated to have a second-order rate constant of about \\(\\pu{E10 dm3 mol-1 s-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02089 "diffusion-controlled rate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02091 "endergonic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02091 "This is usually taken to be a reaction for which the overall standard Gibbs energy change \\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is positive. Some workers use this term with respect to a positive value of \\(\\Delta H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) at the absolute zero of temperature.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02091 "endoergic reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02092 "end-group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02092 "A constitutional unit that is an extremity of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule. An end-group is attached to only one constitutional unit of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02093 "endoenzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02093 "Enzymes that cut internal bonds of a polymer. Endonucleases are able to cleave phosphodiester bonds within a nucleic acid chain by hydrolysis either randomly or at specific base sequences (cf. restriction enzymes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02094 "<i>endo</i>, <i>exo</i>, <i>syn</i>, <i>anti</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02094 "Descriptors of the relative orientation of groups attached to non-bridgehead atoms in a bicyclo[x.y.z]alkane (x ≥ y &gt; z &gt; 0).  If the group is orientated towards the highest numbered bridge (z bridge, e.g. C-7 in example below) it is given the description exo; if it is orientated away from the highest numbered bridge it is given the description endo. If the group is attached to the highest numbered bridge and is orientated towards the lowest numbered bridge (x bridge, e.g. C-2 in example below) it is given the description syn; if the group is orientated away from the lowest numbered bridge it is given the description anti.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02095 "endothermic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02095 "A reaction for which the overall standard enthalpy change \\(\\Delta H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is positive.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02097 "end-to-end distance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02097 "The length, \\(r\\), of the end-to-end vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02098 "end-to-end vector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02098 "The vector, \\(r\\), connecting the two ends of a linear polymer chain in a particular conformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02099))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02099 "ene reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02099 "The addition of a compound with a double bond having an allylic hydrogen (the 'ene') to a compound with a multiple bond (the 'enophile') with transfer of the allylic hydrogen and a concomitant reorganization of the bonding, as illustrated below for propene (the 'ene') and ethene (the 'enophile'). The reverse is a 'retro-ene' reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02100 "energized species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02100 "In an elementary process, a species having sufficient energy to become an activated complex, but which does not have the structure of the activated complex. If it is not inactivated by collisions it can become an activated complex and pass at once into products, after undergoing vibration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02101 "energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02101 """In mechanics the sum of potential energy and kinetic energy. In thermodynamics the internal energy or internal energy increase, \\(\\Delta U\\), is the sum of heat and work brought to the system. Only changes in energy are measurable. For photons \\[E=h\\ 
u \\]where \\(h\\) is the Planck constant and \\(
u \\) the frequency of radiation. In relativistic physics \\[E=m\\ c^{2}\\] where \\(c\\) is the speed of light and \\(m\\) the mass.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02102 "energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02102 "Energy of the individual particles or photons of which a radiation consists.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02103 "energy dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02103 "The separation of characteristic photons according to their energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02105))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02105 "energy dispersive X-ray fluorescence analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02105 "A method of X-ray fluorescence analysis where element specificity is obtained by measuring the energy spectrum of the induced X-radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02106 "energy flux density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02106 "For mono-directional radiation, the energy traversing in a time interval over a small area perpendicular to the direction of the energy flow, divided by that time interval and by that area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02109 "energy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02109 "This is defined by the equation: \\[U^{\\ddagger } = -R\\ T\\ (\\frac{\\partial (\\ln I_{0})}{\\partial T^{-1}})_{p,c_{j},...}\\] where \\(I_{0}\\) is the exchange current. At any overpotential \\(\\eta \\) it is defined by the equation: \\[U^{\\ddagger }\\left(\\eta \\right) = -R\\ (\\frac{\\partial (\\ln (|I|))}{\\partial T^{-1}})_{p,\\eta ,c_{j},...}\\] where \\(I\\) is the current passing from the electrode into the electrolyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02113))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02113 "energy resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02113 "A measurement, at given energy, of the smallest difference between the energies of two particles or photons capable of being distinguished by a radiation spectrometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02114))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02114 "energy storage efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02114 "The rate of the Gibbs energy storage in an endothermic photochemical reaction divided by the incident irradiance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02115 "energy threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02115 "The limiting kinetic energy of a incident particle or energy of an incident photon below which a specified process cannot take place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02116 "energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02116 "From a phenomenological point of view, the term is used to describe the process by which a molecular entity absorbs light and a phenomenon originates from the excited state of another molecular entity. In mechanistic photochemistry the term has been reserved for the photophysical process in which an excited state of one molecular entity (the donor) is deactivated to a lower-lying state by transferring energy to a second molecular entity (the acceptor) which is thereby raised to a higher energy state. The excitation may be electronic, vibrational, rotational or translational. The donor and acceptor may be two parts of the same molecular entity, in which case the process is called intramolecular energy transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02117 "energy transfer plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02117 "A plot of the quenching rate constant of an excited molecular entity by a series of quenchers versus the excited state energy of the quenchers. Alternatively, a plot of the rate constant for the sensitization of a reaction versus the excited state energy of different sensitizers. This type of plot is used to estimate the energy of the excited molecular entity quenched (in the former case) or produced (in the latter case).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02117 "Hammond–Herkstroeter plot")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02117 "Herkstroeter plot")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02118))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02118 "energy yield of luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02118 "The ratio of the energy emitted as luminescence to the energy absorbed by a species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02119 "enforced concerted mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02119 "Variation of reaction parameters in a series of reactions proceeding in non-concerted steps may lead to a situation where the putative intermediate will possess a lifetime shorter than a bond vibration, so that the steps become concerted. The transition state structure will lie on the coordinate of the More O'Ferrall–Jencks diagram leading to that of the putative intermediate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02120))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02120 "enhanced phosphorescence analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02120 "A use of luminescence quenching effects to enhance sensitivity. The strong depopulation of the fluorescing singlet excited state by external heavy atom perturbers can lead to a large population of the phosphorescing triplet excited state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02121 "enhancement reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02121 "A reaction which enables a favourable measurement to be made in which the constituent which is finally measured has not been magnified in any way (cf. amplification reaction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02122))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02122 "enhancer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02122 "A fluorescent compound which accepts energy and thus enhances or promotes the emission from a sample containing a chemically or enzymatically generated excited molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02123 "enolates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02123 "Salts of enols (or of the tautomeric aldehydes or ketones), in which the anionic charge is delocalized over oxygen and carbon, or similar covalent metal derivatives in which the metal is bound to oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02124 "enols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02124 """Alkenols; the term refers specifically to vinylic alcohols, which have the structure $\\ce{HOCR'=CR2}$. Enols are tautomeric with aldehydes ($\\ce{R'} = \\ce{H}$) or ketones ($\\ce{R'\\!
eq H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02126))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02126 "enoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02126 "Monosaccharides having a carbon–carbon double bond anywhere in the backbone chain. Glycals (term not recommended) designates the enoses that are generated by formal elimination of the hemiacetal hydroxy group and an adjacent hydrogen atom. Thus glycals are cyclic enol ethers. Unsaturated hexoses, for example, are called hexenoses, e.g. the hex-1-enopyranose derived from ᴅ-glucopyranose):")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02126 "glycals")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02127))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02127 "enrichment factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02127 "The factor by which the ratio of two substances in the feed must be multiplied to give their ratio after treatment. \\[\\frac{Q_{\\rm{A}}}{Q_{\\rm{B}}} = S_{\\rm{A,B}}\\ \\frac{Q_{\\rm{A}}^{'}}{Q_{\\rm{B}}^{'}}\\] where \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}\\) and \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}^{'}\\) are the final and initial amounts of species A and \\(Q_{\\rm{B}}\\) and \\(Q_{\\rm{B}}^{'}\\) are the final and initial amounts of species B. Hence \\(S_{\\rm{A,B}}=\\frac{E_{\\rm{A}}}{E_{\\rm{B}}}\\) where \\(E\\) is the fraction extracted. In terms of \\(D\\),\\(n\\),\\(r\\) (where \\(n\\) is the number of stages and \\(r\\) the phase ratio), \\[S_{\\rm{A,B}} = \\frac{1- (1\\,+\\,r\\ D_{\\rm{A}})^{-n}}{1- (1\\,+\\,r\\ D_{\\rm{B}})^{-n}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02129))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02129 "<i>ent</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02129 "A prefix used to indicate the enantiomer for natural products and related molecules where the trivial name only refers to one enantiomer, e.g. ent-kaurene is the enantiomer of kaurene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02130))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02130 "entering group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02130 "An atom or group that forms a bond to what is considered to be the main part of the substrate during a reaction. For example: the attacking nucleophile in a bimolecular nucleophilic substitution reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02131))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02131 "enthalpimetric analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02131 "The generic designation for a family of analytical methods in which the enthalpy change of a chemical reaction is measured, directly or indirectly, in order to perform a quantitative determination of a reactant or catalyst. In general, at least one reactant is a liquid or solution. It is a subset of calorimetry in which the system is maintained at constant (usually atmospheric pressure), and the emphasis is on a quantitative determination in a reasonable length of time. Analytical solution calorimetry is a synonymous designation that may be used but is not preferred. 'Thermochemical analysis' has been used but is not recommended because of potential confusion and overlap with the methods of thermal analysis. The adjective 'thermometric' is also associated with the names of some of these methods, but is not recommended as a general designation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02131 "analytical solution calorimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02131 "enthalpimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02131 "thermochemical analysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02140 "enthalpogram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02140 "A plot of temperature versus time or heat change versus time which is produced in direct-injection enthalpimetry. Use of the term 'thermogram' is not recommended because it is too general and overlaps with other fields.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02140 "thermogram")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02141 "enthalpy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02141 "Internal energy of a system plus the product of pressure and volume. Its change in a system is equal to the heat brought to the system at constant pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02142 "enthalpy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02142 "The standard enthalpy of activation \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the enthalpy change that appears in the thermodynamic form of the rate equation obtained from conventional transition state theory. This equation is only correct for a first order reaction, for which the rate constant has the dimension reciprocal time. For a second order reaction, for which the rate constant has the dimension (reciprocal time) × (reciprocal concentration), the left hand side should be read as \\(k\\ c^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), where \\(c^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) denotes the standard concentration (usually \\(\\pu{1 mol dm-3}\\)). \\[k = \\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ \\rm{e}^{\\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R}}\\ \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R\\ T}}\\] The quantity \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the standard entropy of activation, and care must be taken with standard states. In this equation \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the Boltzmann constant, \\(T\\) the absolute temperature, \\(h\\) the Planck constant, and \\(R\\) the gas constant. The enthalpy of activation is approximately equal to the activation energy; the conversion of one into the other depends on the molecularity. The enthalpy of activation is always the standard quantity, although the word standard and the superscript \\(^{\\circ}\\) on the symbol are often omitted. The symbol is frequently (but incorrectly) written \\(\\Delta H^{\\ddagger }\\), where the standard symbol is omitted and the \\(\\ddagger \\) is placed after the \\(H\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02143 "enthalpy of immersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02143 "Synonymous with enthalpy of wetting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02144 "enthalpy of wetting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02144 "Referred to unit of mass of the solid, the difference (at constant temperature) between the enthalpy of a solid completely immersed in a wetting liquid, and that of the solid and the liquid taken separately. It must be specified whether the solid in the initial state is in contact with vacuum or with the vapour of the liquid at a given partial pressure. Measurements of the enthalpy of wetting of a solid equilibrated with varying relative pressures of the vapour of a pure wetting liquid may be used to derive the differential enthalpy of adsorption of the vapour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02145 "entitic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02145 "Modifier used to denote divided by number of entities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02146 "entitic quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02146 "One which refers to a single entity (particle) of a system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02147 "entrainment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02147 "The act of forming a mist or fog: droplets of a liquid carried off by the vapours of a boiling liquid or from a liquid through which bubbles of gas or vapour are passing rapidly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02148 "entrance channel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02148 "The region of a potential-energy surface or hypersurface that corresponds to molecular configurations that are closer in geometry to those of the reactants than to those of the products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02149))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02149 "entropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02149 "Quantity the change in which is equal to the heat brought to the system in a reversible process at constant temperature divided by that temperature. Entropy is zero for an ideally ordered crystal at 0 \\(\\rm{K}\\). In statistical thermodynamics \\[S=k\\ \\ln W\\] where \\(k\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(W\\) the number of possible arrangements of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02150 "entropy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02150 "The entropy change that appears in the thermodynamic form of the rate equation obtained from conventional transition-state theory.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02151 "entropy unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02151 "Non-SI unit of molar entropy, \\(\\rm{e.u.} = \\pu{4.184 J K-1 mol-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02152 "envelope conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02152 "The conformation (of symmetry group \\(C_{\\rm{s}}\\)) of a five-membered ring in which four atoms are coplanar, and one atom (the flap) projects out of the plane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02158 "enzyme repression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02158 "The mode by which the synthesis of an enzyme is prevented by repressor molecules. In many cases, the end product of a synthesis chain (e.g. an amino acid) acts as a feed-back corepressor by combining with an intracellular aporepressor protein, so that this complex is able to block the function of an operator. As a result, the whole operon is prevented from being transcribed into mRNA, and the expression of all enzymes necessary for the synthesis of the end product amino acid is abolished.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02159 "enzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02159 "Macromolecules, mostly of protein nature, that function as (bio)catalysts by increasing the reaction rates. In general, an enzyme catalyses only one reaction type (reaction specificity) and operates on only one type of substrate (substrate specificity). Substrate molecules are attacked at the same site (regiospecificity) and only one or preferentially one of the enantiomers of chiral substrates or of racemic mixtures is attacked (stereospecificity).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02160 "enzyme substrate electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02160 "A sensor in which an ion-selective electrode is covered with a coating containing an enzyme which causes the reaction of an organic or inorganic substance (substrate) to produce a species to which the electrode responds. Alternatively, the sensor could be covered with a layer of substrate which reacts with the enzyme, co-factor or inhibitor to be assayed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02162 "enzymic decomposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02162 "Decomposition of organic materials (e.g. starch, sugars, proteins, etc.) can be achieved by enzymic decomposition, in which the enzyme converts a high molecular mass compound into lower molecular mass species. The process can be regarded as an example of enzymic degradation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02163 "epicadmium neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02163 "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than the effective cadmium cut-off energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02164 "epigenetic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02164 "Descriptive term for processes that change the phenotype without altering the genotype.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02165))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02165 "epihalohydrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02165 "Compounds having the (halomethyl)oxirane skeleton:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02166 "epimerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02166 "Interconversion of epimers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02167 "epimers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02167 "Diastereoisomers that have the opposite configuration at only one of two or more tetrahedral stereogenic centres present in the respective molecular entities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02168 "epi-phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02168 "The less dense phase in a distribution system. The term is often used when two non-aqueous phases are present or when the solvent is an aqueous solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02169 "episulfonium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02169 "Ions derived from thiiranes, in which a trivalent sulfur atom bears a positive charge:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02170 "epithermal neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02170 "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than that of thermal agitation. The term is often restricted to energies just above thermal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02171 "epitope")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02171 "Any part of a molecule that acts as an antigenic determinant. A macromolecule can contain many different epitopes each capable of stimulating production of a different specific antibody.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02173 "epoxy compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02173 "Compounds in which an oxygen atom is directly attached to two adjacent or non-adjacent carbon atoms of a carbon chain or ring system; thus cyclic ethers. The term epoxides represents a subclass of epoxy compounds containing a saturated three-membered cyclic ether; thus oxirane derivatives, e.g. 1,2-epoxypropane, or 2-methyloxirane (an epoxide); 9,10-epoxy-9,10-dihydroanthracene (an epoxy compound).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02174 "equatorial")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02174 "For the use of this term in the context of cyclohexane see axial, equatorial. For the use of this term in the context of a bipyramidal structure see apical, basal, equatorial.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02175 "equilibration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02175 "The operation by which a system of two or more phases is brought to a condition where further changes with time do not occur. This term is not synonymous with pre-equilibrium and should not be used in that sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02177 "equilibrium constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02177 """Quantity characterizing the equilibrium of a chemical reaction and defined by an expression of the type \\(K_{x}=\\underset{\\begin{array}{c}
\\rm{B}
\\end{array}}{\\Pi }x_{\\rm{B}}^{
u _{\\rm{B}}}\\), where \\(
u _{\\rm{B}}\\) is the stoichiometric number of a reactant (negative) or product (positive) for the reaction and \\(x\\) stands for a quantity which can be the equilibrium value either of pressure, fugacity, amount concentration, amount fraction, molality, relative activity or reciprocal absolute activity defining the pressure based, fugacity based, concentration based, amount fraction based, molality based, relative activity based or standard equilibrium constant (then denoted \\(K^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)), respectively.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02179 "equilibrium dialysate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02179 "The colloid-free solution obtained at equilibrium in dialysis. Its composition approaches that of the dispersion medium (more precisely, the limit to which the composition of the dispersion medium tends at large distances from the particles). In the dialysis equilibrium an osmotic pressure difference exists between sol and equilibrium dialysate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02180 "equilibrium distance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02180 "Internuclear distance at the minimum of the electronic (or potential) energy surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02181 "equilibrium film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02181 "A liquid film of thickness, or thicknesses, at which it is stable or metastable with respect to small thickness changes. Unless the area of the film is small, its composition may not be the same over its area and the (metastable) equilibrium thickness may be characteristic of the local condition only.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02184 "equilibrium reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02184 "When the reactants in a chemical reaction are initially in a Boltzmann distribution the reaction is referred to as an equilibrium reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02186 "equilibrium sedimentation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02186 "A method by which the distribution of the concentration of the solute or dispersed component in a dilute solution or dispersion along the centrifuge cell is measured at sedimentation equilibrium and the results are interpreted in terms of molar masses or their distribution, or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02189 "equivalence postulate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02189 "The working hypothesis that the chain monomeric units are geometrically equivalent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02190))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02190 "equivalent chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02190 "A hypothetical freely jointed chain with the same mean-square end-to-end distance and contour length as an actual polymer chain in a theta state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02191 "equivalent diameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02191 "The diameter of a spherical particle which will give identical geometric, optical, electrical or aerodynamic behaviour to that of the particle (non-spherical) being examined; sometimes referred to as the Stokes diameter for particles in non-turbulent flows.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02192 "equivalent entity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02192 "Entity corresponding to the transfer of a $\\ce{H^{+}}$ ion in a neutralization reaction, of an electron in a redox reaction, or to a magnitude of charge number equal to 1 in ions. Examples: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) $\\ce{H2SO4}$, \\(\\frac{1}{5}\\) $\\ce{KMnO4}$, \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) $\\ce{Fe^{3+}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02193))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02193 "erg")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02193 "cgs unit of energy, \\(\\rm{erg} = 10^{-7}\\ \\rm{J}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02194 "error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02194 "Result of a measurement minus the true value of the measurand. Since a true value cannot be determined, in practice the conventional true value is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02211 "<i>erythro</i> structures")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02211 """The relative configuration at two contiguous carbon atoms in the main chain bearing, respectively, substituents $\\ce{a}$ and $\\ce{b}$ ($\\ce{a} 
eq \\ce{b}$), is designated by the prefix erythro or erythro or threo, as appropriate, by analogy with the terminology for carbohydrate systems in which the substituents are $\\ce{OH}$. Examples:  Similar systems in which a higher level of substitution exists may be treated analogously if the erythro or threo designation is employed to denote the relative placements of those two substituents, one for each backbone carbon atom, which rank highest according to the Sequence Rule.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02212))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02212 "<i>erythro</i>, <i>threo</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02212 "Descriptors of the diastereoisomers of an acyclic structure or partial structure having two stereogenic centres. This notation is derived from carbohydrate nomenclature. The extension of this system has given rise to conflicting interpretations of these prefixes. It is recommended that for such cases the l, u or R*, S* system should be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02214 "escape depth")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02214 "The distance into the sample measured from the physical surface from which all but a fraction \\(\\frac{1}{e}\\) of the particles or radiation detected have originated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02218 "Esin and Markov coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02218 "The left-hand side of one of the various cross-differential relationships that can be obtained from the Gibbs adsorption equation when only one chemical potential (\\(\\mu \\)) is considered as variable, viz. \\[(\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial \\mu })_{T,p,\\sigma }=- (\\frac{\\partial \\mathit{\\Gamma }}{\\partial \\sigma })_{T,p,\\mu }\\] where \\(E\\) is the potential difference, \\(T\\) is the temperature, \\(p\\) is the pressure, \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}\\) is the surface excess and \\(\\sigma \\) is the charge density.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02219 "esters")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02219 """Compounds formally derived from an oxoacid $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$, ($l 
eq 0$) and an alcohol, phenol, heteroarenol, or enol by linking with formal loss of water from an acidic hydroxy group of the former and a hydroxy group of the latter. By extension acyl derivatives of alcohols, etc. Acyl derivatives of chalcogen analogues of alcohols (thiols, selenols, tellurols) etc. are included. E.g. $\\ce{R'C(=O)(OR)}$, $\\ce{R'C(=S)(OR)}$, $\\ce{R'C(=O)(SR)}$, $\\ce{R'S(=O)2(OR)}$, $\\ce{(HO)2P(=O)(OR)}$, $\\ce{(R'S)2C(=O)}$, $\\ce{ROCN}$ (but not $\\ce{R–NCO}$) ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02221))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02221 "ethers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02221 """Compounds ROR ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). (Compounds $\\ce{R3SiOR}$, silicon analogues of ethers, are trialkylsilyloxy compounds). E.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2OCH2CH3}$ .""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02223 "euatmotic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02223 "An isothermal, reversible reaction between two (or more) solid phases during the heating of a system as a result of which a single vapour phase is produced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02225 "eutectic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02225 "An isothermal, reversible reaction between two (or more) solid phases during the heating of a system, as a result of which a single liquid phase is produced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02226))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02226 "evaluation function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02226 "The inverse of the calibration function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02227 "evaporation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02227 "The physical process by which a liquid substance is converted to a gas or vapour. This may occur at or below the normal boiling point of the liquid (the temperature at which a liquid boils at 1 atmosphere pressure) and the process is endothermic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02228 "even-electron ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02228 "An ion containing no unpaired electrons, for example $\\ce{CH3^{+}}$, in its ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02229 "evolved gas analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02229 "A technique in which the nature and/or amount of volatile product(s) released by a substance subjected to a controlled temperature program is (are) determined. The method of analysis should always be clearly stated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02230 "evolved gas detection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02230 "A technique in which the evolution of gas from a substance is detected as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02232 "exa")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02232 "SI prefix for \\(10^{18}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{E}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02236 "excess Rayleigh ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02236 "The difference between the Rayleigh ratio for a dilute solution and for pure solvent. If the scattering intensity is not reduced to the Rayleigh ratio, the difference between the scattering intensities for a dilute solution and that for pure solvent is named 'excess scattering'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02237))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02237 "excess volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02237 "For a pure liquid, despite its low compressibility, the variation of density near a solid surface can be detected and measured. The total volume \\(V\\) of a system consisting of solid and pure liquid is different from (usually less than) that calculated assuming a constant liquid density. If the densities of bulk solid (\\(\\rho ^{\\mathrm{sol}}\\)) and liquid (\\(\\rho ^{\\rm{l}}\\)) are known then an excess volume (usually negative) can be defined as: \\[V^{\\sigma }=V- V^{\\mathrm{sol}}- V^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}=V- \\frac{m^{\\mathrm{sol}}}{\\rho ^{\\mathrm{sol}}}- \\frac{m^{\\rm{l}}}{\\rho ^{\\rm{l}}}\\] where \\(m^{\\mathrm{sol}}\\) is the mass of solid, \\(V^{\\mathrm{sol}}\\) its volume calculated from the bulk density, \\(V^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the initial volume of liquid and \\(m^{\\rm{l}}\\) is the mass of liquid. The excess mass is given by: \\[m^{\\sigma }=m^{\\rm{l}}- (V- V^{\\mathrm{sol}})\\ \\rho ^{\\rm{l}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02238 "exchange current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02238 "The common value \\(I_{0}\\) of the anodic and cathodic partial currents when the reaction is at equilibrium \\[I_{0}=I_{\\rm{a}}=- I_{\\rm{c}}\\] For an electrode at equilibrium at which only one reaction is significant \\(I=0\\). When more than one reaction is significant at a given electrode, subscripts to \\(I_{0}\\) may be used to distinguish exchange currents. \\(I\\) is not usually zero when only one of these reactions is at equilibrium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02239 "exchange extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02239 "An extraction operation or process in which a metal from one phase is exchanged with the equivalent amount of a second metal from the other phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02240 "exchange-inversion transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02240 "A transition between antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic coupling between two subarrays of magnetic atoms. Example: Ordered $\\ce{FeRh}$ changes from antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic coupling with the simple cubic $\\ce{Fe}$ array in a first-order transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02241))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02241 "exchange labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02241 "Labelling of a substance by isotope exchange.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02242 "excimer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02242 "An electronically excited dimer, 'non-bonding' in the ground state. For example, a complex formed by the interaction of an excited molecular entity with a ground state partner of the same structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02243 "excimer laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02243 "A source of pulsed coherent radiation obtained from an exciplex. The proper name is exciplex laser. Typical lasing species are noble gas halides ($\\ce{XeCl}$, $\\ce{KrF}$, etc.) emitting in the UV domain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02244 "excimer-luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02244 "An effect accompanying concentration quenching in some cases, namely the formation of a new bimolecular species which is capable of emission (excimer- and exciplex-luminescence).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02245 "excipient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02245 "Any more or less inert substance added to a drug to give suitable consistency or form to the drug.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02246 "exciplex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02246 "An electronically excited complex of definite stoichiometry, 'non-bonding' in the ground state. For example, a complex formed by the interaction of an excited molecular entity with a ground state counterpart of a different structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02248 "excitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02248 "Process causing the transition of a system from one state to another of higher energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02249 "excitation-emission spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02249 "The three-dimensional spectrum generated by scanning the emission spectrum at incremental steps of excitation wavelength (\\(x\\) axis = emission wavelength, \\(y\\) axis = excitation wavelength, \\(z\\) axis = emission flux) is called a (fluorescence, phosphorescence) excitation-emission spectrum (or EES). The spectra are particularly useful for investigating samples containing more than one emitting species. Corrected EES are obtained if (a) the emission is corrected for instrumental response with wavelength, and (b) the exciting radiation flux in photons \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\) is held constant for all excitation wavelengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02250 "excitation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02250 "The minimum energy required to bring a system to a specified higher energy level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02251))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02251 "excitation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02251 "Kinetic energy of bombarding particles at the surface of the solid.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02251 "initial energy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02251 "acceleration energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02252 "excitation level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02252 "X-ray levels have various degrees of ionization -- single, double or higher -- and may in some cases also be electrically neutral. An excitation level (exciton) is an electrically neutral X-ray level with an expelled electron bound in the field of a core electron vacancy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02253 "excitation spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02253 "A plot of the spectral radiant exitance or of the spectral photon exitance against the frequency (or wavenumber, or wavelength) of excitation. When corrected for wavelength dependent variations in the excitation radiant power this is called a corrected excitation spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02256 "excitation transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02256 "Synonymous with energy transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02257 "excited state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02257 "State of a system with energy higher than that of the ground state. This term is most commonly used to characterize a molecule in one of its electronically excited states, but can also refer to vibrational and/or rotational excitation in the electronic ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02258 "exciton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02258 "In some applications it is useful to consider electronic excitation as if a quasi-particle, capable of migrating, were involved. This is termed an exciton. In organic materials two models are used: the band or wave model (low temperature, high crystalline order) and the hopping model (higher temperature, low crystalline order or amorphous state). Energy transfer in the hopping limit is identical with energy migration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02259))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02259 "excluded volume of a macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02259 "The volume from which a macromolecule in a dilute solution of a macromolecule effectively excludes all other macromolecules. The excluded volume of a macromolecule depends on the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing of solvent and polymer, i.e. on the thermodynamic quality of the solvent, and is not a measure of the geometrical volume of that macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02260 "excluded volume of a segment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02260 "The volume from which a segment of a macromolecule in solution effectively excludes all other segments, i.e. those belonging to the same macromolecule as well as those belonging to other macromolecules. The excluded volume of a segment depends on the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing of solvent and polymer, i.e. on the thermodynamic quality of the solvent, and is not a measure of the geometrical volume of that segment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02261 "exclusion chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02261 "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on exclusion effects, such as differences in molecular size and/or shape or in charge. The term size-exclusion chromatography may also be used when separation is based on molecular size. The terms gel filtration and gel-permeation chromatography (GPC) were used earlier to describe this process when the stationary phase is a swollen gel. The term ion-exclusion chromatography is specifically used for the separation of ions in an aqueous phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02261 "gel-permeation chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02261 "gel-filtration chromatography")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02262 "exergonic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02262 "This expression is often applied to a reaction for which the overall standard Gibbs energy change \\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is negative. Some workers use this term with reference to a negative value of \\(\\Delta H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) at the absolute zero of temperature.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02262 "exoergic reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02263 "exfoliated graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02263 "The product of very rapid heating (or flash heating) of graphite intercalation compounds, such as graphite hydrogensulfate of relatively large particle diameter (flakes). The vaporizing intercalated substances force the graphite layers apart. The exfoliated graphite assumes an accordion-like shape with an apparent volume often hundreds of times that of the original graphite flakes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02265))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02265 "exit channel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02265 "This expression refers to the region of a potential-energy surface or hypersurface that corresponds to molecular configurations that are closer to those of the products than to those of the reactants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02267 "exoenzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02267 "Enzymes that cleave monomers (sometimes also di- or oligomers) from one end of a polymer chain. Exonucleases are able to cleave nucleotides, one by one, from either the 5- or the 3- (or both) ends of nucleic acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02268))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02268 "exon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02268 "A section of DNA which carries the coding sequence for a protein or part of it. Exons are separated by intervening, non-coding sequences (cf. intron). In eukaryotes most genes consist of a number of exons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02269 "exothermic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02269 "A reaction for which the overall standard enthalpy change \\(\\Delta H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is negative.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02270 "expansion factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02270 "The ratio of a dimensional characteristic of a macromolecule in a given solvent at a given temperature to the same dimensional characteristic in the \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B8}\\) state at the same temperature. The most frequently used expansion factors are: expansion factor of the mean-square end-to-end distance, \\(\\alpha _{r} = \\sqrt{\\frac{ < r^{2} > }{< r^{2} >_{0}}}\\); expansion factor of the radius of gyration \\(\\alpha _{s} = \\sqrt{\\frac{ < s^{2} > }{ < s^{2} >_{0}}}\\); viscosity expansion factor \\(\\alpha _{\\eta } = (\\frac{\\left[\\eta \\right]}{\\left[\\eta \\right]_{\\unicode[Times]{x3B8} }})^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\) where \\([\\eta]\\) and \\([\\eta]_{\\uptheta}\\) are the intrinsic viscosity in a given solvent and in the \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B8}\\) state at the same temperature, respectively. Expansion factors defined by different dimensional characteristics are not exactly equal, nor need they have a constant ratio as a function of relative molecular mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02273 "experimental perturbational calculation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02273 "To assess the uncertainty of derived final results from a least-squares procedure one can investigate the sensitivity of output data for errors in the input data by means of this calculation. One performs the calculations many times with perturbed input data and studies the relation between the changes (perturbations) in the input data and the changes in the output data. The perturbations are preferably generated using random numbers with a distribution function chosen to mimic the distribution of uncertainties in the primary results.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02274 "explosivity limits")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02274 "The concentration limits, usually of a substance in air, between which combustion will be self-sustaining.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02274 "explosion limits")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02274 "flammable limits")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02275))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02275 "exponential decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02275 "Variation of a quantity (generally the activity of a quantity of a radionuclide) according to the law \\(A=A_{0}\\ \\rm{e}^{-\\lambda\\ t}\\) where \\(A\\) and \\(A_{0}\\) are the values of the quantity being considered at time \\(t\\) and zero respectively, and \\(\\lambda\\) is an appropriate constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02277 "expression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02277 "The cellular production of the protein encoded by a particular gene. The process includes transcription of DNA, processing of the resulting mRNA product and its translation into an active protein.A recombinant gene inserted into a host cell by means of a vector is said to be expressed if the synthesis of the encoded protein can be demonstrated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02278 "extended-chain crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02278 "A polymer crystal in which the chains are in an essentially fully extended conformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02279 "extended Hammett equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02279 "This term applies in a general way to any multiparametric extension of the Hammett equation. It is sometimes used specifically for a form of dual substituent-parameter equation in which the actual value of the correlated property \\(P\\) under the influence of the substituent $\\ce{X}$ is used, rather than the value relative to that for $\\ce{X}$ = $\\ce{H}$. An intercept term \\(h\\) corresponding to the value of \\(P\\) for $\\ce{X}$ = $\\ce{H}$ is introduced, e.g. \\[P=\\alpha \\ \\sigma _{I}+\\beta \\ \\sigma _{R}+h\\] The equation may be applied to systems for which the inclusion of further terms to represent other effects, e.g. steric, is appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02280))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02280 "extended X-ray absorption fine structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02280 "Structure in X-ray absorption spectra remote from the edge and extending to higher energies, due mainly to the scattering of the expelled electron by neighbouring atoms. Structure closer to the absorption edge is sometimes called near-edge X-ray absorption fine structure (NEXAFS).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02281))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02281 "extensive quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02281 "Physical quantity whose magnitude is additive for subsystems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02282 "extent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02282 "A quantity measured by its area, \\(A\\) (or \\(S\\)). For solids, a real (true, actual) and a geometric surface (interface) area, \\(A_{\\rm{r}}\\) and \\(A_{\\rm{g}}\\), respectively, may be defined if asperities are present whose height is orders of magnitude greater than the atomic or molecular size. The geometric surface is the projection of the real surface on a plane parallel to the macroscopic, visible phase boundary. If asperities are of the order of the atomic size, the surface of the solid may be described as stepped. (High index faces of crystals are stepped surfaces but may be ideally smooth in the sense of the roughness factor.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02283 "extent of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02283 """Extensive quantity describing the progress of a chemical reaction equal to the number of chemical transformations, as indicated by the reaction equation on a molecular scale, divided by the Avogadro constant (it is essentially the amount of chemical transformations). The change in the extent of reaction is given by \\(\\mathrm{d}\\xi =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{
u _{\\rm{B}}}\\), where \\(
u _{\\rm{B}}\\) is the stoichiometric number of any reaction entity B (reactant or product) and \\(\\mathrm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the corresponding amount.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02283 "advancement")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02290))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02290 "external standard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02290 "A compound present in a standard sample of known concentration and volume which is analysed separately from the unknown sample under identical conditions. It is used to facilitate the qualitative indentification and/or quantitative determination of the sample components. The volume of the external standard (standard sample) need not to be known if it is identical to that of the unknown sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02291 "external surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02291 "When a porous solid consists of discrete particles it is inconvenient to describe the outer boundary of the particles as the external surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02292))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02292 "exterplex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02292 "Termolecular analogue of an exciplex. Use of this term is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02295 "extra-column volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02295 "The volume between the effective injection point and the effective detection point, excluding the part of the column containing the stationary phase. It is composed of the volumes of the injector, connecting lines and detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02296))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02296 "extract")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02296 "The separated phase (often but not necessarily organic) that contains the material extracted from the other phase. Where appropriate the term 'loaded solvent' may be used, but is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02297 "extract")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02297 "To transfer a solute from a liquid phase to another immiscible or partially miscible liquid phase in contact with it. The term is also applied to the dissolution of material from a solid phase with a liquid in which it is not wholly soluble (i.e. leaching).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02298 "extractability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02298 "A property which qualitatively indicates the degree to which a substance is extracted. The term is imprecise and generally used in a qualitative sense. It is not a synonym for fraction extracted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02299 "extractant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02299 "The active component(s) primarily responsible for transfer of a solute from one phase to the other.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02299 "extracting agent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02301 "extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02301 "Distribution and partition are often used as synonyms for the general phenomenon of extraction where appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02302 "extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02302 "In connection with processes, this term often refers to the initial transfer step whereby the main solute, often together with impurities, is transferred from feed to solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02304 "extraction constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02304 "The extraction constant at zero ionic strength, \\(K_{\\rm{ex}}^{\\rm{0}}\\), is the equilibrium constant of the distribution reaction expressed in terms of the reacting species. Thus, for the gross reaction: \\[\\rm{M}_{\\rm{aq}}^{n+} + n\\rm{HL}_{\\rm{org}} \\rightleftharpoons  \\rm{ML}_{n,\\rm{org}} + n\\rm{H}_{\\rm{aq}}^{+}\\] in which the reagent $\\ce{HL}$ initially dissolved in an organic phase reacts with a metal ion $\\ce{M^{n}}$ in aqueous solution to form a product $\\ce{ML_{n}}$ which is more soluble in the organic phase than in water, \\[K_{\\rm{ex}}^{\\rm{0}} = \\frac{a_{\\rm{ML } n,\\rm{org}}\\hspace{2pt}a_{\\rm{H}^{+},\\rm{aq}}^{n}}{a_{\\rm{M}^{n+},\\rm{aq}}\\hspace{2pt}a_{\\rm{HL},\\rm{org}}^{n}}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02304 "extraction equilibrium constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02305))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02305 "extraction factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02305 "The ratio of the total mass of a solute in the extract to that in the other phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02305 "mass distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:E02305 "concentration factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02306 "extraction fractionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02306 "A process in which a polymeric material, consisting of macromolecules differing in some characteristic affecting their solubility, is separated from a polymer-rich phase into fractions by successively increasing the solution power of the solvent, resulting in the repeated formation of a two-phase system in which the more soluble components concentrate in the polymer-poor phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02309 "extrapolated range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02309 "The distance from a radiation source, calculated by extrapolation to zero of the flux density, of the tangent to the flux density versus distance curve, taken at the point where the flux density has decreased to one half of its initial value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02310 "extremophiles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02310 "Organisms which require extreme physico-chemical conditions for their optimum growth and proliferation. Extremophilic microorganisms are e.g. thermophiles or psychrophiles, halophiles, alkalophiles or acidophiles, osmophiles and barophiles, based on their growth at extremes of temperature, salt concentration, pH, osmolarity or pressure, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E02311))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E02311 "extrusion transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E02311 "A transformation in which an atom or group $\\ce{Y}$ connected to two other atoms or groups $\\ce{X}$ and $\\ce{Z}$ is lost from a molecule, leading to a product in which $\\ce{X}$ is bonded to $\\ce{Z}$, e.g.  or  The reverse of an extrusion is called insertion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:E04391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:E04391 "π-electron acceptor/donor group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:E04391 "A substituent capable of a $\\ce{+R}$ (e.g.$\\ce{NO2}$) or $\\ce{-R}$ (e.g. $\\ce{OCH3}$) effect, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET02156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET02156 "enzyme induction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET02156 "The process whereby an (inducible) enzyme is synthesized in response to a specific molecule (cf. inducer).The inducer molecule (often a substrate that needs the catalytic activity of the inducible enzyme for its metabolism) combines with a repressor and thereby prevents the blocking of an operator by the repressor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET02276))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET02276 "exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET02276 "For X- or gamma-radiation in air: the sum of the electrical charges of all the ions of one sign produced when all electronss liberated by photonss in a suitably small element of volume of air completely stopped, divided by the mass of the air in the volume element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06758 "electromigration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06758 "The transport of particles under the influence of an electric field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06759))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06759 "Einstein equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06759 "In its most general form, the Einstein equation relates the atomic mobility \\(b_{i}\\) of a species \\(i\\) to the diffusion coefficient \\(D_{i}\\): i.e., \\(D_{i}=k\\ T\\ b_{i}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06763 "entatic state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06763 "A state of an atom or group which, due to its binding in a protein, has its geometric or electronic condition adapted for function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06811 "exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06811 "Concentration, amount or intensity of a particular physical or chemical agent or environmental agent that reaches the target population, organism, organ, tissue, or cell, usually expressed in numerical terms of concentration, duration, and frequency (for chemical agents and micro-organisms) or intensity (for physical agents).Note: This differs from the definition of exposure in radiation chemistry.Process by which a substance becomes available for absorption by the target population, organism, organ, tissue or cell, by any route.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06822 "endocytosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06822 "Uptake of material into a cell by invagination of the plasma membrane and its internalisation in a membrane-bounded vesicle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06823 "enzyme induction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06823 "Process whereby an enzyme is synthesised in response to the presence of a specific substance or to other agents such as heat or a metal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06851 "environmental stress cracking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06851 "Cracking caused by the combined actions of (i) mechanical stress and (ii) chemical agents or radiation or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06890))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06890 "electro-dialysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06890 "Membrane-based separation process in which ions are driven through an ion-selective membrane under the influence of an electric field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06891 "electro-osmosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06891 "Process in which water is transported across the thickness of an anion- exchange or cation-exchange membrane under an applied electric field.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET06891 "electrosmosis")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET06891 "electroosmosis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06895 "enantiotropic mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06895 "A mesophase that is thermodynamically stable over a definite temperature or pressure range.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06907 "environmental monitoring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06907 "Continuous or repeated measurement of agents in the (working) environment to evaluate environmental exposure and possible damage by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and resultant health effects.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET06907 "ambient monitoring")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06922))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06922 "electro-osmotic hold-up time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06922 "Time required for a liquid in a capillary to move due to electro-osmosis through the effective length of the capillary, \\(L_{\\rm{eff}}\\). This time is usually measured as the migration time of a neutral compound, called an electro-osmotic flow marker which is assumed to have an electro-osmotic mobility that is negligible compared to that of the analyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06923 "electro-osmotic mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06923 """Electro-osmotic velocity divided by electric field (strength) \\[\\mu _{\\rm{eo}}=\\frac{
u _{\\rm{eo}}}{E}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET06925))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET06925 "electrophoretic velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET06925 "The velocity of a charged analyte under the influence of an electric field relative to the background electrolyte. Also called rate of migration (in electrophoresis).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07022 "electron-counting rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07022 "Rules establishing correspondence between the topology of a molecular structure and the number of electrons which may be placed into its bonding molecular orbitals. In the origin of various electron-counting rules lies a general assumption that the completeness of the valence electron shell of a molecular entity belonging to a certain structural type serves as the major criterion of structural stability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07023 "electron-deficient compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07023 "Molecules or ions that contain too few electrons to allow their bonding to be described exclusively in terms of two-center, two-electron, i.e., covalent, bonds. Such molecules or certain fragments in these are often held together by the multicenter bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07024 "electron density function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07024 "The electron probability distribution function, \\(\\rho \\), defined as \\[\\rho (\\mathbf{r}) = n\\ \\int \\Psi ^{\\rm{*}}\\left[\\mathbf{r}(1),\\mathbf{r}(2)\\,\\rm{...}\\,\\mathbf{r}(n)\\right]\\ \\Psi \\left[\\mathbf{r}(1),\\mathbf{r}(2)\\,\\rm{...}\\,\\mathbf{r}(n)\\right]\\rm{d}\\mathbf{r}(2)\\,\\rm{...}\\,\\rm{d}\\mathbf{r}(n)\\] where \\(\\Psi \\) is an electronic wave-function and integration is made over the coordinates of all but the first electron of \\(n\\). The physical interpretation of the electron density function is that \\(\\rho \\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{\\mathbf{r}}\\) gives the probability of finding an electron in a volume element \\(\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{\\mathbf{r}}\\), i.e., electron density in this volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07025 "electronic chemical potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07025 "The quantity that measures the escaping tendency of electrons from a species in its ground state. It is the negative of the absolute electronegativity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07026 "electronic state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07026 "An arrangement allowed by the laws of quantum mechanics of electrons within an atom, molecule (or system of molecules).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07027 "electronic stability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07027 "Unavailability of another electronic structure (different electronic state) of lower energy with the same number of electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07028 "energy gradient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07028 "First derivatives of the total energy with respect to nuclear coordinates, i.e., negative values of forces on the nuclei. Evaluation of energy gradient plays a central role in searching potential energy surfaces for stationary points. A widespread technique is based on the minimization of the gradient norm (the square of energy gradient).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07029 "energy hypersurface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07029 "The notion of hypersurface is used to stress the multidimensionality of PESs. In a molecular system consisting of \\(N\\) atomic nuclei, the number of the independent coordinates that fully determine the PES is equal to \\(3\\ N-6\\) (or \\(3\\ N-5\\) if the system is linear).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET07029 "potential energy surface")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07030 "equalization of electronegativity, principle of")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07030 "The postulate that in a molecule all the constituent atoms should have the same electronegativity value, which would be the geometric mean of the electronegativities of isolated atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07031))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07031 "equilibrium geometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07031 "Molecular geometry that corresponds to the true minimum on the respective potential energy surface. While information relating to the equilibrium geometry is provided by calculations within the adiabatic approximation (minimization of the total energy with respect to any independent geometrical parameter), various experiments yield some effective geometries for the molecule which are averaged over molecular vibrations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07032 "extended Hückel MO method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07032 "A semi-empirical all-valence electron quantum mechanical method which uses the same approximations, apart from $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}approximation}$ and neglect of overlap integrals, as those of the Hückel molecular orbital theory. The method reproduces relatively well the shapes and the order of energy levels of molecular orbitals. The account for overlap makes it possible to describe the net destabilization caused by interaction of two doubly occupied orbitals, which effect is not reproduced by HMO theory.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET07032 "EHMO method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07034 "exchange repulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07034 "The correction to the Coulomb repulsion between two electrons in orbitals \\(\\Psi _{i}\\) and \\(\\Psi _{j}\\) for the case when the electrons possess parallel spins. It is to be subtracted from the Coulomb repulsion to give the total energy of the electron–electron interaction. In the Hartree–Fock theory the magnitude of the exchange repulsion is given by the exchange integral \\[K_{ij}=\\int \\int \\Psi _{i}^{\\rm{*}}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{1}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{j}^{\\rm{*}}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{1}\\right)\\ \\left(\\frac{e^{2}}{r_{12}}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{i}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{2}\\right)\\ \\Psi _{j}\\left(\\mathbf{r}_{2}\\right)  \\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}_{1}  \\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}_{2}=\\lt ij|ji \\gt\\] For the case of electrons with opposite spins \\(K_{ij}\\) vanishes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07171 "electroluminescent polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07171 "Polymeric material that shows luminescence when an electric current passes through it such that charge carriers can combine at luminescent sites to give rise to electronically excited states of luminescent groups or molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07205 "electrically conducting polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07205 "Polymeric material that exhibits bulk electric conductivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07249 "exfoliation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07249 "Process by which the layers of a multi-layered structure separate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07267 "extender")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07267 "Substance, especially a diluent or modifier, added to a polymer to increase its volume without substantially altering the desirable properties of the polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07367 "electron back-transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07367 "Thermal inversion of excited-state electron transfer restoring the donor and acceptor in their original oxidation state. In using this term one should also specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor. This term is preferred to back electron-transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07368))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07368 "electrostriction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07368 "Contraction of a dielectric in an electric-field gradient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07369 "El-Sayed rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07369 "The rate of intersystem crossing is relatively large if the radiationless transition involves a change of orbital type.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07370 "emission anisotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07370 "Used to characterize luminescence (fluorescence, phosphorescence) polarization resulting from photoselection. Defined as: \\[r = \\frac{I_{\\parallel} - I_{\\perp }}{I_{\\parallel} + 2I_{\\perp }}\\] where \\(I_{\\parallel}\\) and \\(I_{\\perp }\\) are the intensities measured with the linear polarizer for emission parallel and perpendicular, respectively, to the electric vector of linearly polarized incident electromagnetic radiation (which is often vertical). The quantity \\(I_{\\parallel} + 2I_{\\perp }\\) is proportional to the total fluorescence intensity \\(I\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07371 "entrainment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07371 "Term used in photostimulated radical nucleophilic substitution to designate the induction of the reaction of a less reactive (or non-reactive) nucleophile with a substrate by the addition of catalytic amounts of another nucleophile, more reactive at initiation. Often, the non-reactive nucleophile at initiation is, however, quite reactive at propagation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07372 "excimer lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07372 "Non-coherent source of ultraviolet radiation capable of producing quasi-monochromatic radiation from the near UV (\\(\\lambda = 354\\ \\rm{nm}\\)) to the vacuum UV (\\(\\lambda = 126\\ \\rm{nm}\\)). The operation of the excimer lamps relies on the radiative decomposition of excimers or exciplexes created by various types of discharges.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07373))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07373 "exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07373 "Term generally applied to the time-integrated radiation incident from all upward directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07374))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07374 "<i>E</i>/<i>Z</i> photoisomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07374 "General designation of a photoinduced geometrical isomerization of a C–C double bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07547 "elastomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07547 "Polymer that displays rubber-like elasticity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07565 "entanglement network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07565 "Polymer network with junction points or zones formed by physically entangled chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07598 "elastically active network chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07598 "Segment of a chain between two successive crosslinks in a polymer network that is long enough to show entropic elasticity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07615 "electron beam curing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07615 "Curing induced by electron beam irradiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ET07615 "EB curing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02313 "<i>fac</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02313 "An affix used in names to designate three groups occupying the corners of the same face of an octahedral coordination sphere, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02314 "factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02314 "Proportionality constant between two quantities of the same dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02316 "fallout")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02316 "A measurement of air contamination consisting of the mass rate at which solid particles deposit from the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02319 "fanning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02319 "In blast furnace operation, the idling period between the blowing periods when the blast pressure is reduced to a minimum. This also applies to plume behaviour under inversion conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02320 "farad")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02320 "SI derived unit of electric capacitance, \\(\\pu{F} = \\pu{C V-1} = \\pu{m-2 kg-1 s4 A2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02321 "faradaic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02321 "A current corresponding to the reduction or oxidation of some chemical substance. The net faradaic current is the algebraic sum of all the faradaic currents flowing through an indicator or working electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02322 "faradaic current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02322 "Measures the rate of the interfacial reaction, the proportionality constant being the charge number of the reaction multiplied by the Faraday constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02323))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02323 "faradaic demodulation current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02323 "Component of the current that is due to the demodulation associated with an electrode reaction and that appears if an indicator or working electrode is subjected to the action of two intermodulated applied potentials of different frequency.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02324))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02324 "faradaic rectification current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02324 "A component of the current that is due to the rectifying properties of an electrode reaction and that appears if an indicator or working electrode is subjected to any periodically varying applied potential while the mean value of the applied potential is controlled.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02325))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02325 "Faraday constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02325 "Fundamental physical constant representing molar elementary charge: \\(F = \\pu{9.64853399(24)E4 C mol-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02326))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02326 "Faraday cup collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02326 "A hollow collector, open at one end and closed at the other, used to collect beams of ions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02326 "cylinder collector")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02327 "fast atom bombardment ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02327 "This term is used to describe ionization of any species by causing interaction of the sample (which may be dissolved in a solvent matrix) and a beam of neutral atoms having high translational energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02328))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02328 "fast-atom bombardment mass spectrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02328 "A method in which ions are produced in a mass spectrometer from non-volatile or thermally fragile organic molecules by bombarding the compound in the condensed phase with energy-rich neutral particles.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02328 "FAB mass spectrometry")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02329 "fast neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02329 "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than some specified value. This value may vary over a wide range and will be dependent upon the application.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02330))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02330 "fatty acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02330 "Aliphatic monocarboxylic acids derived from or contained in esterified form in an animal or vegetable fat, oil or wax. Natural fatty acids commonly have a chain of 4 to 28 carbons (usually unbranched and even-numbered), which may be saturated or unsaturated. By extension, the term is sometimes used to embrace all acyclic aliphatic carboxylic acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02331 "feedback")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02331 "The process whereby the output of a device is used to modify the operation of an analytical instrument.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02333 "feed-back inhibition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02333 "A metabolic control mechanism in which the end product of a biochemical sequence is able to inhibit the activity of an early enzyme in the sequence, thereby controlling the metabolic flux through this pathway. As an example, isoleucine controls its own synthesis by inhibiting threonine deaminase; adenosine 5-triphosphate (ATP) and citrate control glycolysis by inhibiting phosphofructokinase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02333 "end product inhibition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02334 "feed rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02334 "The amount of reactant fed per unit time to the inlet of the reactor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02335 "femto")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02335 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-15}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{f}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02336 "fenestranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02336 "Compounds of the general formula shown above. They may be considered to be spiro compounds having bridges of carbon atoms connecting the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!'}$ positions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02337 "fermentation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02337 "In metabolism, the dehydrogenating degradation of organic substance by organisms or cells under anaerobic conditions in which electrons are transferred to metabolites which accumulate and are excreted in reduced form. Fermentation is only possible if the organism is able to gain energy by this process.In microbiology, the process in which cells (microorganisms, plant or animal cells) are cultured in a bioreactor in liquid or solid medium to convert organic substances into biomass (growth) or into products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02338 "fermenter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02338 "A bioreactor which enables optimal fermentation conditions to be maintained, allowing addition of nutrients, removal of products and insertion of measuring and/or control probes as well as other necessary equipment (e.g. for heating, cooling, aeration, agitation, sterilization, etc.) under sterile conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02339 "fermi")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02339 "Non-SI unit of length, \\(\\pu{f} = \\pu{E-15 m}\\), used in nuclear physics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02340 "Fermi energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02340 "The total energy of an electron in an uncharged metal at the Fermi level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02341))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02341 "Fermi level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02341 "The chemical potential of electrons in a solid (metals, semiconductors or insulators) or in an electrolyte solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02342 "fermion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02342 "Particle of half-integer spin quantum number following Fermi–Dirac statistics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02345 "ferrocenophanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02345 "Compounds in which the two ring components of ferrocene are linked by one or more bridging chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02346 "ferroelastic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02346 "A transition in which a crystal switches from one stable orientation state into another that is equally stable by the application of a mechanical stress along an appropriate direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02347 "ferroelectric transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02347 "A transition from a ferroelectric to either another ferroelectric, or a paraelectric, or an antiferroelectric state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02348 "ferroic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02348 "A general term for ferroelastic, ferroelectric, antiferroelectric, ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic and ferrimagnetic transitions in which a suitable driving force switches a ferroic crystal from one orientation state, or domain state, to another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02350 "fertile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02350 "Of a nuclide, capable of being transformed, directly or indirectly, into a fissile nuclide by neutron captureOf a material: containing one or more fertile nuclides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02351 "<i>f</i>-functional branch point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02351 "Branch point from which f linear chains emanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02353 "fibrous activated carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02353 "An activated carbon in the form of fibres, filaments, yarns or rovings and fabrics or felts. Such fibres differ from carbon fibres used for reinforcement purposes in composites by their high surface area, high porosity and low mechanical strength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02355 "fibrous crystal in polymers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02355 "A type of crystal significantly longer in one dimension than in either of the other two. Fibrous crystals may comprise essentially extended chains parallel to the fibre axis; however, macroscopic polymer fibres containing chain-folded crystals are also known.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02357 "field desorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02357 "A term used to describe the formation of ions in the gas phase from a material deposited on a solid surface (known as an 'emitter') in the presence of a high electrical field. 'Field desorption' is an ambiguous term because it implies that the electric field desorbs a material as an ion from some kind of emitter on which the material is deposited. There is growing evidence that some of the ions formed are due to thermal ionization and some to field ionization of vapour evaporated from material on the emitter. Because there is generally little or no ionization unless the emitter is heated by an electric current, 'field desorption' is a misnomer. The term is, however, firmly implanted in the literature and most users undertand what is going on regardless of the implications of the term. In addition, no better simple term has been suggested to take its place and so, reluctantly, it is recommended that it be retained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02358 "field effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02358 "An experimentally observable effect symbolized by \\(F\\) (on reaction rates, etc.) of intramolecular coulombic interaction between the centre of interest and a remote unipole or dipole, by direct action through space rather than through bonds. The magnitude of the field effect (or 'direct effect') depends on the unipolar charge/dipole moment, orientation of dipole, shortest distance between the centre of interest and the remote unipole or dipole, and on the effective dielectric constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02360))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02360 "field ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02360 "The removal of electrons from any species by interaction with a high electrical field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02361 "field level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02361 "Logarithm of the ratio of two values of a field quantity, usually amplitude. Levels are expressed in different ways: \\(L_{F}=\\ln (\\frac{F}{F_{0}})\\ N_{\\rm{p}}\\), where \\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\) is the symbol for the unit neper coherent with the SI, or \\(L_{F}=20\\ \\log _{10}(\\frac{F}{F_{0}})\\ \\mathrm{dB}\\), where \\(\\mathrm{dB}\\) is the symbol for the unit decibel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02362))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02362 "filamentous carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02362 "A carbonaceous deposit from gaseous carbon compounds, consisting of filaments grown by the catalytic action of metal particles.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02362 "fibrous carbon")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02363))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02363 "filler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02363 "Filler (also called grist) is a petroleum- or coal-based coke fraction of a green, carbon mix or formulation. Coarse particles, \\(>0.425\\ \\mathrm{mm}\\), are sometimes referred to as tailings; fine particles, \\(")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02363 "grist")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02364))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02364 "filler coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02364 "The main constituent of a carbon artifact, introduced as solid component (predominantly in the form of particulate carbon) into the 'carbon mix' from which polygranular carbon and graphite materials are obtained by heat treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02365 "filling solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02365 "Solution containing the anion to which the reference electrode of the operational pH cell is reversible, e.g. chloride for silver-silver chloride electrode. In the absence of a bridge solution, a high concentration of filling solution comprising cations and anions of almost equal mobility is employed as a means of maintaining the liquid junction potential small and approximately constant on substitution of test solution for standard solution(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02366 "film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02366 "A generic term referring to condensed matter restricted in one dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02368))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02368 "film badge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02368 "A package containing one or more small photographic films for approximate measurement of radiation exposure, used for the purpose of monitoring personnel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02370 "film element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02370 "A small homogeneous part of a film including the two interfaces and any fluid between them.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02381 "film tension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02381 "The contractile force per unit length, \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3A3}_{\\rm{f}}\\), exerted by an equilibrium film in contact with the bulk phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02384 "filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02384 "A porous material on which solid particles present in air or other fluid which flows through it are largely caught and retained. Filters are made with a variety of materials: cellulose and derivatives, glass fibre, ceramic, synthetic plastics and fibres. Filters may be naturally porous or be made so by mechanical or other means. membrane/ceramic filters are prepared with highly controlled pore size in a sheet of suitable material such as polyfluoroethylene, polycarbonate or cellulose esters. Nylon mesh is sometimes used for reinforcement. The pores constitute 80–85% of the filter volume commonly and several pore sizes are available for air sampling (\\(0.45{-}0.8\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) are commonly employed).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02385 "filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02385 "Material interposed in the path of radiation to modify the spectral distribution of the radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02387 "filter spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02387 "A spectrometer which has one or more spectral filters for isolating one or more spectral bands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02388 "filtration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02388 "The process of segregation of phases; e.g. the separation of suspended solids from a liquid or gas, usually by forcing a carrier gas or liquid through a porous medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02389 "fine structure constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02389 "Atomic fundamental physical constant, \\(\\alpha =\\frac{\\mu _{0}\\ e^{2}\\ c_{0}}{2\\ h} = 7.297\\ 353\\ 08\\ \\left(33\\right)\\times 10^{-3}\\), where \\(\\mu _{0}\\) is the permeability of vacuum, \\(e\\) the elementary charge, \\(h\\) the Planck constant, and \\(c_{0}\\) the speed of light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02390 "first-order phase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02390 "A transition in which the molar Gibbs energies or molar Helmholtz energies of the two phases (or chemical potentials of all components in the two phases) are equal at the transition temperature, but their first derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure (for example, specific enthalpy of transition and specific volume) are discontinuous at the transition point, as for two dissimilar phases that coexist and that can be transformed into one another by a change in a field variable such as pressure, temperature, magnetic or electric field. Example: The transition on heating $\\ce{CsCl}$ to \\(752\\ \\rm{K}\\) at which it changes from the low-temperature, $\\ce{CsCl}$-type structure to the high-temperature $\\ce{NaCl}$-type structure. Synonymous with discontinuous phase transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02390 "discontinuous phase transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02391 "Fischer projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02391 "A projection formula in which vertically drawn bonds are considered to lie below the projection plane and horizontal bonds to lie above that plane. Thus for the molecule Cabcd:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02391 "Fischer–Tollens projection")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02392))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02392 "Fischer–Rosanoff convention")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02392 "An arbitrary convention according to which (+)-glyceraldehyde, now known to be (R)-2,3-dihydroxypropanal, was named ᴅ-glyceraldehyde (with the enantiomer ʟ-glyceraldehyde and its racemate ᴅʟ-glyceraldehyde) and taken to have the absolute configuration represented by the Fischer projection formula shown in the diagram.  The atom numbered 1 according to normal nomenclature rules is conventionally placed at the top of the main chain, which is drawn vertically and other groups are drawn on either side of that main chain. The convention is still in use for $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino acids}$ and for sugars.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02392 "Rosanoff convention")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02394 "fissile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02394 "Of a nuclide: capable of undergoing fission by interaction with (slow) neutrons.Of a material: containing one or more fissile nuclides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02395 "fissionable")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02395 "Synonymous with fissile.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02397))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02397 "fission fragments")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02397 "Nuclei resulting from fission and possessing kinetic energy acquired from the fission process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02398 "fission neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02398 "Neutrons originating in the fission process which have retained their original energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02400 "fission products")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02400 "Nuclides produced by fission and the daughter products of these nuclides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02401 "fission yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02401 "The fraction of fissions leading to fission products of a given type.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02402 "fixed ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02402 "In an ion exchanger, the non-exchangeable ions which have a charge opposite to that of the counter-ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02403 "fixed neutral loss scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02403 "A scan which determines, in a single experiment, all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios which react to the loss or gain of a selected neutral mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02404 "fixed neutral loss spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02404 "A spectrum obtained when data are acquired that determine all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react by the loss (gain) of a selected neutral mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02405 "fixed precursor ion scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02405 "A scan which determines, in a single experiment, all the daughter ion mass-to-charge ratios that are produced by the reaction of a selected parent ion mass-to-charge ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02406))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02406 "fixed product ion scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02406 "A scan which determines, in a single experiment, all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react to produce a selected daughter ion mass-to-charge ratio (sometimes called a 'parent ion scan').")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02407))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02407 "fixed product ion spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02407 "A spectrum obtained when data are acquired that determine all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react to produce a selected daughter ion mass-to-charge ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02409 "flame-in-tube atomizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02409 "An atomizer in which hydrogen, used as carrier gas, is partially combusted in the inlet arm of a T-shaped tube furnace by introducing a limited amount of oxygen or air.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02410 "flame ionization detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02410 "The gases emerging from the column are fed into a hydrogen flame across which an electrical potential is placed. Certain molecules ionize easily in the flame and the current produced is proportional to the instantaneous flow rate of the eluted component. The detector is relatively insensitive to inorganic molecules and is most used for organic compounds. Concentrations below \\(1\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\) are easily detected. The linear dynamic range is between \\(10^{3}\\) and \\(10^{5}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02411 "flame photometric detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02411 "The eluent from the column is fed into a hydrogen-rich flame and produces light emission. Optical filters are used to select the wavelength range of the emission which is characteristic of specific atoms (usually sulfur or phosphorus). The detector is very specific, depending on the choice of optical filters. It can detect the S- and P-containing compounds down to \\(10^{-3}\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\), but the detector is non-linear.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02412 "flame photometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02412 "The use of emission spectroscopy in the ultraviolet and visible regions to identify and estimate the amounts of various elements which are excited in a flame, an arc or high voltage spark.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02416 "flash fluorimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02416 "The term used when decay times luminescence are measured using a pulsed source of radiation. It is often necessary to separate the signal due to the light flash from luminescence emission signal by a deconvolution technique in order to obtain the correct decay curve for emission. Decay times corrected for this effect are termed corrected decay times of fluorescence or phosphorescence.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02416 "phosphorimetry")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02417 "flash lamps")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02417 "Lamps which contain an inert gas which can be rapidly pulsed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02418 "flash photolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02418 "A technique of transient spectroscopy and transient kinetic studies in which a light pulse is used to produce transient species. Commonly, an intense pulse of short duration is used to produce a sufficient concentration of a transient species suitable for spectroscopic observation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02419))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02419 "flash point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02419 "The lowest temperature at which a substance, e.g. fuel oil, will give off a vapour that will flash or burn momentarily when ignited.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02420 "flash vacuum pyrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02420 "Thermal reaction of a molecule by exposing it to a short thermal shock at high temperature, usually in the gas phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02421))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02421 "flat band potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02421 "If effects of surface states are negligible, \\(\\Delta \\varphi ^{\\rm{sc}}\\), the potential drop associated with the space charge in the semiconductor, vanishes as the charge on the semiconductor becomes zero. The potential of the semiconductor corresponding to this condition is the flat band potential, which plays the same role as the potential of zero charge for metals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02423 "flavins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02423 "Derivatives of the dimethylisoalloxazine (7,8-dimethylbenzo[g]pteridine- 2,4(3H,10H)-dione) skeleton, with a substituent on the 10 position. (Riboflavin, having a 10-ᴅ-ribityl group, is the most prominent member).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02424 "flavonoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02424 "Flavonoids, isoflavonoids and neoflavonoids are natural products derived from 2-phenylchromen-4-one (flavone), 3-phenylchromen-4-one and 4-phenylcoumarin, respectively. Derivations include reduction of the 2(3) carbon-carbon double bond (flavanones), reduction of the keto group (flavanols), and hydroxylation at various positions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02425 "flavoproteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02425 "Flavins tightly bound or covalently attached to a protein chain, commonly through the 8-methyl carbon atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02428 "floc")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02428 "When a sol is colloidally unstable the formation of aggregates is called coagulation or flocculation. Some authors distinguish between coagulation and flocculation, the latter implying the formation of a loose or open network which may or may not separate macroscopically. The loose structure formed in this way is called a floc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02430 "floccule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02430 "A small loosely aggregated mass of material suspended in or precipitated from a liquid; a cluster of particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02431))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02431 "Flory–Huggins theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02431 "A thermodynamic theory of polymer solutions, first formulated independently by Flory and by Huggins, in which the thermodynamic quantities of the solution are derived from a simple concept of combinatorial entropy of mixing and a reduced Gibbs energy parameter, the 'χ parameter'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02432 "flotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02432 "The removal of matter by entrainment at an interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02433 "flow analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02433 "The generic name for all analytical methods that are based on the introduction and processing of test samples in flowing media.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02436 "flow injection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02436 "The introduction of a sample or reagent into a continuous stream by use of a rapid delivery device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02437 "flow injection enthalpimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02437 "A term used to describe several related methods in which a transient temperature change in a flowing liquid stream, caused by a chemical reaction, is used to quantitatively determine an analyte. The analyte is introduced as a discrete liquid sample (i.e. test portion) into the flowing stream. This definition limits the scope to experiments with primarily an analytical emphasis, performed in a flow injection calorimeter. This maintains a clear distinction from classical flow calorimetric methods where reagents are combined continuously in flowing streams, or where the heat effect is measured via the flowing of a fluid over a reaction vessel. An acceptable synonym is enthalpimetric flow injection analysis. Other non-recommended terms which have been used in the literature include 'peak enthalpimetry' and 'enzyme thermistor'. Flow enthalpimetry has been used to describe the flow injection technique, but its use for this purpose is discouraged.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02437 "enthalpimetric flow injection analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02437 "peak enthalpimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02437 "flow enthalpimetry")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02438 "flow-programmed chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02438 "A procedure in which the rate of flow of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02439 "flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02439 "The volume of mobile phase passing through the column in unit time. The flow rate is usually measured at column outlet, at ambient pressure (\\(p_{\\rm{a}}\\)) and temperature (\\(T_{\\rm{a}}\\), in \\(\\rm{K}\\)); this value is indicated with the symbol \\(F\\). If a water-containing flowmeter was used for the measurement (e.g. the so-called soap bubble flowmeter) then \\(F\\) must be corrected to dry gas conditions in order to obtain the mobile phase flow rate at ambient temperature (\\(F_{\\rm{a}}\\)): \\[F_{\\rm{a}}=F\\ (1- \\frac{p_{\\rm{w}}}{p_{\\rm{a}}})\\] where \\(p_{\\rm{w}}\\) is the partial pressure of water vapour at ambient temperature. In order to specify chromatographic conditions in column chromatography, the flow-rate (mobile phase flow rate at column temperature, \\(F_{\\rm{c}}\\)) must be expressed at \\(T_{\\rm{c}}\\) (kelvin), the column temperature: \\[F_{\\rm{c}}=F_{\\rm{a}}\\ \\frac{T_{\\rm{c}}}{T_{\\rm{a}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02440 "flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02440 "The volume rate of one component, $\\ce{\\textbf{X}}$ (e.g. air, $\\ce{O2}$), of the unburnt gas mixture (such as $\\ce{C2H2}$, $\\ce{O2}$, etc.) in the burner tip. Ambient temperature and pressure must be stated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02441 "flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02441 "Quantity \\(X\\) (e.g. heat, amount, mass, volume, ...) transferred in a time interval divided by that time interval. General symbols: \\(q_{X}\\), \\(\\overset{.}{X}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02442 "flow rate of unburnt gas mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02442 "The volume rate of the gas mixture of the burner tip. It depends on operating conditions such as gas pressure; the ambient temperature and pressure should also be stated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02443 "flow reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02443 "A reactor through which the reactants pass while catalysis is in progress.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02444 "flow resistance parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02444 "A term used to compare packing density and permeability of columns packed with different particles; it is dimensionless. \\[\\mathit{\\Phi} = \\frac{d_{\\rm{p}}^{2}}{B_{0}}\\] where \\(d_{\\rm{p}}\\) is the average particle diameter. In open-tubular columns \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} = 32\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02446 "flue gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02446 "Waste gas from the combustion process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02447 "flue gas scrubber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02447 "Equipment for removing fly ash and other objectionable materials from the products of combustion by means of sprays or wet baffles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02448))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02448 "fluence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02448 "At a given point in space, the radiant energy incident on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. It is used in photochemistry to specify the energy delivered in a given time interval (for instance by a laser pulse).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02449))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02449 "fluid coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02449 "The carbonization product of high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions (heavy residues of petroleum or coal processing) produced by the fluid coking process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02450 "fluidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02450 "Reciprocal of dynamic viscosity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02451 "fluidized bed")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02451 "A catalyst bed in which the flow of gases is sufficient to cause the finely divided particles to behave like a fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02453 "fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02453 "Luminescence which occurs essentially only during the irradiation of a substance by electromagnetic radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02455 "fluorescence error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02455 "Some samples are excited to fluorescence by the incident radiation beam. When added to the transmitted radiation beam this fluorescence results in an erroneous reading called the fluorescence error. It is particularly noticeable if the photodetector is situated close to the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02457 "fluorescence yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02457 "For a given transition from an excited state of a specified atom, the ratio of the number of excited atoms which emit a photon to the total number of excited atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02458 "fluorimeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02458 "An instrument used to measure the intensity and the wavelength distribution of the light emitted as fluorescence from a molecule excited at a specific wavelength or wavelengths within the absorption band of a particular compound. Characteristic fluorescence bands may be used to identify specific pollutants such as the polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons. Excitation spectra of impurities can be observed by scanning the wavelength of the excitation light which is incident on the sample over a range of wavelengths and observing the relative intensity of the fluorescence emitted at a given wavelength. These spectra are also characteristic of the impurity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02459))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02459 "fluorocarbons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02459 "Compounds consisting wholly of fluorine and carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02461))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02461 "flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02461 "Flow rate of X through a cross-section perpendicular to the flow divided by the cross-sectional area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02462))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02462 "flux depression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02462 "The lowering of the particle (or photon) flux density in the neighbourhood of an object due to absorption of particles (or photons) in the object.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02463))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02463 "fluxional")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02463 "A chemical species is said to be fluxional if it undergoes rapid degenerate rearrangements (generally detectable by methods which allow the observation of the behaviour of individual nuclei in a rearranged chemical species, e.g. NMR, X-ray). Example: bullvalene (1 209 600 interconvertible arrangements of the ten CH groups).  The term is also used to designate positional change among ligands of complex compounds and organometallics. In these cases, the change is not necessarily degenerate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02465))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02465 "fly ash")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02465 "Finely divided particles of ash entrained in flue gases resulting from the combustion of fuel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02467 "foam")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02467 "A dispersion in which a large proportion of gas by volume in the form of gas bubbles, is dispersed in a liquid, solid or gel. The diameter of the bubbles is usually larger than \\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\), but the thickness of the lamellae between the bubbles is often in the usual colloidal size range. The term froth has been used interchangeably with foam. In particular cases froth may be distinguished from foam by the fact that the former is stabilized by solid particles (as in froth flotation q.v.) and the latter by soluble substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02468 "foam fractionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02468 "A method of separation in which a component of the bulk liquid is preferentially adsorbed at the liquid/vapour (L/V) interface and is removed by foaming.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02469 "foaming agent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02469 "A surfactant which when present in small amounts facilitates the formation of a foam, or enhances its colloidal stability by inhibiting the coalescence of bubbles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02470 "fog")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02470 "A general term applied to a suspension of droplets in a gas. In meteorology, it refers to a suspension of water droplets resulting in a visibility of less than \\(1\\ \\rm{km}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02471))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02471 "fog horizon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02471 "The top of a fog layer which is confined by a low-level temperature inversion and gives the appearance of the horizon (which it actually obscures) when viewed from above, against the sky.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02472 "folates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02472 "A group of heterocyclic compounds based on the pteroic acid skeleton (below) conjugated with one or more ʟ-glutamate units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02473 "fold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02473 "The loop connecting two different stems in a folded chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02474))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02474 "fold domain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02474 "A portion of a polymer crystal wherein the fold planes have the same orientation. The sectors of lamellar crystals frequently represent fold domains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02475))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02475 "folded-chain crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02475 "A polymer crystal consisting predominantly of chains that traverse the crystal repeatedly by folding as they emerge at its external surfaces. The re-entry of the chain into the crystal is assumed to be adjacent or near-adjacent within the lattice.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02476))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02476 "fold plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02476 "A crystallographic plane defined by a large number of stems that are connected by chain folds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02477))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02477 "fold surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02477 "A surface approximately tangential to the folds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02478 "foot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02478 "Non-SI unit of length, \\(\\pu{ft} = \\pu{12 in} = \\pu{0.3048 m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02480 "force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02480 "Derivative of momentum with respect to time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02481 "force constants")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02481 "The coefficients in an expansion of the intramolecular potential function in terms of a definite set of coordinates, the values of which define the deformation of the molecule away from its equilibrium configuration. In order to define force constants, one has to indicate both the definition of each coordinate and the expansion of the potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02484 "formamidine disulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02484 "The compound $\\ce{H2NC(=NH)SSC(=NH)NH2}$ and its derivatives formed by substitution at nitrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02485 "formation constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02485 "For a mononuclear binary complex, if a central atom (central group) $\\ce{M}$ (the 'metal') and a ligand $\\ce{L}$ have been defined, then in the following expressions \\(K_{n}\\) is the stepwise formation constant, and \\(\\beta_{n}\\) is the cumulative formation constant for the complex $\\ce{ML_{n}}$. They can both be referred to as stability constants (stepwise and cumulative). \\[K_{n}=K(\\rm{ML}_{n- 1}+\\rm{L}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm{ML}_{n})\\] \\[\\beta_{n} = K(\\rm{M}+n\\rm{L}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm{ML}_{n})\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02486 "formazans")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02486 "The parent compound $\\ce{H2NN=CHN=NH}$ and its derivatives formed by substitution at carbon and/or nitrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02487 "Förster cycle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02487 "An indirect method of determination of excited state equilibria, such as \\(\\rm{p}K^{\\rm{*,a}}\\) values, based on ground state thermodynamics and electronic transition energies. This cycle considers only the difference in molar enthalpy change (\\(\\Delta \\Delta H\\)) of reaction of ground and excited states, neglecting the difference in molar entropy change of reaction of those states (\\(\\Delta \\Delta S\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02488 "Förster excitation transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02488 "A mechanism of excitation transfer which can occur between molecular entities separated by distances considerably exceeding the sum of their van der Waals radii. It is described in terms of an interaction between the transition dipole moments (a dipolar mechanism). The transfer rate constant \\(k_{D \\rightarrow  A}\\) is given by: \\[k_{\\ce{D -> A}} = \\frac{K^{2}J \\pu{8.8E-28 mol}}{n^{4} \\tau_{0} r^{6}}\\] where \\(K\\) is an orientation factor, \\(n\\) the refractive index of the medium, \\(\\tau _{0}\\) the radiative lifetime of the donor, \\(r\\) the distance (cm) between donor (D) and acceptor (A), and \\(J\\) the spectral overlap (in coherent units \\(\\pu{cm6 mol-1}\\)) between the absorption spectrum of the acceptor and the fluorescence spectrum of the donor. The critical quenching radius, \\(r_{0}\\), is that distance at which \\(k_{D \\rightarrow  A}\\) is equal to the inverse of the radiative lifetime.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02488 "dipole–dipole excitation transfer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02490 "fossil fuel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02490 "A fuel such as coal, oil and natural gas which has formed over many years through the decomposition of deposited vegetation which was under extreme pressure of an overburden of earth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02491 "fouling agent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02491 "Fouling agents (or mechanical inhibitors) are inhibitory substances bound by neither covalent nor other strong bonds to the active centres: the interaction is usually of the van der Waals, H-bond or sometimes ionic, type. They form protective layers or block pores, thus physically impeding access of reactants to the active centres. The fouling agents which cause real problems are those which have a long standing effect and do not disappear spontaneously. Carbon deposits act, partially or totally, this way (see coking). Other examples are vanadium and nickel sulfide deposits in hydrotreating catalysts.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02491 "mechanical inhibitor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02492 "Fourier transform ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02492 "A high-frequency mass spectrometer in which the cyclotron motion of ions, having different mass/charge ratios, in a constant magnetic field is excited essentially simultaneously and coherently by a pulse or a radio-frequency electric field applied perpendicular to the magnetic field. The excited cyclotron motion of the ions is subsequently detected on so-called receiver plates as a time domain signal that contains all the cyclotron frequencies that have been excited. Fourier transformation of the time domain signal results in the frequency domain FT-ICR signal which, on the basis of the inverse proportionality between frequency and the mass/charge ratio, can be converted into a mass spectrum. The term is sometimes contracted to Fourier transform mass spectrometer (FT-MS).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02492 "FT-ICR mass spectrometer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02493 "Fourier transform spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02493 "A scanning interferometer, containing no principal dispersive element, which first splits a beam into two or more components, then recombines these with a phase difference. The spectrum is obtained by a Fourier transformation of the output of the interferometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02494 "fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02494 "In general, a ratio of two quantities of the same kind, the numerator quantity applying to one constituent (or part) of the system and the denominator to the sum of quantities for all constituents (parts) of the system. When applied to mixtures fractions represent a group of three quantities: mass fraction, volume fraction, and amount fraction (or amount fraction equal to the number fraction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02495 "fractional change of a quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02495 "A term which may be expressed infinitesimally at time \\(t\\) by the differential \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}Q(t)}{Q(t)}\\). For a finite time interval the quotient is \\[\\frac{\\Delta Q\\left(t_{1};t_{2}\\right)}{Q\\left(t_{1}\\right)} = \\frac{\\left[Q\\left(t_{2}\\right)\\,-\\,Q\\left(t_{1}\\right)\\right]}{Q\\left(t_{1}\\right)}\\] The quantities \\(Q(t_{1})\\) and \\(Q(t_{2})\\) are of the same kind and have the same type of component. Fractional change has dimension one. Examples are: mass fractional change, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m(t)}{m(t)}\\); amount of substance fractional change, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n(t)}{n(t)}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02496 "fractional selectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02496 "The term selectivity (\\(S\\)) is used to describe the relative rates of two or more competing reactions on a catalyst. Such competition includes cases of different reactants undergoing simultaneous reactions or of a single reactant taking part in two or more reactions. For the latter case, \\(S\\) may be defined in two ways. The first of these defines a fractional selectivity, \\(S_{\\rm{F}}\\), for each product by the equation \\[S_{\\rm{F}}=\\frac{\\xi _{i}}{\\sum \\xi _{i}}\\] The second defines relative selectivities, \\(S_{\\rm{R}}\\), for each pair of products by \\[S_{\\rm{R}}=\\frac{\\xi _{i}}{\\xi _{j}}\\] In each case, \\(\\xi _{i}\\) and \\(\\xi _{j}\\) are the rates of increase of the extent of reactions i and j respectively, i.e. \\[\\xi _{i}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi _{i}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] and \\[\\xi _{j}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi _{j}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] where \\(\\xi _{i}\\) and \\(\\xi _{j}\\) are the extents of reactions \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02497 "fractionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02497 "A process by means of which macromolecular species differing in some characteristic (chemical composition, relative molecular mass, branching, stereoregularity, etc.) are separated from each other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02501 "fraction collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02501 "A device for recovering fractional volumes of the column effluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02503 "fraction extracted")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02503 "The fraction of the total quantity of a substance extracted (usually by the solvent) under specified conditions, i.e. \\(E_{\\rm{A}} = \\frac{Q_{\\rm{A}}}{Q_{\\rm{A}}^{'}}\\) where \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the mass of A extracted and \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}^{'}\\) is the total mass of A present at the start.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02503 "recovery factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02506 "fragmentation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02506 "The heterolytic cleavage of a molecule according to the general reaction:  where $\\ce{{a}-b}$ is an electrofuge and $\\ce{X}$ is a nucleofuge (which may emerge from the reaction in combined form), and the middle group affords the unsaturated fragment $\\ce{c=d}$. For example, The breakdown of a radical into a diamagnetic molecule or ion and a smaller radical, e.g. The breakdown of a radical ion in a mass spectrometer or in solution, forming an ion of lower molar mass and a radical, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02507 "fragmentation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02507 "A fragmentation reaction may be written: \\[\\rm{M}_{1}^{+}\\rightarrow \\rm{M}_{2}^{+}+\\rm{M}_{3}\\] The decomposition of a metastable ion of mass-to-charge ratio \\(\\frac{m_{1}}{z_{1}}\\) into an ion of mass-to-charge ratio \\(\\frac{m_{2}}{z_{2}}\\) after electric acceleration of the metastable ion and before magnetic deflection gives rise to a peak in the mass spectrum at an apparent mass, \\(\\frac{m_{2}^{2}\\ z_{1}}{m_{1}\\ z_{2}^{2}}\\). The symbol \\(m^{*}\\) should be used to indicate the apparent mass of the product ions giving rise to this peak. Traditionally, the peak itself has been called a metastable peak and this should preferably be expressed as metastable ion peak. It is nevertheless recommended that the former term should be retained. The word metastable should never be used as a noun. Its use as an adjective should be limited to such terms as metastable ion, where it is used correctly, and to the special case of metastable peak discussed above. It should never be used in such terms as metastable reaction, metastable decomposition, metastable studies, etc. It should be clear that the metastable ion is the ion that undergoes fragmentation; it is not detected. When a reaction is written with an asterisk above the arrow as shown: \\[\\rm{M}_{1}^{+}\\overset{\\rm{*}}{\\rightarrow }\\rm{M}_{2}^{+}+\\rm{M}_{3}\\] this means that the reaction has been confirmed by the observation of a metastable peak. The textual description of such a process may be written as, for example, (\\(m^{*}\\), \\(43\\rightarrow 28\\), calcd. 18.2, obsd. 18.3) meaning 'for the fragmentation \\(\\frac{m}{z}\\ 43\\rightarrow \\frac{m}{z}\\ 28\\) a metastable peak at \\(\\frac{m}{z}\\) 18.3 (calculated 18.2) has been observed'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02508 "fragment ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02508 "An electrically charged dissociation product of an ionic fragmentation. Such an ion may dissociate further to form other electrically charged molecular or atomic moieties of successively lower formula weight.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02509 "frame-shift mutation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02509 "A point mutation involving either the deletion or insertion of a nucleotide in a gene. By the frame-shift mutation, the normal reading frame used when decoding the nucleotide triplets in the gene is altered to another reading frame.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02510 "Franck–Condon principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02510 "Classically, the Franck–Condon principle is the approximation that an electronic transition is most likely to occur without changes in the positions of the nuclei in the molecular entity and its environment. The resulting state is called a Franck–Condon state, and the transition involved, a vertical transition. The quantum mechanical formulation of this principle is that the intensity of a vibronic transition is proportional to the square of the overlap integral between the vibrational wavefunctions of the two states that are involved in the transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02512))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02512 "franklin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02512 "esu unit of electric charge, \\(\\rm{Fr}\\approx 3.335\\ 64\\times 10^{-10}\\ \\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02513 "free charge density on the interface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02513 "The physical charge density believed to occur on either side of the electrical double-layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02514 "free electron laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02514 "Source of coherent radiation in which the active medium is an electron beam moving at speeds close to the speed of light in the spatially periodic magnetic field produced by an array of magnets (the wiggler). The emitted wavelength, \\(\\lambda _{\\rm{L}}\\), is approximately given by \\[\\frac{\\lambda _{\\omega }}{4\\ E^{2}}\\] with \\(\\lambda _{\\omega }\\) being the wiggler period and \\(E\\) the electron's energy in \\(\\mathrm{MeV}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02516 "freely draining")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02516 "An adjective referring to a chain macromolecule the segments of which produce such small frictional effects when moving in a medium that the hydrodynamic field in the vicinity of a given segment is not affected by the presence of other segments. Thus, the solvent can flow virtually undisturbed through the domain occupied by a freely draining macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02517 "freely jointed chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02517 "A hypothetical linear chain molecule consisting of infinitely thin rectilinear segments uniform in length; each segment can take all orientations in space with equal probability, independently of its neighbours. For models in which the segments are not all uniform in length, the name 'random-walk chain' has been used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02518 "freely rotating chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02518 "A hypothetical linear chain molecule, free from short-range and long-range interactions, consisting of infinitely thin rectilinear segments (bonds) of fixed length, jointed at fixed bond angles; the torsion angles of the bonds can assume all values with equal probability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02520))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02520 "free rotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02520 "In a stereochemical context the rotation about a bond is called 'free' when the rotational barrier is so low that different conformations are not perceptible as different chemical species on the time scale of the experiment. The inhibition of rotation of groups about a bond due to the presence of a sufficiently large rotational barrier to make the phenomenon observable on the time scale of the experiment is termed hindered rotation or restricted rotation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02521 "free-running laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02521 "A term applied to a pulsed laser, meaning that the laser emission lasts as long as the pumping process is sufficient to sustain lasing conditions. Typical pulse durations are in the \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{s}\\)-\\(\\rm{ms}\\) range, depending on the pumping source. When the operation mode of a pulsed laser is not specified as mode-locked, Q-switched, or anything else, it must be considered as free-running.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02523 "freezing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02523 "The conversion of a liquid to a solid by lowering the temperature and/or the application of pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02524 "freezing out")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02524 "A term used in combustion for the analysis of incinerator flue gas components (largely the organic fraction) in which a series of traps at progressively lower temperatures is employed. In more general use, the term implies the removal of a condensable gas or liquid by condensation in a trap at low temperatures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02525 "frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02525 "Reciprocal of the period.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02526 "frequency distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02526 "If the range of observed measurement values is subdivided into a regular sequence of smaller intervals, this distribution is a plot of the frequency of occurrence of values falling into each interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02528 "frictional coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02528 "A tensor correlating the frictional force, \\(F\\), opposing the motion of a particle in a viscous fluid and the velocity \\(u\\) of this particle relative to the fluid. In the case of an isolated spherical particle in a viscous isotropic fluid, \\(f\\) is a constant and \\(F=f\\ u\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02530 "friction factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02530 "Quotient of frictional force and normal force, for a sliding body. Formerly called frictional coefficient or coefficient of friction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02530 "friction coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02531 "fringed-micelle model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02531 "A model of crystallinity in which the crystallized segments of a macromolecule belong predominantly to different crystals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02532 "frontal chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02532 "A procedure in which the sample (liquid or gas) is fed continuously into the chromatographic bed. In frontal chromatography no additional mobile phase is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02533 "frontier orbitals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02533 "The Highest-energy Occupied Molecular Orbital (HOMO) (filled or partly filled) and Lowest-energy Unoccupied Molecular Orbital (LUMO) (completely or partly vacant) of a molecular entity. Examination of the mixing of frontier molecular orbitals of reacting molecular entities affords an approach to the interpretation of reaction behaviour; this constitutes a simplified perturbation molecular orbital theory of chemical behaviour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02534 "fronting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02534 "Asymmetry of a peak such that, relative to the baseline, the front is less steep than the rear. In paper chromatography and thin-layer chromatography, it refers to the distortion of a spot showing a diffuse region in front of the spot in the direction of flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02535 "front surface geometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02535 "A system where excitation and observation are from the same face of the sample (\\(\\alpha\\ \\angle\\ 90^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), \\(\\beta\\ \\angle\\ 180^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) where \\(\\alpha \\) = angle of incidence of the exciting beam on the plane surface of the sample, and \\(\\beta \\) = angle between the exciting direction and observation direction.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02536 "frost point hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02536 "Instrument in which the sample is passed over a cooled surface. The temperature at which frost forms on it is a function of the water content of the gas passing over the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02537 "froth flotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02537 "The removal of particulate matter by foaming.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02538))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02538 "Frumkin effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02538 "In an electrode reaction, when reactants or intermediates are adsorbed, the rate of reaction may no longer be related to the concentration by a simple law. The deviation may be due to either entropic or energetic effects or both. The situation best understood is that where a reactant is non-specifically adsorbed in the outer Helmholtz plane (inner boundary of the diffuse layer). The effect of such adsorption on electrode kinetics is usually termed the Frumkin effect. Rate constants, transfer coefficients etc. corrected for this effect are frequently called 'true' rate constants etc. It would be preferable to describe them as 'corrected for the Frumkin effect', but in any case, if such a correction is carried out, the basis on which it is made should be clearly described.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02540 "fuel cycle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02540 "The sequence of steps, such as utilization, reprocessing, and refabrication, through which nuclear fuel may pass.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02540 "nuclear fuel cycle")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02541 "fuel element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02541 "The smallest structurally discrete part of a reactor which has fuel as its principal constituent.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02541 "nuclear fuel element")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02542 "fuel reprocessing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02542 "The processing of nuclear fuel, after its use in a reactor, to remove fission products and recover fissile and fertile material.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02542 "nuclear fuel reprocessing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02543 "fugacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02543 "Of a substance B, \\(f_{\\rm{B}}\\) or \\(\\tilde{p}_{\\rm{B}}\\), in a gaseous mixture is defined by \\(f_{\\rm{B}}=\\lambda _{\\rm{B}}\\ \\lim _{p\\rightarrow 0}\\frac{p_{\\rm{B}}}{\\lambda _{\\rm{B}}}\\), where \\(p_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the partial pressure of B and \\(λ_{\\rm{B}}\\) its absolute activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02544 "fugacity coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02544 "Ratio of fugacity to the partial pressure of a gaseous constituent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02545 "fulgides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02545 "Dialkylidenesuccinic anhydrides (generally photochromic).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02546 "full energy peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02546 "Of a spectrum of radiation, the part of the spectral response curve corresponding to the total absorption in a detecting material of the energy of the detected radiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:F02546 "total absorption peak")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02547 "fullerenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02547 "Compounds composed solely of an even number of carbon atoms, which form a cage-like fused-ring polycyclic system with twelve five-membered rings and the rest six-membered rings. The archetypal example isfullerene, where the atoms and bonds delineate a truncated icosahedron. The term has been broadened to include any closed cage structure consisting entirely of three-coordinate carbon atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02548))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02548 "fulminates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02548 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RON=C{:}}$. So called because fulminic acid (actually $\\ce{HC#N^{+}–O^{−}}$ formonitrile oxide) was previously considered to be $\\ce{HON=C{:}}$.Salts of fulminic acid, e.g. $\\ce{Na^{+}[–C#N^{+}O^{−}]}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02549 "fulvalenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02549 "The hydrocarbon fulvalene and its derivatives formed by substitution (and by extension, analogues formed by replacement of one or more carbon atoms of the fulvalene skeleton by a heteroatom).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02550 "fulvenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02550 "The hydrocarbon fulvene and its derivatives formed by substitution (and by extension, analogues formed by replacement of one or more carbon atoms of the fulvene skeleton by a heteroatom).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02551 "fume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02551 "Fine solid particles (aerosol), predominantly less than \\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter, which result from the condensation of vapour from some types of chemical reaction. Usually this is formed from the gaseous state generally after volatilization from melted substances and often accompanied by chemical reactions such as oxidation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02552))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02552 "fumes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02552 "In popular usage, a term often taken to mean airborne effluents, unpleasant and malodorous, which might arise from chemical processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02553 "fumigation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02553 "An atmospheric phenomenon in which pollution, retained by an inversion layer near its level of emission, is brought rapidly to ground level as the inversion breaks up. This term also applies to the exposure of material (e.g. grain) to chemicals to kill insects, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02554 "functional class name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02554 "A name that expresses the characteristic group as a class term written as a separate word following the name of a parent structure or a name derived from a parent structure. In the latter case, when the derived name is that for a substituent group (formerly called a 'radical'), the method has been called 'radicofunctional nomenclature'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02555 "functional group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02555 "Organic compounds are thought of as consisting of a relatively unreactive backbone, for example a chain of \\(\\rm{sp}^{3}\\) hybridized carbon atoms, and one or several functional groups. The functional group is an atom, or a group of atoms that has similar chemical properties whenever it occurs in different compounds. It defines the characteristic physical and chemical properties of families of organic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02556 "functional parent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02556 "A structure the name of which implies the presence of one or more characteristic groups and which has one or more hydrogen atoms attached to at least one of its skeletal atoms or one of its characteristic groups, or in which at least one of its characteristic groups can form at least one kind of functional modification.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02558 "furanocoumarins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02558 "An alternative name for furocoumarins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02559 "furanoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02559 "Cyclic hemiacetal forms of monosaccharides in which the ring is five-membered (i.e. a tetrahydrofuran skeleton).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02561 "furnace pyrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02561 "A flowing stream of gas (hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, chlorine, etc.) required to produce volatile species of the elements being determined, is passed over the test sample in a heated furnace. The analytes leave the furnace in the gas stream, or are entrained by a carrier gas. The analytes in the gas stream may be collected in an absorbing solution, on a carbon or other filter or by condensation on a cool surface. In the case of mercury this can also be done by amalgamation with a noble metal. The analytes may then be swept and released from the trap, by heating, into a sampling source for analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02562 "furocoumarins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02562 "Derivatives (mostly of natural origin) of the linear furocoumarin skeleton, psoralen, or its angular isomer, angelicin, variously substituted with hydroxy, methoxy, alkyl, or hydroxymethyl groups, among others.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02563 "fusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02563 "The amalgamation of two distinct cells or macromolecules into a single integrated unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:F02564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:F02564 "fusion name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:F02564 "A composite name for a polycyclic parent structure having the maximum number of non-cumulative double bonds and at least one ortho fusion. Name formation involves the dissection of the structure into contiguous components having recognized trivial or semisystematic names, one of which is selected as the 'base component'. attachment of the other components is described by prefixes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT06771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT06771 "ferredoxin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT06771 "A protein containing more than one iron and acid-labile sulfur, that displays electron-transfer activity but not classical enzyme function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT06786))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT06786 "Fenton reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT06786 "$\\ce{Fe^{2+} + H2O2 -> Fe^{3+} + OH^{.} + OH^{−}}$ . This is the iron-salt-dependent decomposition of dihydrogen peroxide, generating the highly reactive hydroxyl radical, possibly via an oxoiron(IV) intermediate. Addition of a reducing agent, such as ascorbate, leads to a cycle which increases the damage to biological molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT06824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT06824 "first-pass effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT06824 "Biotransformation and, in some cases, elimination of a substance in the liver after absorption from the intestine and before it reaches the systemic circulation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT06825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT06825 "fractionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT06825 "Process of classification of an analyte or a group of analytes from a sample according to physical (e.g., size, solubility) or chemical (e.g., bonding, reactivity) properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT06876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT06876 "fouling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT06876 "Process resulting in loss of performance of a membrane due to the deposition of suspended or dissolved substances on its external surfaces, at its pore openings, or within its pores.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07172))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07172 "ferroelectric polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07172 "Polymer in which spontaneous polarization arises when dipoles become arranged parallel to each other by electric fields.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07173 "ferromagnetic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07173 "Polymer that exhibits magnetic properties because it has unpaired electron spins aligned parallel to each other or electron spins that can easily be so aligned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07174 "functional polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07174 "Polymer that bears specified chemical groups.Polymer that has specified physical, chemical, biological, pharmacological, or other uses which depend on specific chemical groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07301 "fibrillar morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07301 "Morphology in which phase domains have shapes with one dimension much larger than the other two dimensions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07375))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07375 "fatigue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07375 "Loss of performance over time, due to chemical degradation. The major cause of fatigue is oxidation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07376 "fluence rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07376 "Total radiant power, \\(P\\), incident from all directions onto a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:FT07376 "radiant energy fluence rate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07377 "fluorescence lifetime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07377 "Parameter describing the time evolution of the decay of the fluorescent radiant intensity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07378 "fluorescence resonance energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07378 "Term frequently and inappropriately applied to resonance energy transfer in the sense of Förster-resonance-energy transfer (FRET), which does not involve the emission of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07379 "fluorogenic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07379 "Property of a material in which fluorescence is induced or enhanced by the addition of another molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07380))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07380 "fluorophore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07380 "Molecular entity (often organic) that emits fluorescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07381 "Förster-resonance-energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07381 "Non-radiative excitation transfer between two molecular entities separated by distances considerably exceeding the sum of their van der Waals radii. It describes the transfer in terms of the interaction between the transition (dipole) moments of the entities in the very weak dipole-dipole coupling limit. It is a Coulombic interaction frequently called a dipole-dipole coupling. The transfer rate constant from donor to acceptor, \\(k_{\\rm{T}}\\), is given by \\[k_{\\rm{T}} = k_{\\rm{D}}\\left ( \\frac{R_{0}}{r} \\right )^{6}=\\frac{1}{\\tau _{D}^{0}}\\left ( \\frac{R_{0}}{r}\\right )^{6}\\] where \\(k_{\\rm{D}}\\) and \\(\\tau_{\\rm{D}}^{0}\\) are the emission rate constant and the lifetime of the excited donor in the absence of transfer, respectively, \\(r\\) is the distance between the donor and the acceptor and \\(R_{0}\\) is the critical quenching radius or Förster radius, i.e., the distance at which transfer and spontaneous decay of the excited donor are equally probable (\\(k_{\\rm{T}} = k_{\\rm{D}}\\)) (see Note 3). \\(R_{0}\\) is given by \\[R_{0} = Const.\\left ( \\frac{\\kappa^{2}\\mathit{\\Phi}_{D}^{0}J }{n^{4}} \\right )^{1/6}\\] where \\(\\kappa\\) is the orientation factor, \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} _{D}^{0}\\) is the fluorescence quantum yield of the donor in the absence of transfer, \\(n\\) is the average refractive index of the medium in the wavelength range where spectral overlap is significant, \\(J\\) is the spectral overlap integral reflecting the degree of overlap of the donor emission spectrum with the acceptor absorption spectrum and given by \\[J = \\int _{\\lambda }I_{\\lambda}^{D}(\\lambda)\\epsilon _{A}\\left ( \\lambda \\right )\\lambda^{4}\\rm{d}\\lambda\\] where \\(I_{\\lambda}^{D}(\\lambda)\\) is the normalized spectral radiant intensity of the donor so that \\(\\int_{\\lambda}I_{\\lambda}^{D}(\\lambda)\\rm{d}\\lambda = 1\\). \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}({\\lambda})\\) is the molar decadic absorption coefficient of the acceptor. See Note 3 for the value of \\(Const.\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07382))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07382 "Fourier-transform spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07382 "Measurement technique whereby spectra are collected based on measurements of the temporal coherence of a radiative source, using time-domain measurements of the electromagnetic radiation or other type of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07383 "frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07383 """The number of waveperiods per time interval. The linear frequency, \\(
u\\), is the number of cycles per time interval. SI unit \\(\\rm{Hz =  s}^{-1}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07384 "frequency-domain fluorometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07384 "Technique that permits recovery of the parameters characterizing a fluorescence decay. Instead of using an exciting visible, UV, or infrared radiation pulse (see single-photon timing), the sample is excited by sinusoidally modulated radiation at high frequency. The fluorescence response is sinusoidally modulated at the same frequency as, but delayed in phase and partially demodulated with respect to the excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07385 "FRET")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07385 "Acronym for Förster-resonance-energy transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07505 "functionality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07505 "Number of covalent bonds that a monomer molecule or monomeric unit in a macromolecule or oligomer molecule can form with other reactants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07549 "fractal agglomerate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07549 "Agglomerate having the same fractal dimension as the constituent particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:FT07550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:FT07550 "fractal dimension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:FT07550 "Parameter that provides a mathematical description of the fractal structure of a polymer network, an aggregated particulate sol, or of the particles that comprise them.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:FT07550 "Hausdorff dimension")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:FT07550 "mass fractal dimension")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02566 "γ-cascade")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02566 "Two or more different gamma rays emitted successively from one nucleus when it passes through one or more energy levels.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02571 "<em>G</em> value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02571 "The number of specified chemical events in an irradiated substance produced per \\(100\\ \\rm{eV}\\) of energy absorbed from ionizing radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02572 "gain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02572 "The gain of a photomultiplier is given by the equation \\(G=k\\ \\sigma ^{n}\\), where \\(k\\) is the efficiency of collection of photoelectrons on the first dynode, \\(\\sigma \\) is the secondary emission ratio, i.e. the number of secondary electrons emitted for each electron incident on the dynode and \\(n\\) is the number of dynodes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02574 "Galvani potential difference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02574 "An electric potential difference between points in the bulk of two phases. It is measurable only when the two phases have identical composition (e.g. two copper wires). It is the difference of inner electric potentials in two phases. Symbol: \\(\\Delta _{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\phi \\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02576 "gas analysis installation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02576 "An assembly for the purpose of determining one or more components in a gaseous mixture. It generally comprises the following elements: sample probe, region of primary treatment of the gases, region of secondary treatment of gases, points for venting to the atmosphere and for calibration sample injection, transfer line and the analytical unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02577 "gas black")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02577 "Finely divided carbon (graphite) produced by incomplete combustion or thermal decomposition of natural gas.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02577 "carbon black")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02577 "channel black")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02577 "furnace black")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02578 "gas chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02578 "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a gas. Gas chromatography is always carried out in a column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02579 "gas constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02579 "Fundamental physical constant, \\(R = \\pu{8.314472(15) J K-1 mol-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02580 "gaseous diffusion separator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02580 "Instrumentation to separate a gas mixture into its components by means of differences in the diffusion rates of the component molecules. This method has been used in separation of various isotopes of uranium (by means of $\\ce{UF6}$) and hydrogen ($\\ce{H2O}$, $\\ce{HDO}$, $\\ce{D2O}$, $\\ce{HTO}$, etc.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02581 "gas-filled phototube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02581 "Similar in construction to a vacuum phototube except that it is filled with a noble gas (usually $\\ce{Ar}$) at a pressure of about \\(10\\ \\rm{Pa}\\). Photoelectrons accelerated by the anode electric potential ionize gas atoms. The additional electrons provide a substantial intrinsic gain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02582 "gas-filled X-ray detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02582 "A cylindrical cathode with a window, an axial wire anode and an ionizable gas. The gas may be continuously replenished giving a flow-through detector or the detector may be sealed. Following an original ionizing event, electron multiplication occurs through a process of gas amplification in the high electric field surrounding the anode wire. The gain of this process is defined as the number of electrons collected on the anode wire for each primary electron produced. For X-rays having energies higher than the excitation potential of the detector gas, the spectral responsivity function has a second peak in addition to the main peak that is called the escape peak. The escape peak has a mean pulse height proportional to the difference between the photon energy of the incident X-rays and of the spectral characteristic line of the detector gas. A quenching gas, a molecular gas, is added to the detector gas in order to neutralize the detector gas ions and to absorb secondary electrons as well as UV radiation resulting from neutralization of detector gas ions. According to the potential applied to the anode, the detector can work as an ionization chamber, proportional counter, or Geiger counter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02585 "gas laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02585 "A laser in which the active medium is a gas containing a laser active species. It is typically composed of a plasma discharge tube containing a gas that can be excited with an electric discharge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02586 "gas-liquid chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02586 "Comprises all gas-chromatographic methods in which the stationary phase is a liquid dispersed on a solid support. Separation is achieved by partition of the components of a sample between the phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02587 "gas-phase acidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02587 "The negative of the Gibbs energy (\\(\\Delta G_{\\rm{r}}^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)) change for the reaction:  in the gas phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02588 "gas-phase basicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02588 "The negative of the Gibbs energy (\\(\\Delta G_{\\rm{r}}^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)) change associated with the reaction: in the gas phase.  Also called absolute or intrinsic basicity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02589 "gas-phase-grown carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02589 "Carbon fibres grown in an atmosphere of hydrocarbons with the aid of fine particulate solid catalysts such as iron or other transition metals and consisting of graphitizable carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02590 "gas sensing electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02590 "A sensor composed of an indicator and a reference electrode in contact with a thin film of solution which is separated from the bulk of the sample solution by a gas-permeable membrane or an air gap. This intermediate solution interacts with the gaseous species (penetrated through the membrane or an air gap) in such a way as to produce a change in a measured constituent (e.g. the $\\ce{H^{+}}$ activity) of the intermediate solution. This change is then sensed by the ion-selective electrode and is related to the partial pressure of the gaseous species in the sample. [Note: In electrochemical literature the term gas electrode is used for the classical, redox-equilibrium-based gas electrodes as well, such as the hydrogen or the chlorine gas electrodes ($\\ce{Pt(s)}$|$\\ce{H2(g)}$|$\\ce{H^{+}(aq)}$ or $\\ce{Pt(s)}$|$\\ce{Cl2(g)}$|$\\ce{Cl^{-}(aq)}$]. These electrodes respond both to the partial pressure of the gas ($\\ce{H2}$ or $\\ce{Cl2}$) and to the ionic activities ($\\ce{H^{+}}$ or $\\ce{Cl^{-}}$). The Clark oxygen electrode fits under this classification although, in contrast to other gas sensors, it is an amperometric and not a potentiometric device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02591 "gas-solid chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02591 "Comprises all gas chromatographic methods in which the stationary phase is an active solid (e.g. charcoal, molecular sieves). Separation is achieved by adsorption of the components of a sample. In gas chromatography the distinction between gas-liquid and gas-solid may be obscure because liquids are used to modify solid stationary phases, and because the solid supports for liquid stationary phases affect the chromatographic process. For classification by the phases used, the term relating to the predominant effect should be chosen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02592 "gated photodetector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02592 "Where photodetectors are switched on (or off) usually in a repetitive manner employing electronic switches, they are described as gated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02593 "gauche")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02593 "Synonymous with a synclinal alignment of groups attached to adjacent atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02595 "gauche effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02595 "The stabilization of the gauche (synclinal) conformation in a two carbon unit bonded vicinally to electronegative elements e.g. 1,2-difluoroethane.The destabilization of the gauche (synclinal) conformation in a two carbon unit bonded vicinally to large, soft and polarizable elements such as sulfur and bromine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02596))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02596 "gauss")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02596 "emu unit of magnetic flux density, \\(\\rm{G}=10^{-4}\\ \\rm{T}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02597 "Gaussian band shape")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02597 """A band shape described by the Gaussian function: \\[F\\left(
u - 
u _{0}\\right) = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\ \\rm{e}^{-a^{2}\\ (
u - 
u _{0})^{2}}\\] In this equation, \\(a^{-1}\\) is proportional to the width of the band, and \\(
u _{0}\\) is the frequency of the band maximum.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02598 "Geiger counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02598 "A gas-filled X-ray detector in which gas amplification reaches saturation and proportionality no longer exists. The output signal does not depend on the incident energy. The time taken for the counter to recover from the saturation is called dead time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02599 "Geiger–Muller counter tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02599 "A counter tube operated under such conditions that the magnitude of each pulse is independent of the amount of energy deposited in it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02600 "gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02600 "Non-fluid colloidal network or polymer network that is expanded throughout its whole volume by a fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02602 "geminate pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02602 "Pair of molecular entities in close proximity in solution within a solvent cage and resulting from reaction (e.g. bond scission, electron transfer, group transfer) of a precursor that constitutes a single kinetic entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02603 "geminate recombination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02603 "This expression refers to the reaction, with each other, of two transient species produced from a common precursor in solution. If reaction occurs before any separation by diffusion has occurred, this is termed primary geminate recombination. If the mutually reactive entities have been separated, and come together by diffusion, this is termed secondary geminate recombination. This is illustrated in the reaction diagram below:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02604 "gene")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02604 "Structurally, a basic unit of hereditary material; an ordered sequence of nucleotide bases that encodes one polypeptide chain (via mRNA). The gene includes, however, regions preceding and following the coding region (leader and trailer) as well as (in eukaryotes) intervening sequences (introns) between individual coding segments (exons). Functionally, the gene is defined by the cis-trans test that determines whether independent mutations of the same phenotype occur within a single gene or in several genes involved in the same function.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02604 "cistron")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02605 "gene amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02605 "An increase in the number of copies of a specific gene in an organism. This can lead to the production of a corresponding protein at elevated levels.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02606 "gene library")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02606 "A collection of cloned DNA fragments from a variety of species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02607 "gene manipulation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02607 "The use of in vitro techniques to produce DNA molecules containing novel combinations of genes or altered sequences, and the insertion of these into vectors that can be used for their incorporation into host organisms or cells in which they are capable of continued propagation of the modified genes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02608 "general acid–base catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02608 "Catalysis by acids or bases in solution is said to be general when it is possible to detect catalysis brought about by species other than the ions formed from the solvent itself (e.g. when water is the solvent, by species other than $\\ce{H^{+}}$ and $\\ce{OH^{−}}$ ions).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02609 "general acid catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02609 "The catalysis of a chemical reaction by a series of Brønsted acids (which may include the solvated hydrogen ion) so that the rate of the catalysed part of the reaction is given by \\(\\sum k_{\\rm{HA}}\\ [HA]\\) multiplied by some function of substrate concentrations. (The acids $\\ce{HA}$ are unchanged by the overall reaction.) General catalysis by acids can be experimentally distinguished from specific catalysis by hydrogen cations (hydrons) by observation of the rate of reaction as a function of buffer concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02610))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02610 "general base catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02610 "The catalysis of a chemical reaction by a series of Brønsted bases (which may include the lyate ion) so that the rate of the catalysed part of the reaction is given by \\(\\sum k_{\\rm{B}}\\ [\\rm{B}]\\) multiplied by some function of substrate concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02611 "general force field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02611 "A force field expressed in terms of \\(3\\ N-6\\) basis coordinates: \\[V=\\frac{1}{2}\\, \\sum f_{ij}\\ S_{i}\\ S_{j}\\] \\[V=\\frac{1}{2}\\, \\sum F_{ij}\\ S_{i}\\ S_{j}\\] where \\(f_{ij}\\) (or \\(F_{ij}\\)) are force constants and the basis coordinates \\(S\\) (or sometimes \\(s\\)) may be internal symmetry coordinates, local symmetry coordinates or any others suitable to the problem, but the number of the coordinates has to be reduced to \\(3\\, N-6\\) (\\(3\\, N-5\\) for linear molecules), \\(N\\) being the number of atoms in the molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02612 "generalized transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02612 "Any form of transition-state theory (TST), such as microcanonical variational TST, canonical variational TST, and improved canonical variational TST, in which the transition state is not necessarily located at the saddle point, is referred to as generalized transition-state theory.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02613))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02613 "generally labelled tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02613 "A tracer in which the position of the label is not defined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02614 "generation time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02614 "The average time required by a cell for the completion of one cell cycle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02615 "genetic code")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02615 "The set of rules which governs the relationship between the linear order of the nucleotides in an mRNA molecule and the sequence of the amino acids in the protein which it encodes. The genetic code is a triplet code, nearly universal. It is not overlapping: a mutation which alters only a single nucleotide in a gene can change only one amino acid in the encoded protein. It is degenerate: it assigns each of the 20 amino acids to one or more of 61 of the 64 possible nucleotide triplets (cf. codon) that can be constructed from four nucleotides. The remaining three triplets are used to signal the termination of a genetic message.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02616 "genome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02616 "The complete set of chromosomal and extrachromosomal genes of an organism, a cell, an organelle or a virus; the complete DNA component of an organism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02617))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02617 "genotype")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02617 "The genetic constitution of an organism as revealed by genetic or molecular analysis, i.e. the complete set of genes, both dominant and recessive, possessed by a particular cell or organism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02618 "geometrical equivalence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02618 "The symmetry correspondence among units belonging to the same chain. The symmetry elements always bear a special relationship to the chain axis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02619 "geometric attenuation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02619 "The reduction of a radiation quantity due to the effect only of the distance between the point of interest and the source, excluding the effect of any matter present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02621 "geometric mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02621 "The nth root of the product of the absolute values of the observations, taken with the proper sign. It can be calculated with the formula: \\[\\bar{x}_{\\rm{g}} = (\\Pi |x_{i}|)^{\\frac{1}{n}}\\] The \\(\\mathit{\\Pi}\\) product is taken from i = 1 to n.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02621 "logarithmic mean")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02623 "geometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02623 "A term used colloquially to signify the arrangement in space of the various components in an experiment, particularly the source and the detector in radiation measurements.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02623 "counting geometry")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02624 "geometry factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02624 "The average solid angle in steradians at source subtended by the aperture or sensitive volume of the detector, divided by $4\\uppi\\!$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02626 "germylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02626 "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{R2Ge{:}}$. The older synonym germylenes is no longer recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02626 "germylenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02627 "Gibbs adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02627 "The surface excess amount or Gibbs adsorption of component \\(i\\), \\(n_{i}^{\\sigma }\\), which may be positive or negative, is defined as the excess of the amount of this component actually present in the system over that present in a reference system of the same volume as the real system and in which the bulk concentrations in the two phases remain uniform up to the Gibbs dividing surface. That is \\[n_{i}^{\\sigma }=n_{i}- V^{\\alpha }\\ c_{i}^{\\alpha }- V^{\\beta }\\ c_{i}^{\\beta }\\] where \\(n_{i}\\) is the total amount of the component \\(i\\) in the system, \\(c_{i}^{\\alpha }\\) and \\(c_{i}^{\\beta }\\) are the concentrations in the two bulk phases \\(\\alpha \\) and \\(\\beta \\), and \\(V^{\\alpha }\\) and \\(V^{\\beta }\\) are the volumes of the two phases defined by the Gibbs surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02629 "Gibbs energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02629 "Enthalpy minus the product of thermodynamic temperature and entropy. It was formerly called free energy or free enthalpy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02629 "Gibbs energy function")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02629 "free energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02630))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02630 "Gibbs energy diagram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02630 "A diagram showing the relative standard Gibbs energies of reactants, transition state, reaction intermediates and products, in the same sequence as they occur in a chemical reaction. These points are often connected by a smooth curve (a 'Gibbs energy profile', commonly still referred to as a 'free energy profile') but experimental observation can provide information on relative standard Gibbs energies only at the maxima and minima and not at the configurations between them. The abscissa expresses the sequence of reactants, products, reaction intermediates and transition states and is usually undefined or only vaguely defined by the reaction coordinate (extent of bond breaking or bond making). In some adaptations the abscissas are however explicitly defined as bond orders, Brønsted exponents, etc. Contrary to statements in many text books, the highest point on a Gibbs energy diagram does not necessarily correspond to the transition state of the rate-limiting step. For example, in a stepwise reaction consisting of two reaction steps:  one of the transition states of the two reaction steps must (in general) have a higher standard Gibbs energy than the other, whatever the concentration of D in the system. However, the value of that concentration will determine which of the reaction steps is rate-limiting. If the particular concentrations of interest, which may vary, are chosen as the standard state, then the rate-limiting step is the one of highest Gibbs energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02630 "energy profile")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02630 "Gibbs energy profile")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02630 "free energy profile")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02631 "Gibbs energy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02631 "The standard Gibbs energy difference between the transition state of a reaction (either an elementary reaction or a stepwise reaction) and the ground state of the reactants. It is calculated from the experimental rate constant \\(k\\) via the conventional form of the absolute rate equation: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G = R\\ T\\ \\left[\\ln (\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}}{h}) - \\ln (\\frac{k}{T})\\right]\\] where \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(h\\) the Planck constant (\\(\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}}{h} = 2.083\\ 58\\times 10^{10}\\ \\rm{K}^{-1}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\)). The values of the rate constants, and hence Gibbs energies of activation, depend upon the choice of concentration units (or of the thermodynamic standard state).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02631 "standard free energy of activation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02632 "Gibbs energy of repulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02632 "Indicated by \\(G_{\\rm{r}}\\) or \\(G_{\\rm{el}}\\) if the repulsion is due to electric effects (\\(g_{\\rm{r}}\\) or \\(g_{\\rm{el}}\\) is taken for unit area of each of two flat and parallel surfaces). \\(G_{\\rm{r}}\\) (or \\(G_{\\rm{el}}\\)) is defined as \\[G_{\\rm{r}}\\,\\rm{(}\\rm{or}\\,G_{\\rm{el}}\\rm{)} = \\left[\\int _{\\rm{final distance}}^{\\infty }\\rm{Force}  \\;\\rm{d (distance)}\\right]_{T,p}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02634 "Gibbs film elasticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02634 "Pertains to a film element of a soap film changing in area at constant mass and is the differential change of its surface tension with relative change in area \\(A\\), \\[E=A\\ (\\frac{\\partial \\sigma }{\\partial A})_{T,p,n_{i}}\\] where \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, \\(p\\) is the pressure, and \\(n_{i}\\) is the amount of substance of the species \\(i\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02635))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02635 "Gibbs surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02635 "A geometrical surface chosen parallel to the interface and used to define the volumes of the bulk phases in the calculation of the extent of adsorption, and of other surface excess properties. Also referred to as Gibbs dividing surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02636))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02636 "giga")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02636 "SI prefix for \\(10^{9}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{G}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02637 "glass electrode error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02637 "Deviation of a glass electrode from the hydrogen-ion response function. An example often encountered is the error due to sodium ions at alkaline \\(\\rm{pH}\\) values, which by convention is regarded as positive.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02638 "glass laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02638 "A solid state laser in which the active medium is a glass host that supports the active species. The neodymium-doped phosphate glass laser is one example.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02639))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02639 "glass-like carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02639 "An agranular non-graphitizable carbon with a very high isotropy of its structural and physical properties and with a very low permeability for liquids and gases. The original surfaces and the fracture surfaces have a pseudo-glassy appearance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02640 "glass transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02640 "A second-order transition in which a supercooled melt yields, on cooling, a glassy structure. Below the glass-transition temperature the physical properties vary in a manner similar to those of the crystalline phase. Example: Lithium disilicate crystals melt at \\(1305\\ \\rm{K}\\); the melt can be supercooled to the glass-transition temperature at approximately \\(773\\ \\rm{K}\\) below which the viscous liquid freezes to a rigid amorphous glass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02641))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02641 "glass-transition temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02641 "The temperature at which the viscosity of the glass is \\(10^{13}\\ \\rm{dPa}\\ \\rm{s}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02642))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02642 "globular-chain crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02642 "A type of crystal comprised of macromolecules having globular conformations. Globular-chain crystals usually occur with globular proteins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02643))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02643 "glove box")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02643 "An enclosure in which material may be manipulated in isolation from the operator's environment. This manipulation is effected by gauntlet gloves or flexible plastic devices fixed to ports in the walls of the box.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02645 "glycans")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02645 "Synonymous with polysaccharides. Glycans composed of a single type of monosaccharide residue (homopolysaccharide, synonym homoglycan) are named by replacing the ending '-ose' of the sugar by '-an', e.g. mannans, fructans, xylans, arabinans. dextrans and dextrins belong to the class glucans.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02647))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02647 "glycerides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02647 "Esters of glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) with fatty acids, widely distributed in nature. They are by long-established custom subdivided into triglycerides, 1,2- or 1,3-diglycerides, and 1- or 2-monoglycerides, according to the number and position of acyl groups (not, as one might suppose, the number of glycerol residues). The recommended method for naming individual glycerides is mono-, di- or tri-O-acylglycerol, as appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02648 "glycerophospholipid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02648 "Any derivative of glycerophosphoric acid that contains at least one O-acyl, or O-alkyl, or O-(1-alkenyl) group attached to the glycerol residue.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02650 "glyco-amino-acid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02650 "A saccharide attached to a single amino acid by any kind of covalent bond. A glycosyl-amino-acid is a compound consisting of saccharide linked through a glycosyl linkage (O-, N-, or S-) to an amino acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02651 "glycoconjugate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02651 "A type of compound consisting of carbohydrate units covalently linked with other types of chemical constituent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02653 "glycolipids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02653 "Naturally occurring 1,2-di-O-acylglycerols joined at oxygen 3 by a glycosidic linkage to a carbohydrate part (usually a mono-, di- or tri-saccharide). Some substances classified as bacterial glycolipids have the sugar part acylated by one or more fatty acids and the glycerol part may be absent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02654 "glycols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02654 "Dihydric alcohols, also known as diols, in which the two hydroxy groups are on different carbon atoms, usually but not necessarily adjacent. E.g. $\\ce{HOCH2CH2OH}$ 'ethylene glycol' (ethane-1,2-diol), $\\ce{HO[CH2]4OH butane-1,4-diol}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02656 "glycopeptides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02656 "Compounds in which a carbohydrate component is linked to a peptide/protein component.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02656 "glycoproteins")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02658 "glycosamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02658 "A term synonymous with amino sugars; now restricted to some trivial names.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02659 "glycosaminoglycan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02659 "The carbohydrate units in proteoglycans are polysaccharides that contain amino sugars; these polysaccharides are known as glycosaminoglycans.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02660 "glycoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02660 "A less frequently used term for monosaccharides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02661))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02661 "glycosides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02661 "Originally mixed acetals resulting from the attachment of a glycosyl group to a non-acyl group $\\ce{RO– (which itself may be derived from a saccharide and chalcogen replacements thereof ($\\ce{RS\\bond{–}}$, $\\ce{RSe\\bond{–}}$).  The bond between the glycosyl group and the $\\ce{OR}$ group is called a glycosidic bond. By extension, the terms N-glycosides and C-glycosides are used as class names for glycosylamines and for compounds having a glycosyl group attached to a hydrocarbyl group respectively.   These terms are misnomers and should not be used. The preferred terms are glycosylamines and C-glycosyl compounds, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02663 "glycosylamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02663 "Compounds having a glycosyl group attached to an amino group, $\\ce{NR2}$; less elegantly called N-glycosides, e.g. N,N-dimethyl-$\\upbeta\\!$-ᴅ-glucopyranosylamine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02665 "glycosyl group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02665 "The structure obtained by removing the hydroxy group from the hemiacetal function of a monosaccharide and, by extension, of a lower oligosaccharide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02668 "grab sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02668 "The taking of a sample (often in an evacuated bulb) in a very short time; preferred terms are instantaneous sampling or instantaneous sampling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02669))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02669 "gradient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02669 "A differential ratio; the change in a quantity such as the mixing ratio of an impurity in air, the temperature of the air, etc., with height or distance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02670 "gradient elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02670 "A procedure in which the composition of the mobile phase is changed continuously or stepwise during the elution process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02671 "gradient layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02671 "The chromatographic bed used in thin-layer chromatography in which there is a gradual transition in some property.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02672))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02672 "gradientless reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02672 "Stirred flow or recirculation reactors, characterized ideally by very small concentration and temperature gradients within the catalyst region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02673 "gradient packing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02673 "Synonymous with gradient layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02674 "gradual potential-energy surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02674 "These terms relate to the steepness of the energy rise along the reaction path of a potential-energy surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02675))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02675 "graft copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02675 "A copolymer that is a graft polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02676 "graft copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02676 "Polymerization in which a graft copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02678))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02678 "graft macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02678 "A macromolecule with one or more species of block connected to the main chain as side chains, these side chains having constitutional or configurational features that differ from those in the main chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02679 "graft polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02679 "A polymer composed of graft macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02680))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02680 "gram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02680 "cgs base unit of mass, \\(\\pu{g} = \\pu{E-3 kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02681 "Gram stain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02681 "A set of two stains that are used to stain (crystal violet and iodine) and counterstain (fuchsin) bacteria. gram-negative bacteria are completely decolorized after treating the stained bacteria with ethanol or acetone and can be visualized by the counterstain. They have a complex cell wall in which the peptidoglycan layer is covered by lipopolysaccharides in an outer membrane structure. gram-positive bacteria resist decolorization by ethanol or acetone, they retain the initial gram stain. They have a cell wall consisting predominantly of peptidoglycan not overlayed by an outer membrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02682 "granular carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02682 "The term granular carbon is equivalent to coarse particulate carbon. This is a carbon material consisting of separate particles or grains which are monolithic, on the average larger than about \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter, but smaller than about \\(1\\ \\rm{cm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02683 "graphene layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02683 "A single carbon layer of the graphite structure, describing its nature by analogy to a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon of quasi infinite size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02684))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02684 "graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02684 "An allotropic form of the element carbon consisting of layers of hexagonally arranged carbon atoms in a planar condensed ring system graphene layers The layers are stacked parallel to each other in a three-dimensional crystalline long-range order. There are two allotropic forms with different stacking arrangements, hexagonal and rhombohedral. The chemical bonds within the layers are covalent with \\(\\rm{sp}^{2}\\) hybridization and with a C–C distance of \\(141.7\\ \\rm{pm}\\). The weak bonds between the layers are metallic with a strength comparable to van der Waals bonding only.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02686))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02686 "graphite fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02686 "Carbon fibres consisting mostly of synthetic graphite for which three-dimensional crystalline order is confirmed by X-ray diffraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02687))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02687 "graphite material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02687 "A material consisting essentially of graphitic carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02688 "graphite whiskers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02688 "Thin, approximately cylindrical filaments in which graphene layers are arranged in a scroll-like manner. There is, at least in part, a regular stacking of the layers as in the graphite lattice, giving rise to h,k,l, X-ray reflections. The physical properties of graphite whiskers approach, along the cylinder axis, those of graphite.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:G02688 "carbon whiskers")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02689))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02689 "graphitic carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02689 "All varieties of substances consisting of the element carbon in the allotropic form of graphite irrespective of the presence of structural defects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02690))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02690 "graphitizable carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02690 "A non-graphitic carbon which upon graphitization heat treatment converts into graphitic carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02691 "graphitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02691 "A solid-state transformation of thermodynamically unstable non-graphitic carbon into graphite by means of heat treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02692 "graphitization heat treatment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02692 "A process of heat treatment of a non-graphitic carbon industrially performed at temperatures in the range between \\(2500\\) and \\(3300\\ \\rm{K}\\), to achieve transformation into graphitic carbon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02693 "graphitized carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02693 "A graphitic carbon with more or less perfect three-dimensional hexagonal crystalline order prepared from non-graphitic carbon by graphitization heat treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02694))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02694 "gravimetric method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02694 "A determination by weight; e.g. in the older method of $\\ce{Cl^{−}}$ ion determination, a weighed amount of sample is dissolved in water, $\\ce{AgNO3}$ solution added, $\\ce{AgCl}$ is precipitated, dried and weighed. From the known mass fraction of silver in $\\ce{AgCl}$, the weights of the initial sample and that of the $\\ce{AgCl}$ precipitated, the percentage of chlorine in the sample can be calculated readily.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02695 "gravitational constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02695 "Universal fundamental physical constant in Newton's law of gravitation, \\(G = \\pu{6.67259(85)E-11 m3 kg-1 s-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02696 "gray")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02696 "SI derived unit of energy imparted to an element of matter by ionizing radiation divided by the mass of that element (absorbed dose of radiation) equal to one joule per kilogram and admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health, \\(\\pu{Gy} = \\pu{J kg-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02697))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02697 "green coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02697 "Green coke (raw coke) is the primary solid carbonization product from high boiling hydrocarbon fractions obtained at temperatures below \\(900\\ \\rm{K}\\). It contains a fraction of matter that can be released as volatiles during subsequent heat treatment at temperatures up to approximately \\(1600\\ \\rm{K}\\). This mass fraction, the so-called volatile matter, is in the case of green coke between 4 and 15 wt.%, but it depends also on the heating rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02698))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02698 "greenhouse effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02698 "Heating effect produced by certain gases (e.g. $\\ce{CO2}$, $\\ce{O3}$, etc.), which by virtue of their characteristic infrared absorption, lower the earth to space transmission of long wavelength radiation but allow transmission of shorter wavelength radiation inward from the sun to the earth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02699 "Grignard reagents")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02699 "Organomagnesium halides, $\\ce{RMgX, having a carbon–magnesium bond (or their equilibrium mixtures in solution with $\\ce{R2Mg}$ + $\\ce{MgX}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02701))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02701 "grit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02701 "Airborne solid particles in the atmosphere which are of natural or manmade origin and which remain in suspension for some time; in the United Kingdom the size of the grit particles is defined as greater than \\(75\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter (retained on a 200 mesh British Standard sieve).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02702 "ground level concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02702 "The concentration of a chemical species, normally a pollutant, in air; usually measured at a specific height above the ground.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02703 "ground level inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02703 "The inversion of the normal temperature gradient in the atmosphere; the temperature of the air increases with increasing height of the air above the ground. This leads to poor mixing of gases released below the inversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02704))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02704 "ground state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02704 "The state of lowest Gibbs energy of a system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02705 "group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02705 "A defined linked collection of atoms or a single atom within a molecular entity. This use of the term in physical organic and general chemistry is less restrictive than the definition adopted for the purpose of nomenclature of organic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02706))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02706 "group electronegativity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02706 "Synonymous with substituent electronegativity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02707))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02707 "group preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02707 "An operation (process) as a result of which several microcomponents are isolated in one step. This may be achieved in one of two ways, by transportation either of the matrix or of the microcomponents into a second phase. The first method is widely used in the analysis of relatively simple substances and materials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02708))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02708 "growth curve")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02708 "Curve giving the activity of a radioactive nuclide as a function of time and showing the increase of the activity through the decay of the precursor or as a result of activation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02709 "growth rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02709 "The measure (\\(\\rm{h}^{-1}\\),\\(\\rm{d}^{-1}\\)) of the rate of growth or multiplication of an organism or a culture, usually expressed as specific growth rate (the increase of mass or cell number per time unit referred to the unit of mass, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}(\\ln X)}{\\rm{d}t}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02710 "Grunwald–Winstein equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02710 "The linear free energy relation: \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{k_{\\rm{s}}}{k_{0}}) = m\\ Y\\] expressing the dependence of the rate of solvolysis of a substrate on ionizing power of the solvent. The rate constant \\(k_{0}\\) applies to the reference solvent (ethanol–water, 80:20, v/v) and \\(k_{\\rm{s}}\\) to the solvent s, both at \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\). The parameter \\(m\\) is characteristic of the substrate and is assigned the value unity for tert-butyl chloride. The value \\(Y\\) is intended to be a quantitative measure of the ionizing power of the solvents. The equation was later extended to the form: \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{k_{\\rm{s}}}{k_{0}}) = m\\ Y+l\\ N\\] where \\(N\\) is the nucleophilicity of the solvent and \\(l\\) its susceptibility parameter. The equation has also been applied to reactions other than solvolysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02711 "guest")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02711 "An organic or inorganic ion or molecule that occupies a cavity, cleft or pocket within the molecular structure of a host molecular entity and forms a complex with it or that is trapped in a cavity within the crystal structure of a host.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02712 "Guinier plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02712 "A diagrammatic representation of data on scattering from large particles, obtained at different angles but at the same concentration, constructed by plotting \\(\\log _{10}\\Delta R\\left(\\theta \\right)\\) or \\(\\log _{10}P\\left(\\theta \\right)\\) versus \\((\\sin \\frac{\\theta}{2})^{2}\\) or \\(q^{2}\\) and (usually) used for the evaluation of the radius of gyration. \\(\\Delta R(\\theta)\\) is the excess Rayleigh ratio, \\(P\\left(\\theta \\right)\\) the particle scattering function, \\(\\theta \\) the scattering angle and \\(q\\) the length of the scattering vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02713 "gustiness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02713 "Intensity of turbulence; the ratio of the root mean square of wind velocity fluctuations to the mean wind velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT06826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT06826 "genomics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT06826 "Science of using DNA- and RNA-based technologies to demonstrate alterations in gene expression.Method providing information on the consequences for gene expression of interactions of the organism with environmental stress, xenobiotics, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT06961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT06961 "genetic algorithm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT06961 "A genetic algorithm is an optimization algorithm based on the mechanisms of Darwinian evolution which uses random mutation, crossover and selection procedures to breed better models or solutions from an originally random starting population or sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07138))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07138 "grafting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07138 "Reaction in which one or more species of block are connected to the main chain of a macromolecule as side-chains having constitutional or configurational features that differ from those in the main chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07386 "gated photochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07386 "Special form of photochromism in which one or both forms of a photochromic system are transformed reversibly, either chemically or electrochemically, into a non-photochromic form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07387 "geminate ion pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07387 "Ion pair formed from a precursor that constitutes a single kinetic entity, i.e., by electron transfer or ion transfer in an encounter complex (cf. collision complex) or by ionic dissociation of a single molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07388 "Gibbs energy of photoinduced electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07388 "For photoinduced electron transfer between an acceptor (\\(\\rm{A}\\)) and a donor (\\(\\rm{D}\\)) (either one of them may be the electronically excited molecular entity) of any charge type, \\(z(\\rm{A})\\) and \\(z(\\rm{D})\\), the change in standard Gibbs energy can be approximated as (the notation is for the case of neutral species \\(\\rm{D}\\) and \\(\\rm{A}\\)) \\[\\Delta _{\\rm{ET}}G^{\\rm{o}} = N_{\\rm{A}}\\left \\{e\\, [E^{\\rm{o}}(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet }/\\rm{D})- E^{\\rm{o}}(\\rm{A}/\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet }) ]+w(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet }\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet })-w(\\rm{DA})\\right \\} - \\Delta E_{0,0}\\] where \\(e = 1.602\\ 176\\ 487 \\times 10^{-19}\\, \\rm{C}\\) is the elementary charge, \\(N_{\\rm{A}} = \\pu{6.02214076E23 mol-1}\\) is the Avogadro constant, \\(E^{\\rm{o}}(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet}/\\rm{D})/\\rm{V}\\) is the standard electrode potential of the donor cation radical resulting from the electron transfer, \\(E^{\\rm{o}}(\\rm{A}/\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet})/\\rm{V}\\) is the standard electrode potential of the acceptor (both relative to the same reference electrode) and \\(\\Delta E_{0,0}/\\pu{J mol-1}\\) is the vibrational zero electronic energy of the excited partner (provided that a vibrationally equilibrated excited state at energy \\(E_{0,0}\\) takes part in the reaction), all data referring to the same solvent. \\(w(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet }\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet })\\) and \\(w(\\rm{DA})\\) are the electrostatic work terms that account for the effect of Coulombic attraction in the products and reactants, respectively \\[w(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet }\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet })/J=\\frac{z(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet })z(\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet })e^{2}}{4\\pi \\epsilon _{0}\\epsilon _{r}a}\\] \\[w(\\rm{DA})/J =\\frac{z(\\rm{D})z(\\rm{A})e^{2}}{4\\pi \\epsilon _{0}\\epsilon _{r}a}\\] where \\(a\\) is the distance of the charged species after electron transfer, \\(\\varepsilon_{r}\\) is the relative medium static permittivity (formerly called dielectric constant), \\(\\varepsilon_{0} \\approx 8.854 \\times 10^{-12}\\ \\rm{C}^{2}\\ \\rm{J}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-1}\\) is the electric constant (vacuum permittivity), and \\(z(\\rm{X})\\) the charge of the species \\(X\\).  In SI units the factor \\(\\frac{e^{2}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon _{0}} = 2.307 \\times 10^{-28}\\ \\rm{J m}\\). For the case of neutral species \\(\\rm{A}\\) and \\(\\rm{D}\\), \\(z(\\rm{D})=z(\\rm{A})=0\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07389 "global analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07389 "Method for kinetic analysis of time-resolved emission or absorption data.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07535 "gel point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07535 "Point of incipient network formation in a process forming a chemical or physical polymer network.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:GT07535 "gelation point")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07536 "gel time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07536 "Time interval from the start of a network-forming process to the gel point.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:GT07536 "gelation time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07537 "gelation temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07537 "Temperature threshold for the formation of a thermoreversible gel.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:GT07537 "gel temperature")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07551 "gel fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07551 "Mass fraction of the network material resulting from a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07552))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07552 "green body")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07552 "Object formed from a preceramic material prior to pyrolysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07553 "hybrid material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07553 "Material composed of an intimate mixture of inorganic components, organic components, or both types of component.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07620 "gel aging")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07620 "Time-dependent changes in the chemical or the physical structure and the properties of a gel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:GT07621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:GT07621 "gelation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:GT07621 "Process of passing through the gel point to form a gel or network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H01881))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H01881 "η")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H01881 "An affix giving a topological indication of the bonding between a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ ligand and the central atom in a coordination entity. A right superscript numerical index indicates the number of ligating atoms in the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system of the ligand which bind to the central atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H01881 "hapto")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H01881 "eta")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02714))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02714 "haem")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02714 "An alternative spelling for heme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02715 "half-chair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02715 "The conformation of a six-membered ring structure in which four contiguous atoms are in a plane and the other two atoms lie on opposite sides of the plane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02716 "half life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02716 "For a given reaction the half life \\(t_{1/2}\\) of a reactant is the time required for its concentration to reach a value that is the arithmetic mean of its initial and final (equilibrium) values. For a reactant that is entirely consumed it is the time taken for the reactant concentration to fall to one half its initial value: The half life of a reaction has meaning only in special cases:  For a first-order reaction, the half life of the reactant may be called the half life of the reaction.  For a reaction involving more than one reactant, with the concentrations of the reactants in their stoichiometric ratios, the half life of each reactant is the same, and may be called the half life of the reaction.   If the concentrations of reactants are not in their stoichiometric ratios, there are different half lives for different reactants, and one cannot speak of the half life of the reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02717))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02717 "half life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02717 "For a single radioactive decay process, the time required for the activity to decrease to half its value by that process.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02717 "radioactive half life")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02720))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02720 "half-peak potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02720 "In linear-sweep voltammetry, triangular-wave voltammetry, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry, and similar techniques, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the difference between the total current and the residual current is equal to one-half of the peak current. This potential is attained in the interval in which the rate of the charge-transfer process, and hence the (absolute value of the) current, increase monotonically with time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02721))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02721 "half thickness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02721 "The thickness of a specified substance which, when introduced into the path of a given beam of radiation, reduces the value of a specified radiation quantity by one half.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02722 "half-wave potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02722 "The potential of a polarographic or voltammetric indicator electrode at the point, on the rising part of a polarographic or voltammetric wave, where the difference between the total current and the residual current is equal to one-half of the limiting current. The quarter-wave potential, the three-quarter-wave potential, etc., may be similarly defined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02723 "half-width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02723 "The full width of a spectral band at a height equal to half of the height at the band maximum. Also known as full width at half maximum (FWHM). The dimension of band width should be either inverse length (wavenumbers) or inverse time (frequencies) so that the values give an indication of the energies. Note the hyphen in half-width. Half bandwidth has the meaning of half-width at half maximum.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02723 "full width at half maximum")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02724))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02724 "halirenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02724 "Cyclic cations having the structure:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02725))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02725 "halochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02725 "The colour change which occurs on addition of acid (or base, or a salt) to a solution of a compound. A chemical reaction (e.g. ion formation) transforms a colourless compound into a coloured one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02726 "haloforms")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02726 "Trihalomethanes $\\ce{CHX3}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02727))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02727 "halohydrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02727 "A traditional term for alcohols substituted by a halogen atom at a saturated carbon atom otherwise bearing only hydrogen or hydrocarbyl groups (usually used to mean $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}halo alcohols}$). E.g. $\\ce{BrCH2CH2OH}$ 'ethylene bromohydrin' (2-bromoethanol), $\\ce{ClCH2CH2CH2OH}$ 'trimethylene chlorohydrin' (3-chloro-propan-1-ol), $\\ce{PhCH(OH)CH2Cl}$ 'styrene chlorohydrin' (2-chloro-1-phenylethanol).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02728 "halonium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02728 "Ions of the form $\\ce{R2X^{+}}$, where $\\ce{X}$ is any halogen ($\\ce{X} = \\ce{Br^{+}}$, bromonium ions; $\\ce{X} = \\ce{Cl^{+}}$, chloronium ions; $\\ce{X} = \\ce{F^{+}}$, fluoronium ions; $\\ce{X} = \\ce{I^{+}}$, iodonium ions). They may be open-chain or cyclic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02729 "halophiles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02729 "Organisms which require a minimum concentration of sodium chloride in their environment (cf. extremophiles).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02730 "hamiltonian operator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02730 "Quantum mechanical operator of total energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02732 "Hammett equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02732 "The equation in the form: \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{k}{k_{0}}) = \\rho \\ \\sigma \\] or \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{K}{K_{0}}) = \\rho \\ \\sigma \\] applied to the influence of meta- or para-substituents $\\ce{X}$ on the reactivity of the functional group $\\ce{Y}$ in the benzene derivative m- or $\\ce{p-XC6H4Y}$. \\(k\\) or \\(K\\) is the rate or equilibrium constant, respectively, for the given reaction of m- or $\\ce{p-XC6H4Y}$; \\(k_{0}\\) or \\(K_{0}\\) refers to the reaction of $\\ce{C6H5Y}$, i.e. $\\ce{X} = \\ce{H}$; is the substituent constant characteristic of m- or $\\ce{p-X}$: is the reaction constant characteristic of the given reaction of $\\ce{Y}$. The equation is often encountered in a form with \\(\\log _{10}k_{0}\\) or \\(\\log _{10}K_{0}\\) written as a separate term on the right hand side, e.g. \\[\\log _{10}k = \\rho \\ \\sigma +\\log _{10}k_{0}\\] or \\[\\log _{10}K = \\rho \\ \\sigma +\\log _{10}K_{0}\\] It then signifies the intercept corresponding to $\\ce{X} = \\ce{H}$ in a regression of \\(\\log _{10}k\\) or \\(\\log _{10}K\\) on \\(\\sigma \\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02732 "Hammett relation")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02732 "ρσ-equation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02734))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02734 "Hammond principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02734 "The hypothesis that, when a transition state leading to an unstable reaction intermediate (or product) has nearly the same energy as that intermediate, the two are interconverted with only a small reorganization of molecular structure. Essentially the same idea is sometimes referred to as 'Leffler's assumption', namely, that the transition state bears the greater resemblance to the less stable species (reactant or reaction intermediate/product). Many text books and physical organic chemists, however, express the idea in Leffler's form, but attribute it to Hammond. As a corollary, it follows that a factor stabilizing a reaction intermediate will also stabilize the transition state leading to that intermediate. The acronym 'Bemahapothle' (Bell, Marcus, Hammond, Polanyi, Thornton, Leffler) is sometimes used in recognition of the principal contributors towards expansion of the original idea of the Hammond postulate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02734 "Hammond postulate")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02734 "Leffler's assumption")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02735))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02735 "handedness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02735 "This term has been used in two ways, either chirality or chirality sense")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02736 "Hansch constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02736 "A measure of the capability of a solute for hydrophobic lipophilic interaction based on the partition coefficient \\(P\\) for distribution of the solute between octan-1-ol and water. The most general way of applying \\(P\\) in correlation analysis, QSAR, etc. is as log \\(P\\), but the behaviour of substituted benzene derivatives may be quantified by a substituent constant scale, \\(\\pi \\), which is defined in a way analogous to the Hammett $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!}$ scale. There are various scales, depending on the substrate series used as reference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02737))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02737 "Hantzsch–Widman name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02737 "A name for a heteromonocyclic parent hydride having no more than ten ring members formed by the citation of 'a' prefixes denoting the heteroatoms followed by an ending (the 'stem') defining the size of the ring.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02738))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02738 "hapten")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02738 "A low-molecular weight molecule which contains an antigenic determinant but which is not itself antigenic unless complexed with an antigenic carrier. Examples of haptens are dinitrophenols, phosphorylcholine and dextran.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02740 "hard acid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02740 "A Lewis acid with an acceptor centre of low polarizability. Other things being approximately equal, complexes of hard acids and bases or soft acids and bases have an added stabilization (sometimes called 'HSAB' rule). For example the hard O- (or N-) bases are preferred to their S- (or P-) analogues by hard acids. Conversely a soft acid possesses an acceptor centre of high polarizability and exhibits the reverse preference for coordination of a soft base. These preferences are not defined in a quantitative sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02741))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02741 "hard amorphous carbon films")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02741 "A synonym for diamond-like carbon films")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02742))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02742 "hard base")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02742 "A Lewis base with a donor centre (e.g. an oxygen atom) of low polarizability the converse applies to soft bases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02744 "harmonic frequency generation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02744 """Production of coherent radiation of frequency \\(k\\ 
u \\) (\\(k=2,3,...\\)) from coherent radiation of frequency. In general, this effect is obtained through the interaction of laser light with a suitable optical medium with non-linear polarizability. The case \\(k=2\\) is referred to as frequency doubling, \\(k=3\\) is frequency tripling and \\(k=4\\) is frequency quadrupling. Even higher integer values of \\(k\\) are possible.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02745 "harmonic mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02745 "The number of observations, divided by the sum of reciprocals of the observations. Symbol: \\(\\bar{x}_{\\rm{h}}\\). It can be calculated with the formula: \\[\\bar{x}_{\\rm{h}} = \\frac{n}{\\sum x_{j}^{-1}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02746 "harpoon mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02746 "Reaction sequence (thermal or photoinduced) between neutral molecular or atomic entities in which long-range electron transfer is followed by a considerable reduction of the distance between donor and acceptor sites as a result of the electrostatic attraction in the ion pair created.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02747 "hartree")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02747 "Atomic unit of energy, \\(E_{\\rm{h}}\\approx 4.359\\ 7482(26)\\times 10^{-18}\\ \\rm{J}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02748 "Hartree energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02748 "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of energy: \\(E_{\\rm{h}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{\\hslash }^{2}}{m_{\\rm{e}}\\ a_{0}^{2}} = 4.359\\ 7482(26)\\times 10^{-18}\\ \\rm{J}\\), where \\(\\mathrm{\\hslash}\\) is the Planck constant divided by \\(2\\ \\pi \\), \\(m_{\\rm{e}}\\) the electron rest mass and \\(a_{0}\\) the Bohr radius.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02749 "Haworth representation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02749 "The Haworth representation of the cyclic forms of monosaccharides can be derived from the Fischer projection, as follows. The monosaccharide is depicted with the carbon-chain horizontal and in the plane of the paper, the potential carbonyl group being to the right. The oxygen bridge is then depicted as being formed behind the plane of the paper. The heterocyclic ring is therefore located in a plane approximately perpendicular to the plane of the paper and the groups attached to the carbon atoms of that ring are above and below the ring. The carbon atoms of the ring are not shown. Groups that appear to the right of the vertical chain in the Fischer projection (structures $\\ce{A}$, $\\ce{D}$) then appear below the plane of the ring in the Haworth representation (structures $\\ce{B}$, $\\ce{C}$, $\\ce{E}$). However, at the asymmetric carbon atom ($\\ce{C-5}$ in $\\ce{A}$; $\\ce{C-4}$ in $\\ce{D}$) involved via oxygen in ring formation with the carbon atom of the carbonyl group a formal double inversion must be envisaged to obtain the correct Haworth representation. In the pyranose forms of ᴅ-aldohexoses $\\ce{C-6}$ will always be above the plane, In the furanose forms of ᴅ-aldohexoses the position of $\\ce{C-6}$ will depend on the configuration at $\\ce{C-4}$; it will, for example, be above the plane in ᴅ-glucofuranoses (e.g. $\\ce{C}$) but below the plane in ᴅ-galactofuranoses (e.g. $\\ce{E}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02750))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02750 "haze")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02750 "A state of reduced visibility (\\(1{-}2\\ \\rm{km}\\)) resulting from the increased light scatter due to the presence of fine dust or aerosol particles ($\\ce{H2SO4}$, $\\ce{NH4HSO4}$, products of the ozone-terpene reactions, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02751 "haze horizon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02751 "The top of a haze layer which is confined by a low-level temperature inversion so that it gives the appearance of the horizon which it may obscure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02752 "heat")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02752 "Energy transferred from a hotter to a cooler body due to a temperature gradient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02753 "heat capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02753 "Heat brought to a system to increase its temperature divided by that temperature increase. At constant volume \\(C_{V}=(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial T})_{V}\\) at constant pressure \\(C_{p}=(\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial T})_{p}\\), where \\(U\\) is the internal energy and \\(H\\) the enthalpy of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02754))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02754 "heat capacity of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02754 "A quantity related to the temperature coefficient of \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\) (enthalpy of activation) and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S\\) (entropy of activation) according to the equations: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}C_{p} = (\\frac{\\partial \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }H}{\\partial T})_{p}=T\\ (\\frac{\\partial \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S}{\\partial T})_{p}\\] If the rate constant is expressible in the form \\[\\ln k = \\frac{a}{T}+b+c\\ \\ln T + \\rm{d}T,\\] then: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}C_{p} = (c - 1)\\ R+2\\ \\rm{d}(R\\ T)\\] SI unit: \\(\\pu{J mol-1 K-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02755 "heat flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02755 "Heat transferred through a cross section perpendicular to the flow in a small time interval divided by that time interval and the cross sectional area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02756 "heavy atom effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02756 "The enhancement of the rate of a spin-forbidden process by the presence of an atom of high atomic number, which is either part of, or external to, the excited molecular entity. Mechanistically, it responds to a spin–orbit coupling enhancement produced by a heavy atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02756 "external heavy atom effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02757))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02757 "heavy atom isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02757 "An isotope effect due to isotopes other than those of hydrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02758 "heavy water")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02758 "Water containing a significant fraction (up to 100 per cent) of deuterium in the form of $\\ce{D2O}$ or $\\ce{HDO}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02759))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02759 "hecto")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02759 "SI prefix for \\(10^{2}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{h}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02760 "height equivalent to an effective theoretical plate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02760 "The column length divided by the effective theoretical plate number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02761))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02761 "height equivalent to a theoretical plate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02761 "The column length divided by the theoretical plate number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02762 "helicenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02762 "ortho-Fused polycyclic aromatic or heteroaromatic compounds in which all rings (minimum five) are angularly arranged so as to give helically shaped molecules, which are thus chiral, e.g. hexahelicene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02763 "helicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02763 "The chirality of a helical, propeller or screw-shaped molecular entity. A right-handed helix is described as P (or plus), a left-handed one as M (or minus).    The application of this system to the description of conformations considers the torsion angle between two specified (fiducial) groups that are attached to the atoms linked by that bond. The sign of the smaller torsion angle between the fiducial groups defines the chirality sense of the helix. Rules for the selection of fiducial groups according to priority are given by R.S. Cahn, C.K. Ingold and V. Prelog, Angew. Chem. 78, 413-447 (1966), Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 5, 385-415, 511 (1966).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02764))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02764 "helion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02764 "Nucleus of the $\\ce{^{3}He}$ atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02765 "helium–cadmium laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02765 "A continuous wave laser emitting mainly at \\(325.0\\) and \\(441.6\\ \\rm{nm}\\) from singly ionized cadmium.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02765 "cadium–helium laser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02766 "helium dead-space")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02766 "The position of the Gibbs surface is often defined experimentally as that surface which encloses the volume of space from which the solid excludes helium gas (the so-called helium dead-space), and is associated with the assumptions that the volume of the solid is unaffected by the adsorption of component i, and that helium is not adsorbed by the solid. This requires that the measurement of the helium dead-space be made at a sufficiently high temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02767 "helium ionization detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02767 "A weak beta source and a high potential raise the helium atom of the carrier gas to a metastable state. All other substances having an ionization potential lower than \\(18\\ \\rm{eV}\\) are ionized, and the current that results is used to measure the components. The detector is usually employed to measure inorganic compounds at concentrations between \\(0.1\\) and \\(10\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\). It has a linear range of about \\(10^{2}\\) but is somewhat unstable and requires great care to ensure the helium purity and to eliminate all leaks in the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02768))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02768 "helium–neon laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02768 "A continuous wave laser emitting mainly at \\(632.8\\ \\rm{nm}\\), \\(1152.3\\ \\rm{nm}\\), and \\(3391.3\\ \\rm{nm}\\) from excited neutral $\\ce{Ne}$ atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02769 "helix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02769 "The molecular conformation of a spiral nature, generated by regularly repeating rotations around the backbone bonds of a macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02770 "helix residue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02770 "The smallest set of one or more successive configurational base units that generates the whole chain through helical symmetry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02771 "helix sense")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02771 "The right-handed sense of a helix traces out a clockwise rotation moving away from the observer; the left-handed sense of a helix traces out a counterclockwise rotation moving away from the observer, e.g. the ...\\(\\mathrm{TG}^{+}\\) \\(\\mathrm{TG}^{+}\\) \\(\\mathrm{TG}^{+}\\) … helix of isotactic poly(propylene) is left-handed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02772 "Helmholtz energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02772 "Internal energy minus the product of thermodynamic temperature and entropy. It was formerly called free energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02772 "Helmholtz energy function")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02773 "hemes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02773 "Complexes consisting of an iron ion coordinated to a porphyrin acting as a tetradentate ligand, and to one or two axial ligands.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02773 "heme derivatives")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02774))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02774 "hemiacetals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02774 """Compounds having the general formula $\\ce{R2C(OH)OR'}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02775 "hemiaminals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02775 """$\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}Amino}$ alcohols, improperly called carbinolamines (the adducts of ammonia, or of primary or secondary amines to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones) $\\ce{R2C(OH)NR2}$. Compounds of structure $\\ce{R2C(OR')NR2}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$) are hemiaminal ethers, or $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino}$ ethers.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02776 "hemiketals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02776 """Hemiacetals having the structure $\\ce{R2C(OH)OR}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), derived from ketones by formal addition of an alcohol to the carbonyl group. This term, once abandoned, has been reinstated as a subclass of hemiacetals.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02778 "hemins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02778 "Chloro(porphyrinato)iron(III) complexes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02779 "hemochromes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02779 "Iron–porphyrin complexes, having one or two basic ligands (e.g. piperidine, amines).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02780 "hemoglobins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02780 "Heme derivatives having a protein chain as axial ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02781))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02781 "Henderson–Hasselbalch equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02781 "An equation of the form: \\[\\rm{pH} = \\rm{p}K_{\\rm{a}}- \\log _{10}(\\frac{\\left[\\rm{HA}\\right]}{\\left[\\rm{A}^{-}\\right]})\\] for the calculation of the \\(\\rm{pH}\\) of solutions where the ratio \\(\\frac{\\left[\\rm{HA}\\right]}{\\left[\\rm{A}^{-}\\right]}\\) is known.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02782 "henry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02782 "SI derived unit of inductance, \\(\\pu{H} = \\pu{V A-1 s} = \\pu{m2 kg s-2 A-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02783 "Henry's law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02783 "The partial pressure (fugacity) of a solute (B) in a solution is directly proportional to the rational chemical activity (\\(a_{x}\\)) of the solute; this relationship is called Henry's law: \\[p_{\\rm{B}}=\\frac{a_{x,\\rm{B}}}{\\alpha _{x,\\rm{B}}^{\\infty }}\\] where \\(\\alpha _{x,\\rm{B}}^{\\infty }\\) is the rational solubility coefficient for infinite dilution, i.e. for pure solvent. For the solvent (A) the relationship is called Raoult's law, and the proportionality factor is the fugacity of the pure solvent, \\(\\overset{˜}{p}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\ast }\\): \\[p_{\\rm{A}}=\\overset{˜}{p}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\ast }\\ a_{\\rm{A}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02785))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02785 "hertz")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02785 "SI derived unit of frequency equal to one cycle per second, \\(\\rm{Hz} = \\rm{s}^{-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02785 "cycles per second")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02786))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02786 "Herz compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02786 "1,2$\\uplambda\\!$4,3-Benzodithiazolium chlorides (formed in the reaction of aniline and derivatives thereof with disulfur dichloride).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02787 "hetarenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02787 "Synonymous with heteroarenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02788))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02788 "hetaryl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02788 "Synonymous with heteroaryl groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02789))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02789 "hetarynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02789 "Synonymous with heteroarynes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02790 "heteroalkenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02790 "Analogues of alkenes in which a doubly bonded carbon atom is replaced by a heteroatom, e.g. $\\ce{H2Si=CH2}$ methylidenesilane (silene less preferred), $\\ce{MeN=CH2}$ N-methylmethanimine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02791))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02791 "heteroarenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02791 "Heterocyclic compounds formally derived from arenes by replacement of one or more methine ($\\ce{–C=}$) and/or vinylene ($\\ce{–CH=CH\\bond{–}}$) groups by trivalent or divalent heteroatoms, respectively, in such a way as to maintain the continuous $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system characteristic of aromatic systems and a number of out-of-plane $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ corresponding to the Hückel rule (\\(4\\ n+2\\)); an alternative term is hetarenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02792 "heteroaryl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02792 "The class of heterocyclyl groups derived from heteroarenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from any ring atom; an alternative term is hetaryl. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02793 "heteroarynes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02793 "Compounds derived from heteroarenes by replacement of a formal carbon-carbon double bond by a formal triple bond (with loss of two hydrogen atoms). Also known as hetarynes and as 1,2-didehydroheteroarenes. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02794 "heterobimetallic complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02794 "A metal complex having two different metal atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02795 "heterochain polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02795 "A class of polymer in which the main chain is constructed from atoms of two or more elements. Heterochain polymers are named by placing the names or symbols of all the elements in the main chain, immediately before the expresssion '-chain polymer', e.g. (oxygen, carbon)-chain polymer or (O, C)-chain polymer; (oxygen, nitrogen, carbon)-chain polymer or (O, N, C)-chain polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02796 "heteroconjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02796 """[obsolete] Association between a base and the conjugate acid of a different base through a hydrogen bond ($\\ce{B'\\bond{...}HB^{+}}$ or $\\ce{A'H\\bond{...}A^{-}}$). The term has its origin in the conjugate acid–base pair and is in no way related to conjugated systems of orbitals. Heteroassociation is a more appropriate term.
[obsolete] Some authors refer to conjugated systems containing a heteroatom, e.g. pyridine, as 'heteroconjugated systems'. This usage is discouraged since it inappropriately suggests an analogy to homoconjugation, and conflicts with the currently accepted definition of that term.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02797))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02797 "heterocumulenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02797 "Cumulenes in which one or more carbon atoms of the cumulative bond system have been replaced by heteroatoms. E.g. $\\ce{O=C=C=C=O}$, but not $\\ce{CH2=C=O}$, ketene, nor $\\ce{O=C=O}$, carbon dioxide, which are heteroallenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02798 "heterocyclic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02798 "Cyclic compounds having as ring members atoms of at least two different elements, e.g. quinoline, 1,2-thiazole, bicyclo[3.3.1]tetrasiloxane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02799))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02799 "heterocyclyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02799 "Univalent groups formed by removing a hydrogen atom from any ring atom of a heterocyclic compound. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02800 "heterodetic cyclic peptide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02800 "A peptide consisting only of amino-acid residues, but in which the linkages forming the ring are not solely eupeptide bonds; one or more is an isopeptide, disulfide, ester, or other bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02801))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02801 "heterodisperse")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02801 "Describes a colloidal system in which all the particles are of different sizes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02802 "heteroexcimer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02802 "Synonymous with exciplex.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02805))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02805 "heterogeneous diffusion rate constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02805 "Defined by the equation: \\[k_{\\rm{d}}=\\frac{I_{\\rm{l}}}{n\\ F\\ c\\ A}\\] where the limiting current \\(I_{\\rm{l}}\\) is assumed to be due to the diffusion species of concentration \\(c\\) and of diffusion coefficient \\(D\\). \\(n\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction written so that the stoichiometric coefficient of this species is unity. \\(A\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode, and \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02808))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02808 "heteroleptic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02808 "Transition metal or Main Group compounds having more than one type of ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02809 "heterolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02809 "The cleavage of a covalent bond so that both bonding electrons remain with one of the two fragments between which the bond is broken, e.g.  Heterolytic bond fission is a feature of many bimolecular reactions in solution (e.g.electrophilic substitution, nucleophilic substitution).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02809 "heterolytic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02810 "heterolytic bond-dissociation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02810 "The energy required to break a given bond of some specific compound by heterolysis. For the dissociation of a neutral molecule $\\ce{AB}$ in the gas phase into $\\ce{A^{+}}$ and $\\ce{B^{−}}$ the heterolytic bond-dissociation energy $D(\\ce{A^{+}B^{−}})$ is the sum of the bond dissociation energy, $D(\\ce{A–B})$, and the adiabatic ionization energy of the radical $\\ce{A^{.}}$; minus the electron affinity of the radical $\\ce{B^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02811 "heterolytic dissociative adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02811 "Relates in the usual sense to the formal nature of the cleavage of a single bond. If in the bond of the adsorptive $\\ce{A:B}$, $\\ce{A}$ or $\\ce{B}$ retains the electron pair, the adsorption is heterolytic dissociative adsorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02812 "heteropolysaccharide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02812 "The class name for polysaccharides composed of two or more different kinds of monomeric units (i.e. monosaccharides).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02812 "heteroglycan")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02816))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02816 "heterotrophic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02816 "Descriptive of organisms which are not able to synthesize cell components from carbon dioxide as sole carbon source. Heterotrophic organisms use preformed oxidizable organic substrates such as glucose as carbon and energy sources.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02816 "heterotrophic organisms")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02818 "hexagonal graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02818 "The thermodynamically stable form of graphite with an ABAB stacking sequence of the graphene layers. The exact crystallographic description of this allotropic form is given by the space group \\(d_{6h}^{4} - P6_{3}/\\rm{mmc}\\) (unit cell constants: \\(a=245.6\\ \\mathrm{pm}\\), \\(c=670.8\\ \\mathrm{pm}\\)). Hexagonal graphite is thermodynamically stable below approximately \\(2600\\ \\rm{K}\\) and \\(6\\ \\rm{GPa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02819 "<i>hexahedro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02819 "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a hexahedron (e.g. cube).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02820))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02820 "<em>hexaprismo</em>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02820 "An affix used in names to denote twelve atoms bound into a hexagonal prism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02822 "higher-order transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02822 "A general term used to describe a transition in which the first and second derivatives of the molar Gibbs energy or molar Helmholtz energy (or chemical potential) with respect to temperature and pressure are continuous, but derivatives of some higher order are discontinuous at the transition point. Synonymous with smooth transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02822 "smooth transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02823 "highly oriented pyrolytic graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02823 "Highly oriented pyrolytic graphite (HOPG) is a pyrolytic graphite with an angular spread of the c-axes of the crystallites of less than 1 degree.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02824 "high-pressure graphitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02824 "A term which refers to the solid-state transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment under elevated pressure (e.g. \\(100\\) to \\(1000\\ \\rm{MPa}\\)) so that a definitely higher degree of graphitization is achieved at lower temperature and/or for a shorter heat treatment time than in heat treatment of the same non-graphitic material at atmospheric pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02825 "high-pressure mercury lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02825 "Radiation source containing mercury at a pressure of ca. \\(8\\ \\rm{MPa}\\) (ca. \\(80\\ \\rm{bar}\\)) or higher which emits lines over a background continuum between about \\(200\\) and \\(1400\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02825 "high-pressure mercury arc")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02825 "hot quartz lamp")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02827 "Hildebrand parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02827 "A parameter measuring the cohesion of a solvent (energy required to create a cavity in the solvent).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02829 "histones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02829 "A class of basic proteins associated with DNA in the chromosomes of eukaryotic cells forming the nucleosome as the basic subunit of chromatin. They contain an unusually large proportion of the basic amino acids arginine and lysine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02830))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02830 "Hofmann rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02830 "'The principal olefin formed in the decomposition of quaternary ammonium hydroxides that contain different primary alkyl groups is always ethylene, if an ethyl group is present.' Originally given in this limited form by A. W. Hofmann, the rule has since been extended and modified as follows: 'When two or more alkenes can be produced in a $\\upbeta\\!$-elimination reaction, the alkene having the smallest number of alkyl groups attached to the double bond carbon atoms will be the predominant product.' This orientation described by the Hofmann rule is observed in elimination reactions of quaternary ammonium salts and tertiary sulfonium salts, and in certain other cases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02831 "hold-back carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02831 "A carrier used to prevent a particular species from following other species in a chemical or physical operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02832))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02832 "hold-up volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02832 "The volume of eluent required to elute a component, the concentration of which in the stationary phase is negligible compared to that in the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02833 "hold-up volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02833 "The volume of the mobile phase (or the corresponding time) required to elute a component the concentration of which in the stationary phase is negligible compared to that in the mobile phase. In other words, this component is not retained at all by the stationary phase. Thus, the hold-up volume (time) is equal to the retention volume (time) of an unretained compound. The hold-up volume (time) includes any volumes contributed by the sample injector, the detector, and connectors. \\[t_{\\rm{M}}=\\frac{V_{\\rm{M}}}{F_{\\rm{c}}}\\] In gas chromatography this term is also called the gas hold-up volume (time). The corrected gas hold-up volume (\\(V_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}\\)) is the gas hold-up volume multiplied by the compression (compressibility) correction factor (\\(j\\)): \\[V_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}=V_{\\rm{M}}\\ j\\] Assuming that the influence of extra column volume on \\(V_{\\rm{M}}\\) is negligible, \\[V_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}=V_{\\rm{G}}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02833 "hold-up time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02834 "hole burning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02834 "The photo bleaching of a feature, normally a narrow range, within an inhomogeneous broader absorption or emission band. The holes are produced by the disappearance of resonantly excited molecules as a result of photophysical or photochemical processes. The resulting spectroscopic technique is site-selection spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02835 "hole transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02835 "Charge migration process in which the majority carriers are positively charged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02836 "holoenzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02836 "An active enzyme consisting of the apoenzyme and coenzyme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02838 "homo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02838 "a) An acronym for Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital (HOMO). See: frontier orbitals.b) A prefix (consisting of lower case letters, homo), used to indicate a higher homologue of a compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02839 "homoaromatic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02839 "Whereas in an aromatic molecule there is continuous overlap of p-orbitals over a cyclic array of atoms, in a homoaromatic molecule there is a formal discontinuity in this overlap resulting from the presence of a single \\(\\rm{sp}^{3}\\) hybridized atom at one or several positions within the ring; p-orbital overlap apparently bridges these \\(\\rm{sp}^{3}\\) centres, and features associated with aromaticity are manifest in the properties of the compound. Pronounced homoaromaticity is not normally associated with neutral molecules, but mainly with species bearing an electrical charge, e.g. the 'homotropylium' cation, ($\\ce{C8H9^{+}}$):  In bis, tris, (etc.) homoaromatic species, two, three, (etc.) single \\(\\rm{sp}^{3}\\) centres separately interrupt the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02840 "homochain polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02840 "A class of polymer in which the main chain is constructed from atoms of a single element. Homochain polymers are named by placing the name or symbol of the element in the main chain immediately before the expression '-chain polymer', e.g. carbon-chain polymer or C-chain polymer; sulfur-chain polymer or S-chain polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02842 "homoconjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02842 "[obsolete] Association between a base and its conjugate acid through a hydrogen bond ($\\ce{B\\bond{...}HB^{+}}$ or $\\ce{AH\\bond{...}A^{-}}$). Homoassociation is a more appropriate term for this phenomenon.The orbital overlap of two $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}systems}$ separated by a non-conjugating group, such as $\\ce{CH2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02843))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02843 "homocyclic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02843 "Cyclic compounds having as ring members atoms of the same element only, e.g. benzene, pentazole, cyclohexasilane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02844 "homodetic cyclic peptide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02844 "A cyclic peptide in which the ring consists solely of amino-acid residues in eupeptide linkage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02845 "homogeneity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02845 "The degree to which a property or a constituent is uniformly distributed throughout a quantity of material. A material may be homogeneous with respect to one analyte or property but heterogeneous with respect to another. The degree of heterogeneity (the opposite of homogeneity) is the determining factor of sampling error.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02845 "material heterogeneity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02845 "material homogeneity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02850 "homoleptic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02850 "Transition metal or Main Group compounds having only one type of ligand are said to be homoleptic, e.g. $\\ce{TaMe5}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02850 "hemileptic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02851 "homolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02851 "The cleavage of a bond ('homolytic cleavage' or 'homolytic fission') so that each of the molecular fragments between which the bond is broken retains one of the bonding electrons. A unimolecular reaction involving homolysis of a bond (not forming part of a cyclic structure) in a molecular entity containing an even number of (paired) electrons results in the formation of two radicals:  It is the reverse of colligation. Homolysis is also commonly a feature of bimolecular substitution reactions (and of other reactions) involving radicals and molecules.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02851 "homolytic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02852 "homolytic dissociative adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02852 "Relates in the usual sense to the formal nature of the cleavage of a single bond. If the electron pair in the bond of the adsorptive $\\ce{A{:}B}$ is divided in the course of its dissociative adsorption, the adsorption is homolytic dissociative adsorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02853))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02853 "homomorphic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02853 "Superposable ligands are called homomorphic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02854 "homopolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02854 "A polymer derived from one species of (real, implicit or hypothetical) monomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02855))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02855 "homopolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02855 "Polymerization in which a homopolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02856 "homopolysaccharide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02856 "The class name for polysaccharides composed of only one kind of monosaccharide.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02856 "homoglycan")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02857 "homotopic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02857 "Atoms or groups of a molecule which are related by an n-fold rotation axis (n = 2, 3, etc.) are called homotopic. For example, chiral tartaric acid (\\(C_{2}\\) axis), chloroform (\\(C_{3}\\) axis) and cyclohexaamylose ($\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}cyclodextrin}$, \\(C_{6}\\) axis) have respectively two homotopic carboxyl groups, three homotopic chlorine atoms and six homotopic ᴅ-glucose residues.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02858 "horizontal elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02858 "A mode of operation in which the paper or plate is in a horizontal position and the mobile-phase movement along the plane depends on capillary action.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02858 "horizontal development")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02859 "host")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02859 "A molecular entity that forms complexes with organic or inorganic guests, or a chemical species that can accommodate guests within cavities of its crystal structure. Examples include cryptands and crowns (where there are ion-dipole attractions between heteroatoms and positive ions), hydrogen-bonded molecules that form 'clathrates' (e.g. hydroquinone and water), and host molecules of inclusion compounds (e.g. urea or thiourea). van der Waals forces and hydrophobic interactions bind the guest to the host molecule in clathrates and inclusion compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02860 "host-vector system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02860 "A compatible combination of host (e.g. bacteria) and vector (e.g. plasmid) that allows propagation of DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02861 "hot atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02861 "An atom in an excited energy state or having kinetic energy above the ambient thermal level, usually as a result of nuclear processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02862))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02862 "hot cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02862 "A heavily shielded enclosure for highly radioactive materials. It may be used for their handling or processing by remote means or for their storage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02863 "hot ground state reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02863 "A hot state reaction of the ground electronic state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02865 "hot state reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02865 "A reaction proceeding from an ensemble of molecular entities possessing a higher average vibrational, rotational or translational energy than they would at thermal equilibrium with the surrounding medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02866 "hour")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02866 "Non-SI unit of time, \\(\\rm{h} = 3600\\ \\rm{s}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02867))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02867 "Hückel (4<em>n</em> + 2) rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02867 "Monocyclic planar (or almost planar) systems of trigonally (or sometimes digonally) hybridized atoms that contain $\\ce{(4n + 2) \\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ (where $n$ is a non-negative integer) will exhibit aromatic character. The rule is generally limited to $n = 0 – 5$. This rule is derived from the Hückel MO calculation on planar monocyclic conjugated hydrocarbons $\\ce{(CH)_{m}}$ where $m$ is an integer equal to or greater than 3 according to which $\\ce{(4n + 2) \\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ are contained in a closed-shell system. Examples of systems that obey the Hückel rule include:  Systems containing $\\ce{4n\\;\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ (such as cyclobutadiene and the cyclopentadienyl cation) are 'antiaromatic'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02868 "Huggins coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02868 "A parameter in the Huggins equation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02869 "Huggins equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02869 "The equation describing the dependence of the reduced viscosity, \\(\\frac{\\eta _{\\rm{i}}}{c}\\), on the mass concentration of a polymer, \\(c\\), for dilute polymer solutions of the form: \\[\\frac{\\eta _{\\rm{i}}}{c} = \\left[\\eta \\right]+k_{\\rm{H}}\\ \\left[\\eta \\right]^{2}\\ c\\] where \\(k_{\\rm{H}}\\) is the Huggins coefficient and \\([\\eta]\\) is the intrinsic viscosity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02870 "humidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02870 "A general term referring to the water content of a gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02871 "Hund rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02871 "Of the different multiplets resulting from different configurations of electrons in degenerate orbitals of an atom those with greatest multiplicity have the lowest energy (multiplicity rule).Among multiplets having the same multiplicity, the lowest-energy one is that with the largest total orbital angular momentum (angular momentum rule) (valid if the total orbital angular momentum is a constant of motion).In configurations containing shells less than half full of electrons, the term having the lowest total angular momentum \\(J\\) lies lowest in energy, whereas in those with shells more than half filled, the term having the largest value of \\(J\\) lies lowest (fine structure rule). Hund rules apply if the 'Russell–Saunders' coupling scheme is valid. Sometimes the first rule is applied with questionable validity to molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02874 "hybridization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02874 "Linear combination of atomic orbitals on an atom. Hybrid orbitals are often used in organic chemistry to describe the bonding molecules containing tetrahedral (\\(\\mathrm{sp}^{3}\\)), trigonal (\\(\\mathrm{sp}^{2}\\)) and digonal (\\(\\mathrm{sp}\\)) atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02875 "hybridoma")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02875 "A hybrid cell line resulting from the fusion of a specific antibody-producing spleen cell (lymphocyte) with a myeloma cell, which has the growth characteristics of the myeloma component and the antibody-secreting characteristics of the lymphocyte, and will multiply to become a source of pure monoclonal antibody.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02876 "hydration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02876 "Addition of water or of the elements of water (i.e. H and OH) to a molecular entity. For example, hydration of ethene:  The term is also used in a more restricted sense for the process:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02879))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02879 "hydrazides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02879 """Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by replacing $\\ce{–OH}$ by $\\ce{–NRNR2}$ ($\\ce{R}$ groups are commonly $\\ce{H}$), as in carbohydrazides, $\\ce{RC(=O)NHNH2}$, sulfonohydrazides, $\\ce{RS(=O)2NHNH2}$, and phosphonic dihydrazides, $\\ce{RP(=O)(NHNH2)2}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02880 "hydrazidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02880 "Compounds, $\\ce{RC(NHNH2)2=NNH2}$, derived from carboxylic acids by replacing $\\ce{–OH}$ by $\\ce{–NHNH2}$ (or N-substituted analogues) and $\\ce{=O}$ by $\\ce{=NNH2}$ (or substituted analogues). A specific hydrazidine is named as a hydrazide hydrazone, e.g. hexanohydrazide hydrazone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02881))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02881 "hydrazines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02881 "Hydrazine (diazane), $\\ce{H2NNH2}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. When one or more substituents are acyl groups, the compound is a hydrazide. N-Alkylidene derivatives are hydrazones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02883))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02883 "hydrazo compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02883 "Compounds containing the divalent hydrazo group, $\\ce{–NHNH\\bond{–}}$, such as hydrazoarenes (1,2-diarylhydrazines or 1,2-diaryldiazanes, usually with both aryl groups the same) and their N-substituted derivatives $\\ce{ArNRNRAr}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02884 "hydrazones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02884 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=NNR2}$, formally derived from aldehydes or ketones by replacing $\\ce{=O}$ by $\\ce{=NNH2}$ (or substituted analogues).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02885 "hydrazonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02885 """Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by replacing a double-bonded oxygen atom by $\\ce{=NNR2}$, as in carbohydrazonic acids, $\\ce{RC(OH)=NNH2}$ and sulfonohydrazonic acids, $\\ce{RS(=O)(=NNH2)OH}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02889 "hydrocarbons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02889 "Compounds consisting of carbon and hydrogen only.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02890))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02890 "hydrocarbylene groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02890 "Divalent groups formed by removing two hydrogen atoms from a hydrocarbon, the free valencies of which are not engaged in a double bond, e.g. 1,3-phenylene, $\\ce{–CH2CH2CH2–}$ propane-1,3-diyl, $\\ce{–CH2–}$ methylene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02891 "hydrocarbyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02891 "Univalent groups formed by removing a hydrogen atom from a hydrocarbon, e.g. ethyl, phenyl.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02892 "hydrocarbylidene groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02892 "Divalent groups, $\\ce{R2C=}$, formed by removing two hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom of a hydrocarbon, the free valencies of which are engaged in a double bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02893 "hydrocarbylidyne groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02893 "Trivalent groups, $\\ce{RC#}$, formed by removing three hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom of a hydrocarbon, the free valencies of which are engaged in a triple bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02894 "hydrocarbylsulfanyl nitrenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02894 "Nitrenes substituted with hydrocarbyl sulfanyl groups, . Sulfenyl nitrenes is an older synonymous term. E.g. MeSN methylsulfanylnitrene or methylthionitrene. The synonymous term 'thiazynes' (from the third canonical form; confusable with the hetarynes derivable from 1,2- and 1,4-thiazine) is best avoided.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02894 "sulfenyl nitrenes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02894 "thiazynes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02895 "hydrocracking unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02895 "Used in the thermal decomposition of heavy (high molecular weight) hydrocarbons to smaller (low molecular weight) hydrocarbons; high pressures of hydrogen and a special catalyst are employed. Sulfur compounds in the fuel are reduced to $\\ce{H2S}$, and the final hydrocarbon product can be obtained relatively sulfur-free.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02896 "hydrodynamically equivalent sphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02896 "A hypothetical sphere, impenetrable to the surrounding medium, displaying in a hydrodynamic field the same frictional effect as an actual polymer molecule. The size of a hydrodynamically equivalent sphere may be different for different types of motion of the macromolecule, e.g. for diffusion and for viscous flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02897 "hydrodynamic volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02897 "The volume of a hydrodynamically equivalent sphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02898 "hydrogen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02898 "The general name for the atom $\\ce{H}$, without regard for its nuclear mass, either for hydrogen in its natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes. The systematic name for atomic hydrogen is monohydrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02899 "hydrogen bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02899 "A form of association between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen atom attached to a second, relatively electronegative atom. It is best considered as an electrostatic interaction, heightened by the small size of hydrogen, which permits proximity of the interacting dipoles or charges. Both electronegative atoms are usually (but not necessarily) from the first row of the Periodic Table, i.e. $\\ce{N}$, $\\ce{O}$ or $\\ce{F}$. Hydrogen bonds may be inter-molecular or intramolecular. With a few exceptions, usually involving fluorine, the associated energies are less than \\(\\pu{20\\!-\\! 25 kJ mol-1}\\) (\\(\\pu{5\\!{-}\\! 6 kcal mol-1}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02900))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02900 "hydrogen gas electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02900 "A thin foil of platinum electrolytically coated with a finely divided deposit of platinum or palladium metal, which catalyses the electrode reaction: \\[\\rm{H}^{+}+\\rm{e}\\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}\\rm{H}_{2}\\] in solutions saturated with hydrogen gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02901))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02901 "hydrolases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02901 "Enzymes that catalyse the cleavage of $\\ce{C–O}$, $\\ce{C–N}$, $\\ce{C–C}$ and other bonds by reactions involving the addition or removal of water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02902 "hydrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02902 "Solvolysis by water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02903 "hydrometeor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02903 "Any condensed water particle in the atmosphere of sufficient size to be potentially capable of undergoing precipitation (in fogs, clouds, some hazes, raindrops, snowflakes, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02904))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02904 "hydron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02904 "The general name for the cation $\\ce{H^{+}}$; the species $\\ce{H^{−}}$ is the hydride anion and $\\ce{H}$ is the hydro group. These are general names to be used without regard to the nuclear mass of the hydrogen entity, either for hydrogen in its natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02905 "hydroperoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02905 "Monosubstitution products of hydrogen peroxide (dioxidane), $\\ce{HOOH}$, having the skeleton $\\ce{ROOH}$, in which $\\ce{R}$ is any organyl group. Compounds in which $\\ce{R}$ is acyl are known as peroxy acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02906 "hydrophilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02906 "'Water loving'. The capacity of a molecular entity or of a substituent to interact with polar solvents, in particular with water, or with other polar groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02907 "hydrophobic interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02907 "The tendency of hydrocarbons (or of lipophilic hydrocarbon-like groups in solutes) to form intermolecular aggregates in an aqueous medium, and analogous intramolecular interactions. The name arises from the attribution of the phenomenon to the apparent repulsion between water and hydrocarbons. However, the phenomenon ought to be attributed to the effect of the hydrocarbon-like groups on the water-water interaction. The misleading alternative term 'hydrophobic bond' is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02908 "hydropolysulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02908 "Compounds having the structures $\\ce{RS2H}$, $\\ce{RS3H}$ ... $\\ce{RSnH}$, in which $\\ce{Sn}$ is a chain of sulfur atoms, and $\\ce{R}$ is hydrocarbyl. Some people exclude hydrodisulfides from the class hydropolysulfides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02909 "hydrosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02909 "The gaseous, liquid and solid water of the earth (oceans, ice caps, lakes, rivers, etc.) as distinguished from the lithosphere and the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02910))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02910 "hydrosulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02910 "Term used in radicofunctional nomenclature of thiols. Hydrosulfides is not commonly used as a class name for thiols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02911 "hydroxamic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02911 """Compounds, $\\ce{RC(=O)NHOH}$, derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by replacing $\\ce{–OH}$ by $\\ce{–NHOH}$, and hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. Specific examples are preferably named as N-hydroxy amides.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02912))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02912 "hydroximic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02912 """Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by replacing $\\ce{=O}$ by $\\ce{=NOH}$ ($\\ce{=NOR}$), as in carbohydroximic acids, $\\ce{RC(OH)=NOH}$, and sulfonohydroximic acids, $\\ce{RS(=O)(=NOH)OH}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02913))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02913 "hydroxylamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02913 "Hydroxylamine, $\\ce{H2N–OH}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02914 "hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02914 "Instrument used to measure the water vapour content of the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02922))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02922 "hygrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02922 "The measurement or indication of the water content of the ambient air or of a sample of gas.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02922 "moisture analysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02923 "hyperchromic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02923 "The increase in the intensity of a spectral band due to substituents or interactions with the molecular environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02924))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02924 "hyperconjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02924 "In the formalism that separates bonds into $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\uppi\\! types}$, hyperconjugation is the interaction of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ (e.g. $\\ce{C–H}$, $\\ce{C–C}$, etc.) with a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ network. This interaction is customarily illustrated by contributing structure, e.g. for toluene (below), sometimes said to be an example of 'heterovalent' or 'sacrificial hyperconjugation', so named because the contributing structure contains one two-electron bond less than the normal Lewis formula for toluene:  At present, there is no evidence for sacrificial hyperconjugation in neutral hydrocarbons. The concept of hyperconjugation is also applied to carbenium ions and radicals, where the interaction is now between $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ and an unfilled or partially filled $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or p-orbital. A contributing structure illustrating this for the tert-butyl cation is:  This latter example is sometimes called an example of 'isovalent hyper-conjugation' (the contributing structure containing the same number of two-electron bonds as the normal Lewis formula). Both structures shown on the right hand side are also examples of 'double bond-no-bond resonance'. The interaction between filled $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or p-orbitals and adjacent antibonding  $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ is referred to as 'negative hyperconjugation', as for example in the fluoroethyl anion:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02926))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02926 "<i>hypho</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02926 "An affix used in names to designate an open structure, especially a boron skeleton, more closed than a klado structure but more open than an arachno structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02927))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02927 "hypochromic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02927 "The opposite of hyperchromic effect (i.e. a decrease in intensity).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02928 "hypo-phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02928 "The denser phase in an extraction system. This term is often used when two non-aqueous phases are present or when the solvent is an aqueous phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02929 "hypsochromic shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02929 "Shift of a spectral band to higher frequency or shorter wavelength upon substitution or change in medium. It is informally referred to as blue shift.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:H02930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:H02930 "hysteresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:H02930 "Regarding a material quantity or instrument's reading; the dependence of a value on the direction of change from a previous characteristic value. It may be quantified by the difference between the upscale and downscale variation starting from fixed lower and upper measurement points (inversion).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:H02930 "electrode memory")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06787 "Haber–Weiss reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06787 "The Haber–Weiss cycle consists of the following two reactions: $\\ce{H2O2 + OH^{.} -> H2O + O2^{−} + H^{+}}$and$\\ce{H2O2 + O2^{-} -> O2 + OH^{-} + OH^{.}}$ The second reaction achieved notoriety as a possible source of hydroxyl radicals. However, it has a negligible rate constant. It is believed that iron(III) complexes can catalyse this reaction: first, Fe(III) is reduced by superoxide, followed by oxidation by dihydrogen peroxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06827 "hazard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06827 "Set of inherent properties of a substance, mixture of substances, or a process involving substances that, under production, usage, or disposal conditions, make it capable of causing adverse effects to organisms or the environment, depending on the degree of exposure; in other words, it is a source of danger.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06886))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06886 "hemodialysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06886 "Dialysis process in which undesired metabolites and toxic by-products, such as urea and creatine, are removed from blood.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06908 "health surveillance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06908 "The procedure for effects of work on the health of employees.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06962))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06962 "Hansch analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06962 "Hansch analysis is the investigation of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a series of compounds and their physicochemical substituent or global parameters representing hydrophobic, electronic, steric and other effects using multiple regression correlation methodology.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06963))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06963 "hydrophilicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06963 "Hydrophilicity is the tendency of a molecule to be solvated by water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT06964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT06964 "hydrophobicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT06964 "Hydrophobicity is the association of non-polar groups or molecules in an aqueous environment which arises from the tendency of water to exclude non-polar molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07035 "Hückel molecular orbital theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07035 "The simplest molecular orbital theory of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}conjugated}$ molecular systems. It uses the following approximations: $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ approximation; LCAO representation of the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}molecular orbitals}$; neglect of electron-electron and nuclear-nuclear repulsions (in fact, the assumption that these cancel). The diagonal elements of the effective Hamiltonian, coulombic integrals, and the off-diagonal elements, resonance integrals, (accounted for only directly bonded atoms) are chosen as empirical parameters, all overlap integrals being neglected.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:HT07035 "HMO theory")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07041 "harmonic approximation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07041 """The approximation of the full nuclear potential of a molecular system in its equilibrium geometry and in the vicinity of the respective minimum on the potential energy surface by the function \\[V=\\frac{1}{2}\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i,j=1
\\end{array}}^{3\\ N}\\frac{\\partial ^{2}V}{\\partial q_{i}\\partial q_{j}}\\] where \\(q_{i}\\) are mass-weighted cartesian displacements of nuclei relative to their equilibrium positions. The approximation allows one to describe vibrational motion in terms of independent vibrational modes (normal modes) each of which is governed by a simple one-dimensional harmonic potential.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07048 "homodesmotic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07048 "A subclass of isodesmic reactions in which reactants and products contain equal numbers of carbon atoms in corresponding states of hybridization; moreover, there is matching of the carbon-hydrogen bonds in terms of the number of hydrogen atoms joined to the individual carbon atoms. To achieve all this matching, one should significantly extend the number and types of reference molecules. In the aliphatic series of hydrocarbons these are propane, isobutane and neopentanes as well as propene and isobutene; for aromatics—buta-1,3-diene, 2-vinylbutadiene (3-methylidenehexa-1,5-diene) and 2,3-divinylbutadiene (3,4-bismethylidenehexa-1,5-diene). Thus to assess strain energy of cyclopropane and aromatic stabilization of benzene the following homodesmotic reactions are to be respectively analysed.$\\ce{c-(CH2)3 + 3CH3–CH3 -> 3CH3CH2CH3}$\\(\\Delta H_{\\exp}^{\\circ} = \\pu{-26.5 kcal/mol}\\) (\\(\\pu{110.9 kJ/mol}\\)).$\\ce{C6H6 + 3CH2=CH2 -> 3CH2=CH–CH=CH2}$(trans)\\(\\Delta H_{\\rm{calc}}^{\\circ} \\rm{(MP2/6{-}31G^{**})} = \\pu{23.9 kcal/mol}\\) (\\(\\pu{100.0 kJ/mol}\\)) Due to closer matching of the hybridization states of the atoms of reactants and products as compared to isodesmic reactions, the homodesmotic reactions give more accurate estimates of the intrinsic strain and the cyclic delocalization. The definition may be extended to molecules with heteroatoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07049))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07049 "hybrid orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07049 "An atomic orbital derived through hybridization of atomic orbitals with different angular momentum quantum numbers located at a given atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07050 "hydrogen bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07050 "A particular type of multicenter (three center - four electron) $\\ce{X–H...Y}$ in which the central hydrogen atom covalently linked to an electronegative atom $\\ce{X}$ ($\\ce{C}$, $\\ce{N}$, $\\ce{O}$, $\\ce{S}$...) forms an additional weaker bond with atom $\\ce{Y}$ ($\\ce{N}$, $\\ce{O}$, $\\ce{S}$...) in the direction of its lone electron pair orbital. The energy of hydrogen bonds, which is usually in the range of \\(\\pu{3\\!{-}\\! 15 kcal/mol}\\) (\\(\\pu{12\\!{-}\\! 65 kJ/mol}\\)), results from the electrostatic interaction and also from the orbital interaction of the antibonding $\\upsigma^{\\ast}(\\ce{XH})MO$ of the molecule acting as the hydrogen donor and the non-bonding lone electron pair $MO{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{Y}}$ of the hydrogen acceptor molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07051 "hypercoordination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07051 "A property of main-group atoms in molecular entities to acquire coordination numbers greater than four (which would comply with the Lewis octet rule). Hypercoordination may be associated with hypervalency, but usually is referred to peculiar atomic centres in the electron-deficient species with multicentre $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$, in which the bonding power of a pair of electrons is spread over more than two atoms. An example of a hypercoordinated atom is the five-coordinate carbon atom in the methanium cation, where three $\\ce{C–H}$ bonds may be regarded as normal two center - two electron bonds and the bonding in the remaining $\\ce{CH2}$ fragment is governed by the three-centre, two-electron bond. A particular case of a hypercoordinated atom is the hydrogen atom included into a hydrogen bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07052))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07052 "hyperfine coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07052 "The interaction between the spin magnetic moment of an unpaired electron and the nuclear spin magnetic moments resulting in the splitting of the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ (spin up) and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$ (spin down) energy levels in an external magnetic field and, thus, in the multiplet pattern of the ESR spectra of radical-like species and transition metal compounds. Two main contributions to the hyperfine coupling are usually considered, Fermi contact and dipolar interactions. The contact interaction is isotropic and related to the unpaired spin density at the nucleus, \\(|\\psi _{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3}}|^{2}\\). The dipolar interaction is anisotropic, and related to \\(r^{-3}\\), where \\(r\\) is the distance between the atom holding the unpaired electron and the nucleus with non-zero spin.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07053 "hyperpolarizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07053 "The energy of a molecule in an external electrostatic field can be expanded as \\[E=E^{\\rm{o}}- \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}_{i}\\ F_{i}- \\frac{1}{2}\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3B1} _{ij}\\ F_{i}\\ F_{j}- \\frac{1}{6}\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} _{ijk}\\ F_{i}\\ F_{j}\\ F_{k}- \\frac{1}{24}\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3B3} _{ijkl}\\ F_{i}\\ F_{j}\\ F_{k}\\ F_{l}- \\rm{...}\\] where \\(E^{\\rm{o}}\\) is the unperturbed energy, \\(F_{i}\\) is the component of the field in the i direction, \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}_{i}\\) is the permanent dipole moment, \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B1}_{ij}\\) is the polarizability tensor, and \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B2}_{ijk}\\) and \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B3}_{ijkl}\\) are the first and second hyperpolarizability tensors, respectively. \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B2}\\) is a third order symmetric tensor that measures the second order response of the molecular electric dipole moment to the action of an external electric field and is thus often referred to as dipole hyperpolarizability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07054 "hypervalency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07054 "The ability of an atom in a molecular entity to expand its valence shell beyond the limits of the Lewis octet rule. Hypervalent compounds are common for the second and subsequent row elements in groups 15–18 of the periodic table. A description of the hypervalent bonding implies a transfer of the electrons from the central (hypervalent) atom to the nonbonding molecular orbitals which it forms with (usually more electronegative) ligands. A typical example of the hypervalent bond is a linear three-centre, four-electron bond, e.g. that of $\\ce{F_{ap}–P–F_{ap}}$ fragment of $\\ce{PF5}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07207))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07207 "halato-telechelic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07207 "Polymer composed of linear macromolecules having ionic or ionizable end-groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07232 "homologous polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07232 "Mixture of two or more fractions of the same polymer, each of which has a different molar-mass distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07293 "hard-segment phase domain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07293 "Phase domain of microscopic or smaller size, usually in a block, graft, or segmented copolymer, comprising essentially those segments of the polymer that are rigid and capable of forming strong intermolecular interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07390 "half-life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07390 "Time necessary for thermal bleaching to half of the absorbance of the coloured form of a photochromic compound at a specific wavelength during one cycle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07391 "half-life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07391 "Time needed for a concentration of the entity to decrease to \\(1/2\\) of its original value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07392))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07392 "heliochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07392 "Applied to photochromic compounds possessing a high efficiency for colouring with near-ultraviolet radiation and a low efficiency for bleaching with visible radiation, but a moderate efficiency for thermal fading at ambient temperature. These compounds are activated by unfiltered sunlight and deactivated under diffuse daylight conditions, therefore being suitable for sun-lens applications.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07393))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07393 "hula-twist mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07393 "Volume-conserving mechanism of photoisomerization of a double bond in a conjugated system involving simultaneous configurational and conformational isomerization, e.g., the photoinitiated concerted rotation of two adjacent double and single bonds.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:HT07393 "HT mechanism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07394 "hyperfine")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07394 "Interaction between the electron spin S and the nuclear spin I.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:HT07394 "hyperfine interaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07519 "hydrogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07519 "Gel in which the swelling agent is water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07556 "hybrid polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07556 "Polymer or polymer network comprised of inorganic and organic components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07582 "homogeneous polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07582 "Polymer blend that is a single-phase structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07622 "hipping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07622 "Isostatic pressing process carried out at elevated temperatures.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:HT07622 "hot isostatic pressing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:HT07623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:HT07623 "hydrolysis ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:HT07623 "Mole ratio of water to alkoxy groups used in sol-gel processing of metal alkoxides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I00954))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I00954 "identity period")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I00954 "The shortest distance along the chain axis for translational repetition of the chain structure. The chain identity period is usually denoted by \\(c\\). Synonymous with (chain) repeating distance.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I00954 "chain repeating distance")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I00954 "repeating distance")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I00954 "chain identity period")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02931))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02931 "<i>icosahedro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02931 "An affix used in names to denote twelve atoms bound into a triangular icosahedron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02932 "icosanoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02932 "Unsaturated $\\ce{C20}$ fatty acids and skeletally related compounds. The spelling icosanoids is preferred over the spelling eicosanoids for consistency with icosanoic acid. Specialists working in this field commonly use eicosanoids, however.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I02932 "eicosanoids")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02933 "ideal adsorbed state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02933 "The adsorbed state in a system following Langmuir's isotherm.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02934 "ideal dilute solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02934 "Dilute solution in which the solute may be regarded as obeying Henry's law, so that all the solute activity coefficients may be approximated to 1.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02935))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02935 "ideal gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02935 "Gas which obeys the equation of state \\(p\\, V=n\\, R\\, T\\) (the ideal gas law; \\(p\\) is the pressure, \\(V\\) the volume, \\(n\\) the amount of molecules, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature). For an ideal gas the fugacity, \\(f\\), of each constituent B is equal to its partial pressure, \\(p_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(f_{\\rm{B}}=p_{\\rm{B}}=x_{\\rm{B}}\\ p\\), where \\(x_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the amount fraction of B.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02938))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02938 "ideal mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02938 "A mixture of substances B, C, ... is called an ideal mixture when \\(a_{\\rm{B}}=x_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(a_{\\rm{C}}=x_{\\rm{C}}\\), ... or \\(f_{\\rm{B}}=1\\), \\(f_{\\rm{C}}=1\\) where \\(a_{\\rm{B}}\\) and \\(f_{\\rm{B}}\\) are the relative activity and the activity coefficient, respectively, of a substance B in a liquid or solid mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02940 "identity reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02940 "A chemical reaction whose products are chemically identical with the reactants, for example the bimolecular self exchange reaction of $\\ce{CH3I}$ with $\\ce{I^{-}}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I02940 "degenerate chemical reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02941 "illuminance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02941 "Luminous flux received by a surface divided by the area of that surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02942 "image converter tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02942 "An electron tube that produces on its fluorescent screen an image of the irradiation pattern of its photosensitive input surface. An image converter which produces an image with enhanced radiance is sometimes called an image intensifier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02943 "image dissection tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02943 "A two-dimensional radiation detector in which the electron image produced by a photo-emitting surface, usually a photocathode, is focused in the plane of a defining aperture. Magnetic or electric fields scan this image across the defining aperture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02944 "imaging")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02944 "The use of a photosensitive system for the capture, recording and retrieval of information associated with an object using electromagnetic energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I02944 "photoimaging")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02945 "imbalance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02945 "The situation in which reaction parameters that characterize different bond forming or bond breaking processes in the same reaction have developed to different extents as the transition state is approached along some arbitrarily defined reaction coordinate. For example, in the nitroalkane anomaly, the Brønsted \\(\\beta \\) exponent for proton removal is smaller than the Brønsted \\(\\alpha \\) for the nitroalkane, because of imbalance between the amount of bond breaking and resonance delocalization in the transition state. Imbalance is common in reactions such as elimination, addition and other complex reactions that involve proton (hydron) transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02946))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02946 "imbibition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02946 "The uptake of a liquid by a gel or porous substance. It may or may not be accompanied by swelling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02948 "imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02948 """Diacyl derivatives of ammonia or primary amines, especially those cyclic compounds derived from diacids, e.g. 
In additive nomenclature, in which imide is analogous to oxide, the term is used to name compounds of the type $\\ce{R3Y^{+}-N^{-}R}$ ($\\ce{Y = N, P}$) and $\\ce{R2Z^{+}-N^{-}R}$ ($\\ce{Z = O, S, Se, Te}$), which are the products of formal attachment of an $\\ce{RN=}$ group to $\\ce{N}$, $\\ce{P}$, $\\ce{O}$, $\\ce{S}$, $\\ce{Se}$, $\\ce{Te}$. E.g. amine imides, azomethine imides.Salts having the anion $\\ce{RN^{2−}}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02949))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02949 "imidic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02949 """Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce{R_{k}E(=O)_{l}(OH)_{m}}$ ($l 
eq 0$) by replacing $\\ce{=O}$ by $\\ce{=NR}$; thus tautomers of amides. In organic chemistry an unspecified imidic acid is generally a carboximidic acid, $\\ce{RC(=NR)(OH)}$. E.g. $\\ce{RS(=NH)2(OH)}$ a sulfonodiimidic acid.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02950 "imidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02950 "Analogues of cyclic acid anhydrides in which $\\ce{=O}$ has been replaced by $\\ce{=NR}$ and $\\ce{–O\\bond{–}}$ by $\\ce{–NR\\bond{–}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02953))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02953 "imidoyl carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02953 "Carbenes having the structure $\\ce{RC(=NR)C{:}–R}$. Imidoyl is a shortened but imprecise term for carboximidoyl, $\\ce{RC(=NH)\\bond{–}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02954))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02954 "imidoyl nitrenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02954 "Nitrenes having the structure .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02957))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02957 "imines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02957 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RN=CR2}$ ($\\ce{R} = \\ce{H}$, hydrocarbyl). Thus analogues of aldehydes or ketones, having NR doubly bonded to carbon; aldimines have the structure $\\ce{RCH=NR}$, ketimines have the structure $\\ce{R'2C=NR}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$). Imines include azomethines and Schiff bases. Imine is used as a suffix in systematic nomenclature to denote the $\\ce{C=NH}$ group excluding the carbon atom.An obsolete term for azacycloalkanes.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02958))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02958 "iminium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02958 "Salts in which the cation has the structure $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}R2}$. Thus N-hydronated imines and their N-substituted derivatives. The synonymous terms imonium compounds and immonium compounds are irregularly formed and should not be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02959 "imino acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02959 "An obsolete term, which should not be used, for imidic acids.Any carboxylic acids having an imino substituent, $\\ce{HN=}$, replacing two hydrogens. A shortened form of imino carboxylic acid.Obsolescent term for azaalkanoic acids and azacycloalkane-2-carboxylic acids, e.g. proline:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02960 "imino carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02960 "This term systematically means carbenes bearing an imino or N-substituted imino group, $\\ce{RN=}$, somewhere in the molecule. E.g. $\\ce{R–C:–CH2C(=NR)R}$. It is listed here in order to warn against its misuse for alkylideneamino carbenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02962))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02962 "iminoxyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02962 "Synonymous with alkylideneaminoxyl radicals. The term can be regarded as a contraction of iminyloxyl radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02964 "iminylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02964 "Cations having the structure $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}}$. A contraction of alkaniminylium ions. Alkylideneaminylium ions is a synonymous term. A subclass of nitrenium ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02965 "iminyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02965 "Radicals having the structure $\\ce{R2C=N^{.}}$. A contraction of alkaniminyl radicals, e.g. $\\ce{CH3–CH=N^{.}}$, ethaniminyl. A synonymous term is alkylideneaminyl radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02966 "immersional wetting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02966 "A process in which a solid or liquid, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, is covered with a liquid, $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, both of which were initially in contact with a gas or liquid, $\\ce{\\updelta\\!}$, without changing the area of the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}}$interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02967 "immission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02967 "A Germanic term, pronounced in English, 'eye-mission'; the transfer of pollutants from the atmosphere to a 'receptor'; for example, pollutants retained by the lungs. It does not have the same meaning as ground level concentration, but is the opposite in meaning to emission. This term has not been used commonly in the English language.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02968 "immission dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02968 "The integral of the immission flow into the receptor over the exposure period.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02969))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02969 "immission flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02969 "The immission rate divided by the unit surface area of the receptor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02970 "immission rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02970 "The mass (or other physical quantity) of pollutant transferring per unit time into a receptor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02971))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02971 "immobile adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02971 "A situation in which the freedom of molecules of adsorbate to move about the surface is limited. Adsorption is immobile when \\(k\\ T\\) is small compared to \\(\\Delta E\\), the energy barrier separating adjacent sites. The adsorbate has little chance of migrating to neighbouring sites and such adsorption is necessarily localized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02972 "immobilized phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02972 "A stationary phase in which is immobilized on the support particles, or on the inner wall of the column tubing e.g. by in situ polymerization (cross-linking) after coating.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02973 "immobilization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02973 "The technique used for the physical or chemical fixation of cells, organelles, enzymes, or other proteins (e.g. monoclonal antibodies) onto a solid support, into a solid matrix or retained by a membrane, in order to increase their stability and make possible their repeated or continued use. The principle is also used for affinity chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02976 "immobilized enzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02976 "A soluble enzyme bound to an insoluble organic or inorganic matrix, or encapsulated within a membrane in order to increase its stability and make possible its repeated or continued use.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02977 "immune response")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02977 "Selective reaction of the body to substances that are foreign to it, or that the immune system identifies as foreign, shown by the production of antibodies and antibody-bearing cells or by a cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02978 "immunization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02978 "The administration of an antigen to an animal in order to stimulate the production of antibodies by that organism. Also, the administration of antigens, antibodies or lymphocytes to an animal to generate the corresponding active, passive or adaptive immunity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02979 "immunoassay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02979 "A ligand-binding assay that uses a specific antigen or antibody, capable of binding to the analyte, to identify and quantify substances. The antibody can be linked to a radiosotope (radioimmunoassay, RIA), or to an enzyme which catalyses an easily monitored reaction (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, ELISA), or to a highly fluorescent compound by which the location of an antigen can be visualized (immunofluorescene).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02980 "immunochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02980 "Study of biochemical and molecular aspects of immunology, especially the nature of antibodies, antigens and their interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02981 "immunogen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02981 "A substance that elicits a cellular immune response and/or antibody production (cf. antigen).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02982 "immunoglobulin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02982 "A protein of the globulin-type found in serum or other body fluids that possesses antibody activity. An individual Ig molecule is built up from two light (L) and two heavy (H) polypeptide chains linked together by disulfide bonds. Igs are divided into five classes based on antigenic and structural differences in the H chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02983))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02983 "immunoradiometric assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02983 "An assay based on the reversible and non-covalent binding of an antigen by a specific antibody labelled with a radioactive nuclide as a tracer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02984 "immunosuppression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02984 "Reduction in the functional capacity of the immune response; may be due to:  Inhibition of the normal response of the immune system to an antigen.   Prevention, by chemical or biological means, of the production of an antibody to an antigen by inhibition of the processes of transcription, translation or formation of tertiary structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02985 "impaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02985 "A forcible contact of particles of matter with a surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02987 "impact parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02987 "In simple hard sphere collision theory the distance of closest approach that would result for two particles in a collision if the particle trajectories were undeflected by the collision.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02988 "impedance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02988 "Complex representation of potential difference divided by the complex representation of the current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02989))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02989 "impingement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02989 "Equivalent to impaction; often refers to impaction on a liquid surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02990 "impinger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02990 "A sampling instrument employing impingement for the collection of particulate matter. Common types are: the midget impinger employing impingement in \\(1{-}10\\ \\rm{cm}^{3}\\) water,  the standard impinger employing impingement in \\(75\\ \\rm{cm}^{3}\\) water and  dry impingers.  Impingers are also suitable for sampling certain gases and vapours.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02991 "imprecision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02991 "Variation of the result in a set of replicate measurements. This can be expressed, e.g. as the standard deviation or coefficient of variation (relative standard deviation). This term may have a more general meaning, e.g. if the replicates constitute a batch or involve different instruments, laboratories and analyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02992 "impregnation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02992 "The modification of the separation properties of the chromatographic bed used in planar chromatography by appropriate additives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02993 "improved canonical variational transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02993 "A modification of canonical variational transition-state theory in which, for energies below the threshold energy, the position of the dividing surface is taken to be that of the microcanonical threshold energy. This forces the contributions to rate constant to be zero below the threshold energy. A compromise dividing surface is then chosen so as to minimize the contributions to the rate constant made by reactants having higher energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02995))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02995 "inaccuracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02995 "A quantitative term to describe the (lack of) accuracy of a chemical measurement process; comprises the imprecision and the bias. Inaccuracy must be viewed as a two-component quantity (vector); imprecision and bias should never be combined to give a scalar measure for chemical measurement process inaccuracy. (One or the other component may, however, be negligible under certain circumstances.) Inaccuracy should not be confused with uncertainty. Inaccuracy (imprecision, bias) is characteristic of the measurement process, whereas error and uncertainty are characteristics of a result. (The latter characteristic, of course, derives from the imprecision and bounds for bias of the chemical measurement process.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02996 "inch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02996 "Non-SI unit of length, \\(\\pu{in} = \\pu{0.0254 m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02997 "incinerator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02997 "Equipment in which solid, liquid or gaseous combustible wastes are ignited and burned. Types include flue-fed and multiple-chamber incinerators with several stages of combustion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02998))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02998 "inclusion compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02998 "A complex in which one component (the host) forms a cavity or, in the case of a crystal, a crystal lattice containing spaces in the shape of long tunnels or channels in which molecular entities of a second chemical species (the guest) are located. There is no covalent bonding between guest and host, the attraction being generally due to van der Waals forces. If the spaces in the host lattice are enclosed on all sides so that the guest species is 'trapped' as in a cage, such compounds are known as clathrates or 'cage compounds'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I02998 "inclusion complex")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I02999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I02999 "incoherent radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I02999 "Not having the properties of coherent radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03003 "increment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03003 "An individual portion of material collected by a single operation of a sampling device. Increments may be reduced individually or tested either (a) individually or (b) combined with other increments with the resulting composite reduced in size and tested as a single unit. Increments are created by the sampling operation and are usually taken from parts of a lot separated in time or space. Increments of a bulk population correspond to units of a packaged population.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03004 "indicated hydrogen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03004 "Under certain circumstances it is necessary to indicate in the name of a ring, or ring system, containing the maximum number of non-cumulative double bonds, one or more positions where no multiple bond is attached. This is done by specifying the presence of an 'extra' hydrogen atom at such positions by citation of the appropriate numbered locant followed by an italicized capital H.Example:  A second type of indicated hydrogen (sometimes referred to as 'added hydrogen') describes hydrogen atoms added to a specific structure as a consequence of the addition of a suffix or a prefix describing a structural modification. This type of indicated hydrogen is normally cited in parentheses after the locant of the additional feature.Example:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03004 "added hydrogen")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03006 "indicator electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03006 "An electrode that serves as a transducer responding to the excitation signal (if any) and to the composition of the solution being investigated, but that does not affect an appreciable change of bulk composition within the ordinary duration of a measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03008 "indifferent absorbing ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03008 "An ion which absorbs through coulomb forces only and which is hence only repelled by surfaces of like sign, attracted by surfaces of opposite sign, and is not absorbed on an uncharged surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03009 "indifferent electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03009 "Synonymous with supporting electrolyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03010 "indirect amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03010 "A type of amplification reaction where the constituent required is associated with some other constituent which is then amplified and measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03011 "indirect reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03011 "A chemical process in which the reactive complex has a lifetime that is longer than its period of rotation is called an indirect reaction (in contrast to a direct reaction). In a molecular-beam experiment the products of such reactions are scattered at random with reference to the centre of mass of the system. Indirect reactions are also called complex-mode reactions. (They are sometimes called complex reactions, but this usage is not recommended in view of the danger of confusion with composite reactions, which occur in more than one step).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03014 "individual perception threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03014 "A term used in odour testing which signifies the lowest concentration of a particular species at which a subject indicates both an initial positive and repeated response.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03015 "induced radioactivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03015 "Radioactivity induced by irradiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03016 "induced reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03016 "A reaction which occurs or is accelerated only if another reaction is simultaneously occurring in the same system. The substance, which causes an induced reaction, is termed inductor. The inductor, unlike the catalyst, is used up irreversibly during the process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03017 "inducer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03017 "A small molecule that triggers gene transcription by binding to a regulator protein (cf. enzyme induction). It acts by combining with the corresponding repressor protein to bring about an allosteric change so that the repressor is made incapable of combining with the operator of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03018))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03018 "induction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03018 "An increase in the rate of synthesis of an (inducible) enzyme in response to the action of an inducer or environmental conditions. An inducer is often the substrate of the inducible enzyme or a structurally similar substance (gratuitous inducer) that is not metabolized.The experimental elicitation of lytic growth by a prophage from lysogenic bacteria.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03019 "induction period")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03019 "The initial slow phase of a chemical reaction which later accelerates. Induction periods are often observed with radical reactions, but they may also occur in other systems (for example before steady-state concentration of the reactants is reached).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03021 "inductive effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03021 "In strict definition, an experimentally observable effect (on rates of reaction, etc.) of the transmission of charge through a chain of atoms by electrostatic induction. A theoretical distinction may be made between the field effect, and the inductive effect as models for the coulomb interaction between a given site within a molecular entity and a remote unipole or dipole within the same entity. The experimental distinction between the two effects has proved difficult, except for molecules of peculiar geometry, which may exhibit 'reversed field effects'. Ordinarily the inductive effect and the field effect are influenced in the same direction by structural changes in the molecule and the distinction between them is not clear. This situation has led many authors to include the field effect in the term 'inductive effect'. Thus the separation of values into inductive and resonance components does not imply the exclusive operation of a through-bonds route for the transmission of the non-conjugative part of the substituent effect. To indicate the all-inclusive use of the term inductive, the phrase 'so-called inductive effect' is sometimes used. Certain modern theoretical approaches suggest that the 'so-called inductive effect' reflects a field effect rather than through-bonds transmission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03022 "inductomeric effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03022 "A molecular polarizability effect occurring by the inductive mechanism of electron displacement. The consideration of such an effect and the descriptive term have been regarded as obsolescent or even obsolete, but in recent years theoretical approaches have reintroduced substituent polarizability as a factor governing reactivity, etc. and its parametrization has been proposed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03025 "inelastic scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03025 "If in a molecular collision there is transfer of energy among degrees of freedom, but no chemical reaction occurs, there is said to be inelastic scattering.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03026 "inert")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03026 "Stable and unreactive under specified conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03027 "inert gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03027 "A non-reactive gas under particular conditions. For example, nitrogen at ordinary temperatures and the noble gases (helium, argon, krypton, xenon and radon) are unreactive toward most species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03028 "inertial defect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03028 "Difference of principal moments of inertia \\(\\mathit{\\Delta} = I_{C} - I_{A} - I_{B}\\). For planar molecules it is close to zero and for non-planar molecules it is negative.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03029 "inertial separator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03029 "Any dry type collector which utilizes the relatively greater inertia of particles to effect their removal from a gas stream; e.g. cyclonic and impingement separators, gravity settling chambers and high-velocity gas reversal chambers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03030 "infinite source thickness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03030 "For a specified radiation, the minimum thickness of a flat preparation of a radioactive material where the intensity of the specified radiation at the surface does not increase when the thickness is increased by adding more of similar radioactive material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03032 "information theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03032 "As applied in reaction dynamics, information theory analyses the deviations of experimentally-determined energy distributions from predictions made on the basis of equal a priori probabilities for all possible outcomes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03033 "inherent viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03033 "The ratio of the natural logarithm of the relative viscosity, \\(\\eta _{\\rm{r}}\\), to the mass concentration of the polymer, \\(c\\), i.e. \\[\\eta _{\\rm{inh}}\\equiv \\eta _{\\ln} = \\frac{\\ln \\eta _{\\rm{r}}}{c}\\] The quantity \\(\\eta _{\\ln }\\), with which the inherent viscosity is synonymous, is the logarithmic viscosity number.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03033 "logarithmic viscosity number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03034 "inhibition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03034 "The decrease in rate of reaction brought about by the addition of a substance inhibitor), by virtue of its effect on the concentration of a reactant, catalyst or reaction intermediate. For example, molecular oxygen and p-benzoquinone can react as 'inhibitors' in many reactions involving radicals as intermediates by virtue of their ability to act as scavengers toward these radicals. If the rate of a reaction in the absence of inhibitor is \\(v_{0}\\) and that in the presence of a certain amount of inhibitor is \\(v\\), the degree of inhibition (\\(i\\)) is given by: \\[i=\\frac{v_{0}- v}{v_{0}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03035 "inhibitor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03035 "A substance that diminishes the rate of a chemical reaction; the process is called inhibition. Inhibitors are sometimes called negative catalysts, but since the action of an inhibitor is fundamentally different from that of a catalyst, this terminology is discouraged. In contrast to a catalyst, an inhibitor may be consumed during the course of a reaction. In enzyme-catalysed reactions an inhibitor frequently acts by binding to the enzyme, in which case it may be called an enzyme inhibitor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03036 "inhibitory concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03036 "Concentration of a substance that causes a defined inhibition of a given system: \\(\\rm{IC}_{50}\\) is the median concentration that causes 50% inhibition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03037 "inhibitory dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03037 "Dose of a substance that causes a defined inhibition of a given system: \\(\\rm{ID}_{50}\\) is the median dose that causes 50% inhibition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03038))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03038 "inhomogeneity error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03038 "An error due to inhomogenous distribution of absorbing species in the analytical sample. It may be observed by probing the sample volume with an incident beam of radiation of reduced cross-section.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03040 "initial rate method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03040 "A mode of measurement in a kinetic method of analysis, in which the initial reaction rate is determined (usually by the slope method) and utilized for the measurement of concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03041 "initial state correlations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03041 "These are correlations between the states of the products of a reaction and the initial states of the reactants. The correlations may relate to energy levels, quantum numbers, and symmetries.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03042 "initiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03042 "A reaction or process generating free radicals (or some other reactive reaction intermediates) which then induce a chain reaction. For example, in the chlorination of alkanes by a radical mechanism the initiation step is the dissociation of molecular chlorine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03043))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03043 "initiator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03043 "A substance introduced into a reaction system in order to bring about an initiation reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03044))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03044 "injection temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03044 "The temperature at the point of injection.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03045 "in-laboratory processing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03045 "The selection, removal and preparation of the test (or analytical) portions from the laboratory sample. The processing may include a reduction in the size of the unit(s) (division) and particle size (reduction), as well as mixing to achieve homogeneity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03046 "inner electric potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03046 "Quantity the gradient of which is equal to the negative of the electric field strength within the phase concerned. It can be calculated from the model of the phase. It is sometimes called the Galvani potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03047 "inner filter effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03047 "In an emission experiment, an apparent decrease in emission quantum yield and/or distortion of bandshape as a result of reabsorption of emitted radiation.During a light irradiation experiment, absorption of incident radiation by a species other than the intended primary absorber.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03048 "inner Helmholtz plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03048 "The locus of the electrical centres of specifically adsorbed ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03049))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03049 "inner layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03049 "The region between the outer Helmholtz plane and the interface.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03049 "compact layer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03050 "inner orbital X-ray emission spectra")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03050 "X-ray spectral lines generated by the transition of electrons between 'inner orbitals'. The term 'inner orbital' serves to designate the sharp, not degenerate electronic levels of atoms in a solid and therefore is a general term for all orbitals except the valence band.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03052))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03052 "inner-sphere electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03052 "Historically an electron transfer between two metal centres sharing a ligand or atom in their respective coordination shells. The definition has more recently been extended to any situation in which the interaction between the donor and acceptor centres in the transition state is significant (\\(\\pu{\\gt 20 kJ mol-1}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03053 "inoculation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03053 "The introduction of a small sample of organisms (microbial, plant or animal cells) into a culture medium to act as a seed for the production of large numbers of the same organism by growth and propagation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03054 "inositols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03054 "Cyclohexane-1,2,3,4,5,6-hexols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03055))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03055 "in-out isomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03055 "Isomerism found in bicyclic systems having long enough bridges to allow the bridgehead exocyclic bond or lone pair of electrons to point either inside the structure or outside.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03056 "input rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03056 "The number of samples that are processed by the instrument divided by time of operation. It should be stated if the residence time is included or not.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03057 "insert")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03057 "A sequence of foreign DNA introduced into a restriction site (insertion site, cloning site) of a vector DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03058 "insertion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03058 "A chemical reaction or transformation of the general type: $$\\ce{X-Z + Y -> X-Y-Z}$$ in which the connecting atom or group $\\ce{Y}$ replaces the bond joining the parts $\\ce{X}$ and $\\ce{Z}$ of the reactant $\\ce{XZ}$. An example is the carbene insertion reaction:  The reverse of an insertion is called an extrusion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03059 "<i>in situ</i> microanalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03059 "Term used for direct analytical investigation of the microstructural domains of a solid by focused beams of particles and radiation. Analytical characterization includes obtaining information about type, quantity and distribution of the elements, their state of chemical bonding, morphology and crystalline (geometric) and electronic structure of the individual phases. The combination of this information serves as a basis for the property-relevant characterization of solids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03060 "<i>in situ</i> micro-X-ray diffraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03060 "Any technique which utilizes the diffraction of X-rays generated in a microstructural domain of a solid under bombardment with a finely focused electron beam, thus providing an X-ray diffraction pattern of this microstructural domain. The pattern can be recorded with a film either on the reflection or transmission side of the specimen (in the latter case the crystalline sample has to be a thin film or a small particle).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03060 "Kossel-technique")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03061 "instability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03061 "A change which takes place in an instrument reading over a stated period of unattended operation for a given value of the air quality characteristic. It can be characterized by the variation with time of its mean, specifying the drift and by the dispersion. Span instability is the change which takes place in instrument span over a stated period of unattended operation. Zero instability is the change in instrument reading in response to a zero sample over a stated period of unattended operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03062 "instantaneous current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03062 "At a dropping electrode, the total current that flows at the instant when a time \\(t\\) has elapsed since the fall of the preceding drop. At any other electrode, the total current that flows at the instant when a time \\(t\\) has elapsed since the beginning of an electrolysis. The instantaneous current is usually time-dependent and may have the character of an adsorption, catalytic, diffusion, double-layer, or kinetic current, and may include a migration current. A plot of the dependence of instantaneous current on time is commonly called an 'i-t curve'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03063 "instantaneous rate of flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03063 "The rate of increase of the mass of a drop at a particular instant \\(t\\) seconds after it has begun to form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03064 "instantaneous sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03064 "Obtaining a sample of the atmosphere in a period which is short compared with the duration of the sampling exercise. Such samples are often called 'grab' samples, a term not recommended. These are useful for the analysis of hydrocarbons and other complicated mixtures of trace gases which are relatively stable in a stainless steel canister or tank and can be transported back to the laboratory for chromatographic or other analysis which cannot be done satisfactorily in the field.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03064 "spot sampling")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03065 "instrumental activation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03065 "A kind of activation analysis in which element specificity is obtained by using appropriate irradiation conditions, radiation measurement techniques and mathematical techniques for the interpretation of the measurement results.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03066 "instrumental dependability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03066 "This relates to the frequency of failures which interrupt the operation of an instrument. It can be quantified, e.g. by the infrequency of breakdowns or by the availability of the instrument for use when required.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03067 "instrumental indication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03067 "The observed deflection or rest point multiplied by the value of the division for the load in question.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03068))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03068 "integral capacitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03068 "Integral capacitance (per unit area of electrode) is given by \\[K=\\frac{Q}{E- E_{Q=0}}\\] where \\(Q\\) is the electric charge (per unit area of electrode), \\(E\\) is the potential of the electrode with respect to a reference electrode, and \\(E_{Q=0}\\) is the potential at the point of zero charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03069 "integral detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03069 "A device which measures the accumulated quantity of sample component(s) reaching the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03070))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03070 "integrating sphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03070 "A hollow sphere having a highly reflecting inside surface used as a device to collect, with very high efficiency, light scattered or emitted from a sample contained in it or located outside and near one of the ports. Small ports allow the entrance of light and access to a detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03071 "intended crossing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03071 "Synonymous with avoided crossing. The term 'intended' should not be used in this context since it is an anthropomorphic term.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03072 "intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03072 "The traditional term for photon flux, fluence rate, irradiance or radiant power (radiant flux). In terms of an object exposed to radiation, the term should now be used only for qualitative descriptions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03073 "intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03073 "The ratio of intensity of a separated ion beam to the intensity of the separated ion beam which has the greatest intensity. This ratio is generally equated to the normalized ratio of the heights of the respective peaks in the mass spectrum, with the height of the base peak being taken as 100.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03073 "intensity relative to base peak")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03074 "intensive quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03074 "Physical quantity whose magnitude is independent of the extent of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03075 "interaction distance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03075 "The farthest distance of approach of two particles at which it is discernible that they will not pass at the impact parameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03076 "intercalation compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03076 "Compounds resulting from reversible inclusion, without covalent bonding, of one kind of molecule in a solid matrix of another compound, which has a laminar structure. The host compound, a solid, may be macromolecular, crystalline or amorphous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03077 "intercalation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03077 "Reaction, generally reversible, that involves the penetration of a host material by guest species without causing a major structural modification of the host.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03077 "insertion reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03080 "interconal region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03080 "The region of a Bunsen flame where the combustion zone has the form of a cone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03081 "interconvertible enzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03081 "An enzyme existing in at least two well defined, reversibly convertible forms, produced by covalent modifications of amino acid side chains under biological conditions. Covalent modifications that occur as intermediates in the catalytic process are not included in this definition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03082 "interface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03082 "The plane ideally marking the boundary between two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03083 "interfacial concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03083 "The interfacial concentration \\(c_{\\rm{B},\\rm{e}}\\) (or simply \\(c_{\\rm{e}}\\)) of a species B is the concentration of that species at the boundary of the electrical double-layer facing the solution, i.e. just outside the region where the departures from the electroneutrality of the solution are significant. This concept is mainly used in the usual case where the thickness of the electric double layer is very small as compared to that of the diffusion layer. It is often calculated from theory or derived from measurements of the limiting current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03084 "interfacial double-layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03084 "The Coulombic interaction of interfacial charges (e.g. ions) and the magnetic or electrostatic interaction of interfacial molecules lead to particularly complex interfacial structures. Complex interfacial profiles that can be approximated by two distinct sub-layers with different physical properties (e.g. structure and/or nature and/or composition), are referred to as interfacial double-layers. Examples of such approximated complex profiles are: the electrical double-layer consisting of a surface charge layer (i.e. a two dimensional distribution of one type of ions) and a diffuse charge layer (counter-ions distributed over the space region next to the surface); the approximated profile of the orientation angle of anisotropic liquid molecules within a 'double-layer' consisting of a distribution of so-called anchored molecules which are perturbed (strongly bound and orientated) by the surface, and the adjacent, so-called, transition layer, i.e. the region where the surface perturbation is damped.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03085 "interfacial layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03085 "The inhomogeneous space region intermediate between two bulk phases in contact, and where properties are significantly different from, but related to, the properties of the bulk phases. Examples of such properties are: compositions, molecular density, orientation or conformation, charge density, pressure tensor, electron density, etc. The interfacial properties vary in the direction normal to the surface. Complex profiles of interfacial properties occur in the case of multicomponent systems with coexisting bulk phases where attractive/repulsive molecular interactions involve adsorption or depletion of one or several components. This interfacial region may also be regarded as a distinct, though not autonomous, phase and be called the interphase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03085 "interfacial region")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03085 "interphase")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03086 "interfacial layer width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03086 "The characteristic length defined by the variance of the Gaussian function fitted to the gradient of the electron density profile established by X-ray specular reflectivity studies of the liquid interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03089 "interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03089 "A systematic error in the measure of a signal caused by the presence of concomitants in a sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03092 "interfering lines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03092 "Radiation from nearby spectral lines may perturb the measurement of the intensity of the line wanted. These lines are termed interfering lines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03093 "interfering substance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03093 "Any substance, other than the ion being measured, whose presence in the sample solution affects the measured emf of a cell. Interfering substances fall into two classes: 'electrode/electrochemical' interferences and 'chemical' interferences. Examples of the first class include:Those substances which give a similar response to the ion being measured and whose presence generally results in an apparent increase in the activity (or concentration) of the ion to be determined (e.g. $\\ce{Na^{+}}$ for the $\\ce{Ca^{2+}}$ electrode).Electrolytes present at a high concentration that give rise to appreciable liquid junction potential differences or results in a significant activity coefficient decrease, or incipient Donnan exclusion failure. The second class of substances that should be recognized as chemical interferences includes: Species that interact with the ion being measured so as to decrease its activity or apparent concentration. The electrode continues to report the true activity (e.g. $\\ce{CN^{−}}$ present in the measurement of $\\ce{Ag^{+}}$), but a considerable gap will occur between the activity and concentration of the ions even in very dilute solutions. Under these circumstances the determination of ionic concentration may be problematic. Substances interacting with the membrane itself, blocking the surface or changing its chemical composition [i.e. organic solvents for the liquid or poly(vinyl chloride) (PVC) membrane electrodes] are grouped as interferences or electrode poisons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03095 "interferons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03095 "A class of glycoproteins (with sugar groups attached at specific locations) important in immune function. They are able to inhibit the multiplication of viruses in cells.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03096 "intermediate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03096 "A molecular entity with a lifetime appreciably longer than a molecular vibration (corresponding to a local potential energy minimum of depth greater than \\(R\\ T\\)) that is formed (directly or indirectly) from the reactants and reacts further to give (either directly or indirectly) the products of a chemical reaction; also the corresponding chemical species.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03096 "reaction intermediate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03097 "intermediate neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03097 "Neutrons of kinetic energy between the energies of slow and fast neutrons. In reactor physics, the range might be \\(1\\ \\rm{eV}\\) to \\(0.1\\ \\rm{MeV}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03098 "intermolecular")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03098 "Descriptive of any process that involves a transfer (of atoms, groups, electrons, etc.) or interactions between two or more molecular entities.Relating to a comparison between different molecular entities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03100 "internal absorptance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03100 "Absorptance when surface effects and effects of the cuvette such as reflection losses are excluded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03102 "internal conversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03102 "A photophysical process. An isoenenergetic radiationless transition between two electronic states of the same multiplicity. When the transition results in a vibrationally excited molecular entity in the lower electronic state, this usually undergoes deactivation to its lowest vibrational level, provided the final state is not unstable to dissociation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03103 "internal energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03103 "Quantity the change in which is equal to the sum of heat, \\(q\\), brought to the system and work, \\(w\\), performed on it, \\(\\Delta U=q+w\\). Also called thermodynamic energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03103 "thermodynamic energy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03105))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03105 "internal filling solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03105 "Aqueous electrolyte solution, which may be gelled, containing fixed concentration of hydrogen ions, e.g. $\\ce{HCl}$ or a buffer solution, and a fixed concentration of the ion to which the internal reference electrode is reversible, e.g. chloride ion in the case of silver-silver chloride, or calomel electrodes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03106 "internal reference electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03106 "An 'inner' reference electrode which is contained inside an ion-selective electrode assembly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03108))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03108 "internal standard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03108 "A compound added to a sample in known concentration to facilitate the qualitative indentification and/or quantitative determination of the sample components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03109 "internal surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03109 "With respect to porous solids, the surface associated with pores communicating with the outside space. Since the accessibility of pores may depend on the size of the fluid molecules, the extent of the accessible internal surface may depend on the size of the molecules comprising the fluid, and may be different for the various components of a fluid mixture (molecular sieve effect).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03111))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03111 "internal valence force field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03111 "A force field expressed in terms of bond-stretching, angle-bending, torsional and other displacements directly connected to the structural parameters of the molecule: \\[V=\\frac{1}{2}\\ \\sum k_{ij}\\ R_{i}\\ R_{j}\\] or \\[V=\\frac{1}{2}\\ \\sum K_{ij}\\ R_{i}\\ R_{j}\\] where \\(k\\) (or \\(K\\)) are the force constants and \\(R\\) the internal valence coordinates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03114))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03114 "international unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03114 "The unit of enzyme activity proposed by the International Union of Biochemistry in 1964. Specifically, it is the amount of enzyme that catalyses the conversion of one micromole of substrate per minute under the specified conditions of the assay method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03115 "interparticle porosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03115 "The interparticle volume of a packed column per unit column volume: \\[\\varepsilon = \\frac{V_{0}}{V_{\\rm{c}}}\\] It is also called the interstitial fraction of the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03116 "interparticle volume of the column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03116 "The volume occupied by the mobile phase between the particles in the packed section of a column. It is also called the interstitial volume or the void volume of the column. In liquid chromatography, the interparticle volume is equal to the mobile-phase hold-up volume (\\(V_{\\rm{M}}\\)) in the ideal case, neglecting any extra-column volume. In gas chromatography, the symbol \\(V_{\\rm{G}}\\) may be used for the interparticle volume of the column. In the ideal case, neglecting any extra-column volume, \\(V_{\\rm{G}}\\) is equal to the corrected gas hold-up volume (\\(V_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}\\)). \\[V_{\\rm{G}}=V_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}=V_{\\rm{M}j}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03117 "interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03117 "Polymer comprising two or more networks that are at least partially interlaced on a molecular scale but not covalently bonded to each other and cannot be separated unless chemical bonds are broken.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03119 "interphase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03119 "A transition that occurs at boundaries between phases. Example: The precipitation of second phases or the initiation of new phase growth at an interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03120))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03120 "interstitial fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03120 "The interstitial volume per unit volume of a packed column. Defined by: \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{I}}=\\frac{V_{\\rm{I}}}{X}\\) where \\(V_{\\rm{I}}\\) is the interstitial volume and \\(X\\) the column volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03121 "interstitial velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03121 "The linear velocity of the mobile phase inside a packed column calculated as the average over the entire cross section. This quantity can, under idealized conditions, be calculated from the equation: \\[u=\\frac{F}{\\varepsilon_{\\rm{I}}}\\] where \\(F\\) is the nominal linear flow and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{I}}\\) the interstitial fraction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03122))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03122 "interstitial volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03122 "The volume \\(V_{\\rm{G}}\\) of the column not occupied by the liquid phase and its solid support, or by the active solid. It does not include any volume external to the column, such as the volume of the sample injector or of the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03123 "intersystem crossing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03123 "A photophysical process. An isoenergetic radiationless transition between two electronic states having different multiplicities. It often results in a vibrationally excited molecular entity in the lower electronic state, which then usually deactivates to its lowest vibrational level.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03123 "state crossing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03124 "interval analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03124 "A method to estimate uncertainties in the results of a least-squares procedure. The input data are primary values plus limits of intervals expressing their uncertainty. The output consists of derived values plus limits of intervals expressing a pessimistic estimate of maximal uncertainty.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03125))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03125 "intervalence charge transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03125 "Electron transfer (thermal or photoinduced) between two metal sites differing only in oxidation state. Quite often such electron transfer reverses the oxidation states of the sites. The term is frequently extended to the case of metal-to-metal charge transfer between non-equivalent metal centres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03126))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03126 "interzonal region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03126 "The region of the Bunsen flame confined by the inner and outer zones, where in many instances the conditions for flame analysis are optimum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03128))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03128 "intra-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03128 "The prefix for an elementary reaction or some part thereof indicating that the bond(s) undergoing primitive changes form part of a ring in the transition state of the elementary reaction. This prefix is used for nonpericyclic processes only and is replaced by 'cyclo' for pericyclic processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03130))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03130 "intramolecular")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03130 "Descriptive of any process that involves a transfer (of atoms, groups, electrons, etc.) or interactions between different parts of the same molecular entity.Relating to a comparison between atoms or groups within the same molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03131))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03131 "intramolecular catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03131 "The acceleration of a chemical transformation at one site of a molecular entity through the involvement of another functional ('catalytic') group in the same molecular entity, without that group appearing to have undergone change in the reaction product. The use of the term should be restricted to cases for which analogous intermolecular catalysis by chemical species bearing that catalytic group is observable. intramolecular catalysis can be detected and expressed in quantitative form by a comparison of the reaction rate with that of a comparable model compound in which the catalytic group is absent, or by measurement of the effective molarity of the catalytic group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03132 "intramolecular isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03132 "A kinetic isotopic effect observed when a single substrate, in which the isotopic atoms occupy equivalent reactive positions, reacts to produce a non-statistical distribution of isotopomeric products. In such a case the isotope effect will favour the pathway with lower force constants for displacement of the isotopic nuclei in the transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03133))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03133 "intraphase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03133 "A transition that occurs within a phase. Example: initiation of phase growth at an intragranular feature, such as twin planes and dislocations in steels.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03134 "intrinsic activation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03134 "If \\(E_{\\rm{a,1}}\\) and \\(E_{\\rm{a,- 1}}\\) are the activation energies for a reaction in forward and reverse directions, the lesser of the two is sometimes called the intrinsic activation energy. It is the activation energy for the reaction in the exothermic direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03135 "intrinsic barrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03135 "The Gibbs energy of activation (\\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G\\)) in the limiting case where \\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} = 0\\), i.e. when the effect of thermodynamic driving force is eliminated. According to the Marcus equation, the intrinsic barrier is related to the reorganization energy, \\(\\lambda \\), of the reaction by the equation: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G = \\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03136 "intrinsic detector efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03136 "The ratio of the number of particles or photons detected to the number of similar particles or photons which have struck the envelope limiting the sensitive volume of a radiation detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03138))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03138 "intrinsic full energy peak efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03138 "The detector efficiency when considering only events where the total energy of the radiation is absorbed in the sensitive volume of the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03139 "intrinsic photopeak efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03139 "The detector efficiency when only counts resulting from the photoelectrical effect are considered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03140 "intrinsic viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03140 "The limiting value of the reduced viscosity, \\(\\frac{\\eta _{\\rm{i}}}{c}\\) or the inherent viscosity, \\(\\eta _{\\rm{inh}}\\), at infinite dilution of the polymer, i.e. \\[[\\eta] = \\lim _{c\\rightarrow 0}\\frac{\\eta _{\\rm{i}}}{c}=\\lim _{c\\rightarrow 0}\\eta _{\\rm{inh}}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03140 "limiting viscosity number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03141 "intron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03141 "An intervening section of DNA occuring almost exclusively within a eukaryotic gene but which is not translated to amino acid sequences in the gene product. The introns are removed from the premature mRNA through a process called splicing to form an active mRNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03142 "inverse isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03142 "A kinetic isotope effect in which \\(\\frac{k^{\\rm{l}}}{k^{\\rm{h}}}1\\). The isotope effect will normally be 'normal' when the frequency differences between the isotopic transition state are smaller than in the reactants. Conversely, an inverse isotope effect can be taken as evidence for an increase in the corresponding force constants on passing from the reactant to the transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03145 "inverse square law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03145 "The intensity of radiation from a point source in free space is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the source. Presence of absorbers necessitates corrections to this law.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03146 "inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03146 "A symmetry operation involving a centre of inversion (\\(i\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03147 "inversion height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03147 "The height above ground level at which there is change in sign of the normal temperature 'lapse rate', \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}T}{\\rm{d}z}\\) (the rate of change of the temperature with height). Several temperature inversions may be present in the air over a given site at different altitudes as result of various meteorological factors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03149))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03149 "inversion point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03149 "The temperature (pressure) at which one polymorph of a solid phase changes into another at constant pressure (temperature).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03151 "inverted micelle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03151 "The reversible formation of association colloids from surfactants in non-polar solvents leads to aggregates termed inverted (or inverse, reverse or reversed) micelle. Such association is often of the type: \\[\\rm{Monomer}\\rightleftarrows \\rm{Dimer}\\rightleftarrows \\rm{Trimer}\\rightleftarrows \\rm{ ... }n\\rm{-mer}\\] and the phenomenon of critical micelle concentration (or an analogous effect) is consequently not observed. In an inverted micelle the polar groups of the surfactants are concentrated in the interior and the lipophilic groups extend towards and into the non-polar solvent.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03151 "inverse micelle")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03151 "reverse micelle (or reversed micelle)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03152 "inverted region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03152 "In plots relating rate constants to charges in standard Gibbs energy (\\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)) for electron transfer a region may occur in which the rate constants decrease as the exergonicity of the reaction increases. This region is often referred to as the inverted region and its presence is predicted by the theory developed for outer sphere electron transfer for the case \\(-\\Delta G^{\\circ} \\gt \\lambda \\) in the Marcus equation, \\(\\lambda\\) being the reorganization energy. Note the similarity to the energy gap law for radiationless conversion of an excited state.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03152 "Marcus inverted region")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03153 "<i>in vitro</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03153 "In glass, referring to a study in the laboratory usually involving isolated organ, tissue, cell, or biochemical systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03154 "<i>in vivo</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03154 "In the living body, referring to a study performed on a living organism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03158 "ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03158 "An atomic or molecular particle having a net electric charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03161 "ion collector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03161 "Device for the capture of selected ions such as a Faraday cup collector (with a d.c. amplifier) or an electron multiplier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03162 "ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03162 "A high-frequency mass spectrometer in which the ions to be detected, with a selected value of the quotient mass/charge, absorb maximum energy through the effect of a high-frequency electric field and a constant magnetic field perpendicular to the electric field. Maximum energy is gained by the ions which satisfy the cyclotron resonance condition and as a result they are separated from ions of different mass/charge.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03162 "ICR mass spectrometer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03165))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03165 "ion energy loss spectra")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03165 "Spectra that show the loss of translational energy of ions involved in ion/neutral species reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03167 "ion exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03167 "In surface chemistry, if the adsorption of one or several ionic species is accompanied by the simultaneous desorption (displacement) of an equivalent amount of one or more other ionic species, this process is called ion exchange.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03168 "ion-exchange chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03168 "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences in the ion-exchange affinities of the sample components. Present day ion-exchange chromatography on small particle high efficiency columns, and usually utilizing conductometric or spectroscopic detectors, is often referred to as ion chromatography (IC).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03169 "ion-exchange isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03169 "The concentration of a counter-ion in the ion exchanger expressed as a function of its concentration in the external solution under specified conditions and at constant temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03171 "ion exchanger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03171 "A solid or liquid, inorganic or organic substance containing exchangeable ions with others of the same charge, present in a solution in which the ion exchanger is considered to be insoluble. It is recognized that there are cases where liquid exchangers are employed and where it may be difficult to distinguish between the separation process as belonging to ion exchange or liquid-liquid distribution, but the broad definition given here is regarded as that which is most appropriate. A monofunctional ion exchanger contains only one type of ionogenic group, a bifunctional ion exchanger two types and a polyfunctional ion exchanger more than one type. In a macroporous ion exchanger the pores are large compared to atomic dimensions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03173 "ion-free layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03173 "An interphase in which only non-specifically adsorbed ions are present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03175 "ionic conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03175 "Defined for ionic species $\\ce{B}$ by \\(\\lambda = |z_{\\rm{B}}|\\ F\\ u_{\\rm{B}}\\) where \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the charge number of the ionic species $\\ce{B}$, \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant, and \\(u_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the electric mobility of species $\\ce{B}$. In most current practice \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) is taken as unity, i.e. ionic conductivity is taken as that of species such as $\\ce{Na^{+}}$, $\\ce{Ca^{2+}}$/2, $\\ce{La^{3+}}$/3 etc. To avoid ambiguity the species considered should be clearly stated, e.g. as \\(\\lambda (\\rm{Ca}^{\\rm{2+}}/2)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03176 "ionic copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03176 "A copolymerization which is an ionic polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03177 "ionic dissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03177 "The decomposition of an ion into another ion of lower mass and one or more neutral species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03178 "ionic polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03178 "A chain polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are ions or ion pairs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03180 "ionic strength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03180 "On a molality basis, \\[I_{m} = \\frac{1}{2}\\ \\sum m_{\\rm{B}}\\ z_{\\rm{B}}^{2}\\] where the sum goes over all the ions B. \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the charge number of ion B. The ionic strength on a concentration basis is defined analogously \\[I_{c} = \\frac{1}{2}\\ \\sum c_{\\rm{B}}\\ z_{\\rm{B}}^{2}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03181 "ionic transport number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03181 "Quotient of the current carried by an ionic component and the total current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03183 "ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03183 "The generation of one or more ions. It may occur, e.g. by loss of an electron from a neutral molecular entity, by the unimolecular heterolysis of such an entity into two or more ions, or by a heterolytic substitution reaction involving neutral molecules, such as:    The loss of an electron from a singly, doubly, etc. charged cation is called second, third, etc. ionization. This terminology is used especially in mass spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03188 "ionization buffer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03188 "A buffer added to increase the free-electron concentration by the flame gases, thus repressing and stabilizing the degree of ionization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03189 "ionization by sputtering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03189 "Ionization by bombardment of a solid specimen with accelerated ions or electrons or fast neutrals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03190))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03190 "ionization chamber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03190 "A radiation detector which employs an electric field for the collection at the electrodes of charges associated with the ions produced in the sensitive volume by ionizing radiation, without charge multiplication. The solid angle ($\\ce{2\\Uppi\\!}$, $\\ce{4\\uppi\\!}$) subtended when using the detector, is sometimes specified.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03194 "ionization cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03194 "A measure of the probability that a given ionization process will occur when an atom or molecule interacts with an electron or a photon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03196 "ionization efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03196 "The ratio of the number of ions formed to the number of electrons or photons used in an ionization process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03197 "ionization efficiency curve")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03197 "The number of ions produced as a function of the energy of the electrons or photons used to produce ionization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03199 "ionization energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03199 "Minimum energy required to eject an electron out of a neutral atom or molecule in its ground state. The adiabatic ionization energy refers to the formation of the molecular ion in its ground vibrational state and the vertical ionization energy applies to the transition to the molecular ion without change in geometry. This quantity was formerly called 'ionization potential'. The second ionization energy of an atom is the energy required to eject the second electron from the atom (energy for the process $\\ce{X^{+} -> X^{2+} + e^{-}}$).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03199 "ionization potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03214 "ionizing collision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03214 "An ion/neutral species interaction in which an electron or electrons are stripped from the ion and/or the neutral species in the collision. Generally, this term has come to be used to describe collisions of fast-moving ions with a neutral species in which the neutral species is ionized with no change in the number of charges carried by the ion. Care should be taken when this term is used to emphasize if charge stripping of the ion has taken place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03215))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03215 "ionizing power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03215 "A term to denote the tendency of a particular solvent to promote ionization of an uncharged or, less often, charged solute. The term has been used both in a kinetic and in a thermodynamic context.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03216 "ionizing radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03216 "Any radiation consisting of directly or indirectly ionizing particles or a mixture of both, or photons with energy higher than the energy of photons of ultraviolet light or a mixture of both such particles and photons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03217 "ionizing voltage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03217 "The voltage difference through which electrons are accelerated before they are used to bring about electron impact ionization. To obtain the true ionizing voltage, corrections for any contact or surface potentials must be made.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03218 "ion kinetic energy spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03218 "A spectrum obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the translational energy-to-charge ratios of the ionic species contained within it. A radial electric field (E) achieves separation of the various ionic species in this way.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03219 "ion laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03219 "A gas laser in which the active species is an ion formed in the electric discharge. Most practically important lasers of this type have continuous wave (CW) output. Examples are the argon ion, krypton ion and helium-cadmium (HeCd) lasers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03221))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03221 "ion microscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03221 "Use of the secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS) technique to obtain micrographs of the elemental (or isotopic) distribution at the surface of a sample with a spatial resolution of \\(2\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) or better.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03223 "ion/molecule reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03223 "An ion/neutral species reaction in which the neutral species is a molecule. The use of ion-molecule reaction is not recommended; the hyphen suggests a reaction of a species that is both an ion and a molecule and is not the intended meaning.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03225 "ion/neutral species exchange reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03225 "In this reaction an association reaction is accompanied by the subsequent or simultaneous liberation of a different neutral species as product.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03226))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03226 "ion/neutral species reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03226 "A process wherein a charged species interacts with a neutral reactant to produce either chemically different species or changes in the internal energy of one or both of the reactants. The contrasting expression ion/neutral reaction is not ideal, simply because the word neutral is not a noun. However, any alternatives such as ion/neutral species reaction are so clumsy as to mitigate against their general acceptance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03228 "ionogenic groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03228 "The fixed groupings in an ion exchanger which are either ionized or capable of dissociation into fixed ions and mobile counter-ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03229 "ionomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03229 "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which a small but significant proportion of the constitutional units has ionic or ionizable groups, or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03230 "ionomer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03230 "A macromolecule in which a small but significant proportion of the constitutional units have ionizable or ionic groups, or both. Some protein molecules may be classified as ionomer molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03231))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03231 "ion pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03231 "A pair of oppositely charged ions held together by coulomb attraction without formation of a covalent bond. Experimentally, an ion pair behaves as one unit in determining conductivity, kinetic behaviour, osmotic properties, etc. Following Bjerrum, oppositely charged ions with their centres closer together than a distance: \\[q = \\frac{8.36\\times 10^{6}\\ z^{+}\\ z^{-}}{\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\ T}\\ \\rm{pm}\\] are considered to constitute an ion pair ('Bjerrum ion pair'). [\\(z^{+}\\) and \\(z^{-}\\) are the charge numbers of the ions, and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\) is the relative permittivity (or dielectric constant) of the medium.] An ion pair, the constituent ions of which are in direct contact (and not separated by an intervening solvent or other neutral molecule) is designated as a 'tight ion pair' (or 'intimate' or 'contact ion pair'). A tight ion pair of $\\ce{X^{+}}$ and $\\ce{Y^{−}}$ is symbolically represented as $\\ce{X^{+}Y^{−}}$. By contrast, an ion pair whose constituent ions are separated by one or several solvent or other neutral molecules is described as a 'loose ion pair', symbolically represented as $\\ce{X^{+}}\\,\\|\\,\\ce{Y^{−}}$. The members of a loose ion pair can readily interchange with other free or loosely paired ions in the solution. This interchange may be detectable (e.g. by isotopic labelling) and thus afford an experimental distinction between tight and loose ion pairs. A further conceptual distinction has sometimes been made between two types of loose ion pairs. In 'solvent-shared ion pairs' the ionic constituents of the pair are separated by only a single solvent molecule, whereas in 'solvent-separated ion pairs' more than one solvent molecule intervenes. However, the term 'solvent-separated ion pair' must be used and interpreted with care since it has also widely been used as a less specific term for 'loose' ion pair.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03232 "ion-pair formation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03232 "Involves an ionization process in which a positive fragment ion and a negative fragment ion are among the products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03233 "ion pair return")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03233 "The recombination of a pair of ions $\\ce{R^{+}}$ and $\\ce{Z^{−}}$ formed from ionization of $\\ce{RZ}$. If the ions are paired as a tight ion pair and recombine without prior separation into a loose ion pair this is called 'internal ion-pair return':   It is a special case of 'primary geminate recombination'. If the ions are paired as a loose ion pair and form the covalent chemical species via a tight ion pair, this is called 'external ion-pair return':  It is a special case of 'secondary geminate recombination'. When the covalent molecule RZ is reformed without direct evidence of prior partial racemization or without other direct evidence of prior formation of a tight ion pair, (e.g. without partial racemization if the group R is suitably chiral) the internal ion-pair return is sometimes called a 'hidden return'. External (unimolecular) ion-pair return is to be distinguished from 'external (bimolecular) ion return', the (reversible) process whereby dissociated ions are converted into loose ion pairs:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03238 "ion probe microanalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03238 "Any technique in which the specimen is bombarded by a focused beam of (primary) ions (diameter less than \\(10\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) and the (secondary) ions ejected from the specimen are detected after passage through a mass spectrometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03243 "ion scattering spectrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03243 "Any technique using low energy (\\(10\\ \\rm{keV}\\)) ions in which the bombarding particles scattered by the sample are detected and recorded as a function of energy and/or angle. This technique is used mainly for determining the composition and structure of the first few atomic layers of a sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03244 "ion-selective electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03244 "An electrochemical sensor, based on thin films or selective membranes as recognition elements, and an electrochemical half-cell equivalent to other half-cells of the zeroth (inert metal in a redox electrolyte), 1st, 2nd and 3rd kinds. These devices are distinct from systems that involve redox reactions (electrodes of zeroth, 1st, 2nd and 3rd kinds), although they often contain a 2nd kind electrode as the 'inner' or 'internal' reference electrode. The potential difference response has, as its principal component, the Gibbs energy change associated with permselective mass transfer (by ion-exchange, solvent extraction or some other mechanism) across a phase boundary. The ion-selective electrode must be used in conjunction with a reference electrode (i.e. 'outer' or 'external' reference electrode) to form a complete electrochemical cell. The measured potential differences (ion-selective electrode vs. outer reference electrode potentials) are linearly dependent on the logarithm of the activity of a given ion in solution. Comment: The term 'ion-specific electrode' is not recommended. The term 'specific' implies that the electrode does not respond to additional ions. Since no electrode is truly specific for one ion, the term 'ion-selective' is recommended as more appropriate. 'Selective ion-sensitive electrode' is a little-used term to describe an ion-selective electrode. 'Principal' or 'primary' ions are those which an electrode is designed to measure. It is never certain that the 'principal' ion is most sensitively measured, e.g. nitrate ion-selective electrodes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03245 "ion-selective electrode cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03245 "An ion-selective electrode in conjunction with a reference electrode. Generally, the cell contains two reference electrodes, internal and external, and the thin film or membrane recognition-transduction element. However, besides this conventional type of cell (with solution contact on both sides of the membrane) there are cell arrangements with wire contact to one side of the membrane (all solid state and coated wire types).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03247 "ion source")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03247 "Generally an assembly composed of: (i) an ionization chamber in which a stream of electrons flows from a hot filament across a stream of gas to collector (the potential between filament and collector is usually between 50 and 70 \\(\\rm{V}\\)); (ii) a device for the acceleration of these ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03249 "ion trap mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03249 "An arrangement in which ions with a desired range of quotients mass/charge are first made to describe stable paths under the effect of a high-frequency electric quadrupole field, and are then separated and presented to a detector by adjusting the field so as to selectivity induce path instability according to their respective mass/charge ratios.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03251))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03251 "<i>ipso</i>-attack")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03251 "The attachment of an entering group to a position in an aromatic compound already carrying a substituent group (other than hydrogen). The entering group may displace that substituent group but may also itself be expelled or migrate to a different position in a subsequent step. The term 'ipso-substitution' is not used, since it is synonymous with substitution. For example:   where E + is an electrophile and Z is a substituent (other than hydrogen).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03253 "iridoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03253 "Cyclic monoterpenoids having the iridane skeleton (1-isopropyl-2,3-di-methylcyclopentane), the traditional, but not undisputed, numbering of which is shown in the diagram.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03254))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03254 "irradiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03254 "Radiant power, \\(P\\), of all wavelengths incident from all upward directions on a small element of surface containing the point under consideration divided by the area of the element. SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03255))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03255 "irradiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03255 "Exposure to ionizing radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03256 "irregular macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03256 "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of more than one type of constitutional unit or a macromolecule the structure of which comprises constitutional units not all connected identically with respect to directional sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03257 "irregular polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03257 "A polymer composed of irregular macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03258 "irreversible transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03258 "A transition that changes the state of a system which cannot be readily reversed to restore the system to its original state. Example: The conversion of $\\ce{TiO2}$ (anatase) to $\\ce{TiO2}$ (rutile).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03260 "isobar")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03260 "Lines on a plot joining points of equal barometric pressure in the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03261 "isobaric mass-change determination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03261 "A technique in which the equilibrium mass of a substance at constant partial pressure of the volatile product(s) is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. The record is the isobaric mass-change curve. The mass should be plotted on the ordinate decreasing downwards and temperature on the abscissa increasing from left to right.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03262 "isobaric separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03262 "Chromatographic separation carried out using constant inlet and outlet pressure conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03263 "isobars")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03263 "Different nuclides of equal mass number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03265))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03265 "isoclinic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03265 "A wavelength, wavenumber, or frequency at which the first derivative of an absorption spectrum of a sample does not change upon a chemical reaction or physical change of the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03267 "isocoumarins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03267 "Isocoumarin (1H-isochromen-1-one) and its derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03268))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03268 "isocratic analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03268 "A procedure in which the composition of the mobile phase remains constant during the elution process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03269 "isocyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03269 "The isocyanic acid tautomer, $\\ce{HN=C=O}$, of cyanic acid, $\\ce{HOC≡N}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives RN=C=O.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03270 "isocyanides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03270 "The isomer $\\ce{HN+\\!#C^{-}}$ of hydrocyanic acid, $\\ce{HC#N}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce{RNC}$ ($\\ce{RN^{+}\\!#C^{-}}$).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03270 "isonitriles")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03271 "isocyclic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03271 "A less preferred synonym for homocyclic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03272 "isodesmic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03272 "A reaction (actual or hypothetical) in which the types of bonds that are made in forming the products are the same as those which are broken in the reactants, e.g.   Such processes have advantages for theoretical treatment. The Hammett equation as applied to equilibria [cf. (a)] essentially deals with isodesmic processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03273 "isodiazenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03273 "Compounds having the structure . These compounds can also be called diazanylidenes or hydrazinylidenes. They have also been called by the trivial name azamines and the incorrectly formed name 1,1-diazenes. They should not be called 'aminonitrenes'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03273 "azamines")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03273 "diazanylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03273 "hydrazinylidenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03274 "isoelectric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03274 "A macro-ion of a polyampholyte (in particular a protein) is said to be isoelectric if it exhibits no electrophoresis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03275))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03275 "isoelectric point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03275 "The \\(\\rm{pH}\\) value at which the net electric charge of an elementary entity is zero. \\(\\rm{pI}\\) is a commonly used symbol for this kind-of-quantity. It should be replaced by \\(\\rm{pH(I)}\\) because it is a \\(\\rm{pH}\\) determined under that particular condition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03276))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03276 "isoelectronic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03276 "Two or more molecular entities are described as isoelectronic if they have the same number of valence electrons and the same structure, i.e. number and connectivity of atoms, but differ in some of the elements involved. Thus: $\\ce{CO}$, $\\ce{N2}$ and $\\ce{NO^{+}}$ are isoelectronic. $\\ce{CH2=C=O}$ and $\\ce{CH2=N=N}$ are isoelectronic. $\\ce{CH3COCH3}$ and $\\ce{CH3N=NCH3}$ have the same number of electrons, but have different structures, hence they are not described as isoelectronic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03277 "isoemissive point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03277 "Wavelength, wavenumber or frequency at which the total intensity of emission of light by a sample does not change during a chemical reaction or physical change. The terms isolampsic and isostilbic are sometimes used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03278 "isoentropic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03278 "A reaction series is said to be isoentropic if the individual reactions of the series have the same standard entropy of activation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03279 "isoenzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03279 "One of a group of related enzymes catalysing the same reaction but having different molecular structures and characterized by varying physical, biochemical and immunological properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03280))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03280 "isoequilibrium relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03280 "A relationship analogous to the isokinetic relationship but applied to equilibrium data. The equation defining the isoequilibrium temperature \\(\\beta \\) is: \\[\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}H - \\beta\\ \\Delta _{\\rm{r}}S = \\rm{constant}\\] where \\(\\Delta H\\) and \\(\\Delta S\\) are enthalpy and entropy of reaction, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03282 "isoionic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03282 "A macro-ion of a polyampholyte (in particular a protein) is said to be isoionic if besides the polyampholyte and $\\ce{H^{+}}$ or $\\ce{OH^{−}}$ ions (in general ions of the solvent) no other ions are present in the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03283 "isoionic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03283 "The \\(\\rm{pH}\\) value at which the net electric charge of an elementary entity in pure water equals zero.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03284 "isokinetic line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03284 "A line in a given surface connecting points with equal wind speed; also called isotach or isovel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03285 "isokinetic relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03285 "When a series of structurally related substrates undergo the same general reaction or when the reaction conditions for a single substrate are changed in a systematic way, the enthalpies and entropies of activation sometimes satisfy the relation: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H - \\beta \\ \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S = \\rm{constant}\\] where the parameter \\(\\beta \\) is independent of temperature. This equation (or some equivalent form) is said to represent an 'isokinetic relationship'. The temperature \\(T=\\beta \\) (at which all members of a series obeying the isokinetic relationship react at the same rate) is termed the 'isokinetic temperature'. Supposed isokinetic relationships as established by direct correlation of \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\) with \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S\\) are often spurious and the calculated value of \\(\\beta \\) is meaningless, because errors in \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H\\) lead to compensating errors in \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S\\). Satisfactory methods of establishing such relationships have been devised.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03286))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03286 "isokinetic sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03286 "A technique for collecting airborne particulate in which the sampling device has a collection efficiency of unity for all sizes of particles in sampled air, regardless of wind velocity and direction of the instrument. The air stream entering the collector has a velocity (speed and direction) equal to that of the air in the gas stream just ahead of the sampling port of the collector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03287))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03287 "isolated double bonds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03287 "Those bonds which are neither conjugated nor cumulative as in  or the B ring of")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03288))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03288 "isolobal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03288 "The term is used to compare molecular fragments with each other and with familiar species from organic chemistry. Two fragments are isolobal if the number, symmetry properties, approximate energy and shape of the frontier orbitals and the number of electrons in them are similar.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03289))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03289 "isomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03289 "One of several species (or molecular entities) that have the same atomic composition (molecular formula) but different line formulae or different stereochemical formulae and hence different physical and/or chemical properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03290))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03290 "isomerases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03290 "Enzymes that catalyse intramolecular rearrangements. Isomerases are classified into racemases and epimerases, cis-trans isomerases, intramolecular lyases and other isomerases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03291 "isomeric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03291 "Adjective derived from isomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03292))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03292 "isomeric state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03292 "A nuclear state having a mean lifetime long enough to be observed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03293 "isomeric transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03293 "A spontaneous transition between two isomeric states of a nucleus.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03294))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03294 "isomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03294 "The relationship between isomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03295 "isomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03295 "A chemical reaction, the principal product of which is isomeric with the principal reactant. An intramolecular isomerization that involves the breaking or making of bonds is a special case of a molecular rearrangement. Isomerization does not necessarily imply molecular rearrangement (e.g. in the case of the interconversion of conformational isomers).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03296))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03296 "isomer shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03296 "Measure of the energy difference between the source (\\(E_{\\rm{s}}\\)) and the absorber (\\(E_{\\rm{a}}\\)) transition. The measured Doppler velocity shift, \\(\\delta \\), is related to the energy difference by \\[E_{\\rm{a}}- E_{\\rm{s}}=\\frac{\\delta \\ E_{\\gamma }}{c}\\] where \\(E_{\\gamma }\\) is the Mössbauer gamma energy and \\(c\\) is the speed of light in vacuum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03298 "isometric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03298 "Two molecular entities that are superposable or can be made superposable by reflection of one of them in a mirror are isometric; otherwise they are anisometric.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03299 "isomorphous structures")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03299 "In the crystalline state, polymer chains are generally parallel to one another but neighbouring chains of equivalent conformation may differ in chirality and/or orientation. Chains of identical chirality and conformation are isomorphous. Chains of opposite chirality but equivalent conformation are enantiomorphous. For example, two ...$\\ce{TG^{+}TG^{+}TG^{+}}$... helices of isotactic poly(propylene) are isomorphous. Isotactic poly(propylene) chains of the ...$\\ce{TG^{+}TG^{+}TG^{+}}$... and ...$\\ce{G^{+}TG^{+}TG^{+}T}$... types are mutually enantiomorphous. With regard to orientation, consider a repeating side group originating at atom \\(A_{1}^{i}\\), the first atom of the side group being \\(B_{\\alpha }^{i}\\). For certain chain symmetries (helical, for instance) the bond vectors \\(\\overset{\\rightarrow }{\\mathbf{b}}(A_{1}^{i},B_{\\alpha }^{i})\\) have the same components (positive or negative) \\(\\frac{\\overset{\\rightarrow }{\\mathbf{b}}\\cdot \\overset{\\rightarrow }{\\mathbf{c}}}{|\\overset{\\rightarrow }{\\mathbf{c}}|}\\) along the c axis for every \\(i\\).  Two equivalent (isomorphous or enantiomorphous) chains in the crystal lattice, having identical components of the bond vectors along $c$, both positive or both negative, are designated isoclined; two equivalent chains having bond vectors along $c$ of the same magnitude but opposite sign are designated anticlined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03302 "isooptoacoustic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03302 "Wavelength, wavenumber or frequency at which the total energy emitted by a sample as heat does not change upon a chemical reaction in or a physical change of the sample. Its position depends on the experimental conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03304 "isopotential point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03304 "For an ion-selective electrode cell, there is often a particular activity of the measured ion for which the emf of the cell is independent of temperature. That activity, and the corresponding potential difference, defines the isopotential point. The specification of the ion-selective electrode and outer reference electrode must be described. Comment: When an isothermal cell is used with identical reference electrodes, the isopotential point is the activity of sensed species that gives zero net membrane potential, e.g. sensed activity is the same in the inner and outer (test) solution. Calibration lines for different cell temperatures have different slopes, but intersect at a common activity point. Cells with temperature gradients are not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03306 "isoprenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03306 "Compounds formally derived from isoprene (2-methylbuta-1,3-diene), the skeleton of which can generally be discerned in repeated occurrence in the molecule. The skeleton of isoprenoids may differ from strict additivity of isoprene units by loss or shift of a fragment, commonly a methyl group. The class includes both hydrocarbons and oxygenated derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03307))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03307 "isopycnic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03307 "An adjective describing components of a multicomponent system with equal partial specific volumes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03308))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03308 "isopycnic separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03308 "Chromatographic separations carried out using constant density conditions. The temperature and pressure may be altered during the run (originally the term 'isoconfertic separation' was used but this term is not recommended).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03308 "isoconfertic separation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03309 "isorefractive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03309 "An adjective describing components of a multicomponent system having zero refractive index increments with respect to each other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03310 "isosbestic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03310 "Wavelength, wavenumber or frequency at which the total absorbance of a sample does not change during a chemical reaction or a physical change of the sample.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03310 "isoabsorption point")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03311))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03311 "isoselective relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03311 "A relationship analogous to the isokinetic relationship, but applied to selectivity data of reactions. At the isoselective temperature, the selectivities of the series of reactions following the relationship are identical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03312 "isoselenocyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03312 "Selenium analogues of isocyanates $\\ce{RN=C=Se}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03313 "isosteric enthalpy of adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03313 "When the addition of the differential amount of component \\(i\\) \\(\\mathrm{d}n_{i}^{\\sigma }\\) or \\(\\mathrm{d}n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}\\) is effected at constant pressure \\(p\\), the differential molar enthalpy of adsorption, \\(\\Delta _{a}H_{i}^{\\sigma}\\) or \\(\\Delta _{a}H_{i}^{\\rm{s}}\\) also called the isosteric enthalpy of adsorption (\\(q^{\\rm{st}}\\)) is defined as: \\[\\Delta _{a}H_{i}^{\\sigma} = -q^{\\rm{st},\\sigma} = U_{i}^{\\sigma} - H_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\] \\[\\Delta _{a}H_{i}^{\\rm{s}} = -q^{\\rm{st},\\sigma} = H_{i}^{\\sigma} - H_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\] where \\(H_{i}^{\\rm{s}}=(\\frac{\\partial H^{\\rm{s}}}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{s}}})_{T,p,m,n_{j}^{\\rm{s}}}\\) and \\(H_{i}^{\\rm{g}}\\) is the partial molar enthalpy of component \\(i\\) in the gas phase, i.e. \\((\\frac{\\partial H^{\\rm{g}}}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{g}}})_{T,p,n_{i}^{\\rm{g}}}\\)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03314 "isostilbic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03314 "Same as isoemissive point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03315 "isotactic macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03315 "A tactic macromolecule, essentially comprising only one species of configurational base unit, which has chiral or prochiral atoms in the main chain in a unique arrangement with respect to its adjacent constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03316 "isotactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03316 "A polymer composed of isotactic macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03318))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03318 "isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03318 "Lines joining points of equal temperature in the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03319 "isothermal chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03319 "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is kept constant during the separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03320 "isothiocyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03320 "Sulfur analogues of isocyanates $\\ce{RN=C=S}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03321 "isotones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03321 "Nuclides having the same neutron number but different atomic numbers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03322 "isotope dilution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03322 "Mixing of a given nuclide with one or more of its isotopes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03323))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03323 "isotope dilution analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03323 "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the measurement of the isotopic abundance of a nuclide after isotope dilution with the test portion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03327 "isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03327 "The effect on the rate or equilibrium constant of two reactions that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more of their otherwise chemically identical components is referred to as a kinetic isotope effect or a thermodynamic (or equilibrium) isotope effect, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03328))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03328 "isotope exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03328 "A chemical reaction in which the reactant and product chemical species are chemically identical but have different isotopic composition. In such a reaction the isotope distribution tends towards equilibrium (as expressed by fractionation factors) as a result of transfers of isotopically different atoms or groups. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03329 "isotope exchange analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03329 "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the isotope exchange between isotopes of the element to be determined and other isotopes of this element in different valency states or in different molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03330))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03330 "isotope pattern")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03330 "Set of peaks related to ions with the same chemical formula but containing different isotopes; e.g. the 16 and 17 mass/charge peaks in a $\\ce{CH4}$ sample arising from $\\ce{^{12}CH4^{+}}$ and $\\ce{^{13}CH4^{+}}$ ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03331 "isotopes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03331 "Nuclides having the same atomic number but different mass numbers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03332))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03332 "isotopic abundance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03332 "The relative number of atoms of a particular isotopes in a mixture of the isotopes of an element, expressed as a fraction of all the atoms of the element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03334 "isotopically deficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03334 "An isotopically labelled compound may be designated as isotopically deficient when the isotopic content of one or more elements has been depleted, i.e. when one or more nuclide(s) is (are) present in less than a natural ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03335 "isotopically enriched ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03335 "When the abundance of a particular nuclide is increased above the level at which it occurs in nature and is incorporated in a molecule, the term 'isotopically enriched ion' is used to describe any ion enriched in the isotope.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03336 "isotopically labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03336 "Describes a mixture of an isotopically unmodified compound with one or more analogous isotopically substituted compound(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03337 "isotopically modified")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03337 "Describes a compound that has a macroscopic composition such that the isotopic ratio of nuclides for at least one element deviates measurably from that occurring in nature. It is either an isotopically substituted compound or an isotopically labelled compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03338 "isotopically substituted")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03338 "Describes a compound that has a composition such that essentially all the molecules of the compound have only the indicated nuclide at each designated position. For all other positions, the absence of nuclide indication means that the nuclide composition is the natural one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03339 "isotopically unmodified")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03339 "Describes a compound that has a macroscopic composition such that its constituent nuclides are present in the proportions occurring in nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03340 "isotopic carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03340 "A carrier which differs only in isotopic composition from the trace it has to carry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03341))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03341 "isotopic enrichment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03341 "Any process by which the isotopic abundance of a specified isotopes in a mixture of isotopes of an element is increased.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03342 "isotopic enrichment factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03342 "For a material enriched in a specified isotope, the ratio between the isotopic abundance and the natural abundance of that isotope.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03343 "isotopic fractionation factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03343 """The ratio \\[\\frac{(x_{1}/x_{2})_{\\rm{A}}}{(x_{1}/x_{2})_{\\rm{B}}}\\] where \\(x\\) is the abundance, expressed as the atom fraction of the isotope distinguished by the subscript numeral, when the two isotopes are equilibrated between two different chemical species $\\ce{A}$ and $\\ce{B}$ (or between specific sites A and B in the same or different chemical species). The term is most commonly met in connection with deuterium solvent isotope effects, when the fractionation factor \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) expresses the ratio: \\[\\varPhi = \\frac{(x_{\\rm{D}}/x_{\\rm{H}})_{\\rm{solute}}}{(x_{\\rm{D}}/x_{\\rm{H}})_{\\rm{solvent}}}\r
\\] for the exchangeable hydrogen atoms in the chemical species (or sites) concerned. The concept is also applicable to transition states.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03344 "isotopic ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03344 "Any ion containing one or more of the less abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the elements that make up its structure, e.g. $\\ce{CH2D^{+}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03345 "isotopic labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03345 "Labelling in which the resulting product is only different from the initial one by its isotopic composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03346 "isotopic molecular ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03346 "A molecular ion containing one or more of the less abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the atoms that make up the molecular structure. Thus, for ethyl bromide there exist isotopic molecular ions such as: $\\ce{^{13}CCH5Br^{+.}}$, $\\ce{C2H4DBr^{+.}}$, $\\ce{C2H5^{81}Br^{+.}}$, $\\ce{^{13}C2H5^{81}Br^{+.}}$, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03348 "isotopic scrambling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03348 "The achievement, or the process of achieving, an equilibrium distribution of isotopes within a specified set of atoms in a chemical species or group of chemical species (for an example, see diagram).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:I03348 "scrambling")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03349 "isotopic separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03349 "An operation for the purpose of modifying an isotopic abundance in a mixture of isotopes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03350 "isotopic tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03350 "A tracer which only differs in isotopic composition from the substance to be traced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03351 "isotopologue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03351 "A molecular entity that differs only in isotopic composition (number of isotopic substitutions), e.g. $\\ce{CH4}$, $\\ce{CH3D}$, $\\ce{CH2D2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03352 "isotopomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03352 "Isomers having the same number of each isotopic atom but differing in their positions. The term is a contraction of 'isotopic isomer'. Isotopomers can be either constitutional isomers (e.g. $\\ce{CH2DCH=O}$ and $\\ce{CH3CD=O}$) or isotopic stereoisomers [e.g. $\\ce{(\\textit{R})-}$ and $\\ce{(\\textit{S})-CH3CHDOH}$ or $\\ce{(\\textit{Z})-}$ and $\\ce{(\\textit{E})-CH3CH=CHD}$].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03353 "isotropic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03353 "A quantity which is independent of direction. Anisotropic and nonisotropic refer to quantities which are direction dependent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03354))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03354 "isotropic carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03354 "A monolithic carbon material without preferred crystallographic orientation of the microstructure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03355 "isotropic pitch-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03355 "Carbon fibres obtained by carbonization of isotropic pitch fibres after these have been stabilized (i.e. made non-fusible).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03356))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03356 "isoureas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03356 "The imidic acid tautomer of urea, $\\ce{H2NC(=NH)OH}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:I03358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:I03358 "isozyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:I03358 "Synonymous with isoenzyme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06772 "ionophore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06772 "A compound which can carry specific ions through membranes of cells or organelles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06773 "ion pumps")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06773 "Ion pumps enable ions to flow through membranes in a thermodynamically uphill direction by the use of an energy source such as ATP or light. They consist of sugar-containing hetero-peptide assemblies, which open and close upon the binding and subsequent hydrolysis of ATP, usually transporting more than one ion towards the outside or the inside of the membrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06779 "iron-sulfur cluster")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06779 "A unit comprising two or more iron atoms and bridging sulfur ligands in an iron-sulfur protein. The recommended designation of a cluster consists of the iron and sulfur content, in square brackets, for example, $\\ce{[2\\! Fe-2\\! S]}$, $\\ce{[3\\! Fe-4\\! S]}$. The possible oxidation levels are indicated by the net charge excluding the ligands, for example $\\ce{[4\\! Fe-4\\! S]^{2+}}$; $\\ce{[4\\! Fe-4\\! S]^{+}}$ or ($\\ce{[4\\! Fe-4\\! S]^{2+;1+}}$) cluster.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06780 "iron-sulfur proteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06780 "Proteins in which non-haem iron is coordinated with cysteine sulfur and usually also with inorganic sulfur. Divided into three major categories: rubredoxins; 'simple iron-sulfur proteins', containing only iron-sulfur clusters; and 'complex iron-sulfur proteins', containing additional active redox centres such as flavin, molybdenum or haem. In most iron-sulfur proteins, the clusters function as electron-transfer groups, but in others they have other functions, such as catalysis of hydratase/dehydratase reactions, maintenance of protein structure, or regulation of activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06828 "incidence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06828 "Number of occurrences of illness commencing, or of persons falling ill, during a given period in a specific population: usually expressed as a rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06941 "ideal chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06941 "Chromatographic process, where no peak-broadening effects (such as diffusion, slow mass transfer, etc.) operate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT06944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT06944 "ideal, non-linear chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT06944 "Chromatographic process, where only the curvature of the distribution isotherm determines the shape of the peaks while other peak-broadening processes are neglected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07055))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07055 "individual gauge for localized orbitals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07055 "A method of calculation of nuclear shieldings, in which localized molecular orbitals associated with inner shell, bonding orbitals, and lone pairs have unique origins for the calculation of diamagnetic and paramagnetic terms. With this method, satisfactory estimates of NMR chemical shifts for elements in the first and second rows can be achieved in ab initio calculations with basis sets of moderate size provided sufficiently accurate molecular geometries are used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07056 "instability of Hartree–Fock solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07056 "Existence of additional solutions to the equations of the Hartree–Fock method occurring usually in the case when potential energy surfaces of different electronic states are drawn close together. Within the spin-restricted Hartree–Fock method (RHF), singlet and triplet instabilities are distinguished. The former involves the existence of another solution with lower energy and the electron distribution of lower symmetry. It may be regarded as an indication that the initially assumed molecular shape needs correction. The triplet instability involves rejection of the condition of double occupancy of molecular orbitals and thus transition to the spin-unrestricted Hartree–Fock method (UHF). The triplet instability is a necessary, but insufficient, condition for the conclusion as to the biradical character of the ground state of a given system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07057 "intrinsic reaction coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07057 "A minimum-energy reaction path on a potential energy surface in mass-weighted coordinates, connecting reactants to products via the transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07058 "ionic bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07058 "The bond between atoms with sharply different electronegativities. In strict terms, an ionic bond refers to the electrostatic attraction experienced between the electric charges of a cation and an anion, in contrast with a purely covalent bond. In practice, it is preferable to consider the amount of ionic character of a bond rather than referring to purely ionic or purely covalent bonds. The relationship was proposed (L. Pauling) for the estimation of ionic character of a bond between atoms A and B: \\[\\rm{Amount of ionic character} = 1 - e^{-1/4}\\ (\\chi _{\\rm{A}}\\,-\\,\\chi_{\\rm{B}})\\] where \\(\\chi _{\\rm{A}}\\) and \\(\\chi _{\\rm{B}}\\) are the Pauling electronegativities of atoms A and B. This type of bonding is realized mostly in solids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07059 "isoconjugate systems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07059 "Molecular entities with similar conjugate networks which have the same number of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$, e.g. cyclopentadienide anion and pyrrole or benzene and pyridine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07060 "isogyric reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07060 """A reaction in which the number of electron pairs is conserved, for example: 
$$\\ce{CH4(^{1}\\textit{A}_{1}) + H(^{2}\\textit{S}) -> CH3(^{2}\\textit{A}^{'}_{2}) + H2(^{1}\\Sigma^{+}_{\\rm{g}})}$$""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07061 "isostructural reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07061 "A ligand exchange reaction, where the structural type of the metal complex remains the same. $$\\ce{ML_{n} + X -> ML_{n-1}X + L + \\Delta\\textit{E}}$$ Although these types of reactions are not necessarily isodesmic reactions, a substantial degree of error cancellation for the calculated energy, $\\Delta E$, is achieved because the coordination sphere of the metal atom $\\ce{M}$ is conserved.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07139 "interchange reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07139 "Reaction that results in an exchange of atoms or groups between a polymer and low-molar-mass molecules, between polymer molecules, or between sites within the same macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07155))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07155 "ion-exchange polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07155 "Polymer that is able to exchange ions (cations or anions) with ionic components in solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07175 "impact-modified polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07175 "Polymeric material whose impact resistance and toughness have been increased by the incorporation of phase microdomains of a rubbery material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07208))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07208 "intrinsically conducting polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07208 "Electrically conducting polymer composed of macromolecules having fully conjugated sequences of double bonds along the chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07210 "ionene")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07210 "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which ionized or ionic groups are parts of main chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07212))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07212 "ionic aggregates in an ionomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07212 "Domains enriched with ionic groups within an ionomer matrix.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07213))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07213 "ionic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07213 "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing ionic or ionizable groups, or both, irrespective of their nature, content, and location. Synonym: ion-containing polymer.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:IT07213 "ion-containing polymer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07215))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07215 "ionomer cluster")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07215 "Ionic aggregate, in a polymer matrix of low polarity, formed through interactions of ionomer multiplets.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07216 "ionomer multiplet")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07216 "Ionic aggregate, in a polymer matrix of low polarity, formed through the association of ion-pairs in an ionomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07233 "isomorphic polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07233 "Polymer blend of two or more different semi-crystalline polymers that are miscible in the crystalline state as well as in the molten state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07239 "immiscibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07239 "Inability of a mixture to form a single phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07240 "immiscible polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07240 "Polymer blend that exhibits immiscibility.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:IT07240 "heterogeneous polymer blend")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07246 "impregnation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07246 "Penetration of monomeric, oligomeric, or polymeric liquids into an assembly of fibres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07248 "intercalation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07248 "Process by which a substance becomes transferred into pre-existing spaces of molecular dimensions in a second substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07253 "interfacial adhesion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07253 "Adhesion in which interfaces between phases or components are maintained by intermolecular forces, chain entanglements, or both, across the interfaces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07396 "IC")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07396 "Frequently used acronym for internal conversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07397))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07397 "ICT")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07397 "Acronym for intramolecular charge transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07398 "intramolecular charge transfer emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07398 "Electronic emission from an ICT state.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:IT07398 "ICT emission")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07399))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07399 "infrared")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07399 "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from \\(780\\ \\rm{nm}\\) to about \\(20\\,000\\ \\rm{nm}\\).According to the CIE, the range between \\(780\\ \\rm{nm}\\) and \\(1\\ \\rm{mm}\\) is subdivided into:IR-A \\(780\\)–\\(1400\\ \\rm{nm}\\)IR-B \\(1.4\\)–\\(3\\ \\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)IR-C \\(3\\ \\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)–\\(1\\ \\rm{mm}\\)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07400 "intersection space")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07400 "(3N − 8)-dimensional subspace of the (3N − 6)-dimensional nuclear coordinate space consisting of an infinite number of conical intersection points (N is the number of nuclei).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07401 "intramolecular charge transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07401 "Process that changes the overall charge distribution in a molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07403 "ISC")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07403 "Frequently used acronym for intersystem crossing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07404 "isolampsic point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07404 "Same as isoemissive point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07514 "inorganic–organic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07514 "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure comprising inorganic and organic units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07515))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07515 "inorganic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07515 "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure that does not include carbon atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07624 "<i>in situ</i> composite formation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07624 "Process for preparing a polymer composite by  forming the filler or reinforcement in an existing polymer or  polymerizing monomers in the presence of dispersed filler.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:IT07625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:IT07625 "isostatic pressing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:IT07625 "Application of a hydrostatic pressure through a liquid to achieve densification prior to the production of a uniform compact monolith through ceramization of the densified liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03359))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03359 "<i>j</i>-value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03359 "An effective first order rate constant for a photochemical reaction (photodecomposition, etc.) of light-absorbing impurities in the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03360))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03360 "Jablonski diagram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03360 "Originally, a diagram showing that the fluorescent state of a molecular entity is the lowest excited state from which the transition to the ground state is allowed, whereas the phosphorescent state is a metastable state below the fluorescent state, which is reached by radiationless transition. In the most typical cases the fluorescent state is the lowest singlet excited state and the phosphorescent state the lowest triplet state, the ground state being a singlet. Presently, modified Jablonski diagrams are frequently used and are actually state diagrams in which molecular electronic states, represented by horizontal lines displaced vertically to indicate relative energies, are grouped according to multiplicity into horizontally displaced columns. Excitation and relaxation processes that interconvert states are indicated in the diagram by arrows. Radiative transitions are generally indicated with straight arrows (→), while radiationless transitions are generally indicated with wavy arrows ⇝.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03361 "Jahn–Teller effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03361 "Molecular distortions due to an electronically degenerate @G02704-1@ state. For non-linear molecular entities in a geometry described by a point symmetry group possessing degenerate irreducible representations there always exists at least one non-totally symmetric vibration that makes electronically degenerate states unstable at this geometry. The nuclei are displaced to new equilibrium positions of lower symmetry causing a splitting of the originally degenerate states (first-order Jahn–Teller effect).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03362))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03362 "Jahn–Teller transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03362 "A displacive crystal distortion to lower symmetry that cooperatively removes a localized-electron orbital degeneracy at an array of like atoms so as to leave the atoms in the centre of symmetry of their distorted sites. Example: The crystallographic distortion in $\\ce{Mn3O4}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03363))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03363 "joule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03363 "SI derived unit of energy, \\(\\pu{J} = \\pu{N m} = \\pu{kg m2 s-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03364))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03364 "junction point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03364 "Branch point in a polymer network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:J03365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:J03365 "junction unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:J03365 "A non-repeating atom or non-repeating group of atoms between blocks in a block macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:JT07602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:JT07602 "junction-point density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:JT07602 "Number of junction points per unit volume in a polymer network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03366 "κ")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03366 "An affix in the name of a polydentate chelate complex indicating single ligating atom attachments of a polyatomic ligand to a coordination centre. A right superscript numerical index indicates the number of such attachments.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03366 "kappa")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03367 "Kamlet–Taft solvent parameters")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03367 "Parameters of the Kamlet–Taft solvatochromic relationship which measure separately the hydrogen bond donor ($\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$), hydrogen bond acceptor ($\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$), and dipolarity/polarizability ($\\ce{\\uppi\\!^{\\ast}}$) properties of solvents as contributing to overall solvent polarity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03369 "Kaptein–Closs rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03369 "Rules used to predict the sign of CIDNP effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03370 "Kasha rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03370 "Polyatomic molecular entities luminesce with appreciable yield only from the lowest excited state of a given multiplicity. There are exceptions to this rule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03371 "Kasha–Vavilov rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03371 "The quantum yield of luminescence is independent of the wavelength of exciting radiation. There are exceptions to this rule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03371 "Vavilov rule")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03372 "katal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03372 "Unit for catalytic activity coherent with the SI, equal to the catalytic activity that catalyses a reaction rate of one mole per second in an assay system. \\(\\pu{1 kat} = \\pu{1 mol s-1}\\). The katal is recommended for use in the specific context of enzymes and clinical chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03373))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03373 "Kekulé structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03373 "A representation of an aromatic molecular entity (such as benzene), with fixed alternating single and double bonds, in which interactions between multiple bonds are assumed to be absent. For benzene:  are the Kekulé structures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03374))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03374 "kelvin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03374 "The kelvin, symbol \\(\\pu{K}\\), is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Boltzmann constant \\(k\\) to be \\(\\pu{1.380649E-23}\\) when expressed in the unit \\(\\pu{J K-1}\\), which is equal to \\(\\pu{kg m2 s-2 K-1}\\), where the kilogram, metre and second are defined in terms of h, c and ΔνCs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03376 "ketals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03376 """Acetals derived from ketones by replacement of the oxo group by two hydrocarbyloxy groups $\\ce{R2C(OR)2}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). This term, once abandoned, has been reinstated as a subclass of acetals.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03377 "ketazines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03377 "Azines of ketones $\\ce{R2C=NN=CR2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03378 "ketenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03378 "Compounds in which a carbonyl group is connected by a double bond to an alkylidene group $\\ce{R2C=C=O}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03379 "ketenimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03379 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=C=NR}$. Thus imino analogues of ketenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03381 "ketimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03381 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=NR'}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03383 "ketoaldonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03383 "Monosaccharides in which a structure containing a keto group and a carboxylic acid group is in equilibrium with a hemiacetal structure. Specific compounds are named using the -ulosonic acid suffix.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03384 "ketoaldoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03384 "Monosaccharides which contain both an aldehydic and a ketonic carbonyl group in equilibrium with intramolecular hemiacetal forms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03385 "keto carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03385 "The term has imprecisely been used to designate carboxylic acyl carbenes. keto carbenes are carbenes bearing an oxo function at an unspecified site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03386 "ketones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03386 "Compounds in which a carbonyl group is bonded to two carbon atoms $\\ce{R2C=O}$ (neither $\\ce{R}$ may be $\\ce{H}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03387 "ketoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03387 "Ketonic parent sugars (polyhydroxyketones $\\ce{H–[CHOH]_{n}–C(=O)[CHOH]_{m}–H}$ with three or more carbon atoms) and their intramolecular hemiacetals. The oxo group is usually at $\\ce{C-2}$. E.g. ᴅ-fructose:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03388 "ketoximes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03388 """Oximes of ketones, $\\ce{R2C=NOH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03389 "ketyls")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03389 "Radical anions (or the corresponding salts) derived from ketones by addition of an electron $\\ce{R2C^{.}–O^{−}  R2C^{−}–O^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03390 "kilo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03390 "SI prefix for \\(10^{3}\\) (symbol: \\(\\rm{k}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03391 "kilogram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03391 "The kilogram, symbol \\(\\pu{kg}\\), is the SI unit of mass. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Planck constant \\(h\\) to be \\(\\pu{6.62607015E-34}\\) when expressed in the unit \\(\\pu{J s}\\), which is equal to \\(\\pu{kg m2 s-1}\\), where the metre and the second are defined in terms of c and ΔνCs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03392))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03392 "kind-of-property")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03392 "An attribute of phenomena, bodies or substances that may be distinguished qualitatively. Examples: Colour (value: green, blue), transparency, length (value: long, short; \\(2\\ \\rm{m}\\), \\(5\\ \\rm{m}\\)), amount-of-substance (value: \\(\\pu{2 mol}\\), \\(\\pu{5 mol}\\)). Kind-of-property includes the concept kind-of-quantity. All kinds-of-property may be related to nominal (e.g. green, blue) and ordinal scales (e.g. small, large), but kinds-of-quantity are generally related to difference [e.g. \\(10\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\) (i.e. \\(10\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\) more than an arbitrary zero)] or ratio scales (length \\(2\\ \\rm{m}\\) or \\(5\\ \\rm{m}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03393))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03393 "kind-of-quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03393 "Abstract property common to several real quantities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03394 "kinematics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03394 "The study of the properties of motion that are independent of the nature of the forces. In reaction dynamics the term is often applied in particular to effects that occur when atomic masses are changed, the forces remaining the same.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03395 "kinematic viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03395 "Dynamic viscosity divided by the density of the fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03396 "kinetic activity factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03396 "A factor involving activity coefficients that appears as a multiplier in the rate equation. For a bimolecular reaction the factor is \\[\\frac{y_{\\rm{A}}\\ y_{\\rm{B}}}{y_{\\ddagger}}\\] where \\(y_{\\rm{A}}\\), \\(y_{\\rm{B}}\\) and \\(y_{\\ddagger }\\) are the activity coefficients for the reactants A and B and the activated complex, respectively. For a unimolecular reaction the factor is \\[\\frac{y_{\\rm{A}}}{y_{\\ddagger}}\\] and for a trimolecular reaction it is \\[\\frac{y_{\\rm{A}}\\ y_{\\rm{B}}\\ y_{\\rm{C}}}{y_{\\ddagger}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03398 "kinetic control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03398 "The term characterizes conditions (including reaction times) that lead to reaction products in a proportion governed by the relative rates of the parallel (forward) reactions in which the products are formed, rather than by the respective overall equilibrium constants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03399))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03399 "kinetic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03399 "A faradaic current that corresponds to the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance formed by a prior chemical reaction from another substance that is not electroactive, and that is partially or entirely controlled by the rate of that reaction. The reaction may be heterogeneous, occurring at an electrode-solution interface (surface reaction), or it may be homogeneous, occurring at some distance from the interface (volume reaction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03401 "kinetic electrolyte effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03401 "The general effect of an added electrolyte (i.e. an effect other than, or in addition to, that due to its possible involvement as a reactant or catalyst) on the observed rate constant of a reaction in solution. At low concentrations (when only long-range coulombic forces need to be considered) the effect on a given reaction is determined only by the ionic strength of the solution and not by the chemical identity of the ions. For practical purposes, this concentration range is roughly the same as the region of validity of the Debye–Hückel limiting law for activity coefficients. At higher concentrations, the effect of an added electrolyte depends also on the chemical identity of the ions. Such specific action can usually be interpreted as the incursion of a reaction path involving an ion of the electrolyte as reactant or catalyst, in which case the action is not properly to be regarded just as a kinetic electrolyte effect. Kinetic electrolyte effects are usually (too restrictively and therefore incorrectly) referred to as 'kinetic salt effects'. A kinetic electrolyte effect ascribable solely to the influence of the ionic strength on activity coefficients of ionic reactants and transition states is called a 'primary kinetic electrolyte effect'. A kinetic electrolyte effect arising from the influence of the ionic strength of the solution upon the pre-equilibrium concentration of an ionic species that is involved in a subsequent rate-limiting step of a reaction is called a 'secondary kinetic electrolyte effect'. A common case encountered in practice is the effect on the concentration of hydrogen ion (acting as catalyst) produced from the ionization of a weak acid in a buffer solution. Synonymous with kinetic equivalence.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03401 "kinetic ionic-strength effect")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03401 "salt effect")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03401 "kinetic salt effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03402 "kinetic energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03402 "Energy of motion: for a body of mass \\(m\\), \\[E_{\\rm{k}}=\\frac{m\\ v^{2}}{2}\\] where \\(v\\) is the speed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03403 "kinetic equivalence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03403 "Two reaction schemes are kinetically equivalent if they imply the same rate law. For example, consider the two schemes (i) and (ii) for the formation of $\\ce{C}$ from $\\ce{A}$: \\[(\\rm{i}) \\qquad \\ce{A [k_{1},\\ce{OH^{-}}][k_{-1},\\ce{OH^{-}}] B ->[k_{2}] C}\\] Providing that $\\ce{B}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate. \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{k_{1} k_{2}[\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{k_{2} + k_{-1} [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] \\[(\\rm{ii}) \\qquad \\ce{A [k_{1}][k_{-1}] B ->[k_{2}][\\ce{OH^{-}}] C}\\] Providing that $\\ce{B}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{k_{1} k_{2}[\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{k_{-1} + k_{2} [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] Both equations for \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t}\\) are of the form: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{r [\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{1 + s [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] where \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are constants (sometimes called 'coefficients in the rate equation'). The equations are identical in their dependence on concentrations and do not distinguish whether $\\ce{OH^{−}}$ catalyses the formation of $\\ce{B}$, and necessarily also its reversion to $\\ce{A}$, or is involved in its further transformation to $\\ce{C}$. The two schemes are therefore kinetically equivalent under conditions to which the stated provisos apply.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03403 "kinetic ambiguity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03405 "kinetic isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03405 "The effect of isotopic substitution on a rate constant is referred to as a kinetic isotope effect. For example, in the reaction:  the effect of isotopic substitution in reactant $\\ce{A}$ is expressed as the ratio of rate constants \\(\\frac{k^{\\rm{l}}}{k^{\\rm{h}}}\\), where the superscripts \\(\\rm{l}\\) and \\(\\rm{h}\\) represent reactions in which the molecules $\\ce{A}$ contain the light and heavy isotopes, respectively. Within the framework of transition state theory in which the reaction is rewritten as:  and with neglect of isotopic mass on tunnelling and the transmission coefficient, \\(\\frac{k^{\\rm{l}}}{k^{\\rm{h}}}\\) can be regarded as if it were the equilibrium constant for an isotope exchange reaction between the transition state $\\ce{[TS]^{\\ddagger}}$ and the isotopically substituted reactant $\\ce{A}$, and calculated from their vibrational frequencies as in the case of a thermodynamic isotope effect. Isotope effects like the above, involving a direct or indirect comparison of the rates of reaction of isotopologues, are called 'intermolecular', in contrast to intramolecular isotope effects, in which a single substrate reacts to produce a non-statistical distribution of isotopomeric product molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03406))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03406 "kinetic method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03406 "An analytical method in which the rate of a reaction or a related quantity is measured and utilized to determine concentrations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03407))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03407 "kinetic resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03407 "The achievement of partial or complete resolution by virtue of unequal rates of reaction of the enantiomers in a racemate with a chiral agent (reagent, catalyst, solvent, etc.).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03407 "asymmetric destruction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03410 "<i>klado</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03410 "An affix used in names to designate a very open polyboron structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03411 "Koopmans' theorem")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03411 "Directly relates experimental ionization potentials with energy levels of molecular orbitals. The theorem states that the ionization potential required to remove an electron from the orbital \\(\\Psi _{\\rm{i}}\\) is given by the negative value of the energy of the orbital, \\(-\\varepsilon_{\\rm{i}}\\), as calculated within the Hartree–Fock approximation. The theorem is not applied to localized molecular orbitals, which are not eigenfunctions of the effective hamiltonian.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03412 "Koppel–Palm solvent parameters")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03412 "Parameters to measure separately the ability of a solvent to enter into non-specific solvent–solute interactions (permittivity \\(\\varepsilon\\) and refractive index \\(\\eta _{\\rm{D}}\\)) and specific solvent–solute interaction (solvent basicity or nucleophilicity \\(B\\) and solvent acidity or electrophilicity \\(E\\)) as contributing to overall solvent polarity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03415 "Krafft point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03415 "The temperature (more precisely, narrow temperature range) above which the solubility of a surfactant rises sharply. At this temperature the solubility of the surfactant becomes equal to the critical micelle concentration. It is best determined by locating the abrupt change in slope of a graph of the logarithm of the solubility against \\(t\\) or \\(\\frac{1}{T}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03416 "Kratky plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03416 "A diagrammatic representation of scattering data on large particles, obtained at different angles but at the same concentration, constructed by plotting \\((\\sin \\frac{\\theta }{2})^{2}\\ \\Delta R\\left(\\theta \\right)\\) vs. \\((\\sin \\frac{\\theta}{2})^{2}\\), or \\(q^{2}\\ \\Delta R\\left(\\theta \\right)\\) vs. \\(q\\) and used for the determination of molecular shape. \\(\\Delta R(\\theta)\\) is the excess Rayleigh ratio, \\(P(\\theta)\\) the particle scattering function, \\(\\theta \\) the scattering angle and \\(q\\) the length of the scattering vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03417 "krypton ion laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03417 "A CW or pulsed laser emitting lines from \\(337\\) to \\(859\\ \\rm{nm}\\) from singly ionized krypton. Principal emissions are at \\(530.9\\), \\(568.2\\), \\(647.1\\) and \\(752.5\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03418 "lambda")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03418 "Signifies, with its superscript, the bonding number, i.e. the sum of the number of skeletal bonds to an atom and the number of hydrogen atoms associated with it in a parent hydride, real or hypothetical. Its usage in names of compounds is generally restricted to non-standard valence states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03422))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03422 "<i>l</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03422 "An obsolete prefix for (−)-,See: d, l, dlFor like and unlike diastereoisomersSee: l, u")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03423 "<i>l</i>, <i>u</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03423 "Stereodescriptors of diastereoisomers with two chirality elements. When the two chirality elements are both R or both S the molecular entity is described as having an l relationship (l = like), when one chirality element is R and the other S the molecular entity is described as having a u relationship (u = unlike). The notation can be extended to molecules with more than two chirality elements whereby the l, u relationships of the chiral elements are considered in pairs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03424 "label")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03424 "A marker, tag or indicator distinguishable by the observer but not by the system and used to identify a tracer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03425 "labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03425 "Providing a substance with a label.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03432 "labile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03432 "The term has loosely been used to describe a relatively unstable and transient chemical species or (less commonly) a relatively stable but reactive species. It must therefore not be used without explanation of the intended meaning.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03433 "laboratory sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03433 "The sample or subsample(s) sent to or received by the laboratory. When the laboratory sample is further prepared (reduced) by subdividing, mixing, grinding or by combinations of these operations, the result is the test sample. When no preparation of the laboratory sample is required, the laboratory sample is the test sample. A test portion is removed from the test sample for the performance of the test or for analysis. The laboratory sample is the final sample from the point of view of sample collection but it is the initial sample from the point of view of the laboratory. Several laboratory samples may be prepared and sent to different laboratories or to the same laboratory for different purposes. When sent to the same laboratory, the set is generally considered as a single laboratory sample and is documented as a single sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03434))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03434 "lachrymator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03434 "A substance which produces a flow of tears in a person or animal (e.g. acetyl chloride, acrylaldehyde, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03435))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03435 "lactams")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03435 "Cyclic amides of amino carboxylic acids, having a 1-azacycloalkan-2-one structure, or analogues having unsaturation or heteroatoms replacing one or more carbon atoms of the ring. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03436 "lactides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03436 """Cyclic esters derived by multiple esterification between two (usually) or more molecules of lactic acid or other hydroxy carboxylic acids.\r
 They are designated as dilactides, trilactides, etc. according to the number of hydroxy acid residues, e.g. a dilactide (a 1,4-dioxane-2,5-dione):""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03437 "lactims")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03437 "Tautomeric forms of lactams, having an endocyclic carbon-nitrogen double bond. Thus, cyclic carboximidic acids, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03438 "lactols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03438 "Cyclic hemiacetals formed by intramolecular addition of a hydroxy group to an aldehydic or ketonic carbonyl group. They are thus 1-oxacy-cycloalkan-2-ols or unsaturated analogues, e.g. [obsolete] This term has also been used for hydroxy lactones, but such use is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03439 "lactones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03439 "Cyclic esters of hydroxy carboxylic acids, containing a 1-oxacycloalkan-2-one structure, or analogues having unsaturation or heteroatoms replacing one or more carbon atoms of the ring.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03440 "ladder chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03440 "A double-strand chain, consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings, with adjacent rings having two or more atoms in common. Alternatively, a ladder chain is a double-strand chain with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through four atoms, two on one side and two on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03441 "ladder macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03441 "A double-strand macromolecule consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings, with adjacent rings having two or more atoms in common. Alternatively, a ladder macromolecule is a double-strand macromolecule with constitutional unit joined to each other through four atoms, two on one side and two on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03443 "lag phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03443 "The growth interval (adaption phase) between inoculation and start of the exponential phase during which there is little or no growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03445))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03445 "Lambert law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03445 "The fraction of light absorbed by a system is independent of the incident radiant power (\\(P_{\\lambda }^{0}\\)). This law holds only if \\(P_{\\lambda }^{0}\\) is small, scattering is negligible, and multiphoton processes, excited state populations, and photochemical reactions are negligible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03446 "lamellar crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03446 "A type of crystal with a large extension in two dimensions and a uniform thickness. A lamellar crystal is usually of a thickness in the \\(5{-}50\\ \\rm{nm}\\) range, and it may be found individually or in aggregates. The parallel chain stems intersect the lamellar plane at an angle between \\(45^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and \\(90^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\). The lamellae often have pyramidal shape owing to differences in the fold domains; as a result, one can deduce different fold planes and fold surfaces from the lamellar morphology.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03447 "lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03447 "A source of incoherent radiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03447 "light source")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03448))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03448 "lamp black")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03448 "A special type of carbon black produced by incomplete combustion of a fuel rich in aromatics that is burned in flat pans. lamp black is characterized by a relatively broad particle size distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03450 "Landolt reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03450 "A slow chemical reaction, in which the formation of a product is delayed by a suitable reagent added for the purpose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03451 "Langmuir–Hinshelwood mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03451 "A mechanism for surface catalysis in which the reaction occurs between species that are adsorbed on the surface is often known as a Langmuir–Hinshelwood mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03453 "Langmuir–Rideal mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03453 "A mechanism for surface catalysis in which the reaction occurs between a reactant molecule in the gas phase and one that is adsorbed on the surface.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03453 "Rideal–Eley mechanism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03454))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03454 "Laporte rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03454 "This rule states that for monophotonic radiative transitions in centrosymmetric systems, the only non-vanishing electric-dipole transition moments are those which connect an even term (g) with an odd term (u).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03455 "lapse rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03455 "The variation of an atmospheric variable with height; unless otherwise stated the variable is temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03457 "large particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03457 "A particle with dimensions comparable with the wavelength of the radiation in the medium or larger. In practice a particle must be treated as large if its largest dimension exceeds about one-twentieth of the wavelength employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03458 "lariat ethers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03458 "Crown ethers having a side chain that holds one or more additional coordinating sites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03459))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03459 "laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03459 "A source of ultraviolet, visible, or infrared radiation which produces light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation from which the acronym is derived. The light emitted is coherent except for superradiance emission. The essential elements of a laser are: 1, an active medium; 2, a pumping process to make a population inversion; and 3, suitable geometry of optical feedback elements. The active medium consists of a host material (gas, liquid or solid) containing an active species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03461))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03461 "laser beam ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03461 "Ionization by irradiation of a specimen with a laser beam.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03469 "laser ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03469 "Occurs when a sample is irradiated with a laser beam. In the irradiation of gaseous samples, ionization occurs via a single- or multi-photon process. In the case of solid samples, ionization occurs via a thermal process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03473 "laser micro emission spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03473 "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam (diameter less than \\(10\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) in the ultraviolet or visible range under conditions of vapourization and thermal excitation of electronic states of sample material and in which the photon emission spectrum is observed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03474))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03474 "laser micro mass spectrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03474 "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam (diameter less than \\(10\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) in the ultraviolet or visible range under conditions of vapourization and ionization of sample material and in which the ions generated are recorded with a mass spectrometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03477))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03477 "laser Raman microanalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03477 "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam (diameter less than \\(10\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) in the ultraviolet or visible range and the intensity versus wavelength function of the Raman radiation is recorded yielding information about vibrational states of the excited substance and therefore also about functional groups and chemical bonding.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03479))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03479 "lasing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03479 "The process of light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03481 "latent image")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03481 "The primary result of radiation absorption in a photoimaging system which is susceptible to development.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03482))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03482 "lateral order")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03482 "Order in the side-by-side packing of the molecules of a linear polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03483))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03483 "lateral resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03483 "For qualitative analysis, this should be defined as the minimum distance of two points (areas) on the specimen corresponding to signal levels of 16 and 84%. For electron signals originating from top surface layers like secondary or Auger electrons and secondary ions the lateral resolution for qualitative purposes corresponds to the beam diameter. For signals originating in a greater depth of the sample (back scattered electrons, X-rays) the lateral resolution is worse than the corresponding value of the beam diameter due to the diffusion of the primary electrons. The lateral resolution may be determined exactly with a sandwich specimen or a sharp edge specimen, or approximately with a specimen showing a regular microstructure of known dimensions. Recommended abbreviation: Lat. Res. (qualitative); unit: \\(\\rm{m}\\); range: \\(\\rm{nm}\\) or \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC} \\rm{m}\\). For quantitative analysis, this should be defined as the minimum distance of two points (areas) on the specimen corresponding to signal ratios of \\(10^{4}\\). This condition assures that the value for the lateral resolution defines the diameter of the analytical area which yields the total analytical signal. Due to the effect of electron diffusion in a solid and 'tail effects' in ion beam analysis the quantitative lateral resolution is significantly larger than the beam diameter. Recommended abbreviation: Lat. Res. (quantitative); unit: \\(\\rm{m}\\); range: \\(\\rm{nm}\\) or \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC} \\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03484 "latex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03484 "An emulsion or sol in which each colloidal particle contains a number of macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03485 "lath crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03485 "A lamellar crystal prevailingly extended along one lateral dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03486 "lattice distortion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03486 "Structural disorder resulting from misalignment of the unit cells within the crystals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03487 "laws of distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03487 "During the formation of a mixed crystal from a solution containing two components 'A' and 'B', the latter may be distributed according to the equation \\[K_{\\rm{A},\\rm{B}}=\\frac{b\\ (a_{0}- a)}{a\\ (b_{0}- b)}\\] In this homogeneous distribution, \\(a_{0}\\) and \\(b_{0}\\) are the respective concentrations in the solution before crystallization and \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the respective concentrations in the solution after crystallization. \\(K_{\\rm{A},\\rm{B}}\\) is usually called the separation factor. The term homogeneous distribution coefficient is not recommended. Alternatively the distribution of the micro-component may follow the equation of Doerner and Hoskins \\[\\ln (\\frac{a_{0}}{a}) = \\lambda \\ \\ln (\\frac{b_{0}}{b})\\] (logarithmic distribution) where \\(\\lambda \\) is usually called the logarithmic distribution coefficient, the meaning of the other symbols remaining the same. Exactly homogeneous or logarithmic distributions are extreme cases and very seldom encountered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03488 "layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03488 "Any conceptual region of space restricted in one dimension, within or at the surface of a condensed phase or a film. The usage of the term 'film' for an adsorption layer is confusing and is discouraged. The term double-layer applies to layers approximated by two 'distinct' sublayers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03489))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03489 "layer equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03489 "Saturation of the stationary phase with the mobile phase via the vapour phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03491 "leader sequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03491 "A polynucleotide region between promoter and structural gene, necessary for the correct transcription of DNA into mRNA.An N-terminal extension of polypeptides (also known as the signal sequence) which is necessary for the transport of the protein into or through the membrane or for its secretion into the extracellular medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03492 "least-squares technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03492 "A procedure for replacing the discrete set of results obtained from an experiment by a continuous function. It is defined by the following. For the set of variables \\(y,x_{0},x_{1},\\,...\\) there are \\(n\\) measured values such as \\(y_{i},x_{0i},x_{1i},\\,...\\) and it is decided to write a relation: \\[y = f\\left(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K};x_{0},x_{1},\\,...\\right)\\] where \\(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K}\\) are undetermined constants. If it is assumed that each measurement \\(y_{i}\\) of \\(y\\) has associated with it a number \\(w_{i}^{-1}\\) characteristic of the uncertainty, then numerical estimates of the \\(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K}\\) are found by constructing a variable \\(S\\), defined by \\[S = \\sum_{i}(w_{i}\\ (y_{i}- f_{i}))^{2}\\] and solving the equations obtained by writing \\[\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial a_{j}}\\ \\overset{˜}{a}_{j}=0\\] \\(\\overset{˜}{a}_{j}=\\rm{all}\\:\\, a\\) except \\(a_{j}\\). If the relations between the \\(a\\) and \\(y\\) are linear, this is the familiar least-squares technique of fitting an equation to a number of experimental points. If the relations between the \\(a\\) and \\(y\\) are non-linear, there is an increase in the difficulty of finding a solution, but the problem is essentially unchanged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03493 "leaving group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03493 "An atom or group (charged or uncharged) that becomes detached from an atom in what is considered to be the residual or main part of the substrate in a specified reaction. For example, in the heterolytic solvolysis of benzyl bromide in acetic acid:  the leaving group is $\\ce{Br^{−}}$; in the reaction:  the leaving group is $\\ce{NMe3}$ in the electrophilic nitration of benzene, it is $\\ce{H^{+}}$. The term has meaning only in relation to a specified reaction. The leaving group is not, in general, the same as the substituent group present in the substrate (e.g. bromo and trimethylammonio in the substrates of the first two examples above). A slightly different usage of the term prevails in the (non-mechanistic) naming of transformations, where the actual substituent group present in the substrate (and also in the product) is referred to as the leaving group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03494 "lecithins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03494 "Choline esters of phosphatidic acids. Specific compounds should be named systematically.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03495 "lectins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03495 "Glycoproteins isolated from plants but recently found also in animals and microorganisms that react specifically with terminal glycosidic residues of other molecules (e.g. cell wall polysaccharides); some causing cells to agglutinate (cf. agglutination).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03497 "left-to-right convention")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03497 "Arrangement of the structural formulae of the reactants so that the bonds to be made or broken form a linear array in which the electrons move from left to right.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03498))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03498 "length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03498 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based. Special symbols are used for different types of lengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03499 "lethal concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03499 "Concentration of a potentially toxic substance in an environmental medium that causes death following a certain period of exposure (denoted by LC).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03500 "lethal dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03500 "Amount of a substance or physical agent (radiation) that causes death when taken into the body by a single absorption (denoted by LD).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03501 "lethal synthesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03501 "Metabolic formation of a highly toxic compound from one that is relatively non-toxic (bioactivation), often leading to death of affected cells. Synonymous with suicide metabolism.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03501 "suicide metabolism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03502 "leuco bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03502 "Colourless compounds formed by reduction of triphenylmethane dyes. Generally, they are amino or hydroxy derivatives of triphenylmethane. Use of this class name, which is more or less parochial to the technology of dyes, is not endorsed. E.g. $\\ce{(p-Me2NC6H4)2CHPh}$, the leuco base of Malachite Green.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03503 "leuco compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03503 "Dihydroxy polycyclic aromatic compounds which on oxidation form polycyclic quinone dyes (intermediates in vat dyeing). Use of this class name, which is more or less parochial to the technology of dyes, is not endorsed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03504 "leukotrienes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03504 "Linear $\\ce{C20}$ endogenous metabolites of arachidonic acid (icosa-5,8,11,14-tetraenoic acid) containing a terminal carboxy function and four or more double bonds (three or more of which are conjugated) as well as other functional groups. A subclass of icosanoids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03505 "level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03505 "Logarithm of the ratio of a quantity and a reference value of that quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03506 "levelling effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03506 "The tendency of a solvent to make all Brønsted acids whose acidity exceeds a certain value appear equally acidic. It is due to the complete transfer to a protophilic solvent of a hydron from a dissolved acid stronger than the conjugate acid of the solvent. The only acid present to any significant extent in all such solutions is the lyonium ion. For example, the solvent water has a levelling effect on the acidities of $\\ce{HClO4}$, $\\ce{HCl}$ and $\\ce{HI}$: aqueous solutions of these acids at the same (moderately low) concentrations have the same acidities. A corresponding levelling effect applies to strong bases protogenic solvents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03507 "level width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03507 "Planck constant over \\(2\\ \\pi\\) divided by the mean lifetime.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03508 "Lewis acid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03508 "A molecular entity (and the corresponding chemical species) that is an electron-pair acceptor and therefore able to react with a Lewis base to form a Lewis adduct, by sharing the electron pair furnished by the Lewis base. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03509 "Lewis acidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03509 "The thermodynamic tendency of a substrate to act as a Lewis acid. Comparative measures of this property are provided by the equilibrium constants for Lewis adduct formation of a series of Lewis acids with a common reference Lewis base.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03510 "Lewis adduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03510 "The adduct formed between a Lewis acid and a Lewis base.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03511 "Lewis base")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03511 "A molecular entity (and the corresponding chemical species) able to provide a pair of electrons and thus capable of coordination to a Lewis acid, thereby producing a Lewis adduct.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03512))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03512 "Lewis basicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03512 "The thermodynamic tendency of a substance to act as a Lewis base. Comparative measures of this property are provided by the equilibrium constants for Lewis adduct formation for a series of Lewis bases with a common reference Lewis acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03513 "Lewis formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03513 "Molecular structure in which the valency electrons are shown as dots so placed between the bonded atoms that one pair of dots represents two electrons or one covalent (single) bond, e.g.  A double bond is represented by two pairs of dots, etc. Dots representing non-bonded outer-shell electrons are placed adjacent to the atoms with which they are associated, but not between the atoms. Formal charges (e.g. +, −, 2+, etc.) are attached to atoms to indicate the difference between the positive nuclear charge (atomic number) and the total number of electrons (including those in the inner shells), on the formal basis that bonding electrons are shared equally between atoms they join. (Bonding pairs of electrons are usually denoted by lines, representing covalent bonds, as in line formulae.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03514 "<span class=\"s\">LIDAR</span>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03514 "An acronym for Light Detection And Ranging. It is a real time remote sensing technique for the determination of aerosol and trace gas concentrations in air by measurement of scattered laser radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03515))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03515 "lifetime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03515 "Lifetime of a molecular entity, which decays by first-order kinetics, is the time needed for a concentration of the entity to decrease to \\(1/\\rm{e}\\) of its original value, i.e., \\(c(t = \\tau) = \\frac{c(t\\,=\\,0)}{e}\\). Statistically, it represents the life expectation of the entity. It is equal to the reciprocal of the sum of the first-order rate constants of all processes causing the decay of the molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03516 "lifetime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03516 "The time required for the luminescence intensity to decay from some initial value to \\(\\frac{1}{\\rm{e}}\\) of that value (\\(\\rm{e} = 2.718\\,...\\)). Lifetimes can be measured by 'phase fluorimetry' (phosphorimetry) where the phase shift between the sinusoidally modulated exciting light and the emitted light is measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03517 "ligand field splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03517 "The removal of a degeneracy of atomic or molecular levels in a molecule or ion with a given symmetry, induced by the attachment or removal of ligands to produce reduced symmetries.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03518 "ligands")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03518 "In an inorganic coordination entity, the atoms or groups joined to the central atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03519 "ligand to ligand charge transfer transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03519 "An electronic transition of a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer between two ligands has occurred.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03519 "LLCT transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03520))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03520 "ligand to metal charge transfer transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03520 "An electronic transition in a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer from a ligand to a metal centre has occurred.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03520 "LMCT transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03521 "ligases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03521 "Enzymes that catalyse the ligation of two molecules with concomitant hydrolysis of the pyrophosphate bond in adenosine 5'-triphosphate (ATP) or a similar triphosphate, forming $\\ce{C–C}$, $\\ce{C–O}$, $\\ce{C–S}$, $\\ce{P–O}$ or $\\ce{C–N}$ bonds. In recombinant DNA technology, ligases covalently join together two sequences of DNA (e.g. host DNA and foreign DNA) by a phosphodiester bond.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03521 "synthetases")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03522 "ligate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03522 "To coordinate to a central atom as a ligand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03523 "light-atom anomaly")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03523 "A dynamical effect that arises for a process $$\\ce{A + B-C -> A-B + C}$$ when the species $\\ce{A}$ is light (e.g. a hydrogen atom) compared to $\\ce{B}$ and $\\ce{C}$. The vibrational excitation of the product $\\ce{A–B}$ is low, since the light atom $\\ce{A}$ approaches to within the bonding distance of $\\ce{BC}$ before the $\\ce{C}$ atom retreats. The energy of reaction is therefore released as repulsion between $\\ce{A–B}$ and $\\ce{C}$, with the result that there is translational excitation of the products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03524 "light polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03524 "A light beam is said to be linearly polarized if the end-point of the electric vector moves in a straight line when viewed along the direction of propagation of the beam. If it moves along a circle the beam is circularly polarized and if it moves along an ellipse the beam is elliptically polarized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03525 "light scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03525 "The redirection of a light beam due to interactions with molecules (Rayleigh and Raman scattering) and aerosols (Mie scattering). Scattered light received at any point in the atmosphere (sometimes called sky radiation) is a very important component of the total radiation received from the sun; it is dependent on the solar zenith angle, elevation aerosol concentration etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03527 "lignans")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03527 "Plant products of low molecular weight formed primarily from oxidative coupling of two p-propylphenol moieties at their $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}carbon atoms}$; products with units coupled in other ways are neolignans, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03528 "lignins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03528 "Macromolecular constituents of wood related to lignans, composed of phenolic propylbenzene skeletal units, linked at various sites and apparently randomly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03529))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03529 "limiting adsorption current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03529 "The potential-independent value that is approached by an adsorption current as the rate of reduction or oxidation of the electroactive substance is increased by varying the applied potential. The terms adsorption current and limiting adsorption current should not be applied to faradaic currents that have been increased or decreased by adding a non-electroactive surfactant to a solution containing an electroactive substance, nor to apparent waves resulting from the effect of adsorption or desorption on double-layer currents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03530 "limiting catalytic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03530 "The potential-independent value that is approached by a catalytic current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03531 "limiting condition of operation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03531 "Range of physical and operational parameters in which the method meets given values of performance characteristics with 95% probability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03532 "limiting current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03532 "The limiting value of a faradaic current that is approached as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the potential. It is independent of the applied potential over a finite range, and is usually evaluated by subtracting the appropriate residual current from the measured total current. A limiting current may have the character of an adsorption, catalytic, diffusion, or kinetic current, and may include a migration current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03534 "limiting diffusion current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03534 "The potential-independent value that is approached by a diffusion current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03535 "limiting kinetic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03535 "The potential-independent value that is approached by a kinetic current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03537 "limiting migration current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03537 "The limiting value of a migration current, which is approached as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03540 "limit of detection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03540 "The limit of detection, expressed as the concentration, \\(c_{\\rm{L}}\\), or the quantity, \\(q_{\\rm{L}}\\), is derived from the smallest measure, \\(x_{\\rm{L}}\\), that can be detected with reasonable certainty for a given analytical procedure. The value of \\(x_{\\rm{L}}\\) is given by the equation \\[x_{\\rm{L}}=\\overline{x}_{\\rm{bi}}+k\\ s_{\\rm{bi}}\\] where \\(\\overline{x}_{\\rm{bi}}\\) is the mean of the blank measures, \\(s_{\\rm{bi}}\\) is the standard deviation of the blank measures, and \\(k\\) is a numerical factor chosen according to the confidence level desired.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03541 "limit test")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03541 "Acute toxicity test in which, if no ill-effects occur at a pre-selected maximum dose, no further testing at greater exposure levels is required.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03546 "linear chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03546 "A chain with no branch points intermediate between the boundary units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03547 "linear copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03547 "A copolymer composed of linear macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03549 "linear electron accelerator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03549 "An evacuated metal tube in which electrons pass through a series of small gaps (usually in the form of cavity resonators in the high frequency range) so arranged and spaced that, at a specific excitation frequency, the stream of electrons on passing through successive gaps gains additional energy from the electric field in each gap.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03550 "linear energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03550 "The average energy locally imparted to a medium by a charged particle of specified energy, per unit distance traversed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03551 "linear free-energy relation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03551 "A linear correlation between the logarithm of a rate constant or equilibrium constant for one series of reactions and the logarithm of the rate constant or equilibrium constant for a related series of reactions. Typical examples of such relations (also known as linear Gibbs energy relations) are the Brønsted relation, and the Hammett equation.The name arises because the logarithm of an equilibrium constant (at constant temperature and pressure) is proportional to a standard free energy (Gibbs energy) change, and the logarithm of a rate constant is a linear function of the free energy (Gibbs energy) of activation.It has been suggested that this name should be replaced by linear Gibbs energy relation, but at present there is little sign of acceptance of this change. The area of physical organic chemistry which deals with such relations is commonly referred to as 'Linear Free-Energy Relationships'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03551 "linear Gibbs energy relation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03553 "linearity of responsivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03553 "The extent to which the output of the detector is directly proportional to the incident radiant power at a given wavelength and at constant irradiation geometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03554 "linear macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03554 "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the multiple repetition in linear sequence of units derived, actually or conceptually, from molecules of low relative molecular mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03555 "linear polarizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03555 "An optical device which allows the transmission of radiation of which the electric vector is restricted to one plane resulting in linearly polarized radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03556 "linear polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03556 "A polymer composed of linear macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03557 "linear pulse amplifier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03557 "A pulse amplifier which, within the limits of its normal operating characteristics, delivers an output pulse of amplitude proportional to that of the input pulse.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03558 "linear range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03558 "Concentration range over which the intensity of the signal obtained is directly proportional to the concentration of the species producing the signal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03559 "linear solvation energy relationships")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03559 "Equations involving the application of solvent parameters in linear or multiple (linear) regression expressing the solvent effect on the rate or equilibrium constant of a reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03560 "linear strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03560 "Change of length divided by the original length.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03560 "relative elongation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03561 "line formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03561 "A two-dimensional representation of molecular entities in which atoms are shown joined by lines representing single or multiple bonds, without any indication or implication concerning the spatial direction of bonds. For example, methanol is represented as:  (The term should not be confused with the representation of chemical formulae by the 'Wiswesser line notation', a method of string notation. Formulae in this notation are also known as 'Wiswesser line formulae'.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03562 "lineic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03562 "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by length. Lineic mass of a thread is the mass of the thread divided by its length.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03563 "line-of-centres model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03563 "A form of the collision theory of chemical reactions in which the assumption is made that reaction can only occur if on collision the component of energy along the line of centres of the masses of the colliding species is greater than a particular threshold energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03564 "line repetition groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03564 "The possible symmetries of arrays extending in one direction with a fixed repeating distance. linear polymer chains in the crystalline state must belong to one of the line repetition groups. Permitted symmetry elements are: the identity operation (symbol $\\rm{l}$); the translation along the chain axis (symbol t); the mirror plane orthogonal to the chain axis (symbol \\(m\\)) and that containing the chain axis (symbol \\(d\\)); the glide plane containing the chain axis (symbol \\(c\\)); the inversion centre, placed on the chain axis (symbol \\(i\\)); the two-fold axis orthogonal to the chain axis (symbol 2); the helical, or screw, symmetry where the axis of the helix coincides with the chain axis. In the latter case, the symbol is \\(\\rm{s}\\left(A*M/N\\right)\\), where \\(\\rm{s}\\) stands for the screw axis, \\(A\\) is the class of the helix, * and / are separators, and \\(M\\) is the integral number of residues contained in \\(N\\) turns, corresponding to the identity period (\\(M\\) and \\(N\\) must be prime to each other). The class index \\(A\\) may be dropped if deemed unnecessary, so that the helix may also be simply denoted as \\(\\rm{s}\\left(M/N\\right)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03565 "line-shape analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03565 "Determination of rate constants for a chemical exchange from the shapes of spectroscopic lines of dynamic processes. The method is most often used in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03566 "Lineweaver–Burk plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03566 "A plot of the reciprocal of velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction (ordinate) versus the reciprocal of substrate concentration (abscissa). The plot is used to graphically define the maximum velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction and the Michaelis constant for the enzyme.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03567 "line width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03567 "Width of a spectral line in terms of wavelength, frequency or wavenumber. Usually defined as full or half width of the line at half of the maximum intensity (FWHM or HWHM, respectively).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03568 "line width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03568 "The full width at half maximum peak-height. The natural line width is the theoretical value of the full width at half maximum of the nuclear transition, usually calculated from lifetime data.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03569 "linked scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03569 "A scan, in an instrument comprising two or more analysers, in which two or more of the analyser fields are scanned simultaneously so as to preserve a pre-determined relationship between parameters characterizing these fields. Often, these parameters are the field strengths, but they may also be the frequencies in the case of analysers in which alternating fields are employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03570 "lipid film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03570 "Established term for W/O/W films (i.e. oil film in water).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03571 "lipids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03571 "A loosely defined term for substances of biological origin that are soluble in nonpolar solvents. They consist of saponifiable lipids, such as glycerides (fats and oils) and phospholipids, as well as nonsaponifiable lipids, principally steroids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03572 "lipophilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03572 "Literally 'fat-loving'. Applied to molecular entities (or parts of molecular entities) having a tendency to dissolve in fat-like (e.g. hydrocarbon) solvents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03573 "lipophobic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03573 "Literally, 'fat-rejecting'; the converse of lipophilic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03574 "lipopolysaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03574 "Natural compounds consisting of a trisaccharide repeating unit (two heptose units and octulosonic acid) with oligosaccharide side chains and 3-hydroxytetradecanoic acid units (they are a major constituent of the cell walls of gram-negative bacteria).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03575 "lipoproteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03575 "Clathrate complexes consisting of a lipid enwrapped in a protein host without covalent binding in such a way that the complex has a hydrophilic outer surface consisting of all the protein and the polar ends of any phospholipids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03576 "liposome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03576 "Originally a lipid droplet in the endoplasmic reticulum of a fatty liver. Now applied to an artificially formed lipid droplet, small enough to form a relatively stable suspension in aqueous media and with potential use in drug delivery.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03577 "Lippman's equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03577 """An equation which gives the electric charge per unit area of an interface (electrode): \\[(\\frac{\\partial \\gamma }{\\partial E_{\\rm{A}}})_{T,p,\\mu _{i}
eq \\mu }=- Q_{\\rm{A}}\\] where \\(\\gamma \\) is the interfacial tension, \\(E_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the potential of a cell in which the reference electrode has an interfacial equilibrium with one of the ionic components of A, \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the charge on unit area of the interface, \\(\\mu _{i}\\) is the chemical potential of the combination of species \\(i\\) whose net charge is zero, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature and \\(p\\) is the external pressure. Since more than one type of reference electrode may be chosen, more than one quantity \\(Q\\) may be obtained. Consequently \\(Q\\) cannot be considered as equivalent to the physical charge on a particular region of the interphase. It is in fact an alternative way of expressing a surface excess or combination of surface excess of charged species.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03578 "liquid chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03578 "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a liquid. Liquid chromatography can be carried out either in a column or on a plane. Present-day liquid chromatography generally utilizing very small particles and a relatively high inlet pressure is often characterized by the term high-performance (or high-pressure) liquid chromatography, and the acronym HPLC.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03579 "liquid-crystal transitions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03579 "A liquid crystal is a molecular crystal with properties that are both solid- and liquid-like. Liquid crystals are composed predominantly of rod-like or disc-like molecules, that can exhibit one or more different, ordered fluid phases as well as the isotropic fluid; the translational order is wholly or partially destroyed but a considerable degree of orientational order is retained on passing from the crystalline to the liquid phase in a mesomorphic transition.Transition to a nematic phase.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a nematic phase in which the mean direction of the molecules is parallel or antiparallel to an axis known as the director.Transition to a cholesteric phase. A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a cholesteric phase in which there is simply a spiralling of the local orientational order perpendicular to the long axes of the molecules. Transition to a smectic state.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to yield a  smectic state in which there is a one-dimensional density wave which produces very soft/disordered layers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03580 "liquid excimer laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03580 "A liquid laser in which excimer species in liquids are used as the active medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03581 "liquid-gel chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03581 "Includes gel-permeation and ion-exchange chromatography. Liquid is the mobile phase, gel the stationary phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03582 "liquid ion exchange")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03582 "A term used to describe a liquid-liquid extraction process that involves a transfer of ionic species from the extractant to the aqueous phase in exchange for ions from the aqueous phase. The term does not imply anything concerning the nature of the bonding in the extracted complex. The term solvent ion exchange is not recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03582 "solvent ion exchange")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03583 "liquid ion laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03583 "A liquid laser in which rare earth ions in solution are used as the laser active medium. An example is $\\ce{KrF}$ in a liquid $\\ce{Kr}$ laser.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03584))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03584 "liquid junction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03584 "Any junction between two electrolyte solutions of different composition. Across such a junction there arises a potential difference, called the liquid junction potential. In the operational pH cell the junction is between the test, or pH standard, solution and the filling solution or the bridge solution of the reference electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03585 "liquid laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03585 "A laser in which a liquid solution is the active species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03586 "liquid-liquid distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03586 "The process of transferring a dissolved substance from one liquid phase to another (immiscible or partially miscible) liquid phase in contact with it. Although extraction, partition and distribution are not synonymous, extraction may replace distribution where appropriate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03587 "liquid-liquid extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03587 "This term may be used in place of liquid-liquid distribution when the emphasis is on the analyte(s) being distributed (or extracted).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03588 "liquid-phase loading")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03588 "A term used in partition chromatography to express the relative amount of the liquid stationary phase in the column packing. It is equal to the mass fraction (%) of liquid stationary phase in the total packing (liquid stationary phase plus support).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03589 "liquid scintillation detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03589 "A scintillation detector in which the test portion is mixed with a liquid scintillator.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03590 "liquidus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03590 "A line on a binary phase diagram (or surface on a ternary phase diagram) that indicates the temperature at which solidification begins on cooling or at which melting is completed on heating under equilibrium conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03591 "liquid volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03591 "That volume occupied by the liquid phase in the column. Defined by \\[V_{\\rm{L}}=\\frac{w_{\\rm{L}}}{\\rho _{\\rm{L}}}\\] where \\(w_{\\rm{L}}\\) is the weight of the liquid in the column, and \\(\\rho _{\\rm{L}}\\) is the density at the column temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03592 "lithometeor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03592 "A particle of dry substance in the atmosphere, as contrasted to a hydrometeor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03593 "lithosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03593 "The crust of the earth, usually thought of as discrete from and in contact with the hydrosphere and the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03594 "litre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03594 "Non-SI unit of volume, \\(\\pu{L} = \\pu{E-3 m3}\\). The other allowed symbol is \\(\\mathrm{l}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03595 "live time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03595 "For a measurement, the time during which a radiation measuring assembly is capable of processing events occurring in the radiation detector. It equals the clock time minus the integrated resolving or dead time (to be distinguished from 'life time').")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03596))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03596 "living copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03596 "A copolymerization which is a living polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03597 "living polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03597 "A chain polymerization from which chain transfer and chain termination are absent. In many cases, the rate of chain initiation is fast compared with the rate of chain propagation, so that the number of kinetic-chain carriers is essentially constant throughout the polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03598 "load")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03598 "The total weight acting, after counterbalancing, upon the terminal bearing which carries the object being weighed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03599 "loading capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03599 "The maximum concentration of solute(s) that a solvent can contain under specified conditions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03599 "maximum loading")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03599 "saturation capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03599 "saturation loading")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03600 "local conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03600 "The conformation of a macromolecule at the scale of the constitutional units. In the polymer literature, local conformation is sometimes referred to as microconformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03602 "local efficiency of atomization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03602 "The substance fraction of atomized component in the component consumed. The efficiency of atomization is measured in a given part of the flame, usually the observation space; \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{a}} = \\varepsilon_{\\rm{n}}\\ \\chi _{\\rm{s}}\\ \\chi _{\\rm{v}}\\ \\chi _{\\rm{a}}\\). The signal is a function of the product \\(q_{\\rm{v}}\\ \\varepsilon_{\\rm{a}}\\), but \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{a}}\\) is also a function of \\(q_{\\rm{v}}\\), usually decreasing at high volume rates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03603 "local flame temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03603 "The effective thermodynamic temperature in the observation space as measured by a specific sensor for a specified element (in \\(\\rm{K}\\)). The temperature of a flame (or other plasma) is not homogeneous. It is usually lower at the borders of the flame. It is therefore appropriate to speak of an effective temperature which represents an average value of all temperatures throughout the observation space. The flame temperature depends on several factors such as: kind of plasma, kind of gas or gas mixture and concentration gradient of the thermometric species in the observation space.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03604 "local fraction atomized")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03604 "The substance fraction of the atomized component in the total volatilized component. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame, usually the observation space. The fraction atomized is the result of chemical reactions in the gaseous state. It depends on the bond strength of the compounds that the component may form within the flame and on the composition and temperature of the flame. When analysing elements that tend to become oxidized in the flame, it may be advisable to use as fuel gas mixtures with a reducing component such as $\\ce{C2H2}$ or $\\ce{N2O}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03605 "local fraction desolvated")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03605 "The amount-of-substance fraction of component in a desolvated state in the amount of component entering the flame. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame, usually the observation space. Because it varies with height in the flame as a result of progressive evaporation of aerosol droplets, it is appropriate to term the expression local. The fraction desolvated does not account for losses by incomplete volatilization of the dry aerosol (which largely depends upon the nature and concentration of the component). Such losses are described by local fraction volatilized, which usually depends on the solute. Since \\(\\chi _{\\rm{s}}\\) varies markedly with the height in the flame, its observed value represents an average. Local fraction desolvated depends on the solvent, the temperature of the flame and the time the component takes to travel from the tip of the burner to the height in the flame considered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03606 "local fraction volatilized")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03606 "The substance fraction of the volatilized component in the total desolvated component. The gaseous state includes free atoms, molecules and radicals. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame, usually the observation space. The fraction volatilized varies inversely with the size of the desolvated particles. Since \\(\\chi _{\\rm{v}}\\) varies markedly with the height in the flame, its observed value represents an average.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03608 "localized-itinerant transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03608 "A transition of either of two types: (a) a Mott transition (single-valent systems). (b) a small-polaron to itinerant-electron transition (mixed-valent systems).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03611 "logarithmic normal distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03611 "A continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form: \\[f_{\\rm{w}}(x)\\ \\rm{d}x = \\frac{1}{a\\ \\sqrt{\\pi \\ x}}\\ \\exp (- \\frac{1}{a^{2}}\\ \\ln ^{2}\\frac{x}{b})\\ \\rm{d}x\\] where \\(x\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive adjustable parameters.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03613))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03613 "logit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03613 "In competitive binding assays, the logit-log dose relationship, in which the response is defined by: \\(R=\\mathrm{logit}\\left(y\\right)=\\log _{10}(\\frac{y}{1- y})\\) where \\(y=\\frac{b}{b_{0}}\\) with \\(b\\) = fraction of tracer bound and \\(b_{0}\\) = value of \\(b\\) with no unlabelled ligand in the system. Logit transformed assay data frequently yield straight-line dose-response curves, amenable to statistical analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03614 "log-normal distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03614 "A distribution function \\(F(y)\\), in which the logarithm of a quantity is normally distributed, i.e. \\(F(y)=f_{\\rm{gauss}}(\\ln y)\\) where \\(f_{\\rm{gauss}}(x)\\) is a Gaussian distribution. The size distributions of atmospheric aerosols are often described using this distribution function, although the term also applies to gaseous pollutants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03615 "London–Eyring–Polanyi method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03615 "A semiempirical method of calculating a potential-energy surface, based on a simplified quantum-mechanical equation (the London equation).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03615 "LEP method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03616 "London–Eyring–Polanyi–Sato method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03616 "A modification of the London–Eyring–Polanyi proposed in 1955 by S. Sato.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03616 "LEPS method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03617))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03617 "London forces")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03617 "Attractive forces between apolar molecules, due to their mutual polarizability. They are also components of the forces between polar molecules. Also called 'dispersion forces'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03617 "dispersion forces")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03618 "lone pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03618 "Two paired electrons localized in the valence shell on a single atom. Lone pairs should be designated with two dots. The term 'nonbonding electron pair' is more appropriate, and is found in many modern text books.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03618 "electron pair")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03619 "long chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03619 "A chain of high relative molecular mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03621 "longitudinal order")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03621 "Order in the atomic positions along the chains of a linear polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03622 "long-lived collision complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03622 "When a potential-energy surface is such that the system performs a number of vibrations and rotations in the region of a col, the corresponding intermediate is known as a long-lived collision complex, and the reaction is said to be indirect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03623 "long-range intramolecular interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03623 "An interaction between segments, widely separated in sequence along the chain, that occasionally approach one another during molecular flexing. This type of interaction is closely related to the excluded volume of a segment, the latter quantity reflecting interactions involving segments and solvent molecules. If no confusion can occur, the word 'intramolecular' may be omitted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03624 "long spacing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03624 "The average separation between stacked lamellar crystals, usually measured by small-angle X-ray or neutron diffraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03625 "loose end")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03625 "Polymer chain within a network which is connected by a junction-point at one end only.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03628))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03628 "Lorentzian band shape")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03628 """This band shape is described by the function: \\[F\\left(
u - 
u _{0}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\pi }\\ \\gamma \\ ((
u - 
u _{0})^{2}+\\gamma ^{2})^{-1}\\] where \\(
u_{0}\\) is the mean band position, \\(\\gamma \\) is the half band width at half maximum, and \\(F\\ (
u - 
u _{0})\\) is the frequency distribution function.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03629 "Lorenz–Mie theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03629 "Theory of light scattering by isotropic, homogeneous spheres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03630))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03630 "lot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03630 "A quantity of material which is assumed to be a single population for sampling purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03631 "low energy electron diffraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03631 "Any technique which measures the angular intensity distribution of electrons reflected from a crystalline surface under bombardment with low energy electrons (\\(E_{0} < 500\\ \\rm{eV}\\)) in larger angles of incidence. The diffraction pattern also provides very surface sensitive information on the atomic arrangement of the top layers of a solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03634 "lowest-observed-effect-level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03634 "Lowest concentration or amount of a substance, found by experiment or observation, that causes any alteration in morphology, functional capacity, growth, development, or life span of target organisms distinguishable from normal (control) organisms of the same species and strain under the same defined conditions of exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03636))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03636 "low pressure electrical discharge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03636 "Low pressure electrical discharge sources are radiation sources in which radiation is produced by electrical discharges in gases at low pressures, i.e. pressures from \\(10^{2}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\) to \\(10^{-3}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03637 "low-pressure mercury lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03637 "Resonance lamp that contains mercury vapour at pressures of about \\(0.1\\ \\rm{Pa}\\) (\\(0.75\\times 10^{-3}\\ \\rm{Torr}\\); \\(1\\ \\rm{Torr} = 133.33\\ \\rm{Pa}\\)). At \\(\\pu{25^{\\circ}C}\\), such a lamp emits mainly at \\(253.7\\) and \\(184.9\\ \\rm{nm}\\). They are also called germicidal lamps. There are cold- and hot-cathode as well as cooled electrode-less (excited by microwaves) low-pressure mercury lamps. The Wood lamp is a low-pressure mercury arc with an added fluorescent layer that emits in the UV-A spectral region (\\(315\\)-\\(400\\ \\rm{nm}\\)).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03637 "low-pressure mercury arc")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03638 "low temperature UV–VIS absorption spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03638 "The measurement of the absorption of UV–VIS radiation by a solute in a highly viscous or solid matrix at low temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03639))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03639 "lumen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03639 "SI derived unit of luminous flux, \\(\\rm{lm} = \\rm{cd sr}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03640 "luminance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03640 "Photometric counterpart of radiance, producing the visual sensation called brightness. Typical units are: \\(\\pu{candela m-2}\\) (\\(\\mathrm{nit}\\)), \\(\\pu{candela cm-2}\\) (\\(\\mathrm{stilb}\\)), \\(\\mathrm{foot}\\ \\mathrm{lambert}\\) (\\(2.426\\ \\rm{nit}\\)). As with all photometric quantities, luminance does not refer to a specific wavelength, but applies to light emitted by a standard source (formerly a 'standard international candle', now a blackbody radiator emitting at the temperature of solidifying platinum, \\(2042\\ \\rm{K}\\)). Conversion from photometric units to radiometric units (e.g. \\(\\rm{J s}^{-1}\\)) requires convolution over wavelength of the relative spectral response of the human eye (photopic response tables).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03641))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03641 "luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03641 "Spontaneous emission of radiation from an electronically or vibrationally excited species not in thermal equilibrium with its environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03644))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03644 "luminescence quenching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03644 "Radiationless redistribution of excitation energy via interaction (electronic energy or charge transfer) between an emitting species and the quencher.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03645 "luminescence spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03645 "The instrument used to measure luminescence emission spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03646))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03646 "luminous flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03646 "Of a source of luminous intensity \\(I\\) in an element of solid angle \\(\\rm{d}\\varOmega\\) is given by \\(\\rm{d}\\varPhi = I\\ \\rm{d}\\varOmega\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03647))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03647 "luminous intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03647 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03648 "luminous quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03648 "Group of quantities characterizing visible light in terms of the appearance to the human eye. The symbols of luminous quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript v. The units for luminous quantities are derived from the SI base unit candela.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03649))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03649 "lumiphore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03649 "A part of a molecular entity (or atom or group of atoms) in which electronic excitation associated with a given emission band is approximately localized. (Analogous to chromophore for absorption spectra).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03649 "luminophore")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03651 "lux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03651 "SI derived unit of illuminance, \\(\\rm{lx} = \\rm{lm m}^{-2}=\\rm{cd sr m}^{-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03652))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03652 "lyases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03652 "Enzymes cleaving $\\ce{C–C}$, $\\ce{C–O}$, $\\ce{C–N}$ and other bonds by other reactions than by hydrolysis or oxidation. Lyases catalyse the addition of molecules to unsaturated compounds or the elimination of molecules creating an unsaturated residue.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03653 "lyate ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03653 "The anion produced by hydron removal from a solvent molecule. For example, the hydroxide ion is the lyate ion of water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03654 "lyonium ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03654 "The cation produced by hydronation of a solvent molecule. For example, ($\\ce{CH3OH2^{+}}$) is the lyonium ion of methanol.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03655))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03655 "lyophilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03655 "The terms lyophilic (hydrophilic, lipophilic, oleophilic, etc.) and lyophobic (lipophobic, etc.) may be used to describe the character of interaction of a particular atomic group with the medium. In this usage the terms have the relative qualitative meaning of 'solvent preferring' (water-preferring, fat-preferring, etc.) and 'solvent rejecting' (water-rejecting, fat-rejecting, etc.) respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03656 "lyophilic sols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03656 "These comprise both association colloids in which aggregates of small molecules are formed reversibly and macromolecules in which the molecules themselves are of colloidal size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03658 "lysimeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03658 "Laboratory column of selected representative soil or a protected monolith of undisturbed field soil with which it is possible to sample and monitor the movement of water and substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06764))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06764 "ligand field")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06764 "Ligand field theory is a modified crystal field theory that assigns certain parameters as variables rather than taking them as equal to the values found for free ions, thereby taking into account the potential covalent character of the metal-ligand bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06788))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06788 "low-spin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06788 "In any coordination entity with a particular $\\rm{d^{n}}$ ($1 \\lt \\pu{n} \\lt 9$) configuration and a particular geometry, if the n electrons are distributed so that they occupy the lowest possible energy levels, the entity is a low-spin complex. If some of the higher energy d orbitals are occupied before all the lower energy ones are completely filled, then the entity is a high-spin complex.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06829 "latent period")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06829 "Delay between exposure to a harmful substance and the manifestations of a disease or other adverse effects.Period from disease initiation to disease detection.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:LT06829 "latency")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06854 "liquid-crystal state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06854 "A mesomorphic state having long-range orientational order and either partial positional order or complete positional disorder.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06855))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06855 "liquid-crystalline phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06855 "A phase occurring over a definite temperature range within the liquid-crystalline state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06856 "liquid crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06856 "A substance in the liquid-crystalline state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06865 "liquid-crystal dendrimer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06865 "A highly-branched oligomer or polymer of dendritic structure containing mesogenic groups that can display mesophase behaviour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06867))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06867 "liquid-crystal polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06867 "A polymer material that, under suitable conditions of temperature, pressure, and concentration, exists as an LC mesophase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06877))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06877 "Langmuir–Blodgett membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06877 "Synthetic composite membrane formed by sequential depositing of one or more monolayers of surface-active component onto a porous or non-porous support.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:LT06877 "LB membrane")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06883))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06883 "liquid membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06883 "Liquid phase existing either in supported or unsupported form that serves as a membrane barrier between two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06896 "lyotropic mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06896 "A mesophase formed by dissolving an amphiphilic mesogen in a suitable solvent, under appropriate conditions of concentration, temperature, and pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06909 "lowest observed adverse effect level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06909 "Lowest concentration or amount of a substance, found by experiment or observation, which causes an adverse alteration of morphology, functional capacity, growth, development, or life span of a target organism distinguishable from normal (control) organisms of the same species and strain under defined conditions of exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06919))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06919 "liquid-coated stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06919 "A material in which a liquid stationary phase is coated on the surface of the solid support.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:LT06919 "liquid-coated stationary phase material")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06935))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06935 "linear distribution isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06935 "A @DT06932-1@ which can be approximated as \\(c_{\\rm{s}} = K_{\\rm{C}}.c_{\\rm{M}}\\) where \\(K_{\\rm{C}}\\) is a constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06939))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06939 "linear chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06939 "Chromatographic process, where the retention is governed by a linear distribution isotherm.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT06965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT06965 "lipophilicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT06965 "Lipophilicity represents the affinity of a molecule or a moiety for a lipophilic environment. It is commonly measured by its distribution behaviour in a biphasic system, either liquid-liquid (e.g., partition coefficient in 1-octanol/water) or solid-liquid (retention on reversed-phase high-performance liquid chromatography (RP-HPLC) or thin-layer chromatography (TLC) system).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07064 "least motion principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07064 """The statement that those elementary reactions are the most favoured which exhibit the fewest possible alterations in the positions of the atomic nuclei and in the electronic configuration. The most frequently used mathematical formulation of the principle rests on a mechanical model of a molecule in which the energy of structural deformation, when reactants (r) turn into products (p), is assumed to be proportional to the sum of the squares of the changes in the positions of the nuclei common to both reactants and products \\[E=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}f_{i}\\ (q^{\\rm{p}_{i}}- q^{\\rm{r}_{i}})^{2}\\] where \\(f_{i}\\) is the force constant (in many applications set equal to unity). The equation coincides with the relationship for the potential energy of small vibrations, hence it is valid only at a very early stage of a reaction. This is one of the reasons why many reactions violate the principle of least motion.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:LT07064 "principle of least motion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07065 "Lewis octet rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07065 "A classical rule for describing the electronic configuration of atoms in molecules: the maximum number of electron pairs that can be accommodated in the valence shell of a first-row element is four. For the second and subsequent row elements there are many exceptions to this rule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07066 "localized molecular orbitals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07066 "The molecular orbitals located on certain fragments of a molecular system and spatially separated from each other as much as possible. The LMOs are derived from the electron occupied canonical molecular orbitals by subjecting them to a unitary transformation determined by an appropriate physical criterion, e.g. by maximizing the sum of squares of the centroids of occupied MOs (the Foster–Boys procedure) or by minimizing the sum of the exchange (or Coulomb) repulsion integrals between the occupied MOs (the Edmiston–Ruedenberg procedure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07067 "low-spin state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07067 "When the separation between the highest occupied and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals is not large, two alternative electronic states may be considered. The state with two electrons paired up in the HOMO is called a low-spin state. The low-spin state is the ground state when the one-electron energy needed to promote an electron to the LUMO is larger than the Coulomb and exchange repulsion energies required to pair up two electrons in the HOMO.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07156 "living polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07156 "Polymer with stable, polymerization-active sites formed by a chain polymerization in which irreversible chain transfer and chain termination are absent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07176 "liquid-crystalline polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07176 "Polymeric material that, under suitable conditions of temperature, pressure, and concentration, exists as a liquid-crystalline mesophase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:LT07176 "liquid crystal polymer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07408))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07408 "LADS")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07408 "Acronym for lifetime-associated difference spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07409 "Landau–Zener model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07409 """Within the Born–Oppenheimer approximation, a semi-classical model for the probability, \\(P\\), of hopping from one electronic state to another of the same or different multiplicity \\[P=\\exp (- \\frac{4\\ \\pi ^{2}\\ \\varepsilon_{12}^{2}}{h\\ 
u\\ |s_{1}-s_{2}|})\\] where \\(\\varepsilon_{12}\\) is the potential energy gap between the two electronic states at a surface crossing point, \\(|s_{1}- s_{2}|\\) is the difference in slopes between the intersecting potential energy curves at this point and \\(
u\\) is the nuclear relative velocity with which the system passes the point of closest approach.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07410 "LAS")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07410 "Acronym for lifetime-associated spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07411 "laser dye")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07411 "Organic dyes typically used dissolved in an organic solvent. The dye solution is circulated continuously through the laser chamber of the dye laser, in which it is excited (pumped) by flash lamps or by another laser.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07412 "laser-jet photochemical technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07412 "Technique by which high concentrations of excited states are generated in a jet, permitting the readily determination of spectroscopic properties of those states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07413))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07413 "LED")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07413 "Acronym for light-emitting diode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07414))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07414 "light-emitting diode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07414 "Semiconductor (often a combination of gallium, arsenic and phosphorous or gallium and nitrogen) containing an n region (where electrons are more numerous than positive charges) separated from a p region (where positive charges are more numerous than negative charges). Upon application of a voltage, charges move and emission of ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation is produced each time a charge recombination takes place. Although a LED emits incoherent monochromatic light, normally a very narrow frequency range is obtained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:LT07415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:LT07415 "linear dichroism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:LT07415 "For a uniaxial sample with sample axis \\(Z\\) is defined as: \\[\\Delta A_{\\rm{l}} = A_{Z} - A_{Y}\\] where \\(A_{Z}\\) and \\(A_{Y}\\) are the absorption spectra measured with the electric vector of linearly polarized light along and perpendicular to the sample axis, respectively. For an isotropic sample \\(\\Delta A_{\\rm{l}} = 0\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03659 "µ-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03659 "An affix used in names to signify that a group so designated bridges two or more centres of coordination.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03659 "mu-")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03662))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03662 "macrocycle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03662 "A cyclic macromolecule or a macromolecule cyclic portion of a macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03663 "macrolides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03663 "Macrocyclic lactones with a ring of twelve or more members.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03664))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03664 "macrometeorology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03664 "Study of the largest-scale aspects of the atmosphere, e.g. general global circulation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03666 "macromolecular isomorphism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03666 "Statistical co-crystallization of different constitutional repeating units which may either belong to the same copolymer chains (copolymer isomorphism) or originate from different homopolymer chains (homopolymer isomorphism). Isomorphism is a general term: in the strict sense, the crystal structure is essentially the same throughout the range of compositions; in isodimorphism or isopolymorphism, there are two or more crystal structures, respectively, depending on composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03667))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03667 "macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03667 "A molecule of high relative molecular mass, the structure of which essentially comprises the multiple repetition of units derived, actually or conceptually, from molecules of low relative molecular mass.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03667 "polymer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03667 "macromolecular")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03667 "polymeric")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03668 "macromonomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03668 "Polymer or oligomer the molecules of which each have one end-group that acts as a monomer molecule, so that each polymer or oligomer molecule contributes only a single monomer unit to a chain of the product polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03669))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03669 "macromonomeric unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03669 "The largest constitutional unit contributed by a single macromonomer molecule to the structure of a macromolecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03669 "macromonomer unit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03670 "macromonomer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03670 "A macromolecule that has one end-group which enables it to act as a monomer molecule, contributing only a single monomeric unit to a chain of the final macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03672))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03672 "macropore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03672 "Pore with width exceeding about \\(0.05\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) or \\(50\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (\\(500\\ \\rm{Å}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03673 "macroradical")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03673 "A macromolecule which is also a free radical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03674 "macroscopic cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03674 "The cross-section per unit volume of a given material for a specified process. For a pure nuclide, it is the product of the microscopic cross-section and the number of target nuclei per unit volume; for a mixture of nuclides, it is the sum of such products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03676 "macroscopic film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03676 "A film which has lateral dimensions above \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03677))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03677 "macroscopic kinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03677 "A term applied to a kinetic study in which the interest is in the behaviour of bulk systems, e.g. in changes in concentrations of reactants and products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03680))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03680 "magnetic deflection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03680 "The deflection of an ion beam as a result of the motion of the ions in a magnetic field (magnetic sector). Generally the direction of motion of the ions is at right angles to the direction of the magnetic field, and the motion is uniform.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03681 "magnetic equivalence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03681 "Nuclei having the same resonance frequency in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy and also identical spin-spin interactions with the nuclei of a neighbouring group are magnetically equivalent. The spin-spin interaction between magnetically equivalent nuclei does not appear, and thus has no effect on the multiplicity of the respective NMR signals. Magnetically equivalent nuclei are necessarily also chemically equivalent, but the reverse is not necessarily true.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03682 "magnetic field scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03682 "The usual method of producing a momentum (mass) spectrum, by varying the strength of the magnetic field.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03682 "<em>B</em> scan")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03683 "magnetic field strength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03683 "Magnetic flux density divided by the permeability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03684))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03684 "magnetic flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03684 "Scalar product of magnetic flux density and area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03685 "magnetic flux density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03685 "Magnetic flux density at the nucleus (from experiment) in those cases in which the magnetic hyperfine interaction can be described by an effective field. In other cases the vector components of the magnetic hyperfine interaction should be reported if possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03686))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03686 "magnetic flux density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03686 "Magnetic induction is a vector characterizing a magnetic field. The force acting on a moving charge in a magnetic field is the charge multiplied by the vector product of the velocity and magnetic flux density. This quantity is sometimes loosely called the magnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03688 "magnetic moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03688 "Vector quantity, the vector product of which with the magnetic flux density of a homogeneous field is equal to the torque.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03689))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03689 "magnetic susceptibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03689 "Relative permeability minus one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03690))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03690 "magnetic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03690 "A transition between disordered and ordered arrays of atomic magnetic moments. Where the ordered phase has a net spontaneous magnetization, \\(M_{\\rm{S}}\\), the magnetic-ordering temperature is called a curie temperature, \\(T_{\\rm{C}}\\); where the net spontaneous magnetization of the ordered phase remains zero, the ordering temperature is called a Néel temperature, \\(T_{\\rm{N}}\\). The temperature at which the two ferromagnetic subarrays of a ferrimagnet just cancel each other is called the compensation point. Materials exhibit ferromagnetic behaviour when unpaired electron spins are aligned in parallel, antiferromagnetic behaviour when the alignment is antiparallel, and ferrimagnetic behaviour if the alignment of the spins is antiparallel with unequal numbers in the two orientations or if the spins are canted. Therefore, ferromagnetic, ferrimagnetic and weak ferromagnetic transitions involve a net magnetic moment change, whereas antiferromagnetic transitions have zero net magnetic moment change.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03691 "magnetizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03691 "Tensor quantity relating the induced magnetic moment, \\(m_{\\rm{i}}\\), to the applied magnetic flux density \\(B\\), \\(m_{\\rm{i}}=\\xi \\ B\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03692 "magnetization transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03692 "NMR method for determining kinetics of chemical exchange by perturbing the magnetization of nuclei in a particular site or sites and following the rate at which magnetic equilibrium is restored. The most common perturbations are saturation and inversion, and the corresponding techniques are often called 'saturation transfer' and 'selective inversion-recovery'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03693 "magnetogyric ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03693 "Ratio of the magnetic moment to the angular momentum. It is often misleadingly called the gyromagnetic ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03694))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03694 "main chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03694 "That linear chain to which all other chains, long or short or both, may be regarded as being pendant.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03694 "backbone")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03695 "mancude-ring systems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03695 "Rings having (formally) the maximum number of noncumulative double bonds, e.g. benzene, indene, indole, 4H-1,3-dioxine. Also called mancunide-ring systems.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03695 "mancunide-ring systems")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03697))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03697 "manipulator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03697 "A hand-operated or -controlled device for remotely handling radioactive materials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03698))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03698 "manual")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03698 "Refers to physical human intervention in an analytical procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03699 "mapping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03699 "The determination of the relative positions of genes within the chromosomes of restriction sites along a DNA molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03700 "Marangoni effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03700 "Motions of the surface of a liquid are coupled with those of the subsurface fluid or fluids, so that movements of the liquid normally produce stresses in the surface and vice versa. The movement of the surface and of the entrained fluid(s) caused by surface tension gradients is called the Marangoni effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03701))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03701 "Marcus–Coltrin path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03701 "A path trajectory over a potential-energy surface which corresponds to the outermost vibrational turning points for the molecular species involved. This path was suggested as a device for calculating the probability of quantum-mechanical tunnelling: the trajectory for the system is considered to follow this path.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03702 "Marcus equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03702 "Relation between the rate of outer-sphere electron transfer and the thermodynamics of this process. Essentially, the rate constant within the encounter complex (or the rate constant of intramolecular transfer) is given by the Eyring equation: \\[k_{\\mathrm{ET}}=\\frac{\\kappa _{\\mathrm{ET}}\\ k\\ T}{h}\\ \\exp (- \\frac{\\Delta G^{\\ddagger }}{R\\ T})\\] where \\(k\\) is the Boltzmann constant, \\(h\\) the Planck constant, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(\\kappa _{\\rm{ET}}\\) the so-called electronic transmission factor (\\(\\kappa _{\\rm{ET}}\\sim 1\\) for adiabatic and \\(")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03703 "Marcus–Hush relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03703 "Relationship between the barrier (\\(\\Delta G^{\\ddagger}\\)) to thermal electron transfer, the energy of a corresponding optical charge-transfer transition (\\(\\Delta E_{\\rm{op}}\\)), and the overall change in standard Gibbs energy accompanying thermal electron transfer (\\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)). Assuming a quadratic relation between the energy of the system and its distortions from equilibrium (harmonic oscillator model) the expression obtained is: \\[\\Delta G^{\\ddagger} = \\frac{\\Delta E_{\\rm{op}}^{2}}{4\\ (\\Delta E_{\\rm{op}}\\,-\\,\\Delta G^{o})}\\] The simplest form of this expression obtains for degenerate electron transfer (\\(\\Delta G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)) in e.g. symmetrical mixed valence systems: \\[\\Delta G^{\\ddagger} = \\frac{\\Delta E_{\\rm{op}}}{4}\\] Note that for this situation the Marcus equation reads: \\[\\Delta G^{\\ddagger} = \\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03705 "marker")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03705 "(DNA) A fragment of known size used as reference for analytical purposes.(genetic) A gene with known phenotype and mapped position.(chromatography) A reference substance co-chromatographed with the sample to assist in identifying the components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03706))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03706 "Mark–Houwink equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03706 "The equation describing the dependence of the intrinsic viscosity of a polymer on its relative molecular mass (molecular weight) and having the form: \\[[\\eta] = K\\cdot M_{\\rm{r}}^{a}\\] where \\([\\eta]\\) is the intrinsic viscosity, \\(K\\) and \\(a\\) are constants the values of which depend on the nature of the polymer and solvent as well as on temperature and \\(M_{\\rm{r}}\\) is usually one of the relative molecular mass averages.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03707))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03707 "Markownikoff rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03707 "'In the addition of hydrogen halides to unsymmetrically constituted [unsaturated] hydrocarbons, the halogen atom becomes attached to the carbon bearing the lesser number of hydrogen atoms.' Originally formulated by Markownikoff (Markovnikov) to generalize the orientation in additions of hydrogen halides to simple alkenes, this rule has been extended to polar addition reaction as follows. 'In the heterolytic addition of a polar molecule to an alkene or alkyne, the more electronegative (nucleophilic) atom (or part) of the polar molecule becomes attached to the carbon atom bearing the smaller number of hydrogen atoms.' This is an indirect statement of the common mechanistic observation, that the more electropositive (electrophilic) atom (or part) of the polar molecule becomes attached to the end of the multiple bond that would result in the more stable carbenium ion (whether or not a carbenium ion is actually formed as a reaction intermediate in the addition reaction). Addition in the opposite sense is commonly called 'anti-Markovnikov addition'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03707 "Markownikov rule")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03708))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03708 "martensitic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03708 "A diffusionless transition (first studied in the steel alloy, Martensite), at constant composition, generated by coordinated atomic, ionic or molecular displacements over distances smaller than interatomic distances in the parent phase. The cooperative rearrangements of the crystal structure generally take place progressively by the movement of a two-dimensional interface through the solid. Examples: The face-centred-cubic to body-centred-tetragonal transition of iron containing some carbon; the transition of tetragonal $\\ce{ZrO2}$ to monoclinic $\\ce{ZrO2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03709 "mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03709 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03710 "mass analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03710 "A process by which a mixture of ionic or neutral species is identified according to the mass-to-charge (\\(\\frac{m}{z}\\)) ratios (ions) or their aggregate atomic masses (neutrals). The analysis may be qualitative and/or quantitative.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03711 "mass-average velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03711 """Mass-average velocity: \\[
u_{b} = \\rho ^{-1}\\ \\sum C_{i}M_{i}
u_{i}\\] Cf. molar average velocity: \\[
u_{m} = c_{t}^{-1}\\ \\sum c_{i}
u_{i}\\] with \\(c_{t}=\\sum c_{i}\\) where \\(M_{i}\\) = molar mass, \\(c_{t}\\) = total concentration (\\(\\pu{mol m-1}\\)), \\(\\rho \\) = density of the solution (\\(\\pu{kg m-3}\\)).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03712 "mass balance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03712 "Summation of the masses of a given element in its various compounds before and after reaction (changes) in the atmosphere; provides a test of the completeness of the accounting of the various reaction paths for this element which can be had through the compounds which have been analysed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03713 "mass concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03713 "Mass of a constituent divided by the volume of the mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03715 "mass density gradient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03715 "Change in mass density in a small distance divided by that distance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03716 "mass-distribution function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03716 "A distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with a specific value, or a weight distribution range of values, of the random variable(s) is expressed in terms of mass fraction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03716 "weight-distribution function")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03718))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03718 "mass distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03718 "The fraction (\\(1 - R\\)) of a component in the stationary phase divided by the fraction (\\(R\\)) in the mobile phase: \\[D_{\\rm{m}}=\\frac{\\rm{amount of substance in the stationary phase}}{\\rm{amount of component in the mobile phase}}\\] This term is recommended in preference to the term capacity factor frequently used in the chromatographic literature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03719))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03719 "mass excess")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03719 "Mass of an atom minus the product of mass number and atomic mass constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03720))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03720 "mass flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03720 "Mass of a material crossing a surface divided by the time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03721))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03721 "mass-flow sensitive detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03721 "A device the response of which is proportional to the amount of sample component reaching the detector in unit time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03722 "mass fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03722 "Mass of a constituent divided by the total mass of all constituents in the mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03723 "massic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03723 "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by mass; synonymous with specific.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03724))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03724 "massive transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03724 "A transition in which a crystal structure is changed into a new structure during cooling or heating, predominantly by interface-controlled reactions and the usual nucleation and growth characteristics associated with thermal diffusion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03725))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03725 "mass-law effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03725 "At equilibrium, the product of the activities (or concentrations) of the reacting species is constant. Thus for the equilibrium: \\[\\alpha\\,\\rm{A}\\,+\\,\\beta\\,\\rm{B}\\rightleftharpoons \\gamma\\,\\rm{C}\\,+\\,\\delta\\,\\rm{D}\\] \\[K=\\frac{\\left[\\rm{C}\\right]^{\\gamma }\\ \\left[\\rm{D}\\right]^{\\delta }}{\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]^{\\alpha }\\ \\left[\\rm{B}\\right]^{\\beta }}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03726 "mass number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03726 "Total number of heavy particles (protons and neutrons jointly called nucleons) in the atomic nucleus. Also called nucleon number. Symbol \\(m\\) in mass spectrometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03727))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03727 "mass of the stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03727 "The mass (weight) of the liquid stationary phase or the active solid in the column. The mass (weight) of any solid support is not included. In the case of partition chromatography with a liquid stationary phase it is identical to the liquid phase mass (weight).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03727 "weight of the stationary phase")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03728 "mass peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03728 "A record of the ion current, at a specific mass to charge ratio, received by the collector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03729 "mass range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03729 "The range of mass numbers which can be characterized by a mass spectrometer with sufficient resolution to differentiate adjacent peaks.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03730 "mass resolving power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03730 "Commonly and also acceptably defined in terms of the overlap (or 'valley') between two peaks. Thus for two peaks of equal height, masses \\(m_{1}\\) and \\(m_{2}\\), when there is overlap between the two peaks to a stated percentage of either peak height (10% is recommended), then the resolving power is defined as \\(\\frac{m_{1}}{m_{1} - m_{2}}\\). The percentage overlap (or 'valley') concerned must always be stated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03731 "mass spectrograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03731 "An instrument in which beams of ions are separated (analysed) according to the quotient mass/charge, and in which the deflection and intensity of the beams are recorded directly on photographic plate or film.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03732 "mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03732 "An instrument in which beams of ions are separated (analysed) according to the quotient mass/charge, and in which the ions are measured electrically. This term should also be used when a scintillation detector is employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03733))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03733 "mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03733 "A mass spectrometer in which the ions to be separated absorb maximum energy through the effect of alternating electric fields which are parallel to the path of the ions. These ions are then separated from other ions with different mass/charge by an additional electric field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03736 "mass spectrometer focusing system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03736 "An assembly permitting the separation of ions according to their mass-to-charge ratio.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03736 "mass spectrometer deflection system")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03745 "mass spectrometric detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03745 "A mass spectrometer used as a detector to give qualitative and quantitative data on the various eluted substances. The mass spectrum of the eluted compounds provides evidence beyond the elution time as to the chemical nature of the species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03746 "mass spectrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03746 "The branch of science dealing with all aspects of mass spectroscopes and the results obtained with these instruments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03748 "mass spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03748 "The study of systems by causing the formation of gaseous ions, with or without fragmentation, which are then characterized by their mass-to-charge ratios and relative abundances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03749 "mass spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03749 "A spectrum which is obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the mass-to-charge (\\(\\frac{m}{z}\\)) ratios of the ionic species contained within it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03751 "E/2 mass spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03751 "Processes of the charge-stripping type: \\[\\rm{M}^{+}+\\rm{X}\\rightarrow \\rm{M}^{2+}+\\rm{X}+\\rm{e}\\] occuring in a collision cell (containing a gas, X) located in a field-free region preceding a magnetic and electric sector combination placed in either order, may be detected as follows. If the instrument slits are wide and if the electric sector field \\(E\\) is set to half the value required to transmit the main ion-beam, the only ions to be transmitted will be those with a kinetic energy/charge ratio half, or almost exactly half, that of the main ion beam. The product ions of the charge-stripping process fulfil this condition. If the magnetic field \\(B\\) is scanned, a mass spectrum of such doubly-charged product ions, and thus of their singly-charged precursors, is obtained. Such a spectrum is called an \\(\\frac{E}{2}\\) mass spectrum. Interference from product ions from processes of the type: \\[\\rm{M}_{1}^{+}+\\rm{X}\\rightarrow \\rm{M}_{2}^{+}+\\rm{X}+\\rm{M}_{3}\\] where \\(m_{1}\\), \\(m_{2}\\), and (\\(m_{1}- m_{2}\\)) are the masses of \\(\\rm{M}_{1}^{+}\\), \\(\\rm{M}_{2}^{+}\\) and \\(\\rm{M}_{3}\\), respectively, and where \\(m_{2}=0.5\\ m_{1}\\), can arise in \\(\\frac{E}{2}\\) mass spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03752 "mass-to-charge ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03752 "The abbreviation \\(\\frac{m}{z}\\) is used to denote the dimensionless quantity formed by dividing the mass number of an ion by its charge number. It has long been called the mass-to-charge ratio although \\(m\\) is not the ionic mass nor is \\(z\\) a multiple or the elementary (electronic) charge, \\(e\\). The abbreviation \\(\\frac{m}{e}\\) is, therefore, not recommended. Thus, for example, for the ion ($\\ce{C7H7^{2+}}$), \\(\\frac{m}{z}\\) equals 45.5.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03753 "mass transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03753 "Spontaneous (irreversible) process of transfer of mass across non-homogeneous fields. The driving force can be difference in concentration (in liquids) or partial pressure (in gases) of the component. In fluids, mass transfer may be enhanced by turbulent flow. In biological systems, mass transfer through membranes may result from normal diffusion, facilitated diffusion or active transport.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03754))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03754 "mass transfer coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03754 """The heterogeneous diffusion rate constant defined for the case of the limiting current. A more general definition in terms of the electrode current densities is: \\[k_{\\rm{d}}=\\frac{j\\ 
u }{n\\ F\\ (c_{\\rm{e}}- c_{0})}\\] or \\[k_{\\rm{d}} = \\frac{j\\ 
u \\ (1- t_{\\rm{B}}\\ n\\ 
u ^{-1}\\ z_{\\rm{B}}^{-1})}{n\\ F\\ (c_{\\rm{e}}- c_{0})}\\] where \\(j\\) is the electrode current density, \\(
u \\) is the stoichiometric number, \\(n\\) is the charge number of the electrode reaction, \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant, \\(c_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the interfacial concentration, \\(c_{0}\\) is the bulk concentration, \\(t_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the transport number of species B, and \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the charge number of species B.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03755 "mass-transfer-controlled electrolyte rate constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03755 "In controlled-potential coulometry and related techniques, the empirically evaluated constant of proportionality defined by the equation \\[s_{\\rm{B}}=- \\frac{1}{c_{\\rm{B}}}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}c_{\\rm{B}}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] where \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the bulk concentration of the substance B, and \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}c_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\) is the rate of change of that concentration, resulting from the consumption of B by reduction or oxidation at the working electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03756 "matched cells")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03756 "A pair of cells with closely similar optical properties are called matched cells. One cell is the sample cell while the other, the reference (or blank) cell contains the solvent or a reference solution. In double beam spectrometers, radiation is passed either simultaneously or alternately through the cells. In single beam instruments the cells are moved sequentially into the radiation beam.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03757))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03757 "material safety data sheet")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03757 "Compilation of information required under the US OSHA Hazard Communication Standard on the identity of hazardous substances, health and physical hazards, exposure limits and precautions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03758 "matrix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03758 "The components of the sample other than the analyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03759))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03759 "matrix effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03759 "The combined effect of all components of the sample other than the analyte on the measurement of the quantity. If a specific component can be identified as causing an effect then this is referred to as interference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03760 "matrix isolation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03760 "A term which refers to the isolation of a reactive or unstable species by dilution in an inert matrix (argon, nitrogen, etc.), usually condensed on a window or in an optical cell at low temperature, to preserve its structure for identification by spectroscopic or other means.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03761))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03761 "Mattauch–Herzog geometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03761 "An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrograph in which a deflection of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4\\ \\sqrt{2}}\\) radians in a radial electrostatic field is followed by a magnetic deflection of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) radians.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03762 "maximum allowable concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03762 "The maximum concentration of a pollutant which is considered harmless to healthy adults during their working hours, assuming they breathe uncontaminated air at all other times.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03763 "maximum emission concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03763 "Standards for maximum concentration of air pollutant emission from stationary or mobile sources.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03765 "maximum permissible daily dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03765 "Maximum daily dose of substance whose penetration into a human body during a lifetime will not cause diseases or health hazards that can be detected by current investigation methods and will not adversely affect future generations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03766 "maximum permissible level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03766 "Level, usually a combination of time and concentration, beyond which any exposure of humans to a chemical or physical agent in their immediate environment is unsafe.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03767 "maximum storage life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03767 "Period during which there is no change in concentration in excess of the value of the uncertainty of the concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03768))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03768 "maximum tolerable concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03768 "Highest concentration of a substance in an environmental medium that does not cause death of test organisms or species (denoted by \\(\\rm{LC}_{0}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03769 "maximum tolerable dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03769 "Highest amount of a substance that, when introduced into the body, does not kill test animals (denoted by \\(\\rm{LD}_{0}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03770 "maximum tolerable exposure level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03770 "Maximum amount or concentration of a substance to which an organism can be exposed without leading to an adverse effect after prolonged exposure time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03771 "maximum tolerated dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03771 "High dose used in chronic toxicity testing that is expected on the basis of an adequate subchronic study to produce limited toxicity when administered for the duration of the test period. It should not induce: (a) overt toxicity, for example appreciable death of cells or organ dysfunction, or (b) toxic manifestations that are predicted materially to reduce the life span of the animals except as the result of neoplastic development or (c) 10% or greater retardation of body weight gain as compared with control animals. In some studies, toxicity that could interfere with a carcinogenic effect is specifically excluded from consideration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03772 "McLafferty rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03772 "$\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}Cleavage}$ with concomitant specific transfer of a $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen atom}$ in a six-membered transition state in mono-unsaturated systems, irrespective of whether the rearrangement is formulated by a radical or an ionic mechanism, and irrespective of the position of the charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03773 "mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03773 "The sum of a series of observations divided by the number of observations in the series.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03773 "average")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03774))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03774 "mean activity of an electrolyte in solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03774 """Defined by the equation: \\[a_{\\pm }=\\mathrm{e}^{(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}- \\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\unicode{x29B5}})\\ 
u \\ R\\ T}\\] where \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}\\) is the chemical potential of the solute $\\ce{B}$ in a solution containing B and other species. The nature of $\\ce{B}$ must be clearly stated: it is taken as a group of ions of two kinds carrying an equal number of positive and negative charges, e.g. $\\ce{Na^{+}} + \\ce{NO3^{−}}$ or $\\ce{Ba^{2+}} + \\ce{2Cl^{−}}$ or $\\ce{2Al^{3+}} + \\ce{3SO4^{2−}}$. \\(v\\) is the total number of ions making up the group i.e. 2, 3 and 5 respectively in the above examples. \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\unicode{x29B5}}\\) is the chemical potential of $\\ce{B}$ in its standard state, usually the hypothetical ideal solution of concentration \\(\\pu{1 mol kg -1}\\) and at the same temperature and pressure as the solution under consideration.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03775 "mean catalytic activity rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03775 "The catalytic activity of a specified component in or moved from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved. This kind of quantity should not be confused with rate of reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03776 "mean current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03776 "Defined as \\[j=\\frac{I}{A}\\] where \\(I\\) is the electric current and \\(A\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode. However the nature of the area used in this calculation must be clearly stated. In interpreting the mean current density it is important to know whether the current is uniformly distributed over the electrode interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03777))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03777 "mean exchange current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03777 "Defined by \\[j_{0}=\\frac{I_{0}}{A}\\] where \\(I_{0}\\) is the exchange current of the electrode reaction and \\(A\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03778 "mean free path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03778 "The average distance a molecule travels between collisions. For a molecule, \\(\\lambda = (\\sqrt{2}\\ \\pi \\ n\\ d_{\\rm{m}}^{2})^{-1}\\), where \\(n\\) is the number of molecules per unit volume and \\(d_{\\rm{m}}\\) is their mean diameter. For $\\ce{O2}$ at one atmosphere and \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), this distance is only \\(9.7\\times 10^{-6}\\ \\rm{cm}\\); at \\(10^{-6}\\) atmospheres and \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\) it is \\(9.7\\ \\rm{cm}\\). For an aerosol particle, the mean free path, \\(\\lambda _{\\rm{B}}\\) in the Stokes region (see Stokes law) is given by: \\(\\lambda _{\\rm{B}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3\\ k\\ T}{m}}\\ m\\ B\\) where \\(m\\) is the mass of the particle, \\(k\\) is the Boltzmann constant (\\(1.381\\times 10^{-23}\\ \\rm{J K}^{-1}\\)), \\(T\\) is the temperature (\\(\\rm{K}\\)) and \\(B\\) is the mobility.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03779 "mean interstitial velocity of the carrier gas")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03779 "The interstitial velocity, \\(u\\) of the carrier gas multiplied by the pressure-gradient correction-factor, \\(j\\): \\[\\bar{u} = u\\ j\\] \\[\\bar{u} = \\frac{F\\ j}{\\varepsilon_{\\rm{I}}}\\] where \\(F\\) is the nominal linear flow, \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{I}}\\) is the interstitial fraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03782 "mean linear range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03782 "In a given material, for specified charged particles of a specified energy, the average displacement of the particles before they stop.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03783 "mean mass range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03783 "The mean linear range multiplied by the mass density of the material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03784))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03784 "mean mass rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03784 "The mass of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03785))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03785 "mean residence time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03785 "The mean time during which the molecules remain on the surface of the adsorbent, i.e. the mean time interval between impact and desorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03786))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03786 "mean substance rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03786 "The amount of substance of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved. This kind of quantity usually should be preferred to mean mass rate or mean volume rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03787 "mean volume rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03787 "The volume of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03788))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03788 "measurable quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03788 "An attribute of a substance which may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively. In the context of analytical chemistry, the attribute may refer to a physical quantity such as X- or $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{ray\\ energy}$, or it may refer to a measure of amount such as mass or concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03789))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03789 "measurand")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03789 "Particular quantity subject to measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03790 "measured excitation spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03790 "The spectrum observed by measuring the variation of the luminescence flux from an analyte as a function of the excitation wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03792 "measured spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03792 "Synonymous with spectrogram.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03793 "measured value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03793 "The observed value of weight, volume, meter-reading or other quantity, found in the analysis of a material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03794 "measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03794 "A description of a property of a system by means of a set of specified rules, that maps the property onto a scale of specified values, by direct or 'mathematical' comparison with specified reference(s). The demand for rules makes 'measurement' a scientific concept in contrast to the mere colloquial sense of 'description'. However, in the present definition, 'measurement' has a wider meaning than given in elementary physics. Even a very incomplete description of, for instance, a patient (at a stated time) has to be given by a set of measurements, that are easier to manage and grasp.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03795 "measurement resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03795 "The minimum value above which the difference of two values of air quality characteristic can be distinguished with 95% probability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03796 "measurement result")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03796 """The outcome of an analytical measurement (application of the chemical measurement process), or value attributed to a measurand. This may be the result of direct observation, but more commonly it is given as a statistical estimate derived from a set of observations. The distribution of such estimates (estimator distribution) characterizes the chemical measurement process, in contrast to a particular estimate, which constitutes an experimental result. Additional characteristics become evident if we represent \\(\\hat{x}\\) as follows: \\[\\begin{array}{c}\r
 &&& e  & \\\\\r
 &&& ⎴ ⎴ ⎴ & \\\\\r
\\hat{x}=\\tau +e = &\\tau& + &\\Delta& + &\\delta&= \\mu +\\delta \\\\\r
 & ⎵ ⎵ ⎵ &&& \\\\\r
 & \\mu &&&\r
\\end{array}\\] The true value, \\(\\tau \\), is the value \\(x\\) that would result if the chemical measurement process were error-free. The error, \\(e\\), is the difference between an observed (estimated) value and the true value; i.e. \\(e=\\hat{x}- \\tau \\) (signed quantity). The total error generally has two components, bias (\\(\\mathit{\\Delta}\\)) and random error (\\(\\delta \\)), as indicated above. The limiting mean, \\(\\mu\\), is the asymptotic value or population mean of the distribution that characterizes the measured quantity; the value that is approached as the number of observations approaches infinity. Modern statistical terminology labels this quantity the expectation value or expected value, \\(E(\\hat{x})\\). The bias, \\(\\mathit{\\Delta}\\), is the difference between the limiting mean and the true value; i.e. \\(\\mathit{\\Delta} = \\mu - \\tau \\) (signed quantity). The random error, \\(\\delta \\), is the difference between an observed value and the limiting mean; i.e. \\(\\delta = \\hat{x} - \\mu \\) (signed quantity).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03797))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03797 "measurement solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03797 "The solution that is presented to the measuring device. It comprises the test solution, or an aliquot thereof, after it has undergone any treatment required prior to the presentation to the measuring device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03798 "measurement threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03798 "The minimum concentration of a substance which produces a quantifiable signal with a given uncertainty.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03800 "mechanical entrapment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03800 "The process of random incorporation of comparatively small quantities of other phases (e.g. water, dust, particles, etc.) in the bulk of a precipitate during its formation.The deliberate capture of small quantities of such phases by the deliberate addition of solids to a liquid phase. (The term inclusion is not recommended).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03801))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03801 "mechanical hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03801 "An apparatus containing an element (hair, goldbeater's skin, carbon-film) whose dimension or mass varies as a function of its water content.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03803))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03803 "mechanization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03803 "The use of devices to replace, refine, extend or supplement human effort. The corresponding verb is to mechanize.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03804 "mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03804 "A detailed description of the process leading from the reactants to the products of a reaction, including a characterization as complete as possible of the composition, structure, energy and other properties of reaction intermediates, products and transition states. An acceptable mechanism of a specified reaction (and there may be a number of such alternative mechanisms not excluded by the evidence) must be consistent with the reaction stoichiometry, the rate law and with all other available experimental data, such as the stereochemical course of the reaction. Inferences concerning the electronic motions which dynamically interconvert successive species along the reaction path (as represented by curved arrows, for example) are often included in the description of a mechanism. It should be noted that for many reactions all this information is not available and the suggested mechanism is based on incomplete experimental data. It is not appropriate to use the term mechanism to describe a statement of the probable sequence in a set of stepwise reactions. That should be referred to as a reaction sequence, and not a mechanism.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03804 "reaction mechanism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03805))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03805 "mechanism-based inhibition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03805 "Irreversible inhibition of an enzyme due to its catalysis of the reaction of an artificial substrate. Also called 'suicide inhibition'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03805 "suicide inhibition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03806 "median")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03806 "Depending on whether the number of observations is even or odd, the median can be estimated as follows:   If \\(n=2\\ m+1\\): The middle value of a series of observations, arranged in increasing or decreasing order.   If \\(n=2\\ m\\): The arithmetic mean of the two middle values of a series of observations, arranged in increasing or decreasing order.   Comment: The use of the median when reporting results of chemical analysis is generally not recommended, because its statistical efficiency is less than that of the mean. In certain cases, however, especially when treating small sets of data, the median may offer advantages because it is a so-called 'robust statistic', i.e. it offers considerable resistance to the effects of isolated outliers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03807))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03807 "median effective concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03807 "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to produce a certain effect in 50% of test organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03808))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03808 "median effective dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03808 "Statistically derived dose of a chemical or physical agent (radiation) expected to produce a certain effect in 50% of test organisms in a given population or to produce a half-maximal effect in a biological system under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03809 "median lethal concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03809 "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to kill 50% of organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03810 "median lethal dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03810 "Statistically derived dose of a chemical or physical agent (radiation) expected to kill 50% of organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03811 "median lethal time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03811 "Statistically derived average time interval during which 50% of a given population may be expected to die following acute administration of a chemical or physical agent (radiation) at a given concentration under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03812 "median narcotic concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03812 "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to cause narcotic conditions in 50% of a given population under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03813))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03813 "median narcotic dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03813 "Statistically derived dose of a substance expected to cause narcosis in 50% of test animals under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03814))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03814 "medium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03814 "The phase (and composition of the phase) in which chemical species and their reactions are studied in a particular investigation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03815))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03815 "medium effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03815 "The medium effect on ionic species B due to transfer from solvent \\(\\rm{S}_{1}\\) to solvent \\(\\rm{S}_{2}\\) (number) is defined by \\[R\\ T\\ \\ln \\gamma _{\\rm{S}_{1}}^{\\rm{S}_{2}}\\left(\\rm{B}\\right)=\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{o},\\rm{S}_{2}}- \\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{o},\\rm{S}_{1}}\\] where \\(R\\) is the gas constant, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature and \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{o},\\rm{S}_{i}}\\) is the standard chemical potential of B in solvent \\(\\rm{S}_{i}\\) (where \\(i\\) = 1 or 2), the reference states being the same in both solvents. \\(\\gamma _{\\rm{S}_{1}}^{\\rm{S}_{2}}(\\rm{B})\\) is not an exactly measurable quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03816))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03816 "medium-pressure mercury lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03816 "Non-coherent radiation source containing mercury vapour at pressures ranging from \\(50\\) to several hundred \\(\\mathrm{kPa}\\) (\\(1\\ \\rm{atm} = 101.325\\ \\rm{kPa}\\)). Emits mostly from \\(200\\) to \\(1000\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with the most intense lines approximately at \\(218\\), \\(248\\), \\(254\\), \\(266\\), \\(280\\), \\(289\\), \\(297\\), \\(303\\), \\(313\\), \\(334\\), \\(366\\), \\(406\\), \\(408\\), \\(436\\), \\(546\\) and \\(578\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03816 "medium-pressure mercury arc")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03817 "mega")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03817 "SI prefix for \\(10^{6}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{M}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03818 "meiosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03818 "The reductive cell division which results in daughter cells containing one copy of each of the chromosome of the parent. The entire meiotic process involves two separate divisions (meiosis I and meiosis II). The first division is a true reductive division with the chromosome number being halved, whereas the second division resembles mitosis in many ways. Thus, a diploid parental cell will give rise to haploid daughter cells (cf. ploidy).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03819 "Meisenheimer adduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03819 "A cyclohexadienyl derivative formed as Lewis adduct from a nucleophile (Lewis base) and an aromatic or heteroaromatic compound, also called Jackson–Meisenheimer adduct. In earlier usage the term 'Meisenheimer complex' was restricted to the typical Meisenheimer alkoxide adduct of nitro-substituted aromatic ethers, e.g.  Analogous cationic adducts, such as:  considered to be reaction intermediates in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions, are called 'Wheland intermediates', and sometimes, inappropriately, $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}complexes}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03821))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03821 "melting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03821 "The conversion of a solid to a liquid by the application of heat and/or pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03822 "melting point corrected")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03822 "The term originally signified that a correction was made (not made) for the emergent stem of the thermometer. In current usage it often means that the accuracy of the thermometer was (was not) verified. This current usage is inappropriate and should be abandoned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03823 "membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03823 "A general term which refers to a continuous layer, usually consisting of a semi-permeable material, with controlled permeability covering a structure, such as carbon or an inert metal, or separating two electrolyte solutions. This latter case is the most general form of an ion-selective electrode. The membrane separates the internal components of the ion-selective electrode from the test solution. The membrane of an ion-selective electrode is responsible for the EMF response and selectivity of the entire electrode. Comment: Membranes of sensor electrodes are thought to be practically homogeneous, but an actual membrane may contain inhomogeneous regions, often at surfaces, and connected with materials and preparation methods used. Inhomogeneous regions include low dielectric polymer regions with few charge sites and regions with high local site densities. Surface regions of plasticized liquid membranes often are low in sites and high in plasticizer or exuded impurities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03824 "membrane emf")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03824 "The potential difference \\(E_{\\rm{m}}\\) between two saturated $\\ce{KCl}$ bridges inserted into two solutions separated by a membrane. The solutions need not be in equilibrium with one another and need not contain any colloidal material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03825 "membrane potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03825 "Synonymous with membrane emf.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03826 "membrane sites")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03826 "Membranes frequently contain built-in 'fixed' charged sites (e.g. immobile $\\ce{–(SO3^{−})}$ in polystyrenesulfonate), or intentionally added, hydrophobically trapped, 'mobile' sites [e.g. tetraphenylborate in plasticized poly(vinyl chloride)]. Such membranes with charged sites are named sited membranes. Ions of opposite sign in the membrane are 'counter-ions'. Ions of the same sign as sites are not present in significant quantities, and are co-ions. Sited membranes are 'permselective' to counter-ions i.e. only counter-ions exchange into the membrane and therefore have some mobility in the membrane bulk.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03827 "memory effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03827 "In instruments used for atmospheric trace component analysis, the dependence of an instrument reading on one or several previous sample(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03828 "<i>mer</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03828 "An affix used in names to denote meridional; three groups occupying vertices of an octahedral coordination sphere in such a relationship that one is cis to the two others which are themselves trans, not now recommended for precise nomenclature purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03834 "mercury flow system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03834 "For mercury released directly as atomic vapour, different direct transfer systems are used. In an open dynamic system the liberated analyte is transported by a carrier gas through the sampling or excitation source and swept away. In an open static system the equilibrated gaseous phase is forced into the absorption cell by displacement, e.g. by water. During measurement the gaseous phase is thus static. In a closed system the analyte and carrier gas are circulated through the absorption cell and the generator vessel until equilibrium between the liquid and gaseous phases is established.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03835 "mercury–xenon lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03835 "Non-coherent intense source of ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a mixture of mercury vapour and xenon under high pressure.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03835 "mercury–xenon arc")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03836 "mero")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03836 "A prefix meaning part, partial or fragment, used in formation of compound words, e.g. merocyanines are compounds related to cyanines but have a nitrogen atom at only one end of the chromogenic system instead of at both ends.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03837 "merry-go-round reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03837 "An apparatus in which several samples are rotated around a radiation source in order to expose each to equal amounts of radiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03837 "turntable reactor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03838 "<i>meso</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03838 "A prefix to indicate the presence of a symmetry element of the second kind (see chirality) in a chemical species which is usually one of a set of diastereoisomers that also includes a chiral member.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03839 "<i>meso</i>-compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03839 "A term for the achiral member(s) of a set of diastereoisomers which also includes one or more chiral members. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03840 "mesogenic monomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03840 "A monomer which can impart the properties of liquid crystals to the polymers formed by its polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03841 "mesogenic pitch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03841 "A pitch with a complex mixture of numerous essentially aromatic hydrocarbons. It does not contain anisotropic particles detectable by optical microscopy. Mesogenic pitch is low in quinoline-insoluble fractions and capable of transforming into mesophase pitch during continuous heat treatment above \\(750\\ \\rm{K}\\) by the formation of optically detectable carbonaceous mesophase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03842 "mesoionic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03842 "Dipolar five- (possibly six-) membered heterocyclic compounds in which both the negative and the positive charge are delocalized, for which a totally covalent structure cannot be written, and which cannot be represented satisfactorily by any one polar structure. The formal positive charge is associated with the ring atoms, and the formal negative charge is associated with ring atoms or an exocyclic nitrogen or chalcogen atom. Mesoionic compounds are a subclass of betaines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03843))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03843 "mesolytic cleavage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03843 "Cleavage of a bond in a radical ion whereby a radical and an ion are formed. The term reflects the mechanistic duality of the process, which can be viewed as homolytic or heterolytic depending on how the electrons are attributed to the fragments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03844 "mesomeric effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03844 "The effect (on reaction rates, ionization equilibria, etc.) attributed to a substituent due to overlap of its p- or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ with the p- or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ of the rest of the molecular entity. delocalization is thereby introduced or extended, and electronic charge may flow to or from the substituent. The effect is symbolized by M. Strictly understood, the mesomeric effect operates in the ground electronic state of the molecule. When the molecule undergoes electronic excitation or its energy is increased on the way to the transition state of a chemical reaction, the mesomeric effect may be enhanced by the electromeric effect, but this term is not much used, and the mesomeric and electromeric effects tend to be subsumed in the term resonance effect of a substituent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03845 "mesomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03845 "Essentially synonymous with resonance. The term is particularly associated with the picture of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ as less localized in an actual molecule than in a Lewis formula. The term is intended to imply that the correct representation of a structure is intermediate between two or more Lewis formulae.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03846))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03846 "mesomorphic phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03846 "Often formed by concentrated systems of surfactants. These are states of matter in which anisometric molecules (or particles) are regularly arranged in one (nematic state) or two (smectic state) directions, but randomly arranged in the remaining direction(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03847 "mesomorphic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03847 "A transition that occurs between a fully-ordered crystalline solid and an isotropic liquid. Mesomorphic transitions can occur (i) from a crystal to a liquid crystal (ii) from a liquid crystal to another liquid crystal and (iii) from a liquid crystal to an isotropic liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03848))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03848 "mesopause")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03848 "That region of the atmosphere between the mesosphere and the thermosphere at which the temperature is a minimum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03849))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03849 "mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03849 "A phase occurring over a definite range of temperature, pressure, or concentration within the mesomorphic state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03850 "mesophase pitch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03850 "A pitch with a complex mixture of numerous essentially aromatic hydrocarbons containing anisotropic liquid-crystalline particles (carbonaceous mesophase) detectable by optical microscopy and capable of coalescence into the bulk mesophase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03851 "mesophase pitch-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03851 "Mesophase pitch-based carbon fibres (MPP-based carbon fibres) are carbon fibres obtained from mesogenic pitch after it has been transformed into mesophase pitch (MPP) at least during the process of spinning, and after the spun mesophase pitch fibres have been made non-fusible (stabilized) and carbonized.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03851 "MPP-based carbon fibres")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03852 "mesophiles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03852 "Microorganisms which grow at moderate temperatures in the range \\(20{-}45^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), and which have an optimum growth temperature in the range \\(30{-}39^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03853))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03853 "mesopore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03853 "Pore of intermediate size. [Those with widths exceeding about \\(0.05\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) or \\(50\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (\\(500\\ \\mathring{\\rm{A}}\\)) are called macropores; those with widths not exceeding about \\(2\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (\\(20\\ \\mathring{\\rm{A}}\\)) are called micropores].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03854 "mesoscale")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03854 "In meteorology, the size or scale of phenomena smaller than ordinary cyclones or weather systems but larger than such microscale phenomena as the thickness of the boundary layer, the wakes of objects, etc. Thunderstorms involve mesoscale processes, and other meteorological events the size of cities are usually mesoscale processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03855))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03855 "mesosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03855 "That region of the atmosphere which lies above the stratopause (about \\(4752\\ \\rm{km}\\)) and below the mesopause (about \\(8090\\ \\rm{km}\\)) and in which temperature decreases with increasing height; this is the region in which the lowest temperatures of the atmosphere occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03856 "meso structures")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03856 "Relative configurations of consecutive, but not necessarily contiguous, constitutionally equivalent carbon atoms that have a symmetrically-constituted connecting group (if any) are designated 'meso' or 'racemo', as appropriate. Examples:  (The symbol  represents a symmetrically-constituted connecting group, such as $\\ce{–CH2\\bond{–}}$, $\\ce{–CH2–CH2\\bond{–}}$, or $\\ce{–CR2–CH2–CR2\\bond{–}}$ .)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03857 "messenger RNA")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03857 "An RNA molecule that transfers the coding information for protein synthesis from the chromosomes to the ribosomes. mRNA is formed from a DNA template by transcription. It may be a copy of a single gene or of several adjacent genes (polycistronic mRNA). On the ribosome, the sequence is converted into the programmed amino acid sequence through translation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03858 "metabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03858 "The entire physical and chemical processes involved in the maintenance and reproduction of life in which nutrients are broken down to generate energy and to give simpler molecules (catabolism) which by themselves may be used to form more complex molecules (anabolism). In case of heterotrophic organisms, the energy evolving from catabolic processes is made available for use by the organism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03859 "metabolite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03859 "Any intermediate or product resulting from metabolism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03860 "metal–carbene complexes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03860 "Metal complexes of the type $\\ce{R2CML_{n}}$ ($\\ce{M}$ = metal, $\\ce{L}$ = ligand) in which formally a carbene is coordinated to a metal, e.g.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03860 "carbene metal complexes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03860 "carbene–metal complexes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03861 "metal–carbyne complexes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03861 "Metal complexes of the type $\\ce{RCML_{n}}$ ($\\ce{M}$ = metal, $\\ce{L}$ = ligand) in which formally a carbyne is coordinated to a metal, e.g.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03861 "carbyne metal complexes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03861 "carbyne–metal complexes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03863 "metal–insulator transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03863 "A transition characterized by a sudden change in electrical transport properties (conductivity) due to a reversible change from localized to itinerant behaviour of the electrons. Example: The transition at \\(339\\ \\rm{K}\\) in $\\ce{VO2}$ where it changes from a high-temperature metallic behaviour to a low-temperature semiconductor behaviour. Synonymous with metal–nonmetal transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03863 "metal–nonmetal transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03864))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03864 "metallacycloalkanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03864 "Monocyclic compounds containing a metal atom and saturated carbon atoms as ring members, e.g.:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03865 "metallocenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03865 "Organometallic coordination compounds in which one atom of a transition metal such as iron, ruthenium or osmium is bonded to and only to the face of two cyclopentadienyl $\\ce{[\\upeta\\!^{5}\\mbox{-}(C5H5)]}$ ligands which lie in parallel planes. The term should not be used for analogues having rings other than cyclopentadienyl as ligands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03866 "metallurgical coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03866 "Metallurgical coke is produced by carbonization of coals or coal blends at temperatures up to \\(1400\\ \\rm{K}\\) to produce a macroporous carbon material of high strength and relatively large lump size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03868 "metal to ligand charge transfer transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03868 "An electronic transition of a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer from the metal to a ligand has occurred.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03868 "MLCT transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03869 "metal to metal charge transfer transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03869 "An electronic transition of a bi- or poly-nuclear metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer between two metal centres has occurred.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03869 "MMCT transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03870 "metamagnetic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03870 "An antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic transition for \\(H_{\\rm{applied}}>H_{\\rm{c}}\\) and/or \\(T>T_{\\rm{t}}\\) where \\(H_{\\rm{c}}\\) and \\(T_{\\rm{t}}\\) refer to the critical magnetic field and transition temperature, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03871 "metastability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03871 "A term that describes the state of a phase in which an energy barrier considerably higher than \\(k\\ T\\) must be surmounted before this phase can transform to a phase of lower molar Gibbs energy and molar Helmholtz energy, where \\(k\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03874 "metastable ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03874 "An ion which is formed with sufficient excitation to dissociate spontaneously during its flight from the ion source to the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03875 "metastable state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03875 "An isomeric state in an energy state higher than the ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03876 "metastable state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03876 "Any excited state which in principle, by virtue of the selection rules, cannot radiatively combine with any lower state. It usually has a considerably longer lifetime than an ordinary excited state.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03876 "metastable")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03878 "metathesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03878 "A bimolecular process formally involving the exchange of a bond (or bonds) between similar interacting chemical species so that the bonding affiliations in the products are identical (or closely similar) to those in the reactants. For example:  (The term has its origin in inorganic chemistry with a different meaning, but this older usage is not applicable in physical organic chemistry.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03879))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03879 "methanogens")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03879 "Strictly anaerobic archaebacteria, able to use only a very limited substrate spectrum (e.g. molecular hydrogen, formate, methanol, methylamine, carbon monoxide or acetate) as electron donors for the reduction of carbon dioxide to methane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03880 "method of isotopic perturbation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03880 "NMR shift difference measurement of the isotope effect on a fast (degenerate) equilibrium between two species which are equivalent except for isotopic substitution. This can be used to distinguish a rapidly equilibrating mixture with time-averaged symmetry from a single structure with higher symmetry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03883))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03883 "methylotrophic microorganisms")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03883 "Organisms that use, as substrates for growth, $\\ce{C}$ compounds containing carbon in a lower oxidation state than in carbon dioxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03884 "metre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03884 """The metre, symbol \\(\\pu{m}\\), is the SI unit of length. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the speed of light in a vacuum \\(c\\) to be \\(\\pu{299792458}\\) when expressed in the unit \\(\\pu{m s-1}\\), where the second is defined in terms of the caesium frequency \\(\\Delta 
u_{\\ce{Cs}}\\).""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03884 "meter")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03885 "micellar catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03885 "The acceleration of a chemical reaction in solution by the addition of a surfactant at a concentration higher than its critical micelle concentration so that the reaction can proceed in the environment of surfactant aggregates (micelles). (Rate enhancements may be due, for example, to higher concentration of the reactants in that environment, more favourable orientation and solvation of the species, or enhanced rate constants in the micellar pseudophase of the surfactant aggregate.) Micelle formation can also lead to a decreased reaction rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03886))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03886 "micellar mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03886 "The term usually refers to a neutral micelle and therefore includes an equivalent amount of counter-ions with the surfactant ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03887))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03887 "micellar solubilization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03887 "In a system formed by a solvent, an association colloid and at least one other component (the solubilizate), the incorporation of this other component into or on the micelles is called micellar solubilization, or, briefly, solubilization.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03887 "solubilization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03889 "micelle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03889 "Surfactants in solution are often association colloids, that is, they tend to form aggregates of colloidal dimensions, which exist in equilibrium with the molecules or ions from which they are formed. Such aggregates are termed micelles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03890))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03890 "micelle charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03890 "Usually understood to include the net charge of the surfactant ions and the counterions bound to the micelle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03891 "Michaelis constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03891 "Substance concentration of substrate at which the rate of reaction is equal to one half of the limiting rate (maximum rate).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03892 "Michaelis–Menten kinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03892 """The dependence of an initial rate of reaction upon the concentration of a substrate S that is present in large excess over the concentration of an enzyme or other catalyst (or reagent) E with the appearance of saturation behaviour following the Michaelis-Menten equation: \\[
u=\\frac{V\\ \\left[\\rm{S}\\right]}{K_{\\rm{m}}+\\left[\\rm{S}\\right]}\\] where \\(
u\\) is the observed initial rate, \\(V\\) is its limiting value at substrate saturation (i.e. \\(\\left[\\rm{S}\\right]\\gg K_{\\rm{m}}\\)), and \\(K_{\\rm{m}}\\) the substrate concentration when \\(
u = \\frac{V}{2}\\). The definition is experimental, i.e. it applies to any reaction that follows an equation of this general form. The symbols \\(V_{\\max }\\) or \\(
u_{\\max }\\) are sometimes used for \\(V\\). The parameters \\(V\\) and \\(K_{\\rm{m}}\\) (the 'Michaelis constant') of the equation can be evaluated from the slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\(
u^{-1}\\) vs. \\(\\left[\\rm{S}\\right]^{-1}\\) (a 'Lineweaver–Burk plot') or from slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\(
u\\) vs. \\(\\frac{
u}{\\left[\\rm{S}\\right]}\\) ('Eadie–Hofstee plot'). A Michaelis–Menten equation is also applicable to the condition where E is present in large excess, in which case the concentration \\(\\left[\\rm{E}\\right]\\) appears in the equation instead of \\(\\left[\\rm{S}\\right]\\). The term has sometimes been used to describe reactions that proceed according to the scheme: \\[\\rm{E}+\\rm{S}\\overset{k_{1}}{\\underset{k_{-1}}\\rightleftarrows }\\rm{ES}\\overset{k_{\\rm{cat}}}{\\rightarrow }\\rm{Products}\\] in which case \\(K_{\\rm{m}} = \\frac{k_{-1}+k_{\\rm{cat}}}{k_{1}}\\) (Briggs–Haldane conditions). It has more usually been applied only to the special case in which \\(k_{-1}\\gg k_{\\rm{cat}}\\) and \\(K_{\\rm{m}} = \\frac{k_{-1}}{k_{1}}=K_{s}\\); in this case \\(K_{\\rm{m}}\\) is a true dissociation constant (Michaelis–Menten conditions).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03893 "Michaelis–Menten mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03893 "The Michaelis–Menten mechanism is the simplest mechanism that will explain Michaelis–Menten kinetics. According to the mechanism, a substrate A first combines with a molecule of enzyme E, and this process is followed by a step in which the enzyme-substrate complex EA breaks down (sometimes with the participation of the solvent) into enzyme and reaction products: \\[\\rm{E}+\\rm{A}\\overset{k_{1}}{\\underset{k_{-1}}\\rightleftharpoons }\\rm{EA}\\overset{k_{2}}{\\rightarrow }\\rm{E}+\\rm{Products}\\] If, as is usual, the substrate A is present in great excess of the enzyme it can be shown that steady-state conditions apply, and that the rate equation is: \\[v = \\frac{k_{2}\\ \\left[\\rm{E}\\right]_{0}\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]}{\\frac{k_{-1}\\,+\\,k_{2}}{k_{1}}\\,+\\,\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]}\\] where \\(\\left[\\rm{E}\\right]_{0}\\) is the total concentration of enzyme. This equation is of the form of the Michaelis–Menten equation. Other, more complicated, mechanisms lead to the Michaelis–Menten equation, adherence to which therefore does not require that the Michaelis– Menten mechanism applies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03894 "micro")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03894 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-6}\\) (symbol: \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03895 "microbial leaching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03895 "A process for the solubilization of metals, mostly from poor ores, by lithotrophic bacteria.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03896 "microcanonical rate constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03896 "A rate constant calculated by microcanonical variational transition-state theory and therefore a rate constant that relates to a fixed energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03897 "microcanonical variational transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03897 "A development of transition-state theory in which the dividing surface is varied so as to minimize the rate calculated for a fixed energy. The rate expressions obtained in a microcanonical treatment can be integrated over the energy, taking into account the statistical distribution over energy states, so as to give the canonical, or thermal, rates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03898 "microcarrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03898 "A small, beaded material, derived from silica, glass, dextran or similar materials, used for the immobilization of biocatalysts or as a support for the culture of anchorage-dependent animal cell lines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03899 "microclimatology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03899 "The science that deals with the climate of restricted areas and investigates their phenomena and causes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03900))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03900 "microelectrophoresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03900 "The electrophoresis technique involving the movement of a mass of particles on a small scale (e.g. paper electrophoresis).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03901))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03901 "microgel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03901 "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\(0.1\\) to \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03901 "gel microparticle")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03902 "microheterogeneity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03902 "A particular glycoprotein may occur in forms that differ in the structure of one or more or its carbohydrate units, a phenomenon known as microheterogeneity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03903 "micrometeorology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03903 "The study of the meteorological processes on scales from a millimeter or less up to tens or hundreds of metres; e.g. meteorology of a local site that is usually small and often is confined to a shallow layer of air next to the ground.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03904))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03904 "micro-network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03904 "Polymer network that has dimensions of the order of \\(1\\ \\rm{nm}\\) to \\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03905 "microphotometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03905 "The optical instrument used to measure transmittance in atomic emission spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03906 "micropore")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03906 "Pore with width not exceeding about \\(2.0\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (\\(20\\ \\mathrm{Å}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03907 "micropore filling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03907 "The process in which molecules are adsorbed in the adsorption space within micropores.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03908 "micropore volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03908 "Conventionally measured by the volume of the adsorbed material which completely fills the micropores expressed in terms of bulk liquid at atmospheric pressure and at the temperature of measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03909 "microporous carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03909 "A porous carbon material, usually a char or carbon fibres, which may or may not have been subjected to an activation process to increase its adsorptive properties. A microporous carbon is considered to have a major part of its porosity in pores of less than \\(2\\ \\rm{nm}\\) width and to exhibit apparent surface areas usually higher than \\(200\\ \\) to \\(300\\ \\rm{m}^{2}\\ \\rm{g}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03911 "microscopic cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03911 "A measure of the probability of a specified interaction or reaction between an incident radiation and a target particle or system of particles. It is the reaction rate per target particle for a specified process divided by the flux of the incident radiation. In general, a specification is added of the type of radiation (e.g. neutron, photon), the energy of the incident radiation (e.g. thermal, epithermal, fast) and the type of interaction of reaction (e.g. activation, fission, scattering).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03912))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03912 "microscopic diffusion control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03912 "The observable consequence of the limitation that the rate of a bimolecular chemical reaction in a homogeneous medium cannot exceed the rate of encounter of the reacting molecular entities. If (hypothetically) a bimolecular reaction in a homogeneous medium occurred instantaneously when two reactant molecular entities made an encounter, the rate of reaction would be an encounter-controlled rate, determined solely by rates of diffusion of reactants. Such a hypothetical 'fully diffusion controlled rate' is also said to correspond to 'total microscopic diffusion control', and represents the asymptotic limit of the rate of reaction as the rate constant for the chemical conversion of the encounter pair into product (or products) becomes large relative to the rate constant for separation (or dissociation) of the encounter pair. 'Partial microscopic diffusion control' is said to operate in a homogeneous reaction when the rates of chemical conversion and of separation are comparable. (The degree of microscopic diffusion control cannot usually be determined with any precision.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03912 "encounter control")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03912 "diffusion control")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03912 "transport control")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03913))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03913 "microscopic electrophoresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03913 "A technique in which the electrophoresis of individual particles is observed with the aid of a microscope or ultra-microscope.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03914 "microscopic film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03914 "A film which has lateral dimensions in the range \\(0.1{-}100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03915 "microscopic kinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03915 "This term applies to a kinetic study in which the emphasis is on the interpretation of the behaviour in terms of elementary reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03916))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03916 "microscopic reversibility at equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03916 "The principle of microscopic reversibility at equilibrium states that, in a system at equilibrium, any molecular process and the reverse of that process occur, on the average, at the same rate. This definition corresponds to the statement of the principle that was given by R.C. Tolman in 1924. However, many workers have interchanged the meanings of microscopic reversibility and detailed balance, and it seems best now to regard the two, which are closely related, as synonymous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03917 "microsome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03917 "Artefactual spherical particle, not present in the living cell, derived from pieces of the endoplasmic reticulum present in homogenates of tissues or cells: microsomes sediment from such homogenates when centrifuged at \\(10^{6}\\ \\rm{g}\\) and higher: the microsomal fraction obtained in this way is often used as a source of mono-oxygenase enzymes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03918 "middle atmosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03918 "The combined stratosphere and mesosphere in the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03919))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03919 "Mie scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03919 "The scattering of electromagnetic radiation by spherical particles of any size \\(r\\), relative to the wavelength, \\(\\lambda \\). Since the cases \\(r\\ll \\lambda \\) and \\(r\\gg \\lambda \\) are covered by Rayleigh (dipole) scattering and geometric scattering theories, respectively, Mie scattering often refers to the case of \\(r\\approx \\lambda \\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03920))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03920 "migration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03920 "The (usually intramolecular) transfer of an atom or group during the course of a molecular rearrangement.The movement of a bond to a new position, within the same molecular entity is known as 'bond migration'. Allylic rearrangements, e.g.  exemplify both types of migration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03921))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03921 "migration current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03921 "The difference between the current that is actually obtained, at any particular value of the potential of the indicator or working electrode, for the reduction or oxidation of an ionic electroactive substance and the current that would be obtained, at the same potential, if there were no transport of that substance due to the electric field between the electrodes. The sign convention regarding current is such that the migration current is negative for the reduction of a cation or for the oxidation of an anion, and positive for the oxidation of a cation or the reduction of an anion. Hence the migration current may tend to either increase or decrease the total current observed. In any event the migration current approaches zero as the transport number of the electroactive substance is decreased by increasing the concentration of the supporting electrolyte, and hence the conductivity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03923 "migratory aptitude")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03923 "The term is applied to characterize the relative tendency of a group to participate in a rearrangement. In nucleophilic rearrangements ( migration to an electron-deficient centre), the migratory aptitude of a group is loosely related to its capacity to stabilize a partial positive charge, but exceptions are known, and the position of hydrogen in the series is often unpredictable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03924))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03924 "migratory insertion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03924 "A combination of migration and insertion. The term is mainly used in organometallic chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03925))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03925 "milli")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03925 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-3}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{m}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03926))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03926 "milligram equivalent of readability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03926 "The product of readability and the value of the scale division (in \\(\\rm{mg}\\) per division).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03927))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03927 "millimetre of mercury")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03927 "Non-SI unit for pressure; \\(\\rm{mmHg}\\approx 133.322\\ \\rm{Pa}\\). This old unit is not recognized by CIPM or ISO, but it is used for blood pressure.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03927 "millimeter of mercury")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03928 "milling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03928 "The mechanical reduction of the particle size of a sample by attribution (friction), impact or cutting. The required particle size of a sample is related to the size of the test portion and the number of particles required to ensure homogeneity among test portions. The reduction in particle size may sometimes result in particles of different hardness and density, which produces inhomogeneity during the preparation of the test sample or during the withdrawal of the test portion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03928 "grinding")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03929 "minimum consumption time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03929 "The time during which nebulization must be carried out in order to perform one analysis with a given precision. The minimum consumption time is equal to the minimum volume consumed of the prepared sample divided by the rate of fluid consumption in order to obtain a given precision. It can be reduced by means of integrating the signal or measuring the signal increments (kinetic methods).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03930 "minimum density of states criterion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03930 "This is a criterion used in Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus (RRKM) theory for locating the transition state. The reaction coordinate \\(r\\) at the transition state, according to this criterion, is given by \\(\\frac{\\partial (N\\left(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\right))}{\\partial r}=0\\), where \\(N\\left(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\right)\\) is the density of states at an internal energy \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03931))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03931 "minimum-energy reaction path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03931 "Path orthogonal to the equipotential contours of a potential energy surface that connects the energy minima through a saddle point (transition state) from which it slopes downwards along the steepest descending lines in \\(3N-6\\) configurational space (\\(N\\) is the number of nuclei in the reacting system).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03932 "minimum lethal concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03932 "Lowest concentration of a toxic substance in an environmental medium that kills individual organisms or test species under a defined set of conditions. Synonymous with lowest lethal concentration found.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03932 "lowest lethal concentration found")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03933 "minimum lethal dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03933 "Lowest amount of a substance that, when introduced into the body, may cause death to individual species of test animals under a defined set of conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03935))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03935 "minute")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03935 "Non-SI unit of plane angle. \\[1^{\\prime} = (1/60)^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} = 2.908\\ 882\\,...\\,\\times 10^{-4}\\rm{rad}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03936))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03936 "mist")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03936 "A qualitative term applied to a suspension of droplets in a gas. In the atmosphere a mist produces a generally thin, greyish veil over the landscape. It reduces visibility to a lesser extent than fog but somewhat more than haze (visibility of less than \\(2\\ \\rm{km}\\) but greater than \\(1\\ \\rm{km}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03937))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03937 "mitochondria")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03937 "Organelles appearing in all eukaryotic cells which produce ATP as useful energy for the cell by oxidative phosphorylation. The proteins for the adenosine 5'-triphosphate (ATP)-generating electron transport of the respiration chain are located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Mitochondria contain many enzymes of the citric acid cycle and for fatty acid $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}oxidation}$. Many of them are coded for by nuclear DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03938))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03938 "mitosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03938 "The process whereby a cell nucleus divides into two daughter nuclei, each having the same genetic complement as the parent cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03940 "mixed crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03940 "A crystal containing a second constituent which fits into and is distributed in the lattice of the host crystal. (The use of 'solid solution' for amorphous materials is not recommended.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03940 "solid solution")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03941 "mixed energy release")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03941 "This is an effect found in a process A + BC when the attacking species A is heavy. Because A is heavy the A–B distance continues to decrease while the repulsive energy of B–C is being released.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03942 "mixed indicator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03942 "An indicator containing a supplementary dye selected to heighten the overall colour change.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03943 "mixed labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03943 "A specifically labelled compound is referred to as mixed labelled when the isotopically substituted compound has more than one kind of modified atom e.g. $\\ce{CH3–CH2–[^{18}O][^{2}H]}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03944 "mixed potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03944 """The value of the potential of a given electrode with respect to a suitable reference electrode when appreciable contributions to the total anodic and/or cathodic partial currents are made by species belonging to two or more different couples, but the total current is zero, i.e. \\[I=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}I_{i}=0\\qquad (I_{i}
eq 0)\\] where \\(I_{i}\\) is the partial current of reaction \\(i\\) \\[I_{i}=I_{i,a}+I_{i,c}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03945 "mixing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03945 "The combining of components, particles or layers into a more homogeneous state. The mixing may be achieved manually or mechanically by shifting the material with stirrers or pumps or by revolving or shaking the container. The process must not permit segregation of particles of different size or properties. homogeneity may be considered to have been achieved in a practical sense when the sampling error of the processed portion is negligible compared to the total error of the measurement system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03946))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03946 "mixing control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03946 "The experimental limitation of the rate of reaction in solution by the rate of mixing of solutions of the two reactants. It can occur even when the reaction rate constant is several powers of 10 less than that for an encounter-controlled rate. Analogous (and even more important) effects of the limitation of reaction rates by the speed of mixing are encountered in heterogeneous (solid/liquid, solid/gas, liquid/gas) systems.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03946 "macroscopic diffusion control")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03947))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03947 "mixing height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03947 "The height to which significant mixing of added pollutants occurs within the atmosphere. In reference to stack gases, it is considered the height at which stack effluent begins mixing with the atmosphere as it leaves the stack.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03948 "mixing ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03948 "In meteorology, the dimensionless ratio of the mass of a substance (such as water vapour) in an air parcel to the mass of the remaining substances in the air parcel. For trace substances, this is approximated by the ratio of the mass of the substance to the mass of air. However, in the case of water vapour the mass of dry air is used. In atmospheric chemistry, mixing ratios (molecular, molar, by volume, as well as by weight) are used to describe relative concentrations of atmospheric trace gases and impurities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03949))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03949 "mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03949 "Portion of matter consisting of two or more chemical substances called constituents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03950 "MLCT")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03950 "Abbreviation for Metal to Ligand Charge Transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03951))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03951 "mobile adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03951 "A situation in which molecules of adsorbate are free to move about the surface. Mobility of the adsorbate will increase with temperature and mobile adsorption may be either localized or non-localized. In localized mobile adsorption the adsorbate spends most of the time on the adsorption sites but can migrate or be desorbed and readsorbed elsewhere. In non-localized adsorption the mobility is so great that a small fraction of the adsorbed species is on the desorption sites and a large fraction at other positions on the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03952 "mobile phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03952 "A fluid which percolates through or along the stationary bed, in a definite direction. It may be a liquid (liquid chromatography) or a gas (gas chromatography) or a supercritical fluid (supercritical-fluid chromatography). In gas chromatography the expression carrier gas may be used for the mobile phase. In elution chromatography the expression 'eluent' is also used for the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03954))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03954 "mobile-phase velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03954 "The linear velocity of the mobile phase across the average cross-section of the chromatographic bed or column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03955))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03955 "mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03955 "Drift velocity of electrons or holes divided by the electric field strength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03956 "mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03956 "The velocity of a particle per unit applied force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03957))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03957 "Möbius aromaticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03957 "A monocyclic array of orbitals in which there is a single out-of-phase overlap (or, more generally, an odd number of out-of-phase overlaps) reveals the opposite pattern of aromatic character to Hückel systems; with \\(4\\, n\\) electrons it is stabilized (aromatic), whereas with \\(4\\, n+2\\) it is destabilized (antiaromatic). In the excited state \\(4\\, n+2\\) Möbius $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ systems are stabilized, and \\(4\\, n\\) systems are destabilized. No examples of ground-state Möbius $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ systems are known, but the concept has been applied to transition states of pericyclic reactions. The name is derived from the topological analogy of such an arrangement of orbitals to a Möbius strip.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03958))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03958 "mode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03958 "The value of the variable occurring with the greatest frequency in the series of observations. A bimodal distribution is one which contains two maxima. The use of mode when reporting results of chemical analysis is generally not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03959 "mode-locked laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03959 "A laser in which many resonant modes are coupled in phase, to yield a train of very short pulses (e.g. ps pulses). The coupling of the modes is obtained by modulation of the gain in the resonator, and can be active (electro-optic modulation of the losses or of the pump intensity), or passive (with a saturable absorber).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03960 "moderation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03960 "Reduction of the neutron energy by scattering without appreciable capture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03961 "moderator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03961 "A material used to reduce the neutron energy by scattering without appreciable capture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03962))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03962 "modified active solid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03962 "An active solid the sorptive properties of which have been changed by some treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03963))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03963 "modified Arrhenius equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03963 "This is an extension of the simple Arrhenius equation in which the pre-exponential factor is proportional to \\(T^{n}\\) where \\(T\\) is the temperature and \\(n\\) a constant: \\[k = B\\ T^{n}\\ \\exp(-E_{\\rm{a}}/RT)\\] \\(B\\) is a temperature-independent constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03964 "modified sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03964 "A sample or a known fraction of the parent population in which the analyte has been isolated or (usually) concentrated before being submitted to the laboratory. If the isolation or concentration occurs in the laboratory, the procedure is usually considered part of the preparation of the test sample from the laboratory sample (in-laboratory processing).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03965 "modifier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03965 "A substance added to a solvent to improve its properties e.g. by increasing the solubility of an extractant, changing interfacial parameters or reducing adsorption losses. Additives used to enhance extraction rates should be called accelerators or catalysts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03966 "modulus of elasticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03966 "The normal stress divided by linear strain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03966 "Young's modulus")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03967 "Mohr amplification process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03967 "The process in which iodide is oxidized to iodate to achieve a more favourable measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03968 "moiety")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03968 "In physical organic chemistry moiety is generally used to signify part of a molecule, e.g. in an ester $\\ce{R1COOR2}$ the alcohol moiety is $\\ce{R2O}$. The term should not be used for a small fragment of a molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03970 "molality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03970 "Amount of entities of a solute divided by the mass of the solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03971))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03971 "molar")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03971 "Adjective before the name of an extensive quantity generally meaning division by amount of substance to make it intensive. Molar volume is volume divided by amount of substance. In a few special cases molar is used to denote division by amount concentration (e.g. molar absorption coefficient).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03972 "molar absorption coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03972 "Absorbance divided by the absorption pathlength, \\(l\\), and the amount concentration, \\(c\\): \\[\\varepsilon(\\lambda) = \\frac{1}{c\\, l}\\, \\rm{lg}\\! \\left ( \\frac{P_{\\lambda}^{0}}{P_{\\lambda}}\\right ) = \\frac{A(\\lambda)}{c\\, l}\\] where \\(P_{\\lambda}^{0}\\) and \\(P_{\\lambda}\\) are, respectively, the incident and transmitted spectral radiant power.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03972 "molar decadic absorption coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03974 "molar activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03974 "For a specified isotope, the activity of the compound divided by the amount of the material in moles: \\(A_{\\rm{m}}=\\frac{A}{n}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03977 "molarity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03977 "Term sometimes used for amount concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03978 "molar-mass exclusion limit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03978 "The maximum value of the molar mass or molecular weight of molecules or particles, in a specific polymer solvent system, that can enter into the pores of the porous non-adsorbing material used in size-exclusion chromatography. For particles with molar mass or molecular weight larger than the exclusion limit the separation effect of the size-exclusion chromatography vanishes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03978 "molecular-weight exclusion limit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03979 "molar refraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03979 "Function of the refractive index, \\(n\\), given by \\(R = V_{\\rm{m}}\\ \\frac{n^{2}\\,-\\,1}{n^{2}\\,+\\,2}\\), where \\(V_{\\rm{m}}\\) is the molar volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03980 "mole")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03980 """The mole, symbol mol, is the SI unit of amount of substance. One mole contains exactly \\(\\pu{6.02214076E23}\\) elementary entities. This number is the fixed numerical value of the Avogadro constant, \\(N_{\\rm{A}}\\), when expressed in the unit \\(\\pu{mol-1}\\) and is called the Avogadro number.\r
The amount of substance, symbol \\(n\\), of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom, a molecule, an ion, an electron, any other particle or specified group of particles.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03982 "molecular beams")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03982 "A molecular beam is produced by allowing a gas at higher pressure to expand through a small orifice into a container at lower pressure. The result is a beam of particles (atoms, free radicals, molecules or ions) moving at approximately equal velocities, with few collisions occurring between them. In a crossed molecular-beam experiment a reaction is studied using collimated beams of reactant molecules. For a bimolecular reaction, beams of the two reactants are caused to impinge on one another, often at an angle of 90°. In a beam-gas scattering experiment a collimated beam is introduced into a gas, and the scattering patterns are observed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03984 "molecular conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03984 "The conformation of the macromolecule as a whole. In the polymer literature, molecular conformation is sometimes referred to as macroconformation. In molecular conformations involving parallel stems, the latter may be confined to the same crystal or may also extend over several crystals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03985 "molecular dynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03985 "Synonymous with reaction dynamics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03986 "molecular entity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03986 "Any constitutionally or isotopically distinct atom, molecule, ion, ion pair, radical, radical ion, complex, conformer etc., identifiable as a separately distinguishable entity. Molecular entity is used in this Compendium as a general term for singular entities, irrespective of their nature, while chemical species stands for sets or ensembles of molecular entities. Note that the name of a compound may refer to the respective molecular entity or to the chemical species, e.g. methane, may mean a single molecule of $\\ce{CH4}$ (molecular entity) or a molar amount, specified or not (chemical species), participating in a reaction. The degree of precision necessary to describe a molecular entity depends on the context. For example 'hydrogen molecule' is an adequate definition of a certain molecular entity for some purposes, whereas for others it is necessary to distinguish the electronic state and/or vibrational state and/or nuclear spin, etc. of the hydrogen molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03987 "molecular formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03987 "For compounds consisting of discrete molecules, a formula according with the relative molecular mass (or the structure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03988 "molecular ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03988 "An ion formed by the removal from (positive ions) or addition to (negative ions) a molecule of one or more electrons without fragmentation of the molecular structure. The mass of this ion corresponds to the sum of the masses of the most abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the various atoms that make up the molecule (with a correction for the masses of the electron(s) lost or gained). For example, the mass of the molecular ion of ethyl bromide $\\ce{C2H5^{79}Br}$ will be \\(2\\times 12\\) plus \\(5\\times 1.0078246\\) plus 78.91839 minus the mass of the electron (\\(m_{\\rm{e}}\\)). This is equal to \\(107.95751\\ \\rm{u}-m_{\\rm{e}}\\), \\(u\\) being the unified atomic mass unit based on the standard that the mass of the isotope $\\ce{^{12}C}$ = \\(12\\ \\rm{u}\\) exactly.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03988 "molecular anion")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03988 "molecular cation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03989))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03989 "molecularity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03989 "The number of reactant molecular entities that are involved in the 'microscopic chemical event' constituting an elementary reaction. (For reactions in solution this number is always taken to exclude molecular entities that form part of the medium and which are involved solely by virtue of their solvation of solutes.) A reaction with a molecularity of one is called 'unimolecular', one with a molecularity of two 'bimolecular' and of three 'termolecular'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03990 "molecular kinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03990 "This term is applied to a kinetic study in which there is emphasis on the behaviour of the system at the molecular level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03991 "molecular laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03991 "A gas laser which makes use of electronic, vibrational or rotational energy levels in molecules. The molecules can be excited by an electric discharge, as in the case of the $\\ce{N2}$ or $\\ce{CO2}$ laser, by chemical reactions, as in the $\\ce{HF}$ or $\\ce{DF}$ lasers, or by another laser, as in the $\\ce{CH3F}$ far IR laser.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03993 "molecular mechanics calculation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03993 "An empirical calculational method intended to give estimates of structures and energies for conformations of molecules. The method is based on the assumption of 'natural' bond lengths and angles, deviation from which leads to strain, and the existence of torsional interactions and attractive and/or repulsive van der Waals and dipolar forces between non-bonded atoms. The method is also called '(empirical) force-field calculations'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03993 "force-field calculation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03994 "molecular metal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03994 "A non-metallic material whose properties resemble those of metals, usually following oxidative doping; e.g. polyacetylene following oxidative doping with iodine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03995))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03995 "molecular nucleation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03995 "The initial crystallization of a small portion of a macromolecule, after which further crystallization is thermodynamically favoured. Molecular nucleation may give rise to a new crystal or increase the size of a pre-existing one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03996 "molecular orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03996 "A one-electron wavefunction describing an electron moving in the effective field provided by the nuclei and all other electrons of a molecular entity of more than one atom. Such molecular orbitals can be transformed in prescribed ways into component functions to give 'localized molecular orbitals'. Molecular orbitals can also be described, in terms of the number of nuclei (or 'centres') encompassed, as two-centre, multi-centre, etc. molecular orbitals, and are often expressed as a linear combination of atomic orbitals. An orbital is usually depicted by sketching contours on which the wavefunction has a constant value (contour map) or by indicating schematically the envelope of the region of space in which there is an arbitrarily fixed high (say 96%) probability of finding the electron occupying the orbital, giving also the algebraic sign (+ or −) of the wavefunction in each part of that region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03997 "molecular rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03997 "The term is traditionally applied to any reaction that involves a change of connectivity (sometimes including hydrogen), and violates the so-called 'principle of minimum structural change'. According to this oversimplified principle, chemical species do not isomerize in the course of a transformation, e.g. substitution, or the change of a functional group of a chemical species into a different functional group is not expected to involve the making or breaking of more than the minimum number of bonds required to effect that transformation. For example, any new substituents are expected to enter the precise positions previously occupied by displaced groups. The simplest type of rearrangement is an intramolecular reaction in which the product is isomeric with the reactant (one type of 'intramolecular isomerization'). An example is the first step of the Claisen rearrangement:  The definition of molecular rearrangement includes changes in which there is a migration of an atom or bond (unexpected on the basis of the principle of minimum structural change), as in the reaction:  where the rearrangement stage can formally be represented as the '1,2-shift' of hydride between adjacent carbon atoms in the carbocation:  Such migrations occur also in radicals, e.g.:  The definition also includes reactions in which an entering group takes up a different position from the leaving group, with accompanying migration. An example of the latter type is the 'allylic rearrangement':  A distinction is made between 'intramolecular rearrangements' (or 'true molecular rearrangements') and 'intermolecular rearrangements' (or 'apparent rearrangements'). In the former case the atoms and groups that are common to a reactant and a product never separate into independent fragments during the rearrangement stage (i.e. the change is intramolecular), whereas in an 'intermolecular rearrangement' a migrating group is completely free from the parent molecule and is re-attached to a different position in a subsequent step, as in the Orton reaction:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M03997 "rearrangement")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03998))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03998 "molecular sieve effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03998 "With respect to porous solids, the surface associated with pores communicating with the outside space may be called the internal surface. Because the accessibility of ores may depend on the size of the fluid molecules, the extent of the internal surface may depend on the size of the molecules comprising the fluid, and may be different for the various components of a fluid mixture. This effect is known as the molecular sieve effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M03999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M03999 "molecular spectra")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M03999 "Spectra formed by bands consisting of rotational lines originating from rotational, vibrational and electronic transitions of molecules. These may be emission or absorption spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04002))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04002 "molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04002 "An electrically neutral entity consisting of more than one atom (\\(n>1\\)). Rigorously, a molecule, in which \\(n>1\\) must correspond to a depression on the potential energy surface that is deep enough to confine at least one vibrational state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04004 "molozonides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04004 "1,2,3-Trioxolanes, the primary products of the reaction of ozone at a carbon–carbon double bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04005 "moment of a force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04005 "About a point is the vector product of the radius vector from this point to a point on the line of action of the force and the force, \\(M=r\\times F\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04006 "moment of inertia")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04006 "Of a body about an axis is the sum of the products of its mass elements and the squares of their distances to the axis. principal moments of inertia of a molecule are chosen so that \\(I_{A}\\leq I_{B}\\leq I_{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04007 "momentum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04007 "Vector quantity equal to the product of mass and velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04008 "momentum spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04008 "A spectrum which is obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the momentum-to-charge ratios of the ionic species contained within it. A sector magnetic field achieves separation of the various ionic species in this way. If the ion beam is homogeneous in translational energy, as is the case with sector instruments, separation according to the \\(\\frac{m}{z}\\) ratios is also achieved and a mass spectrum is obtained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04009 "monochromator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04009 "Optical device that transmits a mechanically selectable narrow band of wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation chosen from a wider range of wavelengths available at the input.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04010 "monoclonal antibodies")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04010 "A single species of immunoglobulin molecules produced by culturing a single clone of a hybridoma cell. MAbs recognize only one chemical structure, i.e. they are directed against a single epitope of the antigenic substance used to raise the antibody.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04011 "monodisperse medium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04011 "A colloidal system in which all particles are of (nearly) the same size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04013))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04013 "mono-energetic radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04013 "Radiation consisting of particles of a single kinetic energy or photons of a single energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04014 "monoisotopic mass spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04014 "A spectrum containing only ions made up of the principal isotopes of atoms making up the original molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04015 "monolayer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04015 "A single, closely packed layer of atoms or molecules. The term floating monolayer is used for certain spread monolayers or films which are in the condensed — often solid — state. The term 'Langmuir monolayer' has recently been coined for spread monolayers. This usage is not recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04015 "Langmuir monolayer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04016 "monolayer capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04016 "For chemisorption the amount of adsorbate which is needed to occupy all adsorption sites as determined by the structure of the adsorbent and by the chemical nature of the absorptive. For physisorption, the amount needed to cover the surface with a complete monolayer of atoms or molecules in close-packed array, the kind of close packing having to be stated explicitly when necessary.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04017 "monomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04017 "A substance composed of monomer molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04018))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04018 "monomeric unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04018 "The largest constitutional unit contributed by a single monomer molecule to the structure of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04018 "monomer unit")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04018 "mer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04019 "monomer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04019 "A molecule which can undergo polymerization thereby contributing constitutional units to the essential structure of a macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04021 "monosaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04021 "A term which includes aldoses, ketoses and a wide variety of derivatives. Derivation includes oxidation, deoxygenation, introduction of other substituents, alkylation and acylation of hydroxy groups, and chain branching. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04022 "monotectic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04022 "The reversible transition, on cooling, of a liquid to a mixture of a second liquid and a solid: \\[\\rm{liquid}_{1}\\rightleftarrows \\rm{liquid}_{2}+\\rm{solid}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04023 "monotectoid reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04023 "A reaction in a system containing two solid solution phases, \\(\\alpha\\mathit{'}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\mathit{''}\\) in which \\(\\alpha\\mathit{'}\\) decomposes into \\(\\alpha\\mathit{''}\\) and a new phase \\(\\beta \\): \\[\\alpha\\mathit{'}\\rightleftharpoons \\alpha\\mathit{''}+\\beta \\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04024 "monotectoid temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04024 "The maximum temperature at which a monotectoid reaction can occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04026 "monoterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04026 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C10}$ skeleton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04028 "monotropic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04028 "The irreversible transition from a metastable polymorphic form to the stable polymorph. Example: The transition of metastable $\\ce{CaCO3}$ (aragonite-type) to the stable $\\ce{CaCO3}$ (calcite-type).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04029 "mordant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04029 "Substance that fixes a dyestuff in or on a material by combining with the dye to form an insoluble compound, used to fix or intensify stains in a tissue or cell preparation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04030 "More O'Ferrall–Jencks diagram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04030 "Visualization of the potential energy surfaces for a reacting system, as a function of two chosen coordinates. It is particularly useful to discuss structural effects on the transition state geometry for processes occurring either by stepwise or concerted routes. The use of such diagrams, first suggested for elimination reactions, was later extended to acid–base catalysis and to certain other reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04031))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04031 "Morin transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04031 "A transition specific to $\\ce{\\upalpha-Fe2O3}$ in which there is a change in the direction of the atomic magnetic moments in the antiferromagnetic state from parallel to perpendicular to the c-axis. Synonymous with spin-flop transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04031 "spin-flop transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04032 "morphotropic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04032 "An abrupt change in the structure of a solid solution with variation in composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04033 "Mössbauer effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04033 "Resonance absorption of γ-radiation by nuclei arranged in a crystal lattice in such a way that the recoil momentum is shared by many atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04034 "Mössbauer thickness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04034 "The effective thickness of a source (\\(t_{s}\\)) or absorber (\\(t_{a}\\)) in the optical path.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04035 "most probable distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04035 "A discrete distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form: \\[f_{\\rm{w}}(x) = a^{2}\\ x\\ (1- a)^{x- 1}\\] where \\(x\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\(a\\) is a positive adjustable parameter. For large values of \\(x\\), the most probable distribution converges to the particular case of the Schulz–Zimm distribution with \\(b=1\\). In the literature, this distribution is sometimes referred to as the Flory distribution or the Schulz–Flory distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04037 "Mott transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04037 "A transition occurring only in 'single-valent' systems from strongly correlated (\\(U > W\\)) to weakly correlated (\\(U < W\\)) electrons as a result of a change of bandwidth \\(W\\). \\(W\\) reflects the strength of the interatomic interactions in a periodic array of like atoms and \\(U\\) is a measure of the intra-atomic interactions, i.e. the electrostatic energy involved in the creation of polar states by transferring an electron from one atom to the next, as shown schematically by the disproportionation reaction: \\[\\rm{M}^{n+}+\\rm{M}^{n+}\\rightarrow \\rm{M}^{(n+1)+}+\\rm{M}^{(n- 1)+}\\] A \\(U > W\\) produces a magnetic semiconductor; a \\(U < W\\) gives rise to a metal (normally nonmagnetic). Note also that \\(U\\) and \\(W\\) can be altered by changes in interatomic distances, brought about through temperature or pressure variations or by introducing an alloying element. Synonymous with Mott–Hubbard transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04037 "Mott–Hubbard transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04041 "mucopolysaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04041 "Polysaccharides composed of alternating units from uronic acids and glycosamines, and commonly partially esterified with sulfuric acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04042 "multi-centre bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04042 "Representation of some molecular entities solely by localized two-electron two-centre bonds appears to be unsatisfactory. Instead, multi-centre bonds have to be considered in which electron pairs occupy orbitals encompassing three or more atomic centres. Examples include the three-centre bonds in diborane, the delocalized $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$ of benzene and bridged carbocations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04043))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04043 "multi-centre reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04043 "A synonym for pericyclic reaction. The number of 'centres' is the number of atoms not bonded initially, between which single bonds are breaking or new bonds are formed in the transition state. This number does not necessarily correspond to the ring size of the transition state for the pericyclic reaction. Thus, a Diels–Alder reaction is a 'four-centre reaction'. This terminology has largely been superseded by the more detailed one developed for the various pericyclic reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04044))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04044 "multi-channel pulse height analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04044 "A pulse amplitude analyser which includes a storage function to record the number of pulses received per channel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04046 "multienzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04046 "A protein possessing more than one catalytic function contributed by distinct parts of a polypeptide chain ('domains'), or by distinct subunits, or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04047 "multienzyme complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04047 "A multienzyme with catalytic domains on more than one type of polypeptide chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04048 "multienzyme polypeptide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04048 "A polypeptide chain containing at least two types of catalytic domains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04049))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04049 "multilayer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04049 "A system of adjacent layers or monolayers. The term bilayer applies to the particular case of a multilayer two monolayers thick. Monolayers and multilayers may be alternatively named 'films' provided boundaries can be defined for them.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04050 "multilayer adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04050 "Adsorption such that the adsorption space accommodates more than one layer of molecules, and not all adsorbed molecules are in contact with the surface layer of the adsorbent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04051 "multilayer aggregate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04051 "A stack of lamellar crystals generated by spiral growth at one or more screw dislocations. The axial displacement over a full turn of the screw (Burgers vector) is usually equal to one lamellar thickness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04053 "multiphoton ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04053 "Occurs when an atom or molecule and their concomitant ions have energy states whereby the energy in two or more photons is absorbed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04054 "multiphoton process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04054 "A process involving interaction of two or more photons with a molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04056 "multiple-pass cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04056 "Permits multiple passage of radiation to increase the absorption pathlength. It is constructed in such a way that mirrors either form part of the cell or are mounted in the sample cell holder.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04057 "multiple peak scanning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04057 "An alternative to multiple ion detection which emphasizes that monitoring of peaks rather than detection of ions is the important feature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04058 "multiple scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04058 "Successive rescattering of radiation within the scattering medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04059 "multiplex spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04059 "A spectrometer in which a single photodetector simultaneously receives signals from different spectral bands which are specifically encoded. In the case of frequency multiplexing, each spectral band is modulated at a specific frequency. Decoding is achieved by filtering out, by electronic means, the corresponding signals. Frequency multiplexing may be realized by changing the path difference between the two interfering beams at a uniform rate. Fourier transform of the interferogram so obtained yields the spectrum. This method is called Fourier transform spectrometry (FTS).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04061 "multiplicative name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04061 "A name that expresses the multiple occurrence of identical parent structures, two or more of which are connected by a symmetrical structure expressible by means of a multivalent simple or composite prefix.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04062 "multiplicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04062 "The number of possible orientations, calculated as \\(2\\ S+1\\), of the spin angular momentum corresponding to a given total spin quantum number (\\(S\\)), for the same spatial electronic wavefunction. A state of singlet multiplicity has \\(S=0\\) and \\(2\\ S+1=1\\). A doublet state has \\(S=\\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(2\\ S+1=2\\), etc. Note that when \\(S>L\\) (the total orbital angular momentum possible) there are only \\(2\\ L+1\\) orientations of total angular momentum possible.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:M04062 "spin multiplicity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04063 "multiply labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04063 "A selectively labelled compound may be multiply labelled when in the unmodified compound there is more than one atom of the same element at the position where the isotopic modification occurs, e.g. $\\ce{H}$ in $\\ce{CH4}$, or there are several atoms of the same element at different positions where the isotopic modification occurs, e.g. $\\ce{C}$ in $\\ce{C4H8O}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04065 "multistage sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04065 "Samples taken in a series of steps with the sampling portions constituting the sample (units or increments) at each step being selected from the larger or greater number of portions of the previous step, or from a primary or composite sample. The first set of portions (units or increments) taken from the population available for sampling is the primary sample. The subsequent samples (secondary, tertiary, etc.) are the sets of subsamples, units, items, individuals or increments taken from the preceding step. The units may be different steps of multistage sampling (e.g. pallets, cases, packages).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04066 "multi-strand chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04066 "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through more than four atoms, more than two on at least one side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04067 "multi-strand macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04067 "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through more than four atoms, more than two on at least one side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04068))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04068 "munchnones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04068 "Mesoionic compounds having a 1,3-oxazole skeleton bearing an oxygen atom attached to the 5-position with the following delocalized structure:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04069 "muonium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04069 "Atom-like particle consisting of a positive muon and an electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04071 "mustards")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04071 "Compounds having two $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}haloalkyl groups}$ bound to a sulfur atom, as in $\\ce{(XC2H2C2H2)2S}$, and their analogues, the nitrogen (and phosphorus) mustards, $\\ce{(XC2H2C2H2)2NR}$. Compounds having one $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}haloalkyl group}$ and one $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}hydroxyalkyl group}$ on $\\ce{S}$ are termed hemi- or semi-mustards by some chemists.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04072 "mutagenesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04072 "The introduction of permanant heritable changes i.e. mutations into the DNA of an organism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04073 "mutarotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04073 "The change in optical rotation accompanying epimerization. In sugar chemistry this term usually refers to epimerization at the hemiacetal carbon atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04074 "mutation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04074 "A heritable change in the nucleotide sequence of genomic DNA (or RNA in RNA viruses), or in the number of genes or chromosomes in a cell, which may occur spontaneously or be brought about by chemical mutagens or by radiation (induced mutation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04075 "mutation rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04075 "The frequency (\\(\\rm{h}^{-1}\\)) with which a mutation occurs within a organism or gene. In general, rates of spontaneous mutation vary between one in \\(10^{4}\\) and one in \\(10^{8}\\) per gene per generation, and can be considerably increased by mutagens.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04076 "mutual inductance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04076 "For two thin conducting loops, the magnetic flux through one loop, caused by an electric current in the other loop, divided by that current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:M04077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:M04077 "Myelin cylinders")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:M04077 "Birefringent cylinders which form spontaneously from lipid-containing material in contact with water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06760 "mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06760 """Mass transport is described by a mobility \\(b\\), defined by: \\[b_{i} = \\frac{
u _{i}}{\\boldsymbol{F}_{i}}\\] where \\(
u _{i}\\) is the average drift velocity and \\(\\boldsymbol{F}_{i}\\) is the force on particle \\(i\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06765 "metalloenzyme")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06765 "An enzyme that, in the active state, contains one or more metal ions which are essential for its biological function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06778 "magnetic circular dichroism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06778 "Magnetic circular dichroism is observed when a sample differentially absorbs left- and right- circularly polarized light in a magnetic field parallel to the light beam.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06781))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06781 "magnetic susceptibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06781 "For paramagnetic materials, the magnetic susceptibility may be measured experimentally and used to give information on the molecular magnetic dipole moment, and hence on the electronic structure of the molecules in the material. The paramagnetic contribution to the molar magnetic susceptibility of a material, \\(\\chi \\), is related to the molecular magnetic dipole moment \\(m\\) by the curie relation: \\(\\chi = \\frac{\\rm{constant x}\\ m^{2}}{T}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06795 "magnetic resonance imaging")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06795 "The visualisation of the distribution of nuclear spins (usually water) in a body by using a magnetic field gradient (NMR imaging). A similar technique, but less widely used, is to visualise the distribution of paramagnetic centres (EPR imaging).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06830))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06830 "metabolic half-life")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06830 "Time required for one-half of the quantity of a substance in the body to be metabolised.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:MT06830 "metabolic half-time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06831 "monitoring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06831 "Continuous or repeated observation, measurement, and evaluation of health and/or environmental or technical data for defined purposes, according to prearranged schedules in space and time, using comparable methods for sensing and data collection.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06832))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06832 "Monte Carlo study")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06832 "Simulation and analysis of a sequence of events using random numbers to generate possible outcomes in an iterative process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06833 "mutagen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06833 "Agent that can induce heritable changes (mutations) of the genotype in a cell as a consequence of alterations or loss of genetic material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06853))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06853 "mesomorphic state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06853 "A state of matter in which the degree of molecular order is intermediate between the perfect three-dimensional, long-range positional and orientational order found in solid crystals and the absence of long-range order found in isotropic liquids, gases, and amorphous solids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06858 "mesogenic group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06858 "A part of a molecule or macromolecule endowed with sufficient anisotropy in both attractive and repulsive forces to contribute strongly to mesophase or, in particular, liquid-crystal mesophase formation in low-molar-mass and polymeric substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06869 "main-chain polymer liquid crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06869 "A polymer, whose molecules have mesogenic units only in their main chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06870 "mesogen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06870 "A compound that under suitable conditions of temperature, pressure, and concentration can exist as a mesophase or, in particular, as a liquid-crystal (LC) phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06878 "membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06878 "Structure, having lateral dimensions much greater than its thickness, through which transfer may occur under a variety of driving forces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06887))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06887 "microfiltration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06887 "Pressure-driven membrane-based separation process in which particles and dissolved macromolecules larger than \\(0.1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) are rejected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06910))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06910 "maximum latent period")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06910 "The length of time after which no causal relationship can reasonably be established. This length of time is the period from the last exposure to the point of time at which an exposed person has demonstrated the initial signs or symptoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06924))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06924 "migration time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06924 "The time required for the analyte to move through the effective length of the capillary, \\(L_{\\rm{eff}}\\), that is, from the sample introduction point to the detection point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06927))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06927 "migration time of micelles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06927 "Experimentally determined as the migration time of a compound that is completely retained in the micellar phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06928 "mass distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06928 "Defined as: \\[k_{\\rm{MEKC}} = \\frac{n_{\\rm{mc}}}{n_{\\rm{aq}}} = K\\cdot \\frac{V_{\\rm{mc}}}{V_{\\rm{aq}}}\\] where \\(n_{\\rm{mc}}\\) and \\(n_{\\rm{aq}}\\) are the chemical amounts of the analyte in the micellar and aqueous phases, respectively, \\(K\\) is the distribution constant and \\(V_{\\rm{mc}}\\) and \\(V_{\\rm{aq}}\\) are the corresponding volumes of the phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06929 "mass distribution ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06929 "Defined analogously to mass distribution ratio (in micellar electrokinetic chromatography), \\(k_{\\rm{MEEKC}}\\) by replacing terms for micelles with corresponding terms for micro-emulsion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06966 "MOL file format")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06966 "The MOL file format is used to encode chemical structures, substructures and conformations as text-based connection tables. It is used by MDL Information Systems Inc. (e.g., in their MACCS or ISIS programs).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06967 "molecular connectivity index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06967 "A molecular connectivity index is a numeric descriptor derived from molecular topology.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06968 "molecular design")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06968 "Molecular design is the application of all techniques leading to the discovery of new chemical entities with specific properties required for the intended application.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06969))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06969 "molecular dynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06969 "Molecular dynamics is a simulation procedure consisting of the computation of the motion of atoms in a molecule or of individual atoms or molecules in solids, liquids and gases, according to Newton's laws of motion. The forces acting on the atoms, required to simulate their motions, are generally calculated using molecular mechanics force fields.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06970 "molecular graphics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06970 "Molecular graphics is a technique for the visualization and manipulation of molecules on a graphical display device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06971))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06971 "molecular modeling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06971 "Molecular modeling is the investigation of molecular structures and properties using computational chemistry and graphical visualization techniques in order to provide a plausible three-dimensional representation under a given set of circumstances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06972 "molecular shape")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06972 "The molecular shape is an attribute of a molecule dealing with spatial extension, form, framework, or geometry. It is often described by, e.g., principal axes, ovality, or connectivity indices.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06973 "molfile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06973 "A molfile is a table containing atom type, connectivity and a more or less arbitrary 2D or 3D information about a molecule. Well-known file formats include the MOLfile used by MDL Information Systems Inc. (e.g., in the database MACCS), the MOL2 file used by Tripos Associates (e.g., in the modeling package SYBYL), or the CSSR format.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT06974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT06974 "multivariate statistics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT06974 "Multivariate statistics is a set of statistical tools to analyze data (e.g., chemical and biological) matrices using regression and/or pattern recognition techniques.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07068))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07068 "maximum hardness principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07068 "A chemical system at a given temperature will evolve to a configuration of maximum absolute hardness, $\\upeta\\!$, provided that the potential due to the nuclei, plus any external potential and the electronic chemical potential, remain constant. In terms of molecular orbital theory, the highest value of $\\upeta\\!$ reflects the highest possible energy gap between the lowest unoccupied and highest occupied molecular orbitals; this value correlates with the stability of a system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:MT07068 "principle of maximum hardness")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07069 "molecular graph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07069 "The graph with differently labelled (coloured) vertices (chromatic graph) which represent different kinds of atoms and differently labelled (coloured) edges related to different types of bonds. Within the topological electron distribution theory, a complete network of the bond paths for a given nuclear configuration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07070))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07070 "molecular graph theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07070 "The theory which deals with analyses of all consequences of connectivities inherent in molecular structure and chemical transformations. The theory does not produce numerical data but uses available data and searches for regularities that can be attributed to combinatorial and topological origins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07071 "molecular Rydberg state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07071 "An excited electronic state which is composed primarily of atomic orbitals with principal quantum numbers greater than that of the ground state and the valence excited states. Such electronic states typically have a large polarizability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07072))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07072 "Monte Carlo method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07072 "In mathematics, a method originally used for calculating multiple integrals by means of a random sample. The method is used for numerical modelling of many-particle chemical systems, in particular liquids; it is based on the equilibrium statistical mechanics theory. Observables A are calculated as mean values over a great number (\\(\\cong\\rm{10}^{\\rm{5}}-\\rm{10}^{\\rm{6}}\\)) of instant configurations as determined by coordinates of the particles. \\[< A >\\ = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum _{i=1}^{N}A\\{r_{i}\\}\\] where \\(N\\) is the number of configurations. At the first stage, various configurations are randomly generated and then those energetically unrealizable eliminated. An efficient search for the most probable configurations to be entered into the above expression is provided by the Metropolis algorithm based on the principle of Markov's chain theory. While being elaborated for the study of equilibrium chemical systems, MC method is also applied to studies of the dynamics of chemical reactions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:MT07072 "method of Monte Carlo")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07073 "Morse potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07073 "The empirical function relating the potential energy of a molecule to the inter-atomic distance r accounting for the anharmonicity of bond stretching: \\[E\\left(r\\right) = D_{\\rm{e}}\\ (1- \\rm{e}^{-a\\ (r- r_{\\rm{e}})})^{2}\\] where \\(D_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the bond-dissociation energy, \\(r_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the equilibrium bond length, and \\(a\\) is a parameter characteristic of a given molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07074 "Mulliken population analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07074 "A partitioning scheme based on the use of density and overlap matrixes of allocating the electrons of a molecular entity in some fractional manner among its various parts (atoms, bonds, orbitals). As with other schemes of partitioning the electron density in molecules, MPA is arbitrary and strongly dependent on the particular basis set employed. However, comparison of population analyses for a series of molecules is useful for a quantitative description of intra-molecular interactions, chemical reactivity and structural regularities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07075 "multiconfiguration SCF method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07075 "Configuration-interaction method in which simultaneous optimization of both the shapes of molecular orbitals and contributions from different electronic configurations is carried out by using the variational method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07140 "main-chain scission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07140 "Chemical reaction that results in the breaking of main-chain bonds of a polymer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07141 "Mechano-chemical reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07141 "Chemical reaction that is induced by the direct absorption of mechanical energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07177 "macroporous polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07177 "Glass or rubbery polymer that includes a large number of macropores (\\(50\\ \\rm{nm}\\)–\\(1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter) that persist when the polymer is immersed in solvents or in the dry state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07230 "miscibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07230 "Capability of a mixture to form a single phase over certain ranges of temperature, pressure, and composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07270 "miscibility gap")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07270 "Area within the coexistence curve of an isobaric phase diagram (temperature vs composition) or an isothermal phase diagram (pressure vs composition).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07284 "morphology coarsening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07284 "Process by which phase domains increase in size during the aging of a multiphase material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07285 "morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07285 "Shape, optical appearance, or form of phase domains in substances, such as high polymers, polymer blends, composites, and crystals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07287))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07287 "multiphase copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07287 "Copolymer comprising phase-separated microdomains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07303))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07303 "microdomain morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07303 "Morphology consisting of phase microdomains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07307))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07307 "multicoat morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07307 "Morphology observed in a blend of a block copolymer with the homopolymer of one of the blocks and characterized by alternating concentric shells of the copolymer and the homopolymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07309 "multiple inclusion morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07309 "Multiphase morphology in which dispersed phase domains of one polymer contain and completely encapsulate many phase domains of a second polymer that may have the same composition as the continuous phase domain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:MT07309 "salami-like morphology")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07419))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07419 "magic angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07419 "Upon excitation of an 'isotropic' sample (assuming an ultra short excitation pulse) the relationship between the fluorescence intensity detected at a time \\(t\\) and through a polarization analyser oriented at an angle \\(\\beta\\) with respect to the electric polarization of the exciting beam is given by \\[I(t,\\beta ) \\propto  N(t)\\left [ 1 + (3\\, \\rm{cos}^{2}\\, \\beta - 1)R(t) \\right ]\\] where \\(R(t)\\) is the degree of alignment of the emitting transition dipole in the laboratory frame and \\(N(t)\\) is the excited-state population, both at time \\(t\\). For \\(\\beta = 54.7^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) (the magic angle), the dipole-alignment contribution vanishes and \\(I(t,\\,\\beta = 54.7^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} ) \\propto N(t)\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07422))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07422 "molecular orientation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07422 "Absorption probability (referred to electric dipolar absorption) for a molecular transition with its electric transition moment at an angle \\(\\theta\\) with the electric vector of the light is proportional to \\(\\cos^{2}\\theta\\). For the whole sample it is proportional to the orientation factor \\(K_{\\theta} = \\:< \\cos^{2}\\theta >\\), averaged over all sample molecules. This average is \\(1\\) for a sample with all transition moments perfectly aligned along the electric vector of the light, \\(1/3\\) for an isotropic sample and \\(0\\) for a sample where all transition moments are perpendicular to the electric vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07424 "multireference configuration interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07424 "Configuration-interaction method in which the configurations are built by excitation out of a wavefunction obtained by using, e.g., the multiconfiguration SCF method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07533 "mixed ceramic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07533 "Ceramic material consisting of co-continuous interpenetrating networks of two or more metal carbides, nitrides or oxides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07559 "monolith")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07559 "Shaped, fabricated, intractable article with a homogeneous microstructure which does not exhibit any structural components distinguishable by optical microscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07568 "model network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07568 "Polymer network synthesized using a reactant or reactants of known molar mass or masses and chemical structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:MT07637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:MT07637 "microsyneresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:MT07637 "Syneresis in which the exudation of the liquid is from microscopic regions within a network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N00350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N00350 "ångström")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N00350 "Non-SI unit of length, \\(\\rm{Å} = 10^{-10}\\ \\rm{m}\\), widely used in molecular physics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04078 "n → π* state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04078 "An excited state related to the ground state by a n → π* transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04079 "n → π* transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04079 "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'non-bonding' (lone-pair) n orbital to an 'antibonding' $\\ce{\\uppi\\! orbital}$ designated as $\\ce{\\uppi\\!^{\\ast}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04080 "n-σ* delocalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04080 "Delocalization of a free electron pair (n) into an antibonding $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ ($\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}}$).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04080 "n-σ* no bond resonance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04081 "n → σ* transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04081 "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'non-bonding' (lone-pair) n orbital to an 'antibonding' $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ designated as $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}}$. Such transitions generally involve high transition energies and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04082 "nano")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04082 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-9}\\) (symbol: \\(\\rm{n}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04083 "nanoscopic film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04083 "A film which has lateral dimensions in the range of \\(0.1{-}100\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04084 "naphthenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04084 "Cycloalkanes especially cyclopentane, cyclohexane and their alkyl derivatives. The term seems to be obsolescent, except in the petrochemical industry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04085 "naphthenic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04085 "Acids chiefly monocarboxylic, derived from naphthene. The term seems to be obsolescent, except in the petrochemical industry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04086 "narcissistic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04086 "A chemical reaction that can be described as the conversion of a reactant into its mirror image, without rotation or translation of the product, so that the product enantiomer actually coincides with the mirror image of the reactant molecule. Examples of such reactions are cited under the entries fluxional and degenerate rearrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04087 "natural broadening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04087 "Broadening which has its origin in the finite optical lifetime of one or both levels involved in the transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04088 "natural graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04088 "A mineral found in nature. It consists of graphitic carbon regardless of its crystalline perfection.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04089 "natural isotopic abundance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04089 "Of a specified isotope of an element, the isotopic abundance in the element as found in nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04091 "natural radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04091 "Radiation originating from natural radioactivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04092 "natural radioactivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04092 "Radioactivity of naturally occurring nuclide in materials where the isotopic abundance of that nuclide is natural.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04095 "needle coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04095 "The commonly used term for a special type of coke with extremely high graphitizability resulting from a strong preferred parallel orientation of its turbostratic layer structure and a particular physical shape of the grains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04096 "negative adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04096 "The depletion of one or more components in an interfacial layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04098 "negative ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04098 "An atom, radical, molecule or molecular moiety in the vapour phase which has gained one or more electrons thereby acquiring an electrically negative charge. The use of the term anion as an alternative is not recommended because of its connotations in solution chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04100 "neighbouring group participation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04100 "The direct interaction of the reaction centre (usually, but not necessarily, an incipient carbenium centre) with a lone pair of electrons of an atom or with the electrons of a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ contained within the parent molecule but not conjugated with the reaction centre. A distinction is sometimes made between n-, $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}participation}$. A rate increase due to neighbouring group participation is known as 'anchimeric assistance'. 'Synartetic acceleration' is the special case of anchimeric assistance ascribed to participation by electrons binding a substituent to a carbon atom in a $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}position}$ relative to the leaving group attached to the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}carbon}$ atom. According to the underlying model, these electrons then provide a three-centre bond (or 'bridge') 'fastening together' (as the word 'synartetic' is intended to suggest) the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}carbon}$ atoms between which the charge is divided in the intermediate bridged ion formed (and in the transition state preceding its formation). The term synartetic acceleration is not widely used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04103 "neodymium laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04103 "A CW pulsed laser emitting radiation from excited The $\\ce{Nd^{3+}}$ principally occurring around \\(1.06\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) (the precise position depends on the matrix). The $\\ce{Nd^{3+}}$ is present as a dopant in suitable crystals (e.g. yttrium–aluminium garnet, YAG) or in suitable glasses (phosphate, silicate, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04106 "neper")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04106 "Unit, coherent with the SI, for expressing field levels, \\(\\rm{Np}=1\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04107 "nephelometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04107 "Analytical methods which depend on the measurement of the intensity of scattered light emanating from an illuminated volume of an aerosol. The ratio of scattered intensity to illuminating intensity is compared with a standard of known properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04108))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04108 "Nernst's diffusion layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04108 "A fictitious layer corresponding to the dotted straight lines of the diagram which shows the concentration profile along the direction perpendicular to an electrode surface. The thickness \\(\\delta \\) of this layer is called the effective (or equivalent) thickness of the diffusion layer. Its definition is apparent from the figure. It is the thickness which the diffusion layer would have if the concentration profile were a straight line coinciding with the tangent to the true concentration profile at the interface, and that straight line were extended up to the point where the bulk concentration is reached. \\(\\delta \\) has a formal significance only. It is simply another way of writing the mass transfer coefficient \\(k_{\\rm{d}}\\) defined in terms of a resistivity instead of a conductivity. N04108.png")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04109 "net")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04109 "A two-dimensional lattice (in a particular range of surface coverage and temperature) into which the adsorbate is ordered, especially in some cases of localized adsorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04110))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04110 "net current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04110 "The sum of cathodic and anodic partial currents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04111))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04111 "net electric charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04111 "The algebraic sum of the charges present at the surface of the particle divided by the elementary charge of the proton. The symbol \\(z\\) is also used for charge number of an ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04112))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04112 "network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04112 "A highly ramified macromolecule in which essentially each constitutional unit is connected to each other constitutional unit and to the macroscopic phase boundary by many permanent paths through the macromolecule, the number of such paths increasing with the average number of intervening bonds; the paths must on the average be co-extensive with the macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04113))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04113 "network polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04113 "A polymer composed of one or more networks.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04113 "polymer network")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04115 "neutrino")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04115 "An elementary particle of negligible mass and zero electric charge. There are also a muon neutrino and tau neutrino.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04115 "electron neutrino")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04116 "neutron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04116 "Nuclear particle of zero charge, spin quantum number \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and a mass of \\(1.008\\ 664\\ 904(14)\\ \\rm{u}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04117 "neutron density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04117 "The number of free neutrons divided by the containing volume. Partial densities may be defined for neutrons characterized by such parameters as energy and directions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04118))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04118 "neutron multiplication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04118 "The process in which a neutron produces on the average more than one neutron in a medium containing fissile material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04119 "neutron number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04119 "Number of neutrons in an atomic nucleus.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04120))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04120 "neutron rest mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04120 "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\(m_{\\rm{n}} = \\pu{1.6749286(10)E-27 kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04132 "neutron temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04132 "The temperature assigned to a population of neutrons when this population is approximated by a Maxwellian distribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04133))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04133 "neutrophilic organisms")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04133 "Organisms preferring a neutral medium for growth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04134 "Newman projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04134 "A projection formula representing the spatial arrangement of bonds on two adjacent atoms in a molecular entity. The structure appears as viewed along the bond between these two atoms, and the bonds from them to other groups are drawn as projections in the plane of the paper. The bonds from the atom nearer to the observer are drawn so as to meet at the centre of a circle representing that atom. Those from the further atom are drawn as if projecting from behind the circle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04135 "newton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04135 "SI derived unit of force, \\(\\pu{N} = \\pu{kg m s-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04136 "Newton black film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04136 "In soap films, two types of equilibrium film are often observed, sometimes successively in the same system: one characterized by thicknesses of the order of \\(7\\ \\rm{nm}\\) or more which varies significantly with minor changes in composition such as ionic strength, and the other having a lesser thickness relatively independent of such changes. When a distinction is needed, the former are designated as common black films, and the latter as Newton black films. The current use of first or secondary for the common black film and of second, primary or Perrin's for the Newton black film is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04137))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04137 "Newton diagram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04137 "A vector diagram used to show the relationship between initial and final velocities or momenta in a two-particle scattering process. It is commonly used to relate laboratory and centre-of-mass coordinates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04138))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04138 "Newtonian fluid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04138 "A fluid in which the components of the stress tensor are linear functions of the first spatial derivatives of the velocity components. These functions involve two material parameters (taken as constants throughout the fluid, although depending on ambient temperature and pressure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04140 "<i>nido</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04140 "An affix used in names to denote a nest-like structure, especially a boron skeleton that is almost closed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04141 "Nier–Johnson geometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04141 "An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrometer in which a deflection of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) radians in a radial electrostatic field analyser is followed by a magnetic deflection of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) radians. The electrostatic analyser uses a symmetrical object-image arrangement and the magnetic analyser is used asymmetrically.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04142 "NIH shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04142 "The intramolecular hydrogen migration which can be observed in enzymatic and chemical hydroxylations of aromatic rings. It is evidenced by appropriate deuterium labelling, i.e.  In enzymatic reactions the NIH shift is generally thought to derive from the rearrangement of arene oxide intermediates, but other pathways have been suggested. (NIH stands for U.S. National Institutes of Health where the shift was discovered.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04143 "nimbostratus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04143 "An amorphous, dark grey, rainy cloud layer reaching almost to the ground; \\(300{-}600\\ \\rm{m}\\) vertical velocities of \\(0.05{-}0.2\\ \\rm{m s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04144 "nitramines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04144 "Amines substituted at N with a nitro group (a contracted form of N-nitroamines); they are thus amides of nitric acid, and the class is composed of nitramide, $\\ce{O2NNH2}$, and its derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04145 "nitrenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04145 "The neutral compound $\\ce{HN{:}}$ having univalent nitrogen, and its derivatives $\\ce{RN}$. Aminylenes is a recognized but less widely used synonym. Other names that have been used include aminediyls, imidogens, azenes. They may exist in either a singlet or a triplet electronic state (four spin-paired electrons, or two spin-paired and two with parallel spins, respectively), e.g. $\\ce{CH3N{:}}$ methylnitrene.Until the 1960s, nitrenes had a totally different meaning: analogues of nitrones in which the double-bonded oxygen is replaced by double-bonded carbon, thus azomethine ylides.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04145 "aminylenes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04145 "azenes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04145 "imidogens")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04146 "nitrenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04146 "The cation ($\\ce{H2N{:}^{+}}$) and its N-hydrocarbyl derivatives ($\\ce{R2N{:}^{+}}$), in which the nitrogen has a positive charge, and two unshared electrons. A synonymous but less widely used term is aminylium ions. The alkylidene derivatives of ($\\ce{H2N{:}^{+}}$), ($\\ce{R2C=N{:}^{+}}$), still belong to the class nitrenium ions, but are more precisely designated by the term iminylium ions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04146 "aminylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04146 "imidonium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04146 "imin")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04146 "imine radical")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04147 "nitrification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04147 "Sequential oxidation of ammonium salts to nitrite and nitrate by microorganisms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04148 "nitrile imides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04148 "Ylides having the structure . Also known as 'nitrile imines' or 'nitrilimines', especially in the German literature.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04148 "nitrilimines")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04148 "nitrile imines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04150 "nitrile oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04150 "Ylides having the structure .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04151 "nitriles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04151 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC#N}$; thus C-substituted derivatives of hydrocyanic acid, $\\ce{HC#N}$. In systematic nomenclature, the suffix nitrile denotes the triply bound $\\ce{#N}$ atom, not the carbon atom attached to it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04152 "nitrile sulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04152 "Sulfur analogues of nitrile oxides .")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04153 "nitrile ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04153 "1,3-dipolar compounds having the structure:  The uncharged canonical form  is called an alkylidene- (or hydrocarbylidene-) amino carbene; the name iminyl carbene is incorrect because iminyl is not a recognized prefix for $\\ce{–N=CR2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04155))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04155 "nitrilium betaines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04155 "Derivatives of nitriles having the general structure $\\ce{R–C#N^{+}–Y^{−}}$. A subclass of 1,3-dipolar compounds including nitrile imides, nitrile oxides, nitrile sulfides and nitrile ylides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04156 "nitrilium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04156 "Cations derived formally by attachment of one hydron to the nitrogen atom of a nitrile and hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. E.g.  benzonitrilium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04157))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04157 "nitrimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04157 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{O2NN=CR2}$ (also called N-nitroimines).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04158 "nitro compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04158 "Compounds having the nitro group, $\\ce{NO2}$ (free valence on nitrogen), which may be attached to carbon, nitrogen (as in nitramines), or oxygen (as in nitrates), among other elements (in the absence of specification, C-nitro compounds are usually implied).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04159 "nitrogen fixation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04159 "The assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen by microbial reduction to ammonia and conversion into organonitrogen compounds such as amino acids. Only a limited number of microorganisms are able to fix nitrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04160 "nitrogen laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04160 "A source of pulsed semi-coherent superradiance mainly around \\(337\\ \\rm{nm}\\). The lasing species is molecular nitrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04163 "nitrolic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04163 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(=NOH)NO2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04164 "nitrones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04164 """The N-oxides of imines, that have the structure $\\ce{R2C=N+(O−)R'}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$). Synonymous with azomethine oxides. N-Oxides $\\ce{R2C=N+(O−)H}$ may be included.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04164 "azomethine oxides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04166 "nitrosamides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04166 "Although this term has, regrettably, been used to mean the N-nitroso derivatives of amides, logically it refers to amides of nitrous acid; the unstable parent compound $\\ce{H2NNO}$ is, in fact, named nitrosamide, but its derivatives formed by substitution with hydrocarbyl groups are by long custom called nitrosamines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04167 "nitrosamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04167 "N-Nitroso amines, compounds of the structure $\\ce{R2NNO}$. Compounds $\\ce{RNHNO}$ are not ordinarily isolable, but they, too, are nitrosamines. The name is a contraction of N-nitrosoamine and, as such, does not require the N locant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04168 "nitrosimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04168 "N-Nitroso imines $\\ce{O=NN=CR2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04169 "nitroso compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04169 "Compounds having the nitroso group, $\\ce{–NO}$. attached to carbon, or to another element, most commonly nitrogen or oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04170 "nitrosolic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04170 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(=NOH)NO}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04174 "no carrier added")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04174 "A preparation of a radioactive isotope which is essentially free from stable isotopes of the element in question.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04175 "noise")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04175 "The random fluctuations occurring in a signal that are inherent in the combination of instrument and method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04176 "no-load indication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04176 "The deflection or rest point (no-load reading) multiplied by the value of the division for zero load (rider at zero); symbol \\(i_{0}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04177 "nominal linear flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04177 "The volumetric flowrate of the mobile phase divided by the area of the cross section of the column (\\(\\rm{cm min}^{-1}\\)) i.e. the linear flowrate in a part of the column not containing packing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04178 "nominally labelled tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04178 "A tracer in which the label is present mainly in a specified position.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04180 "nonadiabatic coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04180 "This is momentum coupling between two adiabatic potential-energy surfaces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04183 "nonbonded interactions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04183 "Intramolecular attractions or repulsions between atoms that are not directly linked to each other, affecting the thermodynamic stability of the chemical species concerned.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04184 "non-calorimetric thermophysical measurements")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04184 "These include (\\(p\\),\\(V\\), \\(T\\)) measurements, saturated density measurements, and any other measurements which give information on the (\\(p\\),\\(V\\), \\(T\\)) surface of a fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04185 "nonclassical carbocation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04185 "A carbocation the ground state of which has delocalized (bridged) bonding $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$. (N.B. Allylic and benzylic carbocations are not considered nonclassical.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04187))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04187 "non-crystalline electrodes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04187 "Electrodes in which a support matrix, containing an ion exchanger (either cationic or anionic), a plasticizer solvent, and possibly an uncharged, selectivity-enhancing species, forms the ion-selective membrane which is usually interposed between two aqueous solutions. The support used can be either macroporous [e.g. poly(propylene carbonate) filter, glass frit, etc.] or microporous (e.g. 'thirsty' glass or inert polymeric material such as PVC) yielding with the ion-exchanger and the solvent a 'solidified' homogeneous mixture. These electrodes exhibit a response due to the presence of the ion-exchange material in the membrane. The solvent-polymeric-membrane is an example.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04188 "non-destructive activation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04188 "An activation analysis procedure in which, after the irradiation, no chemical and physical operations are applied which cause a change of any properties of the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04191 "non-draining")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04191 "An adjective describing a chain macromolecule that behaves in a hydrodynamic field as though the solvent within the domain of the macromolecule were virtually immobilized with respect to the macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04192 "non-equilibrium reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04192 "If the reactants in a chemical reaction are not initially present in a Boltzmann distribution the reaction is referred to as a non-equilibrium reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04193))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04193 "non-graphitic carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04193 "All varieties of solids consisting mainly of the element carbon with two-dimensional long-range order of the carbon atoms in planar hexagonal networks, but without any measurable crystallographic order in the third direction (c-direction) apart from more or less parallel stacking.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04194 "non-graphitizable carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04194 "A non-graphitic carbon which cannot be transformed into graphitic carbon solely by high-temperature treatment up to \\(3300\\ \\rm{K}\\) under atmospheric pressure or lower pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04195 "non-isotopic labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04195 "Labelling in which the resulting product has a different chemical composition from the initial one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04196 "non-linearity error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04196 "An error caused by any deviation from linearity of the response of the detector to the measured radiant power.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04197 "non-linear optical effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04197 "An effect brought about by electromagnetic radiation the magnitude of which is not proportional to the irradiance. Non-linear optical effects of importance to photochemistry are harmonic frequency generation, lasers, Raman shifting, upconversion, and others.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04199 "non-polarized interphases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04199 "Interphases for which the exchange of common charged components between the phases proceeds unhindered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04200 "non-radiative decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04200 "The disappearance of an excited species due to a radiationless transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04202 "nonselectively labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04202 "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as nonselectively labelled when the position(s) and the number of the labelling nuclide(s) are both undefined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04204))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04204 "non-specific adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04204 "Ions approach an interface differently depending on the forces in play. Ions are non-specifically adsorbed (positively or negatively) when they are subjected in the interphase only to long-range coulombic interactions (attraction or repulsion). They are believed to retain their solvation shell, and in the position of closest approach to the interface they are separated from it by one or more molecular layers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04205 "non-uniform corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04205 "Corrosion is non-uniform if the time average of the corrosion current through a unit area depends on its position on the surface. Non-uniform corrosion can be due to inhomogeneities of structure or of composition of the corroding material, or to inhomogeneities of the environment. Special cases of non-uniform corrosion such as pitting or intergranular corrosion are sometimes called localized corrosion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04206))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04206 "non-uniform polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04206 "A polymer comprising molecules non-uniform with respect to relative molecular mass or constitution or both.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04206 "polydisperse polymer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04207))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04207 "non-vertical energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04207 "Energy transfer process with a low Franck–Condon factor because the nuclear geometries of the minima on the ground- and excited-state potential energy surfaces of the donor or of the acceptor are strongly displaced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04209))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04209 "no-observed-effect-level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04209 "Greatest concentration or amount of a substance, found by experiment or observation, that causes no alterations of morphology, functional capacity, growth, development, or life span of target organisms distinguishable from those observed in normal (control) organisms of the same species and strain under the same defined conditions of exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04210 "nor-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04210 "Affix used to denote the elimination of one methylene group from a side chain of a parent structure (including a methyl group).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04211 "normal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04211 "The term 'normal' in e.g. 'normal boiling temperature' means the value at a pressure of \\(101325\\ \\rm{Pa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04214 "normal-phase chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04214 "An elution procedure in which the stationary phase is more polar then the mobile phase. This term is used in liquid chromatography to emphasize the contrast to reversed-phase chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04215))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04215 "normal region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04215 "Region for which the rate constants increase with increasing exergonicity of the reaction in plots relating rate constants for electron transfer, or quantities related to it, with the standard Gibbs energy for the reaction (\\(\\Delta _{\\rm{ET}}G^{\\rm{o}}\\)). This region is predicted by the Marcus theory for outer-sphere electron transfer for the case of \\(-\\Delta_{\\rm{ET}} G^{\\circ} \\leq \\lambda \\) in the Marcus equation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04216 "normal stress")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04216 "Force acting normally to a surface divided by the area of the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04217 "normal X-ray level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04217 "Synonymous with diagram level.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04218 "Norrish Type II photoreaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04218 "Intramolecular abstraction of a $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen}$ by an excited carbonyl compound to produce a 1,4-diradical as a primary photoproduct.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04219 "Norrish Type I photoreaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04219 "$\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}Cleavage}$ of an excited carbonyl compound leading to an acyl-alkyl radical pair (from an acyclic carbonyl compound) or an acyl-alkyl diradical (from a cyclic carbonyl compound) as a primary photoproduct.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04224))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04224 "<i>n</i>th order phase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04224 "A transition in which the molar Gibbs energy (or chemical potential) and its \\((n - 1)\\)th-order derivatives are continuous, whereas the \\(n\\)th-order derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure are discontinuous at the transition point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04226))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04226 "nuclear chemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04226 "The part of chemistry which deals with the study of nuclei and nuclear reactions using chemical methods.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04227 "nuclear decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04227 "A spontaneous nuclear transformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04228 "nuclear disintegration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04228 "Nuclear decay involving a splitting into more nuclei or the emission of particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04229 "nuclear fission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04229 "The division of a nucleus into two or more parts with masses of equal order of magnitude, usually accompanied by the emission of neutrons, gamma radiation and, rarely, small charged nuclear fragments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04230 "nuclear fuel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04230 "Material containing fissile nuclides, which when placed in a reactor, enables a chain reaction to be achieved.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04232 "nuclear fusion reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04232 "A reaction between two light nuclei resulting in the production of a nuclear species heavier than either initial nucleus.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04232 "nuclear fusion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04233 "nuclear graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04233 "A polygranular graphite material for use in nuclear reactor cores consisting of graphitic carbon of very high chemical purity. High purity is needed to avoid absorption of low-energy neutrons and the production of undesirable radioactive species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04234))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04234 "nuclear isomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04234 "Nuclides having the same mass number and atomic number, occupying different nuclear energy states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04235))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04235 "nuclear level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04235 "One of the energy values at which a nucleus can exist for an appreciable time (\\(> 10^{-22}\\ \\rm{s}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04236 "nuclear magneton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04236 "Electromagnetic fundamental physical constant \\(\\mu _{\\rm{N}}=\\frac{m_{\\rm{e}}}{m_{\\rm{p}}}\\ \\mu _{\\rm{B}}=5.050\\ 7866(17)\\times 10^{-27}\\ \\rm{J T}^{-1}\\), where \\(m_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the electron rest mass, \\(m_{\\rm{p}}\\) the proton rest mass and \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}\\) the Bohr magneton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04237))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04237 "nuclear particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04237 "A nucleus or any of its constituents in any of their energy states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04238 "nuclear quadrupole moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04238 "A parameter which describes the effective shape of the equivalent ellipsoid of the nuclear charge distribution, \\(Q>0\\) for prolate (e.g. $\\ce{^{57}Fe}$, $\\ce{^{19}7Au}$); \\(Q")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04239 "nuclear reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04239 "A device in which a self-sustaining nuclear fission chain reaction can be maintained and controlled. The term is sometimes applied to a device in which a nuclear fusion reaction can be produced and controlled.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04240 "nuclear transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04240 "The change of one nuclide into another with a different proton number or nucleon number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04241))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04241 "nuclear transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04241 "For a nucleus a change from one quantized energy state into another or a nuclear transformation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04242 "nucleating agent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04242 "A material either added to or present in a system, which induces either homogeneous or heterogeneous nucleation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04243 "nucleation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04243 "The process by which nuclei are formed in solution. The condensation of a single chemical compound is called 'homogeneous nucleation'. The simultaneous condensation of more than one compound is called 'simultaneous nucleation'. The condensation of a compound on a foreign substance is called 'heterogeneous nucleation'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04244 "nucleation and growth")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04244 "A process in a phase transition in which nuclei of a new phase are first formed, followed by the propagation of the new phase at a faster rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04245 "nucleic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04245 "Macromolecules, the major organic matter of the nuclei of biological cells, made up of nucleotide units, and hydrolysable into certain pyrimidine or purine bases (usually adenine, cytosine, guanine, thymine, uracil), ᴅ-ribose or 2-deoxy-ᴅ-ribose and phosphoric acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04246 "nucleofuge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04246 "A leaving group that carries away the bonding electron pair. For example, in the hydrolysis of an alkyl chloride, $\\ce{Cl^{-}}$ is the nucleofuge. The tendency of atoms or groups to depart with the bonding electron pair is called nucleofugality. The adjective is nucleofugal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04247 "nucleon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04247 "Heavy nuclear particle: proton or neutron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04248 "nucleon number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04248 "Synonymous with mass number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04249 "nucleophile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04249 "A nucleophile (or nucleophilic reagent) is a reagent that forms a bond to its reaction partner (the electrophile) by donating both bonding electrons. A 'nucleophilic substitution reaction' is a heterolytic reaction in which the reagent supplying the entering group acts as a nucleophile. For example:  The term 'nucleophilic' is also used to designate the apparent polar character of certain radicals, as inferred from their higher relative reactivity with reaction sites of lower electron density. Nucleophilic reagents are Lewis bases.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:N04249 "nucleophilic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04250 "nucleophilic catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04250 "Catalysis by a Lewis base, involving formation of a Lewis adduct as a reaction intermediate. For example, the hydrolysis of acetic anhydride in aqueous solution catalysed by pyridine:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04251))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04251 "nucleophilicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04251 "The property of being nucleophilic.The relative reactivity of a nucleophilic reagent. (It is also sometimes referred to as 'nucleophilic power'.) Qualitatively, the concept is related to Lewis basicity. However, whereas Lewis basicity is measured by relative equilibrium constants:  nucleophilicity of a Lewis base is measured by relative rate constants of different nucleophilic reagents towards a common substrate, most commonly involving formation of a bond to carbon:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04252 "nucleoproteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04252 "Proteins having nucleic acids as prosthetic groups, and thus yielding nucleic acids (or their cleavage products) as well as amino acids on hydrolytic cleavage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04253 "nucleosides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04253 "Ribosyl or deoxyribosyl derivatives (rarely, other glycosyl derivatives) of certain pyrimidine or purine bases. They are thus glycosylamines or N-glycosides related to nucleotides by the lack of phosphorylation. It has also become customary to include among nucleosides analogous substances in which the glycosyl group is attached to carbon rather than nitrogen ('C-nucleosides').")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04254))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04254 "nucleotide bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04254 "The heterocyclic pyrimidine and purine compounds which are constituents of all nucleic acids. Adenine (A), guanine (G) and cytosine (C) are found in both DNA and RNA, thymine (T) is found (primarily) in DNA and uracil (U) only in RNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04255))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04255 "nucleotides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04255 "Compounds formally obtained by esterification of the 3 or 5 hydroxy group of nucleosides with phosphoric acid. They are the monomers of nucleic acids and are formed from them by hydrolytic cleavage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04256 "nucleus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04256 "The positively charged central portion of an atom, excluding the orbital electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04257 "nuclide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04257 "A species of atom, characterized by its mass number, atomic number and nuclear energy state, provided that the mean lifetime in that state is long enough to be observable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04258 "nuclidic mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04258 "The rest mass of a nuclide expressed in atomic mass units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04259))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04259 "nuisance threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04259 "That concentration of an air pollutant that is considered objectionable. In the case of a substance with an objectionable odour, it is the smallest concentration of the substance which can be detected by a human being (nose).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04260 "number concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04260 "Number of entities of a constituent in a mixture divided by the volume of the mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04261 "number content")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04261 "Number of defined particles, or elementary entities, of a component in a system divided by the mass of that system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04262 "number density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04262 "Number of particles divided by the volume they occupy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04263 "number-distribution function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04263 "A distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with a specific value, or a range of values, of the random variable(s) is expressed in terms of mole fraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04264))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04264 "number flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04264 "Number of defined particles, or elementary entities of a defined component, crossing a cross-section divided by the time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04266))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04266 "number of entities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04266 "Integer number obtained by counting of entities, which are usually molecules, atoms or ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:N04269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:N04269 "numerical value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:N04269 "Quotient of the value of a quantity and the unit used in its expression.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06774))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06774 "nuclearity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06774 "The number of central atoms joined in a single coordination entity by bridging ligands or metal-metal bonds is indicated by dinuclear, trinuclear, tetranuclear, polynuclear, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06834 "necrosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06834 "Sum of morphological changes resulting from cell death by lysis and/or enzymatic degradation, usually affecting groups of cells in a tissue.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06888))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06888 "nanofiltration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06888 "Pressure-driven membrane-based separation process in which particles and dissolved macromolecules smaller than \\(2\\ \\rm{nm}\\) are rejected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06911 "no observed adverse effect level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06911 "Greatest concentration or amount found by experiment or observation, which causes no detectable adverse alteration of morphology, functional capacity, growth, development, or life span of the target organism under defined conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06936))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06936 "non-linear distribution isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06936 "A distribution isotherm which is not linear.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06940 "non-linear chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06940 "Chromatographic process, where the retention is governed by a non-linear distribution isotherm.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06942 "non-ideal chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06942 "Chromatographic process with normal peak-broadening effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06943 "non-ideal, linear chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06943 "Chromatographic process, where the retention is governed by a linear distribution isotherm and normal peak-broadening takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT06945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT06945 "non-ideal, non-linear chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT06945 "Chromatographic process, where both isotherm curvature and other peak-broadening processes (such as diffusion) contribute to the peak shape.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07076 "natural atomic orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07076 "A valence-shell atomic orbital whose derivation involves diagonalising the localized block of the full density matrix of a given molecule associated with basis functions \\(\\chi _{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) on that atom. A distinguishing feature of NAOs is that they meet the simultaneous requirement of orthonormality and maximum occupancy. For isolated atoms, NAOs coincide with natural orbitals. In a polyatomic molecule the NAOs (in contrast to natural orbitals that become delocalised over all nuclear centres) mostly retain one-centre character, and thus are optimal for describing the molecular electron density around each atomic centre.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07077 "natural bond orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07077 "The orbital which is formed from natural hybrid orbitals. For a localized $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between atoms $\\ce{A}$ and $\\ce{B}$, the NBO is: \\[\\sigma _{\\rm{AB}}=c_{\\rm{A}}\\ \\rm{h}_{\\rm{A}}+c_{\\rm{B}}\\ \\mathrm{h}_{\\rm{B}}\\] where \\(\\rm{h}_{\\rm{A}}\\) and \\(\\rm{h}_{\\rm{B}}\\) are the natural hybrids centered on atoms $\\ce{A}$ and $\\ce{B}$. NBOs closely correspond to the picture of localized bonds and lone pairs as basic units of molecular structure, so that is possible to conveniently interpret ab initio wave-functions in terms of the classical Lewis structure concepts by transforming these functions to NBO form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07078 "natural hybrid orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07078 "Symmetrically orthogonalised directed hybrid orbital derived through unitary transformation of natural atomic orbitals centred on a particular atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07079 "natural orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07079 "The orbitals defined (P. Lowdin) as the eigenfunctions of the spinless one-particle electron density matrix. For a configuration interaction (CI) wave-function constructed from orbitals \\(\\varPhi \\), the electron density function, \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3C1}\\), is of the form: \\[\\unicode[Times]{x3C1} = \\sum_{i}\\sum _{j}a_{ij}\\,\\varPhi_{i}^{*}\\,\\varPhi_{j}\\] where the coefficients \\(a_{ij}\\) are a set of numbers which form the density matrix. The NOs reduce the density matrix \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3C1}\\) to a diagonal form: \\[\\unicode[Times]{x3C1} = \\sum _{k}b_{k}\\mathit{\\Phi}_{k}^{*}\\mathit{\\Phi}_{k}\\] where the coefficients \\(b_{k}\\) are occupation numbers of each orbital. The importance of natural orbitals is in the fact that CI expansions based on these orbitals have generally the fastest convergence. If a CI calculation was carried out in terms of an arbitrary basis set and the subsequent diagonalisation of the density matrix \\(\\rm{a}_{ij}\\) gave the natural orbitals, the same calculation repeated in terms of the natural orbitals thus obtained would lead to the wave-function for which only those configurations built up from natural orbitals with large occupation numbers were important.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07080 "natural population analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07080 """The analysis of the electron density distribution in a molecular system based on the orthonormal natural atomic orbitals. Natural populations, \\(n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) are the occupancies of the natural atomic orbitals. These rigorously satisfy the Pauli exclusion principle: \\(0 < n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right) < 2\\). The population of an atom \\(n\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) is the sum of natural populations \\(n\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
\\rm{A}
\\end{array}}n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\). A distinguished feature of the NPA method is that it largely resolves the basis set dependence problem encountered in the Mulliken population analysis method.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07081 "negative hyper-conjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07081 "The effect of donation of electron density from filled $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ or $\\ce{n_{\\uppi\\!}\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ to the symmetry adapted $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}orbital(s)}$ of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}bond(s)}$ of a molecular entity resulting in building $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}character}$ into bonds that nominally possess only $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}character}$. The consequences of the effect are, for example, in elongation of the $\\ce{C–F}$ bond in the $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}fluoroethyl}$ anion and stabilization of the anti-periplanar conformation of fluoromethylamine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07082 "nodal plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07082 "A plane of a system in which the value of the orbital wave-function equals zero. This plane defines a region of zero electron density for the orbital.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07083 "nonbonding molecular orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07083 "A molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons does not contribute (or contributes insignificantly) to the binding energy of the molecule. Generally, a nonbonding MO represents the highest occupied molecular orbital of a molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07084 "nonclassical structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07084 "The structure of molecules or molecular ions that escapes description in terms of conventional rules of valency and stereochemistry. Nonclassical structures are characteristic of carbonium ions with hypercoordinated (see hypercoordination) carbon atoms, e.g., methanium ion 1, pyramidal dication $\\ce{C6H6^{2+}}$ 2 (isomeric to benzene dication), and the molecular species whose structure cannot be adequately represented by the equilibrium (2-norbornyl cation, 3) or resonance of two or more classical structures. From the stereochemical point of view, those structures are assigned to the nonclassical type for which all tetracoordinate carbon bonds extend into a single hemisphere, i.e., the valence angle of a carbon atom is greater than $\\pu{180\\!^{\\circ}}$. A hypothetical example is pyramidane, 4, the structure of which corresponds to a local minimum on the $\\ce{C5H4}$ potential energy surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07085 "non-Kekulé molecules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07085 "Molecules that are fully conjugated, but each of whose Kekulé structures contains at least two atoms that are not $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonded}$. Examples of non-Kekule hydrocarbons are trimethylenemethane (2-methylidenepropane-1,3-diyl) 1, m-benzodiquinomethane (benzene-1,2-diyldimethyl) 2, and 1,2,4,5 - tetramethylenebenzene (benzene-1,2,4,5-tetrayltetramethyl) 3.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07086 "normalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07086 "A mathematical procedure which ensures that the integral of the square of modulus of a wave-function over all space equals 1. The constant required to ensure that a wave-function is normalized is termed the normalization constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07178 "non-linear optical polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07178 "Polymer that exhibits an optical effect brought about by electromagnetic radiation such that the magnitude of the effect is not proportional to the irradiance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07243 "nanocomposite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07243 "Composite in which at least one of the phase domains has at least one dimension of the order of nanometres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07272 "nucleation of phase separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07272 "Initiation of phase domain formation through the presence of heterogeneities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07304 "nanodomain morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07304 "Morphology consisting of phase nanodomains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07425 "non-crossing rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07425 "Electronic states of the same symmetry cannot cross along a reaction coordinate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07426))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07426 "non-linear optical techniques")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07426 "Collective name applied to techniques related to non-linear optical effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07427))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07427 "Norrish–Yang reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07427 "Photochemical process leading to the formation of cyclobutanols from excited carbonyl compounds by intramolecular $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen}$ abstraction (Norrish Type II photoreaction) and subsequent 1,4-diradical combination.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07428 "nucleophilic aromatic photosubstitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07428 "Photosubstitution in aromatic compounds with the participation of nucleophiles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07521 "nanogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07521 "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\(1\\) to \\(100\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07521 "gel nanoparticle")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07522 "neutralized gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07522 "Gel containing acidic or basic groups that have been neutralized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07562 "network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07562 "Highly ramified structure in which essentially each constitutional unit is connected to each other constitutional unit and to the macroscopic phase boundary by many paths through the structure, the number of such paths increasing with the average number of intervening constitutional units; the paths must on average be co-extensive with the structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07603 "network-chain molar mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07603 "Number-average molar mass of polymer chains between two adjacent crosslinks or junction points in a polymer network.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07603 "inter-junction molar mass")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07604 "network defect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07604 "Elastically ineffective chains in a polymer network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07626 "net shaping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07626 "Production of an object in, or as close as possible to, its final shape prior to ceramization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04270 "observation height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04270 "The difference in height between the axis of the observed space (optical axis) and the burner tip (in \\(\\mathrm{mm}\\)). The optical axis of the instrument should be the same as the optical axis of the observation space. Other definitions which express the observation height as a fraction of the whole flame height are not recommended because the height to the tip of the flame is not well defined. The observation height should be stated in the method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04271 "observation pathlength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04271 "The intersection of the optical axis and the observation space (in \\(\\mathrm{mm}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04272 "observation space")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04272 "The intersection of the optical beam and that part of the flame where the net signal is at least half of the maximum net signal. The characteristics of the observation space depend on the temperature of the flame, the stoichiometry of the gases and the properties of the processed fluid (presence of proteins for instance). The observation space in atomic absorption is analogous to the observation space of a cuvette in molecular absorption spectrometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04273 "observation volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04273 "The volume of that part of the flame that is observed through the optical device (in \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{l}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04275))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04275 "occlusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04275 "The process of incorporation of foreign substances as molecular species within precipitates as they are formed.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04275 "molecular occlusion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04276))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04276 "<i>octahedro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04276 "An affix used in names to denote six atoms bound into an octahedron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04277 "odd-electron ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04277 "Synonymous with radical ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04278 "optically detected magnetic resonance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04278 "A double resonance technique in which transitions between spin sublevels are detected by optical means. Usually these are sublevels of a triplet and the transitions are induced by microwaves. For different types of optical detection the following expressions are used: ADMR (absorption), DEDMR (delayed emission, non-specified), DFDMR (delayed fluorescence), FDMR (fluorescence), PDMR (phosphorescence). If a reaction yield is followed the expression RYDMR (reaction yield detected magnetic resonance) is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04279 "odour threshold")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04279 "The concentration of a compound which produces an odour which is detectable by a human being (nose). For certain compounds this threshold is very low (e.g. 1 part in \\(10^{9}\\) for certain sulfides).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04280))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04280 "ohm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04280 "SI derived unit of electrical resistance, \\(\\Omega = \\pu{V A-1} = \\pu{m2 kg s-3 A-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04281))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04281 "olefins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04281 "Acyclic and cyclic hydrocarbons having one or more carbon–carbon double bonds, apart from the formal ones in aromatic compounds. The class olefins subsumes alkenes and cycloalkenes and the corresponding polyenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04282 "oligo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04282 "A prefix meaning 'a few', and used for compounds with a number of repeating units intermediate between those in monomers and those in high polymers. The limits are not precisely defined, and in practice vary with the type of structure being considered, but are generally from 3 to 10, e.g. oligopeptides, oligosaccharides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04283 "oligomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04283 "A substance composed of oligomer molecules. An oligomer obtained by telomerization is often termed a telomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04285 "oligomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04285 "The process of converting a monomer or a mixture of monomers into an oligomer. An oligomerization by chain reaction carried out in the presence of a large amount of chain transfer agent, so that the end-groups are essentially fragments of the chain transfer agent, is termed telomerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04286))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04286 "oligomer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04286 "A molecule of intermediate relative molecular mass, the structure of which essentially comprises a small plurality of units derived, actually or conceptually, from molecules of lower relative molecular mass.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04286 "oligomeric")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04291 "onium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04291 "Cations (with their counter-ions) derived by addition of a hydron to a mononuclear parent hydride of the nitrogen, chalcogen and halogen families.$\\ce{H4N^{+}}$ ammonium$\\ce{H3O^{+}}$ oxonium$\\ce{H2F^{+}}$ fluoronium$\\ce{H4P^{+}}$ phosphonium$\\ce{H3S^{+}}$ sulfonium$\\ce{H2Cl^{+}}$ chloronium$\\ce{H4As^{+}}$ arsonium$\\ce{H3Se^{+}}$ selenonium$\\ce{H2Br^{+}}$ bromonium$\\ce{H4Sb^{+}}$ stibonium$\\ce{H3Te^{+}}$ telluronium$\\ce{H2I^{+}}$ iodonium$\\ce{H4Bi^{+}}$ bismuthoniumDerivatives formed by substitution of the above parent ions by univalent groups. The number of substituted hydrogen atoms is, especially in the case of hydrocarbyl substituents, indicated by the adjectives primary, secondary, tertiary or quaternary. E.g. ($\\ce{Cl2F^{+}}$) dichlorofluoronium, $\\ce{(CH3)2S^{+}H}$ dimethylsulfonium (a secondary sulfonium ion), $\\ce{Cl(CH3)3P^{+}}$ chlorotrimethylphosphonium, $\\ce{(CH3CH2)4N^{+}}$ tetraethylammonium (a quaternary ammonium ion).See also: arsonium compounds, halonium ions, oxonium ions, phosphonium compounds, quaternary ammonium compounds, stibonium compounds, sulfonium compoundsDerivatives formed by substitution of the above parent ions by groups having two or three free valencies on the same atom. Such derivatives are, where possible, designated by a specific class name. E.g. $\\ce{RC#O^{+}}$ hydrocarbylidyne oxonium ions, $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}H2X^{−}}$ iminium compounds, $\\ce{RC#NH^{+}}$ nitrilium ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04294))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04294 "open film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04294 "A film in which mass transfer can occur between the film and the coexisting bulk phases, for all the components. The term partly open film applies to a film in which mass transfer can occur only for certain components. The term closed film applies to those films with fixed mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04295 "open hearth furnace")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04295 "Reverberatory furnace, containing a basin-shaped hearth, for melting and refining suitable types of pig iron, iron ore, and scrap for steel production. A large amount of dust from ore and other materials and splashings from slag are carried away by the waste gases; a supplementary chamber is commonly used for collecting slag and dust.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04297 "open-tubular column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04297 "A column, usually having a small diameter, in which either the inner tube wall, or a liquid or active solid held stationary on the tube wall acts as the stationary phase and there is an open, unrestricted path for the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04298 "operational pH cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04298 "An electrochemical cell which is the basis of practical pH measurement consisting of a hydrogen ion-responsive electrode (hydrogen gas, or glass) and a reference electrode immersed in the test solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04299 "operational pH standard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04299 "Certain substances which meet the criteria of (i) preparation in highly pure state reproducibly and (ii) stability of solution over a reasonable period of time are designated as operational standards in aqueous solution of specified concentration. Their number is in principle unlimited but values are available now for 15 solutions. The values of operational pH standards, i.e. \\(\\mathrm{pH}\\left(\\rm{OS}\\right)\\), are assigned by comparison with the reference value pH standard, i.e. \\(\\mathrm{pH}\\left(\\rm{RVS}\\right)\\), in cells with liquid junction, the operational cells, where the liquid junctions are formed within vertical \\(1\\ \\rm{mm}\\) tubes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04299 "pH standard")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04301 "operon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04301 "A functional unit consisting of a promoter, an operator and a number of structural genes, found mainly in prokaryotes. The structural genes commonly code for several functionally related enzymes, and although they are transcribed as one (polycistronic) mRNA each is independently translated. In the typical operon, the operator region acts as a controlling element in switching on or off the synthesis of mRNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04302 "opposing reactions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04302 "Composite reactions, occurring in forward and reverse directions:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04303))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04303 "optical activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04303 "A sample of material able to rotate the plane of polarization of a beam of transmitted plane-polarized light is said to possess optical activity (or to be optically active). This 'optical rotation' is the classical distinguishing characteristic (sufficient but not necessary) of systems containing unequal amounts of corresponding enantiomers. An enantiomer causing rotation in a clockwise direction (when viewed in the direction facing the oncoming light beam) under specified conditions is called 'dextrorotatory' and its chemical name or formula is designated by the prefix (+)-; one causing rotation in the opposite sense is 'laevorotatory' and designated by the prefix (−)-. Materials with optical activity also exhibit other chiroptic phenomena.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04303 "optically active")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04305))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04305 "optical-beam error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04305 "For wavelength-scanning spectrometers, an error may be introduced if the image position on the photodetector changes. Reflection of the incident radiation, e.g. between the cell walls, is another source of error and results in a measured absorbance slightly higher than the true absorbance. These errors are collectively termed optical-beam errors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04307))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04307 "optical filter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04307 "A device which reduces the spectral range (bandpass, cut-off, and interference filter) or radiant power of incident radiation (neutral density or attenuance filter) upon transmission of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04309 "optically labile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04309 "A term describing a system in which stereoisomerization results in a change of optical rotation with time. (Usage strongly discouraged).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04310 "optical purity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04310 "The ratio of the observed optical rotation of a sample consisting of a mixture of enantiomers to the optical rotation of one pure enantiomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04313 "optical rotatory power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04313 "Angle of optical rotation divided by the optical path length through the medium and by either the mass concentration of the substance giving the specific optical rotatory power \\(\\left[\\alpha \\right]_{\\lambda }^{\\theta }\\) or by amount concentration, giving the molar optical rotatory power, \\(\\alpha _{\\rm{m}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04314 "optical spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04314 "The study of systems by the electromagnetic radiation with which they interact or that they produce. By convention, spectroscopic properties are expressed in terms of wavelength in vacuo, not the wavelength within the medium being studied.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04315 "optical yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04315 "In a chemical reaction involving chiral reactants and products, the ratio of the optical purity of the product to that of the precursor, reactant or catalyst. This should not be confused with enantiomeric excess. The optical yield is in no way related to the chemical yield of the reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04316 "optoacoustic spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04316 "Same as photoacoustic spectroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04317))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04317 "orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04317 "Wavefunction depending explicitly on the spatial coordinates of only one electron.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04317 "atomic orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04317 "molecular orbital")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04318))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04318 "orbital energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04318 "Eigenvalue of a one-electron effective hamiltonian belonging to an orbital.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04319 "orbital steering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04319 "A concept expressing that the stereochemistry of approach of two reacting species is governed by the most favourable overlap of their appropriate orbitals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04320 "orbital symmetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04320 "The behaviour of an atomic or localized molecular orbital under molecular symmetry operations characterizes its orbital symmetry. For example, under a reflection in an appropriate symmetry plane, the phase of the orbital may be unchanged (symmetric), or it may change sign (antisymmetric), i.e. the positive and negative lobes are interchanged. A principal context for the use of orbital symmetry is the discussion of chemical changes that involve 'conservation of orbital symmetry'. If a certain symmetry element (e.g. the reflection plane) is retained along a reaction pathway, that pathway is 'allowed' by orbital symmetry conservation if each of the occupied orbitals of the reactant(s) is of the same symmetry type as a similarly (e.g. singly or doubly) occupied orbital of the product(s). This principle permits the qualitative construction of correlation diagrams to show how molecular orbitals transform (and how their energies change) during idealized chemical changes (e.g. cycloadditions). An idealized single bond is a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$, i.e. it has cylindrical symmetry, whereas a p-orbital or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$bond orbital has $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$, i.e. it is antisymmetric with respect to reflection in a plane passing through the atomic centres with which it is associated. In ethene, the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonding orbital}$ is symmetric with respect to reflection in a plane perpendicular to and bisecting the $\\ce{C–C}$ bond, whereas the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}antibonding orbital}$ is antisymmetric with respect to this operation. Considerations of orbital symmetry are frequently grossly simplified in that, for example, the p-orbitals of a carbonyl group would be treated as having the same symmetry as those of ethene, and the fact that the carbonyl group in, for example, camphor, unlike that in formaldehyde, has no mirror planes would be ignored. These simplified considerations nevertheless afford the basis of one approach to the understanding of the rules which indicate whether pericyclic reactions are likely to occur under thermal or photochemical conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04321 "order–disorder transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04321 "A transition in which the degree of order of the system changes. Three principal types of disordering transitions may be distinguished: (i) positional disordering in a solid, (ii) orientational disordering which may be static or dynamic and (iii) disordering associated with electronic and nuclear spin states. Examples:The transition of $\\ce{LiFeO2}$, with a tetragonal unit cell, in which the $\\ce{Li+}$ and $\\ce{Fe3+}$ cations are perfectly ordered on crystallographically non-equivalent octahedral sites to cubic $\\ce{LiFeO2}$ in which the $\\ce{Li+}$ and $\\ce{Fe3+}$ cations are distributed randomly over all the octahedral sites. The transition of orthorhombic $\\ce{KCN}$ to cubic $\\ce{KCN}$ in which the $\\ce{CN−}$ ions become oriented in any of the eight $[111]$ directions.A superconducting transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04322 "order of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04322 "If the macroscopic (observed, empirical or phenomenological) rate of reaction (v) for any reaction can be expressed by an empirical differential rate equation (or rate law) which contains a factor of the form \\(k\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]^{\\alpha }\\ \\left[\\rm{B}\\right]^{\\beta }\\) ... (expressing in full the dependence of the rate of reaction on the concentrations \\(\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\), \\(\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\) ...) where \\(\\alpha \\), \\(\\beta \\) are constant exponents (independent of concentration and time) and \\(k\\) is independent of \\(\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\) and \\(\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\) etc. (rate constant, rate coefficient), then the reaction is said to be of order \\(\\alpha \\) with respect to A, of order \\(\\beta \\) with respect to B, ... , and of (total or overall) order \\(n=\\alpha +\\beta\\: +\\,...\\) The exponents \\(\\alpha \\), \\(\\beta \\), ... can be positive or negative integral or rational nonintegral numbers. They are the reaction orders with respect to A, B, ... and are sometimes called 'partial orders of reaction'. Orders of reaction deduced from the dependence of initial rates of reaction on concentration are called 'orders of reaction with respect to concentration'; orders of reaction deduced from the dependence of the rate of reaction on time of reaction are called 'orders of reaction with respect to time'. The concept of order of reaction is also applicable to chemical rate processes occurring in systems for which concentration changes (and hence the rate of reaction) are not themselves measurable, provided it is possible to measure a chemical flux. For example, if there is a dynamic equilibrium according to the equation: \\[a\\rm{A}\\rightleftharpoons p\\rm{P}\\] and if a chemical flux is experimentally found, (e.g. by NMR line-shape analysis) to be related to concentrations by the equation: \\[\\frac{\\varphi _{-\\rm{A}}}{\\alpha } = k\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]^{\\alpha }\\ \\left[\\rm{L}\\right]^{\\lambda }\\] then the corresponding reaction is of order \\(\\alpha \\) with respect to A ... and of total (or overall) order \\(n(=\\alpha +\\lambda\\: +\\,...)\\). The proportionality factor \\(k\\) above is called the (\\(n\\)th order) 'rate coefficient'. Rate coefficients referring to (or believed to refer to) elementary reactions are called 'rate constants' or, more appropriately 'microscopic' (hypothetical, mechanistic) rate constants. The (overall) order of a reaction cannot be deduced from measurements of a 'rate of appearance' or 'rate of disappearance' at a single value of the concentration of a species whose concentration is constant (or effectively constant) during the course of the reaction. If the overall rate of reaction is, for example, given by: \\[v=k\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]^{\\alpha }\\ \\left[\\rm{B}\\right]^{\\beta }\\] but [B] stays constant, then the order of the reaction (with respect to time), as observed from the concentration change of A with time, will be \\(\\alpha \\), and the rate of disappearance of A can be expressed in the form: \\[v_{\\rm{A}}=k_{\\rm{obs}}\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]^{\\alpha }\\] The proportionality factor \\(k_{\\rm{obs}}\\) deduced from such an experiment is called the 'observed rate coefficient' and it is related to the \\((\\alpha + \\beta)\\)th order rate coefficient \\(k\\) by the equation: \\[k_{\\rm{obs}}=k\\ \\left[\\rm{B}\\right]^{\\beta }\\] For the common case when \\(\\alpha = 1\\), \\(k_{\\rm{obs}}\\) is often referred to as a 'pseudo-first order rate coefficient' (\\(k_{\\psi }\\)). For a simple (elementary) reactions a partial order of reaction is the same as the stoichiometric number of the reactant concerned and must therefore be a positive integer (see rate of reaction). The overall order is then the same as the molecularity. For stepwise reaction there is no general connection between stoichiometric numbers and partial orders. Such reactions may have more complex rate laws, so that an apparent order of reaction may vary with the concentrations of the chemical species involved and with the progress of the reaction: in such cases it is not useful to speak of orders of reaction, although apparent orders of reaction may be deducible from initial rates. In a stepwise reaction, orders of reaction may in principle always be assigned to the elementary steps.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04323))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04323 "order parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04323 "A normalized parameter that indicates the degree of order of a system. An order parameter of 0 indicates disorder; the absolute value in the ordered state is 1.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04324))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04324 "organelles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04324 "Separated compartments within a cell with specialized functions, e.g. nuclei (containing most of the genetic material), mitochondria (respiratory energy supply for the cell), chloroplasts (location of photosynthesis) etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04325))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04325 "organic dye laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04325 "A liquid laser in which the active medium is an organic dye in a solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04327 "organoheteryl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04327 "Univalent groups containing carbon, which are thus organic, but which have their free valence at an atom other than carbon. This collective term is seldom used; specific subclasses are more frequently encountered (organothio or organylthio, organogermanium or organylgermanium groups). The synonymous term organoelement groups is occasionally encountered. E.g. phenoxy, acetamido, pyridinio ($\\ce{C5H5N^{+}}$), thiocyanato ($\\ce{N#C–S−}$), trimethylsilyl; but not hydroxyphenyl, aminoacetyl.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04327 "organoelement groups")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04328))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04328 "organometallic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04328 "Classically compounds having bonds between one or more metal atoms and one or more carbon atoms of an organyl group. Organometallic compounds are classified by prefixing the metal with organo-, e.g. organopalladium compounds. In addition to the traditional metals and semimetals, elements such as boron, silicon, arsenic and selenium are considered to form organometallic compounds, e.g. organomagnesium compounds $\\ce{MeMgI}$ iodo(methyl)magnesium, $\\ce{Et2Mg}$ diethylmagnesium; an organolithium compound $\\ce{BuLi}$ butyllithium; an organozinc compound $\\ce{ClZnCH2C(=O)OEt}$ chloro(ethoxycarbonylmethyl)zinc; an organocuprate $\\ce{Li^{+}[CuMe2]^{−}}$ lithium dimethylcuprate; an organoborane $\\ce{Et3B}$ triethylborane. The status of compounds in which the canonical anion has a delocalized structure in which the negative charge is shared with an atom more electronegative than carbon, as in enolates, may vary with the nature of the anionic moiety, the metal ion, and possibly the medium; in the absence of direct structural evidence for a carbon–metal bond, such compounds are not considered to be organometallic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04329 "organyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04329 "Any organic substituent group, regardless of functional type, having one free valence at a carbon atom, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2–}$, $\\ce{ClCH2–}$, $\\ce{CH3C(=O)–}$, 4-pyridylmethyl. Organyl is also used in conjunction with other terms, as in organylthio- (e.g. $\\ce{MeS-}$) and organyloxy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04330))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04330 "origin of replication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04330 "A sequence of DNA at which replication is initiated on a chromosome, plasmid or virus.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04330 "ori")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04331 "ortho acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04331 "Hypothetical compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(OH)3}$. Thus, hydrated forms of carboxylic acids. Orthocarbonic acid, $\\ce{C(OH)4}$, is generically included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04332))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04332 "ortho amides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04332 "Hypothetical compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(NH2)3}$, and N-substituted derivatives thereof. $\\ce{(R2N)4C}$ are generically included, but such use is obsolescent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04333 "<i>ortho</i>- and <i>peri</i>-fused")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04333 "Polycyclic compounds in which one ring contains two, and only two, atoms in common with each of two or more rings of a contiguous series of rings. Such compounds have n common faces and less than 2n common atoms. Examples:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04334 "ortho esters")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04334 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(OR')3}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$), or the structure $\\ce{C(OR')4}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{HC(OCH3)3}$ trimethyl orthoformate, $\\ce{C(OCH3)4}$ tetramethyl orthocarbonate.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04335 "<i>ortho</i>-fused")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04335 "Polycyclic compounds in which two rings have two, and only two, atoms in common. Such compounds have n common faces and 2n common atoms. Examples:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04336 "orthokinetic aggregation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04336 "The rate of aggregation is in general determined by the frequency of collisions and the probability of cohesion during collision. If the collisions are caused by hydrodynamic motions (e.g convection or sedimentation) this is then referred to as orthokinetic aggregation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04337 "osazones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04337 "1,2-Bis(arylhydrazones) of ketoaldoses (aldoketoses) formed from aldoses and 2-ketoses by reaction with excess arylhydrazine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04338 "oscillating reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04338 "A reaction is said to be oscillating if the concentration of one or more intermediates does not vary monotonically, but passes through maximum and minimum values.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04339 "oscillator strength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04339 "Measure for integrated intensity of electronic transitions and related to the Einstein transition probability coefficient \\(A_{ij}\\) by: \\[f_{ij} = 1.4992\\times 10^{-14}\\ \\frac{A_{ij}}{s^{-1}}\\ (\\frac{\\lambda }{\\rm{nm}})^{2}\\] where \\(\\lambda \\) is the transition wavelength. There are, however, differing uses of \\(f\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04340 "osmolality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04340 "Quotient of the negative natural logarithm of the rational activity of water and the molar mass of water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04342 "osmotic coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04342 """Quantity characterizing the deviation of the solvent from ideal behaviour referenced to Raoult's law. The osmotic coefficient on a molality basis is defined by: \\[\\phi =\\frac{\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}- \\mu _{\\rm{A}}}{R\\ T\\ M_{\\rm{A}}\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}m_{i}}\\] and on an amount fraction basis by: \\[\\phi =\\frac{\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}- \\mu _{\\rm{A}}}{R\\ T\\ \\ln x_{\\rm{A}}}\\] where \\(\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}\\) and \\(\\mu _{\\rm{A}}\\) are the chemical potentials of the solvent as a pure substance and in solution, respectively, \\(M_{\\rm{A}}\\) is its molar mass, \\(x_{\\rm{A}}\\) its amount fraction, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the temperaure. The latter osmotic coefficient is sometimes called the rational osmotic coefficient.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04343 "osmotic concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04343 "Product of the osmolality and the mass density of water. Formerly called osmolarity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04343 "osmolarity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04344 "osmotic pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04344 "Excess pressure required to maintain osmotic equilibrium between a solution and the pure solvent separated by a membrane permeable only to the solvent: \\[\\mathit{\\Pi} = -\\frac{R\\ T}{V_{\\rm{A}}}\\ \\ln a_{\\rm{A}}\\] where \\(V_{\\mathrm{A}}\\), \\(a_{\\mathrm{A}}\\) are the partial molar volume and activity of solvent \\(\\rm{A}\\) for an incompressible fluid. For ideal dilute solutions, \\(\\mathit{\\Pi} = c_{\\rm{B}}\\ R\\ T=\\rho _{\\rm{B}}\\ \\frac{R\\ T}{M_{\\rm{B}}}\\), where entities \\(\\rm{B}\\) are individually moving solute molecules, ions, etc., regardless of their nature, \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(\\rho _{\\mathrm{B}}\\) are the amount and mass concentration of the solutes, and \\(M_{\\mathrm{B}}\\) is the mass average molar mass of the solutes. The amount is sometimes expressed in osmol (meaning a mole of osmotically active entities), but this usage and the corresponding term osmolarity are discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04346 "osones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04346 "An obsolescent and non-recommended term for 1,2-ketoaldoses, usually derived by hydrolysis of osazones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04347 "osotriazoles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04347 "The 1,2,3-triazoles formed on oxidizing osazones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04348 "Ostwald ripening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04348 "Dissolution of small crystals or sol particles and the redeposition of the dissolved species on the surfaces of larger crystals or sol particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04349 "outer electric potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04349 "Electric potential outside the phase concerned. For a conducting sphere of excess charge \\(Q\\) and radius \\(r\\) in vacuum \\(\\psi =\\frac{Q}{4\\ \\pi \\ ɛ_{0}\\ r}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04350 "outer Helmholtz plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04350 "At an electrified interface, the locus of the electrical centres of non-specifically adsorbed ions in their position of closest approach.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04351 "outer-sphere electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04351 "An outer-sphere electron transfer is a reaction in which the electron transfer takes place with no or very weak (\\(\\pu{4\\!{-}\\! 16 kJ mol-1}\\)) electronic interaction between the reactants in the transition state. If instead the donor and the acceptor exhibit a strong electronic coupling, the reaction is described as inner-sphere electron transfer. The two terms derive from studies concerning metal complexes and it has been suggested that for organic reactions the terms 'nonbonded' and 'bonded' electron transfer should be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04352 "outgassing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04352 "A form of pretreatment in which a catalyst is heated in vacuo to remove adsorbed or dissolved gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04354))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04354 "out-of-plane bending coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04354 "Given by \\[\\Delta \\theta _{i- jkl}=\\frac{\\Delta z_{j}}{r_{eij}}\\ \\sin   \\mathit{\\Phi }_{kil}\\] where the numbering of the atoms is given in the diagram. \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} _{kil}\\) denotes  the angle between the bonds \\(ik\\) and \\(il\\), \\(\\Delta z_{j}\\) the perpendicular distance of the atom \\(j\\) from the instantaneous plane \\(ikl\\) and \\(r_{eij}\\) the equilibrium length of the bond \\(ij\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04355 "output rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04355 "The number of results that is produced by an instrument divided by time of operation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04357 "overlap integral")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04357 "Integral over space of the type \\(\\int \\psi _{\\rm{r}}^{*}\\ \\psi _{\\rm{s}}\\ \\rm{d}\\tau \\) where \\(\\psi_{\\rm{r}}\\) and \\(\\psi_{\\rm{s}}\\) are different wavefunctions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04358 "overpotential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04358 "Deviation of the potential of an electrode from its equilibrium value required to cause a given current to flow through the electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04359))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04359 "oxa-di-π-methane rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04359 "A photochemical reaction of a $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!,\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}unsaturated ketone}$ to form a saturated $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}cyclopropyl ketone}$. The rearrangement formally amounts to a 1,2-acyl shift and 'bond formation' between the former $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}carbon atoms}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04361 "oxidant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04361 "A very qualitative term which includes any and all trace gases which have a greater oxidation potential than oxygen (for example, ozone, peroxyacetyl nitrate, hydrogen peroxide, organic peroxides, $\\ce{NO3}$, etc.). It is recommended that alternative, more definitive terms be used which define the specific oxidant of interest whenever possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04362))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04362 "oxidation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04362 "The complete, net removal of one or more electrons from a molecular entity (also called 'de-electronation').An increase in the oxidation number of any atom within any substrate.Gain of oxygen and/or loss of hydrogen of an organic substrate.All oxidations meet criteria 1 and 2, and many meet criterion 3, but this is not always easy to demonstrate. Alternatively, an oxidation can be described as a transformation of an organic substrate that can be rationally dissected into steps or primitive changes. The latter consist in removal of one or several electrons from the substrate followed or preceded by gain or loss of water and/or hydrons or hydroxide ions, or by nucleophilic substitution by water or its reverse and/or by an intramolecular molecular rearrangement. This formal definition allows the original idea of oxidation (combination with oxygen), together with its extension to removal of hydrogen, as well as processes closely akin to this type of transformation transformation (and generally regarded in current usage of the term in organic chemistry to be oxidations and to be effected by 'oxidizing agents') to be descriptively related to definition 1. For example the oxidation of methane to chloromethane may be considered as follows:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04363))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04363 "oxidation number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04363 "In English is largely synonymous with oxidation state, and may be preferred when the value represents a mere parameter or number rather than being related to chemical systematics or a state of the atom in a compound. Etymologically, it stems from the no-longer-used term 'Stock number' (oxidation number of a central atom; the charge it would bear if all the ligands were removed along with the electron pairs that were shared with the central atom) and the likewise obsolete term Ewens–Bassett number (ion charge).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04363 "Stock number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04365))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04365 "oxidation state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04365 "Gives the degree of oxidation of an atom in terms of counting electrons. The higher the oxidation state (OS) of a given atom, the greater is its degree of oxidation. Definition: OS of an atom is the charge of this atom after ionic approximation of its heteronuclear bonds. The underlying principle is that the ionic sign in an AB molecule is deduced from the electron allegiance in a LCAO-MO model: The bond&#x2019;s electrons are assigned to its main atomic contributor. Homonuclear AA bonds are divided equally. In practical use, the ionic-approximation sign follows Allen electronegativities (see Source). There are two general algorithms to calculate OS:   Algorithm of assigning bonds, which works on a Lewis formula showing all valence electrons in a molecule: OS equals the charge of an atom after its heteronuclear bonds have been assigned to the more electronegative partner (except when that partner is a reversibly bonded Lewis-acid ligand) and homonuclear bonds have been divided equally:    Algorithm of summing bond orders: Heteronuclear-bond orders are summed at the atom as positive if that atom is the electropositive partner in a particular bond and as negative if not, and the atom&#x2019;s formal charge (if any) is added to that sum, yielding the OS. This algorithm works on Lewis formulas and on bond graphs of atom connectivities for an extended solid:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04367 "oxidative addition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04367 "The insertion of a metal complex into a covalent bond involving formally an overall two-electron loss on one metal or a one-electron loss on each of two metals, i.e. \\[\\rm{L}_{n}\\rm{M}^{m}+\\rm{XY}\\rightarrow \\rm{L}_{n}\\rm{M}^{m+2}\\left(\\rm{X}\\right)\\left(\\rm{Y}\\right)\\] or \\[2\\rm{L}_{n}\\rm{M}^{m}+\\rm{XY}\\rightarrow \\rm{L}_{n}\\rm{M}^{m+1}\\left(\\rm{X}\\right)+\\rm{L}_{n}\\rm{M}^{m+1}\\left(\\rm{Y}\\right)\\] In radical chemistry, the term is used to indicate a radical addition to a carbon–carbon double bond, under oxidative conditions. For example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04368))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04368 "oxidative coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04368 "The coupling of two molecular entities through an oxidative process, usually catalysed by a transition metal compound and involving dioxygen as the oxidant; e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04369 "oxidized species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04369 "A term used to characterize the degree of oxidation (or reduction) in atoms, molecules and ions. It can be applied to an atom in a molecule or an ion which has a high oxidation state. An element or atom in a compound can be oxidized by reaction with oxygen, while it can be reduced by reaction with hydrogen. An oxidized species may be formed also through the loss of electrons (either to the positive electrode in a cell, or through transfer to another atom or group of atoms). For example, the sulfur in $\\ce{H2S}$ is reduced sulfur relative to elementary sulfur, while $\\ce{SO2}$ and $\\ce{SO3}$ are oxidized. Metallic iron ($\\ce{Fe}$) is a reduced state of iron, while the $\\ce{Fe2+}$ ion (ferrous ion) and $\\ce{Fe^{3+}}$ ion (ferric ion) are oxidized states of iron. $\\ce{Fe^{3+}}$ is in a higher oxidation state than $\\ce{Fe^{2+}}$ which is in a higher oxidation state than $\\ce{Fe}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04370 "oxidoreductases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04370 "Enzymes that catalyse electron transfer in oxidation-reduction reactions. Oxidoreductases are classified into several groups according to their respective donors or acceptors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04371 "oxime <i>O</i>-ethers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04371 """O-Hydrocarbyl oximes $\\ce{R2C=NOR'}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04372 "oximes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04372 "Compounds of structure $\\ce{R2C=NOH}$ derived from condensation of aldehydes or ketones with hydroxylamine. Oximes from aldehydes may be called aldoximes; those from ketones may be called ketoximes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04372 "isonitroso compounds")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04374))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04374 "oxoacids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04374 "Oxoacids (and its variants oxyacids, oxo acids, oxy-acids, oxiacids, oxacids) is a traditional name for any acid having oxygen in the acidic group. The term stands in contradistinction to `hydracids' (e.g. $\\ce{HCl}$) lacking oxygen. The term oxoacid now refers to a compound which contains oxygen, at least one other element, and at least one hydrogen bound to oxygen, and which produces a conjugate base by loss of positive hydrogen ion(s) (hydrons). E.g. $\\ce{P(OH)3}$, $\\ce{RC(=O)OH}$, $\\ce{HOSOH}$, $\\ce{HOCl}$, $\\ce{HON=O}$, $\\ce{(HO)2SO2}$, $\\ce{RP(=O)(OH)2}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04374 "oxyacid (oxy-acid)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04375))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04375 "oxocarbons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04375 "Compounds consisting wholly of carbon and oxygen, e.g. $\\ce{CO}$, $\\ce{O=C=O}$, $\\ce{O=C=C=C=O}$,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04376 "oxo carboxylic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04376 "Compounds having a carboxy group as well as an aldehydic or ketonic group in the same molecule, e.g. $\\ce{HC(=O)CH2CH2CH2C(=O)OH}$ 5-oxopentanoic acid. In an organic context the term is generally shortened to oxo acids. The full name should be used if confusion with oxoacids seems possible.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04376 "oxo acids")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04377 "oxo compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04377 "Compounds containing an oxygen atom, $\\ce{=O}$, doubly bonded to carbon or another element. The term thus embraces aldehydes, carboxylic acids, ketones, sulfonic acids, amides and esters. Oxo used as an adjective (and thus separated by a space) modifying another class of compound, as in oxo carboxylic acids, indicates the presence of an oxo substituent at any position. To indicate a double-bonded oxygen that is part of a ketonic structure, the term keto is sometimes used as a prefix, but such use has been abandoned by IUPAC for naming specific compounds. A traditional use of keto is for indicating oxidation of $\\ce{CHOH}$ to $\\ce{C=O}$ in a parent compound that contains $\\ce{OH}$ groups, such as carbohydrates, e.g. 3-ketoglucose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04378 "oxonium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04378 "The parent ion $\\ce{H3O^{+}}$ and substitution derivatives thereof.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04379 "oxonium ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04379 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2O^{+}-C^{-}R2}$.See: ylidesA class of 1,3-dipolar compounds of general structure $\\ce{R2C=O^{+}-Y^{-}}$, comprising carbonyl imides, carbonyl oxides and carbonyl ylides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04381 "oxygen-flask combustion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04381 "In order to determine elements which are easily volatilized, or are present as volatile species, organic material may be ashed in oxygen in a closed oxygen combustion system. One such method is oxygen-flask combustion by which the test sample is burned in a closed flask containing oxygen and an absorbing solution in which the analytes are subsequently determined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04382))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04382 "oxylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04382 "Species of the form $\\ce{RO^{+}}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3O^{+}}$ methoxylium, $\\ce{HO^{+}}$ hydroxylium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04383 "ozone hole")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04383 "A region of the stratosphere over Antarctica in which a marked decrease in the concentration of ozone has been observed in the Antarctic spring in recent years. The origin of this phenomenon is not yet established, but several theories based on both physical (transport related) and chemical processes (involvement of the halocarbons and their products of oxidation) have been suggested. The latter explanation appears to be in better accord with recent findings.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04384 "ozonides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04384 "The 1,2,4-trioxolanes formed by the reaction of ozone at a carbon–carbon double bond, or the analogous compounds derived from acetylenic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07087 "open-shell systems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07087 "Atomic or molecular systems in which the electrons are not completely assigned to orbitals in pairs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07179 "optically active polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07179 "Polymer capable of rotating the polarization plane of a transmitted beam of linear-polarized light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07305))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07305 "onion morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07305 "Multiphase morphology of roughly spherical shape that comprises alternating layers of different polymers arranged concentrically, all layers being of similar thickness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07306 "ordered co-continuous double gyroid morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07306 "Co-continuous morphology in which a set of two gyroid-based phase domains exhibits a highly regular, three-dimensional lattice-like morphology with Ia3d space group symmetry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07430 "OLED")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07430 "Acronym for organic light-emitting device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07432 "one-bond-flip")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07432 "Photoisomerization of one double bond in a conjugated double bond system. It is a regular E/Z photoisomerization.  Compare with bicycle-pedal mechanism, hula-twist mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07433 "one-photon photochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07433 "Photochromic process involving a one-photon mechanism. In this case, the photoproduct B is formed from the singlet or from the triplet state of the thermodynamically-stable molecular entity A.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07436 "optical multi-channel analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07436 "Detection system for fast acquisition of spectra. It consists of a 'polychromator' fitted with a detector that enables spatial resolution, e.g., a 'vidicon tube', a 'charge-coupled device' or a 'silicon photodiode array'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07437 "optical parametric amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07437 "Process of signal amplification by a parametric process and a pump wave.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07438 "optical parametric oscillator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07438 "Light source similar to a laser, but based on 'non-linear optical gain' from parametric amplification rather than on stimulated emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07569 "oxide network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07569 "Network comprising only metal–oxygen linkages.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07577 "organic–inorganic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07577 "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure comprised only of carbon but which has side-groups that include inorganic components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07578 "organically-modified ceramic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07578 "Chemically bonded hybrid material which is a crosslinked inorganic–organic polymer.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:OT07578 "organomodified ceramic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07579 "organically modified silica")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07579 "Silica modified by organic groups.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:OT07579 "organically modified silicate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04385 "π – π* state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04385 "An excited state related to the ground state by a π → π* transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04386 "π → π* transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04386 "An electronic transition described approximately as a promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' $\\ce{\\uppi\\! orbital}$ to an 'antibonding' $\\ce{\\uppi\\! orbital}$ designated as $\\ce{\\uppi\\!^{\\ast}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04393))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04393 "packed column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04393 "A tube containing a solid packing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04394 "packing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04394 "The active solid, stationary liquid plus solid support, or swollen gel put in the column. The term packing refers to the conditions existing before the chromatographic run is started (i.e. to the material introduced into the column), whereas the stationary phase refers to the conditions during the run.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04395 "paddlanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04395 "Tricyclic saturated hydrocarbons having two bridgehead carbon atoms joined through four bridges, systematically named tricyclo [\\(m.n.o.p^{\\rm{1,}(m+2)}\\)] alkanes, have been referred to as [\\(m.n.o.p\\)] paddlanes (when \\(p=0\\), the compounds are propellanes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04396 "pair attenuation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04396 "The attenuation coefficient when only the pair production process is taken into account.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04397))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04397 "pair correlation length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04397 "A quantity which approximates to the characteristic length associated with certain number density profiles of diffuse layers. \\(\\xi \\) is defined as the separation between two interacting entities (molecules, macromolecular segments, ions, spins), either in bulk or interfacial regions, beyond which an appropriate function, referred to as pair correlation function, either decays (e.g. to \\(\\frac{1}{e}\\) of an 'initial' value) or vanishes. A characteristic length must be confused neither with the effective thickness of the interfacial layer, nor with the range (effective distance) of the intermolecular (pair) potential. This range is defined by the distance over which this potential and the corresponding correlation function both vanish.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04398 "pair production")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04398 "The simultaneous formation of an electron and a positron as a result of the interaction of a photon of sufficient energy (&gt; \\(1.02\\ \\rm{MeV}\\)) with the field of a particle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04399))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04399 "Pallmann effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04399 "Synonymous with suspension effect  in an ion-selective electrode")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04400 "PAN-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04400 "Carbon fibres obtained from polyacrylonitrile (PAN) precursor fibres by stabilization treatment, carbonization and final heat treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04401 "paraffin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04401 "Obsolescent term for saturated hydrocarbons, commonly but not necessarily acyclic. Still widely used in the petrochemical industry, where the term designates acyclic saturated hydrocarbons, and stands in contradistinction to naphthenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04402 "parallel-chain crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04402 "A type of crystal resulting from parallel packing of stems, irrespective of the stems' directional sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04404 "paramagnetic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04404 "Substances having a magnetic susceptibility greater than 0 are paramagnetic. They are drawn into a magnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04405 "parent hydride")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04405 "An unbranched acyclic or cyclic structure or an acyclic/cyclic structure having a semisystematic or trivial name to which only hydrogen atoms are attached.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04406))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04406 "parent ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04406 "An electrically charged molecular moiety which may dissociate to form fragments, one or more of which may be electrically charged, and one or more neutral species. A parent ion may be a molecular ion or an electrically charged fragment of a molecular ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04407))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04407 "partial anodic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04407 "When a single electrochemical reaction occurs at an electrode (i.e. all other electrochemical reactions may be neglected): \\[I=I_{\\rm{a}}+I_{\\rm{c}}\\] where \\(I\\) is the total current, \\(I_{\\rm{a}}\\) is the positive partial anodic current and \\(I_{\\rm{c}}\\) is the negative partial cathodic current. When more than one reaction is significant the reactions may be numbered and the numbers used as subscripts: \\(I_{\\rm{1,a}}\\), \\(I_{\\rm{1,c}}\\), \\(I_{\\rm{2,a}}\\), etc. Then: \\[I=\\sum I_{i,\\rm{a}}+\\sum I_{i,\\rm{c}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04408))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04408 "partial charge exchange reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04408 "Synonymous with partial charge transfer reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04409 "partial charge transfer reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04409 "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the charge on a multiply-charged reactant ion is reduced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04410 "partial decay constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04410 "For a radionuclide: the probability in unit time for the decay of one of its nuclei by one of several modes of decay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04411 "partial digestion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04411 "In partial digestion and/or selective digestion procedures only part or some of the analytes present are brought into solution. This may be preferred to total decomposition if relative concentrations of the analyte in the test samples provide sufficient information (e.g. materials for geochemical exploration).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04412 "partial isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04412 "The function relating, at constant temperature and pressure, the amount of a particular component in the interfacial layer per unit area (or per unit mass of adsorbent) with its mole fraction (or concentration) in the liquid phase. This function can be evaluated only when the location and the thickness of the interfacial layer have been defined.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04412 "individual isotherm")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04413))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04413 "partial kinetic current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04413 "The current which would flow if mass transport were infinitely fast.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04414))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04414 "partially draining")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04414 "An adjective describing a chain macromolecule that behaves in a hydrodynamic field as though the solvent within the domain of the macromolecule were progressively more immobilized with respect to the macromolecule in the direction from its outer fringes inward. A freely draining macromolecule and a non-draining macromolecule are two extremes of the concept of a partially draining macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04415 "partial mass density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04415 "Change in mass due to addition of a small amount of component to a system divided by the change in volume of the system. The systematic name is partial volumic mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04418 "partial molar quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04418 "Change in the considered extensive quantity when an infinitesimal amount of substance is added to the system at constant temperature, pressure and amounts of all other constituents of the system divided by the added amount.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04421))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04421 "partial rate factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04421 "The rate of substitution at one specific site in an aromatic compound relative to the rate of substitution at one position in benzene. For example, the partial rate factor \\(f_{p}^{\\rm{Z}}\\) for para-substitution in a monosubstituted benzene $\\ce{C6H5Z}$ is related to the rate constants \\(k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{5}\\rm{Z}\\right)\\) and \\(k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{6}\\right)\\) for the total reaction (i.e. at all positions) of $\\ce{C6H5Z}$ and benzene, respectively, and % para (the percentage para-substitution in the total product formed from $\\ce{C6H5Z}$) by the relation: \\[f_{p}^{\\rm{Z}} = \\frac{6\\ k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{5}\\rm{Z}\\right)}{k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{6}\\right)}\\ \\frac{\\%\\ \\rm{para}}{100}\\] Similarly for meta-substitution: \\[f_{m}^{\\rm{Z}} = \\frac{6\\ k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{5}\\rm{Z}\\right)}{2\\ k\\left(\\rm{C}_{6}\\rm{H}_{6}\\right)}\\ \\frac{\\%\\ \\rm{meta}}{100}\\] (The symbols \\(p_{f}^{\\rm{Z}}\\), \\(m_{f}^{\\rm{Z}}\\), \\(o_{f}^{\\rm{Z}}\\) are also in use.) The term applies equally to the ipso position, and it can be extended to other substituted substrates undergoing parallel reactions at different sites with the same reagent according to the same rate law.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04422))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04422 "partial specific volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04422 "Change in volume of a system when a small amount of component is added divided by the mass of the added component. The systematic name is partial massic volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04423 "particle concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04423 "Commonly expressed in several ways: mass concentration (usually as \\(\\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{g}\\ \\rm{m}^{-3}\\)) or number concentration (number of particles \\(\\pu{cm-3}\\)); modern instrumentation allows measurement of the number of particles as a function of size as well as the total number present in a given air volume. For atmospheric aerosols, this is a complex distribution for which diameters range from below \\(0.01\\) to above \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\); the particles making the highest contribution to the total number density are in the size range below \\(0.1\\ \\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\), those contributing most to the total surface area are in the \\(0.1\\) to \\(1.0\\ \\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) range, while those with the highest contribution to the volume or mass of the aerosol come from both the \\(0.1\\) to \\(1.0\\ \\unicode[times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) and \\(1.0\\) to \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) ranges.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04424 "particle density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04424 "The number of particles (nuclear or elementary) divided by the containing volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04426))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04426 "particle induced X-ray emission analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04426 "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with charged particles other than electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04429))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04429 "particle scattering function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04429 "The ratio of the intensity of radiation scattered at an angle of observation \\(\\theta \\) to the intensity of radiation scattered at an angle zero, i.e. \\(P\\left(\\theta \\right)\\equiv \\frac{R\\left(\\theta \\right)}{R\\left(0\\right)}\\). An alternative recommended symbol is \\(P_{\\theta }\\). Synonymous with particle scattering factor.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04429 "particle scattering factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04430 "particle size")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04430 "To describe the size of liquid or solid particles (aerosol) the average or equivalent diameter is used. For non-spherical particles collected in an impactor, for example, the aerodynamic diameter of a particle of arbitrary shape and density refers to the size of a spherical particle of unit density that would deposit on a given impactor surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04431))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04431 "particle size distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04431 "The size of the liquid or solid particles in the atmosphere usually extends from \\(>0.01\\) to \\(< 100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter. In the earth's atmosphere the distribution function which describes the number of particles as a function of diameter, mass or surface area of the aerosol can be determined reasonably well with modern instrumentation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04432 "particular property")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04432 "A property of a given object (phenomenon, body or substance). 'Particular property' includes the concept of particular quantity. The adjective 'particular' may be omitted, if no ambiguity is caused.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04433 "particulate carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04433 "A carbon material consisting of separated monolithic particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04434))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04434 "particulate matter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04434 "A general term used to describe airborne solid or liquid particles of all sizes. The term aerosol is recommended for general use in describing airborne particulate matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04435))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04435 "partition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04435 "This term is often used as a synonym for distribution and extraction. However, an essential difference exists by definition between distribution constant or partition ratio and partition constant. The term partition should be, but is not invariably, applied to the distribution of a single definite chemical species between the two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04436 "partition chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04436 "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences between the solubility of the sample components in the stationary phase ( gas chromatography), or on differences between the solubilities of the components in the mobile and stationary phases (liquid chromatography).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04437 "partition coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04437 "This term is not recommended and should not be used as a synonym for partition constant, partition ratio or distribution ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04438 "partition constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04438 "The ratio of activity of a given species A in the extract to its activity in the other phase with which it is in equilibrium, thus: \\[\\left(K_{\\rm{D}}^{\\rm{o}}\\right)_{\\rm{A}} = \\frac{a_{\\rm{A,org}}}{a_{\\rm{A,aq}}}\\] Its value should not vary with composition but depends on the choice of standard states and on the temperature (and eventually the pressure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04439 "partition function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04439 "For a molecule the ratio of the total number of molecules to their number in the non-degenerate ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04440 "partition ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04440 "The ratio of the concentration of a substance in a single definite form, A, in the extract to its concentration in the same form in the other phase at equilibrium, e.g. for an aqueous/organic system: \\[\\left (K_{\\rm{D}} \\right )_{A} = \\frac{\\left [ A \\right ]_{\\rm{org}}}{\\left [ A \\right ]_{\\rm{aq}}}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04440 "distribution constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04442 "pascal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04442 "SI derived unit of pressure, \\(\\pu{Pa} = \\pu{N m-2} = \\pu{kg m-1 s-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04443 "passivation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04443 "The process of transition from the active to the passive state by formation of the passivating film. Passivation is achieved by an anodic current which at the respective electrode potential must be larger than the maximum current, or by the presence of an oxidized substance in the neighbouring solution which passivates by being reduced (passivator).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04444 "passivation potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04444 "The most negative electrode potential at which the passivating film is formed. The passivation potential is equal to or more positive than the equilibrium potential of formation of the phase constituting the film. Usually, the corrosion current goes through a maximum at the passivation potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04445))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04445 "passive metal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04445 "A metal corroding in the passive state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04446 "passive sampler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04446 "A device for preconcentration of trace substances from gaseous media based on molecular diffusion without controlled conveyance of the gas to be investigated (e.g. work place air).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04447 "passive state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04447 "The passive state is characterized by a contiguous 'passivating film' of solid corrosion products separating the metallic phase from the adjacent electrolyte. Corrosion in the passive state involves growth of the passivating film and/or transfer of metal ions through the film into the electrolyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04448))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04448 "Paterno–Büchi reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04448 "The photocycloaddition of an electronically excited carbonyl group to a ground state olefin yielding an oxetane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04449))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04449 "paucidisperse system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04449 "A colloidal system in which only a few particle-sizes occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04451 "peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04451 "The portion of a differential chromatogram recording the detector response when a single component is eluted from the column. If separation is incomplete, two or more components may be eluted as one 'unresolved peak'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04452))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04452 "peak analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04452 "The extraction of relevant peak parameters (i.e. position, area) from a measured spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04453 "peak area")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04453 "The area enclosed between the peak and the baseline.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04454))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04454 "peak area method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04454 "A kind of peak analysis in which a peak area is calculated by subtracting an estimate of the underlying continuum in a relevant part of a measured spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04455 "peak base")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04455 "The interpolation, in a differential chromatogram, of the baseline between the extremities of the peak.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04456 "peak concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04456 "The highest concentration of a given trace component which was measured with a continuous analyser during a specified sampling period.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04457 "peak current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04457 "In linear-sweep voltammetry, triangular-wave voltammetry, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry, and similar techniques, the maximum value of the faradaic current due to the reduction or oxidation of a substance B during a single sweep. This maximum value is attained after an interval during which the concentration of B at the electrode-solution interface decreases monotonically, while the faradaic current due to the reduction or oxidation of B increases monotonically, with time. It is attained before an interval during which this current decreases monotonically with time because the rate of transport of B toward the electrode-solution interface is smaller than the rate at which it is removed from the interface by electrolysis. The term has also been used to denote the maximum value of the faradaic current attributable to the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance in techniques such as ac polarography, differential pulse polarography, and derivative polarography. However, these techniques give curves that arise in ways different from that cited above, and the terms summit, summit current, and summit potential are therefore recommended for use in connection with such techniques.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04458 "peak elution volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04458 "The volume of mobile phase entering the column between the start of the elution and the emergence of the peak maximum, or the corresponding time. In most of the cases, this is equal to the total retention volume (time). There are, however, cases when the elution process does not start immediately at sample introduction. For example, in liquid chromatography, sometimes the column is washed with a liquid after the application of the sample to displace certain components which are of no interest and during this treatment the sample does not move along the column. In gas chromatography, there are also cases when a liquid sample is applied to the top of the column but its elution starts only after a given period. This term is useful in such cases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04460))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04460 "peak fitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04460 "A kind of peak analysis in which a relevant part of a spectrum is fitted with a theoretical response function.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04462))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04462 "peak height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04462 "The distance between the peak maximum and the peak base, measured in a direction parallel to the axis representing the detector response.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04463))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04463 "peak maximum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04463 "The point on the peak at which the distance to the peak base, measured in a direction parallel to the axis representing the detector response, is a maximum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04464))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04464 "peak potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04464 "In linear-sweep voltammetry, triangular-wave voltammetry, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry, and similar techniques, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the peak current is attained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04465))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04465 "peak resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04465 "The separation of two peaks in terms of their average peak width at base (\\(t_{\\rm{R2}}>t_{\\rm{R1}}\\)): \\[R_{\\rm{s}}=\\frac{t_{\\rm{R2}}\\,-\\,t_{\\rm{R1}}}{\\frac{w_{\\rm{b1}}\\,+\\,w_{\\rm{b2}}}{2}}=\\frac{2\\ (t_{\\rm{R2}}- t_{\\rm{R1}})}{w_{\\rm{b1}}\\,+\\,w_{\\rm{b2}}}\\] In the case of two adjacent peaks it may be assumed that \\(w_{\\rm{b1}}\\approx w_{\\rm{b2}}\\), and thus, the width of the second peak may be substituted for the average value: \\[R_{\\rm{s}}\\approx \\frac{t_{\\rm{R2}}\\,-\\,t_{\\rm{R1}}}{w_{\\rm{b2}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04466))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04466 "peak widths")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04466 "Peak widths represent retention dimensions (time or volume) parallel to the baseline. If the baseline is not parallel to the axis representing time or volume, then the peak widths are to be drawn parallel to this axis. Three peak-width values are commonly used in chromatography. Peak width at base (\\(w_{\\rm{b}}\\)) is the segment of the peak base intercepted by the tangents drawn to the inflection points on either side of the peak. Peak width at half height (\\(w_{\\rm{h}}\\)) is the length of the line parallel to the peak base at 50% of the peak height that terminates at the intersection with the two limbs of the peak. Peak width at inflection points (\\(w_{\\rm{i}}\\)) is the length of the line drawn between the inflection points parallel to the peak base. The peak width at base may be called the 'base width'. However, the peak width at half height must never be called the 'half width' because that has a completely different meaning. Also, the symbol \\(w_{1/2}\\) should never be used instead of \\(w_{\\rm{h}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04467 "pectins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04467 "Polyuronic acids mostly derived from ᴅ-galacturonic acid (abundant in some fruits).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04468 "Peierls transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04468 "A metal-insulator transition in quasi one-dimensional solids that occurs as a result of a band gap opening up at the Fermi energy due to a displacive distortion of the regular array increasing the unit cell length, usually a dimerization; the decrease in electronic energy outweighs the increase in lattice energy. Example: The transition in methylethylmorpholinium tetracyanoquinodimethanide at \\(335\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04469 "pellicular packing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04469 "In this case the stationary phase forms a porous outer shell on an impermeable particle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04470 "penams")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04470 "Natural and synthetic antibiotics containing the 4-thia-1-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptan-7-one structure, generally assumed to have the 5R configuration unless otherwise specified. The numbering of the penam skeleton differs from that of the von Baeyer named bicyclic system. Where they differ the von Baeyer numbering is shown in parentheses in the example.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04472 "pendant group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04472 "An offshoot, neither oligomeric nor polymeric from a chain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04472 "side group")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04473 "penems")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04473 "2,3-Didehydropenams.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04475))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04475 "penicillins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04475 "Substituted penams having the basic structure shown, including the natural penicillins and synthetic analogues.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04476))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04476 "Penning gas mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04476 "A Penning gas mixture consists of a rare gas containing impurity atoms possibly at very low concentrations. The impurity atoms have an ionization potential \\(V_{\\rm{ion}}\\) which is lower than or equal to the metastable potential (\\(V_{\\rm{meta}}\\)) of the parent noble gas. The Penning effect in a Penning gas mixture is the ionization by charge transfer (charge exchange) during collision between a metastable atom and a neutral atom which decreases the average energy to form an ion pair, e.g.  In a glow discharge, this results in an increase of the the ionization coefficient (Townsend first coefficient), a decrease in breakdown potential and a lowering of the cathode fall potential. The magnetic Penning effect describes the increase of the ionization probability of gas in a low pressure electrical discharge resulting from the helical (spiral) movement of electrons in a magnetic field placed normal to the anode-cathode electrical field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04478 "<i>pentaprismo</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04478 "An affix used in names to denote ten atoms bound into a pentagonal prism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04479))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04479 "peptides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04479 "Amides derived from two or more amino carboxylic acid molecules (the same or different) by formation of a covalent bond from the carbonyl carbon of one to the nitrogen atom of another with formal loss of water. The term is usually applied to structures formed from $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}amino acids}$, but it includes those derived from any amino carboxylic acid.  (R may be any organyl group, commonly but not necessarily one found in natural amino acids).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04480 "peptidoglycan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04480 "A glycosaminoglycan formed by alternating residues of ᴅ-glucosamine and either muramic acid {2-amino-3-O-[(S)-1-carboxyethyl]-2-deoxy-ᴅ-glucose} or ʟ-talosaminuronic acid (2-amino-2-deoxy-ʟ-taluronic acid), which are usually N-acetylated or N-glycoloylated. The carboxyl group of the muramic acid is commonly substituted by a peptide containing residues of both ʟ- and ᴅ-amino acids, whereas that of ʟ-talosaminuronic acid is substituted by a peptide consisting of ʟ-amino acids only.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04483))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04483 "percent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04483 "One part in a hundred, \\(\\rm{%} = 0.01\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04485 "percentage exposed")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04485 "The accessibility of the atoms of metal in metallic catalysts, supported or unsupported, depends upon the percentage of the total atoms of metal which are surface atoms. It is recommended that the term percentage exposed be employed for this quantity rather than the term dispersion which has been frequently employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04486 "percentage relative error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04486 "The relative error expressed in percent. It can be calculated from the relative error by multiplying by 100. Comment: The term 'percentage relative error' should always be quoted in full, rather than 'error' or 'percentage error' to avoid confusion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04486 "percentage error")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04487 "percentage standard deviation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04487 "The value of the relative standard deviation, expressed in percent. It can be calculated from the relative standard deviation by multiplying by 100. Comment: It is recommended that the 'relative standard deviation' be reported, rather than the 'percentage standard deviation', in order to avoid confusion where results themselves are expressed as percentages. The term 'coefficient of variation' in place of 'relative standard deviation' is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04490 "perfectly polarized interphase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04490 "Differences from the behaviour of interphases containing only neutral species arise when the interphase may be described as perfectly polarized. This term is used when no charged component is common to both phases adjoining the interphase. This may arise as a result of the equilibrium conditions or from the kinetics of charge transfer and leads to an interphase impermeable to electric charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04491 "pericyclic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04491 "A chemical reaction in which concerted reorganization of bonding takes place throughout a cyclic array of continuously bonded atoms. It may be viewed as a reaction proceeding through a fully conjugated cyclic transition state. The number of atoms in the cyclic array is usually six, but other numbers are also possible. The term embraces a variety of processes, including cycloadditions, cheletropic reactions, electrocyclic reactions, sigmatropic rearrangements, etc. (provided they are concerted).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04492 "perikinetic aggregation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04492 "The rate of aggregation is in general determined by the frequency of collisions and the probability of cohesion during collision. If the collisions are caused by Brownian motion, the process is called perikinetic aggregation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04493 "period")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04493 "Time for one cycle of a periodic phenomenon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04494 "periodic copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04494 "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules comprising more than two species of monomeric units in regular sequence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04495 "periodic copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04495 "A copolymerization in which a periodic copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04496 "periodic voltage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04496 "This general term is applicable to square, triangular, and other wave forms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04497 "peripheral atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04497 "A secondary reference (non-core) atom which at some point in the mechanistic sequence is situated in the molecule containing the core atom(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04499 "periselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04499 "The differentiation between two symmetry-allowed processes, for example the [2+4] vs. [4+6] cycloaddition of cyclopentadiene to tropone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04500 "peritectic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04500 "An isothermal, reversible reaction between two phases, a liquid and a solid, that results, on cooling of a binary, ternary, ... , \\(n\\) system in one, two, ... (\\(n- 1\\)) new solid phases. Synonymous with incongruent reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04500 "incongruent reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04501 "peritectoid reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04501 "An isothermal, reversible reaction in the solid state, that, on cooling of a binary, ternary, ... , \\(n\\) system, results in one, two, ... (\\(n-1\\)) new solid phases. For example, in a binary system containing two solids \\(\\alpha'\\) and \\(\\alpha''\\) \\[\\alpha' + \\alpha'' \\rightarrow \\beta\\] Synonymous with metatectoid reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04501 "metatectoid reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04502 "peritectoid temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04502 "The maximum temperature at which a peritectoid reaction can occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04503 "permeability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04503 "Permeability of vacuum multiplied by the relative permeability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04504 "permeability of vacuum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04504 "Fundamental physical constant by definition equal to \\(4\\ \\pi \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\rm{H m}^{-1}\\), exactly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04505 "permeation chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04505 "Separation based mainly upon exclusion effects, such as differences in molecular size and/or shape (e.g. molecular-sieve chromatography) or in charge (e.g. ion-exclusion chromatography). The term gel-permeation chromatography is widely used for the process when the stationary phase is a gel. The term gel-filtration is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04506 "permeation tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04506 "A device used for dynamic preparation of test gas mixtures by means of controlled permeation of a gaseous analyte out of a container through polymer material into a carrier gas stream. These devices containing certain condensable gases (e.g. $\\ce{NO2}$, $\\ce{SO2}$, etc.) when operated at closely controlled temperatures can be used as primary standards calibrated in terms of the weight loss per unit time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04507 "permittivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04507 "Permittivity of vacuum multiplied by the relative permittivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04508 "permittivity of vacuum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04508 "Fundamental physical constant \\(\\varepsilon_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\mu _{0}\\ c_{0}^{2}}\\) exactly, where \\(\\mu_{0}\\) is the permeability of vacuum and \\(c_{0}\\) the speed of light in vacuum. It is equal to \\(8.854\\ 187\\ 817\\,...\\times\\ 10^{-12}\\ \\rm{F m}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04509 "permselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04509 "A term used to define the preferential permeation of certain ionic species through ion-exchange membranes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04510 "peroxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04510 "Compounds of structure $\\ce{ROOR}$ in which $\\ce{R}$ may be any organyl group (The term is also used in an inorganic sense to denote salts of the anion $\\ce{O2^{2−}}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04511 "peroxisome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04511 "Organelle, similar to a lysosome, characterized by its content of catalase (EC 1.11.1.6), peroxidase (EC 1.11.1.7) and other oxidative enzymes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04513 "peroxy acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04513 "Acids in which an acidic $\\ce{–OH}$ group has been replaced by an $\\ce{–OOH}$ group, e.g. $\\ce{CH3C(=O)OOH}$ peroxyacetic acid, $\\ce{PhS(=O)2OOH}$ benzeneperoxysulfonic acid.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04513 "per acids")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04513 "peracids")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04515))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04515 "persistence length")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04515 "The average projection of the end-to-end vector on the tangent to the chain contour at a chain end in the limit of infinite chain length. The persistence length (symbol a) is the basic characteristic of the worm-like chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04516 "persistent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04516 "A term used to characterize radicals which have lifetimes of several minutes or greater in dilute solution in inert solvents. Persistence is a kinetic or reactivity property. In contrast, radical stability, which is a thermodynamic property, is expressed in terms of the $\\ce{C–H}$ bond strength of the appropriate hydrocarbon. The lifetime of a radical is profoundly influenced by steric shielding of the radical centre by bulky substituents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04517 "perspective formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04517 "A geometric representation of stereochemical features of a molecule or model which appears as a view from an appropriate direction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04518 "perturbed dimensions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04518 "The dimensions of an actual polymer random coil not in a theta state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04519 "pesticide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04519 "Strictly, a substance intended to kill pests: in common usage, any substance used for controlling, preventing, or destroying animal, microbiological or plant pests.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04520))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04520 "pesticide residue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04520 "Any substance or mixture of substances in food for man or animals resulting from the use of a pesticide including any specified derivatives, such as degradation and conversion products, metabolites, reaction products and impurities considered to be of toxicological significance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04521 "peta")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04521 "SI prefix for \\(10^{15}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{P}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04522 "petroleum coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04522 "A carbonization product of high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions obtained in petroleum processing (heavy residues). It is the general term for all special petroleum coke products such as green, calcined and needle petroleum coke.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04523 "petroleum pitch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04523 "A residue from heat treatment and distillation of petroleum fractions. It is solid at room temperature, consists of a complex mixture of numerous predominantly aromatic and alkyl-substituted aromatic hydrocarbons, and exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04524 "pH")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04524 "The quantity pH is defined in terms of the activity of hydrogen(1+) ions (hydrogen ions) in solution: \\[\\rm{pH} = -\\rm{lg}[a(\\rm{H}^{+})] = -\\rm{lg}[\\rm{m}(\\rm{H}^{+})\\gamma_{\\rm{m}}(\\rm{H}^{+})/m^{\\unicode{x29B5} }]\\] where \\(a(\\rm{H}^{+})\\) is the activity of hydrogen ion (hydrogen 1+) in aqueous solution, \\(\\rm{H}^{+}\\rm{(aq)}\\), \\(\\gamma_{\\rm{m}}(\\rm{H}^{+})\\) is the activity coefficient of \\(\\rm{H}^{+}\\rm{(aq)}\\) (molality basis) at molality \\(m(\\rm{H}^{+})\\), and \\(m^{⦵} = \\pu{1 mol kg-1}\\) is the standard molality.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04525 "pH<sub>0.5</sub> or pH<sub>1/2</sub>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04525 "That value of pH in an aqueous phase at which the distribution ratio is unity at equilibrium. 50% of the solute is extracted (\\(E = 0.5\\)) only when the phase ratio is unity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04526 "pharmacodynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04526 "Study of pharmacological actions on living systems, including the reactions with and binding to cell constituents, and the biochemical and physiological consequences of these actions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04528 "phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04528 "An entity of a material system which is uniform in chemical composition and physical state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04530 "phase inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04530 "A term used in two senses which should be specified: density inversion or continuity inversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04531 "phase ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04531 "The ratio of the volume of the mobile phase to that of the stationary phase in a column: \\[\\beta = \\frac{V_{0}}{V_{\\rm{s}}}\\] In the case of open-tubular columns the geometric internal volume of the tube (\\(V_{\\rm{c}}\\)) is to be substituted for \\(V_{0}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04532 "phase ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04532 "The ratio of the quantity of the solvent to that of the other phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04533 "phase rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04533 "The number of degrees of freedom, \\(F\\), that a system containing \\(C\\) components can have when \\(P\\) phases are in equilibrium, is given as: \\[F=C- P+2\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04534 "phase separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04534 "The process by which a single solid (liquid) phase separates into two or more new phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04535 "phase-space theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04535 "This is a theory applied to unimolecular or bimolecular reactions proceeding through long-lived complexes. The probability of reaction is assumed to be proportional to the number of states available to a particular product channel divided by the number of states corresponding to all product channels. The theory is used to predict rates, product energy distributions, product velocity distributions, and product angular momentum distributions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04536 "phase-transfer catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04536 "The phenomenon of rate enhancement of a reaction between chemical species located in different phases (immiscible liquids or solid and liquid) by addition of a small quantity of an agent (called the 'phase-transfer catalyst') that extracts one of the reactants, most commonly an anion, across the interface into the other phase so that reaction can proceed. These catalysts are salts of 'onium ions' (e.g. tetraalkylammonium salts) or agents that complex inorganic cations (e.g. crown ethers). The catalyst cation is not consumed in the reaction although an anion exchange does occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04537 "phase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04537 "A change in the nature of a phase or in the number of phases as a result of some variation in externally imposed conditions, such as temperature, pressure, activity of a component or a magnetic, electric or stress field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04538))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04538 "phenolates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04538 "Synonymous with phenoxides. The term phenolate should not be used for solvates derived from a phenol, for the ending -ate often occurs in names for anions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04539))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04539 "phenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04539 "Compounds having one or more hydroxy groups attached to a benzene or other arene ring, e.g. 2-naphthol:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04540 "phenomenological equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04540 """In the following only media which are isotropic with respect to mass transport (i.e. the transport coefficients are independent of direction) are being considered. In the linear range (not too far from equilibrium), for uniform temperature and neglecting external fields such as the earth's gravitational field, the flux density of species B is related to the gradients of the electrochemical potentials of all species by the phenomenological equation: \\[N_{\\rm{B}}- c_{\\rm{B}}\\ v_{\\rm{A}}=- \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i\\\\
i
eq A
\\end{array}}L_{\\rm{B}i}^{\\rm{A}}\\ 
abla \\mu _{i}\\] with \\[i=\\rm{B},\\rm{C},...\\] where \\(
abla \\mu _{i}\\) is the gradient of the electrochemical potential of species i. The proportionality factors \\(L_{\\rm{B}i}^{\\rm{A}}\\) are called phenomenological coefficients. Their values depend on the frame of reference. The latter is taken here to move with the velocity \\(v_{\\rm{A}}\\) of species A, and hence: \\[L_{\\rm{A}i}^{\\rm{A}}=0\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04541 "phenonium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04541 "The traditional generic name for those cyclohexadienyl cations that are spiro-annulated with a cyclopropane unit. Phenonium ions constitute a subclass of arenium ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04542 "phenotype")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04542 "The observable structural and functional characteristics of an organism determined by its genotype and modulated by its environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04543 "phenoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04543 "Salts or analogous metal derivatives of phenols; synonymous with phenolates. A preferable general term, however, is aryloxides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04544 "pheromone")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04544 "Substance used in olfactory communication between organisms of the same species eliciting a change in sexual or social behaviour. Synonymous with ectohormone, feromone.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04544 "ectohormone")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04544 "feromone")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04545 "pH glass electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04545 "A hydrogen-ion responsive electrode usually consisting of a bulb, or other suitable form, of special glass attached to a stem of high resistance glass complete with internal reference electrode and internal filling solution system. Other geometrical forms may be appropriate for special applications, e.g. capillary electrode for measurement of blood pH.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04546 "pH gradient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04546 "The differential change of pH with distance (\\(\\frac{\\rm{dpH}}{\\rm{d}I}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04547 "phonon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04547 """Elementary excitation in the quantum mechanical treatment of vibrations in a crystal lattice. An energy bundle that behaves as a particle of energy \\(h\\ 
u \\), with \\(
u \\) the vibration frequency and \\(h\\) the Planck constant.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04548))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04548 "phosphanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04548 "The saturated hydrides of tervalent phosphorus having the general formula $\\ce{P_{n}H_{n+2}}$. Individual members having an unbranched phosphorus chain are named phosphane, diphosphane, triphosphane, etc. The name of a saturated hydride of phosphorus wherein one or more phosphorus atoms have a bonding number of 5 is formed by prefixing locants and \\(\\lambda ^{5}\\) symbols to the name of the corresponding phosphane. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce{PH3}$ belong to the class phosphines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04549 "phosphanylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04549 "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{RP{:}}$ (former IUPAC name is phosphinediyls). A common non-IUPAC synonym is phosphinidenes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04549 "phosphinidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04549 "phosphinediyls")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04550 "phosphatidic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04550 "Derivatives of glycerol in which one hydroxy group, commonly but not necessarily primary, is esterified with phosphoric acid and the other two are esterified with fatty acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04551 "phosphazenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04551 "Compounds containing a phosphorus–nitrogen double bond, i.e. derivatives of $\\ce{H3P=NH}$ and $\\ce{HP=NH}$. A multiplicity of such bonds is present in various well-established chain, ring and cage compounds. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04552))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04552 "phosphine oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04552 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R3P=O  R3P^{+}–O^{−}}$ (analogously, phosphine imides and phosphine sulfides).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04553 "phosphines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04553 """$\\ce{PH3}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups $\\ce{R3P}$. $\\ce{RPH2}$, $\\ce{R2PH}$ and $\\ce{R3P}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$) are called primary, secondary and tertiary phosphines, respectively. A specific phosphine is preferably named as a substituted phosphane, e.g. $\\ce{CH3PH2}$ methylphosphane.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04554 "phosphinic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04554 "$\\ce{H2P(=O)OH}$ (phosphinic acid) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04556 "phosphinous acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04556 "$\\ce{H2POH}$ (phosphinous acid) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04557 "phospho")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04557 "A prefix used in biochemical nomenclature in place of phosphono to denote the $\\ce{-P(=O)(OH)2}$ group linked to a heteroatom, e.g. $\\ce{Me3N^{+}-CH2CH2–OP(=O)(OH)O^{-}}$ phosphocholine.An infix used in biochemical nomenclature to name phosphoric diesters, e.g. glycerophosphocholine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04558 "phosphoglycerides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04558 "Phosphoric diesters, esters of phosphatidic acids, generally having a polar head group ($\\ce{OH}$ or $\\ce{N2}$) on the esterified alcohol which typically is 2-aminoethanol, choline, glycerol, inositol, serine. The term includes lecithins, cephalins. E.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04559 "phospholipids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04559 "Lipids containing phosphoric acid as mono- or di-esters, including phosphatidic acids and phosphoglycerides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04560 "phosphonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04560 "$\\ce{HP(=O)(OH)2}$ (phosphonic acid) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04561 "phosphonitriles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04561 "Compounds of stoichiometric composition $\\ce{[X2PN]_{n}}$, in which $\\ce{X}$ is alkoxy, halogen, or other electronegative group, and \\(n\\) is a variable integer, the value of which may not be known.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04562 "phosphonium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04562 "Salts (and hydroxides) $\\ce{[R4P^{+}]X^{−}}$ containing tetracoordinate phosphonium ion and the associated anion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04563 "phosphonium ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04563 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R3P^{+}–C^{−}R2  R3P=CR2}$. Also known as Wittig reagents.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04563 "phosphorus ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04563 "Wittig reagents")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04564 "phosphono")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04564 "A prefix indicating the presence of the group $\\ce{–P(=O)(OH)2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04565 "phosphonous acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04565 "$\\ce{HP(OH)2}$ (phosphonous acid) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04566 "phosphoramides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04566 "Compounds in which one or more of the OH groups of phosphoric acid have been replaced with an amino or substituted amino group; commonly confined to the phosphoric triamides, $\\ce{P(=O)(NR2)3, since replacement of one or two $\\ce{OH}$ groups produces phosphoramidic acids $\\ce{P(=O)(OH)(NR2)2}$, $\\ce{P(=O)(OH)2(NR2)}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04567 "phosphoranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04567 "The mononuclear hydride $\\ce{PH5}$, systematically named \\(\\lambda ^{5}\\)-phosphane, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. By extension, the literature also applies the term to phosphonium ylides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04568 "phosphoranyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04568 "A tetracoordinate phosphorus species which has nine valence-shell electrons $\\ce{R4P^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04569 "phosphorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04569 "From a phenomenological point of view, the term has been used to describe long-lived luminescence. In mechanistic photochemistry, the term designates luminescence involving change in spin multiplicity, typically from triplet to singlet or vice versa. The luminescence from a quartet state to a doublet state is also phosphorescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04571 "phosphoroscope")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04571 "A mechanical device used to separate phosphorescence from fluorescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04574 "photoacoustic detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04574 "A pressure-sensitive detector used to detect intermittent radiation absorbed in a black body or in the sample concerned. The resulting rapid temperature change produces a transient pressure oscillation that is observed with the help of a microphone, or a piezoelectric device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04575 "photoacoustic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04575 "Generation of heat after absorption of radiation, due to radiationless deactivation or chemical reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04576 "photoacoustic spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04576 "A spectroscopic technique based on the photoacoustic effect. A photoacoustic spectrum consists of a plot of the intensity of the acoustic signal detected by a microphone or a 'piezoelectric' detector, against the excitation wavelength or another quantity related to the photon energy of the modulated excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04577 "photoadsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04577 "Irradiation by light (usually visible or ultraviolet) may affect adsorption. In a system containing adsorptive and adsorbent exposure to light may lead to increased adsorption (photoadsorption) or it may lead to desorption of an adsorbate (photodesorption).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04578 "photoaffinity labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04578 "Technique by which a photochemically reactive molecular entity, specifically associated with a biomolecule, is photo-excited in order to covalently attach a label to the biomolecule, usually via intermediates. Identification of the resulting photo-crosslinked product provides structural information on the protein′s binding site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04579 "photoassisted catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04579 "Catalytic reaction involving production of a catalyst by absorption of light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04580 "photocatalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04580 "Change in the rate of a chemical reaction or its initiation under the action of ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation in the presence of a substance—the photocatalyst—that absorbs light and is involved in the chemical transformation of the reaction partners.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04583 "photochemical equivalence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04583 "According to the principle of photochemical equivalence, there is a one-to-one relationship between the number of photons absorbed by a system and the number of excited species produced. The principle often fails for light of high intensity (e.g. laser beams), when molecules may absorb more than one photon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04585 "photochemical reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04585 "Generally used to describe a chemical reaction caused by absorption of ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation. There are many ground-state reactions, which have photochemical counterparts. Among these are photochemical nitrogen extrusions, photocycloadditions, photodecarbonylations, photodecarboxylations, photoenolizations, photo-Fries rearrangement, photoisomerizations, photooxidations, photorearrangements, photoreductions, photosubstitutions, etc.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04585 "photoreaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04586 "photochemical smog")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04586 "Product of photochemical reactions caused by solar radiation and occurring in polluted air as well as in air contaminated by compounds emitted by natural sources, such as those emitted by vegetation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04588 "photochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04588 "The branch of chemistry concerned with the chemical effects of light (far UV to IR).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04589 "photochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04589 "Reversible transformation of a molecular entity between two forms, A and B, having different absorption spectra, induced in one or both directions by absorption of electromagnetic radiation. The spectral change produced is typically, but not necessarily, of visible colour and is accompanied by differences in other physical properties. The thermodynamically stable form A is transformed by irradiation into form B. The back reaction can occur thermally (photochromism of type T) or photochemically (photochromism of type P).  The terms 'switch-on' and 'switch-of' are sometimes used to denote the two directions of the photochromic reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04590 "photoconductive detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04590 "In a photoconductive detector an electric potential is applied across the absorbing region and causes a current to flow in proportion to the irradiance if the photon energy exceeds the energy gap between the valence and the conduction band. Depending on their spectral responsivity function, photoconductive detectors are divided into photoconductive detectors for the visible wavelength range e.g. cadmium sulfide or $\\ce{CdS}$ photoconductive detectors, photoconductive detectors for the near infrared wavelength range e.g. lead sulfide or $\\ce{PbS}$ photoconductive detectors, photoconductive detectors for the infrared wavelength range e.g. silicon doped with arsenide or $\\ce{Si:As}$ photoconductive detectors, and the mercury-cadmium-telluride or $\\ce{HgCdTe}$ photoconductive detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04591 "photoconductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04591 "Electrical conductivity resulting from photoproduction of charge carriers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04592 "photocrosslinking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04592 "The formation of a covalent linkage between two macromolecules or between two different parts of one macromolecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04593 "photochemical curing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04593 "Curing induced by photo-irradiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04593 "photo-curing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04594 "photocurrent yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04594 "The quantum efficiency of electron transport between the two electrodes of a photovoltaic cell or photoelectrochemical cell.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04594 "current yield")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04595 "photodegradation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04595 "The photochemical transformation of a molecule into lower molecular weight fragments, usually in an oxidation process. This term is widely used in the destruction (oxidation) of pollutants by UV-based processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04597 "photodetachment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04597 "Ejection of an electron from a negative ion upon photoexcitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04598 "photodiode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04598 "A two-electrode, radiation-sensitive junction formed in a semiconductive material. A junction is formed by two successive regions of a semiconductive material having, respectively, an excess of electrons (n-type) or holes (p-type). A bias potential applied to the detector creates a region at the interface that is depleted of majority carriers. Each incident photon produces electron-hole pairs in the depletion region resulting in a measurable signal current. The photodiode can be operated either with zero bias in the photovoltaic mode where the photodiode is actually generating the electric potential supplied to the load. In a biased mode, the photoconductive mode, the reverse current is proportional to the irradiation. A Schottky-barrier photodiode is constructed by deposition of a metal film on a semiconductor surface in such a way that no interface layer is present. The barrier thickness depends on the impurity dopant concentration in the semiconductor layer. The incident radiation generates electon-hole pairs within the depletion region of the barrier where they are collected efficiently and rapidly by the built-in field. A PIN (p-intrinsic-n) diode is a planar diffused diode consisting of a single crystal having an intrinsic (undoped or compensated) region sandwiched between p- and n-type regions. A bias potential applied across the detector depletes the intrinsic region of charge carriers, constituting the radiation-sensitive detector volume. The number of electron-hole pairs produced is dependent on the energy of the incident photons. An avalanche photodiode is a photodiode in which the photogenerated electron-hole pairs are accelerated by a bias potential near to breakdown potential so that further electron-hole pairs are formed leading to saturation of the photocurrent. This operational mode for photon counting is the so-called Geiger mode, similar to that of the gas filled Geiger counter. Avalanche photodiodes can also be operated in the proportional mode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04599 "photodiode array")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04599 "An arrangement of a number of photodiodes on a single chip.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04600 "photodynamic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04600 "A term used in photobiology to refer to photoinduced damage requiring the simultaneous presence of light, photosensitizer and molecular oxygen. A sensitized photooxidation which involves molecular oxygen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04601 "photoelectrical effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04601 "The ejection of an electron from a solid or a liquid by a photon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04602 "photoelectric attenuation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04602 "The attenuation coefficient when only the photoelectric process is taken into account.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04604 "photoelectric peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04604 "Of a spectrum of γ-radiation, the part of the spectral response curve corresponding to the absorption in the radiation detector by the photoelectrical effect of the detected $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{energy}$. In most cases the peak also contains events caused by multiple processes and use of the expression full energy peak or full energy peak is to be preferred. Synonymous with photopeak.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04604 "photopeak")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04605 "photoelectrochemical cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04605 "An electrochemical cell in which current and a voltage are simultaneously produced upon absorption of light by one or more of the electrodes. Usually at least one of the electrodes is a semiconductor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04606 "photoelectrochemical etching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04606 "The dissolution of a semiconductor in an electrolytic solution upon exposure to light. Used in the photopatterning of semiconductor surfaces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04607 "photoelectrochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04607 "A term applied to a hybrid field of chemistry employing techniques which combine photochemical and electrochemical methods for the study of the oxidation-reduction chemistry of the ground or excited states of molecules or ions. In general, it is the chemistry resulting from the interaction of light with electrochemical systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04608 "photoelectrolytic cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04608 "A cell in which radiant energy causes a net chemical conversion in the cell, e.g. so as to produce hydrogen as a useful fuel. These cells can be classified as photosynthetic or photocatalytic. In the former case, radiant energy provides a Gibbs energy to drive a reaction such as \\(\\rm{H}_{2}\\rm{O}\\rightarrow \\rm{H}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\rm{O}_{2}\\) , and electrical or thermal energy may be later recovered by allowing the reverse, spontaneous reaction to proceed. In a photocatalytic cell the photon absorption promotes a reaction with \\(\\Delta G < 0\\) so there is no net storage of chemical energy, but the radiant energy speeds up a slow reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04609 "photoelectron spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04609 "A spectroscopic technique which measures the kinetic energy of electrons emitted upon the ionization of a substance by high energy monochromatic photons. A photoelectron spectrum is a plot of the number of electrons emitted versus their kinetic energy. The spectrum consists of bands due to transitions from the ground state of an atom or molecular entity to the ground and excited states of the corresponding radical cation. Approximate interpretations are usually based on 'Koopmans theorem' and yield orbital energies. PES and UPS (UV photoelectron spectroscopy) refer to the spectroscopy using vacuum ultraviolet sources, while ESCA (electron spectroscopy for chemical analysis) and XPS use X-ray sources.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04611 "photoelectron yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04611 "The number of photoelectrons emitted by the sample per incident photon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04612 "photoemissive detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04612 "A detector in which a photon interacts with a solid surface, which is called the photocathode, or a gas, releasing a photoelectron. This process is called the external photoelectric effect. The photoelectrons are collected by an electrode at positive electric potential, i.e. the anode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04613))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04613 "photoexcitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04613 "The production of an excited state by the absorption of ultraviolet, visible, or infrared radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04614 "photo-Fries rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04614 "A photorearrangement of aryl or acyl esters to give the-rearranged product (as well as the-rearranged product):")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04615 "photogalvanic cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04615 "An electrochemical cell in which current or voltage changes result from photochemically generated changes in the relative concentrations of reactants in a solution phase oxidation-reduction couple.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04617))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04617 "photoinduced electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04617 "An electron transfer resulting from an electronic state produced by the resonant interaction of electromagnetic radiation with matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04618 "photoinduced polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04618 "The polymerization of a monomer by a free radical or ionic chain reaction initiated by photoexcitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04619 "photoinitiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04619 "The photoproduction of a free radical or ion capable of initiating a chain reaction such as a polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04620 "photoionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04620 "The term generally used to describe ionization of any species by photons. The process may, for example, be written  Electrons and photons do not 'impact' molecules or atoms; they interact with them in ways that result in various electronic excitations including ionization. For this reason it is recommended that in mass spectrometry the terms 'electron impact' and 'photon impact' not be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04621 "photoionization detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04621 "Radiation from an ultraviolet lamp ionizes certain species in the carrier gas. A potential difference is applied and the resulting ionization current is detected. The detector is only useful for substances with ionization potentials below about \\(11\\ \\rm{eV}\\). This makes it quite useful for detecting one component of a combined eluent when the other component, for instance nitrogen, has a high ionization potential. The detector has a small linear dynamic range and is capable of detecting substances below \\(1\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04622 "photoisomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04622 "Photochemical process leading to an isomerization of the substrate, either by bond rotation, skeletal rearrangement or atom- or group- transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04623 "photoluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04623 "Luminescence from direct photoexcitation of the emitting species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04624 "photolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04624 "The cleavage of one or more covalent bonds in a molecular entity resulting from absorption of light, or a photochemical process in which such cleavage is an essential part. For example:  The term is used incorrectly to describe irradiation of a sample, although in the combination flash photolysis this usage is accepted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04625 "photometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04625 "The measurement of light over wavelengths which produce visual sensation (approximately \\(380{-}780\\ \\rm{nm}\\)). Thus photometric quantities do not refer to a specific wavelength but to the light emitted by a standard source (formerly a 'standard international candle', now a blackbody radiator emitting at the temperature of solidifying platinum, \\(2042\\ \\rm{K}\\)). luminance and illuminance are the photometric analogues of the radiometric quantities radiance and irradiance, respectively, but conversion from photometric units (e.g. \\(\\rm{lm cm}^{-2}\\)) to radiometric units (e.g. \\(\\pu{J s-1 cm-2}\\)) requires convolution over wavelength of the spectral radiation with the relative spectral response of the human eye. The standard response is called the 'spectral luminous efficiency of radiation', and is tabulated for daylight adapted vision in photopic response tables.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04626 "photomultiplier tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04626 "A vacuum phototube with additional amplification by electron multiplication. It consists of a photocathode, a series of dynodes, called a dynode chain on which a secondary-electron multiplication process occurs, and an anode. According to the desired response time, transit time, time spread, gain, or low dark current, different types of dynode structures have been developed, e.g. circular cage structure, linear focused structure, venetian blind structure, box and grid structure. Some special dynode structures permit combination with additional electric or magnetic fields. A strip dynode photomultiplier tube consists of a photocathode followed by thin dynode material on an insulating substrate. In a continuous-strip photomultiplier, two strip dynodes are arranged in parallel. A potential applied to the ends of the two strips produces an electric field across the continuous strip dynodes, giving rise to electron multiplication along the dynodes. In a resistance-strip magnetic photomultiplier, a uniform magnetic field is applied to the planes of the strips, so that the electrons travel in the crossed electric and magnetic fields. A channel photomultiplier tube photocathode consists of a channel electron multiplier (CEM) system for the photoelectrons, and an anode to collect the final electron current. The basic part of the CEM is a tube with a semiconducting inner surface. In general it is curved in order to inhibit the acceleration of positive ions towards the photocathode. A number of small channels called microchannels can be constructed in arrays for imaging applications.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04627 "photon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04627 """Particle of zero charge, zero rest mass, spin quantum number 1, energy \\(h\\ 
u \\) and momentum \\(\\frac{h\\ 
u }{c}\\)(\\(h\\) is the Planck constant, \\(
u \\) the frequency of radiation and \\(c\\) the speed of light), carrier of electromagnetic force.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04629 "photon counting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04629 "Recording of sequential single photons counted by way of recording and counting sequential electron pulses at the anode of the photomultiplier.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04629 "single-photon counting")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04631 "photon exitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04631 "Photon flux, number basis, \\(q_{\\rm{p}}\\), emitted by an element of the surface containing the source point under consideration divided by the area \\(S\\) of that element. SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-2}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04631 "photon emittance")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04631 "specific photon emission")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04632 "photon exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04632 "Photon irradiance, \\(E_{\\rm{p}}\\), integrated over the time of irradiation for a beam incident from all upward directions. SI unit is \\(\\rm{m}^{-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04633))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04633 "photon flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04633 "The number of photons (quanta, \\(N\\)) per unit time. (\\(\\frac{\\rm{d}N}{\\rm{d}t}\\), simplified expression: \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} _{\\rm{p}} = \\frac{N}{t}\\) when the number of photons is constant over the time considered). The SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\). Alternatively, the term can be used with the amount of photons (mol or its equivalent einstein), the SI unit then being \\(\\pu{mol s-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04634 "photon fluence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04634 "Amount of photons (quanta of radiation) incident on a small sphere from all directions, divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere and integrated over time. Photons per surface area (\\(\\rm{quanta m}^{-2}\\)). SI unit is \\(\\rm{m}^{-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04635))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04635 "photon fluence rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04635 "Rate of photon fluence. Total number of photons (\\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\)) incident from all directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of the sphere and per time interval. SI unit is \\(\\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\). Same as photon fluence rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04636))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04636 "photon flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04636 "Number of photons (quanta of radiation, \\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\)) per time interval. SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04637 "photon irradiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04637 "Number of photons (quanta of radiation, \\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\)) per time interval (photon flux), \\(q_{\\rm{p}}\\), incident from all upward directions on a small element of surface containing the point under consideration divided by the area of the element. SI unit is \\(\\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\). Equivalent definition: Integral, taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point, of the expression \\(L_{\\rm{p}}\\, \\rm{cos}\\:\\theta\\, \\rm{d}\\varOmega\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\(\\varOmega\\) and \\(\\theta\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04638 "photon quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04638 "A group of quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation in terms of the number of photons. The symbols of photon quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript p.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04639))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04639 "photon radiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04639 "Number of photons (quanta of radiation, \\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\)) per time interval (photon flux), \\(q_{\\rm{p}}\\), leaving or passing through a small transparent element of surface in a given direction from the source about the solid angle \\(\\varOmega\\), divided by the solid angle and by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given beam direction, \\(\\rm{d}S_{\\perp } = \\rm{d}S\\, cos\\,\\theta\\), with \\(\\theta\\) the angle between the normal to the surface and the direction of the beam. Equivalent definition: Integral taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point, of the expression \\(L_{\\rm{p}}\\, cos\\,\\theta\\,\\rm{d}\\varOmega\\), with \\(L_{\\rm{p}}\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\(\\varOmega\\) and \\(\\theta\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04640 "photooxidation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04640 "Oxidation reactions induced by light. Common processes are: 1, the loss of one or more electrons from a chemical species as a result of photoexcitation of that species; 2, the reaction of a substance with oxygen under the influence of light. When oxygen remains in the product this latter process is also called photooxygenation. Reactions in which neither the substrate nor the oxygen are electronically excited are sometimes called photoinitiated oxidations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04641))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04641 "photooxygenation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04641 "The incorporation of molecular oxygen into a molecular entity. There are three common mechanisms: Type I: the reaction of triplet molecular oxygen with radicals formed photochemically. Type II: the reaction of photochemically produced singlet molecular oxygen with molecular entities to give rise to oxygen containing molecular entities. The third mechanism proceeds by electron transfer producing superoxide anion as the reactive species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04645 "photophoresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04645 "In aerosol physics, the motion of particles due to the influence of light. In many cases, this amounts to a special form of thermophoresis due to the heating of the particles by the light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04646))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04646 "photophosphorylation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04646 "The transformation of light (solar) energy for the reduction of $\\ce{CO2}$ and the generation of adenosine 5'-triphosphate, ATP.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04647))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04647 "photophysical processes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04647 "Photoexcitation and subsequent events which lead from one to another state of a molecular entity through radiation and radiationless transitions. No chemical change results.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04647 "primary photoprocess")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04648 "photopolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04648 "Polymerization processes requiring a photon for the propagation step.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04650 "photoreduction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04650 "Reduction reactions induced by light. Common processes are: 1, addition of one or more electrons to a photoexcited species; 2, the photochemical hydrogenation of a substance. Reactions in which the substrate is not electronically excited are sometimes called photoinitiated reductions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04651 "photoresist")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04651 "A photoimaging material, generally applied as a thin film, whose local solubility properties can be altered photochemically. A subsequent development step produces an image which is useful for the fabrication of microelectronic devices (e.g. integrated circuits).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04652))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04652 "photosensitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04652 "The process by which a photochemical or photophysical alteration occurs in one molecular entity as a result of initial absorption of radiation by another molecular entity called a 'photosensitizer'. In mechanistic photochemistry the term is limited to cases in which the photosensitizer is not consumed in the reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04652 "sensitization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04654 "photostationary state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04654 "A steady state reached by a reacting chemical system when light has been absorbed by at least one of the components. At this state the rates of formation and disappearance are equal for each of the transient molecular entities formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04655))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04655 "photosynthesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04655 "A metabolic process involving plants and some types of bacteria (e.g. Chromatiaceae, Rhodospirillaceae, Chlorobiaceae) in which light energy absorbed by chlorophyll and other photosynthetic pigments results in the reduction of $\\ce{CO2}$ followed by the formation of organic compounds. In plants the overall process involves the conversion of $\\ce{CO2}$ and $\\ce{H2O}$ to carbohydrates (and other plant material) and the release of $\\ce{O2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04656 "photothermal effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04656 "An effect produced by photoexcitation resulting partially or totally in the production of heat.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04657))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04657 "photothermography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04657 "A process utilizing both light and heat, simultaneously or sequentially, for image recording.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04658 "phototransistor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04658 "A bipolar transistor with its base-collector junction acting as a photodiode, which, if irradiated, controls the response of the device. Due to the inherent current gain (of the transistor) the responsivity of the phototransistor is greater than that of photodiodes. A Darlington phototransistor consists of two separate transistors coupled in the high-impedance Darlington configuration with a phototransistor as the input transistor. A field effect phototransistor or photo-FET is a field effect transistor (FET) that employs photogeneration of carriers in the channel region (the neutral region sandwiched between the insulator and the depletion region under the gate of the FET). It is characterized by high responsivity due to the high current gain of the FET.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04659 "photovoltaic cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04659 "A solid state device, usually a semiconductor such as silicon, which absorbs photons with energies higher than or equal to the bandgap energy and simultaneously produces electric power.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04660 "pH-rate profile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04660 "A plot of observed rate coefficient, or more usually its decadic logarithm, against pH of solution, other variables being kept constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04662))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04662 "phthaleins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04662 "3,3-Bis(hydroxyaryl)-2-benzofuran-1(3H)-ones generally derived from the condensation of phthalic anhydride with phenols. E.g. phenolphthalein.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04663 "phthalides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04663 "3,3-Di(hydrocarbyl) or 3-hydrocarbylidene-2-benzofuran-1(3H)-ones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04665 "physical network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04665 "Polymer network with junction points or zones formed by physically interacting chains which need not be permanent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04666 "physical quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04666 "An attribute of a phenomenon, body or substance that may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04666 "measurable quantity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04667))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04667 "physisorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04667 "Adsorption in which the forces involved are intermolecular forces (van der Waals forces) of the same kind as those responsible for the imperfection of real gases and the condensation vapours, and which do not involve a significant change in the electronic orbital patterns of the species involved. The term 'van der Waals adsorption' is synonymous with physical adsorption, but its use is not recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04667 "physical adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04667 "van der Waals adsorption")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04668 "phytotoxicant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04668 "An agent which produces a toxic effect in vegetation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04670 "pico")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04670 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-12}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{p}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04671 "picrates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04671 "Salts or charge-transfer complexes of picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04672))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04672 "piezoluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04672 "Luminescence observed when certain solids are subjected to a change in pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04673 "pile-up")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04673 "The processing by a radiation spectrometer of pulses resulting from the simultaneous adsorption of independent particles or photons in a radiation detector. As a result they are counted as one single particle or photon with energy between the individual energies and the sum of these energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04674 "pinacols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04674 "Tetra(hydrocarbyl)ethane-1,2-diols, $\\ce{R2C(OH)–(OH)R2}$, of which the tetramethyl example is the simplest one and is itself commonly known as pinacol (benzpinacol is the tetraphenyl analogue).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04676 "PIN semiconductor detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04676 "A semiconductor detector consisting of a compensated region between a p and a n region. The compensated region is often referred to as intrinsic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04677))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04677 "pitch")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04677 "A residue from pyrolysis of organic material or tar distillation which is solid at room temperature, consisting of a complex mixture of numerous, essentially aromatic hydrocarbons and heterocyclic compounds. It exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature. When cooled from the melt, pitches solidify without crystallization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04678))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04678 "pitch-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04678 "Carbon fibres obtained from pitch precursor fibres after stabilization treatment, carbonization and final heat treatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04679 "pitting corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04679 "A special type of non-uniform corrosion of passive metals, resulting in the formation of pits, which usually occurs in the presence of certain anions at electrode potentials positive to a critical pitting potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04681 "planar chirality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04681 "Term used by some authorities to refer to stereoisomerism resulting from the arrangement of out-of-plane groups with respect to a plane (chirality plane). It is exemplified by the atropisomerism of (E)-cyclooctene (chiral plane = double bond and attached atoms) or monosubstituted paracyclophane (chiral plane = substituted ring). The configuration of molecular entities possessing planar chirality is specified by the stereodescriptors \\(R_{\\rm{p}}\\) and \\(S_{\\rm{p}}\\) (or \\(P\\) and \\(M\\)).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04681 "planar stereoisomerism")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04682 "planar chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04682 "A separation technique in which the stationary phase is present as or on a plane. The plane can be a paper, serving as such or impregnated by a substrate as the stationary bed (paper chromatography, PC) or a layer of solid particles spread on a support e.g. a glass plate (thin layer chromatography, TLC). Sometimes planar chromatography is also termed open-bed chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04683 "planar film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04683 "A film whose boundaries are represented by ideal parallel planes. The planar film thickness is defined as the distance between these planes. The terms nanometre thick film, micrometre thick film and millimetre thick film refer specifically to film thickness in the ranges \\(0.1{-}100\\ \\rm{nm}\\), \\(0.1{-}100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) and \\(0.1{-}100\\ \\rm{mm}\\), respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04685 "Planck constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04685 "The Planck constant, \\(h\\), is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units, the SI, with \\(h = \\pu{6.62607015E-34 J s}\\). The Planck constant is considered as a fundamental constant of nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04687))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04687 "plasma")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04687 "A gas which is at least partly ionized and contains particles of various types, viz. electrons, atoms, ions and molecules. The plasma as a whole is electrically neutral.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04688 "plasma desorption ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04688 "The ionization of any species by interaction with heavy particles (which may be ions or neutral atoms) formed as a result of the fission of a suitable nuclide adjacent to a target supporting the sample.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04688 "fission fragment ionization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04689))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04689 "plasmid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04689 "An extrachromosomal genetic element consisting generally of a circular duplex of DNA which can replicate independently of chromosomal DNA. R-plasmids are responsible for the mutual transfer of antibiotic resistance among microbes. Plasmids are used as vectors for cloning DNA in bacteria or yeast host cells.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04690))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04690 "plastic flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04690 "Steady flow occurring only above a certain finite stress.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04691 "plastic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04691 "A first-order transition that occurs when a low-temperature phase transforms to an orientationally-disordered high-temperature phase with a strong variation of enthalpy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04692 "plateau border")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04692 "Except for free-floating bubbles, films have to be supported by frames, bulk surfaces or other films. The transition zone separating these from the film proper, always containing some bulk liquid, is called a plateau border.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04693 "plate height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04693 "The column length (\\(L\\)) divided by the plate number: \\[H=\\frac{L}{N}\\] It is also called the height equivalent to one theoretical plate (HETP).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04693 "height equivalent to one theoretical plate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04694))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04694 "plate number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04694 "A number indicative of column performance, calculated from the following equations which depend on the selection of the peak width expression: \\[N=(\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}}}{\\sigma })^{2}=(\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}}}{\\sigma })^{2}\\] \\[N=16\\ (\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}}}{w_{\\rm{b}}})^{2}=16\\ (\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}}}{w_{\\rm{b}}})^{2}\\] \\[N=5.545\\ (\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}}}{w_{\\rm{h}}})^{2}=5.545\\ (\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}}}{w_{\\rm{h}}})^{2}\\] The value of 5.545 stands for \\(8\\ \\ln 2\\). These expressions assume a Gaussian (symmetrical) peak. In these expressions the units for the quantities inside the brackets must be consistent so that their ratio is dimensionless: i.e. if the numerator is a volume, then peak width must also be expressed in terms of volume. In former nomenclature the expressions 'number of theoretical plates' or 'theoretical plate number' were used for the same term. For simplification, the present name is suggested.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04695 "pleiotropic gene")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04695 "A gene affecting more than one (apparently unrelated) characteristic of the phenotype.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04696 "ploidy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04696 "A term indicating the number of sets of chromosomes present in an organism, e.g. haploid (one) or diploid (two).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04697))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04697 "plug-flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04697 "A packed bed flow reactor is commonly called a fixed bed reactor and the term plug-flow is also used to indicate that no attempt is made to back-mix the reaction mixture as it passes through the catalyst bed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04699 "plumbylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04699 "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{R2Pb{:}}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04699 "plumbylenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04700 "plume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04700 "The gaseous and aerosol effluents which are emitted from a chimney and the volume of space which they occupy (often visible). The shape of the plume and the concentrations of pollutants in it at various points along the path of the plume are sensitive functions of the meteorology, local topography and the chemistry which occurs in the plume. Urban plumes are observed over many urban areas and downwind of these areas in which a combination of man sources of pollution are concentrated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04702 "pneumatic detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04702 "A pressure-sensitive detector based on the pressure increase of a gas. A special type is the Golay cell where the pressure change is detected by observing the deflection off one of the chamber walls.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04703 "point group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04703 "The classification of the symmetry elements of an object. It is denoted in the Schoenflies notation by an italic symbol, such as \\(C_{3}\\), \\(D_{2}\\), \\(T_{\\rm{d}}\\), etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04704))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04704 "point of zero charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04704 "A surface charge is at its point of zero charge when the surface charge density is zero. It is a value of the negative logarithm of the activity in the bulk of the charge-determining ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04705 "poise")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04705 "cgs unit of dynamic viscosity, \\(\\rm{P} = 0.1\\ \\rm{Pa s}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04706))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04706 "poison")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04706 "An inhibitory substance characterized by its propensity to attach very strongly, by a true chemical bond (e.g. covalent) to the surface atoms or ions constituting the catalytically active sites. Poisons act in minute quantities. Typical poisons are $\\ce{S}$, $\\ce{As}$, etc. In most cases, activity and/or selectivity cannot be recovered without a drastic change in operating conditions (most often a regeneration). recovery, if at all, takes place very slowly and/or only partially.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04707))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04707 "Poisson distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04707 "A discrete distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form: \\[f_{\\rm{w}}(x)=\\frac{x}{a\\,+\\,1}=\\frac{\\rm{e}^{-a}\\ a^{x-1}}{(x\\,-\\,1)!}\\] where \\(x\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\(a\\) is a positive adjustable parameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04709 "polar effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04709 "For a reactant molecule RY, the polar effect of the group R comprises all the processes whereby a substituent may modify the electrostatic forces operating at the reaction centre Y, relative to the standard \\(\\rm{R}^{\\rm{o}}\\rm{Y}\\). These forces may be governed by charge separations arising from differences in the electronegativity of atoms (leading to the presence of dipoles), the presence of unipoles, or electron delocalization. It is synonymous with electronic effect or 'electrical effect' of a substituent as distinguished from other substituent effects, e.g. steric effects. Sometimes, however, the term 'polar effect' is taken to refer to the influence, other than steric, that non-conjugated substituents exert on reaction rates, i.e. effects connected with electron delocalization between a substituent and the molecular framework to which it is attached are excluded. Polar effect is then not synonymous with electronic effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04710 "polarity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04710 "When applied to solvents, this rather ill-defined term covers their overall solvation capability (solvation power) for solutes (i.e. in chemical equilibria: reactants and products; in reaction rates: reactants and activated complex; in light absorptions: ions or molecules in the ground and excited state), which in turn depends on the action of all possible, nonspecific and specific, intermolecular interactions between solute ions or molecules and solvent molecules, excluding such interactions leading to definite chemical alterations of the ions or molecules of the solute. Occasionally, the term solvent polarity is restricted to nonspecific solute/solvent interactions only (i.e. to van der Waals forces).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04710 "polar solvent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04711 "polarizability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04711 "The ease of distortion of the electron cloud of a molecular entity by an electric field (such as that due to the proximity of a charged reagent). It is experimentally measured as the ratio of induced dipole moment (\\(\\mu _{\\mathrm{ind}}\\)) to the field \\(E\\) which induces it: \\[\\alpha =\\frac{\\mu _{\\rm{ind}}}{E}\\] The units of \\(\\alpha \\) are \\(\\rm{C}^{2}\\ \\rm{m}^{2}\\ \\rm{V}^{-1}\\). In ordinary usage the term refers to the 'mean polarizability', i.e., the average over three rectilinear axes of the molecule. Polarizabilities in different directions (e.g. along the bond in $\\ce{Cl2}$, called 'longitudinal polarizability', and in the direction perpendicular to the bond, called 'transverse polarizability') can be distinguished, at least in principle. Polarizability along the bond joining a substituent to the rest of the molecule is seen in certain modern theoretical approaches as a factor influencing chemical reactivity, etc., and parametrization thereof has been proposed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04712 "polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04712 "The relevant material property that couples with the radiation field. May be called optical or dielectric polarization. Optical spectroscopies may be classified according to the dielectric polarization power-law dependence on the external electric field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04713 "polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04713 "The difference of the electrode potential from the corrosion potential. The symbol should not be used for polarization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04714))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04714 "polarization error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04714 "An error which arises from the fact that the absorbance of a sample (especially a solid) could depend on the polarization of the incident radiation and from the polarization dependent response of the photodetector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04715 "polarized interphases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04715 "Ideally, interphases for which there are no common components between the phases or the exchange of these is hindered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04716 "polarography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04716 "A measure of current as a function of potential when the working electrode is a dropping mercury (or other liquid conductor) electrode and unstirred solutions are used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04720))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04720 "polyaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04720 "A polymerization in which the growth of polymer chains proceeds by addition reactions between molecules of all degrees of polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04721))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04721 "polychromator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04721 "An extension of a monochromator, a number of exit slits being placed in the focal plane, so allowing a number of discrete bands to pass through (to fall, for example, upon a number of photomultipliers).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04722 "polycondensation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04722 "A polymerization in which the growth of polymer chains proceeds by condensation reactions between molecules of all degrees of polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04723 "polycrystalline graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04723 "A graphite material with coherent crystallographic domains of limited size regardless of the perfection and preferred orientation (texture) of their crystalline structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04724))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04724 "polycyclic system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04724 "A molecular system regarded as containing a number of rings equal to the number of scissions required to convert it into an open-chain compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04726 "polydisperse medium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04726 "A colloidal system in which many particle sizes occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04728 "polyelectrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04728 "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which a substantial portion of the constitutional units contains ionic or ionizable groups, or both.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04728 "polymer electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04728 "polymeric electrolyte")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04730 "polygranular carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04730 "A carbon material composed of grains, which can be clearly distinguished by means of optical microscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04731 "polygranular graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04731 "A graphite material composed of grains which can be clearly distinguished by means of optical microscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04732 "polyhedranes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04732 "Polycyclic hydrocarbons of the $\\ce{(CH)_{n}}$ series having skeletons corresponding to the regular and semiregular geometrical solids, e.g. cubane,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04733))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04733 "polyions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04733 "Polycations or polyanions — multiply charged ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04734))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04734 "polyketides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04734 "Natural compounds containing alternating carbonyl and methylene groups ('$\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}polyketones}$'), biogenetically derived from repeated condensation of acetyl coenzyme A (via malonyl coenzyme A), and usually the compounds derived from them by further condensations. Considered by many to be synonymous with the less frequently used terms acetogenins and ketides.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04734 "acetogenins")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04734 "ketides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04735))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04735 "polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04735 "A substance composed of macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04736 "polymer blend")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04736 "Macroscopically homogeneous mixture of two or more different species of polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04737))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04737 "polymer crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04737 "A crystalline domain usually limited by well-defined boundaries.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04738))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04738 "polymer crystallite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04738 "A small crystalline domain. A crystallite may have irregular boundaries and parts of its constituent macromolecules may extend beyond its boundaries. This definition is not identical with that used in classical crystallography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04740 "polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04740 "The process of converting a monomer mixture of monomers into a polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04743))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04743 "polymer–polymer complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04743 "Complex, at least two components of which are different polymers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04744 "polymer-poor phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04744 "That phase of a two-phase equilibrium system, consisting of a polymer and low molecular weight material, in which the polymer concentration is lower. The use of the name 'sol phase' is discouraged. Synonymous with dilute phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04744 "dilute phase")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04745 "polymer-rich phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04745 "That phase of a two-phase equilibrium system, consisting of a polymer and low molecular weight material, in which the polymer concentration is higher. The use of the name 'gel phase' is discouraged. Synonymous with concentrated phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04745 "concentrated phase")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04746 "polymer–solvent interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04746 "The sum of the effects of all intermolecular interactions between polymer and solvent molecules in solution that are reflected in the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04747 "polymolecularity correction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04747 "A correction applied to relationships between a property and the molar mass or relative molecular mass, obtained from polymers non-uniform with respect to relative molecular mass, in order to obtain the corresponding relationship for polymers strictly uniform with respect to relative molecular mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04748 "polymorphic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04748 "A reversible transition of a solid crystalline phase at a certain temperature and pressure (the inversion point) to another phase of the same chemical composition with a different crystal structure.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04748 "enantiotropic transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04749 "polypeptides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04749 "Peptides containing ten or more amino acid residues.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04750))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04750 "polyprenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04750 "A subgroup of prenols in which \\(n\\) in the general formula $\\ce{H–[CH2–C(CH3)=CH–CH2]_{n}–OH}$ is greater than four.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04751 "polyquinanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04751 "Saturated or unsaturated, respectively, polycyclic hydrocarbons consisting of fused five-membered rings, commonly but not necessarily incorporating the skeleton of quinacene (tricyclo[5.2.1.04,10]deca-2,5,8-triene): E.g.  bicyclo[3.3.0]octane, dodecahedrane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04752 "polysaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04752 "Compounds consisting of a large number of monosaccharides linked glycosidically. This term is commonly used only for those containing more than ten monosaccharide residues. Also called glycans.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04753 "polysulfanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04753 "Compounds having an unbranched chain of sulfur atoms ($\\ce{s2}$ or higher) terminating in H: $\\ce{HS_{n}H}$. Some chemists exclude disulfane, $\\ce{HS2H}$, from the class polysulfanes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04754))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04754 "polysulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04754 """Compounds $\\ce{R–[S]_{n}–R}$, with a chain of sulfur atoms (\\(n\\geq 2\\)) and $\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$. Some chemists exclude disulfides, $\\ce{RS2R}$, from the class polysulfides.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04755 "polytopal rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04755 "Stereoisomerization interconverting different or equivalent spatial arrangements of ligands about a central atom or of a cage of atoms, where the ligand or cage defines the vertices of a polyhedron. For example pyramidal inversion of amines, Berry pseudorotation of $\\ce{PF5}$, rearrangements of polyhedral boranes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04756 "polytypic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04756 "A transition of a crystalline structure into one or more forms which differ in the way identical layers of atoms are stacked. Example: $\\ce{ZnS}$ consists of two identical close packings, one of $\\ce{Zn}$ atoms, the other $\\ce{S}$ atoms, with the one displaced to the other along the c-axis through one-quarter of the layer spacing. In sphalerite-type $\\ce{ZnS}$ the layers have the face-centred-cubic (ABC ABC) sequence, in wurtzite-type $\\ce{ZnS}$ they have the hexagonal-close-packed (AB AB) sequence. The transition of sphalerite-type $\\ce{ZnS}$ to wurtzite-type $\\ce{ZnS}$ occurs at \\(1297\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04758 "pooled standard deviation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04758 "A problem often arises when the combination of several series of measurements performed under similar conditions is desired to achieve an improved estimate of the imprecision of the process. If it can be assumed that all the series are of the same precision although their means may differ, the pooled standard deviations \\(s_{\\rm{p}}\\) from \\(k\\) series of measurements can be calculated as \\[s_{\\rm{p}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{(n_{1}- 1)\\ s_{1}^{2}+(n_{2}- 1)\\ s_{2}^{2}+...+(n_{k}- 1)\\ s_{k}^{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}+...+n_{k}- k}}\\] The suffices \\(1,2,...,k\\) refer to the different series of measurements. In this case it is assumed that there exists a single underlying standard deviation \\(\\sigma \\) of which the pooled standard deviation \\(s_{\\rm{p}}\\) is a better estimate than the individual calculated standard deviations \\(s_{1},s_{2},...,s_{k}\\), For the special case where \\(k\\) sets of duplicate measurements are available, the above equation reduces to \\[s_{\\rm{p}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum (x_{i1}- x_{i2})^{2}}{2\\ k}}\\] Results from various series of measurements can be combined in the following way to give a pooled relative standard deviation \\(s_{\\rm{r,p}}\\): \\[s_{\\rm{r,p}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum (n_{i}-1)\\ s_{\\rm{r,}i}^{2}}{\\sum n_{i}- 1}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum (n_{i}-1)\\ s_{i}^{2}\\ x_{i}^{-2}}{\\sum n_{i}-1}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04759))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04759 "population inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04759 "A situation in which a higher energy state is more populated than a lower energy state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04760))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04760 "pore size distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04760 "The distribution of pore volume with respect to pore size; alternatively, it may be defined by the related distribution of pore area with respect to pore size.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04762 "porosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04762 "A concept related to texture, referring to the pore space in a material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04763 "porous-layer open-tabular column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04763 "A column in which there is a porous layer on the inner wall. porosity can be achieved by either chemical means (e.g. etching) or by the deposition of porous particles on the wall from a suspension. The porous layer may serve as a support for a liquid stationary phase or as the stationary phase itself.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04763 "PLOT column")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04764))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04764 "porphyrinogens")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04764 "Hexahydroporphyrins in which the nitrogen atoms and four meso positions are saturated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04765 "porphyrins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04765 "Natural pigments containing a fundamental skeleton of four pyrrole nuclei united through the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox{-}positions}$ by four methine groups to form a macrocyclic structure (porphyrin is designated porphine in Chemical Abstracts indexes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04766 "position-sensitive photomultiplier tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04766 "In position-sensitive photomultiplier tubes spatial resolution is obtained with the help of a partitioned photocathode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04768))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04768 "positive ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04768 "This is an atom, radical, molecule or molecular moiety which has lost one of more electrons thereby attaining an electrically positive charge. The use of the term cation as an alternative is not recommended. The use of mass ion is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04769 "positron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04769 "A positively charged electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04770 "positronium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04770 "Atom-like pair of particles consisting of an anti-electron (positron) and an electron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04771 "post-column derivatization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04771 "A version of reaction chromatography in which the separated components eluting from the column are derivatized prior to entering the detector. The derivatization process is generally carried out 'on-the-fly', i.e. during transfer of the sample components from the column to the detector. Derivatization may also be carried out before the sample enters the column or the planar medium; this is pre-column (preliminary) derivatization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04772 "post-filter effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04772 "This effect arises when the exciting beam does not fill the cell completely and luminescence is absorbed by the analyte and interfering impurities in the non-illuminated region facing the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04773))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04773 "postprecipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04773 "The subsequent precipitation of a chemically different species upon the surface of an initial precipitate usually, but not necessarily, including a common ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04775 "potential at the point of zero charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04775 "The value of the electric potential of an electrode at which one of the charges defined is zero. The reference electrode against which this is measured should always be clearly stated. The potential difference with respect to the potential of zero charge is defined by \\[E_{\\rm{pzc}}=E- E_{\\sigma =0}\\] where the potential at the point of zero charge is that for the given electrode in the absence of specific adsorption (other than that of the solvent).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04776 "potential-determining ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04776 "Those species which by virtue of their electron distribution between the solid and liquid phase (or by their equilibrium with electrons in the solid) determine the difference in Galvani potential between these phases. This definition requires that adsorbed p.d. ions are part of the adsorbent and belong to the category of surface ions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04776 "p.d. ions")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04778 "potential energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04778 "Energy of position or orientation in a field of force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04779 "potential-energy profile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04779 "A curve describing the variation of the potential energy of the system of atoms that make up the reactants and products of a reaction as a function of one geometric coordinate, and corresponding to the 'energetically easiest passage' from reactants to products (i.e. along the line produced by joining the paths of steepest descent from the transition state to the reactants and to the products). For an elementary reaction the relevant geometric coordinate is the reaction coordinate ; for a stepwise reaction it is the succession of reaction coordinates for the successive individual reaction steps. (The reaction coordinate is sometimes approximated by a quasi-chemical index of reaction progress, such as 'degree of atom transfer' or bond order of some specified bond.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04780 "potential-energy surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04780 "A geometric hypersurface on which the potential energy of a set of reactants is plotted as a function of the coordinates representing the molecular geometries of the system. For simple systems two such coordinates (characterizing two variables that change during the progress from reactants to products) can be selected, and the potential energy plotted as a contour map. For simple elementary reactions, e.g. \\(\\rm{A}\\!-\\!\\rm{B}\\,+\\,\\rm{C}\\rightarrow \\rm{A}\\,+\\,\\rm{B}\\!-\\!\\rm{C}\\) , the surface can show the potential energy for all values of the A, B, C geometry, providing that the ABC angle is fixed. For more complicated reactions a different choice of two coordinates is sometimes preferred, e.g. the bond orders of two different bonds. Such a diagram is often arranged so that reactants are located at the bottom left corner and products at the top right. If the trace of the representative point characterizing the route from reactants to products follows two adjacent edges of the diagram, the changes represented by the two coordinates take place in distinct succession; if the trace leaves the edges and crosses the interior of the diagram, the two changes are concerted. In many qualitative applications it is convenient (although not strictly equivalent) for the third coordinate to represent the standard Gibbs energy rather than potential energy. Using bond orders is, however, an oversimplification, since these are not well-defined, even for the transition state. (Some reservations concerning the diagrammatic use of Gibbs energies are noted under Gibbs energy diagram). The energetically easiest route from reactants to products on the potential-energy contour map defines the potential-energy profile.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04780 "potential-energy reaction surface")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04787 "potential temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04787 "The temperature that a dry air parcel would have if lowered or raised adiabatically to a level of 1000 \\(\\rm{mbar}\\) pressure (or other arbitrary standard pressure).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04788))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04788 "potentiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04788 "Dependent action in which a substance or physical agent at a concentration or dose that does not itself have an adverse effect enhances the harm done by another substance or physical agent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04789))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04789 "potentiometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04789 "In electronics, a sensitive voltage measuring device based on a null technique which provides infinite impedance at null.In chemistry, potentiometric methods involve the measurement with a potentiometer of voltage generated in a cell. A high impedance digital voltmeter is often used today as a convenient alternative to a potentiometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04790 "potentiometric detection method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04790 "A detection method in which the potential of a cell is related to the concentration (activity) of a reactant which is a component of the cell fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04791))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04791 "potentiometric selectivity coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04791 "A coefficient which defines the ability of an ion-selective electrode to distinguish a particular ion from others. The selectivity coefficient, \\(K_{\\rm{A,B}}^{\\rm{pot}}\\), is evaluated by means of the emf response of the ion-selective electrode in mixed solutions of the primary ion, A, and interfering ion, B (fixed interference method) or less desirable, in separate solutions of A and B (separate solution method). The activities of the primary ion, A, and the interfering ion, B, at which \\(K_{\\rm{A,B}}^{\\rm{pot}}\\) is determined should always be specified, as the value of \\(K_{\\rm{A,B}}^{\\rm{pot}}\\) is defined by the modified Nikolsky–Eisenman equation. The smaller the value of \\(K_{\\rm{A,B}}^{\\rm{pot}}\\) the greater the electrode's preference for the principal ion, A. Comment: The terms selectivity constant and selectivity factor are frequently used instead of selectivity coefficient. However, in order to standardize the terminology associated with ion-selective electrodes, use of the term selectivity coefficient is recommended, as is the fixed interference method for its evaluation. This selectivity coefficient is not identical to the similar term used in separation science.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04792 "power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04792 "Rate of energy transfer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04793 "power level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04793 "Logarithm of the ratio of a power to a reference power. Levels are expressed in different ways: \\(L_{P}=\\frac{1}{2}\\ \\ln (\\frac{P}{P_{0}})\\ \\mathrm{Np}\\), where \\(\\mathrm{Np}\\) is the symbol for the unit neper coherent with the SI, or \\(L_{P}=10\\ \\log _{10}(\\frac{P}{P_{0}})\\ \\mathrm{dB}\\), where \\(\\mathrm{dB}\\) is the symbol for the unit decibel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04794 "pre-association")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04794 "A step on the reaction path of some stepwise reactions in which the molecular entity C is already present in an encounter pair or encounter complex with A during the formation of B from A, e.g.  In this mechanism the chemical species C may but does not necessarily assist the formation of B from A, which may itself be a bimolecular reaction with some other reagent. Pre-association is important when B is too short-lived to permit B and C to come together by diffusion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04795 "precipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04795 "The sedimentation of a solid material (a precipitate) from a liquid solution in which the material is present in amounts greater than its solubility in the liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04796 "precipitation fractionation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04796 "A process in which a macromolecules material, consisting of macromolecules differing in some characteristic affecting their solubility, is separated from solution into fractions by successively decreasing the solution power of the solvent, resulting in the repeated formation of a two-phase system in which the less soluble components concentrate in the polymer-rich phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04797))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04797 "precipitation from homogeneous solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04797 "The formation of a precipitate which is generated homogeneously and, generally, slowly by a precipitating agent within a solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04799))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04799 "precision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04799 "The closeness of agreement between independent test results obtained by applying the experimental procedure under stipulated conditions. The smaller the random part of the experimental errors which affect the results, the more precise the procedure. A measure of precision (or imprecision) is the standard deviation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04800 "precision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04800 "The standard deviation of the instrument for the stated load (e.g. \\(l_{20}\\) for a \\(20\\ \\rm{g}\\) load). A statement of the procedure, conditions, and experience of the observer should be included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04801))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04801 "precision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04801 "This depends upon the method of weighing and upon the precision of indication, \\(l\\), for the load in question.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04802 "precision of indication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04802 "The standard deviation of the instrumental indication, \\(i\\), for a stated load.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04803))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04803 "preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04803 "An operation (process) as a result of which the ratio of the concentration or the amount of microcomponents (trace constituents) and macrocomponents (matrix) increases. The term enrichment is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04804 "preconcentration coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04804 "This is defined as \\[K = \\frac{Q_{\\rm{T}}/Q_{\\rm{M}}}{Q_{\\rm{T}}^{\\rm{o}}/Q_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}}\\] where \\(Q_{\\rm{T}}\\) and \\(Q_{\\rm{T}}^{\\rm{o}}\\) are the quantities of the microcomponent in the concentrate and in the sample, respectively (mass units or concentration units), and \\(Q_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}\\) and \\(Q_{\\rm{M}}\\) and are the quantities of the matrix before and after preconcentration, respectively. If the recovery is 100%, \\(K=\\frac{Q_{\\rm{M}}^{\\rm{o}}}{Q_{\\rm{M}}}\\). The terms enrichment coefficient and enrichment factor are not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04805))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04805 "precursor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04805 "Of a nuclide, any radioactive nuclide which precedes that nuclide in a decay chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04806 "precursor complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04806 "May either indicate an encounter complex or a collision complex, but furthermore implies that this complex undergoes a reaction (e.g. electron transfer).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04807))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04807 "precursor ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04807 "Synonymous with parent ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04808))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04808 "predissociation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04808 "Dissociation occurring by tunnelling from a 'bound' to an 'unbound' vibronic state. In an absorption spectrum of a molecular entity, the appearance of a diffuse band region within a series of sharp bands is called predissociation, since irradiation with frequencies within the diffuse region leads to effective dissociation. The energy of the band is smaller than that of the dissociation continuum of the bound state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04809 "pre-equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04809 "Preliminary treatment of a solvent in order to convert the extractants into a suitable chemical form.Notes:The use of equilibration in this sense is confusing and should be avoided.The term conditioning may be used as a synonym for pre-equilibration.Preliminary treatment of either phase with a suitable solution of the other phase (in the absence of main extractable solute(s)) so that when the subsequent equilibration is carried out changes in the (volume) phase ratio or in the concentrations of other components are minimized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04810 "pre-equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04810 "A rapidly reversible step preceding the rate-limiting step in a stepwise reaction. For example:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04810 "prior equilibrium")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04811 "pre-exponential factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04811 "Coefficient in front of the exponential factor expressing the empirical temperature dependence of the rate coefficient, \\(k\\), on temperature, \\(T\\), \\(k=A\\ \\exp (- E_{\\rm{a}}/R\\ T)\\), where \\(E_{\\rm{a}}\\) is the activation energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04811 "Arrhenius A factor")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04811 "<i>A</i>-factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04812 "preferential sorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04812 "An equilibrium phenomenon, operative in polymer solutions in multicomponent solvents and in polymer networks swollen by multicomponent solvents, that produces differences in solvent composition in the polymer-containing region and in the pure solvent which is in thermodynamic equilibrium with that region. Synonymous with selective sorption.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04812 "selective sorption")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04813))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04813 "pre-filter effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04813 "This effect arises when the luminescence detector does not see a portion of the luminescent volume where the excitation beam enters the sample. Thus the exciting beam flux is reduced by absorption by the analyte and interfering impurities before it enters the volume observed by the detection system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04814))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04814 "premium coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04814 "An extremely well graphitizing carbon with a high degree of optical anisotropy (isochromatic areas of optical texture above about \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) and which is characterized by a combination of the following properties which differ significantly from those of regular coke: high real density, low reversible thermal expansion and low ash content combined, in most cases, with low sulfur content.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04815))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04815 "premix burner")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04815 "A burner in which fuel and oxidant are thoroughly mixed inside the burner housing before they leave the burner ports and enter the primary-combustion or inner zone of the flame. This type of burner usually produces an approximately laminar flame, and is commonly combined with a separate unit for nebulizing the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04816))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04816 "prenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04816 "Alcohols possessing the general formula $\\ce{H–[CH2C(Me)=CHCH2]_{n}OH}$ in which the carbon skeleton is composed of one or more isoprene units (biogenetic precursors of the isoprenoids).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04817 "pre-polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04817 "A polymer or oligomer composed of pre-polymer molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04818 "pre-polymer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04818 "A macromolecule or oligomer molecule capable of entering, through reactive groups, into further polymerization, thereby contributing more than one monomeric unit to at least one chain of the final macromolecule. A pre-polymer molecule capable of entering into further polymerization through reactive end-groups, often deliberately introduced, is known as a telechelic molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04819 "pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04819 "Normal force acting on a surface divided by the area of that surface. For a mixture of gases the contribution by each constituent is called the 'partial pressure' \\(p_{i} = x_{i} p\\), where \\(x_{i}\\) is the amount fraction of the \\(i\\)th constituent and \\(p\\) is the total pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04821))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04821 "pressure gradient correction factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04821 "A factor that corrects for the compressibility of the carrier gas. The values of the measured quantities obtained after multiplication by the factor \\(j\\) are independent of the pressure drop in the column. If \\(p_{\\rm{i}}\\), \\(p_{\\rm{o}}\\) are respectively the pressures of the carrier gas at the inlet and outlet of the column, then \\(j\\) is given by: \\[j=\\frac{3\\ [(\\frac{p_{\\rm{i}}}{p_{\\rm{o}}})^{2}- 1]}{2\\ [(\\frac{p_{\\rm{i}}}{p_{\\rm{o}}})^{3}- 1]}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04822 "pressure-induced transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04822 "A transition induced by a change of pressure. Example: The room-temperature transition of $\\ce{CdS}$ with a wurtzite-type structure, at \\(1.74\\ \\rm{GPa}\\), to $\\ce{CdS}$ with a $\\ce{NaCl}$-type structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04824 "pressure-sensitive detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04824 "A detector which employs the pressure change which results from the absorption of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04826 "pretreatment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04826 "Following the preparation of a catalyst or following its insertion into a catalytic reactor, it is often subjected to various treatments before the start of a catalytic run. The term pretreatment may, in general, be applied to this set of treatments. In some cases the word activation is used. It implies that the material is converted into a catalyst or into a very much more effective one by the pretreatment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04827 "primary crystallization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04827 "The first stage of crystallization, considered to be ended when most of the spherulite surfaces impinge on each other. In isothermal crystallization, primary crystallization is often described by the Avrami equation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04829 "primary electrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04829 "Electrons generated by a thermal or field emission source used to bombard the specimen for generation of the analytical signals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04831 "primary isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04831 "A kinetic isotope effect attributable to isotopic substitution of an atom to which a bond is made or broken in the rate-controlling step or in a pre-equilibrium step of a specified reaction. The corresponding isotope effect on the equilibrium constant of a reaction in which one or more bonds to isotopic atoms are broken, is called a 'primary equilibrium isotope effect'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04834 "primary mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04834 "A mixture obtained directly from two or more components intended for the preparation of more dilute calibration mixtures (called secondary or tertiary mixtures).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04836 "primary photochemical process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04836 "Any elementary chemical process undergone by an electronically excited molecular entity and yielding a primary photoproduct.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04836 "primary photoreaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04837 "primary pH standards")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04837 "Certain substances which meet the criteria of:   reproducible preparation in a highly pure state;   stability of solution over a reasonable period of time;   having a low value of the residual liquid junction potential;   having pH betwen 3 and 11.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04838 "primary pollutant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04838 "A pollutant emitted directly into the air from identifiable sources (e.g. $\\ce{SO2}$, $\\ce{NO}$, hydrocarbons, etc.). Secondary pollutants, such as ozone, are generated within the atmosphere through chemical changes which occur in primary pollutants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04840 "primary photoproduct")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04840 "The first observable chemical entity which is produced in the primary photochemical process and which is chemically different from the reactant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04841 "primary sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04841 "The collection of one or more increments or units initially taken from a population. The portions may be either combined (composited or bulked sample) or kept separate (gross sample). If combined and mixed to homogeneity, it is a blended bulk sample. The term 'bulk sample' is commonly used in the sampling literature as the sample formed by combining increments. The term is ambiguous since it could also mean a sample from a bulk lot and it does not indicate whether the increments or units are kept separate or combined. Such use should be discouraged because less ambiguous alternative terms (composite sample, aggregate sample) are available. 'Lot sample' and 'batch sample' have also been used for this concept, but they are self limiting terms. The use of 'primary' in this sense is not meant to imply the necessity for multistage sampling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04843))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04843 "primary structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04843 "In the context of macromolecules such as proteins, constitutional formula, usually abbreviated to a statement of the sequence and if appropriate cross-linking of chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04844 "primary structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04844 "The amino acid sequence of the polypeptide chain(s), without regard to spatial arrangement (apart from configuration at the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\mbox{-}carbon atom}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04845 "primitive change")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04845 "One of the conceptually simpler molecular changes into which an elementary reaction can be notionally dissected. Such changes include bond rupture, bond formation, internal rotation, change of bond length or bond angle, bond migration, redistribution of charge, etc. The concept of primitive changes is helpful in the detailed verbal description of elementary reactions, but a primitive change does not represent a process that is by itself necessarily observable as a component of an elementary reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04846))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04846 "principal group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04846 "The characteristic group chosen for citation at the end of a name by a suffix or a class name.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04847 "principal ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04847 "A molecular or fragment ion which is made up of the most abundant isotopes of each of its atomic constituents. In the case of compounds that have been artifically isotopically enriched in one or more positions such as $\\ce{CH3^{13}CH3}$ or $\\ce{CH2D2}$ the principal ion may be defined by treating the heavy isotopes as new atomic species. Thus, in the above two examples, the principal ions would be of masses 31 and 18, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04848))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04848 "principal moments of inertia")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04848 "Moments of inertia about the principal axes of the molecule chosen so as to make all the products of inertia equal to zero, \\(I_{A}\\leq I_{B}\\leq I_{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04849))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04849 "principle of least nuclear motion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04849 "The hypothesis that, for given reactants, the reactions involving the smallest change in nuclear positions will have the lowest energy of activation. (It is also often simply referred to as principle of least motion.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04850))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04850 "principle of microscopic reversibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04850 "In a reversible reaction, the mechanism in one direction is exactly the reverse of the mechanism in the other direction. This does not apply to reactions that begin with a photochemical excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04852 "prior distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04852 "A product state distribution calculated on the basis of some physical model for the reaction. The distribution usually referred to is that of the products at the instant of their formation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04855))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04855 "probability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04855 "Number of favourable events divided by the total number of possible events. It is a number between 0 and 1.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04856 "probability density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04856 "Product of the wave function and its complex conjugate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04857 "probe")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04857 "A specific DNA or RNA sequence which has been labelled by radioactivity, fluorescence labels or chemiluminescence labels and which is used to detect complementary sequences by hybridization techniques such as blotting or colony hybridization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04858 "process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04858 "A phenomenon by which change takes place in a system. In physiological systems, a process may be chemical, physical or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04859 "prochirality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04859 "This term is used in different, sometimes contradictory ways; four are listed below.The geometric property of an achiral object (or spatial arrangement of points or atoms) which is capable of becoming chiral in a single desymmetrization step. An achiral molecular entity, or a part of it considered on its own, is thus called prochiral if it can be made chiral by the replacement of an existing atom (or achiral group) by a different one. An achiral object which is capable of becoming chiral in two desymmetrization steps is sometimes described as proprochiral. For example the proprochiral $\\ce{CH3CO2H}$ becomes prochiral as $\\ce{CH2DCO2H}$ and chiral as $\\ce{CHDTCO2H}$.The term prochirality also applies to an achiral molecule or entity which contains a trigonal system and which can be made chiral by the addition to the trigonal system of a new atom or achiral group. For example addition of hydrogen to one of the enantiotopic faces of the prochiral ketone $\\ce{CH3CH2COCH3}$ gives one of the enantiomers of the chiral alcohol $\\ce{CH3CH2CHOHCH3}$; the addition of $\\ce{CN^{−}}$ to one of the diastereotopic faces of the chiral aldehyde shown below converts it into one of the diastereoisomers of the cyanohydrin. The two faces of the trigonal system may be described as Re and Si. The term prochiral also applies to a tetrahedral atom of an achiral or chiral molecule which is bonded to two stereoheterotopic groups. For example, the prochiral molecule $\\ce{CH3CH2OH}$ can be converted into the chiral molecule $\\ce{CH3CHDOH}$ by the isotopic replacement of one of the two enantiotopic hydrogen atoms of the methylene group. The carbon atom of the methylene group is called prochiral. The prochiral molecule $\\ce{HO2CCH2CHOHCH2CO2H}$ can be converted into a chiral product by esterification of one of the two enantiotopic $\\ce{–CH2CO2H}$ groups. The carbon atom of the $\\ce{CHOH}$ group is called prochiral. The chiral molecule $\\ce{CH3CHOHCH2CH3}$ can be converted into one of the diastereoisomers of $\\ce{CH3CHOHCHDCH3}$ by the isotopic replacement of one of the two diastereotopic hydrogen atoms of the methylene group. The carbon atom of the methylene group is called prochiral. The stereoheterotopic groups in these cases may be described as pro-R or pro-S. Reference to the two stereoheterotopic groups themselves as prochiral, although common, is strongly discouraged.See: chirality centreThe term prochirality is also applied to the enantiotopic faces of a trigonal system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04860))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04860 "prochirality centre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04860 "An atom of a molecule which becomes a chirality centre by replacing one of the two stereoheterotopic ligands attached to it by a different ligand, e.g. C-1 of ethanol; C-3 of butan-2-ol.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04861 "product")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04861 "A substance that is formed during a chemical reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04862))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04862 "product-determining step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04862 "The step of a stepwise reaction in which the product distribution is determined. The product-determining step may be identical to, or occur later than, the rate-controlling step on the reaction coordinate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04863))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04863 "product development control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04863 "The term is used for reactions under kinetic control where the selectivity parallels the relative (thermodynamic) stabilities of the products. Product development control is usually associated with a transition state occurring late on the reaction coordinate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04864))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04864 "product ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04864 "Synonymous with daughter ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04865 "productivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04865 "An economical figure denoting the mass of a product formed per unit reactor volume and unit time. Productivity is often referred to per unit of enzyme or biomass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04866))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04866 "product state distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04866 "The distribution of available energy among the vibrational, rotational and translation degrees of freedom in the product molecules. The distribution usually referred to is that of the products at the instant of their formation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04867))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04867 "<i>pro-E, pro-Z</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04867 "One of a pair of identical groups $\\ce{c}$ attached to a double bond (as in $\\ce{abC=Cc2}$) is described as pro-E if, when it is arbitrarily assigned CIP priority over the other group c, the stereodescriptor of the molecule becomes E. The other group $\\ce{c}$ is then described as pro-Z.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04868 "progenitor ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04868 "Synonymous with precursor ion  in mass spectrometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04869 "program")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04869 "Noun: A set of instructions enabling a device to perform an action. Verb: To provide a set of instructions enabling a device to perform an action.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04870 "programmed-flow chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04870 "A procedure in which the rate of flow of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or whole of the separation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04870 "flow programming")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04871 "programmed-pressure chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04871 "A procedure in which the inlet pressure of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or whole of the separation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04871 "pressure programming")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04872))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04872 "programmed-temperature chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04872 "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04872 "temperature programming")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04873))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04873 "projection formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04873 "A formal two-dimensional representation of a three-dimensional molecular structure obtained by projection of bonds (symbolized as lines) onto a plane with or without the designation of the positions of relevant atoms by their chemical symbols. A projection formula which indicates the spatial arrangement of bonds is called a stereochemical formula or stereoformula. Examples of stereoformulae are Fischer projection, Newman projection, sawhorse projection, wedge projection and zig-zag projection.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04874))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04874 "prolate trochoidal mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04874 "A mass spectrometer in which ions of different mass/charge are separated by means of crossed electric and magnetic fields in such a way that the selected ions follow a prolate trochoidal path. The usual term 'cycloidal' used sometimes is incorrect because the path used is not cycloidal. A cycloid is a special case of a trochoid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04875 "promoter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04875 "The DNA region, usually upsteam to the coding sequence of a gene or operon, which binds and directs RNA polymerase to the correct transcriptional start site and thus permits the initation of transcription.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04875 "promoter gene")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04876 "promoter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04876 "A relatively small quantity of one or more substances, which when added to a catalyst improves the activity, the selectivity, or the useful lifetime of the catalyst. In general, a promoter may either augment a desired reaction or suppress an undesired one.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04878 "prompt coincidence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04878 "The occurrence of two or more events separated by a time interval which is less than a specified small value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04879))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04879 "prompt neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04879 "Neutrons accompanying the fission process without measurable delay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04882))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04882 "propellanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04882 "Tricyclic saturated hydrocarbons, systematically named tricyclo[a.b.c.01,(a+2)]alkanes; have been referred to as [a.b.c]propellanes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04883))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04883 "property")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04883 "A set of data elements (system, component, kind-of-property) common to a set of particular properties, e.g. substance concentration of glucose in blood plasma. Information about identification, time and result is not considered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04884 "prophage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04884 "The latent state of a phage genome in a lysogenic bacterium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04885 "proportional counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04885 "A gas-filled X-ray detector in which the electric potential is high enough for the gain to reach a value in the range from \\(10^{2}\\) to \\(10^{5}\\). Each electron produced by the initial photo-ionization causes one avalanche. Since the number of avalanche events is proportional to the energy of the incident photons, the charge collected by the anode is proportional to the X-ray photon energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04886))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04886 "proportional counter tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04886 "A counter tube operated under such conditions that the magnitude of each pulse is proportional to the amount of energy deposited in it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04887))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04887 "proportional gas-scintillation counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04887 "A proportional counter coupled to an ultraviolet sensitive photomultiplier tube. Initial electrons produced by the interaction of the high-energy photon with the counter fill-gas are accelerated by a high electric field where they acquire sufficient energy to excite the noble gas atoms. The resulting UV radiation is observed by a photomultiplier tube.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04889 "<i>pro-R, pro-S</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04889 "A stereoheterotopic group $\\ce{c}$ (as in tetrahedral $\\ce{Xabc2}$) is described as pro-R if, when it is arbitrarily assigned CIP priority over the other stereoheterotopic group $\\ce{c}$, the configuration of the thus generated chiral centre is assigned the stereodescriptor R. The other group $\\ce{c}$ is then described as pro-S. This method for distinguishing between stereoheterotopic groups can be applied to other kinds of prochiral molecular entities or prochiral parts of molecular entities considered on their own.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04890))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04890 "<i>pros</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04890 "The nitrogen atoms of the imidazole ring of histidine are denoted by pros ('near', abbreviated $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$) and tele ('far', abbreviated $\\ce{\\uptau\\!}$) to show their position relative to the side chain. This recommendation arose from the fact that two different systems of numbering the atoms in the imidazole ring of histidine had both been used for a considerable time (biochemists generally numbering as 1 the nitrogen atom adjacent to the side chain, and organic chemists designating it as 3). The carbon atom between the two ring nitrogen atoms is numbered 2 (as in imidazole), and the carbon atom next to the $\\ce{\\uptau\\!}$ nitrogen is numbered 5. The carbon atoms of the aliphatic chain are designated $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$. This numbering should also be used for the decarboxylation product histamine and for substituted histidine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04891 "prostaglandins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04891 "Naturally occurring compounds derived from the parent $\\ce{C20}$ acid, prostanoic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04892 "prostanoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04892 "The family of natural prostaglandins and prostaglandin-like compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04893 "prosthetic group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04893 "The non-amino acid portion of a conjugated protein. Examples include cofactors such as flavines or cytochromes as well as lipids and polysaccharides, which are the prosthetic groups of lipoproteins and glycoproteins, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04894 "proteases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04894 "Enzymes that catalyse the hydrolysis of proteins. Usually several proteolytic enzymes are necessary for the complete breakdown of polypeptides to their amino acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04895 "protected lyophobic colloid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04895 "A colloidally stable mixture of a lyophobic and a lyophilic colloid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04896 "protective action")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04896 "Addition of small amounts of hydrophilic colloid to a hydrophobic sol may make the latter more sensitive to flocculation by electrolyte. Higher concentrations of the same hydrophilic colloid usually protect the hydrophobic sol from flocculation. This phenomenon is called protective action.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04897 "protein engineering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04897 "A technique used to produce proteins with altered or novel amino acid sequences. The methods used are: Transcription and translation systems from synthesized lengths of DNA or RNA with novel sequences.  Chemical modification of 'normal' proteins.   Solid-state polypeptide synthesis to form proteins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04898 "proteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04898 "Naturally occurring and synthetic polypeptides having molecular weights greater than about 10000 (the limit is not precise).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04899))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04899 "proteoglycan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04899 "A subclass of protein in which the carbohydrate units are polysaccharides that contain amino sugars. The protein is glycosylated by one or more (up to about 100) glycosaminoglycans [linear polymers of up to about 200 repeating disaccharide units that consist of a hexosamine (ᴅ-glucosamine or ᴅ-galactosamine) alternating with a uronic acid (ᴅ-glucuronic or ʟ-iduronic) or a neutral sugar (ᴅ-galactose)].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04903 "protium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04903 "A specific name for the atom $\\ce{^{1}H}$. The cation $\\ce{^{1}H^{+}}$ is a proton, the species $\\ce{1H}$ is a protide anion, and $\\ce{1H}$ is the protio group. The word proton should not be used for $\\ce{H^{+}}$ in its natural abundance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04904))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04904 "protogenic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04904 "Capable of acting as a proton donor (strongly or weakly acidic as a Brønsted acid). The term is preferred to the synonym 'protic' or the more ambiguous expression acidic by itself.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04904 "protogenic solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04904 "protic")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04905 "protolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04905 "This term has been used synonymously with proton (hydron)-transfer reaction. Because of its misleading similarity to hydrolysis, photolysis, etc., its use is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04906 "proton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04906 "Nuclear particle of charge number +1, spin quantum number \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and rest mass of \\(1.007\\ 276\\ 470(12)\\ \\rm{u}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04907 "proton affinity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04907 "The negative of the enthalpy change in the gas phase reaction (real or hypothetical) between a proton (more appropriately hydron) and the chemical species concerned, usually an electrically neutral species to give the conjugate acid of that species. Proton affinity is often, but unofficially, abbreviated as PA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04908 "protonated molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04908 "An ion formed by interaction of a molecule with a proton abstracted from an ion, as often occurs in chemical ionization according to the reaction: \\(\\rm{M}+\\rm{XH}^{+}\\rightarrow \\rm{MH}^{+}+\\rm{X}\\). The symbolism $\\ce{[M + H]^{+}}$ may also be used to represent the protonated molecule. The widely used term 'protonated molecular ion' to describe the $\\ce{MH^{+}}$ ion is not recommended, since it suggests an associated product of a proton with a molecular ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04909 "protonation constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04909 "The equilibrium constant \\(K_{\\rm{H}n}\\) for the addition of the \\(n\\)th proton to a charged or uncharged ligand. The cumulative protonation constant, \\(\\beta_{\\rm{H}n}\\), is the equilibrium constant for the formation of $\\ce{H_{n}L}$ from \\(n\\) $\\ce{H^{+}}$ and $\\ce{L}$. The same equilibrium constant may be described in several ways, depending on how the ligand, $\\ce{L}$, or the acid, has been defined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04911 "proton magnetic moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04911 "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\(\\mu _{\\rm{p}} = 1.410\\ 607\\ 61(47)\\times 10^{-26}\\ \\rm{J T}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04912))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04912 "proton magnetogyric ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04912 "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\(\\gamma _{\\rm{p}} = 2.675\\ 221\\ 28(81)\\times 10^{8}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{T}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04914 "proton rest mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04914 "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\(m_{\\rm{p}} = \\pu{1.6726231(10)E-27 kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04915 "proton transfer reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04915 "A chemical reaction, the main feature of which is the intermolecular or intramolecular transfer of a proton (hydron) from one binding site to another. For example:  In the detailed description of proton transfer reactions, especially of rapid proton transfers between electronegative atoms, it should always be specified whether the term is used to refer to the overall process (including the more-or-less encounter-controlled formation of a hydrogen bonded complex and the separation of the products; see microscopic diffusion control) or just to the proton transfer event (including solvent rearrangement) by itself.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04916))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04916 "protophilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04916 "Capable of acting as proton acceptor, strongly or weakly basic (as a Brønsted base). Also called HBA (hydrogen bond acceptor) solvent.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04916 "protophilic solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04916 "hydrogen bond acceptor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04917 "protoplast")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04917 "A spherical, osmotically-sensitive cell without its cell wall but retaining an intact cell membrane. Protoplasts are used to create hybrid cells via protoplast fusion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04918 "prototrophs")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04918 "Microoganisms capable of growing at or below a temperature optimum of \\(15\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04920))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04920 "pseudo acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04920 "Potentially acidic compounds that require some structural reorganization, such as keto–enol tautomerization, having a non-negligible activation energy, in order to show normal acidic properties, e.g. nitroalkanes:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04921))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04921 "pseudo-asymmetric carbon atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04921 "The traditional name for a tetrahedrally coordinated carbon atom bonded to four different entities, two and only two of which have the same constitution but opposite chirality sense. The r/s descriptors of pseudo-asymmetric carbon atoms are invariant on reflection in a mirror (i.e. r remains r, and s remains s), but are reversed by the exchange of any two entities (i.e. r becomes s, and s becomes r). An example is C-3 of ribaric (C-3 is r) or xylaric acid (C-3 is s) or hyoscyamine (C-3 is r). The hyphen in pseudo-asymmetric may be omitted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04923 "pseudo bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04923 "Hydroxy compounds that give salts with acids by formation of water accompanied by a change of constitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04924))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04924 "pseudo-catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04924 "If an acid or base is present in nearly constant concentration throughout a reaction in solution (owing to buffering or the use of a large excess), it may be found to increase the rate of that reaction and also to be consumed during the process. The acid or base is then not a catalyst and the phenomenon cannot be called catalysis according to the well-established meaning of these terms in chemical kinetics, although the mechanism of such a process is often intimately related to that of a catalysed reaction. It is recommended that the term pseudo-catalysis be used in these and analogous cases (not necessarily involving acids or bases). For example, if a Brønsted acid accelerates the hydrolysis of an ester to a carboxylic acid and an alcohol, this is properly called acid catalysis, whereas the acceleration, by the same acid, of hydrolysis of an amide should be described as pseudo-catalysis by the acid: the 'acid pseudo-catalyst' is consumed during the reaction through formation of an ammonium ion. The terms 'general acid pseudo-catalysis' and 'general base pseudo-catalysis' may be used as the analogues of general acid catalysis and general base catalysis. The term 'base-promoted', 'base-accelerated' or 'base-induced' is sometimes used for reactions that are pseudo-catalysed by bases. However, the term 'promotion' also has a different meaning in other chemical contexts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04925))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04925 "pseudo-co-oligomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04925 "An irregular oligomer, the molecules of which are derived from only one species of monomer but which display a variety of structural features more appropriate for description in co-oligomer terms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04926))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04926 "pseudo-copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04926 "An irregular polymer, the molecules of which are derived from only one species of monomer but which display a variety of structural features more appropriate for description in copolymer terms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04930 "pseudohalogens")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04930 "Compounds that resemble the halogen elements, $\\ce{X2}$, in their chemistry, e.g. $\\ce{(CN)2}$ cyanogen, $\\ce{(SCN)2}$ thiocyanogen, $\\ce{ICN}$ iodine cyanide. Certain ions that have sufficient resemblance to halide ions are sometimes referred to as pseudohalide ions, e.g. $\\ce{N3^{−}}$, $\\ce{SCN^{−}}$, $\\ce{CN^{−}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04932 "pseudopericyclic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04932 "A concerted transformation is pseudopericyclic if the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms at one (or more) of which nonbonding and bonding atomic orbitals interchange roles. A formal example is the enol \\(\\rightarrow \\) enol prototropy of pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone).  Because the \\(\\pi \\)- and \\(\\sigma \\)-atomic orbitals that interchange roles are orthogonal, such a reaction does not proceed through a fully conjugated transition state and is thus not a pericyclic reaction and therefore not governed by the rules that express orbital symmetry restrictions applicable to pericyclic reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04933 "pseudo rate constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04933 "A term sometimes used for a rate coefficient. For example, if the rate equation is: \\[v=k\\ \\rm{[A]}\\ \\rm{[B]}\\] the function \\(k\\ \\rm{[A]}\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant (or first-order rate coefficient) with respect to B. Similarly, \\(k\\ \\rm{[B]}\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant (or first-order rate coefficient) with respect to A. The term pseudo rate constant is particularly useful when the concentration of one reactant is much greater than that of another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04934 "pseudorotation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04934 "Stereoisomerization resulting in a structure that appears to have been produced by rotation of the entire initial molecule and is superposable on the initial one, unless different positions are distinguished by substitution, including isotopic substitution. One example of pseudorotation is a facile interconversion between the many envelope and twist conformers of a cyclopentane due to the out of plane motion of carbon atoms. Another example of pseudorotation (Berry pseudorotation) is a polytopal rearrangement that provides an intramolecular mechanism for the isomerization of trigonal bipyramidal compounds (e.g. \\(\\lambda ^{5}\\)-phosphanes), the five bonds to the central atom E being represented as \\(\\rm{e}^{1}\\), \\(\\rm{e}^{2}\\), \\(\\rm{e}^{3}\\), \\(\\rm{a}^{1}\\) and \\(\\rm{a}^{2}\\). Two equatorial bonds move apart and become apical bonds at the same time as the apical bonds move together to become equatorial.  A related conformational change of a trigonal bipyramidal structure is described as turnstile rotation. The process may be visualized as follows.  An apical and an equatorial bond rotate as a pair ca. 120° relative to the other three bonds. (Doubts have been expressed about the distinct physical reality of this mechanism.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04939))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04939 "psychrometric hygrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04939 "Instrument by which the relative humidity of the atmosphere may be determined. It is generally composed of two temperature sensors, one of which measures the temperature of the air; the other sensor is moistened with water and senses the cooling due to evaporation of water. The temperature difference between the two sensors is a function of relative humidity; sometimes referred to as a 'wet-and-dry bulb' thermometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04940))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04940 "psychrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04940 "The use of a wet-and-dry bulb thermometer for measurement of atmospheric humidity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04941))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04941 "puffing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04941 "An irreversible expansion of some carbon artifacts during graphitization heat treatment between \\(1650\\) and \\(2700\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04942))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04942 "puffing inhibitor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04942 "Metals or metal compounds with a high chemical affinity for the heteroatoms in the carbons. They are distributed as fine particles within the carbon materials to be graphitized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04945 "pulse amplitude analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04945 "A sub-assembly for determining the distribution function of a set of pulses in terms of their amplitude. Synonymous with pulse height analyser.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04945 "pulse height analyser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04946))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04946 "pulse amplitude selector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04946 "A circuit which gives an output pulse for each input pulse whose amplitude lies within a chosen interval. Synonymous with single channel pulse height analyser.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:P04946 "single channel pulse height analyser")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04947))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04947 "pulse duration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04947 "In pulse polarography, differential pulse polarography, Kalousek polarography, and related techniques, the duration of an interval during which the excitation signal deviates from the base line. This interval includes the sampling interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04951))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04951 "pulse reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04951 "In a pulse reactor, a carrier gas, which may be inert or possibly one of the reactants, flows over the catalyst and small amounts of the other reactant or reactants are injected into the carrier gas at intervals. A pulse reactor is useful for exploratory work but kinetic results apply to a transient rather than to the steady state conditions of the catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04952 "pump-probe technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04952 "Transient absorption spectroscopy using two pulses (strong pump and weak probe) and capable of achieving a high temporal resolution. A pump pulse excites the sample and triggers the process under investigation. A second delayed pulse, the probe, monitors an optical property. By varying the time delay between the pump and probe pulses, it is possible to assemble measurements as a function of time. The probe pulse is typically a UV, visible or infrared pulse in which case a snap-shot spectrum is taken as a function of the delay time. Often the probe pulse is generated from a portion of the excitation beam, but it can also be an independently generated electromagnetic pulse.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04953))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04953 "purine bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04953 "Purine and its substitution derivatives, especially naturally occurring examples. The customary numbering shown below is not systematic.purine")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04956 "pyramidal inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04956 "A polytopal rearrangement in which the change in bond directions to a three-coordinate central atom having a pyramidal arrangement of bonds (tripodal arrangement) causes the central atom (apex of the pyramid) to appear to move to an equivalent position on the other side of the base of the pyramid. If the three ligands to the central atom are different pyramidal inversion interconverts enantiomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04957))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04957 "pyranoses")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04957 "Cyclic hemiacetal forms of monosaccharides in which the ring is six-membered (a tetrahydropyran).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04958))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04958 "pyrimidine bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04958 "Pyrimidine and its substitution derivatives, especially naturally occurring examples.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04959 "pyro")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04959 "A prefix designating compounds formed by heating a compound, usually with the elimination of water, carbon dioxide, or other simple molecule, e.g. pyroglutamic acid from glutamic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04960))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04960 "pyroelectric detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04960 "A detector (based on the temperature dependence of ferroelectricity in some crystals) which produces an electrical signal proportional to the energy flux on the collector surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04961 "pyrolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04961 "Thermolysis, usually associated with exposure to a high temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04962))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04962 "pyrolysis-gas chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04962 "A version of reaction chromatography in which a sample is thermally decomposed to simpler fragments before entering the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04963))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04963 "pyrolytic carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04963 "A carbon material deposited from gaseous hydrocarbon compounds on suitable underlying substrates (carbon materials, metals, ceramics) at temperatures ranging from \\(1000\\) to \\(2500\\ \\rm{K}\\) (chemical vapour deposition).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04964 "pyrolytic graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04964 "A graphite material with a high degree of preferred crystallographic orientation of the c-axes perpendicular to the surface of the substrate, obtained by graphitization heat treatment of pyrolytic carbon or by chemical vapour deposition at temperatures above \\(2500\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:P04965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:P04965 "pyrromethenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:P04965 "A less preferred term for dipyrrins. (The term 'pyrromethane' has been used for the system with a $\\ce{–CH2\\bond{–}}$ linkage).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06782 "photosystem")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06782 "A membrane-bound complex in plants and photosynthetic bacteria, responsible for light-harvesting and primary electron transfer. Comprises light-harvesting pigments such as chlorophyll; a primary electron-transfer centre, and secondary electron carriers. In green plant photosynthesis, Photosystem I transfers electrons from plastocyanin to a [2Fe-2S] ferredoxin, and contains iron-sulfur proteins. Photosystem II transfers electrons from the oxygen-evolving complex to plastoquinone, and contains an iron centre.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06790 "phosphorylation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06790 "A process (catalysed by enzymes) involving the transfer of a phosphate group from a donor to a suitable acceptor; in general, an ester linkage is formed, for example: $\\ce{ATP + alcohol -> ADP + phosphate ester}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06791))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06791 "polymerase chain reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06791 "A laboratory technique to rapidly amplify pre-determined regions of double-stranded DNA. Generally involves the use of a heat-stable DNA polymerase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06792 "polyhedral symbol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06792 "The polyhedral symbol indicates the geometrical arrangements of the coordinating atoms about the central atom. It consists of one or more capital italic letters derived from common geometric terms (tetrahedron, square plane, octahedron, etc.) which denote the idealised geometry of the ligands around the coordination centre, and an arabic numeral that is the coordination number of the central atom. The polyhedral symbol is used as an affix, enclosed in parentheses, and separated from the name by a hyphen. Examples are T-4, SP-4, TBPY-5, SPY-5, OC-6, and CU-8.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06835 "pharmacokinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06835 "Process of the uptake of drugs by the body, the biotransformation they undergo, the distribution of the drugs and their metabolites in the tissues, and the elimination of the drugs and their metabolites from the body over a period of time.Study of such processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06836 "phase I reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06836 "Enzymic modification of a substance by oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, hydration, dehydrochlorination, or other reactions catalyzed by enzymes of the cytosol, of the endoplasmic reticulum (microsomal enzymes) or of other cell organelles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06837 "phase II reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06837 "Binding of a substance, or its metabolites from a phase I reaction, with endogenous molecules (conjugation), making more water-soluble derivatives that may be excreted in the urine or bile.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06838 "pinocytosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06838 "Type of endocytosis in which soluble materials are taken up by the cell and incorporated into vesicles for digestion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06839 "plasma")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06839 "Fluid component of blood in which the blood cells and platelets are suspended. Synonym: blood plasma.Fluid component of semen produced by the accessory glands, the seminal vesicles, the prostate, and the bulbo-urethral glands.Cell substance outside the nucleus, i.e., the cytoplasm.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06840 "proteome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06840 "Complete set of proteins encoded by the genome.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06879))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06879 "penetrant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06879 "Entity from a phase in contact with one of the membrane surfaces that passes though the membrane.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT06879 "permeant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06880 "permeate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06880 "Stream containing penetrants that leaves a membrane module.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06881))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06881 "perstraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06881 "Separation process in which membrane permeation and extraction phenomena occur by contacting the downstream with an extracting solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06892 "pervaporation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06892 "Membrane-based process in which the feed and retentate streams are both liquid phases while permeant emerges at the downstream face of the membrane as a vapour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06918 "polymeric stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06918 "Stationary phase (material) based on particles of a cross-linked organic polymeric material. Typical materials are polystyrene divinylbenzene copolymers (PS-DVB) and modified PS-DVB materials.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT06918 "polymeric stationary phase material")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06921))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06921 "perfusion stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06921 "Stationary phase in which the mobile phase primarily travels through the pores of the stationary phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT06921 "perfusion stationary phase material")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06933 "partition isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06933 "Isotherm describing partition of the sample component between the bulk of a liquid stationary phase and a liquid, gaseous or supercritical mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06975))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06975 "partial least squares")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06975 "Partial least squares projection to latent structures (PLS) is a robust multivariate generalized regression method using projections to summarize multitudes of potentially collinear variables.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT06976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT06976 "pattern recognition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT06976 "Pattern recognition is the identification of patterns in large data sets, using appropriate mathematical methodology. Examples are principal component analysis (PCA), SIMCA, partial least squares (PLS) and artificial neural networks (ANN).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07088 "Pariser–Parr–Pople method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07088 "A semi-empirical quantum mechanical method of calculation of the properties of conjugated molecules and ions from self-consistent-field theory and the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ approximation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07088 "PPP method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07089 "Pauli exclusion principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07089 "A rule, complementary to the aufbau principle, of building up the electronic configuration of atoms and molecules: a maximum of two electrons can occupy an orbital and then only providing that the spins of the electrons are paired, i.e. opposed. The principle demands that the wavefunction for a many-electron system must be asymmetric with respect to the permutation of the space-spin coordinates for every pair of electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07090))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07090 "perturbation theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07090 "Along with variational method, the second major quantum-mechanical approximation method. The methods of perturbation theory are based on representation of the Hamiltonian of a system under study, \\(H\\), through the Hamiltonian, \\(H^{0}\\), of a system, whose Schroedinger equation is solvable, and its relatively small perturbation \\(H^{\\rm{'}}\\): \\(H=H^{0}+H^{\\rm{'}}\\). Numerous techniques are derived allowing one to relate the unknown eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the perturbed system to the known eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the unperturbed system. As distinct from the variational method, the methods of perturbation theory are applicable to all the electronic states of an atom or molecule. When \\(H^{\\rm{'}}\\) is time-dependent, the perturbed system does not have stationary states. In this case time-dependent perturbation theory, which is the method of approximate calculation of the expansion of wave-functions of the perturbed system over wave-functions of stationary states of the unperturbed system, must be employed. The applications of this method are associated mostly with studies of light emission and absorption by atoms and molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07091 "pre-reactive complexes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07091 "Weakly bound complexes with a potential minimum that precedes the activation barrier along the reaction path. In contrast with the van der Waals complexes, which fall apart reversibly into their constituents, the pre-reactive complexes may undergo a vigorous reaction to form different products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07142 "polymer complexation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07142 "Process that results in the formation of a polymer–polymer complex or a complex composed of a polymer and a low-molar-mass substance.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07142 "polymer complex formation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07143 "polymer cyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07143 "Chemical reaction that leads to the formation of ring structures in or from polymer chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07144 "polymer degradation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07144 "Chemical changes in a polymeric material that usually result in undesirable changes in the in-use properties of the material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07145 "polymer functionalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07145 "Introduction of desired chemical groups into polymer molecules to create specific chemical, physical, biological, pharmacological, or other properties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07146 "polymer reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07146 "Chemical reaction in which at least one of the reactants is a high-molar-mass substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07147 "polymer-supported reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07147 "Chemical reaction in which at least one reactant or a catalyst is bound through chemical bonds or weaker interactions, such as hydrogen bonds or donor–acceptor interactions, to a polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07148 "protection of a reactive group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07148 "Temporary chemical transformation of a reactive group into a group that does not react under conditions where the non-protected group reacts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07158 "polymer catalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07158 "Polymer that exhibits catalytic activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07159 "polymer-metal complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07159 "Complex comprising a metal and one or more polymeric ligands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07160 "polymer phase-transfer catalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07160 "Polymer that acts as a phase-transfer catalyst and thereby causes a significant enhancement of the rate of a reaction between two reactants located in neighboring phases owing to its catalysis of the extraction of one of the reactants across the interface to the other phase where the reaction takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07161 "polymer-supported catalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07161 "Catalyst system comprising a polymer support in which catalytically active species are immobilized through chemical bonds or weaker interactions such as hydrogen bonds or donor–acceptor interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07162 "polymer reactant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07162 "Reactant (reagent) that is or is attached to a high-molar-mass linear polymer or a polymer network.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07162 "polymer reagent")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07162 "polymer-supported reagent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07163 "prepolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07163 "Polymer or oligomer the molecules of which are capable of entering, through reactive groups, into further polymerization and thereby contributing more than one structural unit to at least one type of chain of the final polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07180 "photo-elastic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07180 "Polymer that, under stress, exhibits birefringence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07181 "photoluminescent polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07181 "Polymer that exhibits luminescence (i.e., fluorescence or phosphorescence arising from photo-excitation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07182 "photosensitive polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07182 "Polymer that responds to ultraviolet or visible light by exhibiting a change in its physical properties or its chemical constitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07183 "piezoelectric polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07183 "Polymer that exhibits a change in dielectric properties on application of pressure.Polymer that shows a change in its dimensions when subjected to an electric field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07185 "polymer compatibilizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07185 "Polymeric additive that, when added to a blend of immiscible polymers, modifies their interfaces and stabilizes the blend.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07186 "polymer drug")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07186 "Polymer that contains either chemically-bound drug molecules or pharmacologically active moieties.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07187))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07187 "polymer gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07187 "Gel in which the network component is a polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07188 "polymer membrane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07188 "Thin layer of polymeric material that acts as a barrier permitting mass transport of selected species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07189 "polymer solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07189 "Polymer that acts like a solvent for compounds of low molar mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07190))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07190 "polymer sorbent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07190 "Polymer that adsorbs or absorbs a certain substance or certain substances from a liquid or a gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07191 "polymer support")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07191 "Polymer to or in which a reagent or catalyst is chemically bound, immobilized, dispersed, or associated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07192 "polymer surfactant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07192 "Polymer that lowers the surface tension of the medium in which it is dissolved, or the interfacial tension with another phase, or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07217 "polyacid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07217 "In polymer terminology, polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing acid groups on a substantial fraction of the constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07219 "polybase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07219 "In polymer terminology, polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing basic groups on a substantial fraction of the constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07220))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07220 "polybetaine")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07220 "Ampholytic polymer in which pendant groups have a betaine-type structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07222))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07222 "polyelectrolyte complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07222 "Neutral polymer-polymer complex composed of macromolecules carrying charges of opposite sign causing the macromolecules to be bound together by electrostatic interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07223 "polyelectrolyte network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07223 "Polymer network containing ionic or ionizable groups in a substantial fraction of its constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07242 "polymer composite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07242 "Composite in which at least one component is a polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07247 "prepreg")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07247 "Sheets of a substrate that have been impregnated with a curable polymer, curable polymers, or liquid reactants, or a thermoplastic, and are ready for fabrication of laminates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07264))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07264 "polymer alloy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07264 "Polymeric material, exhibiting macroscopically uniform physical properties throughout its whole volume, that comprises a compatible polymer blend, a miscible polymer blend or a multiphase copolymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07286))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07286 "phase domain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07286 "Region of a material that is uniform in chemical composition and physical state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07291 "phase interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07291 "Molecular interaction between the components present in the interphases of a multiphase mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07416 "polaron")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07416 "Lattice distortion or polarization caused by photoexcitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07418 "planar intramolecular charge transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07418 "In a PICT state formed by intramolecular charge transfer in an electron donor ($\\ce{D}$)/acceptor ($\\ce{A}$) molecule, the $\\ce{D}$ and $\\ce{A}$ subgroups have an overall planar configuration, with substantial electronic coupling of $\\ce{D}$ and $\\ce{A}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07440 "parametric amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07440 "Amplification of a signal based on parametric processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07441 "parametric processes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07441 "Interaction of photons in a non-linear medium satisfying the energy and momentum conservation laws. During the interaction the photon frequencies are mixed and photon(s) with different frequencies are created.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07441 "optical parametric processes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07442 "Peierls distortion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07442 "Distortion of a regular one-dimensional structure with a partially occupied band to give bond alternation, eventually leading to dimerization or oligomerization. The degree of oligomerization, \\(\\lambda \\) depends on the electronic population of the conduction band indicated by the wave vector of the Fermi level \\(k_{\\mathrm{F}}\\) \\[\\lambda = 2\\ \\pi /k_{\\mathrm{F}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07443 "phosphorescence lifetime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07443 "Parameter describing the time evolution of the decay of the phosphorescence radiant intensity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07444 "photo-Bergman cyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07444 "Intramolecular photoinduced cyclization of enediynes leading to aromatic compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07445))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07445 "photobiology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07445 "Branch of biology concerned with the biological and bioenvironmental effects of ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07446 "photocatalyst")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07446 "Catalyst able to produce, upon absorption of light, chemical transformations of the reaction partners. The excited state of the photocatalyst repeatedly interacts with the reaction partners forming reaction intermediates and regenerates itself after each cycle of such interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07447 "photochemical funnel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07447 "Molecular structure where the excited-state reactant or intermediate is delivered to the ground state to initiate product formation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07448))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07448 "photochemical reaction path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07448 "Sequence of geometries adopted by a molecule after initial electronic excitation, combined with a specification of the electronic state at each geometry, from radiation absorption to product formation. Theoretically, this usually involves the computation of the MERP connecting the Franck–Condon point, located on the potential-energy surface of the spectroscopic state, to the final photoproducts located on the ground-state potential-energy surface. Experimentally, a photochemical reaction path can be probed by detecting transient species or reaction intermediates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07449))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07449 "photo-Claisen rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07449 "Rearrangement closely related to the photo-Fries rearrangement; it is experienced by aryl ethers (instead of aryl esters) and follows an analogous mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07450 "photocyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07450 "Intramolecular photochemical process leading to carbo- or hetero- cyclic ring systems by formation of one new single bond, either by a concerted process (e.g., electrocyclization) or by multi-step processes such as Norrish–Yang reaction, photoinduced electron transfer processes, Yang photocyclization and others.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07451 "photocycloaddition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07451 "Intermolecular photochemical process leading to carbo- or hetero- cyclic ring systems by formation of two new single bonds, either by a concerted process (e.g., a concerted addition) or by multi-step processes (e.g., the Paternò–Büchi reaction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07452))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07452 "photochemical nitrogen extrusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07452 "Photoinduced dinitrogen ($\\ce{N2}$) elimination.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07453 "photodecarbonylation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07453 "Photoinduced extrusion of carbon monoxide ($\\ce{CO}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07454))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07454 "photodecarboxylation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07454 "Photoinduced extrusion of carbon dioxide ($\\ce{CO2}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07455 "photodeconjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07455 "Photoinduced reaction of conjugated compounds (e.g., enones and carboxylic acid derivatives) leading to loss of conjugation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07456 "photohydration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07456 "Photochemical process leading to the addition of water across a $\\ce{C–C}$ double (or triple) bond or to a carbonyl (or carbonyl analogue) group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07458 "photon echo")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07458 "Time-resolved optical spectroscopy in which the inhomogeneous broadening of absorbers is eliminated by the proper choice of geometry in a four-wave mixing experiment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07459))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07459 "photon number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07459 "Total number of photons calculated as \\(N_{\\rm{p}} = n_{\\rm{p}}\\, N_{\\rm{A}}\\) with \\(N_{\\rm{p}}\\) the amount of photons in einsteins and \\(N_{\\rm{A}}\\) the Avogadro constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07460))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07460 "photorearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07460 "Photoisomerization involving changes in the arrangement of the atoms within the molecular species. It may lead to unstable isomers that react further by, e.g., dehydrogenation, deprotonation, or other reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07461))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07461 "photoselection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07461 "Selection of an aligned subset of sample molecules by an exciting light beam. The selected subset will have an orientation distribution that is determined by the electric vector of the beam. If the beam is linearly polarized a uniaxial sample with a known orientation distribution will be produced. Also, the set of remaining molecules will be aligned with a known orientation distribution (residual anisotropy).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07463))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07463 "PLED")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07463 "Acronym for polymer light-emitting diode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07465))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07465 "pump-dump-probe technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07465 "Transient absorption spectroscopy using three pulses (a strong pump, a dump pulse that can alter the course of the reaction photoinduced by the first pulse, and a weak probe). In general, femtosecond laser pulses are used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07506 "pre-gel regime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07506 "Stage of a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process extending up to, but not beyond, the gel point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07507 "pre-gel state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07507 "State of a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process in the pre-gel regime.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07510 "particulate sol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07510 "Sol in which the dispersed phase consists of solid particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07511 "polymeric sol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07511 "Sol in which the dispersed phase consists of particles having a polymeric structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07523 "particulate gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07523 "Gel in which the network component comprises solid particles.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07524 "polyelectrolyte gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07524 "Polymer gel in which the polymer network contains ionic or ionizable groups in a significant fraction of its constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07570 "perfect network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07570 "Polymer network composed of chains all of which are connected at both of their ends to different junction points.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07583 "polymer-derived ceramic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07583 "Ceramic derived from a polymeric ceramic precursor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07594 "permanent crosslink")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07594 "Crosslink formed by covalent bonds, intermolecular or intramolecular interactions that are stable under the conditions of use of the material formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07605 "phantom chain behaviour")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07605 "Hypothetical behaviour in which chains can move freely through one another when a network is deformed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07627 "precipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07627 "Sedimentation of a solid material (a precipitate) from a liquid solution in which the material is present in amounts greater than its solubility in the liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:PT07642))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:PT07642 "peptization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:PT07642 "Reversal of coagulation or flocculation, i.e., the dispersion of agglomerates to form a colloidally stable suspension or emulsion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:PT07642 "deflocculation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q02567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q02567 "γ-quantum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q02567 "A photon of γ-radiation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04967 "Q-switched laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04967 "A laser in which the state of the device introducing important losses in the resonant cavity and preventing lasing operation is suddenly switched to a state where the device introduces very low losses. This increases rapidly the Quality factor of the cavity, allowing the build-up of a short and very intense laser pulse. Typical pulse durations are in the ns range. The Q-switching may be active (a rotating mirror or electro-optic device) or passive (a saturable absorber).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04968))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04968 "quadratic mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04968 "The square root of the expression in which the sum of squared observations is divided by the number \\(n\\). It can be calculated by the formula: \\[\\overline{x}_{\\rm{q}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum x_{i}^{2}}{n}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04969))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04969 "<i>quadro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04969 "An affix used in names to denote four atoms bound into a quadrangle (e.g. square).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04970 "quadrupole ion storage trap")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04970 "An arrangement in which ions with a desired range of quotients mass/charge are trapped by making them describe stable paths under the effect of a static and a high-frequency electric quadrupole field.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Q04970 "Quistor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04971))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04971 "quadrupole mass analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04971 "An arrangement in which ions with a desired quotient mass/charge are made to describe a stable path under the effect of a static and a high-frequency electric quadrupole field, and are then detected. Ions with a different mass/charge are separated from the detected ions because of their unstable paths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04972 "quadrupole splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04972 "The measured Doppler velocity difference between the two peaks seen in quadrupole split spectra from nuclides such as $\\ce{^{57}Fe}$ and $\\ce{^{119}Sn}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04973 "qualitative analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04973 "Analysis in which substances are identified or classified on the basis of their chemical or physical properties, such as chemical reactivity, solubility, molecular weight, melting point, radiative properties (emission, absorption), mass spectra, nuclear half-life, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04974 "qualitative elemental specificity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04974 "Ability of a method to detect one element in the presence of another element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04975))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04975 "quality assurance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04975 "The guarantee that the quality of a product (analytical data set, etc.) is actually what is claimed on the basis of the quality control applied in creating that product. Quality assurance is not synonymous with quality control. Quality assurance is meant to protect against failures of quality control.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04976 "quality control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04976 "The maintenance and statement of the quality of a product (data set, etc.) specifically that it meets or exceeds some minimum standard based on known, testable criteria.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04977 "quality factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04977 "The linear-energy-transfer dependent factor by which absorbed dose is multiplied to obtain dose equivalent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04978 "quality of solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04978 "A qualitative characterization of the polymer–solvent interaction. A solution of a polymer in a 'better' solvent is characterized by a higher value of the second virial coefficient than a solution of the same polymer in a 'poorer' solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04979 "quantized internal energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04979 "The quantized internal energy of a molecule in its electronic ground or excited state can be approximated with sufficient accuracy for analytical purposes by: \\[E_{\\rm{int}}=E_{\\rm{el}}+E_{\\rm{vib}}+E_{\\rm{rot}}\\] where \\(E_{\\mathrm{el}}\\) is the electronic, \\(E_{\\mathrm{vib}}\\) the vibrational and \\(E_{\\mathrm{rot}}\\) the rotational energy, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04980 "quantitative analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04980 "Analyses in which the amount or concentration of an analyte may be determined (estimated) and expressed as a numerical value in appropriate units. qualitative analysis may take place without quantitative analysis, but quantitative analysis requires the identification (qualification) of the analytes for which numerical estimates are given.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04981 "quantitative structure–activity relationships")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04981 "The building of structure–biological activity models by using regression analysis with physicochemical constants, indicator variables or theoretical calculations. The term has been extended by some authors to include chemical reactivity, i.e. activity is regarded as synonymous with reactivity. This extension is, however, discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04982 "quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04982 "Attribute of a phenomenon, body or substance that may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04983))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04983 "quantity calculus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04983 "Algebra with quantities where the symbols of quantities represent products of numerical values and their units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04984 "quantity of dimension one")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04984 "Quantity in the dimensional expression of which all the exponents of the dimensions of the base quantities are zero.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Q04984 "dimensionless quantity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04986 "quantum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04986 "An elementary particle of electromagnetic energy in the sense of the wave-particle duality.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04987 "quantum counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04987 "A medium emitting with a quantum yield independent of the excitation energy over a defined spectral range (e.g, concentrated rhodamine 6G solutions between \\(300\\) and \\(600\\ \\rm{nm}\\)). Also used for devices producing an electrical signal proportional to the photon flux absorbed in a medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04988 "quantum efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04988 "For a primary photochemical process, quantum efficiency is identical to quantum yield.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04991 "quantum yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04991 "The integral quantum yield is \\[\\mathit{\\Phi}(\\lambda) = \\frac{\\rm{number\\ of\\ events}}{\\rm{number\\ of\\ photons\\ absorbed}}\\] For a photochemical reaction, \\[\\mathit{\\Phi}(\\lambda) = \\frac{\\rm{amount\\ of\\ reactant\\ consumed\\ or\\ product\\ formed}}{\\rm{number\\ of\\ photons\\ absorbed}}\\] The differential quantum yield is \\[\\mathit{\\Phi}(\\lambda) = \\frac{{\\rm{d}}x/{\\rm{d}}t}{q_{n,{\\rm{p}}}^{0}[ 1 - 10^{-A(\\lambda)} ]}\\] where \\({\\rm{d}}x/{\\rm{d}}t\\) is the rate of change of a measurable quantity (spectral or any other property), and \\(q_{n\\rm{,p}}^{0}\\) the amount of photons (\\(\\pu{mol}\\) or its equivalent \\(\\rm{einstein}\\)) incident (prior to absorption) per time interval (photon flux, amount basis). \\(A(\\lambda)\\) is the absorbance at the excitation wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04992 "quarter-transition-time potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04992 "In chronopotentiometry (at constant current density), the potential of the indicator electrode at the instant when the time that has elapsed since the application of current is equal to one-fourth of the transition time. Appropriate correction for double-layer charging is needed in practice.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04993 "quartet state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04993 "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04994 "quartz–iodine lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04994 "Wolfram (tungsten) filament high-intensity incandescent lamp containing iodine in a quartz envelope. Used primarily as a source of visible radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04996 "quasi-classical trajectory method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04996 "A procedure for calculating trajectories in which the quantization of the reactants is taken into account, but in which the course of the reaction is treated classically.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Q04996 "QCT method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04997 "quasi-enantiomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04997 "Constitutionally different yet closely related chemical species MX and MY having the opposite chirality sense of the large common chiral moiety M. For example (R)-2-bromobutane is a quasi-enantiomer of (S)-2-chlorobutane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q04999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q04999 "quasi-equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q04999 "In conventional transition-state theory it is assumed that activated complex are formed in a state of equilibrium with the reactants. They are not in classical equilibrium with the reactants; if they were, addition of more activated complexes to the system would cause the equilibrium to shift in favour of the reactants. This would not occur for an activated complex, and the term quasi-equilibrium is used to denote this special type of equilibrium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05000))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05000 "quasi-molecular ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05000 "A protonated molecule or an ion formed from a molecular ion by loss of a hydrogen atom. The use of the term 'pseudo-molecular ion' is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05001))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05001 "quasi-racemic compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05001 "The crystalline product of a 1:1 association between quasi-enantiomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05002))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05002 "quasi-single-strand polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05002 "A regular linear polymer that can be described by a preferred constitutional repeating unit in which only one terminal constituent subunit is connected through a single atom to the other identical constitutional repeating units or to an end group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05003 "quaternary ammonium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05003 "Derivatives of ammonium compounds, $\\ce{(NH4^{+})Y^{−}}$, in which all four of the hydrogens bonded to nitrogen have been replaced with hydrocarbyl groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05004 "quaternary structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05004 "The defined organization of two or more macromolecules with tertiary structure such as a protein that are held together by hydrogen bonds and van der Waals and coulombic forces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05006 "quencher")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05006 "A molecular entity that deactivates (quenches) an excited state of another molecular entity, either by energy transfer, electron transfer, or by a chemical mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05007 "quenching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05007 "Deactivation of an excited molecular entity intermolecularly by an external environmental influence (such as a quencher) or intramolecularly by a substituent through a non-radiative process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05009 "quenching correction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05009 "Correction for errors due to different quenching for standards and test portions. When using liquid scintillation detectors these corrections can be based e.g. on the standard addition or sample channels ratio method or the use of automated external standardization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05010))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05010 "quinarenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05010 "Mancude assemblies of three carbocyclic rings, a six-membered quinonoid ring bonded at the 1,4-positions to odd-membered rings which differ in ring size by two, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05011 "quinhydrones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05011 "Molecular complexes of one equivalent amount of a quinone with one equivalent amount of the corresponding hydroquinone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05012 "quinomethanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05012 "Methylidenecyclohexadienones and dimethylidenecyclohexadienes, formally derived from quinones by replacement of one or both of the quinone oxygens by methylidene groups. (The diradical, triplet state of quinodimethanes can also be called o- or p-xylylenes).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05014 "quinone diazides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05014 "A potentially confusing term for diazooxides; the presence of an azido group, $\\ce{–N3}$, is falsely suggested.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05015 "quinones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05015 "Compounds having a fully conjugated cyclic dione structure, such as that of benzoquinones, derived from aromatic compounds by conversion of an even number of number of $\\ce{–CH=}$ groups into $\\ce{–C(=O)–}$ groups with any necessary rearrangement of double bonds (polycyclic and heterocyclic analogues are included).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05016 "quinonimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05016 "Imines derived from quinones by replacement of one or both oxygens by $\\ce{=NR}$. The term may include both types unless the infix mono or di is included, e.g. o-benzoquinone diimine:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Q05016 "quinone imines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q05017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q05017 "quinonoximes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q05017 "Mono- or di-oximes of quinones; quinone monooximes are tautomeric with nitrosophenols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Q06240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Q06240 "θ state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Q06240 "The state of a polymer solution for which the second virial coefficient is zero.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:QT06977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:QT06977 "quantitative structure–activity relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:QT06977 "Quantitative structure–activity relationships (QSAR) are mathematical relationships linking chemical structure and pharmacological activity in a quantitative manner for a series of compounds. Methods which can be used in QSAR include various regression and pattern recognition techniques.QSAR is often taken to be equivalent to chemometrics or multivariate statistical data analysis. It is sometimes used in a more limited sense as equivalent to Hansch analysis. QSAR is a subset of the more general term SPC.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:QT07469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:QT07469 "quantum mechanics/molecular mechanics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:QT07469 "Hybrid procedure for the treatment of large molecular systems. A crucial part of the system (e.g., the chromophore and its surroundings) is treated explicitly by quantum mechanical (QM) techniques, whereas the rest of the system is approximated by a classical or molecular mechanics (MM) force field treatment.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:QT07469 "QC/MM")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R00002))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R00002 "α-ray spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R00002 "A measuring assembly incorporating a radiation detector and a pulse amplitude, used for determining the energy spectrum of $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ ($\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!}$) radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R02568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R02568 "γ-radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R02568 "Electromagnetic radiation emitted in the process of nuclear transformation or particle annihilation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R02570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R02570 "γ-ray spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R02570 "A measuring assembly for determining the energy spectrum of $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{rays}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05022))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05022 "RM value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05022 "A logarithmic function of the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) value (retardation factor): \\[R_{\\rm{M}}=\\log _{10}(\\frac{1\\,-\\,R_{\\rm{F}}}{R_{\\rm{F}}})=\\log _{10}(\\frac{1}{R_{\\rm{F}}}\\,-\\,1)\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05023 "rabbit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05023 "A small container propelled pneumatically or hydraulically through a tube leading from the laboratory to a location in a nuclear reactor or other device where irradiation of a sample can take place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05025 "racemate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05025 "An equimolar mixture of a pair of enantiomers. It does not exhibit optical activity. The chemical name or formula of a racemate is distinguished from those of the enantiomers by the prefix (±)- or rac- (or racem-) or by the symbols RS and SR.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05026 "racemic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05026 "Pertaining to a racemate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05027 "racemic compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05027 "A crystalline racemate in which the two enantiomers are present in equal amounts in a well defined arrangement within the lattice of a homogeneous crystalline addition compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05028 "racemic conglomerate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05028 "An equimolar mechanical mixture of crystals each one of which contains only one of the two enantiomers present in a racemate. The process of its formation on crystallization of a racemate is called spontaneous resolution, since pure or nearly pure enantiomers can often be obtained from the conglomerate by sorting.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05028 "conglomerate")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05029 "racemic mixture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05029 "The term is confusing since it has been used as a synonym for both racemate and racemic conglomerate. Usage strongly discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05030 "racemization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05030 "The production of a racemate from a chiral starting material in which one enantiomer is present in excess.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05032 "rad")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05032 "Non-SI unit of absorbed dose of radiation, \\(\\rm{rad} = 0.01\\ \\rm{Gy}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05034 "radial electrostatic field analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05034 "An arrangement of two conducting sheets forming a capacitor and giving a radial electrostatic field which is used to deflect and focus ion beams of different energies. The capacitor may be cylindrical, spherical, or toroidal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05035 "radial elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05035 "A mode of operation in which the sample is spotted at a point source at or near the middle of the plane and is carried outward in a circle by the mobile phase, also applied at that place.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05035 "radial development")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05035 "circular elution")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05035 "circular development")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05036 "radian")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05036 "SI derived unit of plane angle, \\(\\rm{rad} = 1\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05037 "radiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05037 "Radiant power, \\(P\\), leaving or passing through a small transparent element of surface in a given direction from the source about the solid angle \\(\\varOmega\\), divided by the solid angle and by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given beam direction, \\(\\rm{d}S_{\\perp } = \\rm{d}S\\, \\rm{cos}\\,\\theta\\)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05039))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05039 "radiant energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05039 "The total energy emitted, transferred or received as radiation in a defined period of time (\\(Q=\\int Q_{\\lambda }  \\ \\mathrm{d}\\lambda \\)). It is the product of radiant power, \\(P\\), and time, \\(t\\): \\(Q=P\\ t\\) when the radiant power is constant over the time considered.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05040 "radiant energy density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05040 "Radiant energy divided by the volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05041 "radiant exitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05041 "Radiant power, \\(P\\), emitted at all wavelengths by an element of surface containing the source point under consideration divided by the area of the source. SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05041 "radiant emittance")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05041 "exitance")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05041 "spherical radiant exitance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05042 "radiant exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05042 "Radiant energy, \\(Q\\), incident from all upward directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. SI unit is \\(\\pu{J m-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05045 "radiant intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05045 "Radiant power, \\(P\\), at all wavelengths per solid angle, \\(\\varOmega\\). The @R05046-2@ emitted in a given direction by a source or an element of the source in a small cone containing the given direction divided by the solid angle of the cone. SI unit is \\(\\rm{W sr}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05046))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05046 "radiant power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05046 "Power emitted, transferred or received as radiation. SI unit is \\(\\pu{J s-1} = \\pu{W}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05047 "radiant quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05047 "Group of quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation in terms of energy. The symbols of radiant quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript e.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05048 "radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05048 "A term embracing electromagnetic waves as well as fast moving particles. In radioanalytical chemistry the term usually refers to radiation emitted during a nuclear process (radioactive decay, nuclear reaction, nuclear fission, accelerators).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05050 "radiation chemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05050 "The part of chemistry which deals with the chemical effects of ionizing radiation, as distinguished from photochemistry associated with visible and ultraviolet electromagnetic radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05051 "radiation constants")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05051 "Fundamental physical constants characterizing black body radiation. The first radiation constant is \\(c_{1} = 2\\ \\pi \\ h\\ c_{0}^{2}=3.741\\,7749(22)\\times 10^{-16}\\ \\rm{W m}^{2}\\), the second is \\(c_{2} = \\frac{h\\ c_{0}}{k}=1.438\\,769(12)\\times 10^{-2}\\ \\rm{m K}\\), where \\(h\\) is the Planck constant \\(c_{0}\\) the speed of light and \\(k\\) the Boltzmann constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05052))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05052 "radiation continuum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05052 "Continuous (in the wavelength, not time sense) radiation arising for example from non-quantized free-free transitions of electrons in the fields of the ions, free-bound transitions or radiative recombinations of electrons and ions, incandescent radiation emitted by hot solids (when the radiation distribution conforms to that described by Planck's law, it is considered black-body radiation) and unresolvable band spectra, i.e. where the spectral lines are wider than the spacings between them.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05053))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05053 "radiation counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05053 "Radiation measuring assembly comprising a radiation detector in which individual ionizing events cause electrical pulses and the associated equipment for processing and counting the pulses. Often an expression is added indicating the type of radiation detector (e.g. scintillation, semiconductor).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05054 "radiation detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05054 "A device in which incident radiation produces a measurable effect. If this effect is a rise in temperature it is called a thermal detector. If it is a rise in pressure it is called a photoacoustic detector. In the case where an electrical signal is produced it is called a photoelectric detector. Photoelectric detectors can be classified as photo-emissive detectors and semiconductor detectors. Where the radiation produces a chemical reaction, it is termed a photochemical detector. A detector yielding an output signal that is independent of the wavelength of the radiation over a specific region is called a nonselective detector. Where it is wavelength specific it is a selective detector. A detector having a quantum efficiency independent of the wavelength is a nonselective quantum counter. Certain detectors are able to distinguish between different quantum energies. This property is described by the energy resolution \\(\\Delta E\\) and the energy resolving power \\(\\frac{E}{\\Delta E}\\). These detectors are called energy dispersive detectors. In X-ray spectroscopy, the reciprocal \\(\\frac{\\Delta E}{E}\\) is often used but this is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05055))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05055 "radiation hazard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05055 "Hazard that exists in a region where there is a radiation field, other than what is considered to be normal background radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05056))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05056 "radiationless deactivation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05056 "Loss of electronic excitation energy without photon emission or chemical change. Often, ultrafast radiationless decays are mediated by conical intersections.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05056 "radiationless decay")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05057 "radiationless transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05057 "A transition between two states of a system without photon emission or absorption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05059 "radiation spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05059 "The components of radiation arranged in order of their wavelengths, frequencies or quantum energies. For particle radiation they are arranged in order of their kinetic energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05060 "radiative absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05060 "A process by which a particle in the ground state or an excited state may undergo transition to a higher energy level by absorption of a photon. For a given particle in the lower state of probability per second of such a transition in a field with a continuous spectrum is proportional to the spectral radiant energy density of the absorption line. The proportionality constant is termed the transition probability for absorption. The transition probability for stimulated emission is defined in a similar way for the reverse radiative de-excitation process that is induced by the same radiation field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05061 "radiative capture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05061 "Capture of a particle by a nucleus followed by immediate emission of gamma radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05062 "radiative de-excitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05062 "The change in the internal energy of a particle may be due to radiative processes, i.e. the emission or absorption of a photon. A particle in an excited state may undergo a transition to a lower energy level by emission of a photon. This is known as radiative de-excitation. If such a transition occurs spontaneously its probability per second for a given excited particle is termed the transition probability for spontaneous emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05063 "radiative energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05063 "Transfer of excitation energy by radiative deactivation of a donor molecular entity and reabsorption of the emitted radiation by an acceptor molecular entity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05063 "trivial energy transfer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05064 "radiative lifetime")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05064 "Lifetime of an excited molecular entity in the absence of radiationless transitions. The reciprocal of the first-order rate constant for the radiative step, or the reciprocal of the sum of these rate constants if there is more than one such step. The equivalent term, natural lifetime, is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05065 "radiative transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05065 "A transition between two states of a molecular entity, the energy difference being emitted or absorbed as photons. In principle, radiative and radiationless transitions can be distinguished in molecules. The first occur by absorption or emission of light quanta, and the latter is the result of the transformation of electronic excitation energy into vibrational/rotational energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05066 "radical")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05066 "A molecular entity such as $\\ce{^{.}CH3}$, $\\ce{^{.}SnH3}$, $\\ce{Cl^{.}}$ possessing an unpaired electron. (In these formulae the dot, symbolizing the unpaired electron, should be placed so as to indicate the atom of highest spin density, if this is possible.) Paramagnetic metal ions are not normally regarded as radicals. However, in the 'isolobal analogy' the similarity between certain paramagnetic metal ions and radicals becomes apparent. At least in the context of physical organic chemistry, it seems desirable to cease using the adjective 'free' in the general name of this type of chemical species and molecular entity, so that the term 'free radical' may in future be restricted to those radicals which do not form parts of radical pairs. Depending upon the core atom that possesses the unpaired electron, the radicals can be described as carbon-, oxygen-, nitrogen-, metal-centered radicals. If the unpaired electron occupies an orbital having considerable s or more or less pure p character, the respective radicals are termed $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}radicals}$. In the past, the term 'radical' was used to designate a substituent group bound to a molecular entity, as opposed to 'free radical', which nowadays is simply called radical. The bound entities may be called groups or substituents, but should no longer be called radicals.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05066 "free radical")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05069 "radical centre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05069 "The atom (or group of atoms) in a polyatomic radical on which an unpaired electron is largely localized. attachment of a monovalent atom to a radical centre gives a molecule for which it is possible to write a Lewis formula in which the normal stable valencies are assigned to all atoms.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05069 "radical centres")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05069 "radical center")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05069 "radical centers")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05071 "radical copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05071 "A copolymerization which is a radical polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05073 "radical ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05073 "A radical that carries an electric charge. A positively charged radical is called a 'radical cation' (e.g. the benzene radical cation $\\ce{C6H6^{.+}}$); a negatively charged radical is called a 'radical anion' (e.g. the benzene radical anion $\\ce{C6H^{.−}}$ or the benzophenone radical anion $\\ce{Ph2C–O^{.−}}$). Commonly, but not necessarily, the odd electron and the charge are associated with the same atom. Unless the positions of unpaired spin and charge can be associated with specific atoms, superscript dot and charge designations should be placed in the order $\\ce{^{.+}}$ or $\\ce{^{.-}}$ suggested by the name 'radical ion'. (e.g. $\\ce{C3H6^{.+}}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05074 "radical pair")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05074 "The term is used to identify two radicals in close proximity in solution, within a solvent cage. They may be formed simultaneously by some unimolecular process, e.g. peroxide decomposition, or they may have come together by diffusion. While the radicals are together, correlation of the unpaired electron spins of the two species cannot be ignored: this correlation is responsible for the CIDNP phenomenon. A radical pair is called geminate provided that each radical partner is a descendant of the same parental pair.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05074 "geminate pair")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05075 "radical polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05075 "A chain polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are radicals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05077 "radioactive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05077 "The property of a nuclide of undergoing spontaneous nuclear transformations with the emission of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05078 "radioactive age")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05078 "The time, estimated from measurement of the isotopic composition, during which the content of a radioactive species within an object has remained unchanged except for nuclear decay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05080 "radioactive contamination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05080 "A radioactive substance in a material or place where it is undesirable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05081 "radioactive cooling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05081 "Of a strongly radioactive material, the decrease of its activity by nuclear decay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05082 "radioactive dating")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05082 "The determination of the radioactive age of an object from its content of radioactive substances and of their daughter products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05083 "radioactive decay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05083 "Nuclear decay in which particles or electromagnetic radiation are emitted or the nucleus undergoes spontaneous fission or electron capture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05084 "radioactive equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05084 "Among the members of a decay chain, the state which prevails when the ratios between the activities of successive members remain constant. (This is not an equilibrium in the strict sense since radioactive decay is an irreversible process).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05085 "radioactive fallout")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05085 "The deposition on the ground of radioactive substances from nuclear explosions and other injections of radioactive material into the atmosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05088 "radioactive source")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05088 "Any quantity of radioactive material which is intended for use as a source of ionizing radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05089 "radioactive tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05089 "A tracer containing a radioactive label.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05090))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05090 "radioactive tracer technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05090 "A technique for investigating recovery and loss of a microcomponent, in which a radioactively-labelled element or compound chemically identical with the microcomponent is added to the sample before preconcentration and its behaviour is followed by sensitive, rapid and selective radioactivity measurements.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05091 "radioactive waste")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05091 "Unwanted radioactive materials obtained in the processing or handling of radioactive materials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05092 "radioactivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05092 "The property of certain nuclides showing radioactive decay.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05094 "radiochemical activation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05094 "A kind of activation analysis in which, after the irradiation, chemical or physical separation is applied.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05095 "radiochemical purification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05095 "Chemical separation applied to a radioactive preparation in order to improve the radiochemical purity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05096 "radiochemical purity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05096 "For a material, the fraction of the stated isotope present in the stated chemical form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05097 "radiochemical separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05097 "Separation by a chemical means of the radioactive isotopes of a specific element from a mixture of radionuclides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05098 "radiochemical yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05098 "The yield of a radiochemical separation expressed as a fraction of the activity originally present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05099))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05099 "radiochemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05099 "That part of chemistry which deals with radioactive materials. It includes the production of radionuclides and their compounds by processing irradiated materials or naturally occurring radioactive materials, the application of chemical techniques to nuclear studies, and the application of radioactivity to the investigation of chemical, biochemical or biomedical problems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05100 "radiochromatograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05100 "A measuring assembly designed to measure the spatial or time distribution of the activity of a mixture of radioactive components after separation by a chromatographic method.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05101 "radiocolloid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05101 "A colloid in which some atoms are radioactive.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05102 "radioenzymatic assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05102 "An assay of the catalytic activity of an enzyme based on the use of a radioactive substrate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05103 "radiograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05103 "A visual representation of an object produced by placing the object between a source of ionizing radiation and a photographic plate or film.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05104))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05104 "radiogravimetric analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05104 "A kind of quantitative analysis in which the activity of a precipitate is used as a measure of its mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05105))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05105 "radioimmunoassay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05105 "An assay based on the reversible and non-covalent binding of an antigen (hapten) by a specific antibody employing radioactivity labelled antigen (hapten) to measure the fraction of the antigen (hapten) bound to a substoichiometric amount of antibody.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05107 "radioiodination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05107 "The process of incorporating the radionuclides of iodine (usually $\\ce{^{125}I}$, $\\ce{^{131}I}$ or $\\ce{^{123}I}$) into, or of covalently linking a radioiodinated substance to, a substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05108))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05108 "radioisotope")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05108 "A radioactive isotope of a specified element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05109 "radioisotope dilution analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05109 "A kind of isotope dilution analysis making use of a radionuclide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05110))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05110 "radioisotope induced X-ray emission analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05110 "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with a radioactive source.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05111))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05111 "radioluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05111 "Luminescence arising from excitation by high-energy particles or radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05112))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05112 "radiolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05112 "The cleavage of one or several bonds resulting from exposure to high-energy radiation. The term is also often used loosely to specify the method of irradiation ('pulse radiolysis') used in any radiochemical reaction, not necessarily one involving bond cleavage.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05113))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05113 "radiometric analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05113 "A kind of quantitative analysis in which measurement of the activity is an essential step.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05114))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05114 "radiometric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05114 "A titration in which a radioactive indicator is used to monitor the end-point of the titration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05115 "radiometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05115 "The measurement of quantities associated with radiant energy. The quantities may also describe the variation of the energy with respect to other variables such as wavelength, time, position, direction (solid angle), area normal to the light or projected area of emitting or receiving surfaces. If the light is monochromatic, it is sometimes convenient to replace the radiant energy by the corresponding number of photons (or quanta) which is obtained by dividing the energy by \\(\\frac{h\\ c}{\\lambda }\\) where \\(h\\) is the Planck constant, \\(c\\) the velocity of light and \\(\\lambda \\) the wavelength of the light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05116 "radionuclide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05116 "A nuclide that is radioactive.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05117 "radionuclidic purity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05117 "For a material, that fraction of the total activity which is present in the form of the stated radionuclide, including daughter products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05118))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05118 "radioreceptor assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05118 "An assay employing a radioactively labelled receptor protein as a tracer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05119 "radiorelease analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05119 "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the release of radioactivity from the reagent by reaction with the analyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05120))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05120 "radiosonde")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05120 "A miniature radio transmitter with instruments in a package that is carried aloft (e.g. by an unmanned balloon) for broadcasting every few seconds by means of precise tone signals or other suitable method, the humidity, temperature, pressure or other parameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05121 "radius of gyration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05121 """A parameter characterizing the size of a particle of any shape. For a rigid particle consisting of mass elements of mass \\(m_{i}\\), each located at a distance \\(r_{i}\\) from the centre of mass, the radius of gyration, \\(s\\), is defined as the square root of the mass-average of \\(r_{i}^{2}\\) for all the mass elements, i.e. \\[s = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum _{i}m_{i}\\ r_{i}^{2}}{\\sum _{i}m_{i}}}\\] For a non-rigid particle, an average over all conformations is considered, i.e. \\[\\sqrt{ < s^{2} > } = \\frac{\\sqrt{< \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}m_{i}\\ r_{i}^{2} > }}{\\sqrt{\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}m_{i}}}\\] The subscript zero is used to indicate unperturbed dimensions, as in \\(\\rm{}_{0}^{1/2}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05122))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05122 "raffinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05122 "The phase remaining after extraction of some specified solute(s). When necessary it should be further specified, e.g. scrub raffinate. The original meaning of raffinate as a 'refined product' has become extended and changed by common usage. The term should normally be applied only to waste streams but the latter may form the feed to a further extraction process for another solute.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05123 "rain out")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05123 "The mechanism by which small particles in the clouds are removed by the formation of raindrops; this is a different mechanism from wash out which is a process which occurs below cloud level. Both the terms rain out and wash out have not always been used in accordance with these definitions. For clarity they should be replaced by the terms, in-cloud scavenging and below-cloud scavenging, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05124 "random coil")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05124 "The complete set of spatial arrangements of a chain molecule with a large number of segments that randomly change mutual orientation with time, under conditions in which it is free from external constraints that would affect its conformation. If the solution of the chain molecules is not in a theta state, the segments change mutual orientation only approximately randomly. Synonymous with statistical coil.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05124 "statistical coil")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05125))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05125 "random coincidence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05125 "A coincidence of events occurring in not physically connected nuclei.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05126))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05126 "random copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05126 "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules in which the probability of finding a given monomeric unit at any given site in the chain is independent of the nature of the adjacent units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05127))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05127 "random copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05127 "A copolymerization in which a random copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05128))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05128 "random error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05128 "Result of a measurement minus the mean that would result from an infinite number of measurements of the same measurand carried out under repeatability conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05129))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05129 "random sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05129 "The sample so selected that any portion of the population has an equal (or known) chance of being chosen. Haphazard or arbitrary choice of units is generally insufficient to guarantee randomness.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05130))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05130 "range")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05130 "The difference between the highest and lowest members of a series. This term is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05134 "range of measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05134 "The range of concentration between the measurement threshold and the maximum usable indication.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05136))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05136 "rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05136 "Derived quantity in which time is a denominator quantity. Rate of \\(x\\) is \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}x}{\\rm{d}t}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05139 "rate-controlling step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05139 """A rate-controlling (rate-determining or rate-limiting) step in a reaction occurring by a composite reaction sequence is an elementary reaction the rate constant for which exerts a strong effect — stronger than that of any other rate constant — on the overall rate. It is recommended that the expressions rate-controlling, rate-determining and rate-limiting be regarded as synonymous, but some special meanings sometimes given to the last two expressions are considered under a separate heading. A rate-controlling step can be formally defined on the basis of a control function (or control factor) CF, identified for an elementary reaction having a rate constant \\(k_{i}\\) by: \\[\\rm{CF}=\\frac{\\partial (\\ln 
u)}{\\partial \\ln k_{i}}\\] where \\(
u\\) is the overall rate of reaction. In performing the partial differentiation all equilibrium constants \\(K_{j}\\) and all rate constants except \\(k_{i}\\) are held constant. The elementary reaction having the largest control factor exerts the strongest influence on the rate \\(
u\\), and a step having a CF much larger than any other step may be said to be rate-controlling. A rate-controlling step defined in the way recommended here has the advantage that it is directly related to the interpretation of kinetic isotope effects. As formulated this implies that all rate constants are of the same dimensionality. Consider however the reaction of A and B to give an intermediate C, which then reacts further with D to give products:      (1)      (2)   Assuming that C reaches a steady state, then the observed rate is given by: \\[
u = \\frac{k_{1}\\,k_{2}\\,\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]}{k_{-1}+k_{2}\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]}\\] Considering \\(k_{2}\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]\\) a pseudo-first order rate constant, then \\(k_{2}\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]\\gg k_{-1}\\), and the observed rate \\(
u = k_{1}\\ \\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\) and \\(k_{\\rm{obs}}=k_{1}\\). Step (1) is said to be the rate-controlling step. If \\(k_{2}\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]\\ll k_{-1}\\), then the observed rate: \\[
u = \\frac{k_{1}\\ k_{2}}{k_{-1}}\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]=K\\ k_{2}\\left[\\rm{A}\\right]\\left[\\rm{B}\\right]\\left[\\rm{D}\\right]\\] where \\(K\\) is the equilibrium constant for the pre-equilibrium (1) and is equal to \\(\\frac{k_{1}}{k_{-1}}\\), and \\(k_{\\rm{obs}}=K\\ k_{2}\\). Step (2) is said to be the rate-controlling step.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05140 "rate-determining step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05140 "These terms are best regarded as synonymous with rate-controlling step. However, other meanings that have been given to them should be mentioned, as it is necessary to be aware of them in order to avoid confusion. Sometimes the term rate-determining is used as a special case of rate-controlling, being assigned only to an initial slow step which is followed by rapid steps. Such a step imposes an upper limit on the rate, and has also been called rate-limiting. In view of the considerable danger of confusion when special meanings are applied to rate-determining and rate-limiting, it is recommended that they be regarded as synonymous, with the meaning explained under the entry rate-controlling step.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05140 "rate-limiting step")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05141 "rate law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05141 "An expression for the rate of reaction of a particular reaction in terms of concentrations of chemical species and constant parameters (normally rate coefficients and partial orders of reaction) only. For examples of rate laws see equations (1)-(3) under kinetic equivalence, and (1) under steady state.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05141 "empirical differential rate equation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05142 "ratemeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05142 "An electronic sub-assembly which gives a continuous indication proportional to the average counting rate over a predetermined time interval (time constant).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05144 "rate of change of a quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05144 "The time derivative \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}Q}{\\rm{d}t}\\) of the value of the quantity. The differential quotient may also be called derivative (or instantaneous) rate of change. Examples are: rate of change of mass, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m}{\\rm{d}t}\\); rate of change of amount of substance, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n}{\\rm{d}t}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05145 "rate of change ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05145 "The quotient of two rates where the quantities are of the same kind in the same system for different components: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}Q_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}Q_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\] For finite time intervals, mean rate of change ratio is: \\[\\frac{\\Delta Q_{1}/\\Delta t}{\\Delta Q_{2}/\\Delta t}=\\frac{\\Delta Q_{1}}{\\Delta Q_{2}}\\ \\Delta t\\] Rate of change ratio has the dimension one. The denominator is often called the reference quantity. Examples are: mass rate ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}m_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\); amount of substance rate ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}n_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05146 "rate of consumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05146 """The rate of consumption of a specified reactant may be defined in two ways:As the negative of the time derivative of the amount of reactant; thus for a reactant B, present at any time in amount \\(n_{\\rm{B}}\\), the rate of its consumption may be defined as: \\[
u (n_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems. Here and elsewhere, when a rate is defined in terms of a time derivative, it must be understood that the definition relates to the process occurring in isolation. In a flow system there may be no actual changes with time, and the time derivative must be inferred. Such an inference is also required for a reaction occurring by a composite mechanism.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of consumption per unit volume; thus for a reactant B the rate of consumption, \\(
u (c_{\\rm{B}}) \\) is given by: \\[
u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume \\(V\\) is constant this reduces to: \\[
u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d[B]}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\(n_{\\rm{B}} = [\\rm{B}]V\\) may be differentiated to give: \\[{\\rm{d}}c_{\\rm{B}} = V\\rm{d[B]} + \\rm{[B]d}V\\] and therefore: \\[
u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d[B]}}{\\rm{d}t} - \\frac{\\rm{[B]}}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}V}{\\rm{d}t}\\] In contrast to the rate of conversion and the rate of reaction, the rate of consumption of a reactant may be specified even for a reaction of time-dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry. The rate of consumption of a reactant is often called its rate of disappearance. However, the former expression is to be preferred since the word disappearance is not appropriately translatable into certain languages. When English is used the word disappearance might be reserved for cases where the reactant is almost completely removed.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05147 "rate of conversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05147 """The rate of conversion for a reaction occurring in a closed system is defined as the time derivative of the extent of reaction: \\[\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\xi }=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi }{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] In view of the definition of extent of reaction it follows that with reference to any species in a reaction showing time-independent stoichiometry \\[\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\xi }=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi }{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{1}{
u _{i}}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}n_{i}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] where \\(n_{i}\\) is the amount of the species at any time and \\(
u _{i}\\) is its stoichiometric coefficient.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05151 "rate of fluid consumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05151 "The volume of fluid consumed by the nebulizer divided by time (in \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{L}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\)). Volume rate has also been called volume flow, flux, aspiration rate, aspiration flow or aspiration flux. The fluid consumed is either aspirated or injected. The volume rate of fluid consumption by nebulizers is usually between \\(10\\) and \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{L}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05152 "rate of formation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05152 """Like the rate of consumption, the rate of formation of a specified product may be defined in two ways:As the time derivative of the amount of a product. Thus for a product $\\ce{Y}$, present at any time in amount \\(n_{\\rm{Y}}\\). the rate of its formation may be given by: \\[
u (n_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of formation per unit volume, denoted \\(
u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) \\): \\[
u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is constant this reduces to: \\[
u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{\\rm{d[Y]}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\(n_{\\rm{Y}} = [\\rm{Y}]V\\) may be differentiated to give: \\[{\\rm{d}}n_{\\rm{Y}} = V\\rm{d[Y]} + \\rm{[Y]d}V\\] and the rate of formation becomes: \\[
u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{\\rm{d[Y]}}{\\rm{d}t} + \\frac{\\rm{[Y]}}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}V}{\\rm{d}t}\\] A rate of formation may be specified even for a reaction of time dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05153 "rate of liquid consumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05153 "The volume of liquid sample that is consumed by the nebulizing system per unit of time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05154 "rate of migration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05154 "The distance of migration divided by time, sometimes called velocity of migration. The symbol \\(v\\) is also used for velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05155))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05155 "rate of nucleation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05155 "The number of nuclei formed in unit time per unit volume of the liquid phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05156 "rate of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05156 """For the general chemical reaction: \\[\\ce{aA + bB -> pP + qQ + \\dots}\\] occurring under constant-volume conditions, without an appreciable build-up of reaction intermediates, the rate of reaction \\(
u\\) is defined as: \\[
u = -\\frac{1}{a}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = -\\frac{1}{b}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{B]}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{p}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{P]}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{q}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{Q]}}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] where symbols placed inside square brackets denote amount (or amount of substance) concentrations (conventionally expressed in units of \\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\)). The symbols \\(R\\) and \\(r\\) are also commonly used in place of \\(
u\\). It is recommended that the unit of time should always be the second. In such a case the rate of reaction differs from the rate of increase of concentration of a product P by a constant factor (the reciprocal of its coefficient in the stoichiometric equation, \\(p\\)) and from the rate of decrease of concentration of the reactant A by \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\). The quantity: \\[\\dot{\\xi} = \\frac{{\\rm{d}}\\xi}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] defined by the equation: \\[\\dot{\\xi} = -\\frac{1}{a} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{A}}}{{\\rm{d}}t} 
= -\\frac{1}{b} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{B}}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{p} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{P}}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{q} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{Q}}}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] (where \\(n_{\\ce{A}}\\) designates the amount of substance A, conventionally expressed in units of mole) may be called the 'rate of conversion' and is appropriate when the use of concentrations is inconvenient, e.g. under conditions of varying volume. In a system of constant volume, the rate of reaction is equal to the rate of conversion per unit volume throughout the reaction. For a stepwise reaction this definition of 'rate of reaction' (and 'extent of reaction', \\(\\xi \\)) will apply only if there is no accumulation of intermediate or formation of side products. It is therefore recommended that the term 'rate of reaction' be used only in cases where it is experimentally established that these conditions apply. More generally, it is recommended that, instead, the terms 'rate of disappearance' or 'rate of consumption' of A (i.e. \\(-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\), the rate of decrease of concentration of A) or 'rate of appearance' of P (i.e. \\(\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{P}]}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\), the rate of increase of concentration of product P) be used, depending on the concentration change of the particular chemical species that is actually observed. In some cases reference to the chemical flux observed may be more appropriate. The symbol \\(
u\\) (without lettered subscript) should be used only for rate of reaction; \\(
u\\) with a lettered subscript (e.g. \\(
u_{\\ce{A}}\\)) refers to a rate of appearance or rate of disappearance (e.g. of the chemical species A).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05157))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05157 "ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05157 "Quotient of quantities of the same kind for different components within the same system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05158 "raw coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05158 "The term raw coke is equivalent to green coke although it is now used less frequently.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05159 "Rayleigh ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05159 "The quantity used to characterize the scattered intensity at the scattering angle \\(\\theta\\), defined as \\(R(\\theta) = \\frac{i_{\\theta }\\,r^{2}}{I\\,f\\,V}\\), where \\(I\\) is the intensity of the incident radiation, \\(i_{\\theta}\\) is the total intensity of scattered radiation observed at an angle \\(\\theta\\) and a distance \\(r\\) from the point of scattering and \\(V\\) is the scattering volume. The factor \\(f\\) takes account of polarization phenomena. It depends on the type of radiation employed.   For light scattering, dependent on the polarization of the incident beam, \\(f=1\\) for vertically polarized light, \\(f = 1 - \\cos^{2}\\theta\\) for horizontally polarized light and \\(f = 1 + \\frac{\\cos^{2}\\theta}{2}\\) for unpolarized light.   For small-angle neutron scattering, \\(f=1\\).   For small-angle X-ray scattering, \\(f \\approx 1\\), if \\(\\theta < \\rm{ca.}\\ 5\\,°\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05160 "Rayleigh scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05160 "The scattering of light by particles and molecules which are much smaller than the wavelength of the light. In the ideal case, the process is one of pure dipole and induced dipole interactions with the electric field of the light wave. The scattering cross section for light of wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) is proportional to \\(\\lambda ^{-4}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05161 "rayon-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05161 "Carbon fibres made from rayon (cellulose) precursor fibres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05162 "reactance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05162 "Imaginary part of the impedance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05163 "reactant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05163 "A substance that is consumed in the course of a chemical reaction. It is sometimes known, especially in the older literature, as a 'reagent', but this term is better used in a more specialized sense as a test substance that is added to a system in order to bring about a reaction or to see whether a reaction occurs (e.g. an analytical reagent).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05163 "reagent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05164 "reacting bond rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05164 "For an internal motion of a molecular entity corresponding to progress over a transition state (energy maximum), any change that makes the motion more difficult will lead to a new molecular geometry at the energy maximum, in which the motion has proceeded further. Changes that make the motion less difficult will have the opposite effect. (This rule corresponds to the Hammond principle).For an internal motion of a molecular entity that corresponds to a vibration, any change that tends to modify the equilibrium point of the vibration in a particular direction will actually shift the equilibrium in that direction.Effects on reacting bonds (bonds made or broken in the reaction) are the most significant. The bonds nearest the site of structural change are those most strongly affected.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05166 "reaction barrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05166 "The energy barrier to chemical reaction. In vibrationally adiabatic transition-state theory its height is the zero-point energy of the activated complex minus that of the reactants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05167 "reaction chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05167 "A technique in which the identities of the sample components are intentionally changed between sample introduction and detection. The reaction can take place upstream of the column when the chemical identity of the individual components passing through the column differs from that of the original sample, or between the column and the detector when the original sample components are separated in the column but their identity is changed prior to entering the detection device.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05168 "reaction coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05168 "A geometric parameter that changes during the conversion of one (or more) reactant molecular entities into one (or more) product molecular entities and whose value can be taken for a measure of the progress of an elementary reaction (for example, a bond length or bond angle or a combination of bond lengths and/or bond angles; it is sometimes approximated by a non-geometric parameter, such as the bond order of some specified bond). In the formalism of 'transition-state theory', the reaction coordinate is that coordinate in a set of curvilinear coordinates obtained from the conventional ones for the reactants which, for each reaction step, leads smoothly from the configuration of the reactants through that of the transition state to the configuration of the products. The reaction coordinate is typically chosen to follow the path along the gradient (path of shallowest ascent/deepest descent) of potential energy from reactants to products. The term has also been used interchangeably with the term transition coordinate, applicable to the coordinate in the immediate vicinity of the potential energy maximum. Being more specific, the name transition coordinate is to be preferred in that context.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05169 "reaction cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05169 "A quantity used in collision theories of reactions in order to interpret calculated or experimental rates. In some collision theories the reaction cross-section is considered to be related to \\(b_{\\max }\\), the maximum value of the impact parameter that allows reaction to occur, by: \\[\\sigma _{\\rm{r}}=P_{\\rm{r}}\\ \\pi \\ b_{\\max }^{2}\\] where \\(P_{\\rm{r}}\\) is the reaction probability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05170 "reaction dynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05170 "A branch of chemical kinetics that is concerned with the intermolecular and intramolecular motions that occur in the elementary act of chemical change, and with the details of the relationships between the quantum states of the reactant molecules and those of the product molecules. It is also known as molecular dynamics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05174 "reaction path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05174 "A synonym for mechanism.A trajectory on the potential-energy surface.A sequence of synthetic steps.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05175 "reaction path degeneracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05175 "A factor that is introduced into rate theory to take account of the fact that the process may be able to occur in different but equivalent ways. Thus the process:  has a reaction path degeneracy of 2 since the chlorine atom can abstract either of the two hydrogen atoms in the hydrogen molecule. The reaction path degeneracy has also been referred to as the statistical factor for the reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05175 "statistical factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05176 "reaction probability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05176 "The probability that a reaction occurs when two particles undergo a collision. If \\(N_{\\rm{c}}\\) is the number of collisions occurring in unit time, and \\(N_{\\rm{r}}\\) the number of reactions occurring in unit time: \\(P_{\\rm{r}}=\\frac{N_{\\rm{r}}}{N_{\\rm{c}}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05177 "reaction stage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05177 "A set of one or more (possibly experimentally inseparable) reaction steps leading to and/or from a detectable or presumed reaction intermediate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05178 "reaction step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05178 "An elementary reaction, constituting one of the stages of a stepwise reaction in which a reaction intermediate (or, for the first step, the reactants) is converted into the next reaction intermediate (or, for the last step, the products) in the sequence of intermediates between reactants and products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05179 "reaction time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05179 "The period of time that elapses between the start of the reaction and the attainment of a given extent of a reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05180 "reactive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05180 "As applied to a chemical species, the term expresses a kinetic property. A species is said to be more reactive or to have a higher reactivity in some given context than some other (reference) species if it has a larger rate constant for a specified elementary reaction. The term has meaning only by reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed set of conditions. It is not to be used for reactions or reaction patterns of compounds in general. The term is also more loosely used as a phenomenological description not restricted to elementary reactions. When applied in this sense the property under consideration may reflect not only rate, but also equilibrium, constants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05181 "reactive adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05181 "Reactive adsorption (and its reverse, reactive desorption) resembles dissociative adsorption (and its reverse, dissociative adsorption) but one fragment adds to an adsorbate rather than to a surface site.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05182 "reactive complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05182 "A species of very short life that occurs as an intermediate in a chemical reaction. An activated complex (or transition state) is a special case.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05184 "reactive scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05184 "If in a molecular-beam experiment a chemical reaction occurs there is said to be reactive scattering.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05185 "reactivity index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05185 "Any numerical index derived from quantum mechanical model calculations that permits the prediction of relative reactivities of different molecular sites. Many indices are in use, based on a variety of theories and relating to various types of reaction. The more successful applications have been to the substitution reactions of conjugated systems where relative reactivities are determined largely by changes of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05186 "reactivity–selectivity principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05186 "This idea may be expressed loosely as: the more reactive a reagent is, the less selective it is. Consider two substrates \\(\\rm{S}^{1}\\) and \\(\\rm{S}^{2}\\) undergoing the same type of reaction with two reagents \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) and \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\), \\(\\rm{S}^{2}\\) being more reactive than \\(\\rm{S}^{1}\\), and \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) more reactive than \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) in the given type of reaction. The relative reactivities (in log units) for the four possible reactions may notionally be represented as shown in the diagram.  With the positions of (\\(\\rm{S}^{1}+\\rm{R}^{1}\\)), (\\(\\rm{S}^{2}+\\rm{R}^{1}\\)) and (\\(\\rm{S}^{1}+\\rm{R}^{2}\\)) fixed, there are three types of positions for (\\(\\rm{S}^{2}+\\rm{R}^{2}\\)). In position (i) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(a\\), is the same as the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, also \\(a\\). In position (ii) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(b\\), is less than the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, i.e. \\(b < a\\). It is this situation which is in accord with the RSP. In position (iii) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(c\\), is greater than the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, i.e. \\(c > a\\). This situation may be described as anti-RSP. There are many examples in which the RSP is followed, but there are also many examples corresponding to situations (i) and (iii). The RSP is in accord with intuitive feeling and certainly holds in the limiting case when reactivity is controlled by diffusion. However, the validity of the RSP is a matter of great controversy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05188))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05188 "readability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05188 "The smallest fraction of a division to which the index scale can be read with ease either by estimation or by the use of a vernier. It should normally be expressed in divisions of the pointer scale.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05189 "reading")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05189 "Datum provided by an instrument.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05192 "real potential of a species in a phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05192 "Defined for species $\\ce{B}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$ as \\[\\alpha _{\\rm{B}}^{\\beta } = \\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\beta }\\,-\\,z_{\\rm{B}}\\ F\\ \\psi ^{\\beta }\\] where \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\beta }\\) is the electrochemical potential of species $\\ce{B}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$, \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the charge number of species $\\ce{B}$, \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant, and \\(\\psi ^{\\beta }\\) is the outer electric potential of phase $\\upbeta\\!$. Since \\(\\psi ^{\\beta }\\) is zero when the charge on the phase $\\upbeta\\!$ is zero, the real potential may be regarded as the value of the electrochemical potential of the uncharged phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05195 "rearrangement ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05195 "An electrically charged species, involving a molecular or parent ion, in which atoms or groups of atoms have transferred from one portion of a molecule or molecular moiety to another during the ionization fragmentation processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05196 "rearrangement stage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05196 "The elementary reaction or reaction stage (of a molecular rearrangement) in which there is both making and breaking of bonds between atoms common to a reactant and a reaction product or a reaction intermediate. If the rearrangement stage consists of a single elementary reaction, this is a 'rearrangement step'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05197 "rebound reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05197 "If in a chemical process, occurring in a molecular beam, the reaction products are scattered backwards with respect to the moving centre of mass of the system, the reaction is called a rebound reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05199 "recognition site")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05199 "A nucleotide sequence to which a protein binds specifically.An amino acid sequence in an antibody molecule to which the specific antigen binds specifically.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05200 "recoil")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05200 "The motion acquired by a particle through a collision with, or the emission of, another particle or electromagnetic radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05201))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05201 "recoil-free fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05201 "The fraction of all Mössbauer gamma rays of the transition which are emitted or absorbed without significant recoil energy loss.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05202 "recoil labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05202 "Labelling by a chemical reaction initiated by recoil.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05205 "reconstructive transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05205 "A transition which involves a major reorganization of the crystal structure and a change of local topology, during which primary bonds are broken and reformed so that there is no immediate relationship between the crystal structures of the parent and product phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05206))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05206 "recovery")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05206 "In toxicology, the process leading to partial or complete restoration of a cell, tissue, organ or organism following its damage from exposure to a harmful substance or agent.Term used in analytical and preparative chemistry to denote the fraction of the total quantity of a substance recoverable following a chemical procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05208))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05208 "recrystallization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05208 "(chemical): The repeated dissolution and precipitation of a solid from a liquid solvent.(metallurgical): The process in which a crystalline solid with a high degree of disorder develops a new microstructure by the nucleation of relatively imperfection-free regions, and their propagation throughout the complete solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05209))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05209 "redox ion exchangers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05209 "Conventional ion exchangers in which reversible redox couples have been introduced as counter-ions either by sorption or complex formation. They closely resemble redox polymers in their behaviour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05210 "redox polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05210 "Polymer containing groups that can be reversibly reduced or oxidized.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05210 "electron-exchange polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05210 "oxidation-reduction polymer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05211 "red shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05211 "Informal term for bathochromic shift.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05212))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05212 "reduced adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05212 """Of component \\(i\\), defined by the equation \\[\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{(n)} = \\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} }- \\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}\\ \\frac{c_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} } - c_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}{c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }- c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}\\] where \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma }^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} }\\), \\(c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2}}\\) are, respectively, the total Gibbs surface concentration and the total concentrations in the bulk phases $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$: \\[\\mathit{\\Gamma}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} }=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}\\mathit{\\Gamma }_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} }\\] \\[c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}c_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\] \\[c^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}\r
i\r
\\end{array}}c_{i}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2}}\\] The reduced adsorption is invariant to the location of the Gibbs surface. Alternatively, the reduced adsorption may be regarded as the Gibbs surface concentration of \\(i\\) when the Gibbs surface is chosen so that \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma }^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} }\\) is zero, i.e. the Gibbs surface is chosen so that the reference system has not only the same volume, but also contains the same total amount of substance(\\(n\\)) as the real system.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05214 "reduced mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05214 "Effective mass in equations of motion for a many particle system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05215))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05215 "reduced mobile phase velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05215 """A term used mainly in liquid chromatography. It compares the mobile phase velocity with the velocity of diffusion into the pores of the particles (the so-called diffusion velocity, \\(u_{\\rm{D}}\\)): \\[
u=\\frac{\\overline{u}}{u_{\\rm{D}}}=\\frac{\\overline{u}\\ d_{\\rm{p}}}{D_{\\rm{M}}}\\] In open-tubular chromatography: \\[
u=\\frac{\\overline{u}\\ d_{\\rm{c}}}{D_{\\rm{M}}}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05216 "reduced osmotic pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05216 "The osmotic pressure divided by mass concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05217))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05217 "reduced sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05217 "A representative part of the primary (composited or gross) sample obtained by a division and reduction process. Typically the mass approximates that of the final laboratory sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05219 "reduced species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05219 "A term used to characterize the degree of reduction (or oxidation) in atoms, molecules and ions. An atom in a molecule or an ion which has a low oxidation state. An element or atom in a compound can be reduced by the reaction of an element or compound with hydrogen, while it can be oxidized by reaction with oxygen. A reduced species can be formed also through the gain of electrons (either at the negative electrode in a cell or through transfer from another atom, ion or group of atoms in a chemical reaction). For example, the carbon atom in $\\ce{CH4}$ and other hydrocarbons is in a reduced state, while the carbon in $\\ce{CO2}$ is in an oxidized state. Similarly the sulfur atom in $\\ce{H2S}$ is in a reduced state while that in sulfuric acid ($\\ce{H2SO4}$) is in an oxidized state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05220))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05220 "reduced viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05220 "The ratio of the relative viscosity increment to the mass concentration of the polymer, \\(c\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{\\eta _{i}}{c}\\), where \\(\\eta _{i}\\) is the relative viscosity increment.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05220 "viscosity number")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05221))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05221 "reducing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05221 "Decreasing the size of the laboratory sample or individual particles, or both. Division of the size of the laboratory sample may be accomplished manually by coning and quartering or by riffling or mechanically by rotary dividers. Reduction of particle size may be accomplished by milling or grinding. Simultaneous division and reduction may also be achieved with mills having stream diverters.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05222))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05222 "reduction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05222 "The complete transfer of one or more electrons to a molecular entity (also called 'electronation'), and, more generally, the reverse of the processes described under oxidation (2) and (3).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05222 "electronation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05223 "reductive elimination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05223 "The reverse of oxidative addition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05224))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05224 "reductones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05224 "Compounds containing an enediol structure stabilized by conjugation and hydrogen bonding with an adjacent carbonyl group, $\\ce{RC(OH)=C(OH)C(=O)R}$. They are strong reducing agents, fairly strong acids and commonly derived from saccharides by oxidation at the carbon atom alpha to the carbonyl function, e.g. ascorbic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05225 "re-extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05225 "Since the prefix 're' can signify 'back' as well as 'again' this term is ambiguous and should be avoided, except where the process of extraction (e.g. from aqueous solution to an organic phase) in a single direction is repeated (following stripping). It should not be used as a synonym for stripping or back-extraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05227 "reference atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05227 "The atom to which a primitive change is nucleophilic, electrophilic, nucleofugal or homolytic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05229 "reference electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05229 "An external electrode system which comprises an inner element, usually mercury–mercury(I) chloride (calomel), silver–silver chloride or thallium amalgam–thallium(I) chloride, a chamber containing the appropriate filling solution and a device for forming a liquid junction, e.g. capillary, ceramic plug, fritted disc or ground glass sleeve.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05230 "reference material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05230 "A substance or mixture of substances, the composition of which is known within specified limits, and one or more of the properties of which is sufficiently well established to be used for the calibration of an apparatus, the assessment of a measuring method or for assigning values to materials. Reference materials are available from national laboratories in many countries [e.g. National Institute for Standards and Technology (NIST), USA.; Community Bureau of Reference, UK].")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05231))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05231 "reference method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05231 "A method having small, estimated inaccuracies relative to the end use requirement. The accuracy of a reference method must be demonstrated through direct comparison with a definitive method or with a primary reference material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05232 "reference procedure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05232 "Agreed method for determining one or more air quality characteristics where it is not practical to produce a reference material; the result obtained is defined as the measure of the air quality characteristic. For example, a gas such as $\\ce{NO2}$ may be somewhat unstable when stored in a tank at low mixing ratios, so that the use of a more stable $\\ce{NO}$ standard mixture in $\\ce{N2}$ may be oxidized to $\\ce{NO2}$ by $\\ce{O3}$ in a specified manner referred to as the $\\ce{NO2}$ reference procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05233 "reference state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05233 "The state in which the element is stable at a chosen standard-state pressure and for a given temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05234))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05234 "reference value pH standard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05234 "An aqueous solution of \\(\\pu{0.05 mol}\\) potassium hydrogen phthalate per kg water which is the reference value for the definition of pH(RVS) values in the temperature range \\(\\pu{0\\!{-}\\! 95 ^{\\circ}C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05235))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05235 "reflectance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05235 "Fraction of incident radiation reflected by a surface or discontinuity, \\(\\rho(\\lambda) = \\frac{P_{\\lambda} ^{\\rm{refl}}}{P_{\\lambda} ^{\\rm{0}}}\\), where \\(P_{\\lambda}^{\\rm{0}}\\) and \\(P_{\\lambda}^{\\rm{refl}}\\) are, respectively, the incident and reflected spectral radiant power.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05235 "reflectance factor")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05235 "reflectivity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05236 "reflection electron energy loss spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05236 "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a focused low energy (\\(E_{0} < 10\\ \\rm{eV}\\)) electron beam and the energy distribution of the reflected electrons is measured. This energy distribution contains features corresponding to discrete losses of energy of the reflected electrons due to excitation of vibrational and plasmon states and provides information on the type and geometric structure of compounds at the surface of the specimen.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05236 "high resolution energy loss spectroscopy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05238))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05238 "reflection high energy electron diffraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05238 "Any technique which measures the angular intensity distribution of electrons 'reflected' from a crystalline surface under bombardment with high energy electrons near grazing incidence. The diffraction pattern provides very surface sensitive information (information depth \\(1\\ \\rm{nm}\\)) on the atomic arrangement of the top layers of a solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05239 "refraction effects")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05239 "The luminescence flux emitted from the interior of a rectangular sample reaching a photodetector place at some distance from the sample is decreased by a factor of approximately \\(n^{2}\\) (where \\(n\\) is the refractive index of the medium) compared with a medium whose refractive index is 1.0. Such effects are termed refraction effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05240))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05240 "refractive index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05240 "The ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to that in a given medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05241))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05241 "refractive index increment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05241 "The change of the solution refractive index, \\(n\\), with solute concentration, \\(C\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{\\partial n}{\\partial C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05242 "regeneration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05242 "Reversal of catalytic deactivation with restoration of the original catalytic activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05243 "regioselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05243 "A regioselective reaction is one in which one direction of bond making or breaking occurs preferentially over all other possible directions. Reactions are termed completely (100%) regioselective if the discrimination is complete, or partially (x%), if the product of reaction at one site predominates over the product of reaction at other sites. The discrimination may also semi-quantitatively be referred to as high or low regioselectivity. (Originally the term was restricted to addition reactions of unsymmetrical reagents to unsymmetrical alkenes.) In the past, the term 'regiospecificity' was proposed for 100% regioselectivity. This terminology is not recommended owing to inconsistency with the terms stereoselectivity and stereospecificity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05244 "regular block")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05244 "A block that can be described by only one species of constitutional repeating unit in a single sequential arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05245 "regular coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05245 "A petroleum coke with good graphitizability, characterized by a combination of properties which differ significantly from those of metallurgical coke but do not reach the quality level of premium coke. These properties are: optical anisotropy, medium reversible thermal expansion and low ash content. Regular coke is mainly used for the production of synthetic carbon and graphite materials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05247 "regular macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05247 "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of a single constitutional unit with all units connected identically with respect to directional sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05248 "regular oligomer molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05248 "An oligomer molecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of a single constitutional unit with all units connected identically with respect to directional sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05249 "regular polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05249 "A polymer composed of regular macromolecules, regular star macromolecules or regular comb macromolecules. A polymer consisting of star macromolecules with arms identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization is considered to be regular. Analogously, a polymer consisting of comb macromolecules with the sub-chains between the branch points of the main chain and the terminal sub-chains of the main chain identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization and the side-chains identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization is considered to be regular.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05250 "regular single-strand polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05250 "A regular linear polymer that can be described by a preferred constitutional repeating unit in which both terminal constituent subunits are connected through single atoms to the other identical constitutional repeating units or to an end group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05252 "regulator gene")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05252 "A gene which codes for a protein (an activator of repressor) having the ability to induce or repress the transcription of other genes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05253 "Reissert compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05253 "Compounds formed by formal addition of an acyl group and a cyano group to a nitrogen atom and carbon atom, respectively, of a nitrogen–carbon bond in quinolines, isoquinolines, and related nitrogen heterocycles, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05255))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05255 "relative")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05255 "Quotient of quantities having the same kind-of-quantity in different systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05257 "relative adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05257 "If \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\sigma}\\) and \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{1}^{\\sigma}\\) are the Gibbs surface concentrations of components i and 1, respectively, with reference to the same, but arbitrarily chosen, Gibbs surface, then the relative adsorption of component i with respect to component 1, is defined as \\[\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(1\\right)} = \\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\sigma}- \\mathit{\\Gamma}_{1}^{\\sigma}\\ \\frac{c_{i}^{\\alpha}- c_{i}^{\\beta}}{c_{1}^{\\alpha}- c_{1}^{\\beta}}\\] and is invariant to the location of the Gibbs surface. Alternatively, \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(1\\right)}\\) may be regarded as the Gibbs surface concentration of \\(i\\) when the Gibbs surface is chosen so that \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\sigma}\\) is zero, i.e. the Gibbs surface is chosen so that the reference system contains the same amount of component 1 as the real system. Hence \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{1}^{\\left(1\\right)}\\equiv 0\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05258 "relative atomic mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05258 "The ratio of the average mass of the atom to the unified atomic mass unit.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05258 "atomic weight")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05259))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05259 "relative biological effectiveness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05259 "The ratio of the appropriate value of the biological effectiveness of the radiation in question to that of X-radiation with an average specific ionization of 100 ion pairs per micron of water, for the particular biological effect under consideration and for the condition under which the radiation is received.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05260 "relative configuration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05260 "The configuration of any stereogenic (asymmetric) centre with respect to any other stereogenic centre contained within the same molecular entity. Unlike absolute configuration, relative configuration is reflection-invariant. Relative configuration, distinguishing diastereoisomers, may be denoted by the configurational descriptors \\(R^{\\rm{*}}\\), \\(R^{\\rm{*}}\\) (or l) and \\(R^{\\rm{*}}\\), \\(S^{\\rm{*}}\\) (or u) meaning, respectively, that the two centres have identical or opposite configurations. For molecules with more than two asymmetric centres the prefix rel- may be used in front of the name of one enantiomer where R and S have been used. If any centres have known absolute configuration then only \\(R^{\\rm{*}}\\) and \\(S^{\\rm{*}}\\) can be used for the relative configuration.See also: α (alpha), β (beta) (Defs. 1 and 3)Two different molecules Xabcd and Xabce, may be said to have the same relative configurations if e takes the position of d in the tetrahedral arrangement of ligands around X (i.e. the pyramidal fragments Xabc are superposable). By the same token the enantiomer of Xabce may be said to have the opposite relative configuration to Xabcd. The terms may be applied to chiral molecular entities with central atoms other than carbon but are limited to cases where the two related molecules differ in a single ligand.Both definitions can be generalized to include stereogenic units other than asymmetric centres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05261 "relative counting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05261 "A measurement in which the activity of a test portion is derived from the ratio between the count rates observed for the test portion and for a radioactive source of known activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05262 "relative density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05262 "Ratio of density to a reference density, usually the density of water at \\(\\pu{4^{\\circ}C}\\); in the older literature this is called specific gravity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05262 "specific gravity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05263 "relative detection limit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05263 "(Often incorrectly referred to as sensitivity). Smallest amount of material detectable ($3\\upsigma\\!$-criterion) in a matrix relative to the amount of material analysed — given in atomic, mole or weight fractions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05263 "detection limit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05264))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05264 "relative electrode potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05264 "The electrode potential of a single electrode is not amenable to direct experimental determination and can only be measured against a reference system. The measured quantity, \\(E\\), is the relative electrode potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05266))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05266 "relative error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05266 "Error of measurement divided by the true value of the measurand. Since a true value cannot be determined, in practice the conventional true value is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05267 "relative humidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05267 "The ratio, often expressed as a percentage, of the partial pressure of water in the atmosphere at some observed temperature, to the saturation vapour pressure of pure water at this temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05269 "relative micellar mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05269 "The relative molecular mass (\\(M_{\\rm{r}}\\)) of a micelle is called the relative micellar mass or micellar weight and is defined as the mass of a mole of micelles divided by the mass of \\(\\frac{1}{12}\\) mole of $\\ce{^{12}C}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05269 "micellar weight")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05270 "relative molar mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05270 "Molar mass divided by \\(\\pu{1 g mol-1}\\) (the latter is sometimes called the standard molar mass).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05271 "relative molecular mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05271 "Ratio of the mass of a molecule to the unified atomic mass unit. Sometimes called the 'molecular weight' or relative molar mass.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05271 "molecular weight")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05271 "relative molar mass")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05272 "relative permeability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05272 "Ratio of the magnetic flux density in a given medium to that in vacuum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05273 "relative permittivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05273 "Ratio of the electric field strength in vacuum to that in a given medium. It was formerly called the dielectric constant.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05273 "dielectric constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05274 "relative preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05274 "An operation (process) as a result of which the ratio of concentration or amounts of microcomponents and main macrocomponents increases. This is a particular example of a separation, but at greatly differing concentrations of components. The ratio of the masses of the initial and final samples is not of great importance. Frequently, the main purpose of relative preconcentration is the replacement of a matrix unsuitable for analysis by a suitable medium. In some cases, it is difficult to trace a boundary between the absolute and the relative preconcentrations as, for example, in preconcentration by zone melting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05276))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05276 "relative retardation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05276 "A term which is equivalent to relative retention used in column chromatography: the ratio of the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) value of a component to the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) value of a standard (reference) substance. Since the mobile phase front is common for the two components, the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) value can be expressed directly as the ratio of the distances travelled by the spot of the compound of interest (\\(b_{i}\\)) and the reference substance (\\(b_{\\rm{st}}\\)) respectively: \\[R_{\\rm{rel}}=\\frac{R_{\\rm{F}\\left(i\\right)}}{R_{\\rm{F}\\left(\\rm{st}\\right)}}=\\frac{b_{i}}{b_{\\rm{st}}}\\] In former nomenclatures the symbol \\(R_{\\rm{s}}\\) was used to express relative retardation in planar chromatography. Because of its identity with the symbol for peak resolution the symbol \\(R_{\\rm{rel}}\\) is suggested for relative retardation in planar chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05277 "relative retention")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05277 "The ratio of the adjusted or net retention volume (time) or retention factor of a component relative to that of a standard, obtained under identical conditions: \\[r=\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}i}^{'}}{V_{\\rm{R}\\left(\\rm{st}\\right)}^{'}}=\\frac{V_{\\rm{N}i}}{V_{\\rm{N}\\left(\\rm{st}\\right)}}=\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}i}^{'}}{t_{\\rm{R}\\left(\\rm{st}\\right)}^{'}}=\\frac{k_{i}}{k_{\\rm{st}}}\\] Depending on the relative position of the peak corresponding to the standard compound in the chromatogram, the value of \\(r\\) may be smaller, larger or identical to unity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05280))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05280 "relative standard deviation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05280 "The standard deviation divided by the mean of the series. It can be calculated with the formula: \\[s_{\\rm{r}}=\\frac{s}{\\overline{x}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05281))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05281 "relative uncertainty")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05281 "Uncertainty divided by the mean value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05282 "relative viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05282 "The ratio of the viscosity of the solution, \\(\\eta \\), to the viscosity of the solvent, \\(\\eta _{\\rm{s}}\\), i.e. \\(\\eta _{\\rm{r}} = \\frac{\\eta}{\\eta _{\\rm{s}}}\\). Synonymous with viscosity ratio.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05282 "viscosity ratio")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05283 "relative viscosity increment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05283 "The ratio of the difference between the viscosities of solution and solvent to the viscosity of the solvent, i.e. \\(\\eta _{i} = \\frac{\\eta - \\eta _{\\rm{s}}}{\\eta _{\\rm{s}}}\\), where \\(\\eta \\) is the viscosity of the solution and \\(\\eta _{\\rm{s}}\\) is the viscosity of the solvent. The use of the term 'specific viscosity' for this quantity is discouraged, since the relative viscosity increment does not have the attributes of a specific quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05284 "relative volumic mass")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05284 "Synonymous with relative density.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05285 "relaxation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05285 "If a system is disturbed from its state of equilibrium it relaxes to that state, and the process is referred to as relaxation. The branch of kinetics concerned with such processes is known as 'relaxation kinetics'. Relaxation techniques include temperature jump and 'pressure jump'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05287))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05287 "relaxation time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05287 "In magnetic resonance spectroscopy the longitudinal relaxation time, \\(T_{1}\\), is associated with spin-lattice relaxation, and the transverse relaxation time, \\(T_{2}\\), with spin-spin relaxation. The definitions are: \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}M_{z}}{\\rm{d}t} = -\\frac{M_{z} - M_{z,\\rm{e}}}{T_{1}}\\) and \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}M_{x}}{\\rm{d}t} = -\\frac{M_{x}}{T_{2}}\\), where \\(M_{z}\\) and \\(M_{x}\\) are the components of magnetization parallel and perpendicular to the static field \\(B\\) and the subscript e denotes the equilibrium value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05288))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05288 "releaser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05288 "A substance which reduces solute-volatilization interferences by forming a compound preferentially with the interferent, thus preventing the reaction of the analyte or interferent from entering a thermally stable compound.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05289))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05289 "rem")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05289 "Non-SI unit of dose equivalent (\\(\\rm{rem} = 0.01\\ \\rm{Sv}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05290))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05290 "Renner–Teller effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05290 "Molecular distortion in linear molecular species with degenerate electronic states (e.g. belonging to the \\(C_{\\infty\\rm{v}}\\) group). The Renner–Teller effect is a particular case of the Jahn–Teller effect. From the point of view of the potential energy surfaces the Renner–Teller effect generates a surface touching (see scheme below).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05291 "reorganization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05291 "The molecular process by which (i) amorphous or poorly ordered regions of a polymer specimen become incorporated into crystals, or (ii) a change to a more stable crystal structure takes place, or (iii) defects within the crystal decrease.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05292))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05292 "reorganization energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05292 "Gibbs energy dissipated when a system that has undergone 'vertical' electron transfer (i.e., electron transfer obeying the Franck–Condon principle) relaxes to the equilibrium state for its new charge distribution. Commonly the total reorganization energy (\\(\\lambda\\)) is written as the sum of an inner contribution (\\(\\lambda_{\\rm{in}}\\)) and an outer contribution (\\(\\lambda_{\\rm{out}}\\)) attributed to nuclear reorganizations of the redox partners and their environment (solvent), respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05293 "repeatability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05293 "The closeness of agreement between independent results obtained with the same method on identical test material, under the same conditions (same operator, same apparatus, same laboratory and after short intervals of time). The measure of repeatability is the standard deviation qualified with the term: `repeatability' as repeatability standard deviation. In some contexts repeatability may be defined as the value below which the absolute difference between two single test results obtained under the above conditions, may be expected to lie with a specified probability.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05297 "replacement name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05297 "A name in which the replacement of an atom or a group of a parent structure by another atom or group is indicated by affixes attached to or inserted into the name of the parent structure. There are two main types of replacement names.   Skeletal replacement name. A name in which the replacement of skeletal atoms and their associated hydrogen atoms is indicated by non-detachable prefixes. When carbon atoms are replaced by heteroatoms this method has been called 'a' nomenclature since the prefixes end in 'a'. Certain names in which the prefix 'thio-', 'seleno-' or 'telluro-' indicates replacement of a skeletal oxygen atom by a sulfur, selenium or tellurium atom, respectively, are also skeletal replacement names.   Functional replacement name. A name containing prefixes or infixes which indicate the replacement of an oxygen atom or hydroxy group of a characteristic group, functional parent or class name by other atoms or groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05298 "replacement operation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05298 "The exchange of one group of atoms or a single non-hydrogen atom for another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05299 "replicate sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05299 "Multiple (or two) samples taken under comparable conditions. This selection may be accomplished by taking units adjacent in time or space. Although the replicate samples are expected to be identical, often the only thing replicated is the act of taking the physical sample. A duplicate sample is a replicate sample consisting of two portions. The umpire sample is usually used to settle a dispute; the replicate sample is usually used to estimate sample variability.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05299 "duplicate sample")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05300))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05300 "replication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05300 "The duplication of DNA as part of the reproductive cycle of a cell or virus. During replication the two DNA strands in the double helix separate, and each strand then acts as a template specifying the base sequence of newly synthesized complementary strands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05301))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05301 "report")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05301 "A combination of specimen information and results. The report should contain information about unequivocal identification of the source and type of material analysed and the requesting agency. It may contain such other information that is pertinent to the correct interpretation of a result (e.g. confidence interval, reference data and interpretative information).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05302 "reprecipitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05302 "The planned repetition of a precipitation in order to remove chemically different species from a previous precipitate or to improve its stoichiometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05303))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05303 "representative sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05303 "A sample resulting from a sampling plan that can be expected to adequately reflect the properties of interest of the parent population. A representative sample may be a random sample or, for example, a stratified sample, depending upon the objective of sampling and the characteristics of the population. The degree of representativeness of the sample may be limited by cost or convenience.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05304 "repression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05304 "Binding of a protein (repressor) to the operator sequence in an operon thus preventing the transcription of the following structural gene(s) into mRNA and consequently synthesis of protein (cf. enzyme repression).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05305))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05305 "reproducibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05305 "The closeness of agreement between independent results obtained with the same method on identical test material but under different conditions (different operators, different apparatus, different laboratories and/or after different intervals of time). The measure of reproducibility is the standard deviation qualified with the term 'reproducibility' as reproducibility standard deviation. In some contexts reproducibility may be defined as the value below which the absolute difference between two single test results on identical material obtained under the above conditions, may be expected to lie with a specified probability. Note that a complete statement of reproducibility requires specification of the experimental conditions which differ.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05306 "repulsive potential-energy surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05306 "A surface for an exergonic reaction $\\ce{A + BC}$ in which the col corresponds to considerable separation between the products $\\ce{A–B + C}$. The energy barrier in the potential-energy profile is in the later stages of the reaction path. On such a surface most of the energy is released after $\\ce{A–B}$ is formed. Repulsive surfaces are also called late-downhill surfaces, and the barrier in such a surface is called a Type-II barrier.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05306 "late-downhill surface")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05308))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05308 "<i>Re</i>, <i>Si</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05308 "A stereoheterotopic face of a trigonal atom is designated Re if the ligands of the trigonal atom appear in a clockwise sense in order of CIP priority when viewed from that side of the face. The opposite arrangement is termed Si.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05309 "residence time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05309 "(instrumentation): The time required for air or reagent parcel to pass from the entrance to the exit of an instrument. Often this is approximated as the ratio of the interior volume of the device to the flow rate.(atmospheric): The average time a molecule or aerosol spends in the atmosphere after it is released or generated there. For compounds with well defined sources and emission rates, this is estimated by the ratio of the average global concentration of a substance to its production rate on a global scale. It is a function of not only the emission rates but the loss rates by chemical and physical removal processes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05310))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05310 "residence time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05310 "The average time a particle or volume element of the culture resides in a bioreactor (or other device) through which a liquid medium continously flows. residence time of the liquid is the reciprocal of dilution rate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05310 "hydraulic retention time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05311))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05311 "residual current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05311 "The current that flows, at any particular value of the applied potential, in the absence of the substance whose behaviour is being investigated (i.e. in a blank solution).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05312 "residual fuel/oil")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05312 "The liquid or semi-liquid, high-boiling fraction of residue from the distillation of petroleum which is used as a fuel. After removal of the lower boiling fraction of crude oil, sold as petroleum gas, the somewhat higher boiling fraction becomes gasoline and diesel oil. A portion of the higher boiling fraction is 'cracked' to yield additional gasoline. Still heavier oils become lubricants. paraffin, asphalt, etc., are also extracted from the crude oil. It is the remaining residual oil, not economically usable for other industrial purposes, which is then sold as a relatively low cost fuel for burning.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05313 "residual liquid junction error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05313 "Error arising from breakdown in the assumption that the liquid junction potential remains constant when solution X is substituted for solution S in the operational cell.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05313 "residual liquid junction potential error")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05314 "residual spectrum/background spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05314 "Set of peaks recorded in the absence of a sample and due either to small air leaks or to the presence of molecules desorbed from the walls of the introduction device or the source, or from the pump fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05315 "resistance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05315 "Electric potential difference divided by the electric current when there is no electromotive force in a conductor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05316 "resistivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05316 "Electric field strength divided by the current density. This quantity is a tensor in an anisotropic medium.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05316 "electric resistivity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05317))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05317 "resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05317 "A characteristic of the separation of two adjacent peaks. It may be expressed according to the equation: \\[R_{\\rm{AB}} = 2\\ \\frac{|d_{\\rm{R}}\\left(\\rm{B}\\right)\\,-\\,d_{\\rm{R}}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)|}{|w\\left(\\rm{B}\\right)\\,+\\,w\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)|}\\] where \\(R_{\\rm{AB}}\\) is the resolution, \\(d_{\\rm{R}}(\\rm{A})\\) and \\(d_{\\rm{R}}(\\rm{B})\\) are the retention distances (time or volume) of each eluted component A and B, and \\(w(\\rm{A})\\) and \\(w(\\rm{B})\\) are the respective widths of each peak at its base.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05318))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05318 "resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05318 "(energy): By analogy with the peak width definition for mass resolution, a peak showing the number of ions as a function of their translational energy should be used to give a value for the energy resolution.($\\pu{10\\%}$ valley definition): Let two peaks of equal height in a mass spectrum at masses \\(m\\) and \\(m- \\Delta m\\) be separated by a valley which at its lowest point is just $\\pu{10\\%}$ of the height of either peak. For similar peaks at a mass exceeding \\(m\\), let the height of the valley at its lowest point be more (by any amount) than ten per cent of either peak height. Then the resolution ($\\pu{10\\%}$ valley definition) is \\(\\frac{m}{\\Delta m}\\). It is usually a function of \\(m\\). The ratio \\(\\frac{m}{\\Delta m}\\) should be given for a number of values of \\(m\\).(peak width definition): For a single peak made up of singly charged ions at mass \\(m\\) in a mass spectrum, the resolution may be expressed as \\(\\frac{m}{\\Delta m}\\) where \\(\\Delta m\\) is the width of the peak at a height which is a specified fraction of the maximum peak height. It is recommended that one of three values $\\pu{50\\%}$, $\\pu{5\\%}$ or $\\pu{0.5\\%}$ should always be used. For an isolated symmetrical peak recorded with a system which is linear in the range between $\\pu{5\\%}$ and $\\pu{10\\%}$ levels of the peak, the $\\pu{5\\%}$ peak width definition is technically equivalent to the $\\pu{10\\%}$ valley definition. A common standard is the definition of resolution based upon \\(\\Delta m\\) being Full Width of the peak at Half its Maximum height, sometimes abbreviated 'FWHM'. This acronym should preferably be defined the first time it is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05319 "resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05319 "Wavenumber, wavelength or frequency difference of two still distinguishable lines in a spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05320))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05320 "resolution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05320 "The separation of a racemate into the component enantiomers.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05320 "optical resolution")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05321 "resolving power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05321 "The ability to distinguish between ions differing in the quotient mass/charge by a small increment. It may be characterized by giving the peak width, measured in mass units, expressed as a function of mass, for at least two points on the peak, specifically at fifty percent and at five percent of the maximum peak height.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05322 "resolving power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05322 "Transition wavenumber (or wavelength or frequency) divided by the resolution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05323))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05323 "resolving time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05323 "The smallest time interval which must elapse between the occurrence of two consecutive ionizing events or signal pulses, in order that the measuring device be capable of fulfilling its function of each of them separately.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05325))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05325 "resolving time correction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05325 "Correction to be applied to the observed number of pulses in order to take into account the number of pulses lost during the resolving time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05326))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05326 "resonance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05326 "In the context of chemistry, the term refers to the representation of the electronic structure of a molecular entity in terms of contributing structures. Resonance among contributing structures means that the wavefunction is represented by 'mixing' the wavefunctions of the contributing structures. The concept is the basis of the quantum mechanical valence bond methods. The resulting stabilization is linked to the quantum mechanical concept of 'resonance energy'. The term resonance is also used to refer to the delocalization phenomenon itself.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05327 "resonance absorption technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05327 "The monitoring of atoms or radicals generated in the gas phase by observing the attenuation of the radiation from a lamp emitting the characteristic resonance radiation of the observed species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05329))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05329 "resonance cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05329 "The cross-section for resonant absorption of the Mössbauer transition $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{ray}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05331 "resonance effect magnitude")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05331 "Ratio of the difference in the transmitted or scattered intensity at resonance maximum and off-resonance, relative to the intensity off-resonance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05333 "resonance energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05333 "The difference in potential energy between the actual molecular entity and the contributing structure of lowest potential energy. The resonance energy cannot be measured, but only estimated, since contributing structures are not observable molecular entities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05334))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05334 "resonance energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05334 "The energy of a particle entering a nuclear reaction, this energy being just sufficiently high to lead to the formation of reaction products in one of their excited states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05335 "resonance fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05335 "Fluorescence from the primary excited atomic or molecular species at the wavelength of the exciting radiation (no relaxation within the excited manifold). This term is also used to designate the radiation emitted by an atom of the same wavelength as the longest one capable of exciting its fluorescence, e.g. \\(122.6\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in the case of the hydrogen atom, and \\(253.7\\ \\rm{nm}\\) in the case of the mercury atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05336 "resonance fluorescence technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05336 "The monitoring of atoms or radicals generated in the gas phase by observing the intensity of fluorescence (exitance) emitted by the species after excitation with radiation of the same wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05337 "resonance integral")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05337 "In Hückel molecular orbital theory, the integral over space of the type \\(\\int \\psi _{r}^{*}\\ \\hat{H}\\ \\psi _{s}  \\ \\rm{d}\\tau \\) where \\(\\hat{H}\\) is the hamiltonian operator and \\(\\psi _{r}\\) and \\(\\psi _{s}\\) are different wavefunctions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05338 "resonance integral")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05338 "The integral, over all or some specified portion of the resonance energy range, of the cross-section divided by the energy of a radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05339))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05339 "resonance lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05339 "Lamp emitting resonance radiation of atoms and their ions. Depending on the requirements, the lamp is filled either with pure vapour of the element or with a mixture of it and other gases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05340 "resonance line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05340 "The longest wavelength capable of exciting fluorescence in an atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05341))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05341 "resonance line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05341 "The radiative decay of an excitation level may proceed to the neutral ground state and would thus occur at the same energy as the corresponding line in the absorption spectrum. Such a line is called a resonance line and the process is called resonance emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05342 "resonance neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05342 "Neutrons whose energy corresponds to the resonance energy of a specified nuclide or element. If the nuclide is not specified, the term refers to resonance neutrons of $\\ce{^{239}U}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05343 "resonance radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05343 "Synonymous with resonance fluorescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05344 "response constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05344 "A quantity whose expression includes a current, whose value is characteristic of a charge-transfer process and the experimental conditions under which it is investigated, and whose nature depends on the technique that is employed. Some typical response constants are the diffusion current constant in polarography, the voltammetric constant in linear-sweep voltammetry, and the chronopotentiometric constant in chronopotentiometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05345 "response time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05345 "The time required for the detector output to go from the initial value to a percentage (e.g. 99%) of the final value. In the case of an exponential behaviour of the detector \\(\\tau _{\\rm{R}}\\) can be related to the time constant \\(\\tau _{\\rm{c}}\\). The rise time \\(\\tau _{\\rm{r}}\\) is the time required for the detector output to vary between given percentages (e.g. from 10% to 90%) of the final value. Similarly, the fall time \\(\\tau _{\\rm{f}}\\) is the time required for the detector output to vary between given percentages (e.g. from 90% to 10%) of the initial value.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05345 "rise time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05346 "response time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05346 "Time which elapses, when there is a stepwise change in the quantity to be measured, between the moment when this change is produced and the moment when the indication reaches a value conventionally fixed at 90% of the final change in indication.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05347 "responsivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05347 "Detector input can be e.g. radiant power, irradiation, radiant energy. It produces a measurable detector output which may be e.g. an electrical charge, an electrical current or potential or a change in pressure. The ratio of the detector output and the detector input is defined as the responsivity. It is given in e.g. ampere/watt, volt/watt. The responsivity is a special case of the general term sensitivity. Dark current is the term for the electrical output of a detector in the absence of input. This is a special case of the general term dark output. For photoconductive detectors the term dark resistance is used. If the responsivity is normalized with regard to that obtained from a reference radiation the resulting ratio is called relative responsivity. For measurements with monochromatic radiation at a given wavelength the term spectral responsivity \\(R(\\lambda)\\) is used. In some cases the 'relative spectral responsivity', where the spectral responsivity is normalized with respect to the responsivity at some given wavelength, is used. The dependence of the spectral responsivity on the wavelength is described by the spectral responsivity function. The useful spectral range of the detector should be given as the wavelength range where the relative responsivity does not fall below a specified value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05348 "rest point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05348 "The position of the pointer with respect to the pointer scale when the motion of the beam has ceased.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05350 "restriction enzymes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05350 "Endonucleases which recognize specific base sequences within a DNA helix, creating a double-strand break of DNA. Type I restriction enzymes bind to these recognition sites but subsequently cut the DNA at different sites. Type II restriction enzymes both bind and cut within their recognition or target sites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05351 "result")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05351 "The final value reported for a measured or computed quantity, after performing a measuring procedure including all subprocedures and evaluations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05352 "retardation factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05352 "The fraction of the sample component in the mobile phase at equilibrium; it is related to the retention factor and other fundamental chromatography terms: \\[R = \\frac{1}{k\\,+\\,1}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05353 "retardation factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05353 "Ratio of the distance travelled by the centre of the spot to the distance simultaneously travelled by the mobile phase: \\[R_{\\rm{F}}=\\frac{b}{a}\\] By definition the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) values are always less than unity. They are usually given to two decimal places. In order to simplify this presentation the \\(hR_{\\rm{F}}\\) values may be used: they correspond to the \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) values multiplied by 100. Ideally, \\(R_{\\rm{F}}\\) values are identical to the \\(R\\) values used in column chromatography.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05353 "RF value")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05355 "retarder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05355 "This term, used mainly in polymerization kinetics, refers to a substance that reduces the rate of reaction. Synonymous with inhibitor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05357 "retention")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05357 "Of atoms undergoing a nuclear transformation, that fraction which remains in or reverts to its initial chemical form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05358 "retention efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05358 "The ratio of the quantity of particles retained by a separator to the quantity entering it (generally expressed as a percentage).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05359))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05359 "retention factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05359 "A measure of the time the sample component resides in the stationary phase relative to the time it resides in the mobile phase; it expresses how much longer a sample component is retarded by the stationary phase than it would take to travel through the column with the velocity of the mobile phase. Mathematically, it is the ratio of the adjusted retention volume (time) and the hold-up volume (time): \\[k=\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}}^{'}}{V_{\\rm{M}}}=\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}}^{'}}{t_{\\rm{M}}}\\] If the distribution constant is independent of sample component concentration, then the retention factor is also equal to the ratio of the amounts of a sample component in the stationary and mobile phases respectively, at equilibrium: \\[k=\\frac{\\rm{amount of component in stationary phase}}{\\rm{amount of component in mobile phase}}\\] If the fraction of the sample component in the mobile phase is \\(R\\), then the fraction in the stationary phase is \\(1 - R\\); thus \\[k=\\frac{1- R}{R}\\] In former nomenclatures and in the literature one may find the expressions partition ratio, capacity ratio, capacity factor or mass distribution ratio to describe this term. In the literature the symbol \\(k^{'}\\) is often used for the retention factor, particularly in liquid chromatography. The original reason for this was to clearly distinguish it from the partition coefficient (distribution constant) for which the symbol K had been utilized. Since, however, the distribution constants are all identified with a subscript, there is no reason to add the prime sign to this symbol. It should be emphasized that all the recognized nomenclatures (IUPAC, BS, ASTM) have always clearly identified the capacity factor with the symbol \\(k\\) and not \\(k^{'}\\). The logarithm of the retention factor is equivalent to the \\(R_{\\rm{M}}\\) value used in planar chromatography. The symbol \\(\\kappa \\) is suggested to express \\[\\kappa =\\log _{10}k=\\log _{10}\\left[\\frac{1- R}{R}\\right]\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05360))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05360 "retention index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05360 "The retention index of a sample component is a number, obtained by interpolation (usually logarithmic), relating the adjusted retention volume (time) or the retention factor of the sample component to the adjusted retention volumes (times) of two standards eluted before and after the peak of the sample component. In the Kováts index or Kováts retention index used in gas chromatography, n-alkanes serve as the standards and logarithmic interpolation is utilized: \\[I = 100\\left[\\frac{\\log _{10}X_{i}- \\log _{10}X_{z}}{\\log _{10}X_{z+1}- \\log _{10}X_{z}}+z\\right]\\] where \\(X\\) refers to the adjusted retention volumes or times, \\(z\\) is the number of carbon atoms of the n-alkane eluting before and \\(\\left(z+1\\right)\\) is the number of carbon atoms of the n-alkane eluting after the peak of interest: \\[V_{\\rm{R}z}^{'} < V_{\\rm{R}i}^{'} < V_{\\rm{R}(z+1)}\\] The Kováts (retention) index expresses the number of carbon atoms (multiplied by 100) of a hypothetical normal alkane which would have an adjusted retention volume (time) identical to that of the peak of interest when analysed under identical conditions. The Kováts retention index is always measured under isothermal conditions. In the case of temperature-programmed gas chromatography a similar value can be calculated utilizing direct numbers instead of their logarithm. Since both the numerator and denominator contain the difference of two values, here we can use the total retention volumes (times). Sometimes this value is called the linear retention index. \\[I^{\\rm{T}} = 100\\left[\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}i}^{\\rm{T}}- t_{\\rm{R}z}^{\\rm{T}}}{t_{\\rm{R}(z+1)}^{\\rm{T}}- t_{\\rm{R}z}^{\\rm{T}}}+z\\right]\\] where \\(t_{\\rm{R}}^{\\rm{T}}\\) refers to the total retention times (chart distances) measured under the conditions of temperature programming. The value of \\(I^{\\rm{T}}\\) will usually differ from the value of \\(I\\) measured for the same compound under isothermal conditions, using the same two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05363))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05363 "retention temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05363 "The column temperature when the peak maximum for a component has been reached in temperature-programmed chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05366 "retention volumes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05366 "Retention measurements (and measurements of hold-up volume and peak width) may be made in terms of times or chart distances as well as volumes. If flow and recorder speeds are constant, the volumes are directly proportional to the times and chart distances. The following definitions are drawn up in terms of volume, and it is recommended that theoretical discussion should be couched in the same terms wherever possible. The total retention volume, \\(V_{\\rm{R}}\\), is the volume of eluent carrier gas admitted to the column between the injection of the sample and the emergence of the peak maximum of the specified component. It includes the hold-up volume. In gas chromatography, the volume of carrier gas is specified at the outlet pressure and temperature of the column. Note: the word 'total' in this definition allows retention time to be used as a general term when specification of a particular quantity is not required. The adjusted retention volume, \\(V_{\\rm{R}}^{'}\\), is the total retention volume less the hold-up volume, \\(V_{\\rm{M}}\\), i.e. \\[V_{\\rm{R}}^{'}=V_{\\rm{R}}- V_{\\rm{M}}=\\overline{V}- V_{\\rm{I}}\\] where \\(\\overline{V}\\) is the peak elution volume and \\(V_{\\rm{I}}\\) the interstitial volume. The net retention volume, \\(V_{\\rm{N}}\\), is the adjusted retention volume multiplied by the pressure-gradient correction factor: \\[V_{\\rm{N}}=j\\ V_{\\rm{R}}^{'}\\] The specific retention volume, \\(V_{\\rm{g}}\\), is the net retention volume per gram of stationary liquid, active solid or solvent-free gel. In liquid chromatography, except when conducted at very high pressures, the compression of the mobile phase is negligible, and the adjusted and net retention volumes are identical. The specific retention volume is then the adjusted retention volume per gram of stationary liquid, active solid, or solvent-free gel. It is recommended that, when appropriate, authors specify the drying conditions. At \\(0\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), \\[V_{\\rm{g}}=273\\ \\frac{V_{\\rm{N}}}{w_{\\rm{L}}\\ T}\\] where \\(w_{\\rm{L}}\\) is the mass of the stationary liquid phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05369 "retinoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05369 "Oxygenated derivatives of 3,7-dimethyl-1-(2,6,6-trimethylcyclohex-1-enyl)nona-1,3,5,7-tetraene as shown and derivatives thereof.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05370 "retro")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05370 "A prefix which indicates a shift by one position of all single and double bonds in a conjugated polyene system. It has been applied only to carotenoids and retinoids, e.g. retro-carotenoids, retro-retinoids. A prefix which indicates the reverse sequence of a peptide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05374))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05374 "reversed direct-injection burner")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05374 "A direct-injection burner combines the function of nebulizer and burner. Most commonly the oxidant is also used for aspirating and nebulizing the sample. However, when the fuel is used for this purpose, the term reversed direct-injection burner is applied.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05375))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05375 "reversed isotope dilution analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05375 "Isotope dilution analysis used for the determination of the isotopic carrier in a solution of a radionuclide with the aid of one of its stable isotopes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05375 "reversed radiochemical isotope dilution analysis")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05376 "reversed-phase chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05376 "An elution procedure used in liquid chromatography in which the mobile phase is significantly more polar then the stationary phase, e.g. a microporous silica-based material with chemically bonded alkyl chains. The term 'reverse phase' is an incorrect expression to be avoided.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05378 "reverse transcriptases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05378 "Enzymes found in retroviruses that can synthesize complementary single-strands of DNA from an mRNA sequence as template. They are used in genetic engineering to produce specific cDNA molecules from purified preparations of mRNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05379 "reversible transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05379 "A transition that returns a system directly to its original state by reversing the process that changed it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05380))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05380 "revolutions per minute")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05380 "Non-SI unit for rotational frequency.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05381))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05381 "rheology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05381 "The study of the flow and deformation of matter under the influence of a mechanical force. It is concerned particularly with material behaviour which cannot be described by the simple linear models of hydrodynamics and elasticity. Some of these departures are due to the presence of colloidal particles or to the influence of surfaces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05382))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05382 "rheopexy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05382 "A phenomenon in which the time of solidification, after discontinuation of a relatively high shear rate, is shortened by applying a small shear rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05384 "rhodamine dyes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05384 "Dyes derived from condensation of phthalic anhydride with m-dialkylaminophenols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05385 "rhombohedral graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05385 "A thermodynamically unstable allotropic form of graphite with an ABCABC stacking sequence of the layers. The exact crystallographic description of this allotropic form is given by the space group \\(D_{3\\rm{d}}^{5}- R\\overline{3}m\\), (unit cell constants: \\(a=256.6\\ \\mathrm{pm}\\), \\(c=1006.2\\ \\mathrm{pm}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05386 "ribonucleic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05386 "Naturally occurring polyribonucleotides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05387 "ribonucleotides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05387 "Nucleotides in which the glycosyl group is a ribosyl group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05388 "ribosomal RNA")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05388 "RNA molecules which are essential structural and functional components of ribosomes, the subcellular units responsible for protein synthesis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05389 "ribosomes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05389 "Subcellular units composed of specific RNA molecules and a large number of proteins that are responsible for protein synthesis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05390))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05390 "Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05390 "A theory of unimolecular gas reactions in which the rate with which the energized reactant molecule breaks down is treated as a function of the energy \\(\\varepsilon\\) that it contains. The theory assumes that the rate is proportional to the number of ways of distributing \\(\\varepsilon\\) among the internal degrees of freedom of the reactant molecule, in such a manner that the critical energy \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{c}}\\) is localized in one particular degree of freedom.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05390 "RRK theory")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05391 "Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05391 "An improved form of Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel (RRK) theory in which account is taken of the way in which the various normal-mode vibrations and rotations contribute to reaction, and allowance is made for the zero-point energies. In this theory the energy \\(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}}\\) in an energized molecule is classified as either active, \\(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*active}}\\), or inactive, \\(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*inactive}}\\). The rate depends upon \\(\\frac{P(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*active}})}{N(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}})}\\), where \\(N(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}})\\) is the density of states having energy between \\(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}}+\\rm{d}\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*}}\\), and \\(P(\\varepsilon^{\\rm{*active}})\\) is the sum of the active quantum states of the activated complex. This extension of RRK theory brings it in line with transition-state theory.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05391 "RRKM theory")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05392))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05392 "riffling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05392 "The separation of a free-flowing sample into (usually) equal parts by means of a mechanical device composed of diverter chutes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05393))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05393 "ring assembly")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05393 "Two or more cyclic systems (single rings or fused systems) which are directly joined to each other by double or single bonds are named ring assemblies when the number of such direct ring junctions is one less than the number of cyclic systems involved. Examples:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05394 "Ringelmann chart")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05394 "A chart which has been used in air pollution evaluation for assigning the degree of blackness of smoke emanating from a source. The observer compares the shades of grey (white to black) with a series of shade diagrams formed by horizontal and vertical black grid lines on a white background. A corresponding number, the Ringelmann number, is then assigned to the describe the best match; numbers range from 0 (white) to 5 (black). This method of pollution evaluation, although relevant to the enforcement of legislation still in force in many countries, is not recommended for use today since more quantitative indicators of the efficiency of the combustion are now available.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05395 "ring-opening copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05395 "A copolymerization which is a ring-opening polymerization with respect to at least one monomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05396 "ring-opening polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05396 "A polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit which is acyclic or contains fewer cycles than the monomer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05397))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05397 "ring reversal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05397 "The interconversion by rotation about single bonds (coupled with angle deformation in the transition state) of cyclic conformers having equivalent ring shapes (e.g. chair → chair) though not necessarily equivalent spatial positions of substituents (e.g. equatorial → axial).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05397 "ring inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05397 "chair–chair interconversion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05398))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05398 "ring-sector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05398 "A sequence of ring atoms, linked together as an unbranched chain and connecting two bridgehead or junctions, but not itself containing any bridgehead or junction, is termed an atomic ring-sector. A bond linking together directly two bridgeheads or junctions is termed a bond ring-sector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05400 "rise time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05400 "The difference between the response time and the dead time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05401 "rise velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05401 "The vertical component of the velocity of the volatilized component in the observation space (in \\(\\rm{mm s}^{-1}\\)). It depends on the flame temperature, the solute nebulized, the observation height and the gas flow rate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05401 "vertical rise velocity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05402 "Ritchie equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05402 "The linear free-energy relation \\[\\log _{10}k_{\\rm{N}} = \\log _{10}k_{0}+N_{\\rm{+}}\\] applied to the reactions between nucleophiles and certain large and relatively stable organic cations, e.g. arenediazonium, triarylmethyl and aryltropylium cations in various solvents. \\(k_{\\rm{N}}\\) is the rate constant for reaction of a given cation with a given nucleophilic system (i.e. given nucleophile in a given solvent). \\(k_{0}\\) is the rate constant for the same cation with water in water, and \\(N_{\\rm{+}}\\) is a parameter which is characteristic of the nucleophilic system and independent of the cation. A surprising feature of the equation is the absence of a coefficient of \\(N_{\\rm{+}}\\), characteristic of the substrate (cf. the \\(s\\) in the Swain–Scott equation), even though values of \\(N_{\\rm{+}}\\) vary over 13 log units. The equation thus involves a gigantic breakdown of the reactivity–selectivity principle. The equation has been extended both in form and in range of application.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05404 "röntgen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05404 "Non-SI unit of exposure to X- or $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{radiation}$: \\(\\rm{R} = \\pu{2.58E-4 C kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05405 "root-mean-square end-to-end distance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05405 "The square root of the mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear polymer chain averaged over all conformations of the chain. For a freely jointed chain consisting of \\(N\\) segments each of length \\(L\\), it is given by: \\[\\rm{}^{1/2} = \\sqrt{N}\\ L\\] The subscript zero is used to indicate unperturbed dimensions, as in \\(\\rm{}_{0}^{1/2}\\). If this term is used repeatedly, and if it is not confusing, the abbreviated name 'end-to-end distance' may be used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05407))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05407 "rotamer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05407 "One of a set of conformers arising from restricted rotation about one single bond.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05408))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05408 "rotational barrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05408 "In a rotation of groups about a bond, the potential energy barrier between two adjacent minima of the molecular entity as a function of the torsion angle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05409 "rotational constants")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05409 "Coefficients of quantum numbers in the rotational term expression and inversely proportional to the principal moments of inertia. Symbols: \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\). \\(\\overset{˜}{A}=\\frac{h}{8\\ \\pi ^{2}\\ c\\ I_{\\rm{A}}}\\) (dimension wavenumber), \\(A=\\frac{h}{8\\ \\pi ^{2}\\ I_{\\rm{A}}}\\) (dimension frequency) where \\(h\\) is the Planck constant and \\(c\\) the speed of light in vacuum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05410 "rotational diffusion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05410 "A process by which the equilibrium statistical distribution of the overall orientation of molecules or particles is maintained or restored. Rotational diffusion may be compared to translational diffusion through which the equilibrium statistical distribution of position in space is maintained or restored.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05411 "rotational diffusion coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05411 "Defined by the equation: \\[D_{\\theta }=\\frac{t_{\\theta }}{\\frac{\\partial (f(\\theta ,\\mathit{\\Phi }))}{\\partial \\theta }\\ \\sin\\,\\theta }\\] where \\(f\\left(\\theta ,\\mathit{\\Phi }\\right)\\:\\sin \\,\\theta \\ \\rm{d}\\theta \\ \\rm{d}\\mathit{\\Phi }\\) is the fraction of particles whose axes make an angle between \\(\\theta \\) and \\(\\theta +\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\) with the direction \\(\\theta =0\\), and have an azimuth between \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) and \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} + \\rm{d}\\mathit{\\Phi}\\); \\(t_{\\theta }\\ \\mathrm{d}\\mathit{\\Phi }\\) is the fraction of particles having an azimuth between \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) and \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} + \\rm{d}\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) whose axis passes from values \\(\\theta \\) in unit time. The axis whose rotational diffusion is considered has to be clearly indicated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05412))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05412 "rotational frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05412 "The number of rotations divided by time: \\[f_{\\rm{rot}}=\\frac{\\rm{d}N}{\\rm{d}t}\\] The synonyms rate of rotation, rate of revolution, centrifugal speed, centrifugation speed, and the traditional units of rotational frequency such as revolutions per minute, r.p.m., ppm, \\(\\rm{rev min}^{-1}\\), \\(\\rm{r min}^{-1}\\), are not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05413))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05413 "rotational term")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05413 "Rotational energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05414))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05414 "rotator phase transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05414 "A transition from a fully-ordered crystal of globular or quasi-spherical molecules to a crystal that retains translational order but exhibits dynamic orientational disorder and is usually mechanically soft (plastic phase). Example: The transition of $\\ce{CBr4}$ to a plastic phase which exists from \\(319\\) to \\(365\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05415 "rotatory power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05415 "The quantitative measure of optical activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05416 "rotaxanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05416 "Molecules in which a ring encloses another, rod-like molecule having end groups too large to pass through the ring opening, and thus holds the rod-like molecule in position without covalent bonding.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05417 "rotenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05417 "Naturally occurring substances containing a cis-fused tetrahydrochromeno[3,4-b]chromene nucleus. Many rotenoids contain an additional ring, e.g rotenone:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05418 "rotometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05418 "A device, based on Stokes law, for measuring rate of fluid flow. It is a tapered vertical tube having a circular cross-section in which a float moves in a vertical path to a height dependent upon the rate of fluid flow upward through the tube.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05419))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05419 "roughness factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05419 "The ratio: \\[f_{\\rm{r}}=\\frac{A_{\\rm{r}}}{A_{\\rm{g}}}\\] where \\(A_{\\rm{r}}\\) is the real (true, actual) surface (interface) area and \\(A_{\\rm{g}}\\) is the geometric surface (interface) area.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05419 "rugosity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05420 "rovibronic state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05420 "A state corresponding to a particular rotational sublevel of a particular vibrational level of a particular electronic state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05423))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05423 "<i>R</i>, <i>S</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05423 "The approved designations (devised by Cahn, Ingold and Prelog) of absolute configuration at four-coordinate (quadriligant) and six-coordinate (sexiligant) stereogenic centres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05424))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05424 "<i>r</i>, <i>s</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05424 "Stereodescriptor s of pseudo-asymmetric atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05427))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05427 "ruby laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05427 "A pulsed source of coherent radiation emitting mainly at \\(\\pu{694.3 nm}\\) from chromium ions ($\\ce{Cr^{3+}}$) in aluminium oxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05428 "rupture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05428 "The formation of a hole which permits coalescence or direct contact of the two phases which it separates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05429))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05429 "Rutherford backscattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05429 "Any technique using high energy particles directed toward a sample, in which the bombarding particles are detected and recorded as function of energy and/or angle. The technique is mostly used for determining depth distributions of elements based on the energy of the backscattered particle. In general, $\\ce{He^{+}}$ or $\\ce{H^{+}}$ particles are used at energies in the order of \\(100\\ \\rm{keV}\\) to some \\(\\mathrm{MeV}\\). Also referred to as backscattering spectrometry (BSS).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05429 "backscattering spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05429 "backscattering spectrometry (BSS)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05430 "Rydberg constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05430 "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\(R_{\\infty} = \\frac{E_{h}}{2\\ h\\ c_{0}} = 1.097\\ 373\\ 1534\\ (13)\\times 10^{7}\\ \\rm{m}^{-1}\\), where \\(E_{h}\\) is the Hartree energy, \\(h\\) the Planck constant, and \\(c_{0}\\) the speed of light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05431))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05431 "Rydberg orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05431 "For an atom, an orbital with principal quantum number greater than that of any occupied orbital of the ground state. For a molecular entity, a molecular orbital which correlates with a Rydberg atomic orbital in an atomic fragment produced by dissociation. Typically, the extension of the Rydberg orbital is large compared to the size of the atom or molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05432))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05432 "Rydberg transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05432 "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' orbital to a Rydberg orbital. Spectral bands corresponding to Rydberg transitions approximately fit the Rydberg formula: \\[\\sigma = I - \\frac{R}{(n\\,-\\,\\mathit{\\Delta })^{2}}\\] where \\(\\sigma \\) is the wavenumber, \\(I\\) the ionization potential of the atom or molecular entity, \\(n\\) a principal quantum number, \\(R\\) the Rydberg constant, and \\(\\mathit{\\Delta}\\) the quantum defect which differentiates between s, p, d, etc., orbitals. The notation used is, e.g. \\(\\pi \\rightarrow \\mathrm{ns}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06783 "redox potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06783 "Any oxidation-reduction (redox) reaction can be divided into two half-reactions: one in which a chemical species undergoes oxidation and one in which another chemical species undergoes reduction. If a half-reaction is written as a reduction, the driving force is the reduction potential. If the half-reaction is written as oxidation, the driving force is the oxidation potential related to the reduction potential by a sign change. So the redox potential is the reduction/oxidation potential of a compound measured under standards conditions against a standard reference half-cell. In biological systems the standard redox potential is defined at pH – 7.0 versus the hydrogen electrode and partial pressure of hydrogen = 1 bar.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06793 "rubredoxin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06793 "An iron-sulfur protein without acid-labile sulfur, in which an iron centre is coordinated by four sulfur-containing ligands, usually cysteine. The function, where known, is an electron carrier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06807))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06807 "risk")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06807 "Probability of adverse effects caused under specified circumstances by an agent in an organism, a population, or an ecological system.Expected frequency of occurrence of a harmful event arising from such an exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06808))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06808 "risk assessment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06808 "Identification and quantification of the risk resulting from a specific use or occurrence of an agent, taking into account possible harmful effects on individuals exposed to the agent in the amount and manner proposed and all the possible routes of exposure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06841 "receptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06841 "Molecular structure in or on a cell that specifically recognizes and binds to a compound and acts as a physiological signal transducer, or mediator of, an effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06871 "rigid chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06871 "The rod-like chain of an MCPLC (main-chain polymer liquid crystal) with direct links between the mesogenic groups for which the persistence length is at least comparable with the contour length and much greater than the diameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06893 "reverse osmosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06893 "Liquid-phase pressure-driven separation process in which applied transmembrane pressure causes selective movement of solvent against its osmotic pressure difference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06912))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06912 "reference dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06912 "An estimate (with uncertainty spanning perhaps an order of magnitude) of daily exposure to the human population (including sensitive subgroups) that is likely to be without appreciable risk of deleterious effects during a lifetime.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06913))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06913 "risk estimation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06913 "Assessment with or without mathematical modeling, of the probability and nature of effects of exposure to a substance based on quantification of dose-effect and dose-response relationships for that substance and the population(s) and environmental components likely to be exposed and on assessment of the levels of potential exposure of people, organisms, and environment at risk.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06978 "receptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06978 "A receptor is a protein or a protein complex in or on a cell that specifically recognizes and binds to a compound acting as a molecular messenger (neurotransmitter, hormone, lymphokine, lectin, drug, etc). In a broader sense, the term receptor is often used as a synonym for any specific (as opposed to non-specific such as binding to plasma proteins) drug binding site, also including nucleic acids such as DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT06979))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT06979 "regression analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT06979 "Regression analysis is the use of statistical methods for modeling a set of dependent variables, Y, in terms of combinations of predictions, X. It includes methods such as multiple linear regression (MLR) and partial least squares (PLS).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07092 "relative hardness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07092 "The difference between the values of the absolute hardnesses (\\(\\eta \\)) of a given molecule and the corresponding reference structure (\\(\\eta _{\\rm{a}}\\)): \\(\\eta _{\\rm{r}} = \\eta - \\eta _{\\rm{a}}\\). The values of relative hardnesses correlate well with the resonance energies of cyclic compounds and may be used as an index of aromaticity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07093 "relativistic effects")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07093 "Corrections to exact non-relativistic energy from the fact that inner shell electrons in heavy atoms move with velocities comparable in order of magnitude to the velocity of light. The most important relativistic effect is spin–orbit coupling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07094 "resonance hybrid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07094 "A localized valence bond representation of a molecule. This describes pictorially an electronic wavefunction that makes some contribution to the state wavefunction of the system. resonance hybrids are needed to describe the state of a system when that system is not adequately described by a single Lewis structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07095 "ribbon delocalisation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07095 "Electron delocalization realized in both acyclic and cyclic $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}conjugated}$, 1, and $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonded}$, 2, ribbon-like systems.  For cyclic systems, a variety of linkage fashions between several ribbons (pericyclic, spirocyclic, longicyclic and laticyclic) is possible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07096 "Rydberg state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07096 "An electronic state that arises by the excitation of a valence electron to a diffuse atomic-like orbital. For instance, the lowest lying Rydberg state (n, 3S) of $\\ce{NH3}$ can be described as resulting from the excitation of a non-bonding orbital to a 3S Rydberg orbital.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07149))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07149 "radiation reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07149 "Chemical reaction that is induced by ionizing radiation with $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{ray}$, X-ray, electron, or other high-energy beams.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07150 "reactive blending")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07150 "Mixing process that is accompanied by the chemical reaction(s) of components of a polymer mixture.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07164 "reactive polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07164 "Polymer having reactive functional groups that can undergo chemical transformation under the conditions required for a given reaction or application.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07166 "resin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07166 "Soft solid or highly viscous substance, usually containing prepolymers with reactive groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07193))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07193 "resist polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07193 "Polymeric material that, when irradiated, undergoes a marked change in solubility in a given solvent or is ablated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07308))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07308 "rod-like morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07308 "Morphology characterized by cylindrical phase domains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07470 "radiation trapping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07470 "Process by which the resonance radiation emitted in a lamp is absorbed and re-emitted many times in the plasma prior to striking the phosphor. This process is called radiation trapping or imprisonment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07471))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07471 "radical photosubstitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07471 "Photo-induced substitution resulting from the reaction of radicals produced by photolysis of an appropriate reagent, such as, e.g., halogenated compounds.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:RT07471 "free-radical photosubstitution")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07472 "Rehm–Weller equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07472 "Empirical correlation found between the observed second-order rate constant, \\(k_{\\rm{q}}\\), for an intermolecular electron-transfer reaction and the Gibbs energy of the photoinduced electron transfer process within the encounter complex (\\(\\Delta _{\\rm{ET}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)): \\[k_{\\rm{q}} = \\frac{k_{\\rm{d}}}{1+ \\frac {k_{\\rm{d}}}{K_{\\rm{d}}\\, \\rm{Z}}\\left [ \\rm{exp}\\left ( \\frac{\\Delta G^{\\ddagger }}{RT} \\right ) + \\rm{exp}\\left ( \\frac{\\Delta_{\\rm{ET}} G^{o }}{RT} \\right ) \\right ]}\\] with \\(k_{\\rm{d}}\\) and \\(k_{\\rm{-d}}\\) the rate constant for the formation and separation, respectively, of the encounter (precursor) complex, \\(K_{\\rm{d}} = \\: ^{k_{\\rm{d}}}\\! /_{k_{\\rm{-d}}}\\), \\(Z\\) the universal collision frequency factor, \\(R\\) the gas constant, \\(T\\) the absolute temperature and \\(\\Delta G^{\\ddagger}\\) the activation Gibbs energy of the forward electron transfer reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07473 "residual emission anisotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07473 "Photoselected molecules hindered in their rotation (e.g., in lipid bilayers or liquid crystals) do not become randomly oriented even after long time periods. Thus, the emission anisotropy does not decay to zero but to a steady value, \\(r_{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\), called residual emission anisotropy. In the case of a single rotational correlation time, \\(\\tau _{\\mathrm{c}}\\) or \\(\\theta \\), the decay of emission anisotropy following $\\ce{\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}pulse}$ excitation is given by: \\[r\\left(t\\right)=(r_{0}- r_{\\mathrm{\\infty }})\\ \\exp (- \\frac{t}{\\tau _{\\mathrm{c}}})+r_{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\] where \\(r_{0}\\) is the fundamental emission anisotropy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07474))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07474 "rotational correlation time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07474 "Parameter describing the time dependence of the tumbling of a molecular entity in a medium of viscosity \\(\\eta\\). The rotational correlation time can be obtained from the decay of the fluorescence or phosphorescence anisotropy and is related to the average molecular rotational diffusion coefficient, \\(D_{\\rm{r}}\\), in turn related to the hydrodynamic molecular volume of the fluorophore, \\(V\\), and to \\(\\eta\\) (see Note 3).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07475))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07475 "rotational relaxation time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07475 "Parameter describing the time-dependence of the tumbling of a molecular entity in a medium of viscosity \\(\\eta\\) as originally defined by Debye, and used by Perrin in the original development of the theories of rotational motion of fluorophores.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07526 "responsive gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07526 "Gel that responds to external electrical, mechanical, thermal, light-induced or chemical stimulation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07527 "rheopexic gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07527 "Gel for which the time of solidification after discontinuation of a relatively high shear rate, is reduced by applying a small shear rate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:RT07527 "rheotropic gel")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07529))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07529 "ringing gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07529 "Gel with energy dissipation in the acoustic frequency range.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:RT07529 "humming gel")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07572 "reversible network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07572 "Polymer network that forms or breaks up as the temperature is changed or under the action of a force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07628))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07628 "reaction injection moulding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07628 "Reactive polymer processing that produces polymer monoliths by low-pressure injection and mixing of low-viscosity precursors into moulds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07629 "reinforced reaction injection moulding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07629 "Reaction injection moulding within which glass fibres are included to increase the strength of the moulding.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:RT07630))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:RT07630 "reactive polymer processing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:RT07630 "Process whereby a polymeric monolith is produced through an in-situpolymerization or polymer modification reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S04387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S04387 "π → σ* transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S04387 "An electronic transition described approximately as a promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' orbital $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ to an 'antibonding' orbital $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!}$ designated as $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}}$. Such transitions generally involve high transition energies and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05434))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05434 "σ, π")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05434 "The terms are symmetry designations, $\\ce{\\uppi\\! molecular orbitals}$ being antisymmetric with respect to a defining plane containing at least one atom (e.g. the molecular plane of ethene) and $\\ce{\\upsigma\\! molecular orbitals}$ symmetric with respect to the same plane. In practice the terms are used both in this rigorous sense (for orbitals encompassing the entire molecule) and also for localized two-centre orbitals or bonds, and it is necessary to make a clear distinction between the two usages. In the case of two-centre bonds, a $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ has a nodal plane that includes the internuclear bond axis, whereas a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ has no such nodal plane. (A $\\ce{\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ in organometallic or inorganic molecular species has two nodes.) Radicals are classified by analogy into $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}radicals}$. Such two-centre orbitals may take part in molecular orbitals of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}}$ or $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$. For example, the methyl group in propene contains three $\\ce{C–H}$ bonds, each of which is of local $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$ (i.e. without a nodal plane including the internuclear axis), but these three '$\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$' can in turn be combined to form a set of group orbitals one of which has $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$ with respect to the principal molecular plane and can accordingly interact with the two-centre orbital of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$ ($\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$) of the double-bonded carbon atoms, to form a molecular orbital of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}symmetry}$. Such an interaction between the $\\ce{CH3}$ group and the double bond is an example of what is called hyperconjugation. This cannot rigorously be described as '$\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}\\uppi\\!}$ conjugation' since $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ here refer to different defining planes, and interaction between orbitals of different symmetries (with respect to the same defining plane) is forbidden.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05434 "sigma, pi")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05435))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05435 "σ → σ* transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05435 "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ to an 'antibonding' $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}orbital}$ designated as \\(\\sigma ^{\\rm{*}}\\). Such transitions generally involve high transition energies, and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05441 "saccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05441 "The monosaccharides (1) and di- (2), oligo- (3 - 10) and polysaccharides (many), which are made up of $n$ monosaccharide units linked to each other by a glycosidic bond. Considered by some to be synonymous with carbohydrates.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05442 "Sackur–Tetrode constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05442 "Fundamental physical constant representing the translational contribution to molar entropy; \\(\\frac{S_{0}}{R} = -1.151\\ 693(21)\\) at \\(1\\ \\rm{K}\\) and standard pressure of \\(100\\ \\rm{kPa}\\), where \\(R\\) is the gas constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05443 "sacrificial acceptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05443 "Molecular entity that acts as the electron acceptor in a photoinduced electron transfer process and is not restored in a subsequent oxidation process but is destroyed by irreversible chemical conversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05444 "sacrificial donor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05444 "Molecular entity that acts as the electron donor in a photoinduced electron transfer process and is not restored in a subsequent reduction process but is destroyed by irreversible chemical conversion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05447 "salt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05447 "A chemical compound consisting of an assembly of cations and anions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05449))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05449 "salt form of an ion exchanger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05449 "The ionic form of an ion exchanger in which the counter-ions are neither hydrogen nor hydroxide ions. When only one valence is possible for the counter-ion, or its exact form or charge is not known, the symbol or the name of the counter-ion without charge is used, e.g. sodium-form or $\\ce{Na}$-form, tetramethylammonium-form, orthophosphate-form. When one of two or more possible forms is exclusively present, the oxidation state may be indicated by a Roman numeral, e.g. $\\ce{Fe^{II}}$ form, $\\ce{Fe^{III}}$ form.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05450 "salting out")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05450 "The addition of particular electrolytes to an aqueous phase in order to increase the distribution ratio of a particular solute.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05451 "sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05451 "A portion of material selected from a larger quantity of material. The term needs to be qualified, e.g. bulk sample, representative sample, primary sample, bulked sample, test sample. The term 'sample' implies the existence of a sampling error, i.e. the results obtained on the portions taken are only estimates of the concentration of a constituent or the quantity of a property present in the parent material. If there is no or negligible sampling error, the portion removed is a test portion, aliquot or specimen. The term 'specimen' is used to denote a portion taken under conditions such that the sampling variability cannot be assessed (usually because the population is changing), and is assumed, for convenience, to be zero. The manner of selection of the sample should be prescribed in a sampling plan.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05451 "analytical sample")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05456 "sample error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05456 "An error which arises when the absorbance of some samples changes with time, e.g. as a result of photochemical reaction, formation of aggregates or adsorption on the cell wall.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05457 "sample handling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05457 "Any action applied to the sample before the analytical procedure. Such actions include the addition of preservatives, separation procedures, storage at low temperature, protection against light and irradiation, loading, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05458 "sample injector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05458 "A device by which a liquid or gaseous sample is introduced into the apparatus. The sample can be introduced directly into the carrier-gas stream, or into a chamber temporarily isolated from the system by values which can be changed so as to make an instantaneous switch of the gas stream through the chamber. The latter is a by-pass injector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05459))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05459 "sampler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05459 "A device used to withdraw and deliver a volume or an amount of a sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05461))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05461 "sample unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05461 "The discrete identifiable portion suitable for taking as a sample or as a portion of a sample. These units may be different at different stages of sampling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05463))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05463 "sampling error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05463 "That part of the total error (the estimate from a sample minus the population value) associated with using only a fraction of the population and extrapolating to the whole, as distinct from analytical or test error. It arises from a lack of homogeneity in the parent population. In chemical analysis, the final test result reflects the value only as it exists in the test portion. It is usually assumed that no sampling error is introduced in preparing the test sample from the laboratory sample. Therefore, the sampling error is usually associated exclusively with the variability of the laboratory sample. Sampling error is determined by replication of the laboratory samples and their multiple analyses. Since sampling error is always associated with analytical error, it must be isolated by the statistical procedure of analysis of variance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05465))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05465 "sampling interval")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05465 "In Tast polarography, square-wave polarography, and similar techniques, the interval during which the current is measured or recorded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05466))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05466 "sampling plan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05466 "A predetermined procedure for the selection, withdrawal, preservation, transportation and preparation of the portions to be removed from a population as samples. Summarizing the test values or observations from the selected portions yields an estimate for the concentration of an analyte or a value for a property determined with a calculable degree of uncertainty at a specified confidence level. A sampling plan includes the designation of the number, location and size of the portions, and instructions for the extent of compositing and for the reduction (in amount and fineness) of the portions to a laboratory sample and to test portions. It may also contain acceptance criteria. Some sampling plans do not include more than instructions for the statistical selection of portions to be removed. Such plans should properly be designated as 'statistical sampling plans'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05467 "sampling time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05467 "In Tast polarography, square-wave polarography, and similar techniques, the duration of the sampling interval.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05468 "sandwich compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05468 "Compounds in which a metal atom is located between the faces of two parallel and planar (or nearly so) ring structures, e.g. bis(\\(\\eta ^{5}\\)-cyclopentadienyl)iron (ferrocene), dibenzenechromium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05469 "sanitary land fill")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05469 "An engineered burial of refuse. The refuse is dumped into trenches and compacted by bulldozer, where, it is hoped, aerobic metabolism by microorganisms decomposes the organic matter to stable compounds ($\\ce{H2O}$, $\\ce{CO2}$, etc.). Moisture is essential for the biological degradation and groundwater assists the process except when it fills air voids and prevents the transport of oxygen to the refuse. Land fills of unsatisfactory design can be major sources of air, water and soil pollution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05470 "saprophyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05470 "A microorganism that feeds on dead and decaying organic matter. Saprophytes excrete enzymes that digest organic residues externally, the low molecular weight compounds formed then being absorbed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05471))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05471 "saturated solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05471 "A solution which has the same concentration of a solute as one that is in equilibrium with undissolved solute at specified values of the temperature and pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05472 "saturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05472 "The state of a saturated solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05473 "saturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05473 "Of an irradiated element for a specified isotope, the steady state reached when the disintegration rate of the nuclide formed is equal to its production rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05474))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05474 "saturation activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05474 "For a specified isotope, the value of the activity of an irradiated element, whence state of saturation is reached.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05476))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05476 "saturation fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05476 "Amount-of-substance of a component (solute) in a solution divided by the amount-of-substance of the component when it is saturating the system at constant temperature and pressure. Also referred to simply as saturation, for example oxygen saturation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05478 "saturation transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05478 "A term used in nuclear magnetic resonance. When a nucleus is strongly irradiated, its spin population may partly be transferred to another nucleus by an exchange process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05479))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05479 "saturation vapour pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05479 "The pressure exerted by a pure substance (at a given temperature) in a system containing only the vapour and condensed phase (liquid or solid) of the substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05480 "sawhorse projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05480 "A perspective formula indicating the spatial arrangement of bonds on two adjacent carbon atoms. The bond between the two atoms is represented by a diagonal line, the left-hand bottom end of which locates the atom nearer the observer and the right-hand top end the atom that is further away. In general a Newman or zig-zag projection is preferred.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05481 "Saytzeff rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05481 "Dehydrohalogenation of secondary- and tertiary-alkyl halides proceeds by the preferential removal of the $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen}$ from the carbon that has the smallest number of hydrogens. Originally formulated by A. Saytzeff (Zaitsev) to generalize the orientation in $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}}$elimination reactions of alkyl halides, this rule has been extended and modified, as follows: When two or more olefins can be produced in an elimination reaction, the thermodynamically most stable alkene will predominate. Exceptions to the Saytzeff rule are exemplified by the Hofmann rule.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05481 "Zaitsev rule")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05482))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05482 "scaler")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05482 "A sub-assembly for counting electrical pulses and containing one or more scaling circuits.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05483))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05483 "scaling circuit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05483 "An electronic circuit which produces an output pulse for each time a specified number of pulses has been received at its input.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05484 "scanning electron microscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05484 "Any analytical technique which involves the generation and evaluation of secondary electrons (and to a lesser extent back scattered electrons) by a finely focused electron beam (typically \\(10\\ \\rm{nm}\\) or less) for high resolution and high depth of field imaging.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05485))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05485 "scanning method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05485 "This term refers to the sequence of control over operating parameters of a mass spectrometer that results in a spectrum of masses, velocities, momenta or energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05486 "scanning transmission electron microscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05486 "A special TEM-technique in which an electron transparent sample is bombarded with a finely focused electron beam (typically of a diameter of less than $\\pu{10 nm}$) which can be scanned across the specimen or rocked across the optical axis and transmitted, secondary, back scattered and diffracted electrons as well as the characteristic X-ray spectrum can be observed. STEM essentially provides high resolution imaging of the inner microstructure and the surface of a thin sample (or small particles), as well as the possibility of chemical and structural characterization of micrometer and sub-micrometer domains through evaluation of the X-ray spectra and the electron diffraction pattern.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05487 "scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05487 "A process in which a change in direction or energy of an incident radiation is caused by interaction with a particle, a system of particles, or a photon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05488 "scattering angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05488 "The angle between the forward direction of the incident beam and a straight line connecting the scattering point and the detector. Synonymous with angle of observation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05488 "angle of observation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05490 "scattering cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05490 "The scattering coefficient per particle (\\(\\frac{\\rm{cm}^{2}}{\\rm{particle}}\\)); \\[b_{\\rm{scat}}=n\\ \\sigma _{\\rm{scat}}\\] where \\(n\\) is the number concentration of particles (\\(\\rm{particles cm}^{-3}\\)) and \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{scat}}\\) is the scattering cross-section. \\(b_{\\rm{scat}}\\), the scattering component of extinction due to gas and particles, is measured in the atmosphere using a nephelometer. For a homogeneous atmosphere it is related in theory to the meteorological range (\\(L_{\\rm{V}}\\)): \\(L_{\\rm{V}}=\\frac{3.9}{b_{\\rm{scat}}}\\); \\(b_{\\rm{scat}}\\) and \\(b_{\\rm{abs}}\\) represent the scattering and absorption coefficients per unit length for a light beam (of path length \\(L\\)) which has a spectral radiance (intensity) \\(I_{0}\\) incident on a sample of air and \\(I\\) is the transmitted spectral radiance (intensity), \\(\\ln (\\frac{I_{0}}{I}) = L\\ (b_{\\rm{scat}}+b_{\\rm{abs}})\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05491 "scattering error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05491 "The presence of particulate matter, emulsions, micelles, etc. may cause radiation scattering which will result in further attenuation of the transmitted beam and the measured absorbance will be too high. This error is called the scattering error.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05492 "scattering matrix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05492 "The Stokes parameters of scattered light are given by the matrix equation \\[(s_{0},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}) = F\\ (s_{0}^{0},s_{1}^{0},s_{2}^{0},s_{3}^{0})\\] where the 4, 4 scattering matrix \\(F\\) is comprised of 16 scattering matrix elements. These matrix elements, which may originate in theory or experiment, provide a complete description of the scattered radiation in terms of the incident radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05493 "scattering plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05493 "The plane containing the incident light beam and the line from the centre of the scattering system to the observer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05494 "scattering vector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05494 "The vector difference between the wave propagation vectors of the incident and the scattered beam, both of length \\(\\frac{2\\ \\pi }{\\lambda }\\), where \\(\\lambda \\) is the wavelength of the scattered radiation in the medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05495))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05495 "scavenger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05495 "A substance that reacts with (or otherwise removes) a trace component (as in the scavenging of trace metal ions) or traps a reactive reaction intermediate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05496 "scavenging")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05496 "Binding radicals or free electrons with a receptive (or reactive) material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05497 "Schenck-sensitization mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05497 "Chemical transformation of one molecular entity caused by photoexcitation of a sensitizer, which undergoes temporary covalent bond formation with the molecular entity (M).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05498))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05498 "Schiff bases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05498 """Imines bearing a hydrocarbyl group on the nitrogen atom $\\ce{R2C=NR'}$ ($\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$). Considered by many to be synonymous with azomethines.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05498 "Schiff's bases")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05499 "Schiller layers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05499 "In some systems, sedimenting particles form layers separated by approximately equal distances of the order of the wavelength of light. This gives rise to strong colours when observed in reflected light and the system is said to form iridescent layers or Schiller layers.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05499 "iridescent layers")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05501 "Schulze–Hardy rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05501 "The generalization that the critical coagulation concentration for a typical lyophobic sol is extremely sensitive to the valence of the counter-ions (high valence gives a low critical coagulation concentration).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05502 "Schulz–Zimm distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05502 "In an assembly of macromolecules, a continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form: \\[f_{\\rm{w}}(x)\\ \\rm{d}x = \\frac{a^{b\\,+\\,1}}{\\varGamma\\left(b\\,+\\,1\\right)}\\ x^{b}\\ \\rm{e}^{-a\\ x}\\ \\rm{d}x\\] where \\(x\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization, \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive adjustable parameters, and (\\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}\\left(b+1\\right)\\)) is the gamma function of (\\(b+1\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05503 "scintillation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05503 "Burst of luminescence of short duration caused by an individual energetic particle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05504 "scintillation counter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05504 "A scintillator coupled to a photomultiplier tube. Incident X-ray photons are converted in the scintillator into bursts of visible light photons, some of which fall on the photocathode and can be measured. For incident photons having energies higher than the absorption edge of the elements contained in the scintillator, an escape peak can be observed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05505 "scintillation detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05505 "A radiation detector using a medium in which a burst of luminescence radiation, produced along the path of an ionizing particle, is quantified.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05506 "scintillation spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05506 "A measuring assembly incorporating a scintillation detector and a pulse amplitude analyser used for determining the energy spectrum of certain types of radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05507 "scintillators")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05507 "Materials used for the measurement of radioactivity, by recording the radioluminescence. They contain compounds (chromophores) which combine a high fluorescence quantum efficiency, a short fluorescence lifetime, and a high solubility. These compounds are employed as solutes in aromatic liquids and polymers to form organic liquid and plastic scintillators, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05508 "s-<i>cis</i>, s-<i>trans</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05508 "The spatial arrangement of two conjugated double bonds about the intervening single bond is described as s-cis if synperiplanar and s-trans if antiperiplanar. This term should not be applied to other systems such as N-alkyl amides (use E/Z or sp/ap).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05510 "scrubber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05510 "An apparatus used in sampling and in flue gas cleaning. The gas is passed through a space containing wetted 'packing' or spray. In general, particles are collected in scrubbers by one or a combination of the following: impingement of particles on a liquid medium; diffusion of the particles onto a liquid medium; condensation of liquid medium vapours on the particles; partitioning of the gas into extremely small elements to allow collection of the particles by Brownian diffusion and gravitation settling on the gas-liquid interface. The devices include spray towers, jet scrubbers, Venturi scrubbers, cyclonic scrubbers, inertial scrubbers, mechanical scrubbers and packed scrubbers. Normally the gas flow in the scrubber is counter to the liquid flow. Efficient scrubbers will collect particles as small as \\(1\\) to \\(2\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\) in diameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05511))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05511 "scrubbing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05511 "The process of selectively removing contaminating solutes (impurities) from an extract that contains these as well as the main extractable solute by treatment with a new immiscible liquid phase. The term stripping has a different meaning and should not be used in this sense although this usage has been customary in certain industries.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05512))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05512 "seco-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05512 "Cleavage of a ring with addition of one or more hydrogen atoms at each terminal group thus created is indicated by the prefix 'seco'. Examples:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05513 "second")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05513 """The second, symbol \\(\\pu{s}\\), is the SI unit of time. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the caesium frequency \\(\\Delta 
u_{\\ce{Cs}}\\), the unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of the caesium 133 atom, to be \\(\\pu{9192631770}\\) when expressed in the unit Hz, which is equal to \\(\\pu{s-1}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05514))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05514 "second")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05514 "Non-SI unit of plane angle: \\(1^{\\prime\\prime} = \\frac{1}{3600}\\ ^{\\unicode{x26ac}} = 4.848\\ 137\\,...\\,\\times\\ 10^{-6}\\ \\rm{rad}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05515))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05515 "secondary crystallization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05515 "Crystallization occurring after primary crystallization, usually proceeding at a lower rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05517 "secondary electron multiplier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05517 "A device to multiply current in an electron beam (or in a photon or particle beam by first conversion to electrons) by incidence of accelerated electrons upon the surface of an electrode which yields a number of secondary electrons greater than the number of incident electrons. These electrons are then accelerated to another electrode (or another part of the same electrode), which in turn emits further secondary electrons so that the process can be repeated. It is recommended that one should refer to the abundance of an ion, to the intensity of an ion beam, and to the height or area of a peak.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05518 "secondary electrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05518 "All electrons emitted from the surface of a solid except back scattered primary electrons. In practice, electrons emitted from the surface of a solid under particle bombardment which have a kinetic energy of less than \\(50\\ \\rm{eV}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05519 "secondary-electron yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05519 "The number of secondary electrons generated per primary electron for a given specimen and experimental conditions. It depends on the (mean) atomic number of the excited area of the sample, the angle between electron beam and sample surface, the primary electron energy, thickness of the sample and sample potentials.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05519 "secondary electron yield")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05521 "secondary fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05521 "Ionization of the analyte element in a sample by characteristic radiation from other elements in the sample resulting in an enhancement of the signal measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05522 "secondary ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05522 "The process in which ions are ejected from a sample surface (which may be a solid or substrate dissolved in a solvent matrix) as a result of bombardment by a primary beam of atoms or ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05523 "secondary isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05523 "A kinetic isotope effect that is attributable to isotopic substitution of an atom to which bonds are neither made nor broken in the rate-controlling step or in a pre-equilibrium step of a specified reaction, and is therefore not a primary isotope effect. One speaks of $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, (etc.) secondary isotope effects, where $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, (etc.) denote the position of isotopic substitution relative to the reaction centre. The corresponding isotope effect on the equilibrium constant of such a reaction is called a 'secondary equilibrium isotope effect'. Secondary isotope effects have been discussed in terms of the conventional electronic effects of physical organic chemistry, e.g. induction, hyperconjugation, hybridization, etc., since these properties are determined by the electron distribution, that depends on vibrationally averaged bond lengths and angles which vary slightly with isotopic substitution. While this usage is legitimate, the term 'electronic isotope effect' should be avoided, because of the misleading implication that such an effect is electronic rather than vibrational in origin.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05526))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05526 "secondary metabolites")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05526 "Metabolites which are produced by routes other than the normal metabolic pathways, mostly after the phase of active growth and under conditions of deficiency. The biological significance of many secondary metabolites is not exactly known.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05527 "secondary pollution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05527 "The products of the primary pollutants which form through photochemical and thermal reactions in the atmosphere ($\\ce{O3}$, peroxyacetyl nitrate, etc.).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05527 "secondary emissions")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05528 "secondary radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05528 "Radiation emitted by any matter irradiated with electromagnetic or ionizing radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05530 "secondary structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05530 "The conformational arrangement ($\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$-helix, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}pleated}$ sheet, etc.) of the backbone segments of a macromolecule such as a polypeptide chain of a protein without regard to the conformation of the side chains or the relationship to other segments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05531 "second-order transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05531 "A transition in which a crystal structure undergoes a continuous change and in which the first derivatives of the Gibbs energies (or chemical potentials) are continuous but the second derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure (i.e. heat capacity, thermal expansion, compressibility) are discontinuous. Example: The order–disorder transition in metal alloys, for example, $\\ce{CuZn}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05531 "continuous transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05532 "secular equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05532 "Radioactive equilibrium where the half life of the precursor isotope is so long that the change of its activity can be ignored during the period of interest and all activities remain constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05533 "sediment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05533 "The highly concentrated suspension which may be formed by the sedimentation of a dilute suspension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05534 "sedimentation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05534 "In the atmospheric sciences, the process of removal of an air borne particle from the atmosphere due to the effect of gravity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05535))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05535 "sedimentation coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05535 "Velocity of sedimentation divided by the acceleration of the force field (gravitation or centrifugation).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05536))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05536 "sedimentation equilibrium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05536 "The equilibrium established in a centrifugal field when there is no net flux of any component across any plane perpendicular to the centrifugal force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05537 "sedimentation field strength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05537 "The potential difference \\(E_{\\mathrm{sed}}\\) per unit length in a sedimentation or centrifugation cell. As the contributions of the interfacial potential differences at the electrodes are not included in \\(E_{\\mathrm{sed}}\\) this quantity, although theoretically important, is not accessible to measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05538))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05538 "sedimentation potential difference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05538 "The potential difference \\(E_{\\mathrm{sed}}\\) or \\(E\\) at zero current caused by the sedimentation of particles in the field of gravity or in a centrifuge, between two identical electrodes at different levels (or at different distances from the centre of rotation). \\(E\\) is positive if the lower (peripheral) electrode is negative. This is also called the Dorn effect.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05538 "Dorn effect")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05538 "sedimentation potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05539))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05539 "sedimentation velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05539 "Velocity of a component in a fluid relative to the fluid in the direction of gravitational or centrifugal acceleration. Also called sedimentation rate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05540 "sedimentation velocity method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05540 "A method by which the velocity of motion of solute component(s) or dispersed particles is measured and the result is expressed in terms of its (their) sedimentation coefficient(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05541 "sedimentation volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05541 "The volume \\(V_{\\mathrm{sed}}\\) of sediment formed in a suspension. If the sediment is formed in a centrifugal field, the strength of this field should be explicitly indicated, otherwise normal gravity is understood.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05542 "segment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05542 "(for bulk materials): Each of the single, large portions of material pre-existing either in space (e.g. bags, bales, drums) or accumulated during a fixed time (e.g. discharge from a conveyor belt) or formable as increments by a sampling device. Segments may be actual or conceptual.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05544 "segregation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05544 "The process that differentiates the composition at an interface or surface from the average or bulk composition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05545))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05545 "segregation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05545 "The rejection of a fraction of macromolecules, or of impurities, or both, from growing crystals. The rejected macromolecules are usually those of insufficient relative molecular mass, or differing in constitution or configuration (e.g. branching, tacticity, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05547 "selected ion monitoring")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05547 "This term is used to describe the operation of the mass spectrometer in which the intensities of several specific ion beams are recorded rather than the entire mass spectrum. An alternative recommended term is multiple ion (peak) monitoring. The use of the terms multiple ion detection and mass fragmentography are not recommended, because in the case of the former, it is the monitoring of several peaks which should be emphasized rather than the detection of different ions, and in the case of the latter it is often not fragments that are being monitored but peaks from molecular ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05548))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05548 "selection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05548 "A laboratory method applying a mixture of microorganisms to particular growth conditions under which only the cells with particular characteristics can survive and may be isolated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05549 "selection rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05549 "A rule that states whether a given transition is allowed or forbidden, on the basis of the symmetry or spin of the wavefunctions of the initial and final states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05550 "selective")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05550 "A term which expresses qualitatively the extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05551 "selective corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05551 "Corrosion of a single phase having more than one component, when the ratio of the corrosion rates of the components differs from the ratio of bulk mole fractions. Selective corrosion changes the composition of the material in the interfacial region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05554 "selective elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05554 "An elution procedure in which a specific eluent is used, e.g. a complexing agent that forms stable non-sorbable complexes with one or a group of the compounds to be separated, but affects the other components only to a negligible extent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05556 "selectively labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05556 "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as selectively labelled when a mixture of isotopically substituted compounds is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound in such a way that the position(s) but not necessarily the number of each labelling nuclide is defined. A selectively labelled compound may be considered as a mixture of specifically labelled compounds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05557 "selective micro-sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05557 "A sample which results where a small portion has been separated from the lot or laboratory sample by selective means such as magnetic-, density-, or manual separation, by micro-drilling, or by centrifugation, e.g. the separation of magnetic materials from a geological material, or the separation of metal particles from a lubricating oil. If individual particles are analysed the term individual particle analysis is applied.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05558 "selective poisoning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05558 "In selective poisoning or selective inhibition, a poison retards the rate of one catalysed reaction more than that of another or it may retard only one of the reactions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05558 "selective inhibition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05559))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05559 "selective preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05559 "An operation (process) as a result of which microcomponents are selectively isolated from a sample. It is used when the simultaneous presence of several components in the concentrate may distort the results of analysis. Selective preconcentration usually is achieved by isolation of the microcomponent to be determined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05560 "selective sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05560 "A sample that is deliberately chosen by using a sampling plan that screens out materials with certain characteristics and/or selects only material with other relevant characteristics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05561 "selective solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05561 "A medium that is a solvent for at least one component of a mixture of polymers, or for at least one block of a block or graft polymer, but a non-solvent for the other component(s) or block(s).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05563 "selectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05563 "It sometimes refers to the discrimination shown by a given reactant A when it reacts with two alternative reactants B and C, or in two different ways (e.g. at two different sites) with a reactant B.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05564 "selectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05564 "(qualitative): The extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure.(quantitative): A term used in conjunction with another substantive (e.g. constant, coefficient, index, factor, number) for the quantitative characterization of interferences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05566 "selectivity coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05566 "The equilibrium coefficient obtained by application of the law of mass action to ion exchange and characterizing quantitatively the ability of an ion exchanger to select one of two ions present in the same solution. The ions involved in the exchange should be specified as subscripts.Examples:Exchange: $\\ce{Mg^{2+}}$ – $\\ce{Ca^{2+}}$ \\[k_{\\rm{Mg}/\\rm{Ca}}=\\frac{\\rm{[Mg]}_{\\rm{S}}/\\rm{[Ca]}_{\\rm{S}}}{\\rm{[Mg]}_{\\rm{M}}/\\rm{[Ca]}_{\\rm{M}}}\\] Exchange: $\\ce{SO4^{2−}}$ – $\\ce{Cl^{−}}$ \\[k_{\\rm{SO}_{4}/\\rm{Cl}} = \\frac{\\rm{[}\\rm{SO}_{4}\\rm{]}_{\\rm{S}}/\\rm{[Cl]}_{\\rm{S}}^{2}}{\\rm{[}\\rm{SO}_{4}\\rm{]}_{\\rm{M}}/\\rm{[Cl]}_{\\rm{M}}^{2}}\\] In the above equations subscript S refers to the ion exchanger ('stationary phase') and M to the external solution ('mobile phase'). For exchanges involving counter-ions differing in their charges, the numerical value of \\(k_{\\rm{A}/\\rm{B}}\\) depends on the choice of the concentration scales in the ion exchanger and the external solution (molal scale, molar scale, mole fraction scale, etc.). Concentration units must be clearly stated for an exchange of ions of differing charges. The corrected selectivity coefficient (\\(k_{\\rm{A}/\\rm{B}}^{a}\\)) is calculated in a way identical to the selectivity coefficient except that the concentrations in the external solutions are replaced by activities. This term should not be used as a synonym for separation factor.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05566 "selectivity factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05567 "selectivity factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05567 "A quantitative representation of selectivity in aromatic substitution reactions (usually electrophilic, for monosubstituted benzene derivatives). If the partial rate factor, \\(f\\), expresses the reactivity of a specified position in the aromatic compound $\\ce{PhX}$ relative to that of a single position in benzene, then the selectivity factor \\(S_{f}\\) (expressing discrimination between p- and m-positions in $\\ce{PhX}$) is defined as: \\[S_{f}=\\log _{10}(\\frac{f_{p}^{\\rm{X}}}{f_{m}^{\\rm{X}}})\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05569 "selectivity ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05569 "Synonymous with selectivity coefficient.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05570 "selenenic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05570 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSeOH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{ArSeOH}$, areneselenenic acids.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05571 "selenides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05571 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSeR}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). They are thus selenium analogues of ethers.Metal salts of selane, $\\ce{H2Se}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05572 "seleninic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05572 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSe(=O)OH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05573 "selenocyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05573 "Salts and esters of selenocyanic acid, $\\ce{HSeCN}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2SeCN}$ ethyl selenocyanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05574 "selenols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05574 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSeH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05575 "selenones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05575 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2Se(=O)2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05576 "selenonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05576 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSe(=O)2OH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05577 "selenoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05577 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2Se=O}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05578 "self-absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05578 "Occurs in emission sources of finite thickness when radiant energy quanta emitted by atoms (or molecules) are absorbed by atoms of the same kind present in the same source. The absorbed energy is usually dissipated by collisional transfer of energy, or through emission of radiant energy of the same or other frequencies. In consequence, the observed radiant intensity of a spectral line (or band component) emitted by a source may be less than the radiant intensity would be from an optically thin source having the same number of emitting atoms. Self-absorption may occur in all emitting sources to some degree, whether they are homogeneous or not.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05579 "self-absorption broadening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05579 "Photons emitted in one region of a source are partly absorbed in their passage through the plasma. Because of the fact that the absorption profile is of the same shape as the emission profile, energy is selectively absorbed from the emission line, i.e. the absorption coefficient is a maximum at the centre of the line or central wavelength. The actual line profile is changed as a result of the lowering of the maximum intensity accompanied by a corresponding increase in apparent halfwidth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05580 "self-absorption effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05580 "The reabsorption of luminescence by the analyte and interfering impurities within the excitation volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05581 "self-absorption factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05581 "The ratio between the quantity of the radiation emitted by the source and the quantity of the radiation as produced by the radioactive nuclei present in the source. Synonymous with source efficiency.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05581 "source efficiency")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05582 "self-diffusion coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05582 "The diffusion coefficient \\(D_{i}^{*}\\) of species \\(i\\) in the absence of a chemical potential gradient. It is related to the diffusion coefficient \\(D_{i}\\) by \\[D_{i}^{*}=D_{i}\\ \\frac{\\partial (\\ln c_{i})}{\\partial \\ln a_{i}}\\] where \\(a_{i}\\) is the activity of \\(i\\) in the solution, and \\(c_{i}\\) is the concentration of \\(i\\). If an isotopically labelled species (\\(i^{*}\\)) is used to study diffusion, the tracer diffusion coefficient, \\(D_{i}^{*}\\), is practically identical to the self-diffusion coefficient provided that the isotope effect is sufficiently small.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05583))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05583 "self inductance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05583 "For a thin conducting loop, the magnetic flux through the loop, caused by an electric current in the loop, divided by that current.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05584))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05584 "self-poisoning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05584 "A product of a reaction may cause poisoning or inhibition. The phenomenon is called self-poisoning or autopoisoning.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05584 "autopoisoning")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05585 "self-quenching")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05585 "Quenching of an excited atom or molecular entity by interaction with another atom or molecular entity of the same species in the ground state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05586 "self-reversal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05586 "A case of self-absorption, when a line is self-absorbed to such an extent that the peak or central wavelength intensity is less than at the wings or non-central wavelengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05587 "self-shielding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05587 "The lowering of the flux density in the inner part of an object due to absorption in its outer layers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05588 "selones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05588 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=Se}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). They are thus selenium analogues of ketones.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05589 "semicarbazones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05589 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=NNHC(=O)NH2}$, formally derived by condensation of aldehydes or ketones with semicarbazide $\\ce{[NH2NHC(=O)NH2]}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05590 "semicoke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05590 "A carbonaceous material intermediate between a fusible mesophase pitch and a non-deformable green coke produced by incomplete carbonization at temperatures between the onset of fusion (of coal, ca. \\(620\\ \\rm{K}\\)), and complete devolatilization. Semicoke still contains volatile matter, therefore.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05591 "semiconductor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05591 "Material whose conductivity, due to charges of both signs, is normally in the range between that of metals and insulators and in which the electric charge carrier density can be changed by external means.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05592 "semiconductor detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05592 "A radiation detector using a semiconductor, in which free electric charges are produced along the path of an ionizing particle, in combination with a high voltage and electrodes to collect the induced electric charges.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05597 "semiconductor-metal transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05597 "Any transition from a semiconductor to a metallic state under the influence of a temperature or pressure change or both. Examples:$\\ce{Ti2O3}$: band-edge crossing in a semiconductor to semimetal transition.SmS: localized level crossing a band edge. In SmS hydrostatic pressure above the critical pressure \\(P_{\\rm{c}}\\approx 0.65\\ \\mathrm{GPa}\\) broadens the Sm-5d band to make the band edge cross the Sm-\\(4\\rm{f}^{6}\\) level.A localized-itinerant transition.A switching transition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05598 "semi-interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05598 "Polymer comprising one or more polymer networks and one or more linear or branched polymers characterized by the penetration on a molecular scale of at least one of the networks by at least some of the linear or branched macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05599 "semioxamazones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05599 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=NNHC(=O)C(=O)NH2}$, formally derived from the condensation of aldehydes or ketones with semioxamazide (the hydrazide of oxamic acid), $\\ce{H2NNHC(=O)C(=O)NH2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05600 "semiquinones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05600 "Radical anions having the structure $\\ce{O^{−}–Z–O^{.}}$ where $\\ce{Z}$ is an ortho- or para-arylene group or analogous heteroarylene group; they are formally generated by the addition of an electron to a quinone.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05601 "semisystematic name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05601 "A name in which at least one part is used in a systematic sense.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05601 "semitrivial name")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05602 "seniority")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05602 "Terms used in reference to priority in a prescribed hierarchical order, a senior feature being preferred.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05603 "sensitive area")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05603 "That area of the detector where an incident radiant power results in a measurable output.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05604))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05604 "sensitive volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05604 "That volume of the detector where an incident radiant power produces a measurable output.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05605))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05605 "sensitivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05605 "Two different measures of sensitivity are recommended. The first, which is suitable for relatively involatile materials as well as gases, depends upon the observed change in ion current for a particular amount or change of flow rate of sample though the ion source. A second method of stating sensitivity, that is most suitable for gases, depends upon the change of ion current related to the change of partial pressure of the sample in the ion source. It is important that the relevant experimental conditions corresponding to sensitivity measurement should always be stated. These include in a typical case details of the instrument type, bombarding electron current, slit dimensions, angular collimation, gain of the multiplier detector, scan speed and whether the measured signal corresponds to a single mass peak or to the ion beam integrated over all masses. Some indication of the time involved in the determination should be given, e.g. counting time or band width. The sensitivity should be differentiated from the detection limit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05606 "sensitivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05606 "The slope of the calibration curve. If the curve is in fact a 'curve', rather than a straight line, then of course sensitivity will be a function of analyte concentration or amount. If sensitivity is to be a unique performance characteristic, it must depend only on the chemical measurement process, not upon scale factors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05608 "sensitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05608 "Addition of small amounts of a hydrophilic colloid to a hydrophobic sol to possibly make the latter more sensitive to flocculation by electrolyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05609 "sensitized luminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05609 "The deactivation of the primarily excited emitter which can lead to the activation of the quencher followed by radiative deactivation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05611))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05611 "separability assumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05611 "This expression refers to the assumption, essential to conventional transition-state theory, that the energy of the system may be expressed as the sum of components associated with different degrees of freedom. In transition-state theory it is assumed that the energy of the motion of the system through the dividing surface of the potential-energy surface is separable into various components. In many practical calculations it is assumed that the energy of the system is separable into electronic, vibrational, rotational and translational energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05612 "separated flame")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05612 "In a premix burner, sometimes provision is made to screen the observed portion of the flame gases from direct contact with the surrounding air. This may be done either mechanically by placing a tube on top of the burner around the flame, which produces a zonal separation (separated flame), or aerodynamically by surrounding the flame with a sheath of inert gas that emerges from openings at the rim of the burner top (shielded flame).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05614 "separation factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05614 "The relative retention value calculated for two adjacent peaks (\\(V_{\\rm{R}2}^{'}>V_{\\rm{R}1}^{'}\\)): \\[\\alpha =\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}2}^{'}}{V_{\\rm{R}1}^{'}}=\\frac{V_{\\rm{N}2}}{V_{\\rm{N}1}}=\\frac{t_{\\rm{R}2}^{'}}{t_{\\rm{R}1}^{'}}=\\frac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}\\] By definition, the value of the separation factor is always greater than unity. The separation factor is also identical to the ratio of the corresponding distribution constants. The separation factor is sometimes also called the 'selectivity'. The use of this expression is discouraged.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05614 "separation coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05615 "separation factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05615 "The ratio of the respective distribution ratios of two extractable solutes measured under the same conditions. \\[\\alpha_{\\rm{A,B}} = \\frac{D_{\\rm{A}}}{D_{\\rm{B}}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05616 "separation number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05616 "This expresses the number of peaks which can be resolved in a given part of the chromatogram between the peaks of two consecutive n-alkanes with \\(z\\) and (\\(z+1\\)) carbon atoms in their molecules: \\[\\rm{SN} = \\frac{t_{\\rm{R}\\left(z\\,+\\,1\\right)}- t_{\\rm{R}z}}{w_{\\rm{h}z}\\,+\\,w_{\\rm{h}\\left(z+1\\right)}} - 1\\] In the German literature the symbol \\(\\rm{TZ}\\) (trennzahl) is commonly used to express the separation number. As the separation number depends on the n-alkanes used for the calculation, they always must be specified with any given \\(\\rm{SN}\\) value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05618 "separation temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05618 "The temperature of the chromatographic bed under isothermal operation. In column chromatography it is called the column temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05620 "sequencing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05620 "Analytical procedures for the determination of the order of amino acids in a polypeptide chain or of nucleotides in a DNA or RNA molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05621 "sequential analyser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05621 "A discontinuous analyser in which at least one sub-assembly operates sequentially.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05622 "sequential indication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05622 "An indication obtained following sequential sampling or received from a sequential cell or from data processing comprising a succession of predetermined repetitive operations (or a combination of the three).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05623 "sequential measuring cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05623 "A measuring cell which operates according to a succession of operations on the sample or on the sensitive elements (or on both), these operations being carried out according to one or more repetitive programs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05624 "sequential sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05624 "Units, increments or samples taken one at a time or in successive predetermined groups, until the cumulative result of their measurements (typically applied to attributes), as assessed against predetermined limits, permits a decision to accept or reject the population or to continue sampling. The number of observations required is not determined in advance, but the decision to terminate the operation depends, at each stage, on the results of the previous observations. The plan may have a practical, automatic termination after a certain number of units have been examined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05625 "sequential spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05625 "A spectrometer which enables the intensity of several spectral bands of radiation to be measured one after the other in time, i.e. sequentially.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05626 "series")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05626 "A number of measured values (\\(x_{1},\\,x_{2},\\,...\\ x_{i},\\,...\\ x_{n}\\)) equivalent to each other with respect to statistical considerations, e.g. the results of repeated analyses using only one analytical method on a substance that is presumed to be homogeneous.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05627))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05627 "sesquiterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05627 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C15}$ skeleton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05628))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05628 "sesterterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05628 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C25}$ skeleton. Sometimes erroneously referred to as sesterpenoids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05629))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05629 "settling chamber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05629 "Chamber designed to reduce the velocity of gases in order to permit the settling out of fly ash. It may be either part of, adjacent to, or external to an incinerator.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05630))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05630 "settling error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05630 "An error caused by allowing insufficient time for the reading to settle or if an absorption peak is scanned too rapidly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05631 "settling velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05631 "The terminal rate of fall of a particle through a fluid as induced by gravity or other external force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05632 "shape selectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05632 "Possibly observed in catalysts with very small pores. The selectivity is largely determined by the bulk or size of one or more reactants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05634 "shear dependent viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05634 "For systems showing non-Newtonian behaviour when measured in steady simple shear, a coefficient \\(\\eta \\) equal to \\(\\frac{\\sigma}{D}\\) at a given value of the shear rate \\(D\\), where \\(\\sigma \\) is the stress; \\(\\eta _{0}\\) is the limiting viscosity at zero shear rate, and \\(\\eta _{\\infty }\\) the limiting viscosity at infinite shear rate; \\(\\rm{[}\\eta _{0}\\rm{]}\\) is the limit of intrinsic viscosity at zero shear.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05635))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05635 "shear modulus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05635 "Shear stress divided by shear strain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05636))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05636 "shear rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05636 "The velocity gradient in a flowing fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05637))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05637 "shear strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05637 "Displacement of one surface with respect to another divided by the distance between them.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05638 "shear stress")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05638 "Force acting tangentially to a surface divided by the area of the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05640 "shear thinning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05640 "If viscosity is a univalued function of the rate of shear, a decrease of the viscosity with increasing rate of shear is called shear thinning, and an increase of the viscosity shear thickening.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05641))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05641 "shear transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05641 "A diffusionless transition that involves a change of the shape of the unit cell by a process that can be described as shear.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05642))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05642 "shear viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05642 """For a Newtonian fluid, the shear viscosity is often termed simply viscosity since in most situations it is the only one considered. It relates the shear components of stress and those of rate of strain at a point in the fluid by: \\[\\sigma _{xy}=\\sigma _{yx}=\\eta \\ (\\frac{\\partial 
u_{x}}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial 
u_{y}}{\\partial x})=2\\ \\eta \\ \\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\gamma }_{yx}\\] where \\(\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\gamma }_{yx}\\), the shear component of rate of strain is defined as follows: \\[\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\gamma }_{yx}=\\frac{1}{2}\\ (\\frac{\\partial 
u_{x}}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial 
u_{y}}{\\partial x})\\] Corresponding relations hold for \\(\\sigma _{xz}\\) and \\(\\sigma _{yz}\\); \\(\\sigma _{xy}\\) is the component of stress acting in the \\(y\\)-direction on a plate normal to the \\(x\\)-axis; \\(
u_{x}\\), \\(
u_{y}\\), \\(
u_{z}\\) are the components of velocity.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05644))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05644 "shielding")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05644 "In the context of NMR spectroscopy shielding is the effect of the electron shells of the observed and the neighbouring nuclei on the external magnetic field. The external field induces circulations in the electron cloud. The resulting magnetic moment is oriented in the opposite direction to the external field, so that the local field at the central nucleus is weakened, although it may be strengthened at other nuclei (deshielding). The phenomenon is the origin of the structural dependence of the resonance frequencies of the nuclei.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05645 "shielding constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05645 "In NMR the difference between the external magnetic flux density and the local magnetic flux density at a resonating nucleus affected by the neighbouring electrons divided by the external flux density, \\(\\sigma =\\frac{B_{0}\\,-\\,B}{B_{0}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05646))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05646 "shish-kebab structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05646 "A polycrystalline morphology of double habit consisting of fibrous crystals overgrown epitaxially by lamellar crystals, the stems of which are parallel to the fibre axis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05647))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05647 "short chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05647 "A chain of low relative molecular mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05649))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05649 "short-range intramolecular interactions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05649 "A steric or other interaction involving atoms or groups or both situated nearby in sequence along the chain. The interacting atoms or groups are typically separated by fewer than ten consecutive bonds in a chain. If no confusion can occur, the word 'intramolecular' may be omitted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05650 "shut-down state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05650 "The condition of an instrument when it is switched off to conserve energy or reagents or to protect working parts. This term is of particular importance in clinical chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05651 "shut-down time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05651 "The time interval between production of the last result of an instrument and shut-down state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05652))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05652 "shuttle vector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05652 "A DNA molecule (e.g. plasmid) that is able to replicate in two different host organisms and can therefore be used to 'shuttle' or convey genes from one to the other.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05653 "SI")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05653 "Système International d'Unités, the international system of units established in 1960 and based on seven base units for the quantities: length, time, mass, electric current, thermodynamic temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05653 "International system of units")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05653 "Système International d'Unités")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05657))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05657 "siemens")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05657 "SI derived unit of electric conductance, \\(\\pu{S} = \\pu{\\Omega^{-1}} = \\pu{m-2 kg-1 s3 A2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05658 "sievert")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05658 "SI derived unit of dose equivalent (the mean specific energy imparted to an element of matter corrected by a quality factor and a modifying factor to take into account the properties of irradiated matter) equal to one joule per kilogram and admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05660 "sigmatropic rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05660 "A molecular rearrangement that involves both the creation of a new $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between atoms previously not directly linked and the breaking of an existing $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$. There is normally a concurrent relocation of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ in the molecule concerned, but the total number of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ does not change. The term was originally restricted to intramolecular pericyclic reactions, and many authors use it with this connotation. It is, however, also applied in a more general, purely structural, sense. If such reactions are intramolecular, their transition state may be visualized as an association of two fragments connected at their termini by two partial $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$, one being broken and the other being formed as, for example, the two allyl fragments in ($\\ce{a'}$). Considering only atoms within the (real or hypothetical) cyclic array undergoing reorganization, if the numbers of these in the two fragments are designated \\(i\\) and \\(j\\), then the rearrangement is said to be a sigmatropic change of order [\\(i\\), \\(j\\)] (conventionally \\(\\rm{[}i\\rm{]}\\leq \\rm{[}j\\rm{]}\\)). Thus the rearrangement (a) is of order, whilst reaction (b) is asigmatropic shift of hydrogen. (N.B. By convention square brackets here refer to numbers of atoms, in contrast with current usage in the context of cycloaddition.)   The descriptors a and s (antarafacial and antarafacial) may also be annexed to the numbers \\(i\\) and \\(j\\); (b) is then described as a [1s,5s] sigmatropic rearrangement, since it is suprafacial with respect both to the hydrogen atom and to the pentadienyl system:  The prefix 'homo' (meaning one extra atom, interrupting conjugation — cf. 'homoaromaticity') has frequently been applied to sigmatropic rearrangements, but is misleading.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05661))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05661 "signal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05661 "A representation of a quantity within an analytical instrument.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05663 "silanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05663 "Saturated silicon hydrides, analogues of the alkanes; i.e. compounds of the general formula $\\ce{SinH2_{n+2}}$. Silanes may be subdivided into silane, oligosilanes and polysilanes. Note hydrocarbyl derivatives and other derivatives are often referred to loosely as silanes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05664))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05664 "silanols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05664 "In a strict sense, hydroxy derivatives of silanes $\\ce{Si_{n}H_{2n+1}OH}$.A name commonly applied to Si-hydrocarbyl derivatives, $\\ce{R3SiOH}$, of silanol, $\\ce{H3SiOH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05665 "silasesquiazanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05665 "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three nitrogen atoms and every nitrogen atom is linked to two silicon atoms, thus consisting of $\\ce{SiH}$ and $\\ce{NH}$ units, and having the general formula $\\ce{(SiH)_{2n}(NH)_{3n}}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05666 "silasesquioxanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05666 "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three oxygen atoms and every oxygen atom is linked to two silicon atoms, and having the general formula $\\ce{(SiH)_{2n}O_{3n}}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05667))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05667 "silasesquithianes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05667 "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three sulfur atoms and every sulfur atom is linked to two silicon atoms, and having the general formula $\\ce{(SiH)_{2n}S_{3n}}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05668 "silathianes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05668 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{H3Si[SSiH2]_{n}SSiH3}$ and branched-chain analogues. They are analogous in structure to siloxanes with $\\ce{–S\\bond{–}}$ replacing $\\ce{–O\\bond{–}}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05669))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05669 "silazanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05669 "Saturated silicon-nitrogen hydrides, having straight or branched chains. They are analogous in structure to siloxanes with $\\ce{–NH\\bond{–}}$ replacing $\\ce{–O\\bond{–}}$, e.g. $\\ce{H3SiNHSiH2NHSiH3}$ trisilazane. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05670 "silicones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05670 """Polymeric or oligomeric siloxanes, usually considered unbranched, of general formula $\\ce{[–OSiR2–]_{n}}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05671 "siloxanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05671 "Saturated silicon-oxygen hydrides with unbranched or branched chains of alternating silicon and oxygen atoms (each silicon atom is separated from its nearest silicon neighbours by single oxygen atoms). The general structure of unbranched siloxanes is $\\ce{H3Si[OSiH2]_{n}OSiH3}$. $\\ce{H3Si[OSiH2]_{n}OSiH[OSiH2OSiH3]2}$ is an example of a branched siloxane. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05672))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05672 "silver film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05672 "When viewed in reflected white light against a black background, transparent films show the classical interference colours of thin plates which permit an estimate of their thickness to be made. When of the order of \\(100\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (\\(1000\\ \\unicode{x212B}\\)) in thickness they appear white (silver film) and when thinner they appear gradually less intensely white, then grey and finally black.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05673 "silylene")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05673 "Generic name for $\\ce{H2Si{:}}$ and substitution derivatives thereof, containing an electrically neutral bivalent silicon atom with two non-bonding electrons. (The definition is analogous to that given for carbene.)The silanediyl group ($\\ce{H2Si}$), analogous to the methylene group ($\\ce{H2C}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05674 "silyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05674 "In a strict sense, the silyl group is $\\ce{H3Si\\bond{–}}$.A name commonly applied to hydrocarbyl derivatives of the silyl group $\\ce{R3Si\\bond{–}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05675))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05675 "silyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05675 "In a strict sense, the silyl radical is $\\ce{H3Si^{.}}$.A name commonly applied to the silicon-centered radicals $\\ce{R3Si^{.}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05676 "simple shear")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05676 "An idealized treatment of a fluid between two large parallel plates (to permit ignoring edge effects) of area \\(A\\), separated by a distance \\(h\\). If one plate moves relative to the other with a constant velocity \\(V\\), requiring a force \\(F\\) acting in the direction of movement, and the density, pressure, and viscosity throughout the fluid are constant, the Newtonian equation can be coupled with the equations of motion and of continuity to show that the velocity gradient in the fluid is constant (= \\(\\frac{V}{h}\\)), and that \\(\\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{\\eta \\ V}{h}\\). This idealized case (simple shear) is sometimes used to define shear viscosity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05677))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05677 "simulation technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05677 "A technique for reducing or eliminating analytical errors resulting from interferences, using a reference solution sufficiently similar in quantitative composition to the sample solutions to be analysed that the interferences in the reference and sample solution are equivalent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05679 "simultaneous pair transitions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05679 "Simultaneous electronic transitions in two coupled absorbers or emitters. Because of the coupling, transitions which are spin-forbidden in one of the centres might become spin-allowed (spin flip).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05680))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05680 "simultaneous reactions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05680 "Composite reactions, in which processes like \\[\\rm{A}\\ \\overset{1}{\\rightarrow }\\ \\rm{Y}\\] and \\[\\rm{B}\\ \\overset{2}{\\rightarrow }\\ \\rm{Z}\\] occur in parallel, can be called simultaneous or parallel reactions. Sometimes there is competition involved, like in the scheme: \\[\\rm{A}\\,+\\,\\rm{B}\\ \\overset{1}{\\rightarrow }\\ \\rm{Y}\\] and \\[\\rm{A}\\,+\\,\\rm{C}\\ \\overset{2}{\\rightarrow }\\ \\rm{Z}\\] where B and C compete with one another for A.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05681 "simultaneous spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05681 "A spectrometer which has more than one detector and enables the intensities of several spectral bands to be measured at the same time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05682 "single-beam spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05682 "A luminescence spectrometer in which a single beam of radiation is used for excitation and a single beam of luminescence radiation is taken from the sample.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05682 "single-beam luminescence spectrometer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05683))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05683 "single cell protein")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05683 "Microbial biomass or proteins extracted therefrom obtained from processes in which bacteria, yeasts, other fungi or algae are cultivated in large quantities as human or animal protein supplement in animal feed or in human nutrition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05685 "single-electron transfer mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05685 "A reaction mechanism characterized by the transfer of a single electron between the species occurring on the reaction coordinate of one of the elementary steps.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05686))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05686 "single escape peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05686 "In a $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}\\rm{ray\\ spectrum}$, the peak due to pair production in the detector and escape, from the sensitive part of the detector, of one of the photons of \\(511\\ \\rm{keV}\\) resulting from annihilation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05687))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05687 "single-focusing mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05687 "An instrument in which an ion beam with a given value of mass/charge is brought to a focus although the initial directions of the ions diverge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05689))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05689 "single-photon timing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05689 "Technique that permits recovery of the parameters characterizing a fluorescence decay after pulse excitation (in particular excited-states lifetimes). It is based on the creation of a time histogram of many stochastic events involving the time delay between the electronic excitation of a molecule or material and its emission of a photon from an excited state. A key to the technique is that no more than one photon strike the detector per pulsed excitation. Excitation is commonly achieved with a flash from a repetitive nanosecond lamp or diode laser or a CW operated laser (mode-locked laser). The essential components of the hardware are a device to measure the excitation-emission delay time and another to determine the relative frequency of photons reaching the detector at each delay time. Delay times are usually measured with a time-to-amplitude-converter (TAC), using voltage to measure the delay between a start and a stop signal. The frequency of events with each delay is stored in a multi-channel analyser. This term is preferred to time-correlated single-photon counting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05690))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05690 "single scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05690 "Radiation scattering in which there is only one scattering encounter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05691 "single-step reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05691 "A reaction that proceeds through a single transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05692 "single-strand chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05692 "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through two atoms, one on each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05693 "single-strand macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05693 "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through two atoms, one on each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05694))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05694 "single-strand polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05694 "A polymer the macromolecules of which are single-strand macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05695 "singlet molecular oxygen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05695 "Oxygen molecule (dioxygen), $\\ce{O2}$, in an excited singlet state. The ground state of $\\ce{O2}$ is a triplet (\\(^{3}\\Sigma_{\\rm{g}}^{-}\\)). The two metastable singlet states derived from the ground-state configuration are \\(^{1}\\Delta_{\\rm{g}}\\) and \\(^{1}\\Sigma_{\\rm{g}}^{+}\\) (the latter with the higher energy).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05695 "singlet molecular dioxygen")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05696 "singlet-singlet absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05696 "Absorption which results in the transition from the singlet ground state of the molecule into singlet excited states (\\(\\rm{S}_{0}\\) → \\(\\rm{S}_{\\rm{n}}\\)) and leads to the UV/visible absorption spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05698))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05698 "singlet-singlet energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05698 "Transfer of excitation from an electronically excited donor in a singlet state to produce an electronically excited acceptor in a singlet state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05699 "singlet state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05699 "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to 0.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05700 "singlet-triplet absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05700 "Absorption which takes place with the transition from the singlet ground state of the molecule to triplet states (\\(\\rm{S}_{0}\\) → \\(\\rm{T}_{\\rm{n}}\\)) and results in the singlet-triplet absorption spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05701))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05701 "singlet-triplet energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05701 "Transfer of excitation from an electronically excited donor in a singlet state to produce an electronically excited acceptor in a triplet state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05702))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05702 "singly labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05702 "A specifically labelled compound in which the isotopically substituted molecule has only one isotopically modified atom e.g. $\\ce{CH3–CH[^{2}H]–OH}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05703 "sink")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05703 "The receptor for material which is removed from the atmosphere. Because of long range transport of many pollutants such as $\\ce{SO2}$, sulfuric acid and its salts, the sink region can be many hundreds of kilometres from the source region of the pollutants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05704))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05704 "sintering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05704 "Temperature-induced coalescence and densification of porous solid particles below the melting points of their major components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05705))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05705 "size-exclusion chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05705 "A separation technique in which separation mainly according to the hydrodynamic volume of the molecules or particles takes place in a porous non-adsorbing material with pores of approximately the same size as the effective dimensions in solution of the molecules to be separated.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05706))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05706 "skeletal atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05706 "An atom of a skeletal structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05707))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05707 "skeletal bond")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05707 "A bond connecting two skeletal atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05708))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05708 "skeletal structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05708 "The sequence of atoms in the constitutional unit(s) of a macromolecule, an oligomer molecule, a block or a chain which defines the essential topological representation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05710 "Slater-type orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05710 "An approximate atomic orbital attempts to allow for electron-electron repulsion by scaling the nuclear charge for each orbital.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05712))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05712 "slow neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05712 "Neutrons of kinetic energy less than some specified value. This value may vary over a wide range and depends on the application. In reactor physics, the value is frequently chosen to be \\(1\\ \\rm{eV}\\); in dosimetry, the effective cadmium cut-off is used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05713))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05713 "small particle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05713 "A particle much smaller than the wavelength of the radiation in the medium. In practice, all dimensions of a particle considered small must be less than about one-twentieth of the wavelength employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05715 "SM-interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05715 "Interference by a substance that produces a signal by a similar mechanism to the analyte, which in the given procedure (including the apparatus used) can not be distinguished from the signal given by the analyte. For example, in a colour reaction with an organic ligand by also reacting with the ligand and producing a coloured compound absorbing at the same wavelength as the compound produced by the analyte or in an atomic spectrochemical analysis by absorbing or emitting at the same wavelength as the analyte.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05716 "smog")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05716 "The term originated in Great Britain as a popular derivation of 'smoke-fog' and appears to have been in common use before World War 1. It originally referred to the heavy pollution derived largely from coal burning (largely smoke filled air, rich in sulfur dioxide), and it probably was largely a reducing atmosphere. More common today in cities is an oxidizing atmosphere which contains ozone and other oxidants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05717))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05717 "smog chamber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05717 "A large confined volume in which sunlight or simulated sunlight is allowed to irradiate air mixtures of atmospheric trace gases (hydrocarbons, nitrogen oxides, sulfur dioxide, etc.) which undergo oxidation. In theory these chambers allow the controlled study of complex reactions which occur in the atmosphere. However, ill-defined wall reactions which generate some molecular and radical species (e.g. $\\ce{HONO}$, $\\ce{CH2O}$, $\\ce{HO}$-radicals, etc.) and remove certain products ($\\ce{H2O2}$, $\\ce{HNO3}$, etc.), the use of reactant concentrations well above those in the atmosphere, ill-defined light intensities and wavelength distribution within the chamber, and other factors peculiar to chamber experiments require that caution be exercised in the extrapolation of results obtained from them to atmospheric systems.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05718))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05718 "smog index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05718 "A mathematical correlation between smog and meteorological and/or pollutant concentrations associated with it. These are qualitative indices which are sometimes used in some urban communities to predict the degree of the air pollution which is expected for the coming day.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05719))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05719 "smoke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05719 "An aerosol originating from combustion, thermal decomposition or thermal evaporation. Its particles may be solid (magnesium oxide smoke) or liquid (tobacco smoke).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05721))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05721 "soap")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05721 "A salt of a fatty acid, saturated or unsaturated, containing at least eight carbon atoms or a mixture of such salts. A neat soap is a lamellar structure containing much (e.g. $\\pu{75\\%}$) soap and little (e.g. $\\pu{25\\%}$) water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05722 "soap curd")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05722 "A gel-like mixture of fibrous soap-crystals ('curd-fibres') and their saturated solution (not a mesomorphic phase).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05723 "soap film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05723 "A term established by usage for A/W/A films stabilized by surfactants although it is not a film of soap, nor is the stabilizing surfactant necessarily a soap. (A/W/A means water film in air).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05726))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05726 "soiling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05726 "Visible damage to materials by deposition of air pollutants.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05727))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05727 "sol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05727 "A fluid colloidal system of two or more components, e.g. a protein sol, a gold sol, an emulsion, a surfactant solution above the critical micelle concentration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05728 "solar conversion efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05728 "The ratio of the Gibbs energy gain per unit time per \\(\\mathrm{m}^{2}\\) of surface exposed to the sun and the solar irradiance, \\(E\\), integrated between \\(\\lambda = 0\\) and \\(\\lambda = \\infty \\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05729 "solar flare")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05729 "A bright eruption of energy from the sun's chromosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05730 "solar radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05730 "The electromagnetic radiation emitted by the sun. The total range of wavelengths of light emitted by the sun (99.9% in the range from \\(150\\ \\rm{to}\\ 4000\\ \\rm{nm}\\)) is filtered on entering the earth's atmosphere, largely through the absorption by oxygen, ozone, water vapour and carbon dioxide. Near sea level only light of wavelengths longer than about \\(290\\ \\rm{nm}\\) is present. The light from \\(290{-}400\\ \\rm{nm}\\) is effective in inducing important photochemical processes since absorption by the important trace gases, ozone, nitrogen dioxide, aldehydes, ketones, etc., is significant in this region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05731 "sol-gel transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05731 "Transition of a sol to a gel at the gel point.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05732 "solid angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05732 "Of a cone, the ratio of the area cut out on a spherical surface (with its centre at the apex of that cone) to the square of the radius of the sphere. It is a quantity of dimension one (dimensionless quantity) with unit steradian (\\(\\mathrm{sr}=1\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05733))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05733 "solidification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05733 "The transition of a liquid or gas into a solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05734))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05734 "solid phase antibody radioimmunoassay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05734 "A kind of radioimmunoassay employing an antibody bound to a solid phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05736))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05736 "solid state lasers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05736 "CW or pulsed lasers in which the active medium is a solid matrix (crystal or glass) doped with an ion (e.g. $\\ce{Nd^{3+}}$, $\\ce{Cr^{3+}}$, $\\ce{Eu^{3+}}$). The emitted wavelength depends on the active ion, the selected optical transition, and the matrix. Some of these lasers are tunable within a very broad range (e.g. from \\(700\\) to \\(1000\\ \\rm{nm}\\) for $\\ce{Ti^{3+}}$ doped sapphire). Pulsed lasers may be free-running, Q-switched, or mode-locked. Some CW lasers may be mode-locked.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05737))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05737 "solid support")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05737 "Normally an inert porous solid, which sorbs the liquid phase. The particle-size range of the support affects column efficiency and the pressure differential necessary to achieve a given flow rate. Modifications have been introduced for the achievement of special separations, in which the solid support is not inert but is an active solid. In capillary columns the inner wall of the column serves as the solid support and obviates the use of additional porous solids for this purpose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05738))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05738 "solidus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05738 "A line on a binary phase diagram (or a surface on a ternary phase diagram) that indicates the temperature at which a system becomes completely solid on cooling or at which melting begins on heating under equilibrium conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05739))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05739 "solid volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05739 "The volume occupied by the solid or the active solid in the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05740 "solubility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05740 "The analytical composition of a saturated solution, expressed in terms of the proportion of a designated solute in a designated solvent, is the solubility of that solute. The solubility may be expressed as a concentration, molality, mole fraction, mole ratio, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05742))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05742 "solubility product")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05742 "The product of the ion activities raised to appropriate powers of an ionic solute in its saturated solution expressed with due reference to the dissociation equilibria involved and the ions present.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05744 "solute")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05744 "The minor component of a solution which is regarded as having been dissolved by the solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05745))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05745 "solute-volatilization interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05745 "Interference due to changes in the volatilization rate of the dry aerosol particles in the case when volatilization of the analyte is incomplete in the presence and/or absence of the concomitant. This interference can either be specific, if the analyte and interferent form a new phase of different thermostability, as when $\\ce{Mg}$ and $\\ce{Al}$ form $\\ce{MgAl2O4}$ in an air–acetylene flame, or non-specific, if the analyte is simply dispersed in a large excess of the interferent, as when $\\ce{Ag}$ is dispersed in $\\ce{ThO2}$. If the interferent has a high boiling point, this latter is sometimes referred to as a blocking interference. It is often difficult to make sharp distinctions between the specific and non-specific solute-volatilization interferences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05746 "solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05746 "A liquid or solid phase containing more than one substance, when for convenience one (or more) substance, which is called the solvent, is treated differently from the other substances, which are called solutes. When, as is often but not necessarily the case, the sum of the mole fractions of solutes is small compared with unity, the solution is called a dilute solution. A superscript attached to the \\(\\infty \\) symbol for a property of a solution denotes the property in the limit of infinite dilution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05747 "solvation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05747 "Any stabilizing interaction of a solute (or solute moiety) and the solvent or a similar interaction of solvent with groups of an insoluble material (i.e. the ionic groups of an ion-exchange resin). Such interactions generally involve electrostatic forces and van der Waals forces, as well as chemically more specific effects such as hydrogen bond formation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05748 "solvatochromic relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05748 "A linear free-energy relationship based on solvatochromism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05749 "solvatochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05749 "The (pronounced) change in position and sometimes intensity of an electronic absorption or emission band, accompanying a change in the polarity of the medium. Negative (positive) solvatochromism corresponds to a hypsochromic (bathochromic) shift with increasing solvent polarity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05751 "solvent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05751 "The term applied to the whole initial liquid phase containing the extractant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05752 "solvent extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05752 "The process of transferring a substance from any matrix to an appropriate liquid phase. If the substance is initially present as a solute in an immiscible liquid phase the process is synonymous with liquid-liquid extraction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05753 "solvent front")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05753 "The front line of the eluent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05755 "solvent isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05755 "A kinetic or equilibrium isotope effect resulting from change in the isotopic composition of the solvent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05756))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05756 "solvent migration-distance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05756 "The distance travelled by the solvent front.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05757))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05757 "solvent parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05757 "Quantitative measures of the capability of solvents for interaction with solutes. Such parameters have been based on numerous different physicochemical quantities, e.g. rate constants, solvatochromic shifts in ultraviolet/visible spectra, solvent-induced shifts in infrared frequencies, etc. Some solvent parameters are purely empirical in nature, i.e. they are based directly on some experimental measurement. It may be possible to interpret such a parameter as measuring some particular aspect of solvent–solute interaction or it may be regarded simply as a measure of solvent polarity. Other solvent parameters are based on analysing experimental results. Such a parameter is considered to quantify some particular aspect of solvent capability for interaction with solutes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05758))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05758 "solvent regeneration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05758 "Treatment of the solvent for re-cycling, e.g. by removal of degradation products or non-strippable solutes. The term solvent purification is synonymous, but the terms scrubbing, stripping and washing should not be used in this context.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05761))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05761 "solvent shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05761 "A shift in the frequency of a spectral band of a chemical species arising from interaction with its solvent environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05762))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05762 "solvolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05762 "Generally, reaction with a solvent, or with a lyonium ion or lyate ion involving the rupture of one or more bonds in the reacting solute. More specifically the term is used for substitution elimination and fragmentation reactions in which a solvent species is the nucleophile ('alcoholysis' if the solvent is an alcohol, etc.).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05763))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05763 "solvophobicity parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05763 "A solvent parameter defined by: \\[Sp = 1 - \\frac{M}{M\\left(\\rm{hexadecane}\\right)}\\] derived from the Gibbs energy of transfer (\\(\\Delta _{\\rm{t}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\)) of a series of solutes from water to numerous aqueous-organic mixtures and to pure solvents: \\[\\Delta _{\\rm{t}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\left(\\rm{to solvent}\\right) = M\\ R_{\\rm{T}}+D\\] where \\(R_{\\rm{T}}\\) is a solute parameter, and \\(M\\) and \\(D\\) characterize the solvent. The \\(M\\) values are used to define a solvent solvophobic effect so that \\(Sp\\) values are scaled from unity (water) to zero (hexadecane).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05764))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05764 "solvus")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05764 "A line on a binary phase diagram (or a surface on a ternary phase diagram) that defines the limit of solid solubility under equilibrium conditions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05765))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05765 "SOMO")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05765 "A Singly Occupied Molecular Orbital (such as the half-filled HOMO of a radical).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05766))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05766 "sonication")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05766 "Irradiation with (often ultra)sound waves, e.g. to increase the rate of a reaction or to prepare vesicles in mixtures of surfactants and water.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05767))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05767 "sonoluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05767 "Luminescence induced by sound waves.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05768))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05768 "soot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05768 "A randomly formed particulate carbon material and may be coarse, fine and/or colloidal in proportions depending on its origin. Soot consists of variable quantities of carbonaceous and inorganic solids together with absorbed and occluded tars and resins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05769))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05769 "sorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05769 "The process by which a substance (sorbate) is sorbed (adsorbed or absorbed) on or in another substance (sorbent).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05770))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05770 "sorption isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05770 "The concentration of a sorbed species in the ion exchanger, expressed as a function of its concentration in the external solution under specified conditions and at constant temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05771))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05771 "sorption techniques")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05771 "Techniques based on the distribution of components being separated between two phases, one of which is stationary and the other mobile. The advantage of some chromatographic methods is the possibility of combining the preconcentration and determination steps, as well as improving the speed of determination, and the possibility of separation of components with similar properties, and of achieving high values of the preconcentration coefficient. The methods enable analyses of microamounts of substances. ion-exchange chromatography is not widely used owing to the great volumes of solutions being treated and, consequently, to a great degree to the variation in the blank, and to some diffusional limitations. Static ion-exchange, which is much simpler and more readily carried out, is practised quite widely.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05772))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05772 "sorptive insertion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05772 "This is analogous to the process of ligand insertion in coordination chemistry. $$\\ce{H^{\\ast} + C2H4(g) -> ^{\\ast}C2H5}$$ This reaction might also be imagined to proceed by adsorption of $\\ce{C2H4}$ followed by ligand migration (an associative surface reaction).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05776))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05776 "space charge")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05776 "At the surface region of a semiconductor the surface excess or deficiency of electrons constitutes a space charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05777))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05777 "space formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05777 "Synonymous with stereochemical formula.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05778))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05778 "space time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05778 "The reciprocal of space velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05779))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05779 "space velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05779 "Defined as \\(v_{m}\\), \\(v_{v}\\), and \\(v_{a}\\) where the \\(v_{i}\\)s represent the rate of feed of the given reactant fed per unit mass, volume or surface area of the catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05780))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05780 "spark ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05780 "Occurs when a solid sample is vaporized and partially ionized by an intermittent discharge. It is recommended that the word 'source' be dropped from this term.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05780 "spark source ionization")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05781))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05781 "spatial-distribution interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05781 "Interference which may occur when changes in concentration of concomitants affect the mass flow rates or mass flow patterns of the analyte species in the flame. If they are caused by changes in volume and rise velocity of the gases formed by combustion, in extreme cases manifesting themselves by changes in the size and/or shape of the flame, they are non-specific and are called flame-geometry interferences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05782))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05782 "spatially resolving detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05782 "A detector for the measurement of the spatial distribution of the radiation. Spatially resolving detectors can be divided into two groups:   the photosensitive area consists of a matrix of discrete photosensitive elements, the pixels (picture elements) forming an array with the facility to separately read out the information, simultaneously or sequentially.   the photosensitive area consists of a single photosensitive element that must be scanned (e.g. image dissection tube.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05783))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05783 "special salt effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05783 "The initial steep rate increase observed in the kinetic electrolyte effect on certain solvolysis reactions, upon addition of some non-common ion salts, especially $\\ce{LiClO4}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05786))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05786 "species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05786 "A taxonomic subdivision of a genus; a group of closely related, morphologically and physiologically similar individuals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05787))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05787 "specific")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05787 "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by mass. Specific volume is the volume of a sample divided by its mass.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05787 "specific quantity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05788))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05788 "specific")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05788 "A term which expresses qualitatively the extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure. Specific is considered to be the ultimate of selective, meaning that no interferences are supposed to occur.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05789))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05789 "specific acid–base catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05789 "Catalysis by acids or bases in solution is said to be specific when the only observable catalytic effects are those due to the ions formed from the solvent itself (e.g. if when water is the solvent the only observable catalysis is that due to the $\\ce{H^{+}}$ and $\\ce{OH^{−}}$ ions).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05790))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05790 "specific activity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05790 "For a specified isotopes, or mixture of isotopes, the activity of a material divided by the mass of the material.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05791))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05791 "specific adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05791 "Ions become specifically adsorbed when short-range interactions between them and the interphase become important. They are believed then to penetrate into the inner layer and may (but not necessarily) come into contact with the surface. They are usually assumed to form a partial or complete monolayer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05792))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05792 "specifically absorbing ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05792 "Ions which possess a chemical affinity for the surface in addition to the Coulomb interaction, where chemical is a collective adjective, embracing all interactions other than purely Coulombic. Examples are van der Waals or hydrophobic bonding, pi-electron exchange and complex formation. Specifically adsorbing ions can adsorb on an initially uncharged surface and hence provide it with a charge. The term specifically adsorbed applies to the sorption of all other ions having an affinity to the surface in addition to the purely Coulombic contribution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05793))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05793 "specifically labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05793 "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as specifically labelled when a unique isotopically substituted compound is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound. In such a case, both position(s) and number of each labelling nuclide are defined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05794))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05794 "specifically labelled tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05794 "A tracer in which the label is present in a specified position.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05795))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05795 "specific burn-up")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05795 "The total energy released through induced nuclear transformations divided by the mass of a nuclear fuel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05796))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05796 "specific catalysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05796 "The acceleration of a reaction by a unique catalyst, rather than by a family of related substances. The term is most commonly used in connection with specific hydrogen-ion or hydroxide-ion (lyonium ion or lyate ion) catalysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05798))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05798 "specific detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05798 "A detector which responds to a single sample component or to a limited number of components having similar chemical characteristics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05800))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05800 "specific heat capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05800 "Heat capacity divided by mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05801))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05801 "specific ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05801 "The number of ion pairs formed per unit distance along the track of an ionizing particle passing through matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05802 "specific permeability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05802 "A term expressing the resistance of an empty tube or packed column to the flow of a fluid (the mobile phase).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05804))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05804 "specific pore volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05804 "The total internal void volume per unit mass of adsorbent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05806 "specific surface area")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05806 "When the area of the interface between two phases is proportional to the mass of one of the phases (e.g. for a solid adsorbent, for an emulsion or for an aerosol), the specific surface area (\\(a\\),\\(s\\) or preferably \\(a_{s}\\)) is defined as the surface area divided by the mass of the relevant phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05807))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05807 "specific volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05807 "Volume of a substance divided by its mass. It is the reciprocal of mass density.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05809))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05809 "specimen")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05809 "A specifically selected portion of a material taken from a dynamic system and assumed to be representative of the parent material at the time it is taken. Although the specimen may not be reproducible in time, e.g. it may be taken from a flowing stream or a portion of blood, no separable sampling error exists since this error is unavoidably included with the corresponding error of the estimate of the property, function or analyte being studied. A specimen may be considered as a special type of sample, taken primarily in time rather than in space. The term 'specimen' has been used both as a representative unit and as a nonrepresentative (often better than most) unit of a population, usually in clinical, biological and mineralogical collections. 'Collections' in this case is used as either a noun or verb. This usage is almost always self-evident, and thus would not be confused with a time-type sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05810))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05810 "spectator mechanism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05810 "A pre-association mechanism in which one of the molecular entities, C, is already present in an encounter pair with A during formation of B from A, but does not assist the formation of B, e.g.  The formation of B from A may itself be a bimolecular reaction with some other reagent. Since C does not assist the formation of A, it is described as being present as a spectator, and hence such a mechanism is sometimes referred to as a spectator mechanism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05811))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05811 "spectator-stripping reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05811 "An extreme type of stripping reaction in which one reaction product has almost the same direction and momentum as one of the reactant molecules had before the reactive collision occurred.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05812 "spectral bandwidth error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05812 "To measure the true shape, particularly the true maximum of an absorption band, the spectral bandwidth \\(\\Delta \\lambda \\) of the instrument must be much less than the width of the absorption band. A spectral bandwidth error results from using too large a bandwidth relative to the absorption band being measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05813))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05813 "spectral distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05813 "The variation of the spectral radiance with wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05814))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05814 "spectral effectiveness")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05814 "The reciprocal of the photon fluence rate, \\(E_{\\rm{p}}^{0}\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\), causing identical photoresponse, \\(\\Delta \\rm{y}\\), per unit time (\\(\\frac{\\Delta \\rm{y}}{\\Delta t}\\)). The effectiveness spectrum is directly proportional to the conversion spectrum of the sensory pigment, if spectral attenuance is negligible.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05814 "spectral photon effectiveness")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05814 "effectiveness")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05816))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05816 "spectral interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05816 "Interference due to the incomplete isolation of the radiation emitted or absorbed by the analyte from other radiation detected by the instrument. Its occurrence may be established by comparing the measures of the analyte-free blank solution and the solvent blank.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05817))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05817 "spectral irradiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05817 "Irradiance, \\(E\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-3}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05818))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05818 "spectral overlap")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05818 "In the context of radiative energy transfer, the integral, \\(J = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f_{\\rm{D}}^{\\prime}(\\sigma)\\ \\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}(\\sigma) {\\rm{d}}\\sigma \\), which measures the overlap of the emission spectrum of the excited donor, D, and the absorption spectrum of the ground state acceptor, A; \\(f_{\\rm{D}}^{'}\\) is the measured normalized emission of D, \\(f_{\\rm{D}}^{'}=\\frac{f_{\\rm{D}}\\left(\\sigma \\right)}{\\int _{0}^{\\infty }f_{\\rm{D}}\\left(\\sigma \\right)  \\ \\mathrm{d}\\sigma }\\), \\(f_{\\rm{D}}(\\sigma)\\) is the photon exitance of the donor at wavenumber \\(\\sigma \\), and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}(\\sigma)\\) is the decadic molar absorption coefficient of A at wavenumber \\(\\sigma \\). In the context of Förster excitation transfer, \\(J\\) is given by: \\[J = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{f_{\\rm{D}}^{^\\prime}(\\sigma)\\ \\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}(\\sigma)}{\\sigma^{4}} {\\rm{d}}\\sigma \\] In the context of Dexter excitation transfer, \\(J\\) is given by: \\[J = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f_{\\rm{D}}(\\sigma)\\ \\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}(\\sigma) {\\rm{d}}\\sigma \\] In this case \\(f_{\\rm{D}}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{A}}\\), the emission spectrum of donor and absorption spectrum of acceptor, respectively, are both normalized to unity, so that the rate constant for energy transfer, \\(k_{\\rm{ET}}\\), is independent of the oscillator strength of both transitions (contrast to Förster mechanism).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05819))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05819 "spectral photon exitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05819 "The photon exitance, \\(M_{\\rm{p}}\\), at wavelength per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-3}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). Alternatively, the term can be used with the amount of photons (mol or its equivalent einstein) the SI unit then being \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-3}\\) and the common unit \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-2 nm-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05820))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05820 "spectral photon flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05820 "The photon flow, \\(\\varPhi_{\\rm{p}}\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). Alternatively, the term can be used with the amount of photons (mol or its equivalent einstein), the SI unit then being \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-1}\\) and the common unit \\(\\pu{mol s-1 nm-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05821))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05821 "spectral photon flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05821 "The photon irradiance \\(E_{\\rm{p}}\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-3}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). Alternatively, the term can be used with the amount of photons (mol or its equivalent einstein), the SI unit then being \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-3}\\) and the common unit \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-2 nm-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05821 "photon irradiance")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05822 "spectral photon radiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05822 "The photon radiance, \\(L_{\\rm{p}}\\), at wavelength per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-3}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). Alternatively, the term can be used with the amount of photons (mol or its equivalent einstein) the SI unit then being \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-3 sr-1}\\) and the common unit \\(\\pu{mol s-1 m-2 sr-1 nm-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05823 "spectral quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05823 """Quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation (radiant power, energy, energy density, intensity, exitance, radiance, irradiance, etc.) derived by differentiation with respect to wavelength, frequency or wavenumber, e.g. spectral (concentration of) irradiance is the derivative of the irradiance with respect to wavelength (\\(E_{\\lambda }\\)), frequency (\\(E_{
u }\\)) or wavenumber (\\(E_{\\stackrel{˜}{
u }}\\)).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05824))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05824 "spectral radiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05824 "The radiance, \\(L\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-3}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05825 "spectral radiant exitance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05825 "The radiant exitance, \\(M\\), at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-3}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05826 "spectral radiant flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05826 "Synonymous with spectral radiant power.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05827 "spectral radiant intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05827 "The radiation intensity, \\(I\\) at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-1}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{W nm}^{-1}\\ \\rm{sr}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05828 "spectral radiant power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05828 "The radiant power at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\rm{W nm}^{-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05828 "total radiant power")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05829 "spectral responsivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05829 "The spectral output quantity of a system such as a photomultiplier, diode array, photoimaging device, or biological unit divided by the spectral irradiance \\(s(\\lambda) = \\frac{\\rm{d}y\\left(\\lambda \\right)}{\\rm{d}E\\left(\\lambda \\right)}\\), simplified expression: \\(s(\\lambda) = \\frac{Y_{\\lambda }}{E_{\\lambda }}\\), where \\(Y_{\\lambda }\\) is the magnitude of the output signal for irradiation at wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) and \\(E_{\\lambda }\\) is the spectral irradiance of the parallel and perpendicular incident beam at the same wavelength.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05831 "spectral sensitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05831 "The process of increasing the spectral responsivity of a (photoimaging) system in a given wavelength region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05832))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05832 "spectral spheradiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05832 "Alternative term suggested for actinic flux.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05833 "spectrochemical buffer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05833 "Buffer added to samples and reference samples with the intention of making the measure of the analytical element less sensitive to changes in concentration of an interferent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05834 "spectrochemical carrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05834 "An additive which gives rise to a gas which can help to transport the vapour of the sample material into the excitation region of the source, e.g. carbon in an air atmosphere when carbon dioxide is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05835 "spectrogram")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05835 "A spectrum as recorded by a spectrometer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05836 "spectrograph")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05836 "A combination of a spectral apparatus and a camera, which enables an image of a spectrum to be recorded. Spectra are recorded by a photographic emulsion or other means, e.g. two-dimensional electronic image sensors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05837 "spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05837 "A general term for describing a combination of spectral apparatus with one or more detectors to measure the intensity of one or more spectral bands.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05847))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05847 "spectroscope")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05847 "A device which enables visual observation and evaluation of optical spectra (usually confined to the visible spectral region).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05848))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05848 "spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05848 "The study of physical systems by the electromagnetic radiation with which they interact or that they produce. Spectrometry is the measurement of such radiations as a means of obtaining information about the systems and their components. In certain types of optical spectroscopy, the radiation originates from an external source and is modified by the system, whereas in other types, the radiation originates within the system itself.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05849))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05849 "spectrum analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05849 "The interpretation of the information present in an energy spectrum in terms of radiation energy and intensity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05851))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05851 "specular reflectance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05851 "Radiant power specularly reflected from the surface of a system, \\(P_{\\mathrm{refl}}\\), divided by the incident power, \\(P_{0}\\): \\[\\rho =\\frac{P_{\\mathrm{refl}}}{P_{0}}\\]")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05851 "reflection factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05852 "speed")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05852 "Scalar quantity equal to the absolute value of the velocity vector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05853))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05853 "speed distribution function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05853 "Probability density of finding a particle with speed within an interval between \\(c\\) and \\(c+\\mathrm{d}c\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05854))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05854 "speed of light in a vacuum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05854 "The speed of light in vacuum, \\(c\\), is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units, the SI, with \\(c = \\pu{299792458 m s-1}\\). The speed of light is considered as a fundamental constant of nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05856))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05856 "spherical carbonaceous mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05856 "A term which describes the morphology of carbonaceous mesophase which is formed in the isotropic pitch matrix. The spherical carbonaceous mesophase usually has a lamellar structure consisting of flat aromatic molecules arranged in parallel layers which are perpendicular to the sphere/isotropic phase interface. On coalescence, this spherical mesophase loses its characteristic morphology and is converted to the bulk mesophase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05857))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05857 "spherical radiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05857 "Same as radiant exitance, \\(M\\). It is the integration of the radiant power, \\(P\\), leaving a source over the solid angle and over the whole wavelength range.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05858))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05858 "spherical radiant exposure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05858 "Synonymous with fluence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05859))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05859 "spherulite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05859 "A polycrystalline, roughly spherical morphology consisting of lath, fibrous or lamellar crystals emanating from a common centre. Space filling is achieved by branching, bending or both, of the constituent fibres or lamellae.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05861 "spin-allowed electronic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05861 "An electronic transition which does not involve a change in the spin part of the wavefunction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05862))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05862 "spin conservation rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05862 "For both radiative and radiationless transitions, the principle that transitions between terms of the same multiplicity are spin-allowed, while transitions between terms of different multiplicity are spin-forbidden.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05862 "Wigner rule")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05864))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05864 "spin density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05864 "The unpaired electron density at a position of interest, usually at carbon, in a radical. It is often measured experimentally by electron paramagnetic resonance [EPR, ESR (electron spin resonance)] spectroscopy through hyperfine coupling constants of the atom or an attached hydrogen.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05865))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05865 "spin-flip transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05865 "A rotation of electron spins, above a critical magnetic field, \\(H_{\\rm{c}}\\), in an antiferromagnet from parallel to largely perpendicular alignment, relative to an applied magnetic field, \\(H_{\\rm{a}}\\), for \\(H_{\\rm{a}}>H_{\\rm{c}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05867))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05867 "spin-glass transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05867 "A second-order transition from a paramagnetic or ferromagnetic state to a spin-glass state in which spins from moment-bearing solute atoms become ordered randomly in a non-magnetic host such that the net magnetization of any region is zero. Examples: $\\ce{Au–Fe}$, $\\ce{Cu–Mn}$ and $\\ce{Mo–Fe}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05868 "spin label")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05868 "A stable paramagnetic group (typically a nitryl radical) that is attached to a part of a molecular entity whose microscopic environment is of interest and may be revealed by the electron spin resonance (ESR) spectrum of the spin label. When a simple paramagnetic molecular entity is used in this way without covalent attachment to the molecular entity of interest it is frequently referred to as a 'spin probe'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05869))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05869 "spinodal decomposition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05869 "A clustering reaction in a homogeneous, supersaturated solution (solid or liquid) which is unstable against infinitesimal fluctuations in density or composition. The solution therefore separates spontaneously into two phases, starting with small fluctuations and proceeding with a decrease in the Gibbs energy without a nucleation barrier.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05870))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05870 "spin–orbit coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05870 "The interaction of the electron spin magnetic moment with the magnetic moment due to the orbital motion of the electron. One consequence of spin-orbit coupling is the mixing of zero-order states of different multiplicity. This effect may result in fine structure called spin-orbit splitting.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05871))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05871 "spin–orbit coupling constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05871 "Coefficient in the spin–orbit coupling term in the hamiltonian.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05872))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05872 "spin-orbit splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05872 "Removal of state degeneracy by spin–orbit coupling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05873))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05873 "spin-Peierls transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05873 "A magneto-elastic transition that occurs in quasi one-dimensional antiferromagnetic materials when the magnetic free energy decrease due to the formation of singlet spin pairs outweighs the increase in lattice free energy occurring as a result of the dimerization of the regular array. Example: The transition in tetrathiafulvalenium bis(dithiolene)cuprate at \\(12\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05875))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05875 "spin–spin coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05875 "The interaction between the spin magnetic moments of different electrons and/or nuclei. It causes, e.g. the multiplet pattern in nuclear magnetic resonance spectra.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05876))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05876 "spin–spin coupling constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05876 "Coefficient of the indirect spin–spin coupling between two nuclei (A and B) in a magnetic resonance hamiltonian.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05877))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05877 "spin-state transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05877 "An electronic transition from a high-spin state to a low-spin state, or vice versa. Example: With an increase in temperature $\\ce{Co^{3+}}$ ions in $\\ce{LaCoO3}$ transform from a low-spin state (\\(\\rm{t}_{\\rm{2g}}^{6}\\rm{e}_{\\rm{g}}^{0}\\)) to the high-spin state (\\(\\rm{t}_{\\rm{2g}}^{4}\\rm{e}_{\\rm{g}}^{2}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05878))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05878 "spin trapping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05878 "In certain reactions in solution a transient radical will interact with a diamagnetic reagent to form a more persistent radical. The product radical accumulates to a concentration where detection and, frequently, identification are possible by EPR/ESR spectroscopy. The key reaction is usually one of attachment; the diamagnetic reagent is said to be a 'spin trap' and the persistent product radical is then the 'spin adduct'. The procedure is referred to as spin trapping, and is used for monitoring reactions involving the intermediacy of reactive radicals at concentrations too low for direct observation. Typical spin traps are C-nitroso compounds and nitrones, to which reactive radicals will rapidly add to form nitryl radicals. A quantitative development, in which essentially all reactive radicals generated in a particular system are intercepted, has been referred to as 'spin counting'. Spin trapping has also been adapted to the interception of radicals generated in both gaseous and solid phases. In these cases the spin adduct is in practice transferred to a liquid solution for observation in order to facilitate interpretation of the EPR/ESR spectra of the radicals obtained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05880))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05880 "spiro chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05880 "A double-strand chain consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings, with adjacent rings having only one atom in common. Alternatively, a spiro chain is a double-strand chain with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through three atoms, two on one side and one on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05881))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05881 "spiro compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05881 "Compounds having one atom (usually a quaternary carbon) as the only common member of two rings, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05882))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05882 "spiro macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05882 "A double-strand macromolecule consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings, with adjacent rings having only one atom in common. Alternatively, a spiro macromolecule is a double-strand macromolecule with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through three atoms, two on one side and one on the other side of each constitutional unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05884 "spiro union")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05884 "A union formed by a single atom which is the only common member of two rings. The common atom is designated as the spiro atom. A free spiro union is one which constitutes the only union direct or indirect between the two rings.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05885))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05885 "splicing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05885 "of RNA: The procedure by which introns are removed from eukaryotic precursor mRNA molecules and adjacent exon sequences are joined together (spliced).of DNA: Manipulation for joining together double-stranded DNA fragments with protruding single-stranded 'sticky ends' by means of ligases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05886))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05886 "spontaneous emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05886 "That mode of emission which occurs even in the absence of a perturbing external electromagnetic field. The transition between states, \\(n\\) and \\(m\\), is governed by the einstein coefficient of spontaneous emission, \\(A_{nm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05887))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05887 "spontaneous fission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05887 "Nuclear fission which occurs without the addition of particles or energy to the nucleus")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05889))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05889 "spot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05889 "A zone in paper and thin-layer chromatography of approximately circular appearance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05891))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05891 "sprayer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05891 "The particular part of a nebulizer where the aspirated liquid is disrupted by the gas-jet into a spray.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05892))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05892 "spreading function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05892 "The normalized signal produced, as a function of elution volume at the outlet of a size-exclusion chromatography set-up, by an instantaneous injection of a uniform sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05893))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05893 "spreading wetting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05893 "A process in which a drop of liquid spreads over a solid or liquid substrate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05894))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05894 "spread monolayer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05894 "It may happen that a component is virtually insoluble in both of two adjoining phases, but is present as a monolayer between them. Such a layer can be produced by spreading.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05895))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05895 "sputtering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05895 "Removal of surface material (atoms, clusters and molecules) by particle bombardment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05896))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05896 "sputter yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05896 "The number of particles sputtered from the surface of a target per primary ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05897 "square-wave current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05897 "In square-wave polarography, the component of the current that is associated with the presence of a substance B. This component may be either faradaic (if B is electroactive) or non-faradaic (if B is surface-active).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05900))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05900 "stable")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05900 "As applied to chemical species, the term expresses a thermodynamic property, which is quantitatively measured by relative molar standard Gibbs energies. A chemical species A is more stable than its isomer B if \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} > 0\\) for the (real or hypothetical) reaction \\[\\rm{A}\\quad \\rightarrow \\quad \\rm{B}\\] under standard conditions. If for the two reactions: \\[\\rm{P}\\rightarrow \\rm{X}+\\rm{Y}\\qquad (\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G_{1}^{\\rm{o}})\\] \\[\\rm{Q}\\rightarrow \\rm{X}+\\rm{Z}\\qquad (\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G_{2}^{\\rm{o}})\\] \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G_{1}^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} > \\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G_{2}^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), P is more stable relative to the product Y than is Q relative to Z. Both in qualitative and quantitative usage the term stable is therefore always used in reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed standard. The term should not be used as a synonym for unreactive or 'less reactive' since this confuses thermodynamics and kinetics. A relatively more stable chemical species may be more reactive than some reference species towards a given reaction partner.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05901))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05901 "stable film, metastable film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05901 "A film often thins gradually to a thickness at which it either ruptures or converts abruptly to an equilibrium film. Rupture under these conditions characterizes unstable films, whereas transition to an equilibrium film characterizes (meta)stable films.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05902))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05902 "stable ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05902 "An ion which is not sufficiently excited to dissociate spontaneously into a daughter ion and associated neutral fragment(s) or to react further in any other way within the time scale of the experiment, e.g. until hitting the detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05903))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05903 "staggered conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05903 "The conformation of groups attached to two adjacent atoms if the torsion angles are such that the groups are as far away as possible from an eclipsed arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05905))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05905 "standard acceleration of free fall")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05905 "Acceleration defined as \\(g_{\\rm{n}} = 9.806\\ 65\\ \\rm{m s}^{-2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05906))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05906 "standard atmosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05906 "Pressure defined as \\(101\\ 325\\ \\rm{Pa}\\) and used as unit of pressure with the symbol \\(\\rm{atm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05907))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05907 "standard atomic weights")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05907 "Recommended values of relative atomic masses of the elements revised biennially by the IUPAC Commission on Atomic Weights and Isotopic Abundances and applicable to elements in any normal sample with a high level of confidence. A normal sample is any reasonably possible source of the element or its compounds in commerce for industry and science and has not been subject to significant modification of isotopic composition within a geologically brief period.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05908))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05908 "standard chemical potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05908 "Of substance B at temperature \\(T\\), \\(\\mu _{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{o}}(T)\\), is the value of the chemical potential under specified standard conditions (in the standard state).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05909))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05909 "standard concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05909 "Chosen value of amount concentration denoted by \\(c^{\\unicode{x29B5}}\\) or \\(c^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and usually equal to \\(\\pu{1 mol dm-3}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05910))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05910 "standard conditions for gases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05910 "Temperature, \\(273.15\\ \\rm{K}\\) (\\(0\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\)) and pressure of \\(10^{5}\\) pascals. IUPAC recommends that the former use of the pressure of 1 atm as standard pressure (equivalent to \\(1.01325\\times 10^{5}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\)) should be discontinued.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05911))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05911 "standard deviation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05911 "The positive square root of the sum of the squares of the deviations between the observations and the mean of the series, divided by one less than the total number in the series. The standard deviation is the positive square root of the variance, a more fundamental statistical quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05912))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05912 "standard electrode potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05912 "The value of the standard emf of a cell in which molecular hydrogen under standard pressure is oxidized to solvated protons at the left-hand electrode.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05913))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05913 "standard electromotive force")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05913 "Quantity defined by \\(E^{\\unicode{x26ac}} = -\\frac{\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{n\\ F}=\\frac{R\\ T}{n\\ F}\\ \\ln K^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), where \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the standard Gibbs energy of the cell reaction in the direction in which reduction occurs at the right-hand electrode in the diagram representing the cell ('reduction at right'), \\(K^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the standard equilibrium constant for this reaction, \\(n\\) its charge number, \\(F\\) the Faraday constant, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05914 "standard entropy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05914 "Entropy change associated with the activation reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05915 "standard equilibrium constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05915 "Quantity defined by \\(K^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} = \\rm{e}^{-\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}/R\\ T}\\) where \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the standard reaction Gibbs energy, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature. Some chemists prefer the name thermodynamic equilibrium constant and the symbol \\(K\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05915 "thermodynamic equilibrium constant")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05916))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05916 "standard Gibbs energy of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05916 "Standard Gibbs energy change associated with the activation reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05917))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05917 "standard hydrogen electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05917 "For solutions in protic solvents, the universal reference electrode for which, under standard conditions, the standard electrode potential ($\\ce{H^{+}}$/$\\ce{H2}$) is zero at all temperatures. The absolute electrode potential of the hydrogen electrode under standard conditions can be expressed in terms of thermodynamic quantities by applying a suitable Born–Haber cycle, thus: \\[E^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\left(\\rm{H}^{+}/\\rm{H}_{2}\\right)\\left(\\rm{abs}\\right)=\\Delta _{\\rm{at}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}+\\Delta _{\\rm{ion}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}+\\frac{\\alpha _{\\rm{H}^{+}}^{\\rm{o,S}}}{F}\\] where \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{at}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{ion}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) are the atomization and ionization Gibbs energies of $\\ce{H2}$, \\(\\alpha _{\\rm{H}^{+}}^{\\rm{o,S}}\\) is the real potential of $\\ce{H2}$ in solvent $\\ce{S}$ and \\(F\\) is the Faraday constant. The recommended absolute electrode potential of the hydrogen electrode is: \\[E^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\left(\\rm{H}^{+}/\\rm{H}_{2}\\right)^{\\rm{H}_{2}\\rm{O}}\\left(\\rm{abs}\\right)=(4.44\\pm 0.02)\\ \\rm{V}\\quad \\rm{at}\\quad 298.15\\ \\rm{K}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05918))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05918 "standard molality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05918 "Chosen value of molality and usually equal to \\(\\pu{1 mol kg -1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05919))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05919 "standard potential of an electrode reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05919 "Synonymous with standard electrode potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05920))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05920 "standard potential of the reaction in a chemical cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05920 "Synonymous with standard electromotive force.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05921))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05921 "standard pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05921 "Chosen value of pressure denoted by \\(p^{\\unicode{x29B5}}\\) or \\(p^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\). In 1982 IUPAC recommended the value \\(10^{5}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\), but prior to 1982 the value \\(101\\ 325\\ \\rm{Pa}\\) (= \\(1\\ \\rm{atm}\\)) was usually used.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05923))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05923 "standard reaction quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05923 "Infinitesimal changes in thermodynamic functions with extent of reaction divided by the infinitesimal increase in the extent when all the reactants and products are in their standard states. For the quantity \\(X\\) they should be denoted by \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{r}}X^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), but usually only \\(\\Delta X^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is used. For specific types of reactions the subscript r is replaced by: f for formation, c for combustion, a for atomization and superscript ‡ for activation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05924))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05924 "standard solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05924 "A solution of accurately known concentration, prepared using standard substances in one of several ways. A primary standard is a substance of known high purity which may be dissolved in a known volume of solvent to give a primary standard solution. If stoichiometry is used to establish the strength of a titrant, it is called a secondary standard solution. The term secondary standard can also be applied to a substance whose active agent contents have been found by comparison against a primary standard. Concentrations of standard solutions may be expressed in \\(\\pu{kmol m-3}\\), \\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\), or in terms more closely related to those used in specific titrations (as titres).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05925))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05925 "standard state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05925 "State of a system chosen as standard for reference by convention. Three standard states are recognized: For a gas phase it is the (hypothetical) state of the pure substance in the gaseous phase at the standard pressure \\(p=p^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), assuming ideal behaviour. For a pure phase, or a mixture, or a solvent in the liquid or solid state it is the state of the pure substance in the liquid or solid phase at the standard pressure \\(p=p^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\). For a solute in solution it is the (hypothetical) state of solute at the standard molality \\(m^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), standard pressure \\(p^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) or standard concentration \\(c^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and exhibiting infinitely dilute solution behaviour. For a pure substance the concept of standard state applies to the substance in a well defined state of aggregation at a well defined but arbitrarily chosen standard pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05926))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05926 "standard subtraction method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05926 "A variation of the standard addition method. In this procedure changes in the potential resulting from the addition of a known amount of a species which reacts stoichiometrically with the ion of interest (e.g. a complexing agent) are employed to determine the original activity or concentration of the ion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05927))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05927 "standard thermodynamic quantities")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05927 "Values of thermodynamic functions in the standard state characterized by a standard pressure, molality or amount concentration, but not by temperature. Standard quantities are denoted by adding the superscript \\(^{⦵}\\) or \\(^{\\circ}\\) to the symbol of the quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05928))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05928 "standard uncertainty")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05928 "Uncertainty of measurement expressed as one standard deviation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05929))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05929 "stand-by state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05929 "The condition of an instrument where the analytical procedure can begin immediately.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05930))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05930 "stannoxanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05930 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{H3S_{n}[OSnH2]_{n}OSnH3}$. Thus, tin analogues of siloxanes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05932))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05932 "stannylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05932 "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{R2Sn{:}}$. The older synonymous term  stannylenes is no longer recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05932 "stannylenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05933))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05933 "star copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05933 "A copolymer where chains having different constitutional or configurational features are linked through a central moiety.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05934))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05934 "Stark effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05934 "The splitting or shift of spectral lines in an electric field. Also called electrochromic effect.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05934 "electrochromic effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05935))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05935 "star macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05935 "A macromolecule containing a single branch point from which linear chains (arms) emanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05936))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05936 "star polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05936 "A polymer composed of star macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05937))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05937 "starting line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05937 "The point or line on a chromatographic layer where the substance to be chromatographed is applied.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05937 "starting point")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05939))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05939 "start-up time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05939 "The time interval between turn-on of the instrument and its stand-by state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05943))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05943 "state-to-state kinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05943 "A branch of chemical kinetics concerned with the dynamics of reactions in which the reactant species are in known quantum states, and in which the quantum states of the products are determined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05944))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05944 "static fields mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05944 "An instrument which can separate selected ion beams with fields which do not vary with time. The fields are generally both electric and magnetic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05945))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05945 "static pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05945 "The pressure of a fluid at rest or in motion exerted perpendicularly to the direction of flow.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05947))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05947 "static stability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05947 "In meteorology, the stability of the atmosphere in the vertical direction to vertical displacements. If \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}\\) is the dry adiabatic lapse rate, dry air is stable if the lapse rate \\(-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}T}{{\\rm{d}}z} \\lt \\Gamma\\), unstable if \\(-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}T}{{\\rm{d}}z} \\gt \\Gamma\\) and neutral \\(-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}T}{{\\rm{d}}z} = \\Gamma\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05948))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05948 "stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05948 "The phase of a culture of microorganisms or animal and plant cells cultured in vitro that follows the exponential growth phase and in which there is little or no growth. In some cases it is a phase of product formation, e.g. formation of secondary metabolites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05949))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05949 "stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05949 "One of the two phases forming a chromatographic system. It may be a solid, a gel or a liquid. If a liquid, it may be distributed on a solid. This solid may or may not contribute to the separation process. The liquid may also be chemically bonded to the solid (bonded phase) or immobilized onto it (immobilized phase). The expression chromatographic bed or sorbent may be used as a general term to denote any of the different forms in which the stationary phase is used. Particularly in gas chromatography where the stationary phase is most often a liquid, the term liquid phase is used for it as compared to the gas phase, i.e. the mobile phase. However, particularly in the early development of liquid chromatography, the term 'liquid phase' had also been used to characterize the mobile phase as compared to the 'solid phase' i.e. the stationary phase. Due to this ambiguity, the use of the term 'liquid phase' is discouraged. If the physical state of the stationary phase is to be expressed, the use of the adjective forms such as liquid stationary phase and solid stationary phase, bonded phase or immobilized phase is proposed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05950))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05950 "stationary-phase fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05950 "The volume of the stationary phase per unit volume of a packed column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05952))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05952 "stationary phase volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05952 "The volume of the liquid stationary phase or the active solid in the column. The volume of any solid support is not included. In the case of partition chromatography with a liquid stationary phase, it is identical to the liquid phase volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05953))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05953 "stationary state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05953 "(in quantum mechanics) A state that does not evolve with time.(in kinetics) See: steady state")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05955))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05955 "statistical copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05955 "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules in which the sequential distribution of the monomeric units obeys known statistical laws.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05956))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05956 "statistical copolymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05956 "A copolymerization in which a statistical copolymer is formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05959))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05959 "statistical segment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05959 "A segment of an actual polymer chain which behaves, with respect to some property, virtually as a segment of a freely jointed chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05961))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05961 "steady state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05961 "The state of a continuous process when it is operating in such a way that the concentration of solutes in exit streams remains constant with respect to time for constant feed concentrations, even though the two phases are not necessarily in thermodynamic equilibrium in any part of the process. The term equilibrium should not be used to describe this situation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05962))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05962 "steady state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05962 "In a kinetic analysis of a complex reaction involving unstable intermediates in lconcentration, the rate of change of each such intermediate is set equal to zero, so that the rate equation can be expressed as a function of the concentrations of chemical species present in macroscopic amounts. For example, assume that $\\ce{\\textbf{X}}$ is an unstable intermediate in the reaction sequence:   Conservation of mass requires that: \\[[\\ce{A}] + [\\ce{X}] + [\\ce{D}] = [\\ce{A}]_{0}\\] which, since \\([\\ce{A}]_{0}\\) is constant, implies: \\[-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{X}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} + \\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{D}]}{{\\rm{d}}t}.\\] Since \\([\\ce{X}]\\) is negligibly small, the rate of formation of $\\ce{\\textbf{D}}$ is essentially equal to the rate of disappearance of $\\ce{\\textbf{A}}$, and the rate of change of \\([\\ce{X}]\\) can be set equal to zero. Applying the steady state approximation (\\({\\rm{d}}[\\ce{X}]/{\\rm{d}}t = 0\\)) allows the elimination of \\([\\ce{X}]\\) from the kinetic equations, whereupon the rate of reaction is expressed: \\[\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{D}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = -\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{k_{1}k_{2}[\\ce{A}][\\ce{C}]}{k_{-1} + k_{2}[\\ce{C}]}\\]Note: The steady-state approximation does not imply that \\([\\ce{X}]\\) is even approximately constant, only that its absolute rate of change is very much smaller than that of \\([\\ce{A}]\\) and \\([\\ce{D}]\\). Since according to the reaction scheme \\({\\rm{d}}[\\ce{X}]/{\\rm{d}}t = k_{2}[\\ce{X}][\\ce{C}]\\), the assumption that \\([\\ce{X}]\\) is constant would lead, for the case in which $\\ce{\\textbf{C}}$ is in large excess, to the absurd conclusion that formation of the product $\\ce{\\textbf{D}}$ will continue at a constant rate even after the reactant $\\ce{\\textbf{A}}$ has been consumed.In a stirred flow reactor a steady state implies a regime so that all concentrations are independent of time.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05962 "stationary state")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05962 "steady state approximation")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05962 "steady state treatment")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05962 "Bodenstein approximation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05964))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05964 "Stefan–Boltzmann constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05964 "Fundamental physical constant \\(\\sigma = \\frac{2\\ \\pi ^{5}\\ k^{4}}{15\\ h^{3}\\ c_{0}^{2}} = 5.670\\ 51(19)\\times 10^{-8}\\ \\rm{W}\\ \\rm{m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{K}^{-4}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05965))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05965 "stem")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05965 "A crystallized, rodlike portion of a polymer chain connected to non-rodlike portions, or chain ends, or both.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05966))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05966 "step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05966 "The portion of an integral chromatogram recording the amount of a component, or the corresponding change in the signal from the detector as the component emerges from the column.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05967))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05967 "step height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05967 "The distance perpendicular to the time or volume axis, through which the baseline moves as a result of a step on an integral chromatogram.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05969))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05969 "stepwise elution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05969 "An elution process in which the composition of the mobile phase is changed in steps during a single chromatographic run.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05970))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05970 "stepwise reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05970 "A chemical reaction with at least one reaction intermediate and involving at least two consecutive elementary reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05971))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05971 "steradian")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05971 "SI derived unit of solid angle, \\(\\mathrm{sr}=1\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05972))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05972 "stereoblock macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05972 "A block macromolecule composed of stereoregular, and possibly nonstereoregular, blocks.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05973))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05973 "stereoblock polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05973 "A polymer composed of stereoblock macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05974))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05974 "stereochemical formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05974 "A three-dimensional view of a molecule either as such or in a projection.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05974 "stereoformula")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05975))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05975 "stereoconvergence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05975 "The predominant formation of the same stereoisomers or stereoisomer mixture of a reaction product when two different stereoisomers of the reactant are used in the same reaction. When that product involved in the reaction is one enantiomer the result has been called enantioconvergence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05976))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05976 "stereodescriptor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05976 "A prefix to specify configuration (absolute or relative) or conformation. For example R, S; r, s; P, M; Re, Si; E, Z; ap, sp; etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05977))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05977 "stereoelectronic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05977 "Pertaining to the dependence of the properties (especially the energy) of a molecular entity in a particular electronic state (or of a transition state) on relative nuclear geometry. The electronic ground state is usually considered, but the term can apply to excited states as well. Stereoelectronic effects arise from the different alignment of electronic orbitals in different arrangements of nuclear geometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05978))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05978 "stereoelectronic control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05978 "Control of the nature of the products of a chemical reaction (or of its rate) by stereoelectronic factors. The term is usually applied in the framework of an orbital approximation. The variations of molecular orbital energies with relative nuclear geometry (along a reaction coordinate) are then seen as consequences of variations in basis-orbital overlaps.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05980 "stereogenic unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05980 "A grouping within a molecular entity that may be considered a focus of stereoisomerism. At least one of these must be present in every enantiomer (though the presence of stereogenic units does not conversely require the corresponding chemical species to be chiral). Three basic types are recognized for molecular entities involving atoms having not more than four substituents:  A grouping of atoms consisting of a central atom and distinguishable ligands, such that the interchange of any two of the substituents leads to a stereoisomer. An asymmetric atom (chirality centre) is the traditional example of this stereogenic unit.   A chain of four non-coplanar atoms (or rigid groups) in a stable conformation, such that an imaginary or real (restricted) rotation (with a change of sign of the torsion angle) about the central bond leads to a stereoisomer.   A grouping of atoms consisting of a double bond with substituents which give rise to cis-trans isomerism.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05980 "stereogen")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S05980 "stereoelement")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05981 "stereoheterotopic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05981 "Either enantiotopic or diastereotopic. (In contrast the term constitutionally heterotopic has been used to describe identical groups located in constitutionally non-equivalent positions).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05982 "stereohomosequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05982 "A configurational homosequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a polymer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05983))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05983 "stereoisomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05983 "Isomerism due to differences in the spatial arrangement of atoms without any differences in connectivity or bond multiplicity between the isomers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05984))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05984 "stereoisomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05984 "Isomers that possess identical constitution, but which differ in the arrangement of their atoms in space.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05985))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05985 "stereomutation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05985 "A change of configuration at a stereogenic unit brought about by physical or chemical means.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05986))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05986 "stereoregular macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05986 "A regular macromolecule essentially comprising only one species of stereorepeating unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05987))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05987 "stereoregular polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05987 "A polymer composed of stereoregular macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05988))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05988 "stereorepeating unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05988 "A configurational repeating unit having defined configuration at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a regular macromolecule, a regular oligomer molecule, a regular block or a regular chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05989))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05989 "stereoselective polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05989 "Polymerization in which a polymer molecule is formed from a mixture of stereoisomeric monomer molecules by incorporation of only one stereoisomeric species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05990))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05990 "stereoselective synthesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05990 "A chemical reaction (or reaction sequence) in which one or more new elements of chirality are formed in a substrate molecule and which produces the stereoisomeric (enantiomeric or diastereoisomeric) products in unequal amounts. Traditionally called asymmetric synthesis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05991))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05991 "stereoselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05991 "The preferential formation in a chemical reaction of one stereoisomer over another. When the stereoisomers are enantiomers, the phenomenon is called 'enantioselectivity' and is quantitatively expressed by the enantiomeric excess; when they are diastereoisomers, it is called 'diastereoselectivity' and is quantitatively expressed by the diastereoisomer excess.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05992))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05992 "stereosequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05992 "A configurational sequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a polymer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05993))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05993 "stereospecifically labelled tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05993 "A tracer in which the label is present in a stereospecific position.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05994))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05994 "stereospecificity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05994 "A reaction is termed stereospecific if starting materials differing only in their configuration are converted into stereoisomeric products. According to this definition, a stereospecific process is necessarily stereoselectivity but not all stereoselective processes are stereospecific. Stereospecificity may be total (100%) or partial. The term is also applied to situations where reaction can be performed with only one stereoisomer. For example, the exclusive formation of trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane upon bromination of cyclohexene is a stereospecific process, although the analogous reaction with (E)-cyclohexene has not been performed.The term has also been applied to describe a reaction of very high stereoselectivity, but this usage is unnecessary and is discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05995))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05995 "stereospecific polymerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05995 "Polymerization in which a tactic polymer is formed. However, polymerization in which stereoisomerism present in the monomer is merely retained in the polymer is not to be regarded as stereospecific. For example, the polymerization of a chiral monomer, e.g. ᴅ-propylene oxide (ᴅ-methyloxirane), with retention of configuration is not considered to be a stereospecific reaction; however, selective polymerization, with retention, of one of the enantiomers present in a mixture of ᴅ- and ʟ-propylene oxide molecules is so classified.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05996))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05996 "steric-approach control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05996 "Control of stereoselectivity of a reaction by steric hindrance towards attack of the reagent, which is directed to the less hindered face of the molecule. Partial bond making is strong enough at the transition state for steric control to take place. This suggests that the transition state should not be close to products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05997))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05997 "steric effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05997 """The effect on a chemical or physical property (structure, rate or equilibrium constant) upon introduction of substituents having different steric requirements. The steric effect in a reaction is ascribed to the difference in steric energy between, on the one hand, reactants and, on the other hand, a transition state (or products). A steric effect on a rate process may result in a rate increase ('steric acceleration') or a decrease ('steric retardation'). (The adjective 'steric' is not to be confused with stereochemical.) Steric effects arise from contributions ascribed to strain as the sum of (1) non-bonded repulsions, (2) bond angle strain and (3) bond stretches or compressions. For the purpose of correlation analysis or linear free-energy relations various scales of steric parameters have been proposed, notably A values, Taft's \\(E_{\\rm{s}}\\) and Charton's \\(
u \\) scales. In a reactant molecule $\\ce{RY}$ and an appropriate reference molecule $\\ce{R^{\\circ}Y}$, the 'primary steric effect' of $\\ce{R}$ is the direct result of differences in compressions which occur because $\\ce{R}$ differs from $\\ce{R^{\\circ}}$ in the vicinity of the reaction centre $\\ce{Y}$. A 'secondary steric effect' involves the differential moderation of electron delocalization by non-bonded compressions. Some authors make a distinction between 'steric' effects attributed to van der Waals repulsions alone, and 'strain' effects, attributed to deviations of bond angles from 'ideal' values.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05998))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05998 "steric factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05998 "A factor introduced into simple versions of the collision theory of reactions to take care of the fact that the reaction probability depends on the certain mutual orientations of the reactant molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S05999))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S05999 "steric factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S05999 "The ratio, \\(\\sigma \\), of the root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a polymer chain with unperturbed dimensions, \\(< r^{2} >_{0}^{1/2}\\), to that of a freely rotating chain with the same structure, \\(< r^{2} >_{0,f}^{1/2}\\), i.e. \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{< r^{2} >_{0}}{< r^{2} >_{0,f}}}\\), in the limit of infinite chain length. The steric factor reflects the effect of hindrance to free rotation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06000))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06000 "steric hindrance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06000 "The original term for a steric effect arising from crowding of substituents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06001))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06001 "steric isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06001 "A secondary isotope effect attributed to the different vibrational amplitudes of isotopologues. For example, both the mean and mean-square amplitudes of vibrations associated with $\\ce{C–H}$ bonds are greater than those of $\\ce{C–D}$ bonds. The greater effective bulk of molecules containing the former may be manifested by a steric effect on a rate or equilibrium constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06003))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06003 "Stern layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06003 "Counter and co-ions in immediate contact with a surface are said to be located in the Stern layer, and form with the fixed charge a molecular capacitor.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06004))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06004 "Stern–Volmer kinetic relationships")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06004 "This term applies broadly to variations of quantum yield of photophysical processes (e.g. fluorescence or phosphorescence) or photochemical reaction (usually reaction quantum yield) with the concentration of a given reagent which may be a substrate or a quencher. In the simplest case, a plot of \\(\\frac{\\mathit{\\Phi}^{0}}{\\mathit{\\Phi}}\\) (or \\(\\frac{M^{0}}{M}\\) for emission) vs. concentration of quencher, \\(\\ce{[Q]}\\), is linear obeying the equation: \\[\\frac{\\mathit{\\Phi}^{0}}{\\mathit{\\Phi}} \\quad {\\rm{or}} \\quad \\frac{M^{0}}{M} = 1 + K_{\\rm{sv}} [\\rm{Q}]\\] In equation (1) \\(K_{\\rm{sv}}\\) is referred to as the Stern–Volmer constant. Equation (1) applies when a quencher inhibits either a photochemical reaction or a photophysical process by a single reaction. \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}^{0}\\) and \\(M^{0}\\) are the quantum yield and emission intensity radiant exitance, respectively, in the absence of the quencher Q, while \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}\\) and \\(M\\) are the same quantities in the presence of the different concentrations of Q. In the case of dynamic quenching the constant \\(K_{\\rm{sv}}\\) is the product of the true quenching constant \\(k_{\\rm{q}}\\) and the excited state lifetime, \\(\\tau^{0}\\), in the absence of quencher. \\(k_{\\rm{q}}\\) is the bimolecular reaction rate constant for the elementary reaction of the excited state with the particular quencher Q. Equation (1) can therefore be replaced by the expression (2): \\[\\frac{\\mathit{\\Phi}^{0}}{\\mathit{\\Phi}} \\quad {\\rm{or}} \\quad \\frac{M^{0}}{M} = 1 + k_{\\rm{q}} \\tau^{0} [{\\rm{Q}}]\\] When an excited state undergoes a bimolecular reaction with rate constant \\(k_{\\rm{r}}\\) to form a product, a double-reciprocal relationship is observed according to the equation: \\[\\frac{1}{\\mathit{\\Phi}_{\\rm{p}}} = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{k_{\\rm{r}} \\tau^{0} [{\\ce{S}}]}\\right) \\frac{1}{A \\cdot B}\\] where \\(\\mathit{\\Phi}_{\\rm{p}}\\) is the quantum efficiency of product formation, \\(A\\) the efficiency of forming the reactive excited state, \\(B\\) the fraction of reactions of the excited state with substrate S which leads to product, and \\(\\ce{[S]}\\) is the concentration of reactive ground-state substrate. The intercept/slope ratio gives \\(k_{\\rm{r}} \\tau ^{0}\\). If \\(\\ce{[S]} = \\ce{[Q]}\\), and if a photophysical process is monitored, plots of equations (2) and (3) should provide independent determinations of the product-forming rate constant \\(k_{\\rm{r}}\\). When the lifetime of an excited state is observed as a function of the concentration of S or Q, a linear relationship should be observed according to the equation: \\[\\frac{\\tau^{0}}{\\tau} = 1 + k_{\\rm{q}} \\tau^{0} [\\ce{Q}]\\] where \\(\\tau^{0}\\) is the lifetime of the excited state in the absence of the quencher Q.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06005))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06005 "steroids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06005 "Naturally occurring compounds and synthetic analogues, based on the cyclopenta[a]phenanthrene carbon skeleton, partially or completely hydrogenated; there are usually methyl groups at C-10 and C-13, and often an alkyl group at C-17. By extension, one or more bond scissions, ring expansions and/or ring contractions of the skeleton may have occurred. Natural steroids are derived biogenetically from triterpenoids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06006))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06006 "sterols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06006 "Natural products derived from the steroid skeleton and containing a hydroxy group in the 3 position, closely related to cholestan-3-ol.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06007))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06007 "stibanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06007 "The saturated hydrides of tervalent antimony, having the general formula $\\ce{Sb_{n}H_{n+2}}$. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce{SbH3}$ belong to the class stibines.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06008))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06008 "stibanylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06008 "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce{RSb{:}}$ (former IUPAC name is stibinediyls). A common non-IUPAC synonym is stibinidenes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06008 "stibinidenes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06009))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06009 "stibines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06009 """$\\ce{SbH3}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups $\\ce{R3Sb}$. $\\ce{RSbH2}$, $\\ce{R2SbH}$ and $\\ce{R3Sb}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$) are called primary, secondary and tertiary stibines, respectively. A specific stibine is preferably named as a substituted stibane. E.g. $\\ce{(CH2=CH)3Sb}$ trivinylstibane.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06011))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06011 "stibonium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06011 "Salts (including hydroxides) containing an atom of tetracoordinate antimony, of the form $\\ce{[R4Sb]^{+}X^{−}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06012))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06012 "sticking coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06012 "The ratio of the rate of adsorption to the rate at which the adsorptive strikes the total surface, i.e. covered and uncovered. It is usually a function of surface coverage, of temperature and of the details of the surface structure of the adsorbent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06013))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06013 "sticking probability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06013 "Often used with the same meaning as sticking coefficient, but in principle it is a microscopic quantity concerned with the individual collision process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06014))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06014 "sticky ends")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06014 "The staggered ends of complementary sequences of DNA which result from cleavage by restriction enzymes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06015))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06015 "stimulated emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06015 "That part of the emission which is induced by a resonant perturbing electromagnetic field. The transition between states, n and m, is governed by the einstein coefficient of stimulated emission, \\(B_{nm}\\). CIDNP emission and lasing action are examples of processes which require stimulated emission.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06016))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06016 "stirred flow reactor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06016 "A reactor within which effective mixing is achieved, often by placing the catalyst in a rapidly-rotating basket.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06017))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06017 "stochastic sampling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06017 "Synonymous with random sampling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06018))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06018 "stochastic theories")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06018 "Theories that treat reaction rates in terms of the probabilities of transitions between the various energy levels in the reactant molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06019 "Stockholm convention")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06019 "IUPAC convention on signs of electromotive forces and electrode potentials.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06021))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06021 "stoichiometric")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06021 "Involving chemical combination in simple integral ratios. Characterized by having no excess of reactants or products over that required to satisfy the balanced chemical equation representing the given chemical reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06023))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06023 "stoichiometric concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06023 "The stoichiometric concentration, \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\), of a component B in a system is given by the expression: \\[c_{\\rm{B}}=\\frac{n_{\\rm{B}}}{V}\\] where \\(n_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the stoichiometric amount of substance of component B in the system and \\(V\\) is the volume of the system. The component added to the system may dissociate or react with other components to form a series of derived components and only a fraction of the original component may actually exist in a free form in the system. It is therefore essential to distinguish between the stoichiometric concentration and the amount-of-substance concentration of the free form of the component in the system. Sometimes stoichiometric quantities are indicated by a subscript (\\(_{\\circ}\\)), e.g. \\(n_{\\rm{o,B}}\\), \\(c_{\\rm{o,B}}\\). In clinical chemistry, the term stoichiometric concentration is rarely employed. Instead, the name of the component is modified to indicate inclusion of the various derived forms, e.g. mixtures of a defined chemical component and its derivatives may be denoted by the plural form of the name of the pure unchanged substance, or to indicate the sum of components specified in individual quantities the specification 'total' may be employed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06024))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06024 "stoichiometric mean molal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06024 """The practical activity coefficient of electrolyte B is given by \\[\\gamma _{\\pm }=\\frac{a_{\\pm }}{\\sqrt{
u _{\\rm{+}}^{
u _{\\rm{+}}}\\ 
u _{\\rm{-}}^{
u _{\\rm{-}}}}\\ \\frac{m_{\\rm{B}}}{m^{\\rm{+}}}}\\] where \\(a_{\\pm }\\) is the mean activity of B in solution, \\(m_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the molality of B, \\(m^{+} = \\pu{1 mol kg-1}\\), \\(
u _{\\rm{+}}\\) is the number of cations and \\(
u _{\\unicode{x2013}}\\) the number of anions in the chosen group B which is taken as the electrolyte. \\[
u = 
u _{\\rm{+}}+
u _{\\rm{-}}\\]""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06024 "practical activity coefficient")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06025))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06025 "stoichiometric number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06025 """A chemical reaction of known stoichiometry can be written in general as: \\[a\\rm{A}\\,+\\,b\\rm{B}\\,+\\,...\\rightarrow\\,...\\,+y\\rm{Y}\\,+\\,z\\rm{Z}\\] For the reaction products Y and Z the numbers \\(y\\) and \\(z\\) are known as the stoichiometric numbers, \\(
u _{\\rm{Y}}\\) and \\(
u _{\\rm{Z}}\\), for Y and Z respectively. For the reactants the stoichiometric numbers are the negatives of the numbers appearing in the equation; for example the stoichiometric number \\(
u _{\\rm{A}}\\) for the reactant A is \\(-a\\). In other words, the stoichiometric numbers are positive for products and negative for reactants.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06026))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06026 "stoichiometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06026 "The term refers to the relationship between the amounts of substances that react together in a particular chemical reaction, and the amounts of products that are formed. The general stoichiometric equation: \\[a\\rm{A}\\,+\\,b\\rm{B}\\,+\\,...\\,\\rightarrow\\,...\\,+\\,y\\rm{Y}\\,+\\,z\\rm{Z}\\] provides the information that a moles of A reacts with b moles of B to produce y moles of Y and z moles of Z. The stoichiometry of a reaction may be unknown, or may be very complex. For example, the thermal decomposition of acetaldehyde yields mainly methane and carbon monoxide, but also a variety of minor products such as ethane, acetone and diacetyl. The stoichiometric equation:  is therefore only an approximate one. Even when the overall stoichiometry of a reaction is well defined, it may be time-dependent in that it varies during the course of a reaction. Thus if a reaction occurs by the mechanism \\(\\rm{A}\\rightarrow \\rm{X}\\rightarrow \\rm{Y}\\), and X is formed in substantial amounts during the course of the process, the relationship between the amounts of A, X and Y will vary with time, and no one stoichiometric equation can represent the reaction at all times.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06027))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06027 "stokes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06027 "cgs unit of kinematic viscosity, \\(\\rm{St} = 10^{-4}\\ \\rm{m}^{2}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06028))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06028 "Stokes law")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06028 """\\(F=6\\ \\pi \\ \\eta \\ r\\ 
u\\), where \\(F\\) is the force exerted on a sphere of radius \\(r\\) which is moving through a fluid of viscosity \\(\\eta \\) with a relative velocity \\(
u\\); this equation holds at low velocities which are free from turbulence (called the stokes region).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06029))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06029 "Stokes number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06029 """Sometimes referred to as the inertial parameter; it is an index of the impactability of an aerosol particle. It is defined by the equation: \\(St = 2\\ \\tau \\ (V_{\\rm{t}}- 
u_{\\rm{t}})\\ D_{\\rm{p}}\\) where \\(D_{\\rm{p}}\\) is the diameter of a small drop, \\(V_{\\rm{t}}- 
u_{\\rm{t}}\\) is the difference in fall velocities of the drop and aerosol particles and \\(\\tau \\) is the characteristic relaxation time of a particle.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06030))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06030 "Stokes parameters")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06030 "Of elliptically polarized incident radiation, these are given by \\[s_{0}^{0}=E_{1}^{0}+E_{2}^{0}\\] \\[s_{1}^{0}=E_{1}^{0}- E_{2}^{0}\\] \\[s_{2}^{0}=2\\ \\sqrt{E_{1}^{0}\\ E_{2}^{0}}\\ \\cos   \\delta ^{0}\\] \\[s_{3}^{0}=2\\ \\sqrt{E_{1}^{0}\\ E_{2}^{0}}\\ \\sin   \\delta ^{0}\\] where \\(E_{1}^{0}\\) and \\(E_{2}^{0}\\) specify the irradiances of the incident light polarized with their electric vectors vibrating perpendicular and parallel to the scattering, respectively and \\(\\delta ^{0}\\) is the phase difference between these electric vectors.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06031))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06031 "Stokes shift")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06031 "The difference (usually in frequency units) between the spectral positions of the band maxima (or the band origin) of the absorption and luminescence arising from the same electronic transition. Generally, the luminescence occurring at a longer wavelength than the absorption is stronger than the opposite. The latter may be called an anti-Stokes shift.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06032))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06032 "Stokes type radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06032 "Fluorescence radiation occurring at wavelengths longer than absorption, i.e. the normal case, is said to be of the Stokes type.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06033))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06033 "stopped flow")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06033 "A technique for following the kinetics of reactions in solution (usually in the millisecond time range) in which two reactant solutions are rapidly mixed by being forced through a mixing chamber. The flow of the mixed solution along a uniform tube is then suddenly arrested. At a fixed position along the tube the solution is monitored (as a function of time following the stoppage of the flow) by some method with a rapid response (e.g. photometry).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06034))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06034 "stopped-flow cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06034 "A small-volume absorption cell connected to a rapid mixing chamber.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06035))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06035 "stopping power")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06035 "Of a substance, for charged particles of specified energy, the average energy loss in passing through a thin layer of that substance, divided by the thickness of that layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06036))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06036 "STP")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06036 "Abbreviation for standard temperature (\\(273.15\\ \\rm{K}\\) or \\(0\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\)) and pressure (\\(10^{5}\\ \\rm{Pa}\\)); usually employed in reporting gas volumes. Note that flow meters calibrated in standard gas volumes per unit time often refer to volumes at \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), not \\(0\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06037))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06037 "strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06037 "Strain is present in a molecular entity or transition structure if the energy is enhanced because of unfavourable bond lengths, bond angles or dihedral angles ('torsional strain') relative to a standard. It is quantitatively defined as the standard enthalpy of a structure relative to a strainless structure (real or hypothetical) made up from the same atoms with the same types of bonding. (The enthalpy of formation of cyclopropane is \\(\\pu{53.6 kJ mol-1}\\), whereas the enthalpy of formation based on three 'normal' methylene groups, from acyclic models, is \\(\\pu{-62 kJ mol-1}\\). On this basis cyclopropane is destabilized by ca. \\(\\pu{115 kJ mol-1}\\) of strain energy.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06037 "steric strain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06038))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06038 "strain-induced transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06038 "A transition in a solid that is initiated by plastic strain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06039))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06039 "stratified film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06039 "A film in which more than two thicknesses coexist in a fixed configuration over significant periods of time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06040))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06040 "stratified sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06040 "A sample consisting of portions obtained from identified subparts (strata) of the parent population. Within each stratum, the samples are taken randomly. The objective of taking stratified samples is to obtain a more representative sample than that which might otherwise be obtained by random sampling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06041))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06041 "stratocumulus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06041 "A layer of patches composed of laminae or globular masses arranged in groups, lines or waves and having a soft, grey appearance; very often the rolls are so close together that their edges join and give the undersurface a wavy character; the process of formation (cumulogenesis) involves the spreading out of the tops of cumulus clouds, the latter having disappeared; \\(")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06042))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06042 "stratopause")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06042 "That region of the atmosphere which lies between the stratosphere and the mesosphere and in which a maximum in the temperature occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06043))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06043 "stratosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06043 "The atmospheric shell lying just above the troposphere which is characterized by an increasing temperature with altitude. The stratosphere begins at the tropopause (about \\(10{-}15\\ \\rm{km}\\) height) and extends to a height of about \\(50\\ \\rm{km}\\), where the lapse rate changes sign at the stratopause and the beginning of the mesosphere.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06044))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06044 "stratus cloud")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06044 "A uniform, featureless layer of cloud resembling fog but not resting on the ground; when this very low layer is broken up into irregular shreds, it is designated as fractostratus; \\(300{-}600\\ \\rm{m}\\); widespread irregular stirring and lifting of the shallow layer of cool, damp air near the ground.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06045))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06045 "stray radiation error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06045 "A spectrometer set to pass radiation of a particular wavelength band always has a small amount of stray radiation of other wavelengths. Since the sample may absorb more (or less) of this stray radiation than of the radiation at the selected wavelength an error can occur. This error is called a stray radiation error.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06047))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06047 "streak tube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06047 "An image converter adapted to provide scanning or time-resolved images. If the image is recorded the whole device is an example of a streak camera.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06048))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06048 "streaming birefringence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06048 "The birefringence induced by flow in liquids, solutions and dispersions of optically anisotropic, anisometric or deformable flow molecules or particles due to a non-random orientation of the molecules or particles. Synonymous with flow birefringence.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06048 "flow birefringence")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06049))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06049 "streaming current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06049 "The electric current \\(I\\) flowing in a streaming cell if the electrodes, which are supposed to be ideally depolarized, are short-circuited. \\(I\\) is positive if the current in the membrane, plug, etc., is from high to low pressure side (and in the outside lead from low to high pressure side).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06050))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06050 "streaming potential difference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06050 "The potential difference \\(E_{\\mathrm{st}}\\) or \\(E\\) at zero current caused by the flow of liquid under a pressure gradient through a membrane, plug or capillary. Identical electrodes must be used on both sides of the membrane, plug, etc. \\(E\\) is positive if the higher potential is on the high pressure side.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06050 "streaming potential")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06051))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06051 "stress-assisted transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06051 "A transition that takes place when an applied stress assists the transition to the new phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06052))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06052 "stress graphitization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06052 "The solid-state transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment combined with application of mechanical stress, resulting in a defined degree of graphitization being obtained at a lower temperature and/or after a shorter time of heat treatment than in the absence of applied stress.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06054))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06054 "stripping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06054 "The process of removing solute(s) from a loaded solvent or extract. Generally this refers to the main solute(s) present.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06054 "back extraction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06057))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06057 "stripping reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06057 "A chemical process, studied in a molecular beam, in which the reaction products are scattered forward with respect to the moving centre of mass of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06058))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06058 "stripping solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06058 "A solution (usually aqueous, sometimes water alone) used for back-extracting the distribuend from the extract (usually organic).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06059))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06059 "strong collision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06059 "A collision between two molecules in which the amount of energy transferred from one to the other is large compared with \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T\\), where \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(T\\) the absolute temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06060))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06060 "structural disorder")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06060 "Any deviation from the ideal three-dimensional regularity of the crystal structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06061))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06061 "structural formula")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06061 "A formula which gives information about the way the atoms in a molecule are connected and arranged in space.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06062))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06062 "structural transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06062 "A reversible or irreversible transition that involves a change of the crystal structure. Example: The transition of $\\ce{NH4Cl}$ at $\\pu{469 K}$ from a $\\ce{CsCl}$-type structure to a $\\ce{NaCl}$-type structure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06063))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06063 "structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06063 "The distribution in space of the atoms or ions in the material part of the catalyst and, in particular, the distribution at the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06064))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06064 "styphnates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06064 "Salts or charge-transfer complexes of styphnic acid (2,4,6-trinitrobenzene-1,3-diol).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06065))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06065 "subchain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06065 "An arbitrarily chosen contiguous sequence of constitutional units in a chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06066))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06066 "subgroup-supergroup transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06066 "A transition in which the space-group symmetry of the lower symmetry phase is a subgroup of that of the higher symmetry phase. Example: The transition of the low-temperature polymorph of quartz characterized by space-group symmetry \\(P3_{1}2\\) (trigonal), to the high temperature polymorph of quartz with space-group symmetry, \\(P6_{2}22\\) (hexagonal). Synonymous with 'symmetry-breaking transition' (note: this term, strictly speaking, is no longer in use).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06066 "symmetry-breaking transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06067))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06067 "subjacent orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06067 "The Next-to-Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital ('NHOMO', also called 'HOMO') and the Second Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital (SLUMO). Subjacent orbitals are sometimes found to play an important role in the interpretation of molecular interactions according to the frontier orbital approach.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06068))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06068 "sublation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06068 "A flotation process in which the material of interest, adsorbed on the surface of gas bubbles in a liquid, is collected on an upper layer of immiscible liquid. There is no liquid-phase mixing in the bulk of the system; as a result recoveries can approach 100%.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06069))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06069 "sublimation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06069 "The direct transition of a solid to a vapour without passing through a liquid phase. Example: The transition of solid $\\ce{CO2}$ to $\\ce{CO2}$ vapour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06071))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06071 "subsample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06071 "A subsample may be: (a) a portion of the sample obtained by selection or division; (b) an individual unit of the lot taken as part of the sample; (c) the final unit of multistage sampling. The term 'subsample' is used either in the sense of a 'sample of a sample' or as a synonym for 'unit'. In practice, the meaning is usually apparent from the context or is defined.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06073))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06073 "substance content")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06073 "Amount-of-substance of a component divided by the mass of the system.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06073 "content")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06074))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06074 "substance flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06074 "Amount-of-substance of a component crossing a surface divided by the time. For clinical chemistry the term component is recommended. ISO uses 'substance'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06075))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06075 "substance fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06075 "Ratio of the amount-of-substance of the component to the total amount-of-substance in the system containing the component.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06076 "substituent atom")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06076 "An atom (group) that replaces one or more hydrogen atoms attached to a parent structure or characteristic group except for hydrogen atoms attached to a chalcogen atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06078 "substitution reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06078 "A reaction, elementary or stepwise, in which one atom or group in a molecular entity is replaced by another atom or group. For example,")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06079 "substitutive name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06079 "A name which indicates the exchange of one or more hydrogen atoms attached to a skeletal atom of a parent structure or to an atom of a characteristic group for another atom or group, which may be expressed by a suffix or by prefixes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06080 "substoichiometric extraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06080 "Solvent extraction in which the amount of reagent used is lower than that dictated by stoichiometry. If the constants of formation and extraction of the complexes are high, the amount of extracted metal is dictated by the amount of extractant introduced.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06081 "substoichiometric isotope dilution analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06081 "A kind of isotope dilution analysis in which the final isotopic abundance is estimated from the amount of the nuclide present in a known quantity of the relevant element separated from the test portion, where this quantity is smaller than the total amount of that element present in the test portion.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06082 "substrate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06082 "A chemical species, the reaction of which with some other chemical reagent is under observation (e.g. a compound that is transformed under the influence of a catalyst). The term should be used with care. Either the context or a specific statement should always make it clear which chemical species in a reaction is regarded as the substrate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06083 "substrate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06083 "The chemical entity whose conversion to a product or products is catalysed by one or several enzymes.A solution or dry mixture containing all ingredients which are necessary for the growth of a microbial culture or for product formation.Component in the nutrient medium, supplying the organisms with carbon (C-substrate), nitrogen (N-substrate), etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06084 "substrate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06084 "The terms substrate and subphase refer to a condensed phase that physically supports a film or layer. The term substrate should be used with care in the light of the existing double meaning of the term substrate in film science and technology, on the one hand, and in biochemistry, on the other. The term subphase applies, mainly, to a liquid phase underlying an adsorption layer or a floating spread monolayer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06085 "subtractive name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06085 "A name for a modified parent structure in which prefixes and/or suffixes indicate the removal of atoms or groups and, where required, replacement by an appropriate number of hydrogen atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06086 "successor complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06086 "The radical ion pair which forms by the transfer of an electron from the donor D to the acceptor A after these species have diffused together to form the precursor or encounter complex: \\[\\rm{A}+\\rm{D}\\rightarrow (\\rm{A D})\\rightarrow (\\rm{A}^{\\cdot - }\\rm{D}^{\\cdot +})\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06088 "sugars")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06088 "A loose term applied to monosaccharides and lower oligosaccharides.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06091 "sulfamic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06091 "$\\ce{H2NS(=O)2OH}$ (sulfamic acid) and its N-hydrocarbyl derivatives. Sulfamic acid is called sulfamidic acid in inorganic chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06092))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06092 "sulfanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06092 "A term including hydropolysulfides, polysulfanes, polysulfides. Use is discouraged because of confusion with the newer systematic name sulfane, $\\ce{H2S}$, and the numerous names derived therefrom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06093))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06093 "sulfatides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06093 "Hydrogen sulfate esters of glycosphingolipids. Specific compounds should be named as glycerosphingolipid derivatives, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06094))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06094 "sulfenamides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06094 """Compounds derived from sulfenic acids, $\\ce{RSOH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), by replacement of $\\ce{–OH}$ by $\\ce{–NR2}$. May alternatively be considered as alkylsulfanylamines, e.g. $\\ce{C2H5SNH2}$ ethanesulfenamide or ethylsulfanylamine.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06094 "alkylsulfanylamines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06095))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06095 "sulfenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06095 "S,S-Dioxides of thioaldehydes and thioketones, $\\ce{R2C=SO2}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06096))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06096 "sulfenic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06096 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSOH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{PhSOH}$ benzenesulfenic acid.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06097 "sulfenium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06097 "A term sometimes erroneously used for sulfenylium ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06098 "sulfenyl groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06098 """Groups having the structure $\\ce{RS–}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanyl groups is derived from sulfane, $\\ce{H2S}$. E.g. $\\ce{CH3–S–}$ methanesulfenyl, methylthio or methylsulfanyl.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06098 "sulfanyl groups")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06099))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06099 "sulfenylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06099 """Cations having the structure $\\ce{RS^{+}}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanylium ions is derived from sulfane, $\\ce{H2S}$. E.g. $\\ce{CH3S^{+}}$ methylsulfanylium or methanesulfenylium ion.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06099 "sulfanylium ions")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06101 "sulfenyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06101 """Sulfur-centered radicals having the structure $\\ce{RS^{.}}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanyl radicals is derived from sulfane, $\\ce{H2S}$. E.g. $\\ce{CH3S^{.}}$ methylsulfanyl or methanesulfenyl radical. An older synonymous term is alkylthio radical.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06101 "thiyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06101 "alkylthio radical")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06101 "sulfanyl radicals")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06102 "sulfides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06102 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSR}$ ($\\ce{R 
eq H}$). Such compounds were once called thioethers.See also: thioacetalsIn an inorganic sense, salts or other derivatives of hydrogen sulfide.A term used in additive nomenclature, see imides (2).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06104))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06104 "sulfimides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06104 "$\\ce{H2S=NH}$, sulfimide, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. They are thus related to sulfoxides in the same way that imines are related to aldehydes or ketones, e.g. $\\ce{(C2H5)2S=NPh}$ S,S-diethyl-N-phenylsulfimide.A term used in Chemical Abstracts Service Index Nomenclature for sulfonylamines, $\\ce{RN=S(=O)2}$.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06104 "sulfilimines")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06104 "sulfimines")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06104 "sulfinimines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06106 "sulfinamides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06106 "Amides of sulfinic acids, $\\ce{RS(=O)OH}$; thus $\\ce{RS(=O)NR2}$, e.g. $\\ce{PhS(=O)NHCH3}$ N-methylbenzenesulfinamide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06107 "sulfinamidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06107 "Amidines of sulfinic acids, $\\ce{RS(=O)OH}$; thus $\\ce{RS(=NR)NR}$, e.g. $\\ce{PhS(=NH)NH2}$ benzenesulfinamidine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06109 "sulfinic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06109 "$\\ce{HS(=O)OH}$, sulfinic acid, and its S-hydrocarbyl derivatives, e.g. $\\ce{(CH3)2CHS(=O)OH}$ propane-2-sulfinic acid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06110))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06110 "sulfinic anhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06110 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RS(=O)OS(=O)R}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06112))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06112 "sulfinylamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06112 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RN=S=O}$. Formerly called thionylamines.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06112 "thionylamines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06113))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06113 "sulfolipids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06113 "Sulfate esters of glycolipids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06114))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06114 "sulfonamides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06114 "Amides of sulfonic acids $\\ce{RS(=O)2NR'2}$, e.g. $\\ce{PhS(=O)2NHCH3}$ N-methylbenzenesulfonamide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06115))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06115 "sulfonamidines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06115 "A term abandoned because of its ambiguous use to mean either $\\ce{RS(=O)=NH2}$ (sulfonimidamide) or $\\ce{RS(=NH)2=NH2}$ (sulfonodiimidamide).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06116))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06116 "sulfonediimines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06116 "Compounds having the structure, $\\ce{RS(=NR)2R}$, formally derived from sulfones by replacing $\\ce{(=O)2}$ by $\\ce{(=NR)2}$, e.g. $\\ce{Ph2S(=NH)2}$ diphenyl sulfonediimine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06117))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06117 "sulfones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06117 """Compounds having the structure, $\\ce{RS(=O)2R}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{C2H5S(=O)2CH3}$ ethyl methyl sulfone.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06118))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06118 "sulfonic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06118 "$\\ce{HS(=O)2OH}$, sulfonic acid, and its S-hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06119))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06119 "sulfonic anhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06119 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RS(=O)2OS(=O)2R'}$, e.g. $\\ce{PhS(=O)2OS(=O)2Ph}$ benzenesulfonic anhydride.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06121))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06121 "sulfonium compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06121 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R3S+}$ and associated anion (generally, but not necessarily, all three $\\ce{R}$ groups are hydrocarbyl). E.g. $\\ce{[(CH3)3S]^{+}Cl^{−}}$, trimethylsulfonium chloride, or")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06122))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06122 "sulfonphthaleins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06122 "3,3-Bis(hydroxyaryl)-3H-2,1-benzoxathiole S,S-dioxides, formed by condensation of o-sulfobenzoic acid with phenols or related compounds, e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06123))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06123 "sulfonylamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06123 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RN=S(=O)2}$. (Indexed by Chemical Abstracts Service at the heading sulfimide). E.g. $\\ce{CH3N=S(=O)2}$ N-sulfonylmethylamine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06124))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06124 "sulfoxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06124 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2S=O}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{Ph2S=O}$ diphenyl sulfoxide.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06125))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06125 "sulfoximides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06125 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2S(=O)=NR}$. (Indexed by Chemical Abstracts Service at the heading sulfoximines). E.g. $\\ce{(CH3)2S(=O)=NPh}$ S,S-dimethyl-N-phenylsulfoximide.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06125 "sulfoximines")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06125 "sulfonimides")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06127))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06127 "sulfur diimides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06127 "The parent compound $\\ce{HN=S=NH}$ and hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06128))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06128 "sultams")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06128 "Sulfonamides in which the S-N bond is part of a ring.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06129))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06129 "sultims")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06129 "Tautomeric forms of sultams, having a sulfur–nitrogen double bond as part of a ring.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06130))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06130 "sultines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06130 "Intramolecular cyclic esters of hydroxy sulfinic acids. (Named by supposed analogy with lactones and sultones; but the -ine ending is unfortunate).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06131))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06131 "sultones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06131 "Intramolecular cyclic esters of hydroxy sulfonic acids, analogous to lactones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06132 "summit current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06132 "In ac polarography, differential pulse polarography, derivative polarography, square-wave polarography, and similar techniques, the maximum value of the component of the current that is associated with the presence of a substance B. Normally this component of the current is faradaic, and the maximum arises because the rate of variation (with applied potential) of the rate of the charge-transfer process passes through a maximum. Similar maxima arise when this component is non-faradaic (and when B is surface-active rather than electroactive). In a case known to be of the latter type, the term apex current is recommended as being more specific.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06133))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06133 "summit potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06133 "In ac polarography, differential pulse polarography, derivative polarography, and similar techniques, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the summit current is attained.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06134))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06134 "sum of states")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06134 "The total number of states for a system corresponding to an energy \\(\\varepsilon\\) less than or equal to a specified value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06135))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06135 "superacid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06135 "A medium having a high acidity, generally greater than that of 100 wt.% sulfuric acid. The common superacids are made by dissolving a powerful Lewis acid (e.g. $\\ce{SbF5}$) in a suitable Brønsted acid such as $\\ce{HF}$ or $\\ce{HSO3F}$. (An equimolar mixture of $\\ce{HSO3F}$ and $\\ce{SbF5}$ is known by the trade name 'magic acid'.) In a biochemical context 'superacid catalysis' is sometimes used to denote catalysis by metal ions analogous to catalysis by hydrogen ions. By analogy, a compound having a very high basicity, such as lithium diisopropylamide, is called a 'superbase'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06137))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06137 "superconducting transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06137 "A transition at the critical temperature, \\(T_{\\rm{c}}\\), below which the resistance of electrical conductors becomes zero and magnetic flux is excluded.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06138))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06138 "supercritical fluid")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06138 "The defined state of a compound, mixture or element above its critical pressure (\\(p_{\\rm{c}}\\)) and critical temperature (\\(T_{\\rm{c}}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06139))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06139 "supercritical fluid chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06139 "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a fluid above and relatively close to its critical temperature and pressure. In general the terms and definitions used in gas or liquid chromatography are equally applicable to supercritical fluid chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06140))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06140 "superequivalent adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06140 "This occurs when the specifically adsorbed amount of charge in the inner Helmholtz plane is higher than the charge on the metal phase, taken with the reverse sign.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06141))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06141 "superexchange interaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06141 "Electronic interaction between two molecular entities mediated by one or more different molecules or ions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06142 "superficial work")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06142 "The reversible work of formation of unit area of new surface by cleavage. It has previously been called specific surface work, surface energy, surface tension or surface free energy. The symbol recommended is \\(\\gamma _{\\pi }\\) in preference to \\(\\sigma \\) because of the confusion with surface charge density. The superficial work \\(\\gamma _{\\pi }\\) is a scalar quantity and consequently is isotropic.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06143))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06143 "superlattice")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06143 "A periodic multilayer which is synthetic and where a unit cell, consisting of successive layers that are chemically different from their adjacent neighbours, is repeated. The term quantum well multilayer applies to the superlattices with artificially created electronic band structures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06144 "superposability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06144 "The ability to bring two particular stereochemical formulae (or models) into coincidence (or to be exactly superposable in space, and for the corresponding molecular entities or objects to become exact replicas of each other) by no more than translation and rigid rotation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06145 "superradiance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06145 "Spontaneous emission amplified by a single pass through a population inverted medium. It is distinguished from true laser action by its lack of coherence. The term superradiance is frequently used in laser technology.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06146 "supersaturation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06146 "In chemistry, an unstable system which has a greater concentration of a material in solution than would exist at equilibrium is said to be supersaturated.In meteorology, supersaturation of an air mass with respect to $\\ce{H2O}$ vapour is of special interest. It is the saturation ratio minus one, or the percent supersaturation is the percent relative humidity minus 100.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06147 "support")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06147 "In multiphase catalysts, the active catalytic material is often present as the minor component dispersed upon a support sometimes called a carrier. The support may be catalytically inert but it may contribute to the overall catalytic activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06148))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06148 "support-coated open-tubular column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06148 "A version of a porous-layer open-tabular (PLOT) column in which the porous layer consists of support particles and was deposited from a suspension.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06148 "SCOT column")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06149))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06149 "supporting electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06149 "An electrolyte solution, whose constituents are not electroactive in the range of applied potentials being studied, and whose ionic strength (and, therefore, contribution to the conductivity) is usually much larger than the concentration of an electroactive substance to be dissolved in it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06150))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06150 "support plate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06150 "The plate that supports the thin layer in thin-layer chromatography.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06151 "suppressor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06151 "A substance which reduces emission, absorption, or light scattering by an interferent, thus removing or lowering spectral interference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06153 "supramolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06153 "A system of two or more molecular entities held together and organized by means of intermolecular (noncovalent) binding interactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06154 "surface")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06154 "The boundary between two phases. It is recommended that for the purpose of surface analysis a distinction be made between 'surface' in general, 'physical surface' and 'experimental surface': Surface — The 'outer portion' of a sample of undefined depth; to be used in general discussions of the outside regions of the sample. Physical Surface — That atomic layer of a sample which, if the sample were placed in a vacuum, is the layer 'in contact with' the vacuum; the outermost atomic layer of a sample. Experimental Surface — That portion of the sample with which there is significant interaction with the particles or radiation used for excitation. It is the volume of sample required for analysis or the volume corresponding to the escape for the emitted radiation or particle, whichever is larger.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06156 "surface amount")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06156 "Amount of substance adsorbed on a surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06157))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06157 "surface barrier semiconductor detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06157 "A semiconductor detector utilizing a junction due to a surface inversion layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06159))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06159 "surface charge density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06159 "Electric charge on a surface divided by the surface area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06161 "surface chemical potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06161 """Defined by: \\[\\mu _{i}^{\\sigma }=(\\frac{\\partial A^{\\sigma }}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\sigma }})_{T,A_{\\rm{S}},n_{j}^{\\sigma }}=(\\frac{\\partial G^{\\sigma }}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\sigma }})_{T,p,\\gamma ,n_{j}^{\\sigma }}\\] \\[\\mu _{i}^{\\rm{S}}=(\\frac{\\partial A^{\\rm{S}}}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{S}}})_{T,V^{\\rm{S}},A_{\\rm{S}},n_{j}^{\\rm{S}}}=(\\frac{\\partial G^{\\rm{S}}}{\\partial n_{i}^{\\rm{S}}})_{T,p,\\gamma ,n_{j}^{\\rm{S}}}\\] where \\(A^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess Helmholtz energy, \\(G^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess Gibbs energy, \\(A^{\\rm{S}}\\) is the interfacial Helmholtz energy, \\(G^{\\rm{S}}\\) is the interfacial Gibbs energy, and \\(A_{\\rm{S}}\\) is the surface area. The quantities thus defined can be shown to be identical, and the conditions of equilibrium of component \\(i\\) in the system to be \\[\\mu _{i}^{\\alpha }=\\mu _{i}^{\\sigma }=\\mu _{i}^{\\rm{S}}=\\mu _{i}^{\\beta }\\] where \\(\\mu _{i}^{\\alpha }\\) and \\(\\mu _{i}^{\\beta }\\) are the chemical potentials of \\(i\\) in the bulk phases $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$. (\\(\\mu _{i}^{\\alpha }\\) or \\(\\mu _{i}^{\\beta }\\) have to be omitted from this equlibrium condition if component \\(i\\) is not present in the respective bulk phase.) The surface chemical potentials are related to the Gibbs energy functions by the equations \\[G^{\\sigma }=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}n_{i}^{\\sigma }\\ \\mu _{i}^{\\sigma }\\] \\[G^{\\rm{S}}=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c}
i
\\end{array}}n_{i}^{\\rm{S}}\\ \\mu _{i}^{\\rm{S}}\\]""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06162))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06162 "surface concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06162 "Amount of substance adsorbed on a surface divided by the surface area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06163 "surface excess conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06163 "The excess conductivity in a surface per unit length and width, symbol \\(\\kappa ^{\\sigma }\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06164 "surface contamination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06164 "Material in the experimental surface which is either not characteristic of the sample or which would not be present if the sample had been prepared in an absolute vacuum by methods not contacting other substances to the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06165))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06165 "surface coverage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06165 "Number of adsorbed molecules on a surface divided by the number of molecules in a filled monolayer on that surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06166 "surface crossing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06166 "In a diagram of electronic energy versus molecular geometry, the electronic energies of two states of different symmetry may be equal at certain geometrical parameters. At this point (unidimensional representation), line or surface (more than one dimension), the two potential-energy surfaces are said to cross one another.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06167 "surface density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06167 "Mass divided by area.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06169 "surface dipole layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06169 "Particles in the surface region of a phase are subjected to orienting forces as a result of the anisotropic force field. Polar molecules (e.g. permanent dipoles) may thus be preferentially oriented in the surface region, while polarizable molecules may be polarized (induced dipoles). The array of oriented polar and/or polarized molecules is called the surface dipole layer with which an electric potential drop is associated, called the surface potential of the phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06170 "surface electric potential")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06170 "Given by \\[\\chi ^{\\beta} = \\mathit{\\Phi }^{\\beta} - \\psi ^{\\beta}\\] where \\(\\psi ^{\\beta }\\) and \\(\\mathit{\\Phi} ^{\\beta}\\) are the outer and inner electric potentials of phase $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06171))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06171 "surface excess")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06171 "For an interface, the adsorption or surface excess of a given component is defined as the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system, and that which would be present (in a reference system) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface (Gibbs dividing surface). For a solid/liquid interface in which no component of the liquid phase penetrates into the solid, the surface excess (or adsorption) of component i is defined as: \\[n_{i}^{\\sigma }=n_{i}- V^{\\rm{l}}\\ c_{i}^{\\rm{l}}\\] where \\(n_{i}\\) is the total amount of i in the system, \\(V^{\\rm{l}}\\) is the volume of an arbitrarily chosen amount of bulk liquid (in the framework of the so-called algebraic method) and \\(c_{i}^{\\rm{l}}\\) is its bulk concentration in the liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06172))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06172 "surface excess concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06172 "A quantity defined by \\[\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i} = \\frac{n_{i}^{\\sigma }}{A_{\\rm{s}}}\\] where \\(n_{i}^{\\sigma}\\) is the surface excess (or adsorption) of component \\(i\\) and \\(A_{\\rm{s}}\\) is the area of the dividing surface or interface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06173))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06173 "surface excess energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06173 "Defined by: \\[U^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3} } = U - U^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }- U^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }= U - V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\ \\frac{U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }} - V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\ \\frac{U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}\\] where \\(V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) satisfy the condition \\(V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} } + V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} } = V\\), the total volume of the system. (\\(\\frac{U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}\\)) and (\\(\\frac{U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}\\)) are the energy densities in the two bulk phases where \\(U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(U_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) are the mean molar energies and \\(V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06174 "surface excess enthalpy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06174 "Defined by: \\[H^{\\sigma }=U^{\\sigma }- \\gamma \\ A_{\\rm{s}}\\] where \\(U^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess energy, \\(\\gamma \\) is the surface tension, and \\(A_{\\rm{s}}\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06175 "surface excess entropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06175 "Defined by: \\[S^{\\unicode[Times]{x3C3}} = S - S^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }- S^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }=S- V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\ \\frac{S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}- V^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\ \\frac{S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}\\] (\\(\\frac{S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }}\\)) and (\\(\\frac{S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }}\\)) are the entropy densities in the two bulk phases, where \\(S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(S_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) are the mean molar entropies and \\(V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) and \\(V_{\\rm{m}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06176 "surface excess Gibbs energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06176 "Defined by \\[G^{\\sigma }=H^{\\sigma }- T\\ S^{\\sigma }=A^{\\sigma }- \\gamma \\ A_{\\rm{s}}\\] where \\(H^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess enthalpy, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, \\(S^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess entropy, \\(A^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess Helmholtz energy, \\(\\gamma \\) is the surface tension, and \\(A_{\\rm{s}}\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06177 "surface excess Helmholtz energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06177 "Defined by: \\[A^{\\sigma }=U^{\\sigma }- T\\ S^{\\sigma }\\] where \\(U^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess energy, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature, and \\(S^{\\sigma }\\) is the surface excess entropy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06178 "surface excess isotherm")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06178 "The function relating, at constant temperature and pressure, \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{n}\\right)}\\), \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{l}\\right)}\\) or \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{v}\\right)}\\), or the respective specific quantities \\(\\frac{n^{\\rm{l}}\\ \\Delta x_{i}^{\\rm{l}}}{m}\\), \\(\\frac{A_{\\rm{s}}\\ \\mathit{\\Gamma }_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{l}\\right)}}{m}\\) or \\(\\frac{V^{\\rm{l}}\\ \\Delta c_{i}^{\\rm{l}}}{m}\\) to the mole fraction (or concentration) of component \\(i\\) in the equilibrium liquid phase. With solutions of more than two components such isotherms are unequivocal functions only when the ratios of the mole fractions (or concentrations) of all other components except \\(i\\) are kept constant. (\\(A_{\\rm{s}}\\) is the area of interface, \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{n}\\right)}\\), \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{l}\\right)}\\), \\(\\mathit{\\Gamma}_{i}^{\\left(\\rm{v}\\right)}\\) are relative adsorptions, \\(\\Delta x_{i}^{\\rm{l}}\\) is the change in mole fraction of \\(i\\) resulting from bringing a specified mass \\(m\\) of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution \\(n^{\\rm{l}}\\), \\(\\Delta c_{i}^{\\rm{l}}\\) is the change in concentration resulting from bringing a specified mass of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution of volume \\(V^{\\rm{l}}\\).)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06179 "surface ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06179 "Takes place when an atom or molecule is ionized when it interacts with a solid surface. ionization occurs only when the work function of the surface, the temperature of the surface, and the ionization energy of the atom or molecule have an appropriate relationship.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06180 "surface ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06180 "Ions that are constituents of the surface or which have a particularly high affinity for the surface or surface sites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06181 "surface layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06181 "The region of space comprising and adjoining the phase boundary within which the properties of matter are significantly different from the values in the adjoining bulk phases.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06181 "interfacial layer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06182 "surface of a phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06182 "The plane ideally marking the boundary between a phase and its environment.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06182 "free surface")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06183))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06183 "surface of tension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06183 "The mechanical properties of an interfacial layer between two fluid phases may be expressed in terms of those of a geometrical surface of uniform tension called the surface of tension, whose location is dependent on the distribution of the stress tensor within the interfacial layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06185 "surface pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06185 "The change of interfacial tension caused by addition of a given species to a base solution. When an area of liquid covered with a spread substance is separated from a clean area of surface by a mechanical barrier, the force acting on unit length of the barrier is called the surface pressure, \\(\\pi \\) or \\(\\pi _{\\rm{s}}\\), and is equal to \\(\\gamma ^{0} - \\gamma \\) where \\(\\gamma ^{0}\\) is the surface tension of the clean surface and \\(\\gamma \\) that of the covered surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06187))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06187 "surface region")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06187 "The tridimensional region, extending from the free surface of a condensed phase towards the interior, where the properties differ from the bulk. The term surface may also be used in this sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06189))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06189 "surface shear viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06189 """For steady state deformations a surface shear viscosity \\(\\eta ^{\\rm{s}}\\), and an area viscosity or surface dilatational viscosity \\(\\zeta ^{\\rm{s}}\\) can be defined. In a Cartesian system with the x-axis normal to the surface, they are defined by the equations: \\[\\eta ^{\\rm{s}} = \\frac{\\sigma _{xy}}{\\frac{\\partial 
u_{y}}{\\partial 
u_{x}}}\\] \\[\\zeta ^{\\rm{s}}=\\frac{\\Delta \\gamma }{\\frac{\\mathrm{d}(\\ln A)}{\\mathrm{d}t}}\\] where \\(\\sigma _{xy}\\) is the shear component of the surface stress tensor, \\(
u_{x}\\) and \\(
u_{y}\\) are the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) components of the surface velocity vector, respectively, \\(A\\) is the surface area, \\(t\\) is the time, and \\(\\Delta \\gamma \\) is the difference between the (steady state) dynamic surface tension and the equilibrium surface tension.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06190))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06190 "surface states")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06190 "Energy levels localized in the surface region of semiconductors, which do not bear any direct relation to the bulk energy distribution, but which can exchange electrons with the bulk. In such a case a space charge may arise even when \\(\\sigma \\) (the free charge density) is zero, i.e. \\(\\sigma =\\sigma _{\\mathrm{sc}}+\\sigma _{\\mathrm{ss}}\\), where \\(\\sigma _{\\mathrm{sc}}\\) is the space charge density and \\(\\sigma _{\\mathrm{ss}}\\) is the charge density associated with the surface states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06191))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06191 "surface stress")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06191 "The work required to form unit area of new surface by stretching under equilibrium conditions. It is numerically equal to the force acting in the jth direction per unit length of exposed edge, the edge being normal to the ith direction, that must be applied to a terminating surface to keep it in equilibrium, the ith and jth directions lying in the plane of the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06192))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06192 "surface tension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06192 "Work required to increase a surface area divided by that area. When two phases are studied it is often called interfacial tension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06193))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06193 "surface work")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06193 "Surface tension is the intensive factor in the differential expression for the work required to increase the area of the surface of tension. Measured under reversible conditions at constant temperature (and normally constant pressure) and referred to unit area, this work, the so-called (differential) surface work, is equal to the static surface tension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06194 "surfactant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06194 "A substance which lowers the surface tension of the medium in which it is dissolved, and/or the interfacial tension with other phases, and, accordingly, is positively adsorbed at the liquid/vapour and/or at other interfaces.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06194 "surface active agent")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06195 "surprisal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06195 "A function relating the prior distribution \\(P_{0}\\) of product states to an observed or calculated product distribution P. It is defined by: \\[s=- \\ln (\\frac{P}{P_{0}})\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06196))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06196 "surprisal analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06196 "The study of product state distributions in terms of surprisal has been referred to as surprisal analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06197))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06197 "suspended matter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06197 "All particulate material which persists in the atmosphere or in a flue gas stream for lengthy periods because the particles are too small in size to have an appreciable falling velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06198))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06198 "suspension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06198 "A liquid in which solid particles are dispersed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06199 "suspension effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06199 "An effect which occurs when ion-selective electrodes are used in concentrated, space-filled suspensions while the external reference electrode remains in the supernatant (suspension-free) solution. The suspensions are specifically solvent-swollen ion exchangers or other materials, like soils and clays, that concentrate ions by adsorption and absorption. Space-filled, gravity-packed suspensions act like a second phase and form apparently an interfacial potential difference (PD) with respect to the supernatant. The measured ion activity in the suspension differs from the value in the supernatant by the interfacial PD, and corresponds to a higher value approximating the activity inside the ion exchanger gel. The effect nearly disappears when the outer reference electrode is placed in the same region of the suspension as the sensor electrode. There are some changes in the junction potential differences of the reference electrode, between suspension and supernatant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06200 "Swain–Lupton equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06200 "A dual parameter approach to the correlation analysis of substituent effects, which involves a field constant (\\(F\\)) and a resonance constant (\\(R\\)). The original treatment was modified later. The procedure has been considerably applied, but also much criticized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06201))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06201 "Swain–Scott equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06201 "The linear free-energy relation of the form: \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{k}{k_{0}})=s\\ n\\] applied to the variation of reactivity of a given electrophilic substrate towards a series of nucleophilic reagents. \\(n\\) is characteristic of the reagent (i.e. a measure of its nucleophilicity) and \\(s\\) is characteristic of the substrate (i.e. a measure of its sensitivity to the nucleophilicity of the reagent). A scale of \\(n\\) values is based on the rate coefficients \\(k\\) for the reaction of methyl bromide with nucleophiles in water at \\(25\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\), \\(s\\) being defined as 1.00 for these reactions and \\(n\\) being defined as 0.00 for the hydrolysis of methyl bromide. (Other scales have been devised.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06202 "swelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06202 "The increase in volume of a gel or solid associated with the uptake of a liquid or gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06203))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06203 "swelling pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06203 "That pressure difference \\(\\mathit{\\Pi} _{\\rm{sw}}\\) or \\(\\mathit{\\Pi}\\) which has to be established between a gel and its equilibrium liquid, to prevent further swelling of the gel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06204))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06204 "switchboard model")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06204 "A model of crystallinity in which the crystallized segments of a macromolecule belong to the same crystal, although the stems are connected randomly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06205 "switching transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06205 "A transition in semiconductive glasses in which, beyond a critical applied voltage, there is an avalanche breakdown of conducting electrons that causes local melting and hence local crystallization that gives metallic conductivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06206))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06206 "sydnone imines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06206 "Imines of sydnones (i.e. having an imino group in place of the exocyclic oxygen atom).  (These structures represent only two canonical forms.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06207))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06207 "sydnones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06207 "Mesoionic compounds having the 1,2,3-oxadiazole skeleton bearing an oxygen atom attached to the 5 position.  (These structures represent only two canonical forms).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06208))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06208 "<i>sym</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06208 "An affix used in names to denote symmetrical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06209))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06209 "symbiosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06209 "The term was originally applied to describe the maximum flocking of either hard or soft ligands in the same complexes. For hydrocarbon molecules, symbiosis implies that those containing a maximum number of $\\ce{C–H}$ bonds (e.g. $\\ce{CH4}$) or $\\ce{C–C}$ bonds (e.g. $\\ce{Me4C}$) are the most stable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06210))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06210 "symbol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06210 "Conventional sign designating a quantity or a unit of measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06211))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06211 "symmetrical films")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06211 "The properties of fluid films depend on the nature of the film phase and that of each of the two neighbouring bulk phases. These films should be described, where appropriate, by three capital letters such as A for air, W for water, O for oil, and S for solid, separated by solidi, the middle letter indicating the film phase. For symmetrical films the first and last symbols are the same, e.g. A/W/A: water film in air, or W/O/W: oil film in water, whereas for unsymmetrical films these are different, e.g. W/O/A: oil film between water and air.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06213))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06213 "symmetry-conserving transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06213 "A transition in which the cell dimensions and/or angles of the one phase differ from those in the other phase, but where the space-group symmetry is conserved. Example: The transition of face-centred cubic $\\ce{Ce}$, upon cooling, to a face-centred cubic phase that is $\\pu{10\\%}$ denser. Upon cooling, enough contraction takes place to allow an overlap of the \\(\\rm{fsp}^{2}\\) configuration and the change from an isolated non-bonding magnetic f electron to a bonding non-magnetic electron pair.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06214))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06214 "symmetry number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06214 "The symmetry number of a molecule is obtained by imagining all identical atoms to be labelled, and then counting the number of different but equivalent arrangements that can be obtained by rotating (but not reflecting) the molecule. In the statistical-mechanical treatment of chemical equilibrium, the partition function for each molecular species must be divided by its symmetry number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06216))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06216 "<i>syn</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06216 "See: torsion angleSee: endo, exo, syn, antiSee: antiFormerly used to describe the stereochemistry of oximes and related systems.See: E, Z")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06218))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06218 "synchronization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06218 "This principle applies to reactions in which there is a lack of synchronization between bond formation or bond rupture and other primitive changes that affect the stability of products and reactants, such as resonance, solvation, electrostatic, hydrogen bonding and polarizability effects. The principle states that a product-stabilizing factor whose development lags behind bond changes at the transition state, or a reactant-stabilizing factor whose loss is ahead of bond changes at the transition state, increases the intrinsic barrier and decreases the 'intrinsic rate constant' of a reaction. For a product-stabilizing factor whose development is ahead of bond changes, or reactant factors whose loss lags behind bond changes, the opposite relations hold. The reverse effects are observable for factors that destabilize a reactant or product.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06219))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06219 "synchronous")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06219 "A concerted process in which the primitive change concerned (generally bond rupture and bond formation) have progressed to the same extent at the transition state is said to be synchronous. The term figuratively implies a more or less synchronized progress of the changes. However, the progress of the bonding change (or other primitive change) has not been defined quantitatively in terms of a single parameter applicable to different bonds or different bonding changes. The concept is therefore in general only qualitatively descriptive and does not admit an exact definition except in the case of concerted processes involving changes in two identical bonds.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06220))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06220 "synchronously excited spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06220 "A two-dimensional spectrum obtained by varying both the excitation and emission wavelengths simultaneously and which corresponds to the curve where a plane, parallel to the z-axis, intersects the excitation-emission spectrum (EES).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06221))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06221 "synchrotron radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06221 "X-Radiation which results from the acceleration of charged particles in circular orbits by strong electric and magnetic fields.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06223))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06223 "syndet")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06223 "A synthetic detergent; a detergent other than soap.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06224))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06224 "syndiotactic macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06224 "A tactic macromolecule, essentially comprising alternating enantiomeric configurational base units, which have chiral or prochiral atoms in the main chain in a unique arrangement with respect to their adjacent constitutional units.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06225))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06225 "syndiotactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06225 "A polymer composed of syndiotactic macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06227 "syneresis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06227 "Spontaneous shrinking of a gel with exudation of liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06228 "synergism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06228 "A term describing the co-operative effect of two (or more) extractants where the distribution ratio for the combination is greater than the largest individual distribution ratio (measured under comparable conditions).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06229))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06229 "synergism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06229 "Pharmacological or toxicological interaction in which the combined biological effect of two or more substances is greater than expected on the basis of the simple summation of the toxicity of each of the individual substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06230))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06230 "synoptic scale")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06230 "In meteorology, the size or scale of ordinary weather systems or cyclones; typically \\(1000\\ \\rm{km}\\) horizontally.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06232))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06232 "syntectic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06232 "A reversible reaction that involves the conversion of two liquid phases, \\(\\rm{l}^{'}\\) and \\(\\rm{l}^{''}\\), into a solid phase $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ on cooling: \\[\\rm{l}^{'}+\\rm{l}^{''}\\rightleftharpoons \\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\] The maximum temperature at which this reaction can occur is the congruent melting point of the solid phase. Example: The conversion of co-existing $\\ce{K}$-rich and $\\ce{Zn}$-rich phases in the $\\ce{K-Zn}$ system to form an intermediate solid phase $\\ce{KZn13}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06233))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06233 "synthetic graphite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06233 "A material consisting of graphitic carbon which has been obtained by graphitizing of non-graphitic carbon by chemical vapour deposition (CVD) from hydrobcarbons at temperatures above \\(2500\\ \\rm{K}\\), by decomposition of thermally unstable carbides or by crystallizing from metal melts supersaturated with carbon.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:S06233 "artificial graphite")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06234))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06234 "system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06234 "Arbitrarily defined part of the universe, regardless of form or size, e.g. for clinical chemistry, patient, patient plasma, patient urine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06235))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06235 "systematic error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06235 "Mean that would result from an infinite number of measurements of the same measurand carried out under repeatability conditions minus a true value of the measurand.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06236))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06236 "systematic name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06236 "A name composed wholly of specially coined or selected syllables, with or without numerical prefixes; e.g. pentane, oxazole.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06237))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06237 "system of units")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06237 "Set of base units, together with derived units, defined in accordance with given rules, for a given system of quantities, e.g. Système International d'Unités (SI).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:S06239))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:S06239 "Szilard–Chalmers effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:S06239 "The rupture of the chemical bond between an atom and the molecule of which the atom is part, as a result of a nuclear reaction of that atom.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06754))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06754 "sialon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06754 "A generic name for compounds or solid solutions in the system $\\ce{M–Si–Al–O–N}$ where $\\ce{M}$ is a metal. (The use of capitals as in SiAlON is discouraged since they suggest a chemical composition.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06775))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06775 "sequence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06775 "The order of neighbouring amino acids in a protein or of the purine or pyrimidine bases in RNA or DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06784))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06784 "Soret band")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06784 "A very strong absorption band in the blue region of the optical absorption spectrum of a haem protein.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06785))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06785 "stability constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06785 "An equilibrium constant that expresses the propensity of a substance to form from its component parts. The larger the stability constant, the more stable is the species. The stability constant (formation constant) is the reciprocal of the instability constant (dissociation constant).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06802))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06802 "systemic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06802 "Consequence that is either of a generalized nature or that occurs at a site distant from the point of entry of a substance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06842 "second messenger")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06842 "Intracellular effector substance increasing or decreasing as a response to the stimulation of a receptor by an agonist, considered as the 'first messenger'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06843))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06843 "serum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06843 "Watery proteinaceous portion of the blood that remains after clotting. Synonym: blood serumClear watery fluid especially that moistening the surface of serous membranes or that exuded through inflammation of any of these membranes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06844))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06844 "stem cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06844 "Multipotent cell with mitotic potential that may serve as a precursor for many kinds of differentiated cells.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06845))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06845 "structure–activity relationship")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06845 "Association between specific aspects of molecular structure and defined biological action.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06848))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06848 "speciation analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06848 "Analytical activities of identifying and/or measuring the quantities of one or more individual chemical species in a sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06861))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06861 "speciation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06861 "Distribution of an element amongst defined chemical species in a system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06868))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06868 "side group or side-chain polymer liquid crystal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06868 "A polymer, the molecules of which have mesogenic units only in side-group's side-chains.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06872))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06872 "semi-rigid chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06872 "A chain for which the contour length is greater than the persistence length but for which their ratio is still below the Gaussian limit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06873))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06873 "spacer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06873 "A flexible segment used to link successive mesogenic units in the molecules of MCPLCs (main-chain polymer liquid crystals) or to attach mesogenic units as side-groups onto the polymer backbone of SGPLCs (side-group polymer liquid crystals).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06914))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06914 "stochastic effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06914 "Consequence for which the probability of occurrence depends on the absorbed dose; hereditary effects and cancer induced by radiation are considered to be stochastic effects. The term 'stochastic' indicates that the occurrence of effects so named would be random. This means that, even for an individual, there is no threshold of dose below which there is absolutely no probability of an effect occurring, and the chance of experiencing the effect increases with increasing dose.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06980))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06980 "simplified molecular input line entry system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06980 "SMILES (Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry System) is a string notation used to describe the nature and topology of molecular structures.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06981))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06981 "structure-based design")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06981 "Structure-based design is a design strategy for new chemical entities based on the three-dimensional (3D) structure of the target obtained by X-ray or nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) studies, or from protein homology models.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST06982))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST06982 "structure-property correlations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST06982 "Structure-property correlations (SPC) refer to all statistical mathematical methods used to correlate any molecular property (intrinsic, chemical or biological) to any other property, using statistical regression or pattern recognition techniques.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07097))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07097 "scaling factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07097 "A variational parameter used as a multiplier of each nuclear Cartesian and electronic coordinate chosen to minimize the variational integral and to make a trial variation function to satisfy the virial theorem. In practical calculations, the numeral factor to scale computed values, e.g. vibrational frequencies, to those found in experiments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07098))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07098 "secular equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07098 "In molecular orbital theory, the determinant of matrix elements whose solution leads to the set of energy levels of molecular orbitals for a given system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07099))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07099 "semi-empirical quantum mechanical methods")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07099 "Methods which use parameters derived from experimental data to simplify computations. The simplification may occur at various levels: simplification of the Hamiltonian (e.g., as in the Extended Hückel method), approximate evaluation of certain molecular integrals (see, for example, zero differential overlap approximation), simplification of the wavefunction (for example, use of a Pi ($\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$) electron approximation as in Pariser–Parr–Pople method), etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07100))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07100 "Slater determinant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07100 "The determinantal representation of many-electron wavefunction \\(\\Psi \\) which conforms to the requirement of the antisymmetry principle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07101))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07101 "soliton")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07101 "Localized excitations propagating in a system with constant velocity and colliding with each other without change in their shapes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07102))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07102 "solvation energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07102 "The change in Gibbs energy when an ion or molecule is transferred from a vacuum (or the gas phase) to a solvent. The main contributions to the solvation energy come from:  the cavitation energy of formation of the hole which preserves the dissolved species in the solvent;  the orientation energy of partial orientation of the dipoles; the isotropic interaction energy of electrostatic and dispersion origin; and  the anisotropic energy of specific interactions, e.g. hydrogen bonds, donor-acceptor interactions etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07103))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07103 "spin contamination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07103 "In unrestricted Hartree–Fock method, the wavefunctions obtained are eigenfunctions of the Hamiltonian and the spin projection \\(S_{\\rm{z}}\\) operators, but not eigenfunctions of the \\(S^{2}\\) operator. As a result, the wavefunctions of the doublet systems are spin-contaminated to some extent by admixtures of quartet, sextet, and higher spin states. The eigenvalues of the \\(S^{2}\\) operator are given as a measure of the spin contamination.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07104))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07104 "spin crossover")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07104 "A type of molecular magnetism that is the result of electronic instability (see electronic stability) caused by external constraints (temperature, pressure, or electromagnetic radiation), which induce structural changes at molecular and lattice levels. The phenomenon is most characteristic of first-row transition metal complexes, e.g. those of $\\ce{Fe^{2+}}$. An example of spin-crossover complexes (the term of spin-state isomers is also used) is $\\ce{[Fe(2-pic)3]Cl2.EtOH}$ (2-pic = 2-picolylamine). At the $\\ce{Fe–N}$ distance of \\(203.2\\ \\rm{pm}\\) (\\(115\\ \\rm{K}\\)), the complex has an electronic low-spin state ($^{1}{\\rm{A}}_{1}$), whereas stretching the bond up to \\(219.9\\ \\rm{pm}\\) at \\(227\\ \\rm{K}\\) induces the transition to a high-spin state ($^{5}{\\rm{T}}_{2}$).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07105))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07105 "spin polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07105 "Static and dynamic spin polarization effects are distinguished. The static polarization of an electron spin occurs in the $\\ce{C-H}$ bonds of aromatic radicals where the $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ closest to an unpaired $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ tends to have its spin parallel to that of the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$. Likewise, static spin polarization arises in the $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}system}$ of conjugated radicals: the electron of the doubly occupied molecular orbital prefers to take a closer spatial position to the odd one, which spin is parallel to the latter. The effect reflects the energy unfavourable situation if an electron of opposite spin were to come nearby.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07106))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07106 "spin projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07106 "A component, \\(M_{S}\\), of the angular spin moment \\(S\\) along an arbitrary axis (usually chosen as the z-direction). \\(M_{S}\\) can take the values between \\(-S\\) and \\(S\\): \\(-S\\), \\(-S + 1\\),..., \\(S- 1\\), \\(S\\). The term is also used to denote an operation (spin-symmetry projection) allowing one to eliminate spin contamination in the cases where wavefunctions are not eigenfunctions of the operator \\(S^{2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07107))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07107 "stereochemical non-rigidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07107 "The capability of a molecule to undergo fast and reversible intramolecular isomerization, the energy barrier to which is lower than that allowing for the preparative isolation of the individual isomers at room temperature. It is conventional to assign to the sterochemically non-rigid systems those compounds whose molecules rearrange rapidly enough to influence NMR line shapes at temperatures within the practical range (from \\(\\pu{-100^{\\circ}C}\\) to \\(\\pu{200^{\\circ}C}\\)) of experimentation. The energy barriers to thus defined rearrangements fall into the range of \\(5\\)–\\(\\pu{20 kcal/mol}\\) (\\(21\\)–\\(\\pu{85 kJ/mol}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07108))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07108 "strain energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07108 "The excess energy due to steric strain of a molecular entity or transition state structure, i.e. distortions relative to a reference (real or hypothetical) 'strainless' structure with the standard bond lengths, bond angles and dihedral angles. The strain energy components involve the following destabilizing terms: non-bonded repulsions, bond-angle distortions, bond stretch or compression, rotation around or twisting of double bonds, and electrostatic strain. In general, the contributions of these components are inseparable and interdependent. A quantitative assessment of strain and strain energies can be made by taking the difference between the heat of formation of the substance under consideration and that of a hypothetical strain-free model. Several approaches to the assessment of strain energies have been developed based on the use of energies of isodesmic and homodesmotic reactions and on the so-called 'strainless increments', i.e. heats of formation of certain groups ($\\ce{CH3}$, $\\ce{CH2}$, $\\ce{CH}$, $\\ce{C}$ etc).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07109))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07109 "structural stability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07109 "Within the Born–Oppenheimer (adiabatic) approximation, this is associated with an energy minimum on a potential energy surface. Structural stability implies that any change in the coordinates of the nuclei can only increase the total energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07110))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07110 "sudden polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07110 "The occurrence of a large intramolecular charge separation in the singlet excited state of polyenes and their derivatives twisted about a double bond. Non-symmetrical substitution or geometrical distortion is effective in polarizing the system. An example is the stabilization of the zwitterionic structure of 90° twisted ethene with one methylene group pyramidalized:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07111))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07111 "supramolecular chemistry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07111 "A field of chemistry related to species of greater complexity than molecules, that are held together and organized by means of intermolecular interactions. The objects of supramolecular chemistry are supermolecules and other polymolecular entities that result from the spontaneous association of a large number of components into a specific phase (membranes, vesicles, micelles, solid state structures etc.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07151 "sol-gel process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07151 "Process through which a network is formed from solution by a progressive change of liquid precursor(s) into a sol, to a gel, and in most cases finally to a dry network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07152 "surface grafting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07152 "Process in which a polymer surface is chemically modified by grafting or by the generation of active sites that can lead to the initiation of a graft polymerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07194))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07194 "shape-memory polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07194 "Polymer that, after heating and being subjected to a plastic deformation, resumes its original shape when heated above its glass-transition or melting temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07195))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07195 "superabsorbent polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07195 "Polymer that can absorb and retain extremely large amounts of a liquid relative to its own mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07227))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07227 "solid polymer electrolyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07227 "Electrically conducting solution of a salt in a polymer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07274 "spinodal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07274 "Curve defining the region of composition and temperature for a binary mixture across which a transition occurs from conditions where single-phase mixtures are metastable to conditions where single-phase mixtures are unstable and undergo phase separation by spinodal decomposition.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07294))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07294 "soft-segment phase domain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07294 "Phase domain of microscopic or smaller size, usually in a block, graft, or segmented copolymer, comprising essentially those segments of the polymer that have glass transition temperatures lower than the temperature of use.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07295 "segmented copolymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07295 "Copolymer containing phase domains of microscopic or smaller size, with the domains constituted principally of single types of structural unit.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07395 "spectral sensitivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07395 "Referred to actinometer based on spectrophotometric measurements, \\(S_{\\rm{ac}}\\left(\\lambda \\right)=\\varPhi \\left(\\lambda \\right)\\ \\varepsilon\\left(\\lambda _{\\rm{obs}}\\right)=\\rm{sensitivity or actinometric factor}\\). \\(\\varPhi \\left(\\lambda \\right)\\) is the quantum yield of the actinometer at the excitation wavelength and \\(\\lambda _{\\rm{obs}}\\) is the observation wavelength, which may be the same as or differ from the former.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07417 "self-localized excitations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07417 "Physical and chemical properties of conjugated organic polymers with $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electrons}$ have been interpreted in terms of self-localized excitations, which are quasi-particles with structural changes over several repeating units. These excitations can be classified into solitons, polarons, bipolarons and excitons according to their charge and spin.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07466))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07466 "solubility parameter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07466 "Parameter used in predicting the solubility of non-electrolytes (including polymers) in a given solvent.For a substance \\(\\rm{B}\\), \\[\\delta _{\\rm{B}} = \\left ( \\frac{\\Delta _{\\rm{vap}}E_{\\rm{m,B}}}{V_{\\rm{m,B}}} \\right )^{1/2}\\] where \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{vap}}E_{\\rm{m,B}}\\) is the molar energy of vaporization at zero pressure and \\(V_{\\rm{m,B}}\\) is the molar volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07476))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07476 "Schenck reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07476 "The diastereoselective ene reaction of singlet molecular dioxygen with alkenes. An example is:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07477))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07477 "singlet–triplet crossing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07477 "Point of intersection between the potential energy surfaces of states of different multiplicity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07478 "solvent-induced symmetry breaking")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07478 "Breaking of symmetry of a molecular species by interactions with the solvent that can modify the molecular charge distribution, to favour asymmetrical configurations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07479))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07479 "spectral fluence rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07479 "Derivative of fluence rate, \\(E_{\\rm{o}}\\), with respect to wavelength, \\(\\lambda\\). SI unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-3}\\); common unit is \\(\\rm{W m}^{-2}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07480 "spectral radiant energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07480 "Derivative of radiant energy, \\(Q\\), with respect to wavelength \\(\\lambda \\). SI unit is \\(\\pu{J m-1}\\); common unit is \\(\\pu{J nm-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07481 "spin-flip method")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07481 "Quantum mechanical method for the calculation of open-shell excited states. The method accurately describes low-lying multi-configurational electronic states of diradicals and triradicals in an efficient and robust single-reference scheme. The target low-spin states (e.g., S = 0 or S = 1/2) are described as spin-flipping excitations (S = −1) from a well-behaved high-spin reference state (e.g., S = 1 or S = 3/4). By employing theoretical models of increasing complexity for the reference (e.g., SCF, MP2, CCSD), the accuracy in the target states’ description can be systematically improved. The SF methods result in multistate single-step computational schemes, e.g., several low-lying states can be computed in a single calculation that includes both dynamical and non-dynamical correlation effects.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ST07481 "SF method")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07482))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07482 "spin-statistical factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07482 "From the possible encounter pairs between states of different spin multiplicity, only those conserving multiplicity in going to products are expected to react.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07508 "slip")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07508 "Ceramic precursor dispersed in a liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07512))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07512 "sonosol")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07512 "Sol produced by the action of ultrasonically induced cavitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07513 "sol fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07513 "Mass fraction of the dissolved or dispersed material, resulting from a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process, that is constituted of molecules of finite (statistically definable) relative molecular masses.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07530 "sonogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07530 "Colloidal gel produced by the action of ultrasonically induced cavitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07538))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07538 "swelling agent")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07538 "Fluid used to swell a gel, network or solid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07566 "sequential interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07566 "Interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the second component network is formed following the formation of the first component network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07567 "simultaneous interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07567 "Interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the component networks are formed concurrently.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07574 "sequential semi-interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07574 "Semi-interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the linear or branched components are formed following the completion of the reactions that lead to the formation of the network(s) or vice versa.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07575 "simultaneous semi-interpenetrating polymer network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07575 "Semi-interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the networks and the linear or branched components are formed concurrently.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07586 "sol-gel material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07586 "Material formed through a sol-gel process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07587 "sol-gel coating")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07587 "Coating formed through a sol-gel process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07588 "sol-gel metal oxide")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07588 "Metal oxide formed through a sol-gel process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07589 "sol-gel silica")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07589 "Silica formed through a sol-gel process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07631 "sedimentation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07631 "Separation of a dispersed system under the action of a gravitational or centrifugal field according to the different densities of the components.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07632 "shrinkage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07632 "Decrease in volume of a network, gel or solid associated with the exudation of a fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07633))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07633 "slip casting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07633 "Procedure in ceramic processing whereby slip is contained in a porous plaster mould prior to pyrolysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07634 "sol-gel critical concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07634 "Concentration of an added electrolyte above which a particulate sol undergoes coagulation instead of gelation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ST07634 "critical concentration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ST07636))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ST07636 "supercritical drying of a gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ST07636 "Drying of a gel using a supercritical fluid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T03420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T03420 "λ-transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T03420 "A second-order or higher-order transition, in which the heat capacity shows either a discontinuity (second-order) or a vertex (higher-order) at the transition temperature. Example: The transition at \\(846\\ \\rm{K}\\), accompanied by a finite change in specific heat capacity at constant pressure \\(C_{p}\\), of the low-temperature polymorph of quartz to the high-temperature polymorph.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T03420 "lambda-transition")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06241))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06241 "θ temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06241 "The temperature at which a solution is in the θ state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06242))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06242 "tactic block")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06242 "A regular block that can be described by only one species of configurational repeating unit in a single sequential arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06243 "tactic block polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06243 "A polymer, the molecules of which consist of tactic blocks connected linearly.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06244))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06244 "tacticity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06244 "The orderliness of the succession of configurational repeating units in the main chain of a regular macromolecule, a regular oligomer molecule, a regular block or a regular chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06245))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06245 "tactic macromolecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06245 "A regular macromolecule in which essentially all the configurational repeating unit are identical.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06246))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06246 "tactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06246 "A polymer composed of tactic macromolecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06247))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06247 "Taft equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06247 "Various equations are associated with R.W. Taft, but the term is most often used to designate the family of equations that emerged from Taft's analysis of the reactivities of aliphatic esters, and which involved the polar substituent constant \\(\\sigma \\rm{*}\\) and the steric substituent constant \\(E_{\\rm{s}}\\): \\[\\log _{10}k = \\log _{10}k_{0}+\\rho \\rm{*}\\ \\sigma \\rm{*}+\\delta \\ E_{\\rm{s}}\\] or the one-parameter forms applicable when the role of either the polar term or the steric term may be neglected. Nowadays \\(\\sigma \\rm{*}\\) is usually replaced by the related constant \\(\\sigma _{I}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06248))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06248 "tagged")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06248 "Made identifiable by a label.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06249))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06249 "tailing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06249 "Asymmetry of a peak such that, relative to the baseline, the front is steeper than the rear. In paper chromatography and in thin-layer chromatography, the distortion of a zone showing a diffuse region behind the zone in the direction of travel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06250 "tandem mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06250 "An arrangement in which ions are subjected to two or more sequential stages of analysis (which may be separated spatially or temporally) according to the quotient mass/charge. A hybrid mass spectometer is an instrument which combines analysers of different types, e.g. magnetic plus electric sector combined with quadrupole. The study of ions involving two stages of mass analysis has been termed mass spectrometry/mass spectrometry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06252))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06252 "tautomerism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06252 "Isomerism of the general form:  where the isomers (called tautomers) are readily interconvertible; the atoms connecting the groups $\\ce{X}$, $\\ce{Y}$, $\\ce{Z}$ are typically any of $\\ce{C}$, $\\ce{H}$, $\\ce{O}$ or $\\ce{S}$, and $\\ce{G}$ is a group which becomes an electrofuge or nucleofuge during isomerization. The commonest case, when the electrofuge is H+, is also known as 'prototropy'. Examples, written so as to illustrate the general pattern given above, include: keto-enol tautomerism, such as:   The grouping Y may itself be a three-atom (or five-atom) chain extending the conjugation, as in:  The double bond between $\\ce{Y}$ and $\\ce{Z}$ may be replaced by a ring, when the phenomenon is called ring-chain tautomerism, as in:")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06252 "cationotropic rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06252 "cationotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06252 "prototropic rearrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06252 "prototropy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06253))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06253 "tautomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06253 "The isomerization by which tautomers are interconverted. It is a heterolytic molecular rearrangement and is frequently very rapid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06256))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06256 "tele-substitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06256 "A substitution reaction in which the entering group takes up a position more than one atom away from the atom to which the leaving group was attached:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06257))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06257 "tellurides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06257 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RTeR}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). Thus tellurium analogues of ethers.Metal salts of tellurane, $\\ce{H2Te}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06258))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06258 "tellurones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06258 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2Te(=O)2}$. Thus tellurium analogues of sulfones.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06260))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06260 "telomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06260 "The formation of an addition oligomer, having uniform end groups $\\ce{X'\\ldots X^{''}}$, by a chain reaction in which a chain transfer limits the length of the polymer ('telomer') produced. An example is the polymerization of styrene in bromotrichloromethane solution ($\\ce{X'} = \\ce{CCl3}$, $\\ce{X^{''}} = \\ce{Br}$), where $\\ce{Cl3C^{.}}$ radicals are formed in the initiation step to produce $\\ce{Cl3C[CH2CHPh]_{n}Br}$, with \\(n\\) greater than 1 and often less than ca. 10:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06261))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06261 "temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06261 "Short form for thermodynamic, Celsius or Fahrenheit temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06262))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06262 "temperature coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06262 "The dependence of a detector on temperature can be described by the temperature coefficient of responsivity and is expressed as percentage change in output per \\(\\mathrm{K}\\). In the case of a nonlinear dependence the temperature and the temperature range should also be stated for which the stated temperature coefficient of responsivity is applicable.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06263))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06263 "temperature effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06263 "A term used for changes in the luminescence parameters caused by changes in temperature, since the type of solvent and its temperature can effect the luminescence yield from an analyte as a result of quenching, exciplex formation, aggregation, etc.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06264))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06264 "temperature inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06264 "A departure from the normal decrease of temperature with increasing altitude. A temperature inversion may be produced, for example, by the movement of a warm air mass over a cool one. Intense surface inversions may form over the land during nights with clear skies and low winds due to the radiative loss of heat from the surface of the earth. The temperature increases as a function of height in this case. Poor mixing of the pollutants generally occurs below the inversion, since the normal convective process which drives the warmer and lighter air at ground level to higher altitudes is interrupted as the rising air parcels encounter the warmer air above. Temperature inversions near the surface are particularly effective in trapping ground level emissions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06265))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06265 "temperature jump")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06265 "A relaxation technique in which the temperature of a chemical system is suddenly raised. The system then relaxes to a new state of equilibrium, and analysis of the relaxation processes provides rate constants.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06265 "T-jump")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06266))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06266 "temperature lapse rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06266 "The rate of change of temperature with altitude (\\(\\frac{\\rm{d}T}{\\rm{d}z}\\)). The rate of temperature decrease with increase in altitude which is expected to occur in an unperturbed dry air mass is \\(9.8\\times 10^{3}\\ ^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C min}^{-1}\\). This is called the dry adiabatic lapse rate. The lapse rate is taken as positive when temperature decreases with increasing height. For air saturated with $\\ce{H2O}$, the lapse rate is less because of the release of the latent heat of water as it condenses. The average tropospheric lapse rate is about \\(6.5\\times 10^{3}\\ ^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\ \\min ^{-1}\\). The lapse rate has a negative value within an inversion layer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06267 "temperature-programmed chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06267 "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06269))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06269 "template")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06269 "The nucleic acid single strand that is copied during replication or transcription.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06270))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06270 "temporary poisoning")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06270 "Weak and reversible adsorption of a poison, such that removal of the poison from the fluid phase results in restoration of the original catalytic activity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06271))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06271 "tera")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06271 "SI prefix for \\(10^{12}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{T}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06272 "term")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06272 "Energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light, when of wavenumber dimension, or energy divided by the Planck constant, when of frequency dimension.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06273))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06273 "term")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06273 "A set of levels which have the same electron configuration and the same value of the quantum numbers for total spin \\(S\\) and total orbital angular momentum \\(L\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06274))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06274 "termination")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06274 "The steps in a chain reaction in which reactive intermediates are destroyed or rendered inactive, thus ending the chain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06274 "chain-ending step")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06274 "chain-termination reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06275))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06275 "terminator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06275 "A sequence of DNA lying beyond the 3' end of the coding segment of a gene which is recognized by RNA polymerase as a signal to stop synthesizing mRNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06277))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06277 "term symbols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06277 "Symbols characterizing the states of atoms and molecules in terms of multiplicity, symmetry of the total electronic wavefunction, and sometimes total (orbital + spin) angular momentum. Examples: \\(^{2}\\rm{P}_{1/2}\\) denotes an atomic state of multiplicity 2 (doublet), electron orbital angular momentum quantum number \\(L=1\\), and orbital plus spin angular momentum quantum number \\(J=\\frac{1}{2}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06278))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06278 "terpenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06278 "Hydrocarbons of biological origin having carbon skeletons formally derived from isoprene $\\ce{[CH2=C(CH3)CH=CH2]}$. This class is subdivided into the $\\ce{C5}$ hemiterpenes, $\\ce{C10}$ monoterpenes, $\\ce{C15}$ sesquiterpenes, $\\ce{C20}$ diterpenes, $\\ce{C25}$ sesterterpenes, $\\ce{C30}$ triterpenes, $\\ce{C40}$ tetraterpenes (carotenoids) and $\\ce{C5_{n}}$ polyterpenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06279))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06279 "terpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06279 "Natural products and related compounds formally derived from isoprene units (see isoprenoids). They contain oxygen in various functional groups. This class is subdivided according to the number of carbon atoms in the same manner as are terpenes. The skeleton of terpenoids may differ from strict additivity of isoprene units by the loss or shift of a fragment, generally a methyl group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06282))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06282 "tertiary structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06282 "The spatial organization (including conformation) of an entire protein molecule or other macromolecule consisting of a single chain.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06283))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06283 "tesla")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06283 "SI derived unit of magnetic flux density, \\(\\pu{T} = \\pu{Wb m-2} = \\pu{kg s-2 A-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06284))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06284 "test portion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06284 "The amount or volume of the test sample taken for analysis, usually of known weight or volume.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06284 "analytical portion")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06285))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06285 "test sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06285 "The sample, prepared from the laboratory sample, from which test portions are removed for testing or for analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06286))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06286 "test solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06286 "The solution prepared from the test portion for the analytical procedure. The proportions of test portion and solvent are normally known.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06286 "analytical solution")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06287))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06287 "tetracyclines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06287 "A subclass of polyketides having an octahydrotetracene-2-carboxamide skeleton, substituted with many hydroxy and other groups, e.g. chlortetracycline:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06289))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06289 "tetrahedral intermediate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06289 "A reaction intermediate in which the bond arrangement around an initially double-bonded carbon atom (typically a carbonyl carbon atom) has been transformed from trigonal to tetrahedral. For example, aldol in the condensation reaction of acetaldehyde (but most tetrahedral intermediates have a more fleeting existence).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06290))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06290 "<i>tetrahedro</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06290 "An affix used in names to denote four atoms bound into a tetrahedron.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06291))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06291 "tetrapyrroles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06291 "Natural pigments containing four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon units linking position 2 of one pyrrole ring to position 5 of the next. porphyrins are macrocyclic tetrapyrroles. E.g. bilin (a linear tetrapyrrole).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06292))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06292 "tetraterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06292 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C40}$ skeleton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06293))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06293 "texture")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06293 "The detailed geometry of the void space in the particles of a catalyst.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06295))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06295 "thermal analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06295 "A group of techniques in which a physical property of a substance and/or its reaction product is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06296))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06296 "thermal black")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06296 "A special type of carbon black produced by pyrolysis of gaseous hydrocarbons in a preheated chamber in the absence of air. Thermal black consists of relatively large individual spheres (\\(100{-}500\\ \\rm{nm}\\) diameter) and aggregates of a small number of pseudospherical particles. The preferred alignment of the layer planes is parallel to the surface of the spheres.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06297))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06297 "thermal column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06297 "A large body of moderator, adjacent to or inside a reactor to provide thermal neutrons for experiments.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06298))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06298 "thermal conductance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06298 "Heat flow rate divided by the temperature difference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06299))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06299 "thermal conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06299 """Tensor quantity relating the heat flux, \\(J_{q}\\), to the temperature gradient, \\(J_{q}=- \\lambda \\ 
abla T\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06300))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06300 "thermal conductivity detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06300 "In general, two cells arranged in a bridge configuration detect the change in thermal conductivity of the gas at the output of the column. This detector is sensitive to any substance with thermal conductivity different from that of the carrier gas. The lowest detectable limit is between \\(0.5\\) and \\(100\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\). The linear dynamic range is of the order of \\(10^{3}\\). This type of detector is often used for measuring components at relatively high concentrations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06302))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06302 "thermal fission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06302 "Fission caused by thermal neutrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06303))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06303 "thermal ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06303 "Takes place when an atom or molecule interacts with a heated surface or is in a gaseous environment at high temperature. Examples of the latter may be a capillary arc plasma, a microwave plasma or an inductively coupled plasma.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06304 "thermal lensing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06304 "A technique that determines the alteration in the refractive index of a medium as a result of the temperature rise in the path of a laser beam absorbed by the medium. The lens produced (usually divergent) causes a change (usually a decrease) in the irradiance measured along the laser beam axis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06306))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06306 "thermally-induced transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06306 "A transition that is induced by a change in temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06307))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06307 "thermal neutrons")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06307 "Neutrons in thermal equilibrium with the medium in which they exist, in general at room temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06308))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06308 "thermal resistance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06308 "Reciprocal of the thermal conductance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06309))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06309 "thermoacoustimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06309 "A technique in which the characteristics of imposed acoustic waves are measured as a function of temperature after passing through a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06312))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06312 "thermochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06312 "A thermally induced transformation of a molecular structure or of a system (e.g. of a solution), thermally reversible, that produces a spectral change, typically, but not necessarily, of visible colour.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06313))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06313 "thermocouple")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06313 "A device based on the thermoelectric effect, by which two junctions between dissimilar conductors (metallic or heavily doped semiconductors) kept at different temperatures generate an electric potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06314))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06314 "thermodilatometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06314 "A technique in which a dimension of a substance under negligible load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. Linear thermodilatometry and volume thermodilatometry are distinguished on the basis of the dimensions measured.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06315))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06315 "thermodynamically equivalent sphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06315 "A sphere, impenetrable to other spheres, displaying the same excluded volume as an actual polymer molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06316))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06316 "thermodynamic control")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06316 "The term characterizes conditions that lead to reaction products in a proportion governed by the equilibrium constant for their interconversion and/or for the interconversion of reaction intermediates formed in or after the rate-limiting step. (Some workers prefer to describe this phenomenon as 'equilibrium control'.)")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06316 "equilibrium control")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06319))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06319 "thermodynamic isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06319 """The effect of isotopic substitution on an equilibrium constant is referred to as a thermodynamic (or equilibrium) isotope effect. For example, the effect of isotopic substitution in reactant A that participates in the equilibrium: \\[\\rm{A}+\\rm{B}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm{C}\\] is the ratio \\(\\frac{K^{\\rm{l}}}{K^{\\rm{h}}}\\) of the equilibrium constant for the reaction in which A contains the light isotope to that in which it contains the heavy isotope. The ratio can be expressed as the equilibrium constant for the isotopic exchange reaction: \\[\\rm{A}^{\\rm{l}}+\\rm{C}^{\\rm{h}}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm{A}^{\\rm{h}}+\\rm{C}^{\\rm{l}}\\] in which reactants such as B that are not isotopically substituted do not appear. The potential energy surfaces of isotopic molecules are identical to a high degree of approximation, so thermodynamic isotope effects can only arise from the effect of isotopic mass on the nuclear motions of the reactants and products, and can be expressed quantitatively in terms of partition function ratios for nuclear motion: \\[\\frac{K^{\\rm{l}}}{K^{\\rm{h}}} = \\frac{(Q_{\\rm{nuc}}^{\\rm{l}}/Q_{\\rm{nuc}}^{\\rm{h}})_{\\rm{C}}}\r
{(Q_{\\rm{nuc}}^{\\rm{l}}/Q_{\\rm{nuc}}^{\\rm{h}})_{\\rm{A}}}\\] Although the nuclear partition function is a product of the translational, rotational and vibrational partition functions, the isotope effect is determined almost entirely by the last named, specifically by vibrational modes involving motion of isotopically different atoms. In the case of light atoms (i.e. protium vs. deuterium or tritium) at moderate temperatures, the isotope effect is dominated by zero-point energy differences.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06319 "equilibrium isotope effect")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06321))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06321 "thermodynamic temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06321 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06322))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06322 "thermoelectrometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06322 "A technique in which an electrical characteristic of a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. The most common measurements are of resistance, conductance or capacitance.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06324))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06324 "thermogravimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06324 "A technique in which the mass of a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06325))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06325 "thermoluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06325 "Luminescence arising from a reaction between species trapped in a rigid matrix and released as a result of an increase in temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06326))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06326 "thermolysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06326 "The uncatalysed cleavage of one or more covalent bonds resulting from exposure of a compound to a raised temperature, or a process in which such cleavage is an essential part.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06327))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06327 "thermomagnetometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06327 "A technique in which a magnetic characteristic of a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06328))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06328 "thermomechanical measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06328 "A technique in which the deformation of a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) under non-oscillatory load is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06331))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06331 "thermometric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06331 "An analytical method in which one reactant (the titrant) is added continuously or stepwise to an adiabatic or isoperibol vessel containing another reactant. The enthalpy change(s) of the ensuing reaction(s) causes a temperature change which, when plotted versus volume of titrant, may be used to find the titration endpoint(s). This is the preferred term for experiments producing plots of temperature versus volume of titrant in which the main goal is a quantitative determination. Nonetheless, when a calorimetric vessel is used, such that the heat capacity is known, thermodynamic parameters may also be estimated from such experiments. An acceptable synonym in that case is enthalpimetric titration. The use of the adjective thermometric is justified because of widespread historical and current usage, and because a titration of necessity implies a chemical reaction. The term  'thermometric enthalpy titration' has been used, but is not recommended. A method in which the titrant is a catalyst for an indicator reaction that occurs after the endpoint for the analyte reaction should be called a thermometric titration with catalytic endpoint detection, not a 'catalytic thermometric titration'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06331 "enthalpimetric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06331 "thermometric enthalpy titration")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06332))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06332 "thermoparticulate analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06332 "A thermoanalytical technique in which the release of particulate matter from a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06333))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06333 "thermophile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06333 "An organism that can tolerate high temperatures and that grows optimally at temperatures above \\(45\\ ^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06335))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06335 "thermopile")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06335 "Several thermocouples connected in series to increase the magnitude of the electric potential.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06336))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06336 "thermoptometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06336 "A technique in which an optical characteristic of a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. Measurements of total light, light of specific wavelength(s), refractive index and luminescence lead to thermophotometry, thermospectrometry, thermorefractometry and thermoluminescence, respectively; observation under the microscope leads to thermomicroscopy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06337))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06337 "thermosonimetry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06337 "A technique in which the sound emitted by a substance (and/or its reaction product(s)) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06338))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06338 "thermosphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06338 "Atmospheric shell extending from the top of the mesosphere to outer space. It is a region of more or less steadily increasing temperature with height, starting at \\(70\\) or \\(80\\ \\rm{km}\\). It includes the exosphere and most or all of the ionosphere (not the D region).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06342 "thickness of diffusion layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06342 "The distance from the electrode where the ratio \\[\\frac{c\\,-\\,c_{\\rm{e}}}{c_{0}\\,-\\,c_{\\rm{e}}}\\] reaches a given value. (\\(c_{\\rm{e}}\\) is the interfacial concentration, \\(c\\) is the concentration, \\(c_{0}\\) is the value in the bulk solution.) If this ratio is selected as, say, 0.99 the corresponding thickness of the diffusion layer is denoted by \\(\\delta_{0.99}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06343 "thickness of electrical double layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06343 "The length characterizing the decrease with distance of the potential in the double layer = characteristic Debye length in the corresponding electrolyte solution = \\(\\kappa^{-1}\\):\\[\\frac{1}{\\kappa } = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\varepsilon_{0}RT}{F^{2}\\sum _{i}c_{i}z_{i}^{2} }}\\]&nbsp;&nbsp;\\[\\frac{1}{\\kappa } = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}RT}{4\\pi F^{2}\\sum _{i}c_{i}z_{i}^{2} }}\\](rationalized four-quantity system)&nbsp;&nbsp;(three-quantity electrostatic system) where \\(\\varepsilon\\) = static permittivity = \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\varepsilon_{0}\\), \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\) = relative static permittivity of solution; \\(\\varepsilon_{0}\\) = permittivity of vacuum, \\(R\\) = gas constant, \\(T\\) = thermodynamic temperature, \\(F\\) = Faraday constant, \\(z_{\\rm{i}}\\) = concentration of species \\(i\\), \\(z_{\\rm{i}}\\) = ionic charge on species \\(i\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06344 "thickness of reaction layer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06344 "When a kinetic current flows, the concentrations of the electroactive substance B and its precursor C at very small distances from the electrode surface are influenced both by mass transfer and by the finite rate of establishment of the chemical equilibrium. As the distance from the electrode surface increases, the chemical equilibrium is more and more nearly attained. The thickness of the reaction layer is the distance from the electrode surface beyond which deviations from the chemical equilibrium between C and B are taken to be negligibly small.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06345))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06345 "thin film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06345 "A film whose thickness is of the order of a characteristic scale or smaller. Since a film may 'look' operationally thin or thick, according to the procedure applied, it is also recommended that the measurement procedure employed be specified (e.g. ellipsometrically thin film, optically thin film, etc.). It is recommended that the physical specification of the film thickness be used, whenever possible (e.g. thick compared to the electron mean free path, thin compared to the optical wavelength, etc.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06346))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06346 "thin-layer chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06346 "Chromatography carried out in a layer of adsorbent spread on a support e.g. a glass plate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06347))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06347 "thio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06347 "This prefix properly denotes replacement of an oxygen by a sulfur, e.g. $\\ce{PhC(=S)NH2}$, thiobenzamide. See also entries prefixed thio-.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06348))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06348 "thioacetals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06348 """A term including monothioacetals having the structure $\\ce{R2C(OR')(SR')}$ (subclass monothioketals, $\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$); and dithioacetals having the structure $\\ce{R2C(SR')2}$ (subclass dithioketals, $\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}, \\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06349))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06349 "thioaldehydes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06349 "Compounds in which the oxygen of an aldehyde has been replaced by divalent sulfur, $\\ce{RC(=S)H}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3CH2C(=S)H propanethial}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06350))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06350 "thioaldehyde <i>S</i>-oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06350 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RC(=S=O)H}$. (Also known by the disapproved term 'sulfines' which includes thioaldehyde S-oxides and thioketone S-oxides).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06350 "sulfines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06351))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06351 "thioanhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06351 "Compounds having the structure acyl-S-acyl. Also called diacylsulfanes, e.g. $\\ce{CH3C(=O)SC(=S)CH2CH3}$ acetic thiopropanoic thioanhydride.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06351 "acid thioanhydrides")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06351 "diacylsulfanes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06352))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06352 "thiocarboxylic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06352 "Compounds in which one or both oxygens of a carboxy group have been replaced by divalent sulfur $\\ce{RC(=O)SH}$ or $\\ce{RC(=S)OH}$ monothiocarboxylic acids, $\\ce{RC(=S)SH}$ dithiocarboxylic acids.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06353))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06353 "thiocyanates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06353 "Salts and esters of thiocyanic acid $\\ce{HSC#N}$, e.g. $\\ce{CH3SC#N}$ methyl thiocyanate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06354))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06354 "thioethers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06354 """Former name for sulfides $\\ce{RSR}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06355))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06355 "thiohemiacetals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06355 """Compounds of structure $\\ce{R2C(SR')OH}$ or $\\ce{R2C(OR')SH}$ (monothiohemiacetals), or $\\ce{R2C(SR')SH}$ (dithiohemiacetals), $\\ce{R'} 
eq \\ce{H}$.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06356))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06356 "thioketones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06356 """Compounds in which the oxygen of a ketone has been replaced by divalent sulfur $\\ce{R2C=S}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{CH3C(=S)CH2CH3}$ butane-2-thione.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06357))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06357 "thioketone <i>S</i>-oxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06357 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{R2C=S=O}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$). (Also known by the disapproved term 'sulfines' which comprises thioaldehyde S-oxides and thioketone S-oxides.)""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06357 "sulfines")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06358))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06358 "thiolates")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06358 "Derivatives of thiols, in which a metal (or other cation) replaces the hydrogen attached to sulfur, e.g. $\\ce{CH3S^{-}Na^{+}}$ sodium methanethiolate.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06359))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06359 "thiols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06359 """Compounds having the structure $\\ce{RSH}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), e.g. $\\ce{MeCH2SH}$ ethanethiol. Also known by the term 'mercaptans' (abandoned by IUPAC).""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06359 "mercaptans")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06361))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06361 "third body")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06361 "A species, other than the reactant itself, which brings about the energization of a molecule that can undergo a unimolecular process, or brings about a combination reaction between atoms or radicals. In the latter case a third body is also called a chaperon.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06366))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06366 "threshold energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06366 "Potential energy gap between reactants and the transition state, sometimes involving the zero point energies, but usually not.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06367 "through-bond electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06367 "Intramolecular electron transfer for which the relevant electronic interaction between the donor and acceptor sites is mediated by through-bond interaction, i.e. via the covalent bonds interconnecting these sites, as opposed to through-space interaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06369 "through-space electron transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06369 "Electron transfer for which the relevant electronic interaction between the donor and acceptor sites is mediated either by direct orbital overlap or by superexchange interaction via intervening molecular entities not covalently bound to the donor or acceptor sites.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06371 "TICT emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06371 "Electronic emission emanating from a TICT state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06372 "TICT state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06372 "The acronym derives from Twisted Internal Charge Transfer state, proposed to be responsible for strongly Stokes-shifted fluorescence from certain aromatics, particularly in a polar medium.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06373))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06373 "tie molecule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06373 "A molecule that connects at least two different crystals.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06375))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06375 "time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06375 "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06376))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06376 "time constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06376 "If the output of a detector changes exponentially with time, the time required for it to change from its initial value by the fraction \\(1 - \\exp(-t/\\tau _{\\rm{c}})\\) (for \\(t=\\tau _{\\rm{c}}\\)) of the final value, is called the time constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06377))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06377 "time-correlated single photon counting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06377 "A technique for the measurement of the time histogram of a sequence of photons with respect to a periodic event, e.g. a flash from a repetitive nanosecond lamp or a CW operated laser (mode locked laser). The essential part is a time-to-amplitude-converter (TAC) which transforms the arrival time between a start and a stop pulse into a voltage. Sometimes called single photon timing.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06377 "single photon timing")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06378))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06378 "time-dependent stoichiometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06378 "A state in which if intermediates are formed in significant amounts during the course of a reaction, the overall stoichiometric equation does not apply throughout.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06379))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06379 "time-independent stoichiometry")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06379 "A state in which the stoichiometric equation applies throughout the course of the reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06380))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06380 "time of centrifugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06380 "The time difference from switching on until switching off. The time for deceleration is not included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06382))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06382 "time-of-flight mass spectrometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06382 "An arrangement using the fact that ions of different mass/charge need different times to travel through a certain distance in a field-free region after they have all been initially given the same translational energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06383))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06383 "time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06383 "The time in which a certain viscosity or yield stress is reached after discontinuation of a shear. These times depend on the values of viscosity or yield stress chosen by the experimenter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06384))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06384 "time-resolved microwave conductivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06384 "Technique allowing the quantitative and qualitative detection of radiation-induced changes in the real, \\(\\Delta \\rm{Re}\\sigma\\), and imaginary, \\(\\Delta \\rm{Im}\\sigma\\), components of the conductivity of a medium by time-resolved measurement of changes in the microwave absorption resulting from the formation of mobile charges or from changes in the dipole moment or polarizability of molecules on excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06385))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06385 "time-resolved spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06385 "The recording of spectra at a series of time intervals after the excitation of the system with a light pulse (or other perturbation) of appropriately short duration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06386))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06386 "titrant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06386 "The solution containing the active agent with which a titration is made.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06387))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06387 "titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06387 "The process of determining the quantity of a substance A by adding measured increments of substance B, with which it reacts (almost always as a standardized solution called the titrant, but also by electrolytic generation, as in coulometric titration) with provision for some means of recognizing (indicating) the endpoint at which essentially all of A has reacted. If the endpoint coincides with the addition of the exact chemical equivalence, it is called the equivalence point or stoichiometric or theoretical endpoint, thus allowing the amount of A to be found from known amounts of B added up to this point, the reacting weight ratio of A to B being known from stoichiometry or otherwise. Terms for varieties of titration can reflect the nature of the reaction between A and B. Thus, there are acid–base, complexometric, chelatometric, oxidation–reduction, and precipitation titrations. Additionally, the term can reflect the nature of the titrant, such as acidimetric, alkalimetric, and iodometric titrations as well as coulometric titrations, in which the titrant is generated electrolytically rather than being added as a standard solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06388))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06388 "titration curve")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06388 "A plot of a variable related to a relevant concentration (activity) as the ordinate vs. some measure of the amount of titrant, usually titration volume (titre) as the abscissa. If the variable is linearly related to concentrations, such as the electrical conductance or the photometric absorbance, the term linear titration curve is used. When a logarithmic expression of the concentration or activity is used, such as the \\(\\rm{pH}\\), \\(\\mathrm{pM}\\), or the electrical potential in \\(\\mathrm{mV}\\), the curve is referred to as a logarithmic titration curve.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06389))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06389 "titration error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06389 "The difference in the amount of titrant, or the corresponding difference in the amount of substance being titrated, represented by the expression: (end-point value - equivalence-point value).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06391))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06391 "titre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06391 "The reacting strength of a standard solution, usually expressed as the weight (mass) of the substance equivalent to \\(1\\ \\rm{cm}^{3}\\) of the solution.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06391 "titer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06394))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06394 "tonne")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06394 "Non-SI unit of mass, \\(\\pu{t} = \\pu{10E3 kg}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06395))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06395 "topochemical reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06395 "A reversible or irreversible reaction that involves the introduction of a guest species into a host structure and that results in significant structural modifications to the host, for example, the breakage of bonds. Example: The insertion of lithium at \\(50\\,^{\\unicode{x26ac}}\\rm{C}\\) into the spinel $\\ce{Li[Mn2]O4}$ with symmetry \\(F\\,\\rm{d3m}\\) to yield a layered structure with symmetry \\(P\\bar{3}\\,\\rm{m1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06395 "topotactic reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06395 "insertion reaction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06396))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06396 "topomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06396 "The identity reaction leading to exchange of the positions of identical ligands. The indistinguishable molecular entities involved are called topomers. For example in the reaction below the two identical ligating atoms $\\ce{N_{\\rm{a}}}$ and $\\ce{N_{\\rm{b}}}$ are interchanged by rotation about the $\\ce{C-aryl}$ bond but may be identified by NMR spectroscopy. This is a degenerate isomerization.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06399))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06399 "topotactic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06399 "A transition in which the crystal lattice of the product phase shows one or more crystallographically equivalent, orientational relationships to the crystal lattice of the parent phase. Example: Transitions in which the anionic array is unchanged during the transition but cation reorganization occurs, as in:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06400))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06400 "torque")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06400 "Sum of moments of forces not acting along the same line.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06401))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06401 "torquoselectivity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06401 "The preference for 'inward' or 'outward' rotation of substituents in conrotatory or disrotatory electrocyclic ring opening reactions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06402 "torr")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06402 "Non-SI unit of pressure, \\(\\rm{torr}\\approx 133.322\\ \\rm{Pa}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06403 "torsional braid analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06403 "A particular case of dynamic thermomechanometry in which the material is supported on a braid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06404))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06404 "torsional stereoisomers")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06404 "Stereoisomers that can be interconverted (actually or conceptually) by torsion about a bond axis. This includes E,Z-isomers of alkenes, atropisomers and rotamers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06406))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06406 "torsion angle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06406 "In a chain of atoms A-B-C-D, the dihedral angle between the plane containing the atoms A,B,C and that containing B,C,D. In a Newman projection the torsion angle is the angle (having an absolute value between 0° and 180°) between bonds to two specified (fiducial) groups, one from the atom nearer (proximal) to the observer and the other from the further (distal) atom. The torsion angle between groups A and D is then considered to be positive if the bond A-B is rotated in a clockwise direction through less than 180° in order that it may eclipse the bond C-D: a negative torsion angle requires rotation in the opposite sense. Stereochemical arrangements corresponding to torsion angles between 0° and ±90° are called syn (s), those corresponding to torsion angles between ±90° and 180° anti (a). Similarly, arrangements corresponding to torsion angles between 30° and 150° or between -30° and -150° are called clinal (c) and those between 0° and 30° or 150° and 180° are called periplanar (p). The two types of terms can be combined so as to define four ranges of torsion angle; 0° to 30° synperiplanar (sp); 30° to 90° and -30° to -90° synclinal (sc); 90° to 150°, and -90° to -150° anticlinal (ac); ±150° to 180° antiperiplanar (ap).  The synperiplanar conformation is also known as the syn- or cis-conformation; antiperiplanar as anti or trans and synclinal as gauche or skew. For macromolecular usage the symbols \\(\\rm{T}\\), \\(\\rm{C}\\), \\(\\rm{G}^{+}\\), \\(\\rm{G}^{-}\\), \\(\\rm{A}^{+}\\) and \\(\\rm{A}^{-}\\) are recommended (ap, sp, +sc, -sc, +ac and -ac respectively).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06409))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06409 "total consumption time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06409 "The time necessary to consume the prepared sample completely (in \\(\\mathrm{s}\\)). It is equal to the volume of the prepared sample divided by the rate of fluid consumption.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06410))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06410 "total ion current")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06410 "(after mass analysis): The sum of the separate ion currents carried by the different ions contributing to the spectrum (this is sometimes called the reconstructed ion current).(before mass analysis): The sum of all the separate ion currents for ions of the same sign before mass analysis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06411))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06411 "totally porous packing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06411 "Packing in which the stationary phase permeates each porous particle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06413))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06413 "total retention volume")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06413 "The volume of mobile phase entering the column between sample injection and the emergence of the peak maximum of the sample component of interest, or the corresponding time. It includes the hold-up volume (time): \\[t_{\\rm{R}}=\\frac{V_{\\rm{R}}}{F_{\\rm{c}}}\\] where \\(F_{c}\\) is the mobile phase flow rate at column temperature.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06413 "time")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06413 "retention time")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06414))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06414 "toxicity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06414 "Capacity to cause injury to a living organism defined with reference to the quantity of substance administered or absorbed, the way in which the substance is administered (inhalation, ingestion, topical application, injection) and distributed in time (single or repeated doses), the type and severity of injury, the time needed to produce the injury, the nature of the organism(s) affected and other relevant conditions.Adverse effects of a substance on a living organism defined with reference to the quantity of substance administered or absorbed, the way in which the substance is administered (inhalation, ingestion, topical application, injection) and distributed in time (single or repeated doses), the type and severity of injury, the time needed to produce the injury, the nature of the organism(s) affected, and other relevant conditions.Measure of incompatibility of a substance with life: this quantity may be expressed as the reciprocal of the absolute value of median lethal dose (\\(\\frac{1}{\\rm{LD}_{50}}\\)) or concentration (\\(\\frac{1}{\\rm{LC}_{50}}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06415))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06415 "toxicodynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06415 "Study of toxic actions on living systems, including the reactions with and binding to cell constituents, and the biochemical and physiological consequences of these actions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06416))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06416 "toxicokinetics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06416 "Process of the uptake of potentially toxic substances by the body, the biotransformation they undergo, the distribution of the substances and their metabolites in the tissues, and the elimination of the substances and their metabolites from the body. Both the amounts and the concentrations of the substances and their metabolites are studied. The term has essentially the same meaning as pharmacokinetics, but the latter term should be restricted to the study of pharmaceutical substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06417))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06417 "toxicology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06417 "Scientific discipline involving the study of the actual or potential danger presented by the harmful effects of substances (poisons) on living organisms and ecosystems, of the relationship of such harmful effects to exposure, and of the mechanisms of action, diagnosis, prevention and treatment of intoxications.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06418))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06418 "toxin")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06418 "Poisonous substance produced by a biological organism such as a microbe, animal or plant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06419))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06419 "toxinology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06419 "Scientific discipline involving the study of the chemistry, biochemistry, pharmacology and toxicology of toxins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06420))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06420 "traceability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06420 "The property of a result or measurement whereby it can be related to appropriate standards, generally international or national standards, through an unbroken chain of comparisons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06421))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06421 "trace element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06421 "Any element having an average concentration of less than about 100 parts per million atoms (\\(\\rm{ppma}\\)) or less than \\(100\\ \\frac{\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{g}}{\\rm{g}}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06422))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06422 "tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06422 "A foreign substance mixed with or attached to a given substance to enable the distribution or location of the latter to be determined subsequently. There are several types of tracers which are used: (i) A physical tracer is one that is attached by physical means to the object being traced; (ii) A chemical tracer is a chemical with properties similar to those of the substance being traced with which it is mixed homogeneously; (iii) An isotopic tracer is a unique isotope, either radioactive or an enriched, uncommon stable isotope, of the element to be traced; (iv) A radioactive tracer is a physical or chemical tracer having radioactivity as its distinctive property which allows detection at small concentrations and hence after large transport distances. The composition of aerosols in the troposphere has been used as a qualitative tracer of air masses. The elemental analyses (determined by neutron activation, X-ray fluorescence, etc.) of the aerosols transported from various sources or source regions sometimes have characteristic patterns which are used to define qualitatively the origin of tropospheric aerosols collected in other geographical regions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06429))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06429 "track")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06429 "The path of an ionizing particle as revealed by a track detector.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06430))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06430 "track detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06430 "A detector which makes the paths of ionizing particles visible, either directly (e.g. cloud chamber) or after suitable treatment (photographic emulsion, polymers).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06431))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06431 "trajectory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06431 "A path taken by a reaction system over potential-energy surface or a diagram or mathematical description that represents that path. A trajectory can also be called a reaction path.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06433))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06433 "<i>trans-</i>")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06433 "A prefix designating two groups directly across a central atom from each other, i.e. in the polar positions on a sphere, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06434))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06434 "transannular strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06434 "In medium-sized ring compounds, the strain due to repulsive nonbonded interactions between substituents or hydrogen atoms attached to non-adjacent ring atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06436))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06436 "transcription")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06436 "The process by which the genetic information encoded in a linear sequence of nucleotides in one strand of DNA is copied into an exactly complementary sequence of RNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06437 "transducer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06437 "An analytical instrument which provides an output quantity having a given relationship to the input quantity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06438 "transduction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06438 "The transfer of genetic information from one bacterium to another by means of a transducing bacteriophage. When the phage is grown on the first host, a fragment of the host DNA can be incorporated into the phage particles. This foreign DNA can be transferred to the second host upon infection with progeny phage from the first experiment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06439))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06439 "transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06439 "Movement of a component within a system or across its boundary. It may be expressed using different kinds of quantities, e.g rates of change \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}Q}{\\rm{d}t}\\) or \\(\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}\\). Examples are: mass rate, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\) or \\(\\frac{\\Delta m_{\\rm{B}}}{\\Delta t}\\); substance rate, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\) or \\(\\frac{\\Delta n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\Delta t}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06440))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06440 "transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06440 "The action of moving material between containers, or containers between transport devices. This term is of particular importance in clinical chemistry.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06441))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06441 "transferability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06441 "Transferability assumes invariance of properties, associated conceptually with an atom or a fragment present in a variety of molecules. The property, such as electronegativity, nucleophilicity, NMR chemical shift, etc. is held as retaining a similar value in all these occurrences.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06442))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06442 "transfer activity coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06442 """A term used to quantify the difference in the free energy of a solute ion in two different standard states often in two different liquid phases. The relationship is \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{t}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}} = 
u \\ R\\ T\\ \\ln \\mathit{\\gamma}_{\\rm{t}}\\) where \\(\\Delta _{\\rm{t}}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) is the transfer Gibbs energy and \\(
u \\) is the number of ions in the solute.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06443))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06443 "transferases")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06443 "Enzymes that catalyse reactions in which a group is transferred from one compound to another. Groups that are transferred are $\\ce{C}$, aldehydic or ketonic residues, acyl, glycosyl, alkyl, nitrogenous, phosphorus and sulfur containing groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06444))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06444 "transfer line")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06444 "Line provided to carry the sample to be analysed from the sample point to the analytical unit without altering the composition of the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06445))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06445 "transfer RNA")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06445 "A single-stranded RNA molecule containing about 70-90 nucleotides, folded by intrastrand base pairing into a characteristic secondary ('cloverleaf') structure that carries a specific amino acid and matches it to its corresponding codon on an mRNA during protein synthesis.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06446))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06446 "transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06446 "The conversion of a substrate into a particular product, irrespective of reagents or mechanisms involved. For example, the transformation of aniline ($\\ce{C6H5NH2}$) into N-phenylacetamide ($\\ce{C6H5NHCOCH3}$) may be effected by use of acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride or ketene. A transformation is distinct from a reaction, the full description of which would state or imply all the reactants and all the products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06447 "transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06447 "A process for genetic alteration of a cell following incorporation of foreign DNA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06450 "transient phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06450 "This expression relates to the period of time that elapses prior to the establishment of a steady state. Initially the concentration of a reactive intermediate is zero, and it rises to the steady-state concentration during the transient phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06450 "induction period")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06451 "transient species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06451 "Relating to a short-lived reaction intermediate. It can be defined only in relation to a time scale fixed by the experimental conditions and the limitations of the technique employed in the detection of the intermediate. The term is a relative one. Transient species are sometimes also said to be 'metastable'. However, this latter term should be avoided, because it relates a thermodynamic term to a kinetic property, although most transients are also thermodynamically unstable with respect to reactants and products.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06451 "transient chemical species")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06453 "transient spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06453 "A technique for the spectroscopic observation of transient species (excited-state molecular entities or reactive intermediates) generated by a pulse of short duration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06455 "transition coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06455 "The reaction coordinate at the transition state corresponding to a vibration with an imaginary frequency. Motion along it in the two opposite senses leads towards the reactants or towards the products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06456 "transition element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06456 "An element whose atom has an incomplete d sub-shell, or which can give rise to cations with an incomplete d sub-shell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06457))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06457 "transition interval")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06457 "The range of concentration over which the eye is able to perceive change in hue, colour intensity, fluorescence or other property of a visual indicator arising from the varying ratio of the two relevant forms of the indicator.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06460))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06460 "transition moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06460 "An electromagnetic wave may induce an oscillating electric moment in a molecule (possibly leading to absorption if the oscillation frequency is equal to the light frequency). The amplitude of this moment is the transition moment between the initial (i) and final (f) states (here assumed to be non-degenerate): \\[M_{\\rm{if}} = \\, < \\rm{f} \\mid M_{\\rm{op}}\\mid \\rm{i} >\\] where \\(M_{\\rm{op}}\\) is the electric dipole moment operator, a vector operator that is the sum of the position vectors of all charged particles weighted with their charge. The transition moment \\(M_{\\rm{if}} \\) is a vector in the molecular framework, characterized both by its direction and its probability.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06460 "transition dipole moment")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06460 "electronic transition moment")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06462))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06462 "transition polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06462 "The direction of the transition moment in the molecular framework.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06467 "transition species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06467 "Any intermediate species in an elementary reaction. An activated complex is an example of a transition species, but the latter term also includes other intermediate species. Transition species have also been called transition states, but there is then danger of confusion with a true activated complex, which is also commonly known as a transition state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06468 "transition state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06468 "In theories describing elementary reaction it is usually assumed that there is a transition state of more positive molar Gibbs energy between the reactants and the products through which an assembly of atoms (initially composing the molecular entities of the reactants) must pass on going from reactants to products in either direction. In the formalism of 'transition state theory' the transition state of an elementary reaction is that set of states (each characterized by its own geometry and energy) in which an assembly of atoms, when randomly placed there, would have an equal probability of forming the reactants or of forming the products of that elementary reaction. The transition state is characterized by one and only one imaginary frequency. The assembly of atoms at the transition state has been called an activated complex. (It is not a complex according to the definition in this Compendium.) It may be noted that the calculations of reaction rates by the transition state method and based on calculated potential-energy surfaces refer to the potential energy maximum at the saddle point, as this is the only point for which the requisite separability of transition state coordinates may be assumed. The ratio of the number of assemblies of atoms that pass through to the products to the number of those that reach the saddle point from the reactants can be less than unity, and this fraction is the 'transmission coefficient' \\(\\kappa \\). (There are also reactions, such as the gas-phase colligation of simple radicals, that do not require 'activation' and which therefore do not involve a transition state.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06469 "transition state analogue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06469 "A substrate designed to mimic the properties or the geometry of the transition state of reaction.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06470 "transition state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06470 "A theory of the rates of elementary reactions which assumes a special type of equilibrium, having an equilibrium constant \\(K^{\\ddagger }\\), to exist between reactants and activated complexes. According to this theory the rate constant is given by: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ K^{\\ddagger }\\] where \\(k_{B}\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(h\\) is the Planck constant. The rate constant can also be expressed as: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ \\exp (\\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R})\\ \\exp (- \\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R\\ T})\\] where \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), the entropy of activation, is the standard molar change of entropy when the activated complex is formed from reactants and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), the enthalpy of activation, is the corresponding standard molar change of enthalpy. The quantities \\(E_{a}\\) (the energy of activation) and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) are not quite the same, the relationship between them depending on the type of reaction. Also: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ \\exp (- \\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R\\ T})\\] where \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), known as the Gibbs energy of activation, is the standard molar Gibbs energy change for the conversion of reactants into activated complex. A plot of standard molar Gibbs energy against a reaction coordinate is known as a Gibbs-energy profile; such plots, unlike potential-energy profiles, are temperature-dependent. In principle the equations above must be multiplied by a transmission coefficient, \\(\\kappa \\), which is the probability that an activated complex forms a particular set of products rather than reverting to reactants or forming alternative products. It is to be emphasized that \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) occurring in the former three equations are not ordinary thermodynamic quantities, since one degree of freedom in the activated complex is ignored. Transition-state theory has also been known as absolute rate theory, and as activated-complex theory, but these terms are no longer recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06470 "conventional transition-state theory")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06471))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06471 "transition structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06471 "A saddle point on a potential-energy surface. It has one negative force constant in the harmonic force constant matrix.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06472))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06472 "transition time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06472 "In chronopotentiometry and related techniques, the time that elapses between the instant at which current is applied and the instant at which the concentration of an electroactive substance B at the electrode-solution interface becomes indistinguishable from zero. In experimental practice the latter time is often taken to be the instant at which the rate of variation of the potential of the indicator electrode attains a maximum value.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06473))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06473 "transition wavenumber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06473 "Difference of term values for the corresponding states (upper minus lower).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06474))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06474 "transit time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06474 "The time needed for the component to pass through the observation space (in \\(\\mathrm{s}\\)). It depends on the geometry of the observation space, the flame temperature and the solute nebulized.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06475))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06475 "translation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06475 "The unidirectional process that takes place on the ribosomes whereby the genetic information present in an mRNA is converted into a corresponding sequence of amino acids in a protein.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06476))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06476 "translational spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06476 "A technique to investigate the distribution of the velocity of product ions from reactions of ions and neutral species.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06478))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06478 "transmission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06478 "The ratio of the number of ions leaving a region of a mass spectrometer to the number entering that region.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06480 "transmission electron energy loss spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06480 "Any technique in which an electron transparent specimen is bombarded with a finely focused electron beam and the energy distribution of the transmitted electrons is measured. This energy spectrum contains features corresponding to discrete losses of energy of the transmitted electrons due to excitation of electronic or plasmon states and provides information on the identity (and in some cases) chemical bonding of the elements in the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06481))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06481 "transmission electron microscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06481 "Any technique in which an electron transparent sample is bombarded with an electron beam and the intensity of the transmitted electrons which is determined by scattering phenomena (electron absorption phenomena) in the interior of the sample is recorded. TEM essentially provides a high resolution image of the microstructure of a thin sample. This technique is often just called 'electron microscopy'. The term transmission electron microscopy is however recommended for the sake of a clear distinction from other electron microscopic techniques.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06481 "electron microscopy")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06483))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06483 "transmission high energy electron diffraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06483 "Any technique which is based on the diffraction of high energy electrons (\\(E_{0} = 10{-}200\\ \\rm{keV}\\)) in crystalline materials and evaluation of the angular distribution of the transmitted electrons. The diffraction pattern represents an image of the reciprocal lattice and therefore contains information about crystal structure. This technique is also often called selected area electron diffraction (SAED). For consistency with other electron diffraction techniques, however, the term THEED is recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06483 "selected area electron diffraction")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06484 "transmittance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06484 "The ratio of the transmitted radiant power (\\(P_{\\lambda }\\)) to that incident on the sample (\\(P_{\\lambda }^{0}\\)): \\[T=\\frac{P_{\\lambda }}{P_{\\lambda }^{0}}\\] Internal transmittance refers to energy loss by absorption, whereas the total transmittance is that due to absorption, reflection, scatter, etc.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06484 "transmission factor")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06486))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06486 "transport")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06486 "The action of moving materials within the analytical instrument. Transport can involve any of several means including pressure flow, where the materials are moved by fluid pressure, either continuously or discontinuously; centrifugal flow, where the materials are moved by centrifugal force; spontaneous motion, where the materials are moved due to their intrinsic properties, e.g. diffusion, capillarity; and discrete transport, where materials are enclosed within a moving container.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06488))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06488 "transport interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06488 "Transport interferences affect the amount of desolvated sample passing through the horizontal flame cross-section per unit time at the observation height. They include factors affecting the rate of liquid consumption, \\(F_{\\rm{l}}\\), the efficiency of nebulization, \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{n}}\\), and the fraction desolvated \\(\\beta_{\\rm{s}}\\). They may be classified as non-specific (and physical).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06489))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06489 "transport number")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06489 "Of ions $\\ce{B}$, the current density due to ions $\\ce{B}$ divided by the sum of current densities of all the ions in the electrolyte. ISO recommends the name current fraction of ions $\\ce{B}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06490 "transposon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06490 "A movable DNA element that can be inserted at new sites into plasmids or chromosomes independently of the host cell recombination system. Prokaryotic transposons can carry genes that confer new phenotypic properties, such as resistance to antibiotics on the host.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06491 "transtactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06491 "A tactic polymer in which the main-chain double bonds of the configurational base units are entirely in the trans-arrangement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06492 "trapping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06492 "The interception of a reactive molecule or reaction intermediate so that it is removed from the system or converted into a more stable form for study or identification.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06493 "travel time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06493 "The time needed for the component to be carried from the burner tip to the observation space (in \\(\\mathrm{s}\\)). Travel time depends on the observation height and the flame rise velocity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06494))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06494 "treated solution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06494 "The test solution that has been subjected to reaction or separation procedures prior to measurement of some property.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06496 "<i>triangulo</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06496 "An affix used in names to denote three atoms bound into a triangle.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06497 "triazanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06497 "Triazane, $\\ce{NH2NHNH2}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06498))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06498 "triazenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06498 "Triazene, $\\ce{NH2N=NH}$, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06499 "triboluminescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06499 "Luminescence resulting from the rubbing together of the surface of certain solids. It can be produced, for example, when solids are crushed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06500 "trimethylenemethanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06500 "The diradical $\\ce{CH2=C(C^{.}H2)2}$, 2-methylenepropane-1,3-diyl, for which no Kekulé structure can be written, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06501 "trioxides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06501 "Organic derivatives of trioxidane, $\\ce{HOOOH}$, e.g. $\\ce{ROOOR'}$. When $\\ce{R'} = \\ce{H}$, the compound is a hydrotrioxide.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06502))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06502 "triple point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06502 "The point in a one-component system at which the temperature and pressure of three phases are in equilibrium. If there are \\(p\\) possible phases, there are \\(\\frac{p!}{(p\\,-\\,3)!\\ 3!}\\) triple points. Example: In the sulfur system four possible triple points (one metastable) exist for the four phases comprising rhombic $\\ce{S}$ (solid), monoclinic $\\ce{S}$ (solid), $\\ce{S}$ (liquid) and $\\ce{S}$ (vapour).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06503))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06503 "triplet state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06503 "A state having a total electron spin quantum number of 1.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06504))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06504 "triplet-triplet absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06504 "Absorption which takes place with the transition from the lowest triplet state of the molecule to higher triplet states (T → \\(T_{n}\\)) thus leading to the triplet-triplet absorption spectrum.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06505))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06505 "triplet-triplet annihilation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06505 "Two atoms or molecular entities both in a triplet state often interact (usually upon collision) to produce one atom or molecular entity in an excited singlet state and another in its ground singlet state. This is often, but not always, followed by delayed fluorescence.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06506))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06506 "triplet-triplet energy transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06506 "Energy transfer from an electronically excited triplet donor to produce an electronically excited acceptor in its triplet state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06507))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06507 "triplet-triplet transitions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06507 "Electronic transitions in which both the initial and final states are triplet states.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06508))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06508 "<i>triprismo</i>-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06508 "An affix used in names to denote six atoms bound into a triangular prism.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06509))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06509 "tritactic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06509 "A tactic polymer that contains three sites of defined stereoisomerism in the main chain of the configurational base unit. Example:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06510))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06510 "triterpenoids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06510 "Terpenoids having a $\\ce{C30}$ skeleton.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06513))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06513 "tritium")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06513 "A specific name for the atom $\\ce{^{3}H}$. The cation $\\ce{^{3}H^{+}}$ is a triton, the species $\\ce{^{3}H^{−}}$ is a tritide anion and $\\ce{^{3}H}$ is the tritio group.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06516))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06516 "trivial name")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06516 "A name having no part used in a systematic sense.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06517))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06517 "Troe expression")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06517 "A semiempirical description of the rate coefficient for a specific three body reaction [e.g. ] which represents well its pressure and temperature dependence in the region of transition between second and third order kinetics.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06518))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06518 "tropilidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06518 "This term has inconsistently been used to mean cyclohepta-1,3,5-trienes or to mean cyclohepta-1,3,5-trienes in dynamic equilibrium with bicyclo-[4.1.0]hepta-2,4-dienes, so that the structure of the bulk substance is indeterminate in the time scale of the method of observation. Use of this term is therefore discouraged.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06519))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06519 "tropolones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06519 "2-Hydroxycyclohepta-2,4,6-trienones and derivatives formed by substitution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06520))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06520 "tropones")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06520 "Compounds that contain the cyclohepta-2,4,6-trienone ring system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06521 "tropopause")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06521 "The region of the atmosphere which joins the troposphere and stratosphere, and where the decreasing temperature with altitude, characteristic of the troposphere ceases, and the temperature increase with height which is characteristic of the stratosphere begins.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06522))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06522 "troposphere")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06522 "The lowest layer of the atmosphere, ranging from the ground to the base of the stratosphere (tropopause) at \\(10{-}15\\ \\rm{km}\\) of altitude depending on the latitude and meteorological conditions. About 70% of the mass of the atmosphere is in the troposphere. This is where most of the weather features occur and where the chemistry of the reactive anthropogenic species released into the atmosphere takes place.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06523))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06523 "tropylium ions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06523 "The delocalized carbenium ion, cycloheptatrienylium, $\\ce{C7H7^{+}}$, derived formally by detachment of one hydride ion from the $\\ce{CH2}$ group of cyclohepta-1,3,5-triene and substitution derivatives thereof.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06524))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06524 "tropyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06524 "The delocalized radicals derived formally by abstraction of one hydrogen from the $\\ce{CH2}$ group of cyclohepta-1,3,5-triene and substitution derivatives thereof.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06525))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06525 "true coincidence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06525 "A coincidence of events occurring in the same atom or in physically connected atoms.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06527))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06527 "true value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06527 "The value that characterizes a quantity perfectly in the conditions that exist when that quantity is considered. It is an ideal value which could be arrived at only if all causes of measurement error were eliminated, and the entire population was sampled.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06528))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06528 "tub conformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06528 "A conformation (of symmetry group \\(D_{\\rm{2d}}\\)) of an eight-membered ring in which the four atoms forming one pair of diametrically opposite bonds in the ring lie in one plane and all other ring atoms lie to one side of that plane. It is analogous to the boat conformation of cyclohexane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06529))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06529 "tunable laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06529 "A high intensity source with narrow spectral bandwidth, with and without frequency doubling and/or Raman shifting. Its use may enable the spectral apparatus to be omitted. Tunable lasers may be either continuous (CW) or pulsed in nature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06530))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06530 "Tung distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06530 "A continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form: \\[f_{\\rm{w}}(x)\\ \\rm{d}x = a\\ b\\ x^{b-1}\\ \\rm{e}^{-a\\ x^{b}}\\ \\rm{d}x\\] where \\(x\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive adjustable parameters.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06531 "tunnelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06531 "The process by which a particle or a set of particles crosses a barrier on its potential-energy surface without having the energy required to surmount this barrier. Since the rate of tunnelling decreases with increasing reduced mass, it is significant in the context of isotope effects of hydrogen isotopes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06531 "quantum-mechanical tunnelling")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06532 "turbidimetric titration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06532 "The process in which a precipitant is added incrementally to a highly dilute polymer solution and the intensity of light scattered by, or the turbidity due to, the finely dispersed particles of the polymer-rich phase is measured as a function of the amount of precipitant added.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06533))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06533 "turbidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06533 "The apparent absorbance of the incident radiation due to scattering. For small particles, direct proportionality exists between turbidity and the Rayleigh ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06534))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06534 "turnover frequency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06534 "Commonly called the turnover number, \\(N\\), and defined, as in enzyme catalysis, as molecules reacting per active site in unit time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06537))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06537 "twisted intramolecular charge transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06537 "In a TICT state formed by intramolecular charge transfer in an electron donor ($\\ce{D}$)/acceptor ($\\ce{A}$) molecule, the $\\ce{D}$ and $\\ce{A}$ subgroups have a mutually perpendicular configuration that leads to electronic decoupling of $\\ce{D}$ and $\\ce{A}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06540))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06540 "two-dimensional chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06540 "A procedure in which parts or all of the separated sample components are subjected to additional separation steps. This can be done e.g. by conducting a particular fraction eluting from the column into another column (system) having different separation characteristics. When combined with additional steps, this may be described as multi-dimensional chromatography. In planar chromatography two-dimensional chromatography refers to the chromatographic process in which the components are caused to migrate first in one direction and subsequently in a direction at right angles to the first one; the two elutions are carried out with different eluents.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06541))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06541 "two-photon excitation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06541 "Excitation resulting from successive or simultaneous absorption of two photons by an atom or molecular entity. This term is used for successive absorption only if some of the excitation energy of the first photon remains in the atom or molecular entity before absorption of the second photon. The simultaneous two-photon absorption can also be called biphotonic excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06542))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06542 "two-photon process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06542 "A photophysical or photochemical event triggered by a two-photon excitation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06543))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06543 "two-site immunoradiometric assay")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06543 "Immunoradiometric assay involving two sets of antibodies, one of which is labelled, that combine with different immunoreactive sites of an antigen molecule.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06812))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06812 "target")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06812 "Any organism, organ, tissue, cell, or cell constituent that is subject to the action of an agent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06846))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06846 "topical effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06846 "Consequence of application of a substance to the surface of the body which occurs at the point of application.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06897))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06897 "thermotropic mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06897 "A mesophase formed by heating a solid or cooling an isotropic liquid, or by heating or cooling a thermodynamically stable mesophase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06898))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06898 "transition temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06898 "The temperature at which the transition from mesophase X to mesophase Y occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06915))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06915 "threshold limit value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06915 "The concentration in air to which it is believed that most workers can be exposed daily without an adverse effect (i.e., effectively, the threshold between safe and dangerous concentrations). The values were established (and are revised annually) by the ACGIH and are time-weighted concentrations (TWA) for a 7- or 8-h workday and 40-h workweek, and thus are related to chronic effects. A short-term exposure limit (STEL) is defined as a 15-min TWA exposure, which should not be exceeded at any time during a workday even if the 8-h TWA is within the TLV-TWA.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT06926))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT06926 "total velocity of the analyte")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT06926 """The sum of electrophoretic velocity, \\(
u_{\\rm{ep}}\\), of an ion and the electroosmotic velocity, \\(
u _{\\rm{eo}}\\). \\[
u_{\\rm{tot}} = 
u_{\\rm{ep}}\\,+\\,
u_{\\rm{eo}}\\] This quantity can be measured experimentally as the effective length of the capillary divided by the migration time (\\(L_{\\rm{eff}}/t_{\\rm{m}}\\)).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07167 "telechelic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07167 "Prepolymer capable of entering into further polymerization or other reactions through its reactive end-groups.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07168 "thermosetting polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07168 "Prepolymer in a soft solid or viscous state that changes irreversibly into an infusible, insoluble polymer network by curing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07268))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07268 "thermoplastic elastomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07268 "Elastomer comprising a thermoreversible network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07484))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07484 "time-dependent density functional theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07484 "Methods for computing accurate excitation energies at a low computational cost in large molecular species within the time-dependent scheme and the density functional theory. It is the only available DFT-based method for computing electronic excitation energies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07487))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07487 "transient-stimulated emission pumping")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07487 "Transient-spectroscopy technique in which the excited state dynamics is probed via stimulating the molecular species from the photo-excited state (produced by a short pump pulse) back to the ground state by means of a short dump pulse at various pump–dump time delays.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07490))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07490 "tungsten-halogen lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07490 "Tungsten lamp.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07491))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07491 "two-photon photochromism")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07491 "Photochromic process involving a two-photon process. The process might involve the simultaneous or the sequential absorption of two photons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07531))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07531 "thermoreversible gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07531 "Swollen network in which the junction points are thermally reversible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07532))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07532 "thixotropic gel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07532 "Gel which has a reduced viscosity on the application of a finite shear but which recovers its original viscosity when the shear is discontinued.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07544))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07544 "theoretical ceramic yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07544 "Ceramic yield based on the stoichiometry of the ceramization process.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07573 "thermoreversible network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07573 "Reversible network that forms or breaks up as the temperature is changed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07576 "transient network")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07576 "Network that exists only transiently.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07595))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07595 "transient crosslink")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07595 "Crosslink formed by intermolecular or intramolecular interactions that are unstable under the conditions of use of the material formed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07600 "thermoreversible junction point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07600 "Junction point in a polymer network that can be destroyed and formed reversibly by a change of temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07601 "transient junction point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07601 "Junction point in a polymer network that exists only for a finite period of time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:TT07616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:TT07616 "thermal curing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:TT07616 "Curing induced by heating.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06546))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06546 "ultimate capacity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06546 "The theoretical maximum capacity of a solvent containing a given concentration of extractant for a solute under any conditions. Where appropriate the term stoichiometric capacity can be used.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06546 "stoichiometric capacity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06547))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06547 "ultrafiltrate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06547 "The solution obtained by ultrafiltration, in general not of the same composition as the equilibrium solution.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06548))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06548 "ultrafiltration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06548 "A separation process whereby a solution containing a solute of molecular size significantly greater than that of the solvent molecule is removed from the solvent by the application of a hydraulic pressure which forces only the solvent to flow through a suitable membrane, usually having a pore size in the range \\(0.001{-}0.1\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06549))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06549 "ultrasonic detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06549 "Sound is generated in a reference cell and a measuring cell. An eluted component passing through the measuring cell changes the velocity of sound in the cell. This change is detected by a phase shift of acoustic signals between the two cells. This detector is usually employed for inorganic gases in the region where thermal conductivity detectors are not sufficiently sensitive. The detection threshold is of the order of \\(0.1\\ \\rm{ppmv}\\). The linear dynamic range is of the order of \\(10^{3}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06550))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06550 "umpire sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06550 "A sample taken, prepared and stored in an agreed upon manner for the purpose of settling a dispute. The agreement usually extends beyond the sample to the basis for reaching a decision (e.g. quantity of material from which taken, use of a third party and criteria serving as the basis for acceptance, rejection or economic adjustment). The term 'reference sample' has been used in this context but this term more properly should be used in conjunction with a 'reference material' or 'reference standard' which has a true or assigned value for a constituent or property. One of the characteristics of a reference material or reference standard is that it must have a negligible sampling error between test portions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06550 "reserve sample")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06551))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06551 "umpolung")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06551 "Any process by which the normal alternating donor and acceptor reactivity pattern of a chain, which is due to the presence of $\\ce{O}$ or $\\ce{N}$ heteroatoms, is interchanged. Reactivity umpolung is most often achieved by temporary exchange of heteroatoms ($\\ce{N}$, $\\ce{O}$) by others, such as $\\ce{P}$, $\\ce{S}$ and $\\ce{Se}$. The original meaning of the term has been extended to the reversal of any commonly accepted reactivity pattern. For example, reaction of $\\ce{R–C#CX}$ ($\\ce{X}$ = halide) as a synthon for $\\ce{'R–C#C^{+}}$ (i.e. electrophilic acetylene) is an umpolung of the normal more common acetylide, $\\ce{R–C#C^{−}}$ (i.e. nucleophilic) reactivity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06552))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06552 "unactivated adsorption process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06552 "If the temperature coefficient of the rate of adsorption is very small, the adsorption process is said to be unactivated (i.e. to have a negligible activation energy). In this case the sticking coefficient at low coverages may be near unity particularly for smaller molecules.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06553))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06553 "uncertainty of measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06553 "Parameter, associated with the result of a measurement, that characterizes the dispersion of the values that could reasonably be attributed to the measurand (the quantity being measured).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06554))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06554 "unified atomic mass unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06554 "Non-SI unit of mass (equal to the atomic mass constant), defined as one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom in its ground state and used to express masses of atomic particles, \\(\\rm{u} \\approx \\pu{1.6605402(10)E-27 kg}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06554 "atomic mass unit")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06555))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06555 "uniform corrosion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06555 "Corrosion is uniform if the time average of the corrosion current through a unit area of any macroscopic dimension is independent of the position on the surface.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06556))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06556 "uniformly labelled")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06556 "A selectively labelled compound where all atoms of a particular element are labelled in the same isotopic ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06557))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06557 "uniformly labelled tracer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06557 "A tracer in which the label is uniformly distributed over its possible positions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06558))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06558 "uniform polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06558 "A polymer composed of molecules uniform with respect to relative molecular mass and constitution.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06558 "monodisperse polymer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06560))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06560 "unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06560 "Each of the discrete, identifiable portions of material suitable for removal from a population as a sample or as a portion of a sample, and which can be individually considered, examined or tested, or combined. In the case of sampling bulk materials (or large packages), the units are increments, created by a sampling device. In the case of packaged materials, the unit may vary with the level of commercial distribution. For example, an individual piece of candy is the sampling unit at the consumer level; a package of individual pieces is the sampling unit at the retail level; a carton of packages is the sampling unit at the wholesale level; a pallet of cartons is the shipping unit at the distribution centre level; and a truckload of pallets is the consignment unit at the manufacture level. Before packaging, the bin containing the individual pieces would be the bulk lot (or batch) for sampling.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06560 "item")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06560 "portion")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06560 "individual")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06561))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06561 "unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06561 "Particular quantity, defined and adopted by convention, with which other quanitities of the same kind are compared in order to express their magnitudes relative to that quantity. Units have conventionally assigned names and symbols.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06562))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06562 "unit cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06562 "The smallest, regularly repeating material portion contained in a parallelepiped from which a crystal is formed by parallel displacements in three dimensions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06563))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06563 "unit system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06563 "Set of base units, together with derived units, defined in accordance with given rules, for a given system of quantities.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06564))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06564 "universal calibration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06564 "A calibration of a size-exclusion chromatography set-up based on the finding that the retention volume of a molecular or particulate species is a single-valued function of an appropriate size parameter of this molecule or particle, irrespective of its chemical nature and structure. The product of the intrinsic viscosity and molar mass, \\([\\eta]M\\), has been widely used as the size parameter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06565))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06565 "universal detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06565 "A detector which responds to every component in the column effluent except the mobile phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06566 "unperturbed dimensions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06566 "The dimensions of an actual polymer random coil in a θ state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06567))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06567 "unreactive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06567 "Failing to react with a specified chemical species under specified conditions. The term should not be used in place of stable, since a relatively more stable species may nevertheless be more reactive than some reference species towards a given reaction partner.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06569 "unstable")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06569 "The opposite of stable, i.e. the chemical species concerned has a higher molar Gibbs energy than some assumed standard. The term should not be used in place of reactive or transient, although more reactive or transient species are frequently also more unstable. (Very unstable chemical species tend to undergo exothermic unimolecular decompositions. Variations in the structure of the related chemical species of this kind generally affect the energy of the transition states for these decompositions less than they affect the stability of the decomposing chemical species. Low stability may therefore parallel a relatively high rate of unimolecular decomposition.)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06571 "unstable ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06571 "An ion which is sufficiently excited to dissociate within the ion source.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06574 "upconversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06574 """The process by which two photons with frequencies \\(
u _{2}\\) and \\(
u _{3}\\) combine in a non-linear medium to produce a higher energy photon with frequency \\(
u _{1}\\) such that \\(
u _{1}=
u _{2}+
u _{3}\\).""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06576 "upper limit of measurement")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06576 "Highest value of the air quality characteristic which can be measured by an instrument; its variations, caused for example by instability, are expected to lie within specified limits. The difference between the lower detection limit and the upper limit of measurement constitutes the dynamic range of the instrument.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06577 "ureides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06577 "N-Acyl or N,N'-diacyl ureas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06578 "urethanes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06578 """An alternative term for the compounds $\\ce{R2NC(=O)OR'}$ ($\\ce{R} 
eq \\ce{H}$), esters of carbamic acids, $\\ce{R2NC(=O)OH}$, in strict use limited to the ethyl esters, but widely used in the general sense, e.g. 'polyurethane resins'.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06578 "urethans")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06579 "uronic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06579 "Monocarboxylic acids formally derived by oxidation to a carboxy group of the terminal $\\ce{–CH2OH}$ group of aldoses, e.g. ᴅ-glucuronic acid.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06579 "glycuronic acids")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06580 "uronium salts")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06580 "Salts of O-hydronated urea, having the structure $\\ce{[HOC(=NH2)NH2]^{+}X^{−}}$ (and O- and N- hydrocarbyl derivatives).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06582 "ultraviolet dose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06582 "A dose of ultraviolet (UV) radiation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06582 "UV dose")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:U06584))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:U06584 "ultraviolet stabilizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:U06584 "A substance added to a sample to prevent photodeterioration by ultraviolet (UV) light.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:U06584 "UV stabilizer")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT06806))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT06806 "uptake")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT06806 "Entry of a substance into the body, into an organ, into a tissue, into a cell, or into the body fluids by passage through a membrane or by other means.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT06882))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT06882 "upstream")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT06882 "Side of a membrane into which penetrants enter from the feed stream.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT06884))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT06884 "uphill transport")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT06884 "Process in which diffusion of a component occurs from a less concentrated stream to a more concentrated permeable stream.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT07280))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT07280 "upper critical solution temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT07280 "Critical temperature above which a mixture is miscible.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT07492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT07492 "ultraviolet")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT07492 "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from about \\(100\\ \\rm{nm}\\) to \\(400\\ \\rm{nm}\\). This region is divided into four sub-bands as follows: Vacuum UV (VUV)&nbsp;\\(100{-}200\\ \\rm{nm}\\)UV-C \\(200{-}280\\ \\rm{nm}\\)UV-B\\(280{-}315\\ \\rm{nm}\\)UV-A\\(315{-}400\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT07493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT07493 "uniaxial sample")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT07493 "Sample characterized by a (unique) sample axis Z with all directions perpendicular to Z being equivalent. In other words, the sample properties are invariant to rotation around Z. Uniaxiality exists in many anisotropic samples and simplifies the interpretation of their spectra considerably.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:UT07638))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:UT07638 "uniaxial pressing")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:UT07638 "Application of pressure in one direction during ceramization to achieve a uniform densification and the production of a compact monolith.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V05019))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V05019 "<i>ρ</i>-value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V05019 "A measure of the susceptibility to the influence of substituent groups on the rate constant or equilibrium constant of a particular organic reaction involving a family of related substrates. Defined by Hammett for the effect of ring substituents in meta- and para-positions of aromatic side-chain reactions by the empirical '\\(\\rho \\sigma \\)-equation' of the general form: \\[\\log _{10}(\\frac{k_{\\rm{X}}}{k_{\\rm{H}}}) = \\rho \\ \\sigma _{\\rm{X}}\\] in which \\(\\sigma _{\\rm{X}}\\) is a constant characteristic of the substituent X and of its position in the reactant molecule. More generally (and not only for aromatic series), \\(\\rho \\)-values (modified with appropriate subscripts and superscripts) are used to designate the susceptibility of reaction series for families of various organic compounds to any substituent effects, as given by the modified set of \\(\\sigma \\)-constants in an empirical \\(\\rho \\sigma \\)-correlation. Reactions with a positive \\(\\rho \\)-value are accelerated (or the equilibrium constants of analogous equilibria are increased) by substituents with positive \\(\\sigma \\)-constants. Since the sign of \\(\\sigma \\) was defined so that substituents with a positive \\(\\sigma \\) increase the acidity of benzoic acid, such substituents are generally described as attracting electrons away from the aromatic ring. It follows that reactions with a positive \\(\\rho \\)-value are considered to involve a transition state (or reaction product) so that the difference in energy between this state and the reactants is decreased by a reduction in electron density at the reactive site of the substrate.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:V05019 "rho-value")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06585))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06585 "vaccine")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06585 "An agent containing antigens produced from killed, attenuated or live pathogenic microorganisms, synthetic peptides or by recombinant organisms, used for stimulating the immune system of the recipient to produce specific antibodies providing active immunity and/or passive immunity in the progeny.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06586))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06586 "vacuum phototube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06586 "A photo-emissive detector inside an evacuated envelope with a transparent window, the photocathode, and the anode. The photocathode can be opaque or semitransparent.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06587))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06587 "vacuum system")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06587 "Those components used to lower the pressure within a mass spectrometer are all parts of the vacuum system. This includes not only the various pumping components but also valves, gauges and associated electronic or other control devices, the chamber in which ions are formed and detected, and the vacuum envelope.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06588))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06588 "valence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06588 "The maximum number of univalent atoms (originally hydrogen or chlorine atoms) that may combine with an atom of the element under consideration, or with a fragment, or for which an atom of this element can be substituted.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06589))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06589 "valence band")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06589 "Highest energy continuum of energy levels in a solid that is fully occupied by electrons at \\(0\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06590))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06590 "valence isomer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06590 "A constitutional isomer interrelated with another by pericyclic reactions. For example, Dewar benzene, prismane and benzvalene are valence isomers of benzene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06591))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06591 "valence tautomerization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06591 "The term describes simple reversible and generally rapid isomerizations or degenerate rearrangements involving the formation and rupture of single and/or double bonds, without migration of atoms or groups; e.g.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06592))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06592 "valence transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06592 "A transition observed in certain rare-earth and actinide materials in which the electronic occupation of the 4f or 5f orbital changes with external conditions for example, temperature and pressure. Example: The transition at approximately \\(0.65\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) where black, semiconducting $\\ce{Sm^{2+}S^{2−}}$ changes to golden metallic $\\ce{(Sm^{3+} + e^{−})S^{2-}}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06593))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06593 "value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06593 "Magnitude of a particular quantity generally expressed as a unit of measurement multiplied by a number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06594))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06594 "value of a division")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06594 "The reciprocal of the sensitivity; like the latter, it usually varies with load. It is determined by empirical calibration.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06597))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06597 "van der Waals forces")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06597 "The attractive or repulsive forces between molecular entities (or between groups within the same molecular entity) other than those due to bond formation or to the electrostatic interaction of ions or of ionic groups with one another or with neutral molecules. The term includes: dipole–dipole, dipole-induced dipole and London (instantaneous induced dipole-induced dipole) forces. The term is sometimes used loosely for the totality of nonspecific attractive or repulsive intermolecular forces.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06598))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06598 "vaporization temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06598 "The temperature of the atomization surface at which analyte loss becomes statistically significant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06599))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06599 "vapour phase interference")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06599 "Interference caused by a change in the fraction of analyte dissociated, ionized or excited in the gaseous phase. (Note: here 'dissociation' means the formation of free neutral atoms from free molecules in the gaseous phase. The term atomization is here not appropriate because the latter also covers the formation of free atomic ions.) These interferences may be called dissociation, ionization and excitation interference, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06600))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06600 "variable")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06600 "The quantity or characteristic measured or computed. The corresponding numerical value may be taken for statistical handling; it may, for example, be a measured value or result.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06601))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06601 "variable pathlength cell")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06601 "A cell whose pathlength can be varied either continuously or in steps by means of spacers.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06602))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06602 "variance")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06602 "The sum of the squares of the standard deviations, expressing the contributions to the overall precision from various sources of uncertainty.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06603))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06603 "variational transition state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06603 "This expression is applied to modifications of conventional transition state theory in which the position of the dividing surface in the potential-energy surface is varied. The rate is calculated with the surface at various positions, and the lowest rate calculated is taken to be closest to the truth.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06606))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06606 "vector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06606 "A DNA molecule (plasmid, virus, bacteriophage, artificial or cut DNA molecule) capable of being replicated and bearing cloning sites for the introduction of foreign DNA, used to introduce this DNA into host cells.Any organism that transmits a disease between two hosts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06607))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06607 "velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06607 "Vector quantity equal to the derivative of the position vector with respect to time (symbols \\(u\\), \\(v\\), \\(w\\) for components of \\(c\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06608))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06608 "velocity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06608 "The flux density \\(N_{\\rm{B}}\\) is related to the velocity by the equation \\[N_{\\rm{B}}=c_{\\rm{B}}\\ v_{\\rm{B}}\\] The vector \\(v_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the macroscopic average velocity at which the species B moves (which is to be distinguished from the random molecular velocity); \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the concentration of species B (\\(\\pu{mol m-3}\\)). Note that the velocity is defined with respect to a frame of reference.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06609))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06609 "verdazyl radicals")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06609 "Relatively stable delocalized radicals, derived from hydrazyl, $\\ce{H2NN^{.}H}$, of the types shown, in particular the six-membered ring:")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06610))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06610 "vertical ionization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06610 "A process whereby an electron is removed from a molecule in its ground or an excited state so rapidly that a positive ion is produced without change in the positions or momenta of the atoms. The resultant ion is often in an excited state.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06612))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06612 "Verwey transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06612 "An electron-ordering transition occurring in a mixed-valent system that results in an ordering of formal valence states in the low-temperature phase. Example: The prototype system, first identified by Verwey, is the ferrospinel magnetite, $\\ce{Fe^{3+}[Fe^{3+}Fe^{2+}]O4}$ in which an ordering of $\\ce{Fe^{3+}}$ and $\\ce{Fe^{2+}}$ ions within octahedral sites is thought to occur below \\(T_{\\rm{v}}\\approx 120\\ \\rm{K}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06614))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06614 "vibrational redistribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06614 "Intramolecular redistribution of energy among the vibrational modes usually giving a statistical distribution of their populations, characterized by the 'vibrational temperature'. For large molecules, this process does not require collisions.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06615))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06615 "vibrational relaxation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06615 "The loss of vibrational excitation energy by a molecular entity through energy transfer to the environment caused by collisions. The molecular entity relaxes into vibrational equilibrium with its environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06616))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06616 "vibrational term")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06616 "Vibrational energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06617))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06617 "vibronic coupling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06617 "Interaction between electronic and vibrational motions in a molecular entity.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06618))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06618 "vibronic transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06618 "A transition which involves a change in both the electronic and vibrational quantum numbers of a molecular entity, as opposed to purely electronic or purely vibrational transitions. The transition occurs between two states, just as in a purely electronic transition, but involves a change in both electronic and vibrational energy.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06619))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06619 "vidicon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06619 "A vacuum tube containing a photosensitive area, or target, and an electron gun to read the signal from the target. The silicon target consists of a two-dimensional array of Si-photodiodes having a common cathode and isolated anodes. irradiation of the target causes the production of electron-hole pairs which, by recombination, leads to a depletion of the surface charge.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06620))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06620 "vinyl carbenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06620 "Carbenes having a vinylic group on a carbenic carbon atom $\\ce{R2C=CRC{:}R  R2C^{+}–CR=C^{−}R}$. E.g. $\\ce{H2C=CHC{:}H}$, prop-2-en-1-ylidene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06621))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06621 "vinylic cations")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06621 "Carbocations having the structure $\\ce{R2C=C^{+}–R}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06622))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06622 "vinylic groups")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06622 "The vinyl group ($\\ce{CH2=CH\\bond{–}}$) and derivatives formed by substitution. Informally, a group, such as $\\ce{–OH}$, attached to the free valence of a (substituted) vinyl group is sometimes referred to as 'vinylic'.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06623))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06623 "vinylidenes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06623 "Carbenes in which the carbenic carbon atom has a double bond to another carbon atom $\\ce{R2C=C{:}}$, e.g. $\\ce{H2C=C{:}}$ ethenylidene.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06624))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06624 "viologens")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06624 "1,1-Di(hydrocarbyl)-4,4'-bipyridinium salts.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06625))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06625 "virial coefficients")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06625 "Coefficients (\\(B\\rm{,}C\\rm{,}\\,...\\)) in the virial equation of state for a real gas \\(p\\ V_{\\rm{m}}=R\\ T\\ (1+\\frac{B}{V_{\\rm{m}}}+\\frac{C}{V_{\\rm{m}}^{2}}+\\,...)\\) where \\(p\\) is the pressure, \\(V_{\\rm{m}}\\) the molar volume, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the thermodynamic temperature.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06626))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06626 "virtual transition")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06626 "A non-observable liquid-crystal transition that occurs below the crystallization temperature and is determined by extrapolation in binary phase diagrams.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06628))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06628 "viscosity function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06628 "A coefficient connecting the intrinsic viscosity, the radius of gyration and the molar mass of a chain macromolecule, according to the equation: \\[\\rm{[}\\eta \\rm{]} = \\frac{\\mathit{\\Phi }\\ 6^{3/2}\\ ( < s^{2} > )^{3/2}}{M}\\] where \\(\\rm{[}\\eta \\rm{]}\\) is the intrinsic viscosity, \\(s\\) is the radius of gyration and \\(M\\) is the molar mass. The viscosity function is often referred to as the Flory constant.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06631))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06631 "visibility")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06631 "Defined as the greatest distance at which a black object of suitable dimensions can be seen and recognized against the horizon sky, or, in the case of night observations, could be seen and recognized if the general illumination were raised to the normal daylight level. The criterion of recognizing the object, not just seeing the object without recognition, is used. Transmissometers, telephotometers and sun photometers are devices used to measure the degree of transmission of light. Nephelometers (integrating) are used to measure visibility by way of the light scattering from aerosols in the air mass.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06632))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06632 "volatilization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06632 "The conversion of a solid or liquid to a gas or vapour by application of heat, by reducing pressure, by chemical reaction or by a combination of these processes.Example: \\(\\rm{W(s)}+\\frac{3}{2}\\rm{O}_{2}\\:\\rm{(g)}\\:\\rightarrow \\:\\rm{WO}_{3}\\:\\rm{(g)}\\)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06633))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06633 "volatilizer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06633 "Material added to a sample to increase its volatilization or that of some component of it. Volatilizers increase the fraction volatilized either by forming more volatile compounds or by increasing the total surface area of all analyte particles (e.g. by explosive disintegration or by dispersal of the analyte in a highly volatile matrix).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06634))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06634 "volt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06634 "SI derived unit of electric potential, \\(\\pu{V} = \\pu{J C-1} = \\pu{m2 kg s-3 A-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06635))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06635 "voltage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06635 "The use of this term is discouraged, and the term applied potential should be used instead, for non-periodic signals. However, it is retained here for sinusoidal and other periodic signals because no suitable substitute for it has been proposed.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06639))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06639 "voltammetric constant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06639 """In linear-sweep voltammetry and related techniques, the empirical quantity defined by the equation \\[\\frac{i_{p}}{A\\ \\sqrt{
u}\\ c_{\\rm{B}}}\\quad \\left(=\\frac{j_{p}}{\\sqrt{
u}\\ c_{\\rm{B}}}\\right)\\] where \\(i_{p}\\) is the peak current, A is the area of the electrode-solution interface, \\(
u\\) is the rate of change of applied potential, and \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the bulk concentration of the substance B whose reduction or oxidation is responsible for the peak in question.""")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06641))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06641 "volume content")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06641 "The volume of an (isolated) specified component (C) divided by the mass of the system (S), e.g. patient–blood volume content. Volume content should not be confused with specific volume (or massic volume), which is the volume of the system divided by the mass of the system.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06642))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06642 "volume flow rate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06642 "Volume of a component crossing a surface divided by the time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06643))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06643 "volume fraction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06643 "Volume of a constituent of a mixture divided by the sum of volumes of all constituents prior to mixing.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06644))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06644 "volume of activation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06644 "A quantity derived from the pressure dependence of the rate constant of a reaction (mainly used for reactions in solution), defined by the equation: \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }V=- R\\ T\\ (\\frac{\\partial (\\ln k)}{\\partial p})_{T}\\] providing that the rate constants of all reactions (except first-order reactions) are expressed in pressure-independent concentration units, such as \\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\) at a fixed temperature and pressure. The volume of activation is interpreted, according to transition state theory, as the difference between the partial molar volumes of the transition state (\\(V\\)) and the sums of the partial volumes of the reactants at the same temperature and pressure, i.e. \\[\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}V = ^{\\ddagger}V- \\sum \\left(r\\ V_{\\rm{R}}\\right)\\] where \\(r\\) is the order in the reactant R and \\(V_{\\rm{R}}\\) its partial molar volume.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06645))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06645 "volume of the stationary phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06645 "The volume of the stationary liquid phase or of the active solid or of the gel in the column. The volume of any solid support is not included.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06648))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06648 "volume strain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06648 "The change of volume divided by the original volume.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:V06648 "bulk strain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06649))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06649 "volumetric flowrate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06649 "The flowrate of the mobile phase (\\(\\pu{cm3 min-1}\\)), normally specified at the column temperature and outlet pressure, although the measurement may be made at ambient temperature and must be corrected accordingly (and possibly also for water vapour present in the flowmeter).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06650))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06650 "volume viscosity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06650 "A quantity $\\upzeta\\!$ which enters into equations at any point where the flow involves a change in volume, i.e. is dilatational. If the deformation is purely dilatational, the average of three normal stress components is: \\[\\sigma =\\frac{1}{3}\\ (\\sigma _{xx}+\\sigma _{yy}+\\sigma _{zz})=- p+\\zeta \\ (\\frac{\\partial v_{x}}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial v_{y}}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial v_{z}}{\\partial z})\\] where \\(p\\) is the hydrostatic pressure at the point considered in the absence of motion.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:V06650 "dilatational viscosity")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:V06651))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:V06651 "volumic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:V06651 "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by volume. charge density may thus be called volumic charge which is the electric charge in a section of space divided by the volume of that section.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:VT07153))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:VT07153 "vulcanization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:VT07153 "Chemical crosslinking of high-molar-mass linear or branched polymers to give a polymer network.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:VT07496))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:VT07496 "visible")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:VT07496 "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from about \\(400\\ \\rm{nm}\\) to \\(760\\ \\rm{nm}\\). This is the wavelength region to which the human eye is sensitive. There are no precise limits for the spectral range of visible radiation since they depend upon the amount of radiant power reaching the retina and on the responsivity of the observer.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:VT07497))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:VT07497 "VUV")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:VT07497 "Acronym for vacuum ultraviolet.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:VT07640))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:VT07640 "viscous sintering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:VT07640 "Sintering process by which it is possible to densify gels to glasses and ceramics at elevated temperatures.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:VT07640 "viscous flow sintering")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06653))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06653 "Walden inversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06653 "Retention of configuration is the preservation of integrity of the spatial arrangement of bonds to a chiral centre during a chemical reaction or transformation. It is also the configurational correlation when a chemical species Xabcd is converted into the chemical species Xabce having the same relative configuration. The configurational change when a chemical species Xabcd (where X is typically carbon), having a tetrahedral arrangement of bonds to X, is converted into the chemical species Xabce having the opposite relative configuration (or when it undergoes an identity reaction in which Xabcd of opposite configuration is produced) is called a Walden inversion or inversion of configuration. The occurrence of a Walden inversion during a chemical transformation is sometimes indicated in the chemical equation by the symbol shown below in place of a simple arrow pointing from reactants to products.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06654))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06654 "wall-coated open-tubular column")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06654 "A column in which the liquid stationary phase is coated on the essentially unmodified smooth inner wall of the tube.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06654 "WCOT column")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06655))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06655 "wash out")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06655 "The removal from the atmosphere of gases and sometimes particles by their solution in or attachment to raindrops as they fall.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06656))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06656 "watt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06656 "SI derived unit of power, \\(\\pu{W} = \\pu{J s-1} = \\pu{m2 kg s-3}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06657))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06657 "wavefunction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06657 "The solution of the Schrödinger equation, eigenfunction of the hamiltonian operator. Complex conjugate functions are denoted by an asterisk read as 'star'.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06657 "state function")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06658))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06658 "wave height")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06658 "The limiting current of an individual wave, frequently expressed in arbitrary units for convenience.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06659))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06659 "wavelength")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06659 "Distance in the direction of propagation of a periodic wave between two successive points where at a given time the phase is the same.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06660))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06660 "wavelength converter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06660 "A wavelength converter converts radiation at one wavelength to radiation at another detectable wavelength or at a wavelength of improved responsivity of the detector. The classical wavelength converter consists of a screen of luminescent material that absorbs radiation and radiates at a longer wavelength. Such materials are often used to convert ultraviolet to visible radiation for detection by conventional phototubes. In X-ray spectroscopy a converter that emits optical radiation is called a scintillator. In most cases wavelength conversion is from short to long wavelength, but in the case of conversion of long to short wavelength the process is sometimes called upconversion. Wavelengths of coherent sources can be converted using nonlinear optical techniques. A typical example is frequency doubling.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06661))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06661 "wavelength dispersion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06661 "Spatial separation of characteristic X-rays according to their wavelengths.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06662))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06662 "wavelength-dispersive X-ray fluorescence analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06662 "A kind of X-ray fluorescence analysis involving the measurement of the wavelength spectrum of the emitted radiation e.g. by using a diffraction grating or crystal.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06663))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06663 "wavelength error")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06663 "The error in absorbance which may occur if there is a difference between the (mean) wavelength of the radiation entering the sample cell and the indicated wavelength on the spectrometer scale.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06664))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06664 "wavenumber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06664 """The reciprocal of the wavelength, \\(\\lambda \\), or the number of waves per unit length along the direction of propagation. The SI unit is \\(\\rm{m}^{-1}\\), but a commonly used unit is \\(\\pu{cm-1}\\). Symbols \\(\\overset{˜}{
u }\\) in a vacuum, \\(\\sigma \\) in a medium.""")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06664 "repetency")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06665))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06665 "weak collision")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06665 "A collision between two molecules in which the amount of energy transferred from one to the other is not large compared to \\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T\\) (\\(k_{\\rm{B}}\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(T\\) the absolute temperature).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06666))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06666 "weber")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06666 "SI derived unit of magnetic flux, \\(\\pu{Wb} = \\pu{V s} = \\pu{m2 kg s-2 A-1}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06667))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06667 "wedge projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06667 "A stereochemical projection, roughly in the mean plane of the molecule, in which bonds are represented by open wedges, tapering off from the nearer atom to the farther atom. It is mainly used to illustrate the conformation of larger cycloalkanes e.g. cyclotetradecane:   equivalent to")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06668))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06668 "weight")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06668 "Force of gravity acting on a body, \\(G=m\\ g\\), where \\(m\\) is its mass and \\(g\\) the acceleration of free fall.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06670))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06670 "weighted mean")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06670 "If in a series of observations a statistical weight (\\(w_{i}\\)) is assigned to each value, a weighted mean \\(\\overline{x}_{w}\\) can be calculated by the formula: \\[\\overline{x}_{w}=\\frac{\\sum w_{i}\\ x_{i}}{\\sum w_{i}}\\]")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06671))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06671 "Weller correlation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06671 "Empirical correlation for the energy of full charge-transfer exciplex relative to the ground state in n-hexane as a function of the electrochemical one electron standard reduction potential of the cation radical produced upon electron donation and standard reduction potential of the acceptor measured in a polar solvent for the donor (D) and the acceptor (A) involved \\[\\Delta H(\\rm{D}^{+}\\rm{A}^{-},\\rm{hexane}) = e\\left [ E^{0}(\\rm{D}^{+\\bullet }/\\rm{D}) - E^{0}(\\rm{A}/\\rm{A}^{-\\bullet }) \\right ]+e\\Delta E\\] with \\(E^{0}(D^{+\\bullet }/D)\\) and \\(E^{0}(A/D^{-\\bullet })\\) the standard electrode potentials of the donor and acceptor, respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06673))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06673 "wet bulb temperature")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06673 "In psychrometry, the temperature of the sensor or the bulb of a thermometer in which a constantly renewed film of water is evaporating. The temperature of the water used to renew the film must be at the temperature of the gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06674))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06674 "wetting tension")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06674 "The work done on a system when the process of immersional wetting involving unit area of phase $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$ is carried out reversibly: \\[w_{\\rm{W}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }=\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }- \\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\] where \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\) and \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\) are the surface tensions between two bulk phases $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, $\\ce{\\updelta\\!}$, respectively.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06674 "work of immersional wetting per unit area")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06676))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06676 "Wiegner effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06676 "Synonymous with suspension effect (in an ion-selective electrode).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06678))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06678 "Wilzbach labelling")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06678 "Labelling of a substance by exposing it to tritium gas.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06679))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06679 "wind rose")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06679 "A diagram designed to show the distribution of wind direction experienced at a given location over a considerable period of time. Usually shown in polar coordinates (distance from the origin being proportional to the probability of the wind direction being at the given angle usually measured from the north). Similar diagrams are sometimes used to summarize the average concentrations of a given pollutant seen over a considerable period of time as a function of direction from a given site (sometimes called a pollution rose).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06679 "pollution rose")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06681))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06681 "Wood horn")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06681 "A mechanical device that acts by absorption as a perfect photon trap.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06682))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06682 "Wood lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06682 "Term used to describe a low-pressure mercury arc possessing a fluorescing layer, which emits in the UV-A region (from \\(315\\) to \\(400\\ \\rm{nm}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06684))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06684 "work")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06684 "Scalar product of force, \\(\\boldsymbol{F}\\), and position change, \\(\\rm{d}\\boldsymbol{r}\\), \\(w = \\int \\boldsymbol{F}\\cdot \\rm{d}\\mathbf{r}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06685))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06685 "work hardening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06685 "Opposite of work softening, in which shear results in a permanent increase of viscosity or consistency with time.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06686))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06686 "working electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06686 "An electrode that serves as a transducer responding to the excitation signal and the concentration of the substance of interest in the solution being investigated, and that permits the flow of current sufficiently large to effect appreciable changes of bulk composition within the ordinary duration of a measurement.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06687))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06687 "work of adhesion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06687 "The work of adhesion per unit area, \\(w_{\\rm{A}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\), is the work done on the system when two condensed phases $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, forming an interface of unit area are separated reversibly to form unit areas of each of the $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox{-}}$ interfaces. \\[w_{\\rm{A}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }=\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }+\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }- \\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\] where \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B2} }\\), \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\) and \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B2} \\unicode[Times]{x3B4} }\\) are the surface tensions between two bulk phases $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$; $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, $\\ce{\\updelta\\!}$ and $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!}$, $\\ce{\\updelta\\!}$ respectively. The work of adhesion as defined above, and traditionally used, may be called the work of separation.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06687 "work of separation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06688))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06688 "work of cohesion per unit area")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06688 "Of a single pure liquid or solid phase $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$, \\(w_{\\rm{C}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) is the work done on the system when a column $\\ce{\\upalpha\\!}$ of unit area is split, reversibly, normal to the axis of the column to form two new surfaces each of unit area in contact with the equilibrium gas phase. \\[w_{\\rm{C}}^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }=2\\ \\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\] where \\(\\gamma ^{\\unicode[Times]{x3B1} }\\) is the surface tension between phase and its equilibrium vapour or a dilute gas phase.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06691))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06691 "work softening")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06691 "The application of a finite shear to a system after a long rest may result in a decrease of the viscosity or the consistency. If the decrease persists when the shear is discontinued, this behaviour is called work softening (or shear breakdown), whereas if the original viscosity or consistency is recovered this behaviour is called thixotropy.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06691 "shear breakdown")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:W06692))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:W06692 "worm-like chain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:W06692 "A hypothetical linear macromolecule consisting of an infinitely thin chain of continuous curvature; the direction of curvature at any point is random. The model describes the whole spectrum of chains with different degrees of chain stiffness from rigid rods to random coils, and is particularly useful for representing stiff chains. In the literature this chain is sometimes referred to as a Porod–Kratky chain. Synonymous with continuously curved chain.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06692 "Porod–Kratky chain")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:W06692 "continuously curved chain")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:WT06852))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:WT06852 "weathering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:WT06852 "Exposure of a polymeric material to a natural or simulated environment.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:WT07250))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:WT07250 "wetting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:WT07250 "Process by which an interface between a solid and a gas is replaced by an interface between the same solid and a liquid.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:WT07499))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:WT07499 "wolfram lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:WT07499 "Incandescent lamp that generates light by passing an electric current through a thin filament wire (usually of wolfram) until it is extremely hot. The lamps are often filled by a halogen gas such as iodine and bromine that allow filaments to work at higher temperatures and higher efficiencies.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06693))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06693 "ξ-")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06693 "A symbol used to denote unknown configuration at a chiral centre.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:X06693 "xi-")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06695))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06695 "xanthene dyes")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06695 "Dyes derived by condensation of phthalic anhydride with resorcinol (and derivatives) or m-aminophenol (and derivatives), of which fluorescein is the prototype (all such dyes have the xanthene nucleus).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:X06695 "fluoresceins")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06696))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06696 "xanthic acids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06696 "Compounds having the structure $\\ce{ROC(=S)SH}$. Thus O-esters of dithiocarbonic acid. Salts and esters of xanthic acid are 'xanthates'. Use of this term is not recommended.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06697))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06697 "xanthophylls")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06697 "A subclass of carotenoids consisting of the oxygenated carotenes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06699))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06699 "xenon lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06699 "An intense source of ultraviolet, visible and near-infrared light produced by electrical discharge in xenon under high pressure.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06700))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06700 "xerogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06700 "Open network formed by the removal of all swelling agents from a gel.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06703))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06703 "X-radiation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06703 "Radiation resulting from the interaction of high energy particles or photons with matter.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06709))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06709 "X-ray escape peak")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06709 "In a gamma or X-ray spectrum, the peak due to the photoelectric effect in the detector and escape, from the sensitive part of the detector, of the X-ray photon emitted as a result of the photoelectric effect.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06710))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06710 "X-ray fluorescence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06710 "The emission of characteristic X-radiation by an atom as a result of the interaction of electromagnetic radiation with its orbital electrons.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06711))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06711 "X-ray fluorescence analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06711 "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with electromagnetic radiation.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06714))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06714 "X-ray intensity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06714 "Essentially all X-ray measurements are made by photon counting techniques but the results are seldom converted to radiant flux or irradiance or radiant exposure. The term photon flux would be appropriate if the measurements were corrected for detector efficiency but this is seldom done for X-ray chemical analysis. Therefore the term X-ray intensity, \\(I\\), is commonly used and expressed as photons/unit time detected. Likewise the term relative X-ray intensity, \\(I_{\\rm{r}}\\), is used to mean the intensity for the analyte in an unknown specimen divided by the intensity for a known concentration of the analyte element.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06715))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06715 "X-ray level")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06715 "An electronic state occuring as the initial or final state of a process involving the absorption or emission of X-ray radiation. It represents a many-electron state which, in the purely atomic case, has total angular momentum (\\(J = L + S\\)) as a well defined quantum number.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06716))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06716 "X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06716 "Any technique in which the sample is bombarded with X-rays and photoelectrons produced by the sample are detected as a function of energy. ESCA (Electron Spectroscopy for Chemical Analysis) refers to the use of this technique to identify elements, their concentrations, and their chemical state within the sample.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06717))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06717 "X-ray satellite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06717 "A weak line in the same energy region as a normal X-ray line. Another name used for weak features is non-diagram line. Recommendations as to the use of these two terms have conflicted. Using the term diagram line as defined here, non-diagram line may well be used for all lines with a different origin. The majority of these lines originate form the dipole-allowed de-excitation of multiply ionized or excited states, and are called multiple-ionization satellites. A line where the initial state has two vacencies in the same shell, notably the K-shell, is called a hypersatellite. Other mechanisms leading to weak spectral features in X-ray emission are, e.g. resonance emission, the radiative Auger effect, magnetic dipole and electric quadrupole transitions and, in metals, plasmon excitation. Atoms with open electron shells, i.e. transition metals, lanthanides and actinides, show a splitting of certain X-ray lines due to the electron interaction involving this open shell. Structures originating in all these ways as well as structures in the valence band of molecules and solid chemical compounds have in the past been given satellite designations.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06718))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06718 "X-ray spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06718 "X-ray spectroscopy consists of three steps: (a) excitation to produce emission lines characteristic of the elements in the material, (b) measurement of their intensity, and (c) conversion of X-ray intensity to concentration by a calibration procedure which may include correction for matrix effects.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:X06719))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:X06719 "x unit")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:X06719 "Non-SI unit of length: \\(\\rm{X}\\approx 1.002\\times 10^{-13}\\ \\rm{m}\\)")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:XT06755))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:XT06755 "xenobiotic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:XT06755 "A xenobiotic (Greek, xenos 'foreign'; bios 'life') is a compound that is foreign to a living organism. Principal xenobiotics include: drugs; carcinogens; and various compounds that have been introduced into the environment by artificial means.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06722))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06722 "yard")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06722 "Non-SI unit of length, \\(\\rm{yd} = 3\\ \\rm{ft} = 0.9144\\ \\rm{m}\\).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06723))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06723 "year")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06723 "Non-SI unit of time, \\(\\rm{a}=31\\ 556\\ 952\\ \\rm{s}\\). The year is not commensurable with the day and not a constant. The value given corresponds to the Gregorian calendar year (\\(\\rm{a}=365.2425\\ \\rm{d}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06724))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06724 "yield")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06724 "Ratio expressing the efficiency of a mass conversion process. The yield coefficient is defined as the amount of cell mass (\\(\\pu{kg}\\)) or product formed (\\(\\pu{kg}\\),\\(\\pu{mol}\\)) related to the consumed substrate (carbon or nitrogen source or oxygen in \\(\\pu{kg}\\) or \\(\\pu{moles}\\)) or to the intracellular ATP production (\\(\\pu{moles}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06727))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06727 "yield stress")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06727 "The shear stress \\(\\sigma _{0}\\) or \\(\\tau _{0}\\) at which yielding starts abruptly. Its value depends on the criterion used to determine when yielding occurs.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06728))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06728 "ylides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06728 "Compounds in which an anionic site $\\ce{Y^{−}}$ (originally on carbon, but now including other atoms) is attached directly to a heteroatom $\\ce{X^{+}}$ (usually nitrogen, phosphorus or sulfur) carrying a formal positive charge. They are thus 1,2-dipolar species of the type $\\ce{R_{m}X^{+}–Y^{−}R^{n}}$. If $\\ce{X}$ is a saturated atom of an element from the first row of the periodic system, the ylide is commonly represented by a charge-separated form; if $\\ce{X}$ is a second, third, etc. row element uncharged canonical forms are available $\\ce{R_{m}X=YR_{n}}$. If $\\ce{X}$ is an unsaturated atom, doubly bonded to another first row element $\\ce{Z}$, the negative charge on $\\ce{Y}$ may be stabilized by $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}}$conjugation, $\\ce{Z=X^{+}–Y^{−}Rn  Z^{−}–X^{+}=YR_{n}}$. Such ylides belong to the class 1,3 dipolar compounds. However, 1,3-dipolar compounds with only sextet-containing canonical forms (e.g. vinylcarbenes) are not ylides. E.g. $\\ce{Ph3P^{+}–C^{−}H2  Ph3P^{+}=CH2}$ (often called a Wittig reagent), $\\ce{(CH3)3N^{+}–C^{−}H2}$, $\\ce{RC#N^{+}N^{−}–R}$, $\\ce{(CH3)2S=CHPh  (CH3)2S^{+}–C^{−}HPh}$. Note that ylide is a complete word, not to be confused with the suffix -ylide, used for some radical anions. Subclasses of ylides: Ylides $\\ce{R_{m}X^{+}–C^{−}R2}$ having the negative charge on carbon are classified by citing the name of the element $\\ce{X}$ before the word ylide. E.g. nitrogen ylide, phosphorus ylide, oxygen ylide, sulfur ylide. A further specification may be achieved by citing the class name of $\\ce{R_{m}X}$ before the word ylide. Thus nitrogen ylides include amine ylides, $\\ce{R3N^{+}–C^{−}R2}$, azomethine ylides $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}R–C^{−}R2}$, nitrile ylides, $\\ce{RC#N^{+}–C^{−}R2}$. Some authors, who wish to express the positive charge on $\\ce{X}$, prefer e.g. ammonium ylides over amine ylides; such usage varies according to the heteroatom $\\ce{X}$ and to national custom. The ylides $\\ce{R_{m}X^{+}–Y^{−}  R_{m}X=Y}$ ($\\ce{Y} = \\ce{O}, \\ce{S}, \\ce{Se}, \\ce{Te}, \\ce{NR}$) are usually named by citing the name of $\\ce{R_{m}X}$ followed by the additive nomenclature term for $\\ce{Y}$ (oxide, sulfide, selenide, telluride, imide, respectively). E.g. amine imides; use of the less systematic synonyms amine imines and aminimines is discouraged. Some classes of ylides are known by trivial names e.g. nitrones, Wittig reagents (synonymous with phosphonium ylides).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06729))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06729 "ynamines")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06729 "N,N-Disubstituted alk-1-yn-1-amines, $\\ce{RC#CNR2}$. By usage, restricted to this type of acetylenic amine.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06730))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06730 "ynols")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06730 "Alk-1-yn-1-ols, $\\ce{RC≡COH}$; tautomeric with ketenes $\\ce{RCH=C=O}$.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06731))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06731 "yocto")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06731 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-24}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{y}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06732))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06732 "yotta")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06732 "SI prefix for \\(10^{24}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{Y}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Y06734))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Y06734 "Yukawa–Tsuno equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Y06734 "A multiparameter extension of the Hammett equation to quantify the role of enhanced resonance effect on the reactivity of meta- and para-substituted benzene derivatives, e.g. \\[\\log _{10}k = \\log _{10}k_{0}+\\rho \\ [\\sigma +r\\ (\\sigma^{+}-\\sigma)]\\] The parameter \\(r\\) gives the enhanced resonance effect on the scale (\\(\\sigma ^{+}\\,-\\sigma\\)) or (\\(\\sigma\\,-\\sigma \\)), respectively.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:YT07500))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:YT07500 "Yang photocyclization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:YT07500 "Intramolecular coupling of diradicals of different sizes (1,4-, 1,5-, 1,6-, 1,7-, 1,8-diradicals, as well as very remote diradicals) formed through intramolecular hydrogen abstraction by an excited ketone, yielding cyclic alcohols of different sizes.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06737))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06737 "<em>Z</em>-value")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06737 "An index of the ionizing power of a solvent based on the frequency of the longest wavelength electronic absorption maximum of 1-ethyl-4-methoxycarbonylpyridinium iodide in the solvent. The Z-value is defined by: \\[Z = \\frac{\\pu{2.859E4}}{\\lambda}\\] where \\(Z\\) is in \\(\\pu{kcal mol-1}\\) and \\(\\lambda \\) is in \\(\\pu{nm}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06737 "Kosower $Z$-value")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06739))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06739 "Zeeman effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06739 "The splitting or shift of spectral lines due to the presence of an external magnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06740))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06740 "zepto")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06740 "SI prefix for \\(10^{-21}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{z}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06741))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06741 "zero field splitting")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06741 "The separation of multiplet sublevels in the absence of an external magnetic field.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06742))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06742 "zero point")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06742 "Value of the pH of a solution, which in combination with a stated outer reference electrode, gives zero emf from the operational cell.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06743))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06743 "zero point of scale")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06743 "The rest point of the properly adjusted balance with no load on the pans and the rider (or chain) in the zero position.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06744))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06744 "zero–zero absorption or emission")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06744 "A purely electronic transition occurring between the lowest vibrational levels of two electronic states.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06744 "0–0 absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06744 "0–0 emission")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06744 "zero-zero absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06744 "zero-zero emission")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06746))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06746 "zetta")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06746 "SI prefix for \\(10^{21}\\) (symbol: \\(\\mathrm{Z}\\)).")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06747))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06747 "zig-zag projection")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06747 "A stereochemical projection for an acyclic molecule (or portion of a molecule) where the main chain is represented by a zig-zag line in the plane and the substituents are shown above or below the plane.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06748))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06748 "Zimm plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06748 "A diagrammatic representation of data on scattering from large particles, corresponding to the equation: \\[\\frac{Kc}{\\Delta R (\\theta)} = \\frac{1}{\\bar{M}_{\\rm{w}} P(\\theta)} + 2\\ A_{2}c + \\ldots\\] and used for the simultaneous evaluation of the mass average molar mass, \\(\\bar{M}_{\\rm{w}}\\), the second virial coefficient of the chemical potential, \\(A_{2}\\), and (usually) the z-average radius of gyration, \\(< s^{2} > _{\\rm{z}}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\); \\(c\\) is the mass concentration of the solute, \\(\\Delta R(\\theta)\\) the excess Rayleigh ratio, and \\(P (\\theta )\\) the particle scattering function that comprises (usually) the z-average radius of gyration. \\(K\\) depends on the solute, the temperature and the type of radiation employed. Several modifications of the Zimm plot are in frequent use; the most common one uses the excess scattering instead of the excess Rayleigh ratio.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06749))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06749 "zone")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06749 "A region in the chromatographic bed where one or more components of the sample are located. The term band may also be used for it.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06750))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06750 "zone melting method of preconcentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06750 "A technique based on the different solubility of microcomponents in the liquid and solid matrix. It has a limited field of application for the analysis of fusible (and stable) substances.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06751))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06751 "Zucker–Hammett hypothesis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06751 "This hypothesis states that, if in an acid catalysed reaction, \\(\\log _{10}k_{1}\\) (first-order rate constant of the reaction) is linear in \\(H_{0}\\) (Hammett acidity function), water is not involved in the transition state of the rate-controlling step. However, if \\(\\log _{10}k_{1}\\) is linear in \\(\\log _{10}\\left[\\rm{H}^{+}\\right]\\) then water is involved. This has been shown to be incorrect by Hammett himself.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06752))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06752 "zwitterions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06752 "Neutral compounds having formal unit electrical charges of opposite sign. Some chemists restrict the term to compounds with the charges on non-adjacent atoms. Sometimes referred to as inner salts, dipolar ions (a misnomer). E.g. $\\ce{H3N^{+}CH2C(=O)O^{−}}$ ammonioacetate (glycine), $\\ce{(CH3)3N^{+}–O^{−}}$ trimethylamine oxide.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06752 "inner salts")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06752 "zwitterionic compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:Z06752 "ampholytes")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:Z06753))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:Z06753 "zygote")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:Z06753 "The cell obtained as a result of complete or partial fusion of meiotically (cf. meiosis) produced cells.")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ZT07132))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ZT07132 "zero differential overlap approximation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ZT07132 "An approach to the systematic neglect of the small-in-value electron repulsion integrals which is used in a number of approximate self-consistent field molecular orbital schemes. It means that all the products of atomic orbitals \\(\\upchi_{\\upmu} \\upchi_{\\upnu}\\) are set to zero and the overlap integral \\(\\rm{S}_{\\upmu\\upnu} = \\updelta_{\\upmu\\upnu}\\) (where \\(\\updelta_{\\upmu\\upnu}\\) is the Kronecker delta). The ZDO approximation greatly simplifies the computation of wavefunctions by eliminating many of two-electron integrals. At the ZDO approximation all three- and four-centered integrals vanish.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ZT07132 "ZDO approximation")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ZT07228))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ZT07228 "zwitterionic polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ZT07228 "Ampholytic polymer containing ionic groups of opposite sign, commonly on the same pendant groups.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:ZT07228 "polymeric inner salt")
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ZT07501))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ZT07501 "$\\pu{\\rm{Z}_{50}}$")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ZT07501 "Number of cycles required to reduce by 50% the initial absorbance of the coloured form of a photochromic compound at a specific wavelength.")
)